(window.LQ_PACKS = window.LQ_PACKS || {})["databricks"] = {"format":"lq-pack","v":1,"subject":{"id":"databricks","title":"Databricks","appTitle":"Databricks Quest","emoji":"🧱","group":"data-cloud","description":"Lakehouse platform, Delta Lake, compute, Unity Catalog, ingestion and Structured Streaming — exam-ready and project-ready.","language":"en","owner":null,"features":{"math":false,"code":true},"hero":{"headline":"Master **Databricks**, one bite at a time.","mantra":"**Spark computes. Storage persists. Delta gives stored files table semantics. Unity Catalog governs the objects.** Databricks integrates the pieces into one platform."},"searchExamples":"VACUUM, widgets, Dedicated, CDC","tutor":{"name":"Brick","avatar":"🦉","domain":"Databricks","prior":"They already know Spark fundamentals (execution hierarchy, partitions, shuffle, Catalyst/AQE, joins, Spark UI) and are preparing for the Databricks Data Engineer exams while building real projects.","examples":"Concrete e-shop examples (orders, customers).","interviewer":"a senior interviewer hiring a Databricks Data Engineer","simulation":"a failing job, PERMISSION_DENIED, missing rows, duplicates, slow query, OOM, MERGE error, wrong compute, stuck stream","terminology":"\"Unity Catalog\", \"Auto Loader\", \"MERGE INTO\", \"Deletion Vectors\", \"Watermarks\"","examinerRole":"Databricks examiner"}},"sources":{"_doc":"Source registry for this subject. Order = chronological (last = newest). Maps chapter files and patch files to the source they came from. Items may carry an explicit 'src' that overrides inheritance.","sources":[{"id":"part1","title":"Databricks — Part 1","subtitle":"Prerequisites, platform tour, Delta Lake, compute, mastery roadmap","pages":"1–402","file":"sources/part1_pages_1-402.pdf","added":"2026-10-06","emoji":"📘"},{"id":"part2","title":"Databricks — Part 2","subtitle":"Unity Catalog, data ingestion (COPY INTO, Auto Loader), Structured Streaming","pages":"403–569","file":"sources/part2_pages_403-569.pdf","added":"2026-10-06","emoji":"📗"}],"chapters":{"ch01":"part1","ch02":"part1","ch03":"part1","ch04":"part1","ch05":"part1","ch06":"part1","ch07":"part1","ch08":"part1","ch09":"part1","ch10":"part1","ch11":"part2","ch12":"part2","ch13":"part2"},"patches":{"ch11_links.json":"part2","ch12_links.json":"part2","ch13_links.json":"part2"}},"chapters":[{"id":"ch01","num":1,"title":"Foundations: Storage, Tables & Transactions","subtitle":"Why a folder of Parquet files isn't a table, and what Delta, Spark and Unity Catalog each add","emoji":"🧱","sourcePages":"1–26","mantra":"Spark computes. Storage persists. Delta gives stored files table semantics. Unity Catalog governs the objects.","objectives":["You can explain why compute and storage have independent lifetimes, and what survives cluster termination.","You can explain why a file is not a table and why Parquet alone is not enough for concurrent updates.","You can explain column pruning and tell it apart from data skipping / partition pruning.","You can define each ACID property with an example and avoid the ACID-vs-CAP consistency trap.","You can replay a Delta log (ADD/REMOVE) to find the current snapshot, and explain physical existence ≠ logical membership.","You can read a Unity Catalog name (catalog.schema.object) and keep metadata, Delta, Spark and storage apart.","You can describe the control plane vs the compute plane (serverless vs classic).","You can debug a slow query layer by layer instead of just scaling the cluster."],"sections":[{"id":"ch01-s01","title":"Orientation: From Spark to Databricks","hook":"You already know Spark's engine. Now you learn the platform built around it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"This course **builds on your Spark knowledge** instead of restarting from zero. Spark ideas come back inside real Databricks debugging cases, not as a separate Spark course."},{"t":"list","items":["Execution hierarchy, partitions and **shuffle** mechanics","Catalyst / AQE, statistics and size estimates","`df.explain(mode=\"extended\")` and `df.explain(mode=\"cost\")`","Spark UI reasoning","Physical join strategies: BHJ, SMJ, SHJ, BNLJ"],"ordered":false},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"BHJ","def":"Broadcast Hash Join: the small side is copied to every executor, so the big side does not shuffle."},{"term":"SMJ","def":"Sort-Merge Join: both sides are shuffled by key, sorted, then merged."},{"term":"SHJ","def":"Shuffled Hash Join: both sides are shuffled, then a hash table is built per partition."},{"term":"BNLJ","def":"Broadcast Nested Loop Join: the fallback for non-equi joins. One side is broadcast and every row pair is compared."},{"term":"AQE","def":"Adaptive Query Execution: Spark re-optimizes the plan at runtime using real shuffle statistics."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Your known weak spot","text":"The thing that still needs work is an **intuitive picture of what physically moves during a shuffle** and how that turns into join execution. Expect it to come back in debugging cases."},{"t":"p","text":"The current **Databricks Data Engineer Associate exam** tests much more than syntax:"},{"t":"table","head":["Area","Exam topics named in the source"],"rows":[["Platform","platform architecture, compute selection"],["Data & tables","Delta Lake, ingestion, PySpark transformations, Liquid Clustering, predictive optimization"],["Operations","Lakeflow Jobs, CI/CD / Automation Bundles"],["Troubleshooting","Spark UI troubleshooting, skew / shuffling / spilling, OOM diagnosis"],["Governance","Unity Catalog, governance / security"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"The exam went operational","text":"The exam guide now leans toward **operational** skills: diagnosing skew, shuffle/spill and OOMs, CI/CD, Lakeflow Jobs and governance. Memorizing will not be enough. You need to be able to *reason about running systems*."},{"t":"diagram","text":"YOUR EXISTING SPARK KNOWLEDGE\n ├─ Spark execution model\n ├─ partitions / shuffle\n ├─ Catalyst / physical plans\n ├─ joins\n ├─ AQE\n ├─ Spark UI / explain()\n └─ DataFrame API\n        │\n        ▼\nPREREQUISITES — ROUND 1      ◄ THIS CHAPTER\n ├─ compute vs storage\n ├─ object storage\n ├─ file ≠ table\n ├─ columnar storage / Parquet\n ├─ database metadata\n ├─ ACID transactions\n ├─ transaction logs\n └─ data lake → lakehouse motivation\n        │\n        ▼\nPREREQUISITES — ROUND 2      (next chapter)\n ├─ ETL vs ELT\n └─ …\n        │\n        ▼\nDATABRICKS PROPER → production, debugging, certification","caption":"The Databricks mastery DAG"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The one-sentence mental model","text":"**Spark computes. Storage persists. Delta gives stored files table semantics. Unity Catalog governs the objects.** Databricks integrates these pieces into one platform."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why learn storage, tables and transactions before you open the Databricks UI?","text":"Much of Databricks' behavior, and many exam traps, come from **mixing up layers**: compute vs storage, file vs table, Delta vs Parquet, Unity Catalog vs Delta. If you can keep these layers apart, the UI and APIs make sense."}]},{"id":"ch01-s02","title":"Compute vs Storage: Independent Lifetimes","hook":"Clusters come and go. Your data must not.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine an e-shop with `customers`, `orders` and `products`. Something must **store** those bytes while nobody is processing them."},{"t":"p","text":"Something else must **execute** a query over them:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount)\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY customer_id;"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Storage","points":["Answers: *Where are the bytes after my program stops running?*","Amazon S3, Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS), Google Cloud Storage (GCS)","Persistent: survives when nothing is running"]},{"title":"Compute","points":["Answers: *Which CPUs and RAM execute my code?*","For Spark: a driver plus executors","Temporary: can disappear after the work is done"]}]},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"s3://company-data/orders/...\nabfss://data@company.dfs.core.windows.net/orders/...\ngs://company-data/orders/...","caption":"Storage locations on S3, ADLS and GCS"},{"t":"diagram","text":"      Driver\n        │\n  ┌─────┼─────────┐\n  ▼     ▼         ▼\n Executor Executor Executor     ◄ COMPUTE (temporary)\n  ▲         ▲\n  │ reads   │ reads\n parquet   parquet\n  file      file\n  └────┬────┘\n  Object storage                ◄ STORAGE (persistent)","caption":"Executors read files from object storage"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Independent lifetimes","text":"The compute resources can disappear afterward. **The data remains.** This separation is central to modern lakehouse systems."},{"t":"p","text":"Why is this useful? Take a **10 TB** dataset with a compute pattern that changes every day:"},{"t":"table","head":["Day","Machines","10 TB dataset"],"rows":[["Monday","20","still there"],["Tuesday","0","still there"],["Wednesday","100","still there"]]},{"t":"p","text":"You do not want your 10 TB to vanish because Tuesday has zero workers. You pay for compute only when you need it and **scale it independently** of the data."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks makes the same split. The **workspace** holds artifacts and configurations, such as notebooks and settings. **Persistent data** usually lives in your cloud storage."},{"t":"table","head":["What","After you terminate the cluster"],"rows":[["Executor RAM","Gone"],["Temporary local Spark / shuffle files","Normally gone with the compute environment"],["Files in object storage (e.g. `s3://shop/orders/`)","Remain"]],"caption":"Exercise 1 in table form"},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Kitchen analogy","text":"Storage is the **pantry**. Compute is the **cooks**. Send all the cooks home and the food is still in the pantry. Hire 100 cooks tomorrow and they all cook from the same pantry."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Don't keep anything important in compute","text":"Shuffle files, local temp files and executor memory belong to the compute environment. Treat them as **disposable**. Only data written to persistent storage survives the cluster."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: 100 TB dataset; 50 workers at 08:00, 200 at 12:00, 0 at 18:00. Why is this a good design?","text":"Persistent storage survives on its own, while compute capacity **scales with the workload**. You add workers at peak, pay nothing at 18:00, and the 100 TB is untouched the whole time."}]},{"id":"ch01-s03","title":"A File Is Not a Table","hook":"Three Parquet files are not yet an `orders` table. Something has to say which files count.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Suppose storage contains:"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"/orders/\n  part-00000.parquet\n  part-00001.parquet\n  part-00002.parquet"},{"t":"p","text":"Those are **files**. You may *logically* read them as \"the `orders` table\", but files alone do not give you full database-table behavior."},{"t":"p","text":"Now run an update on one e-shop order:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"UPDATE orders\nSET status = 'cancelled'\nWHERE order_id = 123;"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: order 123 lives inside part-00001.parquet. Why can't the engine just change that one cell?","text":"Columnar files are **immutable-ish**: values are grouped by column, encoded and compressed. They are not a grid of editable cells. The engine usually writes a **new file version** that contains the changed rows."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Not an Excel cell","text":"Don't picture `UPDATE` as editing a cell in Excel. For columnar files the engine often has to **create a new file** that holds the changed rows."},{"t":"diagram","text":"part-00001.parquet  ──►  old file: NO LONGER part of table\npart-00003.parquet  ──►  new file: NOW part of table\n                (contains order 123 = 'cancelled')\n\n    who records this?  ──►  metadata / transaction state","caption":"After an UPDATE, someone must track which files count"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The missing piece","text":"Something must know *old file → no longer part of the table* and *new file → now part of the table*. That something is **metadata / transaction state**. In Databricks it is the Delta transaction log."}]},{"id":"ch01-s04","title":"What Parquet Gives You: Columnar Storage & Pruning","hook":"Read only the columns, and later only the files, that the query needs.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Take a small `people` table: `id | name | age | country`, with rows (1, Maria, 25, GR), (2, John, 37, US), (3, Anna, 42, DE)."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Row-oriented (naive)","points":["`1, Maria, 25, GR`","`2, John, 37, US`","`3, Anna, 42, DE`","Whole rows stored together"]},{"title":"Columnar (Parquet idea)","points":["`id: 1 2 3`","`name: Maria John Anna`","`age: 25 37 42`","`country: GR US DE`"]}]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Row group","def":"A horizontal slice of rows inside a Parquet file."},{"term":"Column chunk","def":"The data of one column within one row group."},{"term":"Page","def":"The smallest unit inside a column chunk. Encoding and compression are applied at this level."},{"term":"Encodings / compression","def":"Ways to store a column compactly. Similar values sit together, so they compress well."},{"term":"File metadata","def":"Schema and statistics stored in the file footer."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Real Parquet is more sophisticated than this picture, but the picture explains why **analytical engines** like it."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT AVG(age)\nFROM people;"},{"t":"p","text":"This query needs `age`. It does not need `id`, `name`, `country`, `address`, `email`…"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Column pruning","text":"Columnar formats let the engine **skip irrelevant columns**. This is called **column pruning**. It reduces I/O."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"ReadSchema: struct<age:int>","caption":"What you'll see in a Spark plan, even if the table has 100 columns"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"ReadSchema is not cosmetic","text":"`ReadSchema: struct<age:int>` means Spark **requested only the column it needs** from storage. It is evidence that pruning happened, not a decorative line in the plan."},{"t":"p","text":"**Exercise 2.** The table is `sales(sale_id, customer_id, product_id, amount, country, timestamp, salesperson, comments)` and the query is:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT country, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY country;"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Which columns are fundamentally needed from storage?","text":"Only `country` and `amount`. The other six columns can potentially be pruned, which reduces I/O."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Column pruning","points":["Reduces **which columns** are read","Comes from the columnar format","e.g. read 2 of 80 columns"]},{"title":"Data skipping / partition / clustering pruning","points":["Reduces **which files or regions** are scanned","Comes from table layout + statistics","e.g. read only files for one date"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Two different mechanisms (Challenge Q14)","text":"A 5 TB table, and the query needs 2 columns and one date. **Column pruning** cuts the columns. **Data skipping / partition or clustering pruning** cuts the files or regions. They are different mechanisms, and later chapters cover each one in detail."}]},{"id":"ch01-s05","title":"Why Files Need Transactions: ACID","hook":"A reader must never see a state that the table was never in.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine `/orders/` holds `A.parquet`, `B.parquet`, `C.parquet`. **Reader X** starts reading A, B, C."},{"t":"p","text":"At the same moment, **writer Y** runs an update and changes the directory to A, D, E."},{"t":"diagram","text":"time ─────────────────────────────────────────►\nReader X:  read A ....... read B ....... read C?\nWriter Y:        [ replace B,C with D,E ]\n\nOK      : A B C   (old state)\nOK      : A D E   (new state)\nDANGER  : A B E   (a state that never existed)","caption":"Concurrent reader and writer on a plain directory"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: what's wrong with seeing A B E?","text":"A B E may correspond to **no valid state of the table at all**: half old, half new. This is where database transactional semantics come in."},{"t":"table","head":["Letter","Meaning","E-shop example"],"rows":[["**A**tomicity","A transaction happens completely or not at all. The commit becomes visible as **one logical change**.","A pipeline replaces 80 files. Nobody should see 37 old + 43 new."],["**C**onsistency","Each transaction moves the database from one valid state to another and respects enforced invariants.","An order table never ends up violating its enforced rules."],["**I**solation","Concurrent operations don't produce invalid interleavings. A reader sees a coherent snapshot.","Reader R sees version 87 while W creates 88, never half-87-half-88."],["**D**urability","Once a transaction commits, that state survives later process or compute failures.","The cluster crashes after the commit and the new orders are still there."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Two meanings of \"consistency\"","text":"ACID **Consistency** means valid state → valid state, respecting invariants. It is **not** the C in the CAP theorem from distributed systems. Same English word, different concept."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Reader R ──► snapshot v87  (coherent)\nWriter W ──► creating v88\n\nnever: rows from v87 + rows from v88 mixed","caption":"Isolation"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Why Parquet alone can't do this (Q15)","text":"Parquet describes **how data is stored in files**. By itself it gives no transaction history, no atomic commits, no coherent snapshots and no table-state management. Those are what concurrent, database-like modifications need."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a reader sees mixed or partial data","questions":["Is this a plain directory of Parquet files with no transaction log?","Am I reading the files directly by path instead of through the table's committed snapshot?","Was a writer replacing files while I was reading?","Which guarantee failed: atomicity (half-done commit visible) or isolation (interleaving)?"]}]},{"id":"ch01-s06","title":"Delta Lake & the Transaction Log","hook":"Parquet holds the rows. `_delta_log` decides which files are the table.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Traditional **data lakes** gave cheap, scalable storage, but they historically lacked the guarantees expected from databases. Databricks' core answer is **Delta Lake**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Definition","text":"Delta Lake extends **Parquet data files** with a **file-based transaction log**. That gives ACID transactions and scalable metadata management while staying compatible with the Spark APIs. Delta is the **default table format** on Databricks unless you specify otherwise."},{"t":"diagram","text":"my_table/\n ├─ part-00000.parquet\n ├─ part-00001.parquet\n ├─ part-00002.parquet\n └─ _delta_log/\n      └─ …  (one commit per table version)","caption":"A Delta table on storage (simplified)"},{"t":"table","head":["Piece","Role"],"rows":[["Parquet files","the actual rows"],["`_delta_log`","which files make up each valid table version"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Each version is a commit made of **ADD** and **REMOVE** actions. Replay them in order to get a snapshot:"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"version 0:  add A   add B\nversion 1:  remove B   add C\nversion 2:  add D\n\nsnapshot(v0) = A + B\nsnapshot(v1) = A + C\nsnapshot(v2) = A + C + D","caption":"Replaying the log"},{"t":"p","text":"Notice that `B.parquet` may still **physically exist** for some time. The current snapshot simply says *B is not part of the current table*."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The essential insight","text":"**Physical existence of a file ≠ logical membership in the current table.** The relevant snapshot decides membership, not the file listing."},{"t":"list","items":["`DELETE`","`UPDATE`","`MERGE`","Time Travel","`VACUUM`","concurrent writes"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"Every topic above depends on that insight. You will meet each of them in later chapters."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Parquet files ≠ Delta table","text":"`part-0000.parquet`, `part-0001.parquet`… do **not** automatically mean you have a Delta table. Without a `_delta_log` there is no transaction log, so it is not Delta."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Uncommitted files are invisible (Q11)","text":"If Spark has physically written three replacement files but the transaction has **not committed**, readers of the current table must not see them. Creating files is not the same as **publishing a committed version**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Read the table, not the folder","text":"If you list the folder, or read it as plain Parquet, you see **every** physical file, including removed and uncommitted ones. Read through the Delta table so that the log decides what counts."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when files exist in storage but their rows aren't in the table","questions":["Does the current snapshot reference this file, or did a later version REMOVE it?","Did the transaction that wrote this file ever commit?","Was this file dropped into the folder by a plain Parquet write that never touched the log?","Am I treating physical existence as logical membership?"]}]},{"id":"ch01-s07","title":"SQL Operations vs Physical Files","hook":"`DELETE` looks like row surgery. Underneath, it is a file swap plus a commit.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"DELETE FROM orders\nWHERE order_id = 123;"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Beginner model","points":["find row 123","erase row"]},{"title":"Better model (file-oriented storage)","points":["find the file that contains 123 (A.parquet)","write a replacement file without 123 (B.parquet)","commit: REMOVE A, ADD B"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":"A.parquet            B.parquet\n──────────           ──────────\n120                  120\n121                  121\n123   ── rewrite ──► 124\n124                  125\n125\n\ncommit:  REMOVE A.parquet   ADD B.parquet\nbefore:  table → A        after:  table → B","caption":"A DELETE as a file rewrite plus a transaction"},{"t":"flow","items":["Locate file with row","Write replacement file","Commit REMOVE old + ADD new","New snapshot uses new file"],"caption":"DELETE under the hood (foundation model)"},{"t":"p","text":"The operation looks like normal database **DML** (`DELETE`). Underneath it may involve **file-level rewriting** and **transaction metadata**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"This model will get more detail","text":"Modern Delta has extra mechanisms and optimizations that refine this picture in later chapters. The rewrite + REMOVE/ADD model is still the correct foundation."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"\"Deleted\" ≠ physically erased","text":"After the commit, `A.parquet` is no longer part of the table, but it may still **physically exist** for a while. The snapshot changed. The bytes in storage didn't have to."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first (Q3): why can a Delta DELETE create new Parquet files?","text":"Columnar data files are not edited as individual mutable cells. A change may produce **replacement files**, plus transaction metadata recording which files now belong to the table."}]},{"id":"ch01-s08","title":"Data Lake vs Data Warehouse → Lakehouse","hook":"Understand the word *lakehouse* instead of memorizing it.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Data lake (traditional)","points":["Cheap / object storage","Files","Flexible schemas and formats","Huge raw datasets","ML / data-science friendly","Historically weaker transaction/table guarantees"]},{"title":"Data warehouse (traditional)","points":["Structured tables","SQL","Transactions","Governance","BI","Optimized analytical querying","Historically more rigid and separate from raw/ML data"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":"Data lake characteristics\n          +\nDatabase / warehouse semantics\n          │\n          ▼\n      LAKEHOUSE","caption":"The lakehouse idea, roughly"},{"t":"p","text":"**Delta Lake** is a major part of how Databricks gives object-storage data database-like table semantics. Databricks describes Delta as the **optimized storage layer underpinning lakehouse tables**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Not \"Spark with notebooks\"","text":"Databricks is **not** simply Spark with notebooks. The platform connects storage, transactional tables, Spark/Photon compute, governance, orchestration, SQL/BI, ML/AI and DevOps."},{"t":"flow","items":["storage","transactional tables","Spark/Photon compute","governance","orchestration","SQL/BI","ML/AI","DevOps"],"caption":"What Databricks connects"},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Q13: Is Delta Lake a database?","text":"\"Delta Lake is a database\" is imprecise. Delta Lake is a **transactional storage/table layer over files**, not a standalone traditional database server."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: what does the lake bring, and what does the warehouse bring?","text":"The lake brings cheap object storage, files, flexible formats and room for raw/ML data. The warehouse brings tables, SQL, transactions, governance and BI performance. The lakehouse aims to combine both on the same data."}]},{"id":"ch01-s09","title":"Table Metadata & the Unity Catalog Namespace","hook":"How does `prod.sales.orders` turn into actual files? Through metadata.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *\nFROM prod.sales.orders;"},{"t":"p","text":"How does the system know what `prod.sales.orders` means? It needs **metadata**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Metadata (describes the data)","points":["catalog: `prod`","schema: `sales`","table name: `orders`","columns: `order_id BIGINT`, `customer_id BIGINT`, `amount DECIMAL(10,2)`","format: `DELTA`","storage location: `s3://.../orders/`","owner: `data_engineers`","permissions: …"]},{"title":"Data (the rows)","points":["`123, 4921, 79.95`","`124, 8177, 20.30`"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Metadata ≠ data","text":"**Metadata describes the data.** Keep these apart and Unity Catalog becomes easy to understand later."},{"t":"p","text":"In modern Databricks, **Unity Catalog** organizes governed objects with a **three-level namespace**, and it is the centralized governance layer for tables and other data/AI assets."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"catalog.schema.object"},{"t":"diagram","text":"SELECT * FROM ecommerce.silver.orders;\n\necommerce  ◄ catalog\nsilver     ◄ schema\norders     ◄ table (object)","caption":"Reading a three-level name"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Not server.database.table","text":"Don't map `catalog.schema.object` onto `server.database.table` from traditional databases. The first part is a **catalog**, not a server. Exact semantics come later."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Unity Catalog ≠ Delta Lake","text":"Unity Catalog = **metadata + governance** (names, ownership, permissions). Delta Lake = **transactional table format** over files. They work together but are different layers."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: in `company.finance.transactions`, which part is the catalog?","text":"`company` is the catalog, `finance` is the schema and `transactions` is the object (table)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → the full UC hierarchy","text":"Metastore → catalog → schema → object, managed vs external storage, and why UC governs metadata but never stores the bytes are now covered in depth — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch01-s10","title":"The One Diagram: Spark, Delta, UC & Storage","hook":"Four related things, not synonyms. Confusing them is a classic certification trap.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"            YOU\n             │  SQL / Python\n             ▼\n   ┌────────────────────┐\n   │   Compute engine   │\n   │   Spark / Photon   │\n   └─────────┬──────────┘\n             │ reads / writes\n             ▼\n   ┌────────────────────┐\n   │    Delta table     │\n   │ Parquet data files │\n   │ + transaction log  │\n   └─────────┬──────────┘\n             │ physically lives in\n             ▼\n   ┌────────────────────┐\n   │   Cloud storage    │\n   │  S3 / ADLS / GCS   │\n   └────────────────────┘","caption":"Keep this model"},{"t":"p","text":"You already know `spark.read.parquet(...)` and `df.write.parquet(...)`. **Spark** is the processing engine. **Parquet** is a storage file format."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.read.parquet(path)          # Spark reads Parquet files\ndf.write.parquet(path)                  # Spark writes Parquet files\n\ndf.write.format(\"delta\").save(path)    # Parquet files + Delta log\ndf.write.saveAsTable(\"catalog.schema.orders\")  # governed table"},{"t":"flow","items":["Spark","produces Parquet data files","updates Delta transaction metadata"],"caption":"df.write.format(\"delta\").save(...)"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Unity Catalog object   (catalog.schema.orders)\n        │\n        ▼\n   Delta table\n        │\n        ▼\n  cloud storage","caption":"saveAsTable adds a governed layer"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Don't memorize syntax yet","text":"Later chapters take each form apart, including when these snippets are equivalent and when they **absolutely are not**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Databricks-style question:** you run `df.write.saveAsTable(\"prod.sales.orders\")`, then later terminate the compute resource. Does `prod.sales.orders` necessarily disappear?"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Cluster gone ≠ table gone","text":"**No.** The compute environment and the persistent data have **different lifetimes**. This sounds trivial, but it explains a large class of Databricks architecture questions."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Spark vs Delta (Q2)","text":"Spark is mainly a **distributed computation engine**. Delta Lake supplies **transactional table/storage semantics** over data files."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a table \"disappeared\" after the cluster was terminated","questions":["Am I confusing the compute resource (driver, executors, RAM) with the table (files + log in cloud storage)?","Was the data ever persisted with `saveAsTable` or `format(\"delta\").save`, or did it only live in a DataFrame in memory?","Did the write actually commit a table version?","Am I querying the exact `catalog.schema.table` name I wrote to?"]}]},{"id":"ch01-s11","title":"Control Plane vs Compute Plane (Preview)","hook":"One side decides what should run. The other side runs it.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Control plane","points":["Databricks-managed backend / platform services","Includes the web application","Platform coordination","Think: *\"What should run?\"*"]},{"title":"Compute plane","points":["Where your workload actually processes data","Serverless: Databricks-managed serverless compute plane","Classic: cloud infrastructure in the **customer's** cloud environment","Think: *\"Actually run it.\"*"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":"CONTROL PLANE  (Databricks-managed services, web app)\n      │  \"What should run?\"\n      ▼\nCOMPUTE PLANE  (where data is processed)\n   ├─ serverless → Databricks-managed serverless plane\n   └─ classic    → customer's cloud environment\n      \"Actually run it.\"","caption":"First preview. Later chapters make it precise"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Where does compute live?","text":"**Serverless** workloads run on compute that Databricks operates in a **Databricks-managed serverless compute plane**. **Classic** compute uses cloud infrastructure associated with the **customer's** cloud environment."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Restaurant analogy","text":"The control plane is the **front office**: it takes orders and coordinates. The compute plane is the **kitchen** where the cooking happens. With serverless, the restaurant also runs the kitchen. With classic compute, the kitchen sits in your building."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: where does the Databricks web UI belong?","text":"In the **control plane**, which holds the Databricks-managed backend services, including the web application and platform coordination."}]},{"id":"ch01-s12","title":"Debugging Mindset & Round-1 Review","hook":"\"Spark is slow\" isn't a diagnosis. Find the layer first.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.read.table(\"sales.orders\")\ndf.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\").show()","caption":"It's extremely slow. Which layer is responsible?"},{"t":"p","text":"A beginner answers \"Spark\". That's **insufficient**: many layers could be the cause."},{"t":"table","head":["Layer","Hypothesis to test"],"rows":[["storage","slow reads?"],["table layout","too many tiny files?"],["Delta","bad physical organization?"],["Spark","enormous shuffle?"],["data","severe skew?"],["compute","insufficient resources?"],["query","inefficient transformation?"],["statistics","optimizer made a bad choice?"],["network","huge exchange?"]]},{"t":"flow","items":["symptom","identify layer","collect evidence","form hypothesis","check Spark/Databricks metrics","fix","remeasure"],"caption":"Professional debugging loop"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"\"Query slow → increase cluster size\"","text":"Throwing more compute at every slow query is the anti-pattern. It skips identifying the layer and collecting evidence. This is the difference between someone who **uses** Databricks and someone who can **operate** it professionally."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a query is extremely slow","questions":["Which layer could this be: storage, table layout, Delta, Spark, data, compute, query, statistics or network?","What evidence do I have, or have I only guessed?","Am I reading too many tiny files, or a badly organized table?","Is there an enormous shuffle or a huge exchange over the network?","Is one key severely skewed?","Did the optimizer choose badly because of missing or stale statistics?","After my fix, did I remeasure?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**What you should be able to say after Round 1, without notes:**"},{"t":"table","head":["Not the same","Why"],"rows":[["Spark ≠ Databricks","Spark is the engine. Databricks is the whole platform"],["Spark ≠ Delta","computation vs transactional table layer"],["Delta ≠ Parquet","Delta = Parquet + transaction log"],["table ≠ file","a table is logical, files are physical"],["table ≠ storage directory","the snapshot decides membership"],["storage ≠ compute","independent lifetimes"],["file exists ≠ in current table","physical vs logical membership"],["metadata ≠ data","description vs rows"],["Unity Catalog ≠ Delta Lake","governance vs table format"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Self-check","text":"If any line above still feels fuzzy, go back to its section before moving on to Round 2."}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch01-d01","title":"Table \"disappeared\" after the cluster was terminated","section":"ch01-s10","symptom":"You (or a teammate) believe `prod.sales.orders` is gone because the cluster that wrote it was terminated, or a query from new compute can't find it.","askYourself":["Am I confusing the compute resource (driver, executors, RAM) with the table (Parquet files + log in cloud storage)?","Was the data ever persisted with saveAsTable / format(\"delta\").save, or did it only live in a DataFrame in memory?","Did the write actually commit a table version?","Am I querying the exact three-level name (catalog.schema.table) I wrote to?","Is my new compute pointing at the same catalog and storage?"],"steps":[{"do":"Restate the model: compute and persistent storage have independent lifetimes.","why":"Terminating compute removes RAM and temporary shuffle files, not the table."},{"do":"Query the full name from new compute.","why":"Rules out a wrong catalog or schema.","code":"SELECT * FROM prod.sales.orders LIMIT 10;"},{"do":"Check the original write code.","why":"A DataFrame that was never written lived only in executor memory.","code":"df.write.saveAsTable(\"prod.sales.orders\")"},{"do":"Confirm the write committed (a new version exists in the table's log).","why":"Physically written but uncommitted files are not a published table."}],"rootCauses":["Mental model error: equating cluster lifetime with table lifetime","Data was never persisted (only an in-memory DataFrame)","Write never committed","Wrong catalog/schema in the name"],"fix":"Query the table from any compute using its full three-level name. If it was never persisted or committed, write it with saveAsTable (or format(\"delta\").save) and confirm the commit.","mnemonic":"Cluster dies, table stays."},{"id":"ch01-d02","title":"Reader sees a partial / mixed state (e.g. A B E)","section":"ch01-s05","symptom":"A query that ran during an update returns a mix of old and new rows (or duplicates), a state the table was never in.","askYourself":["Is this a plain directory of Parquet files with no transaction log (no `_delta_log/`)?","Am I reading the files directly by path, as plain Parquet, instead of through the table's committed snapshot?","Was a writer replacing files while I was reading?","Which guarantee failed: atomicity (half-finished change visible) or isolation (invalid interleaving)?","Does my reader stay on one snapshot version (e.g. 87) while the writer builds 88?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the table folder for a `_delta_log/` directory.","why":"Without a transaction log, there are no atomic commits or snapshots."},{"do":"Check how the reader reads: table name / Delta format vs plain `spark.read.parquet(path)`.","why":"A plain Parquet read lists every physical file and ignores the log."},{"do":"Line up the timing of the read and the concurrent write.","why":"Confirms an overlap that let the reader see an in-between state."}],"rootCauses":["Plain Parquet directory with no transactional layer","Reader bypasses the Delta log (reads files by path as Parquet)","Writer's partial progress is visible because nothing makes the commit atomic"],"fix":"Store the data as a Delta table and read it through Delta (table name or format(\"delta\")), so every reader sees exactly one committed snapshot.","mnemonic":"ABE is a lie: only ABC or ADE."},{"id":"ch01-d03","title":"Files exist in storage, but their rows aren't in the table","section":"ch01-s06","symptom":"Listing the table folder shows `X.parquet`, but SELECT on the table never returns X's rows.","askYourself":["Does the current snapshot reference X, or did a later version REMOVE it?","Did the transaction that wrote X ever commit?","Was X dropped into the folder by a plain Parquet write that never touched the log?","Am I treating physical existence as logical membership?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm the symptom by querying the table, not the folder.","why":"The folder listing is physical. The table is logical."},{"do":"Replay the log versions (ADD/REMOVE) up to the current one.","why":"Membership is defined by the snapshot.","code":"v0: ADD A ADD B\nv1: REMOVE A ADD C\n…"},{"do":"If X was never ADDed: check whether its writer failed before committing, or wrote plain Parquet.","why":"Uncommitted or out-of-band files are not published versions."}],"rootCauses":["X was removed by a later commit (e.g. UPDATE/DELETE rewrite) but not yet physically cleaned up","X's transaction never committed","X was written by a path that bypassed the Delta log"],"fix":"Accept that removed files are expected leftovers. For missing data, rerun the write through Delta so it commits an ADD. Never point a plain Parquet reader at the folder or touch `_delta_log` by hand.","mnemonic":"Exists ≠ belongs."},{"id":"ch01-d04","title":"Query is extremely slow","section":"ch01-s12","symptom":"`spark.read.table(\"sales.orders\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\").show()` takes far too long.","askYourself":["Which layer could it be: storage, table layout, Delta, Spark, data, compute, query, statistics or network?","What evidence do I actually have, or have I only guessed?","Am I reading too many tiny files or a badly organized table?","Is there an enormous shuffle or a huge exchange over the network?","Is a key (e.g. one country) severely skewed?","Are the resources insufficient, or is the transformation itself inefficient?","Did the optimizer choose badly because statistics are missing or stale?","After the fix, did I remeasure?"],"steps":[{"do":"Name the symptom precisely (which stage, how slow compared with normal).","why":"A vague symptom leads to a vague fix."},{"do":"Pick the candidate layer(s).","why":"\"Spark\" alone is not a diagnosis."},{"do":"Collect evidence and form a hypothesis.","why":"e.g. a scan with a huge file count → tiny files; one long task → skew."},{"do":"Check Spark/Databricks metrics (Spark UI, plan).","why":"Confirms or refutes the hypothesis.","code":"df.explain(mode=\"extended\")"},{"do":"Apply a targeted fix and remeasure.","why":"Only measurement proves the fix worked."}],"rootCauses":["slow storage reads","too many tiny files","bad Delta physical organization","enormous shuffle","severe data skew","insufficient compute","inefficient transformation","bad optimizer choice from statistics","huge network exchange"],"fix":"Follow symptom → identify layer → collect evidence → form hypothesis → check metrics → fix → remeasure. Never default to \"increase cluster size\".","mnemonic":"Layers: \"Some Tables Deliver Slow Data; Check Query Stats Now\" (storage, table layout, Delta, Spark, data, compute, query, statistics, network)."}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Parquet files ≠ Delta table","text":"A folder of `part-*.parquet` files is not automatically a Delta table.","fix":"Delta = Parquet + `_delta_log`. No log means no Delta."},{"title":"UPDATE/DELETE as cell edits","text":"Picturing UPDATE/DELETE as editing a cell in Excel.","fix":"Columnar files get rewritten: write a replacement file, then commit REMOVE old + ADD new."},{"title":"Physical existence = membership","text":"Assuming a file in the table folder is part of the current table.","fix":"The current snapshot (from the log) decides membership. Removed or uncommitted files can still sit in storage."},{"title":"Seeing uncommitted files","text":"Expecting readers to see files that were written but not yet committed.","fix":"Only a committed version is published. Writing files is not committing."},{"title":"ACID consistency vs CAP consistency","text":"Treating the C in ACID as the C in CAP.","fix":"ACID C = valid state → valid state respecting invariants. CAP C is a distributed-systems property."},{"title":"Cluster terminated = table deleted","text":"Believing that terminating compute deletes `prod.sales.orders`.","fix":"Compute and persistent storage have independent lifetimes."},{"title":"Relying on compute-local data","text":"Expecting executor RAM or shuffle/temp files to survive the cluster.","fix":"They are disposable. Persist important data to cloud storage as a table."},{"title":"catalog.schema.object read as server.database.table","text":"Reading `ecommerce.silver.orders` as server/database/table.","fix":"UC: catalog `ecommerce`, schema `silver`, object `orders`."},{"title":"Unity Catalog = Delta Lake","text":"Mixing up the governance layer with the table format.","fix":"UC = metadata + governance. Delta = transactional table layer over files."},{"title":"\"Delta Lake is a database\"","text":"Calling Delta a database server.","fix":"Delta is a transactional storage/table layer over files, not a standalone database server."},{"title":"Databricks = Spark with notebooks","text":"Reducing Databricks to its compute engine.","fix":"It connects storage, Delta tables, Spark/Photon, governance, orchestration, SQL/BI, ML/AI and DevOps."},{"title":"ReadSchema is cosmetic","text":"Ignoring `ReadSchema: struct<age:int>` in a plan.","fix":"It shows the columns Spark actually requests, which is evidence of column pruning."},{"title":"Column pruning = file skipping","text":"Thinking column pruning also skips files or dates.","fix":"Column pruning cuts columns. Data skipping / partition / clustering pruning cuts files or regions."},{"title":"Query slow → bigger cluster","text":"Scaling compute without diagnosing.","fix":"Symptom → identify layer → evidence → hypothesis → metrics → fix → remeasure."},{"title":"Serverless vs classic location","text":"Assuming classic compute runs in the Databricks-managed plane.","fix":"Serverless runs in a Databricks-managed serverless compute plane. Classic runs in the customer's cloud environment."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"What's the chapter mantra?","a":"Spark computes. Storage persists. Delta gives stored files table semantics. Unity Catalog governs the objects.","section":"ch01-s01"},{"q":"Which question does storage answer?","a":"Where are the bytes after my program stops running?","section":"ch01-s02"},{"q":"Which question does compute answer?","a":"Which CPUs and RAM execute my code?","section":"ch01-s02"},{"q":"Name three cloud object stores and their URI schemes.","a":"Amazon S3 `s3://`, ADLS `abfss://`, GCS `gs://`.","section":"ch01-s02"},{"q":"Terminate the cluster: what happens to executor RAM, shuffle files and S3 files?","a":"RAM gone. Shuffle/temp files normally gone. S3 files remain.","section":"ch01-s02"},{"q":"Why must storage and compute have independent lifetimes?","a":"So data survives while compute scales up, down or to zero with the workload.","section":"ch01-s02"},{"q":"Do part-*.parquet files make a table?","a":"Not by themselves. You need metadata/transaction state to know which files make up the table.","section":"ch01-s03"},{"q":"What does UPDATE do physically on columnar files (foundation model)?","a":"Writes a new file version with the changed rows, then the metadata marks old out and new in.","section":"ch01-s03"},{"q":"What is column pruning?","a":"Reading only the columns a query needs, enabled by columnar formats.","section":"ch01-s04"},{"q":"What does `ReadSchema: struct<age:int>` tell you?","a":"Spark requested only the `age` column from storage.","section":"ch01-s04"},{"q":"Name Parquet's internal structures.","a":"Row groups, column chunks, pages, encodings, compression, metadata.","section":"ch01-s04"},{"q":"Column pruning vs data skipping?","a":"Pruning = fewer columns. Skipping/partition/clustering pruning = fewer files or regions.","section":"ch01-s04"},{"q":"Reader reads A B C while the writer changes the folder to A D E. Which results are valid?","a":"A B C or A D E. Never A B E.","section":"ch01-s05"},{"q":"Atomicity in one line?","a":"Completely or not at all: the commit is visible as one logical change.","section":"ch01-s05"},{"q":"ACID consistency vs CAP consistency?","a":"ACID: valid state → valid state respecting invariants. CAP: a different distributed-systems concept.","section":"ch01-s05"},{"q":"Isolation example?","a":"Reader sees coherent v87 while the writer creates v88, never half-and-half.","section":"ch01-s05"},{"q":"Durability in one line?","a":"Committed state survives later process or compute failure.","section":"ch01-s05"},{"q":"What is Delta Lake?","a":"Parquet data files + a file-based transaction log → ACID, scalable metadata, Spark API compatibility.","section":"ch01-s06"},{"q":"What does `_delta_log` record?","a":"Which files make up each valid table version (ADD/REMOVE per commit).","section":"ch01-s06"},{"q":"Physical file existence vs logical membership?","a":"Not the same. The current snapshot decides membership.","section":"ch01-s06"},{"q":"Log: v0 ADD A,B; v1 REMOVE A, ADD C; v2 ADD D; v3 REMOVE B. Snapshot?","a":"C + D.","section":"ch01-s06"},{"q":"Should uncommitted written files be visible?","a":"No. Writing files ≠ publishing a committed version.","section":"ch01-s06"},{"q":"DELETE of one row, file model?","a":"Write a replacement file without the row, then commit REMOVE old + ADD new.","section":"ch01-s07"},{"q":"What does a lakehouse combine?","a":"Data-lake characteristics + database/warehouse semantics.","section":"ch01-s08"},{"q":"Why is \"Delta Lake is a database\" imprecise?","a":"It's a transactional storage/table layer over files, not a standalone database server.","section":"ch01-s08"},{"q":"What does UC's namespace look like?","a":"catalog.schema.object (three levels).","section":"ch01-s09"},{"q":"`company.finance.transactions` = ?","a":"company = catalog, finance = schema, transactions = table/object.","section":"ch01-s09"},{"q":"Metadata vs data?","a":"Metadata describes the data (names, columns, format, location, owner, permissions). Data is the rows.","section":"ch01-s09"},{"q":"Which component executes `df.groupBy(...).sum(...)`?","a":"Spark.","section":"ch01-s10"},{"q":"Which component provides transactional state?","a":"The Delta transaction log.","section":"ch01-s10"},{"q":"Does terminating compute delete `prod.sales.orders`?","a":"No. Compute and persistent data have different lifetimes.","section":"ch01-s10"},{"q":"Control plane vs compute plane?","a":"Control plane: Databricks services and web app (\"what should run?\"). Compute plane: where data is processed (\"actually run it\").","section":"ch01-s11"},{"q":"Where does serverless vs classic compute run?","a":"Serverless: Databricks-managed serverless compute plane. Classic: the customer's cloud environment.","section":"ch01-s11"},{"q":"The professional debugging loop?","a":"Symptom → identify layer → collect evidence → hypothesis → check metrics → fix → remeasure.","section":"ch01-s12"},{"q":"Nine candidate layers for a slow query?","a":"Storage, table layout, Delta, Spark, data, compute, query, statistics, network.","section":"ch01-s12"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch01-e001","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which topic is **NOT** part of *Prerequisites — Round 1* in the mastery DAG?","options":["ACID transactions","columnar storage / Parquet","ETL vs ELT","transaction logs"],"answer":2,"why":["Round 1: needed to understand Delta.","Round 1: explains column pruning.","Correct: this is the first Round 2 topic.","Round 1: the core of Delta Lake."],"explain":"ETL vs ELT opens **Round 2**, which moves from the storage model to the pipeline model. Round 1 is about storage, tables and transactions: compute vs storage, object storage, file ≠ table, Parquet, metadata, ACID, transaction logs and the lakehouse motivation."},{"id":"ch01-e002","type":"cloze","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the chapter mantra.","text":"Spark [[computes]]. Storage [[persists]]. [[Delta|Delta Lake]] gives stored files table semantics. [[Unity Catalog|UC]] governs the objects. Databricks [[integrates]] these pieces into one platform.","bank":["Parquet","Photon","executes","stores","replaces"],"explain":"Each clause names one layer and its single job. Spark (compute) **computes**. Cloud storage **persists**. Delta turns stored Parquet files into **tables** with transactions. Unity Catalog **governs** (names, permissions). Databricks is the platform that **integrates** all of them."},{"id":"ch01-e003","type":"match","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each Spark abbreviation you already studied to its meaning.","pairs":[["BHJ","Broadcast Hash Join"],["SMJ","Sort-Merge Join"],["SHJ","Shuffled Hash Join"],["BNLJ","Broadcast Nested Loop Join"],["AQE","Adaptive Query Execution"]],"explain":"These are the four physical join strategies plus AQE from your Spark foundation. BHJ avoids shuffling the big side by broadcasting the small one. SMJ shuffles and sorts both sides. SHJ shuffles both sides and hashes per partition. BNLJ is the costly fallback for non-equi joins. AQE re-plans at runtime from real statistics."},{"id":"ch01-e004","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The current Databricks Data Engineer Associate exam is mostly about memorizing syntax. Operational topics such as OOM diagnosis and skew are not tested.","answer":false,"explain":"False. The current exam guide explicitly includes Spark UI troubleshooting, skew/shuffle/spill, **OOM diagnosis**, CI/CD/Automation Bundles, Lakeflow Jobs and governance. That is why this course trains reasoning and debugging, not just syntax."},{"id":"ch01-e005","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which of these are named in the source as topics of the current Associate exam? (Select all that apply.)","multi":true,"options":["Liquid Clustering","Predictive optimization","Spark UI troubleshooting","CI/CD / Automation Bundles","Writing custom JVM garbage collectors"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"why":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Not listed. JVM internals are not part of the exam."],"explain":"Liquid Clustering, predictive optimization, Spark UI troubleshooting and CI/CD with Automation Bundles all appear in the exam list. Writing custom JVM garbage collectors does not. The exam tests platform operation, not JVM internals."},{"id":"ch01-e006","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Sort each topic into its place in the mastery DAG.","buckets":["Existing Spark knowledge","Prerequisites Round 1","Prerequisites Round 2"],"items":[{"text":"Catalyst / physical plans","bucket":0},{"text":"AQE","bucket":0},{"text":"DataFrame API","bucket":0},{"text":"compute vs storage","bucket":1},{"text":"ACID transactions","bucket":1},{"text":"transaction logs","bucket":1},{"text":"data lake → lakehouse motivation","bucket":1},{"text":"ETL vs ELT","bucket":2}],"explain":"Your **existing Spark knowledge** covers the execution engine: Catalyst, AQE, the DataFrame API and joins. **Round 1** builds the storage and table model (compute vs storage, ACID, transaction logs, lakehouse motivation). **Round 2** starts the pipeline model with ETL vs ELT."},{"id":"ch01-e007","type":"order","section":"ch01-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Put the learning path in order.","items":["Existing Spark knowledge","Prerequisites Round 1: storage, tables, transactions","Prerequisites Round 2: pipeline model (ETL vs ELT …)","Databricks proper: production, debugging, certification"],"explain":"The path starts from the Spark engine you already know. Round 1 builds the storage model. Round 2 builds the pipeline model. Only then do you enter Databricks proper. Each stage depends on the vocabulary of the stage before it."},{"id":"ch01-e008","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Your Spark cluster (driver, worker1, worker2, worker3) reads `s3://shop/orders/`. You **terminate the cluster**. Sort each item.","buckets":["Disappears with the cluster","Survives"],"items":[{"text":"Executor RAM","bucket":0},{"text":"Spark shuffle files on local worker disks","bucket":0},{"text":"Driver memory","bucket":0},{"text":"Parquet files in `s3://shop/orders/`","bucket":1},{"text":"Files under `abfss://data@company.dfs.core.windows.net/orders/`","bucket":1},{"text":"Temporary local data written by Spark during a job","bucket":0}],"explain":"Everything that lives in the compute environment (executor RAM, driver memory, local shuffle/temp files) disappears with it. Files in persistent object storage, such as `s3://shop/orders/` or an ADLS path, remain. This difference returns later as compute vs Delta tables."},{"id":"ch01-e009","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Storage is the layer that answers *\"Which CPUs and RAM execute my code?\"*","answer":false,"explain":"False. That question belongs to **compute** (driver and executors). Storage answers *\"Where are the bytes after my program stops running?\"* Keeping the two questions apart is the first step of the chapter's mental model."},{"id":"ch01-e010","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After the cluster that ran a big `groupBy` is terminated, what normally happens to the **shuffle files** it wrote?","options":["They are kept in `s3://shop/orders/` next to the data","They normally disappear with the compute environment","They are converted to Parquet and committed to the table","They are moved to the control plane for reuse"],"answer":1,"why":["Shuffle files live on the workers' local disks, not in your object storage path.","Correct: they are temporary and owned by compute.","Nothing commits shuffle output to a table automatically.","The control plane runs platform services. It does not store your shuffle data."],"explain":"Shuffle files are temporary local data owned by the compute environment, so they normally disappear with it. They are never automatically published into the table's storage location. Only data that was explicitly written to persistent storage survives."},{"id":"ch01-e011","type":"match","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each address or component to what it is.","pairs":[["`s3://company-data/orders/`","Amazon S3"],["`abfss://data@company.dfs.core.windows.net/orders/`","Azure Data Lake Storage"],["`gs://company-data/orders/`","Google Cloud Storage"],["Executor","Compute (CPU + RAM)"]],"explain":"`s3://` is Amazon S3, `abfss://…dfs.core.windows.net` is Azure Data Lake Storage, and `gs://` is Google Cloud Storage. All three are **persistent object storage**. An executor is different: it is CPU and RAM, so it belongs to compute."},{"id":"ch01-e012","type":"calc","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A **100 TB** persistent dataset is processed by 50 workers at 08:00 and 200 workers at 12:00. At 18:00 the cluster scales to **0 workers**. How many TB of the dataset still exist in storage at 18:00?","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"TB","explain":"All **100 TB** remain, because storage and compute have independent lifetimes. Scaling compute to zero only stops processing and billing for workers. The trap is to think that 'no cluster' means 'no data'."},{"id":"ch01-e013","type":"free","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q12.** Why is this architecture useful?\n`100 TB persistent dataset` · `08:00 → 50 workers` · `12:00 → 200 workers` · `18:00 → 0 workers`","model":"Because storage and compute are decoupled: the 100 TB lives in persistent object storage that survives independently, while compute can be scaled up (200 workers at peak), down, or to zero according to the workload. You pay for compute only when you need it, and terminating workers never deletes the data.","rubric":["Storage persists independently of compute","Compute scales up/down (even to zero) with workload","Cost or elasticity benefit","Terminating compute does not delete data"],"explain":"Persistent storage survives on its own while compute capacity scales with the workload. You pay for 200 workers only at peak, for zero at night, and the data is never at risk because it does not live on the workers."},{"id":"ch01-e014","type":"odd","section":"ch01-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong?","options":["Amazon S3","Azure Data Lake Storage","Google Cloud Storage","Spark executor"],"answer":3,"explain":"Amazon S3, ADLS and GCS are all **persistent cloud object storage**. A Spark executor is **compute**: CPUs and RAM that disappear when the cluster is terminated."},{"id":"ch01-e015","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"`UPDATE orders SET status='cancelled' WHERE order_id=123` edits the single value for order 123 directly inside the existing Parquet file, like editing a cell in Excel.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Columnar files are not manipulated as individual mutable cells. The engine usually writes a **new file version** with the changed rows, and metadata records that the old file is out and the new file is in."},{"id":"ch01-e016","type":"cloze","section":"ch01-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Fill the gaps.","text":"`part-00000.parquet`, `part-00001.parquet` … are [[physical|physical files]] objects. \"The `orders` table\" is a [[logical]] interpretation of them. Knowing which file currently belongs to the table requires [[metadata|transaction state]].","bank":["executor","partition","temporary"],"explain":"The part files are **physical** objects. \"The orders table\" is a **logical** interpretation of them. The link between the two, saying which files currently belong to the table, is **metadata** (transaction state)."},{"id":"ch01-e017","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The directory `/orders/` contains three `part-0000N.parquet` files. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["They are a full database table with ACID guarantees","They are files you may interpret as a table, but files alone don't provide full table behavior","They are a Delta table, because Delta stores data as Parquet","They are a Unity Catalog object, because they live in cloud storage"],"answer":1,"why":["Plain files have no transaction state, so there is no ACID.","Correct.","Delta also needs a `_delta_log`. Parquet alone is not Delta.","Unity Catalog objects are registered metadata. Files in storage are not automatically UC objects."],"explain":"Files can be *interpreted* as a table, but they don't bring table behavior such as atomic updates, coherent snapshots or a record of which files belong. Those come from metadata and transaction state. Having Parquet files also does not make something a Delta table automatically."},{"id":"ch01-e018","type":"spotbug","section":"ch01-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A teammate wrote these notes. Click the **wrong** statements.","lines":["/orders/ contains part-00000 … part-00002.parquet","These files automatically behave like a full database table","order_id = 123 lives inside part-00001.parquet","UPDATE rewrites only that one cell inside part-00001.parquet, in place","Something must record that the old file is out and the new file is in"],"bugs":[1,3],"fix":"Line 1 → 'These files can be interpreted as a table, but files alone don't provide full table behavior.'\nLine 3 → 'UPDATE typically writes a new file with the changed rows; metadata/transaction state marks the old file as removed and the new file as added.'","explain":"\"These files automatically behave like a full database table\" is wrong: files alone don't give table semantics. \"UPDATE rewrites only that one cell … in place\" is wrong: the engine writes a replacement file instead of editing cells in a columnar file. The other lines describe the correct model."},{"id":"ch01-e019","type":"free","section":"ch01-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In 2–3 sentences, explain why a directory of Parquet files is **not** the same as a table.","model":"Files are physical bytes in storage; a table is a logical object. After an UPDATE/DELETE the engine writes replacement files, so something (metadata/transaction state) must record which files currently belong to the table and which are obsolete. A bare directory has no such record, so it can't provide full table behavior.","rubric":["Files = physical, table = logical","Changes create replacement files","Metadata/transaction state decides membership","Directory alone lacks that"],"explain":"A table is a logical object with defined membership, a schema and transactional behavior. A directory is just bytes. You need metadata or transaction state to know which files make up the table at each moment, especially after updates create replacement files."},{"id":"ch01-e020","type":"odd","section":"ch01-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong?","options":["part-00000.parquet","part-00001.parquet","part-00002.parquet","the `orders` table"],"answer":3,"explain":"The three `part-*.parquet` items are **physical files** in storage. \"The `orders` table\" is a **logical** concept, and you need metadata to know which files make it up."},{"id":"ch01-e021","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"**Exercise 2.** `sales(sale_id, customer_id, product_id, amount, country, timestamp, salesperson, comments)`. Which columns does this query fundamentally need from storage? (Select all.)","code":"SELECT country, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY country;","multi":true,"options":["sale_id","customer_id","amount","country","timestamp","comments"],"answer":[2,3],"why":["Not referenced.","Not referenced.","Needed: aggregated by SUM.","Needed: GROUP BY key.","Not referenced.","Not referenced."],"explain":"Only `country` (grouping key) and `amount` (aggregated value) are needed. Thanks to the columnar format, Spark can prune the other six columns and read less. `sale_id` is a tempting wrong pick, but nothing in the query references it."},{"id":"ch01-e022","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"**Challenge Q5.** Why can `SELECT AVG(age) FROM people;` read far less data from Parquet than from a row-oriented representation?","options":["Parquet files are always smaller than any other format","Parquet is columnar, so Spark can prune and read only the `age` column","Parquet keeps a transaction log that indexes ages","Parquet caches the average in its footer"],"answer":1,"why":["Size alone isn't the point. Being able to read only one column is.","Correct.","A transaction log is a Delta feature, not a Parquet one.","Parquet stores statistics, but this answer misstates the mechanism."],"explain":"Parquet is **columnar**, so Spark can do column pruning and fetch only the `age` column. In a row layout every row carries all its columns together, so you read the bytes of `name`, `email` and so on just to reach `age`. Compression helps too, but the main reason is column access."},{"id":"ch01-e023","type":"cloze","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Fill the gaps.","text":"Avoiding reading irrelevant columns is called [[column pruning]]. In the physical plan for `SELECT AVG(age) FROM people` you may see `ReadSchema: struct<[[age]]:int>` even though the table has 100 columns.","bank":["data skipping","name","shuffle","country"],"explain":"Skipping irrelevant columns is **column pruning**. The physical plan shows it as `ReadSchema: struct<age:int>`: only `age` was requested even if the table has 100 columns."},{"id":"ch01-e024","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"`ReadSchema: struct<age:int>` in a plan is cosmetic. Spark still reads all 100 columns from storage.","answer":false,"explain":"False. ReadSchema shows the columns Spark actually **requests** from the files. A single column there is evidence that column pruning reduced I/O."},{"id":"ch01-e025","type":"calc","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Idealized model: a Parquet table has **100 columns of equal size**. Approximately what **percentage** of the column data does `SELECT AVG(age)` need to read thanks to column pruning?","answer":1,"tolerance":0.1,"unit":"%","explain":"1 of 100 equal-size columns is **1%**. Real numbers differ because columns differ in size and compression, but the order of magnitude shows why column pruning matters for wide tables."},{"id":"ch01-e026","type":"calc","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A table occupies **5 TB**, stored as **50 columns of equal size**. Your query needs **2 columns**. With column pruning alone (no file skipping), roughly how many TB must be read?","answer":0.2,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"TB","explain":"2/50 × 5 TB = **0.2 TB**. Data skipping or partition/clustering pruning could cut this further by scanning only the files for the requested date. That second mechanism is separate from column pruning."},{"id":"ch01-e027","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Sort each situation by the mechanism that reduces the read.","buckets":["Column pruning (fewer columns)","Data skipping / partition / clustering pruning (fewer files/regions)"],"items":[{"text":"Query needs only 2 of 80 columns","bucket":0},{"text":"`ReadSchema: struct<country:string,amount:double>`","bucket":0},{"text":"Spark never fetches the `comments` column","bucket":0},{"text":"Only files for one date are scanned","bucket":1},{"text":"Files whose statistics exclude the filter value are skipped","bucket":1},{"text":"Only one partition directory is read","bucket":1}],"explain":"**Column pruning** reduces *which columns* are read and relies on the columnar format. **Data skipping / partition / clustering pruning** reduces *which files or regions* are scanned and relies on layout and statistics (such as min/max values per file). Q14 asks you to name one of each."},{"id":"ch01-e028","type":"free","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q14 (debugging).** A table occupies 5 TB in cloud storage, but your query needs two columns and rows from one date. Name **two fundamentally different mechanisms** that could reduce what Spark has to read.","model":"1) Column pruning: because the format is columnar, Spark reads only the two needed columns. 2) Data skipping / partition pruning / clustering-based pruning: using table layout and statistics, Spark skips files or regions that cannot contain the requested date.","rubric":["Column pruning (fewer columns)","Data skipping / partition / clustering pruning (fewer files/regions)","States that the two are different axes"],"explain":"The key word is *different*. One mechanism trims the read vertically (columns) and the other trims it horizontally (files or regions). Naming two kinds of pruning that both cut columns would miss the point."},{"id":"ch01-e029","type":"write","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the Exercise 2 query: total `amount` per `country` from `sales`.","solution":"SELECT country, SUM(amount)\nFROM sales\nGROUP BY country;","keywords":["select country","sum(amount)","from sales","group by country"],"lang":"sql","explain":"`GROUP BY country` with `SUM(amount)` needs only those two columns. That makes it a textbook case for column pruning on a columnar format."},{"id":"ch01-e030","type":"spotbug","section":"ch01-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Click the wrong line(s) in this query analysis.","lines":["Query: SELECT country, SUM(amount) FROM sales GROUP BY country","Needed columns: country, amount","Also needed: sale_id (to count rows)","Columnar format lets Spark prune the other columns","Column pruning also lets Spark skip files from other dates"],"bugs":[2,4],"fix":"Remove line 2: sale_id is not needed. Line 4 → 'Skipping files from other dates is data skipping / partition / clustering pruning, a different mechanism from column pruning.'","explain":"\"Also needed: sale_id\" is wrong: `sale_id` is not referenced, so it can be pruned. \"Column pruning also lets Spark skip files\" is wrong: column pruning reduces columns, not files. Skipping files for a date is data skipping or partition/clustering pruning."},{"id":"ch01-e031","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Reader X starts reading A, B, C while writer Y changes the directory to A, D, E. Which results would be **valid** for X to see? (Select all.)","multi":true,"options":["A B C","A D E","A B E","A B C D E"],"answer":[0,1],"why":["Complete old state.","Complete new state.","Half old, half new: invalid.","A union of two states is not a state the table was ever in."],"explain":"Valid results are complete states: the old one (A B C) or the new one (A D E). Mixtures such as A B E, or a union of both like A B C D E, represent **no valid table state**. Preventing them is the job of transactional semantics."},{"id":"ch01-e032","type":"match","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each ACID property to its meaning.","pairs":[["Atomicity","Completely or not at all: one logical change"],["Consistency","Valid state → valid state, respecting invariants"],["Isolation","Concurrent ops don't interleave invalidly; coherent snapshot"],["Durability","Committed state survives later compute failure"]],"explain":"Atomicity is all-or-nothing visibility. Consistency is valid → valid while respecting invariants. Isolation means no invalid interleavings, so each reader gets a coherent snapshot. Durability means committed state survives failures."},{"id":"ch01-e033","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"The C in ACID means the same thing as the C (Consistency) in the CAP theorem.","answer":false,"explain":"False. ACID consistency means a transaction moves the database from one valid state to another while respecting invariants. CAP consistency is a distributed-systems property about replicas agreeing. Same word, different concept, and a classic trap."},{"id":"ch01-e034","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q6.** A reader sees some files from **before** a transaction and some files created **during** it. What's the problem, and which ACID idea is most directly involved?","options":["Data loss; durability","An invalid partial state; atomic visibility / isolation","Slow reads; column pruning","Schema mismatch; consistency in the CAP sense"],"answer":1,"why":["No committed data is lost here.","Correct.","This is about correctness, not speed.","CAP consistency is a different concept."],"explain":"The reader observes an **invalid partial state** that never existed as a committed table. Atomic visibility of commits and isolation between reader and writer are the guarantees that prevent this. Durability is about surviving crashes after the commit, so it is not the answer here."},{"id":"ch01-e035","type":"cloze","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill the gaps about isolation.","text":"Reader R should see a coherent snapshot such as version [[87]] while writer W creates version [[88]], and never a half-87-half-88 dataset. This ACID property is [[isolation]].","bank":["atomicity","durability","86","89"],"explain":"Isolation means reader R keeps a coherent snapshot (version 87) while writer W builds version 88. A dataset that is half 87 and half 88 is an invalid interleaving."},{"id":"ch01-e036","type":"calc","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A pipeline replaces **80 files** atomically. Halfway through the write, 43 new files physically exist. How many of the **new** files may a concurrent reader of the current table see **before the commit**?","answer":0,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"**0.** With atomicity the change becomes visible as one logical commit, so before it a reader sees only the old state and after it only the new one. Seeing 43 new files plus 37 old ones is exactly the partial state atomicity forbids."},{"id":"ch01-e037","type":"free","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":3,"tags":["interview","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q15 (critical thinking).** Why isn't adding ACID semantics to an object store simply a matter of \"store everything as Parquet\"?","model":"Parquet describes how data is stored inside files; by itself it provides no transaction history, no atomic commits, no coherent snapshots for readers, and no table-state management (which files belong to which version). Those are required for database-like concurrent modifications — which is why Delta adds a transaction log on top of Parquet.","rubric":["Parquet = file storage format only","No transaction history / atomic commits","No coherent snapshots / isolation","Needs table-state management (e.g. Delta log)"],"explain":"The trap is to treat a file format as a transaction system. Parquet is about layout inside a file. ACID is about state across files and over time, so it needs a log or protocol on top."},{"id":"ch01-e038","type":"odd","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong?","options":["Atomicity","Isolation","Durability","Column pruning"],"answer":3,"explain":"Atomicity, Isolation and Durability are ACID transaction guarantees. Column pruning is a read optimization made possible by columnar formats and has nothing to do with transactional correctness."},{"id":"ch01-e039","type":"scenario","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A dashboard query on `/orders/` ran while the nightly job was replacing files. It returned rows from `A`, `B` and `E`, a mix that matches neither the old nor the new state.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Whether `/orders/` is a plain Parquet directory (no `_delta_log/`) or a Delta table","ok":true,"fb":"Right. Without a transaction log, nothing makes a commit atomic."},{"text":"Whether the cluster had enough memory","ok":false,"fb":"Memory doesn't cause a mixed table state."},{"text":"Whether the query used column pruning","ok":false,"fb":"Column pruning changes which columns are read, not which version is seen."}]},{"prompt":"It *is* a Delta table. What next?","options":[{"text":"Check whether the dashboard reads the folder as plain Parquet (`spark.read.parquet(path)`) instead of through Delta","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A plain Parquet read lists every physical file and ignores the log's snapshot."},{"text":"Delete the `_delta_log` folder and retry","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys the table state. Never do this."},{"text":"Schedule the dashboard so it never overlaps the job","ok":false,"fb":"That hides the symptom. Proper isolation makes overlap safe."}]},{"prompt":"Which ACID ideas does the fix restore?","options":[{"text":"Atomic visibility of commits + isolation (one coherent snapshot per reader)","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. The reader sees v87 or v88, never half of each."},{"text":"Durability only","ok":false,"fb":"Durability is about surviving failures after a commit."},{"text":"CAP consistency","ok":false,"fb":"A different concept from ACID."}]}],"explain":"Mixed states come from the lack of a transactional layer: no log, or a reader that bypasses it. The fix is to make commits atomic and reads snapshot-based (Delta, read through the table), not to time jobs carefully or add compute."},{"id":"ch01-e040","type":"order","section":"ch01-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the checks for *\"a reader saw a mixed/partial state\"*.","items":["Check for a `_delta_log/` (is there a transaction log?)","Check that the reader goes through the table/Delta, not plain Parquet by path","Confirm a writer was replacing files during the read","Name the violated guarantee: atomicity / isolation","Fix: Delta table + snapshot reads"],"explain":"First establish whether a transactional layer exists at all, then whether the reader actually uses it. Then confirm the overlap with a writer, and finally name the violated guarantee so the fix targets it."},{"id":"ch01-e041","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"**Exercise 3.** Which files make up the current snapshot after v3?","code":"v0: ADD A   ADD B\nv1: REMOVE A   ADD C\nv2: ADD D\nv3: REMOVE B","options":["A + B + C + D","C + D","B + C + D","D only"],"answer":1,"why":["Ignores the REMOVE actions. That is the physical listing, not the snapshot.","Correct.","That is the state after v2. v3 removed B.","C was never removed."],"explain":"Replay the log: v0 → {A, B}. v1 removes A and adds C → {B, C}. v2 adds D → {B, C, D}. v3 removes B → **{C, D}**. A and B may still physically exist, but they are not logical members."},{"id":"ch01-e042","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"**Challenge Q1.** A folder containing `part-0000.parquet`, `part-0001.parquet` and `part-0002.parquet` automatically means you have a Delta table.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Plain Parquet files do not imply a Delta transaction log. A Delta table is Parquet data files **plus** `_delta_log`, which records which files form each version."},{"id":"ch01-e043","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q7.** What is the current snapshot?","code":"v0 ADD A\nv0 ADD B\nv1 REMOVE A\nv1 ADD C\nv2 ADD D\nv3 REMOVE C\nv3 ADD E","options":["A, B, C, D, E","B, C, D, E","B, D, E","D, E"],"answer":2,"why":["The physical files, ignoring every REMOVE.","Forgot v3's REMOVE C.","Correct.","B was never removed."],"explain":"v0: {A, B}. v1: remove A, add C → {B, C}. v2: add D → {B, C, D}. v3: remove C, add E → **{B, D, E}**. If you forget a REMOVE, you get a superset that no version ever had."},{"id":"ch01-e044","type":"calc","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Using the Q7 log (`v0 ADD A, ADD B · v1 REMOVE A, ADD C · v2 ADD D · v3 REMOVE C, ADD E`): **how many files** are in the current snapshot, and how many files were ever written in total? Enter only the **snapshot count**.","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"5 files were written (A–E), but the snapshot holds **3** (B, D, E). The difference (A and C) may still sit physically in storage. That is the gap between physical existence and logical membership."},{"id":"ch01-e045","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q4.** A Parquet file physically exists in storage, but the current Delta snapshot no longer references it. It is still logically part of the current table.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Logical membership is decided by the relevant table snapshot, not by whether a file physically exists. Removed files can linger until they are cleaned up, and they are still not part of the current table."},{"id":"ch01-e046","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q10.** Which component primarily provides **transactional state**?","options":["Spark executor","Parquet alone","Delta transaction log","Unity Catalog permission grants"],"answer":2,"why":["Executors are temporary compute.","Parquet has no transaction history.","Correct.","Grants are governance, not transaction state."],"explain":"The **Delta transaction log** records the ADD/REMOVE commits that define each version. Executors compute and Parquet stores rows. Unity Catalog grants control who may access data, not which files form a version."},{"id":"ch01-e047","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q11.** Spark has physically written three replacement files, but the Delta transaction has not committed. A reader of the current table should already see those three files, because they physically exist.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Physically creating files is not the same as publishing a committed table version. Until the commit lands in the log, readers keep seeing the previous snapshot. That is atomicity and isolation at work."},{"id":"ch01-e048","type":"order","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the snapshots produced by replaying the Exercise 3 log (`v0: ADD A, ADD B · v1: REMOVE A, ADD C · v2: ADD D · v3: REMOVE B`).","items":["v0 → {A, B}","v1 → {B, C}","v2 → {B, C, D}","v3 → {C, D}"],"explain":"Replay strictly in version order and apply each REMOVE and ADD. Every snapshot is a complete, valid state. Applying versions out of order produces a state the table never had."},{"id":"ch01-e049","type":"spotbug","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague replayed this log by hand. Click the line where the replay goes wrong.","lines":["v0 → {A, B}","v1 → {B, C}","v2 → {A, B, C, D}","v3 → {A, B, D, E}"],"bugs":[2,3],"fix":"v2 → {B, C, D}\nv3 → {B, D, E}","explain":"Log: `v0 ADD A, ADD B · v1 REMOVE A, ADD C · v2 ADD D · v3 REMOVE C, ADD E`. The error starts at `v2 → {A, B, C, D}`: v2 only adds D, so the state is {B, C, D}, because A was removed in v1. `v3 → {A, B, D, E}` carries the same mistake forward. The correct final snapshot is {B, D, E}."},{"id":"ch01-e050","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Parquet data files or `_delta_log`? Sort each responsibility.","buckets":["Parquet data files","_delta_log"],"items":[{"text":"The actual rows (e.g. `123, 4921, 79.95`)","bucket":0},{"text":"Columnar encoding and compression","bucket":0},{"text":"Which files make up version 2","bucket":1},{"text":"ADD / REMOVE actions per commit","bucket":1},{"text":"Knowing that B.parquet is no longer in the table","bucket":1},{"text":"Values of the `amount` column","bucket":0}],"explain":"Parquet files hold the **rows**, column-encoded and compressed. The `_delta_log` holds the **table state**: ADD/REMOVE actions and which files make up each version. That state is what makes snapshots and time travel possible."},{"id":"ch01-e051","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which later topics depend on understanding *physical existence ≠ logical membership*? (Select all.)","multi":true,"options":["DELETE / UPDATE / MERGE","Time Travel","VACUUM","Concurrent writes","Column pruning"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"why":["They rewrite and REMOVE files.","It reads old snapshots whose files may still exist.","It deletes files that are no longer referenced.","Writers commit competing versions.","It is about columns, not file membership."],"explain":"DELETE, UPDATE and MERGE create replacement files and REMOVE old ones. Time Travel reads older snapshots. VACUUM physically cleans up files that are no longer referenced. Column pruning is a read optimization that does not depend on this idea."},{"id":"ch01-e052","type":"scenario","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You list `s3://shop/orders/` and see `X.parquet`, but `SELECT` on the Delta table never returns any of X's rows.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Whether the current snapshot (replayed from `_delta_log`) references X.parquet","ok":true,"fb":"Right. Membership comes from the snapshot, not from the folder listing."},{"text":"Increase the cluster size and rerun the query","ok":false,"fb":"This isn't a capacity problem. More compute won't make an unreferenced file appear."},{"text":"Re-read the folder with `spark.read.parquet(...)`","ok":false,"fb":"That bypasses the log and would also return removed or uncommitted data. It hides the problem."}]},{"prompt":"The current snapshot does NOT reference X. Which explanation fits?","options":[{"text":"A later version REMOVEd X (e.g. after an UPDATE/DELETE), or X's transaction never committed","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Both leave a physical file that is not a logical member."},{"text":"S3 is corrupting the file","ok":false,"fb":"Storage is not the suspect. The file reads fine, it just isn't in the table."},{"text":"Column pruning skipped the file","ok":false,"fb":"Column pruning drops columns, not whole files."}]},{"prompt":"What's the correct conclusion?","options":[{"text":"X physically exists but is not logically part of the current table. That is expected behavior","ok":true,"fb":"Yes: physical existence ≠ logical membership."},{"text":"Manually add X to the folder again","ok":false,"fb":"It's already in the folder. Only a commit makes a file part of the table."},{"text":"Delete `_delta_log` so all files are read","ok":false,"fb":"Never do this. You would destroy the table's state and expose stale files."}]}],"explain":"Start from the log, not the folder listing. Check whether X is referenced by the current snapshot. If it isn't, find out why: a later REMOVE, a write that never committed, or a write that bypassed the log. Never point a plain Parquet reader at the folder to \"fix\" it."},{"id":"ch01-e053","type":"order","section":"ch01-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Put the investigation for *\"file exists in storage, rows not in the table\"* in order.","items":["Confirm the rows are missing when querying the table (not the folder)","Replay the log: is the file referenced by the current snapshot?","If not: was it REMOVEd by a later version, or never committed?","Check whether it was written by a plain Parquet write that bypassed the log","Conclude: physical existence ≠ logical membership, so fix the write path if needed"],"explain":"Start by confirming the symptom from the table's point of view. Then consult the log for membership and explain the absence (REMOVE, missing commit, or a write that bypassed the log). Only then decide on a fix."},{"id":"ch01-e054","type":"order","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order what happens (foundation model) when you run `DELETE FROM orders WHERE order_id = 123;`","items":["Locate the file containing order 123 (A.parquet)","Write replacement B.parquet without row 123","Commit: REMOVE A.parquet + ADD B.parquet","New snapshot: table → B"],"explain":"The engine finds the file holding the row, writes a replacement without it, and commits REMOVE old + ADD new as one transaction. Only after the commit does the snapshot point to the new file. Readers before the commit still see the old file."},{"id":"ch01-e055","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"**Challenge Q3.** Why can a Delta `DELETE` operation require creating new Parquet files?","options":["Because Delta keeps every deleted row in a new 'trash' file","Because columnar files aren't edited cell by cell, so changes produce replacement files plus log metadata","Because Spark must convert Parquet to CSV before deleting","Because Unity Catalog requires a new file for every permission change"],"answer":1,"why":["There's no 'trash file' in this model.","Correct.","No format conversion happens.","Permissions don't create data files."],"explain":"Columnar data files are generally not edited as individual mutable cells. A change can mean writing replacement files, plus transaction metadata that marks which files now belong to the table. The other options describe things that aren't true."},{"id":"ch01-e056","type":"write","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the SQL to delete the e-shop order with id 123 from `orders`.","solution":"DELETE FROM orders\nWHERE order_id = 123;","keywords":["delete from orders","where order_id = 123"],"lang":"sql","explain":"This is ordinary DML syntax. On a Delta table it may turn into a file rewrite plus a REMOVE/ADD commit."},{"id":"ch01-e057","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After `DELETE FROM orders WHERE order_id = 123` commits, `A.parquet` (the old file) has necessarily already been physically erased from storage.","answer":false,"explain":"False. The commit removes A from the **current snapshot**, but the file can still physically exist for some time. Logical membership changed, and physical cleanup is a separate matter."},{"id":"ch01-e058","type":"spotbug","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Click the wrong line(s) in this description of a DELETE.","lines":["-- before: table → A.parquet (120,121,123,124,125)","DELETE FROM orders WHERE order_id = 123;","write B.parquet containing 120,121,123,124,125","commit: REMOVE A.parquet","commit: ADD A.parquet","-- after: table → B.parquet"],"bugs":[2,4],"fix":"write B.parquet containing 120,121,124,125\ncommit: ADD B.parquet","explain":"\"write B.parquet containing 120,121,123,…\" is wrong: the replacement must not contain row 123, otherwise nothing is deleted. \"commit: ADD A.parquet\" is wrong: the commit must ADD the new file B. Re-adding A would leave row 123 in the table."},{"id":"ch01-e059","type":"calc","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Foundation model: `A.parquet` holds **1,000,000** rows. `DELETE ... WHERE order_id = 123` matches exactly one row in A. How many rows must be written into the replacement file?","answer":999999,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"The replacement holds every surviving row: 1,000,000 − 1 = **999,999**. Deleting one row can mean rewriting nearly a whole file, which is why later chapters cover optimizations for this."},{"id":"ch01-e060","type":"free","section":"ch01-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain the difference between the *beginner model* and the *file-oriented model* of `DELETE` in 2–3 sentences.","model":"The beginner imagines finding row 123 and erasing it in place. On file-oriented analytical storage, the engine instead writes a replacement file without the row and commits a transaction that REMOVEs the old file and ADDs the new one; the new snapshot points at the replacement.","rubric":["Beginner: find & erase in place","Real: write replacement file","Commit REMOVE old + ADD new","Snapshot switches to new file"],"explain":"The key contrast is between **in-place row surgery** and **file replacement plus commit**. The table changes because the log now points to a different set of files."},{"id":"ch01-e061","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Sort each trait by the **traditional** architecture it describes.","buckets":["Data lake","Data warehouse"],"items":[{"text":"Cheap object storage","bucket":0},{"text":"Flexible schemas / formats","bucket":0},{"text":"ML / data-science friendly raw data","bucket":0},{"text":"Historically weaker transaction guarantees","bucket":0},{"text":"Structured tables with transactions","bucket":1},{"text":"Optimized BI / analytical SQL","bucket":1},{"text":"Historically rigid, separate from ML data","bucket":1}],"explain":"Lakes were about **cheap, flexible, raw** storage that suits ML but had weak table guarantees. Warehouses were about **structured, transactional, governed** SQL/BI but were rigid and separate from raw/ML data. The lakehouse combines both."},{"id":"ch01-e062","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which best describes the **lakehouse** idea?","options":["A warehouse that stores CSV exports","Data-lake characteristics + database/warehouse semantics","A lake with no schemas at all","Spark running inside a traditional database server"],"answer":1,"why":["That's still just a warehouse.","Correct.","That's a raw lake without table semantics.","The lakehouse runs on object storage, not inside a DB server."],"explain":"A lakehouse combines **data-lake characteristics** (cheap object storage, files, flexible formats) with **database/warehouse semantics** (tables, transactions, governance). On Databricks, Delta Lake supplies much of the table side."},{"id":"ch01-e063","type":"free","section":"ch01-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"**Challenge Q13 (interview).** In one sentence, explain why *\"Delta Lake is a database\"* is an imprecise statement.","model":"Delta Lake is a transactional storage/table layer over files (Parquet + a transaction log in object storage), not a standalone traditional database server.","rubric":["Transactional table/storage layer","Over files in storage","Not a standalone database server/engine"],"explain":"A strong answer says what Delta **is** (a table and transaction layer over files in storage) and what it **is not** (a standalone database server with its own engine). Compute comes from Spark/Photon, storage from cloud object storage and governance from Unity Catalog."},{"id":"ch01-e064","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Databricks is essentially \"Spark with notebooks\".","answer":false,"explain":"False. Databricks connects storage, transactional tables (Delta), Spark/Photon compute, governance (Unity Catalog), orchestration, SQL/BI, ML/AI and DevOps. Spark is only the compute part."},{"id":"ch01-e065","type":"odd","section":"ch01-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is **not** a traditional data-lake characteristic?","options":["Cheap object storage","Flexible schemas / formats","Huge raw datasets","Strong transactions with governed BI"],"answer":3,"explain":"Cheap object storage, flexible formats and huge raw datasets describe the lake. Strong transactions with governed BI were the **warehouse's** strengths, and the lakehouse adds them to the lake."},{"id":"ch01-e066","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement about Delta Lake is the most precise?","options":["A standalone database server that replaces Spark","The optimized storage/table layer underpinning lakehouse tables","A compute engine like Photon","The governance layer that grants permissions"],"answer":1,"why":["It's not a server and doesn't replace Spark.","Correct.","Photon/Spark are compute.","That's Unity Catalog."],"explain":"Delta is the **optimized storage/table layer** that underpins lakehouse tables: Parquet plus a transaction log. It is not a server, not a compute engine and not the governance layer."},{"id":"ch01-e067","type":"match","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"**Challenge Q8.** What does each part of `company.finance.transactions` represent in the Unity Catalog naming model?","pairs":[["company","catalog"],["finance","schema"],["transactions","object / table"]],"explain":"Unity Catalog names follow `catalog.schema.object`, so `company` is the catalog, `finance` is the schema and `transactions` is the object (table). The first part is **not** a server."},{"id":"ch01-e068","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Metadata or data? Sort each item for the table `prod.sales.orders`.","buckets":["Metadata","Data"],"items":[{"text":"`amount DECIMAL(10,2)`","bucket":0},{"text":"format: DELTA","bucket":0},{"text":"storage location `s3://.../orders/`","bucket":0},{"text":"owner: data_engineers","bucket":0},{"text":"`123, 4921, 79.95`","bucket":1},{"text":"`124, 8177, 20.30`","bucket":1}],"explain":"Metadata *describes* the table: names, columns and types, format, location, owner, permissions. Data is the rows themselves. Confusing the two is the root of the classic \"Unity Catalog ≠ Delta\" trap."},{"id":"ch01-e069","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague reads `ecommerce.silver.orders` as *server `ecommerce`, database `silver`, table `orders`*. What's wrong?","options":["Nothing, that's exactly the UC model","`ecommerce` is a catalog, not a server: the model is catalog.schema.object","`silver` is the table and `orders` a column","UC uses only two levels: schema.table"],"answer":1,"why":["UC is not server-based naming.","Correct.","`orders` is the table.","UC uses three levels."],"explain":"Unity Catalog uses `catalog.schema.object`, so `ecommerce` is a **catalog**. Mapping it to `server.database.table` from traditional databases is a common mental trap."},{"id":"ch01-e070","type":"cloze","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill the gaps.","text":"Unity Catalog uses a [[three-level|3-level]] namespace: [[catalog]].[[schema]].[[object|table]]. In `ecommerce.silver.orders`, `silver` is the [[schema]].","bank":["server","database","two-level","partition"],"explain":"UC uses a **three-level** namespace: `catalog.schema.object`. Example: `ecommerce.silver.orders` → catalog `ecommerce`, schema `silver`, table `orders`."},{"id":"ch01-e071","type":"write","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a query that reads every column of the `orders` table in schema `silver` of catalog `ecommerce`.","solution":"SELECT *\nFROM ecommerce.silver.orders;","keywords":["select *","from ecommerce.silver.orders"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Use the full three-level name, `catalog.schema.table`, so there is no doubt about which object you mean."},{"id":"ch01-e072","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Unity Catalog is where the actual rows of a table (e.g. `123, 4921, 79.95`) are stored.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Unity Catalog is the **metadata and governance** layer: names, ownership and permissions. The rows live in Parquet data files (managed by Delta) in cloud storage."},{"id":"ch01-e073","type":"spotbug","section":"ch01-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Click the wrong line(s) in this metadata description of `prod.sales.orders`.","lines":["server: prod","database server: sales","table name: orders","format: DELTA","storage location: s3://.../orders/"],"bugs":[0,1],"fix":"catalog: prod\nschema: sales","explain":"\"server: prod\" is wrong: `prod` is the **catalog**. \"database server: sales\" is wrong: `sales` is the **schema**. The UC model is catalog.schema.object, not server.database.table."},{"id":"ch01-e074","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"**Challenge Q9.** Which component normally *executes* `df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")`?","options":["Delta Lake","Spark","Unity Catalog","Parquet"],"answer":1,"why":["Delta gives table semantics, not computation.","Correct.","UC governs names and permissions.","Parquet is just a file format."],"explain":"**Spark** is the compute engine that runs transformations and aggregations. Delta is the table format, Unity Catalog governs objects and Parquet is a file format. None of those execute anything."},{"id":"ch01-e075","type":"free","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"**Challenge Q2.** What is the principal conceptual difference between **Spark** and **Delta Lake**?","model":"Spark is primarily a distributed computation engine (it executes reads, transformations, joins, aggregations), while Delta Lake supplies transactional table/storage semantics (ACID, versions/snapshots via a transaction log) over data files in storage.","rubric":["Spark = distributed computation engine","Delta = transactional table/storage layer","Delta works over data files (Parquet + log)"],"explain":"Separate the *engine* from the *table layer*. Spark does the work in memory on executors. Delta defines what the table is: which Parquet files, which version, with ACID guarantees."},{"id":"ch01-e076","type":"order","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the layers of *the one diagram* from top (you) to bottom.","items":["You (SQL / Python)","Compute engine (Spark / Photon)","Delta table (Parquet files + transaction log)","Cloud storage (S3 / ADLS / GCS)"],"explain":"You send SQL/Python to the **compute engine**, which reads and writes a **Delta table** (Parquet files + log), which physically lives in **cloud storage**. With `saveAsTable`, a Unity Catalog object sits on top of the Delta table as the governed name."},{"id":"ch01-e077","type":"write","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write PySpark that saves DataFrame `df` as a **Delta** table at path `path` (path-based, not a catalog name).","solution":"df.write.format(\"delta\").save(path)","keywords":["df.write","format(\"delta\")",".save("],"lang":"python","explain":"`format(\"delta\")` makes Spark write Parquet data files **and** update the Delta transaction log. `df.write.parquet(path)` would write plain Parquet with no log."},{"id":"ch01-e078","type":"write","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write PySpark that saves `df` as the governed table `orders` in schema `sales` of catalog `prod`.","solution":"df.write.saveAsTable(\"prod.sales.orders\")","keywords":["saveAsTable","prod.sales.orders"],"lang":"python","explain":"`saveAsTable` with a three-level name creates or updates a **Unity Catalog object** backed by a Delta table in cloud storage. That adds a governed layer on top of path-based writes."},{"id":"ch01-e079","type":"match","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each snippet to what it conceptually produces or does.","pairs":[["`spark.read.parquet(...)`","Reads plain Parquet files"],["`df.write.parquet(...)`","Writes Parquet files only, no transaction log"],["`df.write.format(\"delta\").save(...)`","Writes Parquet files + updates Delta log at a path"],["`df.write.saveAsTable(\"catalog.schema.orders\")`","Creates/updates a governed UC table backed by Delta"]],"explain":"`read.parquet` / `write.parquet` handle plain Parquet with no log. `format(\"delta\").save` writes Parquet **plus** the Delta log at a path. `saveAsTable(\"catalog.schema.orders\")` adds a governed UC object over the Delta table. Later chapters cover when these are equivalent and when they are not."},{"id":"ch01-e080","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After `df.write.saveAsTable(\"prod.sales.orders\")`, terminating the compute resource means `prod.sales.orders` necessarily disappears.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Compute and persistent data have different lifetimes. The table's files and log live in cloud storage and its name lives in the catalog, so new compute can query it later."},{"id":"ch01-e081","type":"scenario","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Yesterday you ran `df.write.saveAsTable(\"prod.sales.orders\")`, then terminated the cluster. Today a teammate says: *\"The cluster is gone, so the table is gone, right?\"*","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your first response?","options":[{"text":"No. Compute and persistent data have independent lifetimes, so the table should still exist","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Terminating compute removes RAM and temporary files, not the table in cloud storage."},{"text":"Yes, we must rerun the whole pipeline","ok":false,"fb":"That confuses compute with storage. The data was persisted."},{"text":"It depends on whether the cluster had autoscaling","ok":false,"fb":"Autoscaling changes compute size. It has nothing to do with table persistence."}]},{"prompt":"You start new compute, and the query still can't find the table. What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"That I'm using the exact full name `prod.sales.orders` (catalog.schema.table)","ok":true,"fb":"Good. A wrong catalog or schema is a cheap, common cause."},{"text":"Make the cluster bigger","ok":false,"fb":"Size doesn't affect whether a name resolves."},{"text":"Look for the old executors' shuffle files","ok":false,"fb":"Those were temporary and are gone. They never held the table anyway."}]},{"prompt":"The name is correct, and still nothing. What next?","options":[{"text":"Check whether the original write actually ran and committed (vs. only building a DataFrame in memory)","ok":true,"fb":"Right. If nothing was persisted or committed, there's nothing to find."},{"text":"Conclude that Delta tables only live as long as the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"False premise: Delta tables persist in cloud storage."},{"text":"Read the driver's RAM dump","ok":false,"fb":"The driver's memory is gone and never held the persisted table."}]}],"explain":"Separate the lifetimes: compute is gone, but persistent data and catalog metadata are not. If the table really can't be found, look at the write (did it persist and commit?) and the name (full three-level name). Don't blame the terminated cluster."},{"id":"ch01-e082","type":"order","section":"ch01-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the checks for *\"my table seems gone after the cluster was terminated\"*.","items":["Remind myself: terminating compute doesn't delete persisted tables","Query the exact three-level name from new compute","Verify the write used saveAsTable / format(\"delta\").save, not just an in-memory DataFrame","Verify the write committed a table version","Only then investigate storage / catalog configuration"],"explain":"First fix the mental model (compute ≠ storage). Then check the cheap things: the exact name. Then the write itself: was it persisted and committed? Each step rules out a whole layer."},{"id":"ch01-e083","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Control plane or compute plane?","buckets":["Control plane","Compute plane"],"items":[{"text":"Databricks web application","bucket":0},{"text":"Platform coordination services","bucket":0},{"text":"\"What should run?\"","bucket":0},{"text":"Executors processing your orders data","bucket":1},{"text":"\"Actually run it.\"","bucket":1},{"text":"Serverless compute resources","bucket":1}],"explain":"The **control plane** holds Databricks-managed services: the web app and coordination (\"what should run?\"). The **compute plane** is where data is actually processed (\"actually run it\"), whether serverless or classic."},{"id":"ch01-e084","type":"tf","section":"ch01-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"For serverless workloads, compute runs in a Databricks-managed serverless compute plane. Classic compute uses cloud infrastructure associated with the customer's cloud environment.","answer":true,"explain":"True. This is the source's first-preview distinction. Serverless means Databricks operates the compute. Classic means the compute runs in infrastructure tied to your own cloud environment."},{"id":"ch01-e085","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Where does your workload *actually process data*?","options":["Control plane","Compute plane","Unity Catalog","The Delta transaction log"],"answer":1,"why":["The control plane decides and coordinates, but does not process your data.","Correct.","UC governs objects.","The log records table state."],"explain":"The **compute plane** is where data processing happens. The control plane coordinates and serves the web app. Unity Catalog and the Delta log are not planes: they are governance and table-state layers."},{"id":"ch01-e086","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A team uses **classic** compute. Whose cloud environment hosts the compute infrastructure?","options":["The customer's cloud environment","The Databricks-managed serverless compute plane","The control plane","Unity Catalog's metastore"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","That's serverless.","The control plane hosts platform services, not your classic clusters.","The metastore is metadata, not compute."],"explain":"Classic compute uses cloud infrastructure associated with the **customer's** cloud environment. It's the serverless case where Databricks operates the compute plane, so mixing the two up is a typical trap."},{"id":"ch01-e087","type":"odd","section":"ch01-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong with the **control plane**?","options":["Web application","Platform coordination","\"What should run?\"","Executors processing data"],"answer":3,"explain":"The web application, coordination and \"what should run?\" all describe the control plane. Processing data on executors happens in the **compute plane**."},{"id":"ch01-e088","type":"scenario","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"`spark.read.table(\"sales.orders\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\").show()` is extremely slow. Walk through it like a professional.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your first move?","options":[{"text":"Identify which layer could be responsible (storage, layout, Delta, Spark, data, compute, query, stats, network)","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Finding the layer comes before any fix."},{"text":"Increase the cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"That's the anti-pattern: a fix without a diagnosis."},{"text":"Blame Spark and rewrite in pandas","ok":false,"fb":"\"Spark\" is not a diagnosis, and this throws away distributed compute."}]},{"prompt":"You suspect the table layout. What do you do next?","options":[{"text":"Collect evidence, e.g. check whether the scan reads a huge number of tiny files","ok":true,"fb":"Right. Get evidence for or against the hypothesis."},{"text":"Immediately rewrite the whole table","ok":false,"fb":"That's a fix before any evidence. You might rewrite for nothing."},{"text":"Add more executors","ok":false,"fb":"That doesn't test the hypothesis."}]},{"prompt":"You applied a fix. What's the last step?","options":[{"text":"Remeasure and compare with the original symptom","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A fix isn't done until it has been measured."},{"text":"Assume it worked and move on","ok":false,"fb":"Unverified fixes are guesses."},{"text":"Scale the cluster too, just in case","ok":false,"fb":"Then you can't tell which change helped."}]}],"explain":"Professionals go symptom → layer → evidence → hypothesis → metrics → fix → remeasure. Scaling the cluster blindly may hide the problem, cost more, or change nothing if the cause is in storage, layout, skew or statistics."},{"id":"ch01-e089","type":"order","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Put the professional debugging loop in order.","items":["symptom","identify layer","collect evidence","form hypothesis","check Spark/Databricks metrics","fix","remeasure"],"explain":"Symptom → identify layer → collect evidence → form hypothesis → check Spark/Databricks metrics → fix → remeasure. Skipping to \"increase cluster size\" jumps straight from symptom to an untested fix."},{"id":"ch01-e090","type":"match","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each layer to the hypothesis you'd test for a slow query.","pairs":[["storage","slow reads?"],["table layout","too many tiny files?"],["data","severe skew?"],["statistics","optimizer made a bad choice?"],["network","huge exchange?"],["compute","insufficient resources?"]],"explain":"Each layer has its own typical failure. Storage gives slow reads, table layout gives many tiny files, data gives skew, statistics give bad optimizer choices, and network gives huge exchanges. Naming the layer tells you what evidence to collect."},{"id":"ch01-e091","type":"mcq","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A query is slow. Which response best shows **professional** Databricks operation?","options":["Increase the cluster size right away","Identify the layer, collect evidence, form a hypothesis, check metrics, fix, remeasure","Restart the cluster until it's fast","Convert the table to CSV"],"answer":1,"why":["The classic anti-pattern.","Correct.","Restarting doesn't diagnose anything.","That makes everything worse: no columnar pruning."],"explain":"The professional identifies the layer and gathers evidence before fixing. \"Increase cluster size\" may hide the real cause (tiny files, skew, bad statistics) while costing more."},{"id":"ch01-e092","type":"bucket","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Round-1 review: sort the statements.","buckets":["Correct","Wrong"],"items":[{"text":"Delta = Parquet data files + transaction log","bucket":0},{"text":"Unity Catalog governs objects; Delta is the table format","bucket":0},{"text":"Storage and compute have independent lifetimes","bucket":0},{"text":"Spark = Databricks","bucket":1},{"text":"If a file exists in the table folder, it's in the current table","bucket":1},{"text":"Metadata and data are the same thing","bucket":1},{"text":"A table is just its storage directory","bucket":1}],"explain":"Each true line keeps two layers apart. Each false line merges layers that are only related. The exam often hides exactly these confusions inside longer answer options."},{"id":"ch01-e093","type":"spotbug","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Click the wrong statements in this Round-1 cheat sheet.","lines":["Spark ≠ Databricks","Delta = Parquet","storage ≠ compute","file exists ⇒ in current table","metadata ≠ data","Unity Catalog = Delta Lake"],"bugs":[1,3,5],"fix":"Delta ≠ Parquet (Parquet + transaction log)\nphysical file existence ≠ current table membership\nUnity Catalog ≠ Delta Lake","explain":"\"Delta = Parquet\" is wrong: Delta adds a transaction log to Parquet. \"file exists ⇒ in current table\" is wrong: physical existence ≠ current membership. \"Unity Catalog = Delta Lake\" is wrong: UC is governance and metadata, Delta is the table format."},{"id":"ch01-e094","type":"odd","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are *layers* to suspect for a slow query. Which one is **not** a layer but an anti-pattern fix?","options":["storage","table layout","statistics","increase cluster size"],"answer":3,"explain":"Storage, table layout and statistics are layers you investigate. \"Increase cluster size\" is a fix applied without evidence: the anti-pattern this section warns about."},{"id":"ch01-e095","type":"free","section":"ch01-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview: *\"Our `groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` on `sales.orders` is slow. What do you do?\"* Answer in 3–5 sentences.","model":"I wouldn't start by scaling the cluster. I'd first identify the layer: storage (slow reads), table layout (many tiny files), Delta organization, Spark (huge shuffle), data (skewed country), compute (insufficient resources), query, statistics (bad optimizer choice) or network (huge exchange). Then I'd collect evidence — Spark UI/metrics, the plan — form a hypothesis, apply a targeted fix, and remeasure against the original symptom.","rubric":["Rejects blind cluster scaling","Names multiple candidate layers","Collects evidence (metrics / plan / UI)","Hypothesis → fix","Remeasure"],"explain":"A strong answer names several layers, says how you would get **evidence** for each, and closes with remeasurement. It also explicitly rejects \"just scale the cluster\"."}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch02","num":2,"title":"Data Engineering Building Blocks","subtitle":"ETL/ELT, batch vs streaming vs incremental, checkpoints, CDC, schema contracts and the medallion architecture","emoji":"🧱","sourcePages":"26–52","mantra":"Keep the raw truth, process only what changed, respect the schema contract, and refine data step by step from Bronze to Gold.","objectives":["You can explain where the transformation happens in ETL vs ELT, and why keeping raw data lets you rebuild everything downstream.","You can classify any workload on two independent axes: batch vs streaming, and full recompute vs incremental.","You can explain micro-batches and why streaming still runs on Spark jobs, stages and tasks.","You can explain what a checkpoint remembers and what happens when a stream restarts with or without it.","You can read a CDC stream (I/U/D + sequence + key), apply it correctly, and choose SCD Type 1 vs Type 2.","You can tell schema enforcement from schema evolution and debug a schema mismatch without 'enabling evolution everywhere'.","You can place any dataset in Bronze, Silver or Gold and defend the choice."],"sections":[{"id":"ch02-s01","title":"From Storage Model to Pipeline Model","hook":"Every Databricks product solves one pipeline problem. Learn the problems first and the product names become easy labels.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Round 1 built the **storage model**: who computes, how files are stored, and who governs them."},{"t":"table","head":["Component","Role in one phrase"],"rows":[["Spark","compute (the execution engine)"],["Parquet","file format"],["Delta Lake","transactional table layer"],["Unity Catalog","metadata + governance"],["Cloud storage","durable (permanent) storage"]],"caption":"Recap of Round 1"},{"t":"p","text":"Round 2 builds the **pipeline model**. Before you touch Databricks buttons and APIs, you need to know which problem each tool is trying to solve."},{"t":"diagram","text":"data source\n    │\n    ▼\nETL / ELT\n    │\n    ▼\nbatch ── streaming ── incremental processing\n    │\n    ▼\nCDC (change data capture)\n    │\n    ▼\nschema enforcement / schema evolution\n    │\n    ▼\nBronze → Silver → Gold\n    │\n    ▼\nidentities / permissions          (→ Chapter 3)\n    │\n    ▼\ndev → test → prod → CI/CD         (→ Chapter 3)","caption":"The concept chain of this round"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Problem first, product second","text":"Auto Loader, Lakeflow pipelines, AUTO CDC, Jobs and Bundles are all answers to the problems in this chain. If the problems are clear, you can learn Databricks from zero to production, debugging and certification without memorizing product names blindly."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why not start directly with the Databricks UI?","text":"Because the UI shows you *solutions*. Without knowing the problem (e.g. 'how do I process only new data?'), every button looks like an unrelated product."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Storage model vs pipeline model","text":"The storage model is the warehouse building (shelves, locks, inventory list). The pipeline model is the logistics: how goods arrive, get checked, sorted and shipped to customers."}]},{"id":"ch02-s02","title":"ETL vs ELT","hook":"The real question is WHERE the transformation happens — and whether you keep the raw data.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a company with **PostgreSQL** (`customers`, `orders`), the **Shopify API** (`products`) and **CSV files** (marketing campaigns). You want all of it in your analytics system."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Extract","def":"Take data out of the source, e.g. `SELECT ...` from PostgreSQL → raw orders."},{"term":"Transform","def":"Change the data: clean, cast, validate, join."},{"term":"Load","def":"Write the data into the target system, e.g. the Delta table `company.silver.orders`."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"\" Athens \"            → \"ATHENS\"            (trim + upper case)\n\"19.95\"               → DECIMAL(10,2)       (cast)\nNULL age              → validation / quarantine\ncustomer_id + orders  → JOIN","caption":"Typical transformations"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"ETL (traditional)","points":["Extract → **Transform** → Load","source → extract → ETL server → transform → warehouse","Transform first, load only the clean result","Raw input is usually not kept"]},{"title":"ELT (modern cloud / lakehouse)","points":["Extract → **Load** → Transform","PostgreSQL → raw data → lakehouse → Spark/SQL transformations → clean tables","Load raw first, transform inside the platform","Raw input is kept (this becomes Bronze)"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why ELT fits Databricks:** the lakehouse stores huge amounts of raw data cheaply and brings distributed compute only when needed. So you first keep *exactly what came from the source*, then transform."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"order_id,customer_id,amount\n101,7,\"19.50\"\n102,12,\"52.90\"","caption":"Incoming CSV — note that amount is a quoted string"},{"t":"flow","items":["CSV","parse","validate","amount → DECIMAL","load clean table"],"caption":"ETL-like"},{"t":"flow","items":["CSV","store raw in Bronze","later parse / validate","Silver Delta table"],"caption":"ELT-like"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The ELT superpower: you can change your mind","text":"Tomorrow you discover `\"19.50\"` was **USD, not EUR**. With ELT you still have the original raw dataset, so you just fix the rule and rebuild — you don't necessarily have to ask the source system again."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Don't get stuck on the label","text":"Databricks now uses **ETL** very broadly for data-engineering pipelines, even when the physical architecture is quite ELT-like. In exam questions focus on *where the transformation happens*, not on the acronym."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a nightly API returns JSON. You must (1) never lose the original response, (2) change transformation rules later, (3) rebuild downstream tables. ETL or ELT?","text":"**ELT: Extract → Load raw → Transform.** You first keep an immutable/raw representation and build all downstream datasets from it."}]},{"id":"ch02-s03","title":"Batch vs Streaming (and Micro-batches)","hook":"Is your input finite or endless? That single question separates batch from streaming.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Today you have `orders.csv`: **1 GB, 1,000,000 rows**. You run:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.read.csv(\"/data/orders.csv\")\nresult = (\n    df.groupBy(\"country\")\n      .count()\n)\nresult.write.saveAsTable(\"sales_per_country\")","caption":"A classic batch job"},{"t":"p","text":"The dataset has a clear end: row 1, row 2 … row 1,000,000, **END**. That is a **bounded dataset**."},{"t":"flow","items":["START","read all input","compute","write output","STOP"],"caption":"Batch processing"},{"t":"p","text":"Now think of **Kafka**: 12:00 order 101, 12:00 order 102, 12:01 order 103 … There is no natural END OF FILE. The dataset is logically **unbounded** (events, events, events … ∞)."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Batch","points":["Bounded input (has an END)","Read everything → compute → write → stop","Example: yesterday's orders.csv"]},{"title":"Streaming","points":["Unbounded input (no natural end)","Computation keeps going as new data arrives","Example: Kafka topic of orders"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"In **Spark Structured Streaming** you often write transformations almost like batch DataFrames, but the engine keeps updating the result as data arrives. Databricks describes it as near-real-time processing with **incremental computation** and **fault tolerance**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Streaming ≠ one row at a time","text":"A common mistake is to imagine `event1 → process, event2 → process …`. Spark Structured Streaming often groups incoming records into **micro-batches** and runs each one through normal distributed Spark execution."},{"t":"diagram","text":"events arriving:  1  2  3  4  5\n                  │\n                  ▼\nmicro-batch 1:  [1 2]\nmicro-batch 2:  [3 4]\nmicro-batch 3:  [5]","caption":"Micro-batching"},{"t":"flow","items":["micro-batch","Spark job","stages","tasks","partitions","possible shuffle"],"caption":"Each micro-batch is a normal Spark execution"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Streaming builds on Spark — it doesn't replace it","text":"Everything you learned about jobs, stages, tasks, partitions and shuffles still applies inside every micro-batch. How often a micro-batch starts is controlled by a streaming **trigger** — you'll configure it when we reach Structured Streaming / Auto Loader."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: does a Kafka topic of orders ever reach END OF FILE?","text":"No. New orders can always arrive, so the input is logically unbounded. That is why the computation must keep running (or keep being re-run) instead of 'read everything and stop'."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Micro-batches now in depth: the unbounded-table model, lazy `readStream`, `processingTime` vs `availableNow` triggers, and what happens when a batch takes longer than its trigger (backlog, not parallel batches) — see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, sections 1–4.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch02-s04","title":"Incremental Processing","hook":"Streaming answers 'how often'; incremental answers 'how much do I reprocess'. They are different axes.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Streaming ≠ incremental","text":"Streaming and incremental processing are **not the same thing**. One describes the input/timing, the other describes how much work is redone."},{"t":"table","head":["Day","Total data","Naive pipeline processes","Incremental pipeline processes"],"rows":[["Day 1","100 GB","100 GB","100 GB"],["Day 2","102 GB (+2)","102 GB","2 GB"],["Day 3","105 GB (+3)","105 GB","3 GB"]],"caption":"Why recompute everything when only 3 GB is new?"},{"t":"diagram","text":"previous state\n      +\nnew / changed input\n      =\nnew state\n\nNOT:  recompute everything from zero","caption":"Incremental processing in one picture"},{"t":"p","text":"**Batch can be incremental.** Every night at 02:00 the pipeline runs once and reads only yesterday's rows:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *\nFROM transactions\nWHERE transaction_date = CURRENT_DATE - 1;","caption":"Batch (runs once a day) + incremental (only new rows)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Streaming is usually incremental.** The checkpoint says *processed up to event 100000*; events 100001, 100002, 100003 arrive and only those are processed — the previous 100,000 are not necessarily re-read."},{"t":"table","head":["","Full recompute","Incremental"],"rows":[["Batch","Nightly job re-reads the whole 105 GB","Nightly job reads only yesterday's rows"],["Streaming","Unusual / wasteful","Typical: continue after the checkpoint"]],"caption":"Two independent axes"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Batch + incremental is a valid answer","text":"If a job runs once a day (batch) but reads only rows created since the last run, it is **both batch and incremental**. Exams love this combination."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: 1 TB of historical orders; every night 10 MB of new orders arrive; a 03:00 job processes only those 10 MB. What is it?","text":"**Batch + incremental.** It runs on a schedule over a finite chunk (batch) and touches only new data (incremental). It is not streaming, and it is certainly not a full refresh."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → incremental file ingestion","text":"Chapter 12 shows why re-running `spark.read` over a folder duplicates data, how COPY INTO remembers loaded files, and how Auto Loader with `.trigger(availableNow=True)` gives a scheduled job that is batch-like *and* incremental. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"The streaming engine can itself run **batch + incremental**: `writeStream.trigger(availableNow=True)` on a schedule processes only what's new since the checkpoint, then stops — no hand-written `last_processed_timestamp`. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 4.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch02-s05","title":"Checkpoints: Remembering Where You Stopped","hook":"A stream that crashes must know whether to continue at 901 or start again at 1.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A streaming pipeline has processed **events 1 → 900**. The server crashes. It comes back up. **Where does it continue?**"},{"t":"p","text":"Somewhere there must be state like *\"I've already processed until offset 900.\"* Storing that progress is one of the roles of the **checkpoint**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"SOURCE\n──────────────────────────────────────\n1  2  3  ...  899  900 │ 901  902 ...\n                        ▲\n                   checkpoint = \"900\"\n\nrestart → process 901, 902, ...","caption":"The checkpoint marks committed progress"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without checkpoint","points":["Engine can't easily know whether to start at 1 or continue at 901","Restart from 1 → reprocessing / duplicates","Guessing could skip data"]},{"title":"With checkpoint","points":["Engine reads 'processed until 900'","Resumes at 901","No re-reading of the first 900 events needed"]}]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Offset","def":"A position in the source stream (e.g. event number 900 in a Kafka partition)."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"Stored progress/state of a stream, e.g. the last offset it has fully processed, used to resume after a restart."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Reset checkpoint = amnesia","text":"If a checkpoint is deleted, reset, or the stream is pointed at a new checkpoint location, the engine 'forgets' its progress and may re-ingest old data. A **checkpoint reset** is one of the classic causes of duplicate records (see Chapter 3, debugging case 2)."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself after a streaming restart","questions":["Did the stream restart with the SAME checkpoint location as before?","Has the checkpoint advanced past the last offset I expected?","Did anyone delete or reset the checkpoint (or change its path)?","Are the events after the restart starting right after the last committed offset — not at 1?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Deeper later","text":"We'll study checkpoint internals in depth with Structured Streaming and Auto Loader. For now: checkpoint = 'where I stopped'."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → checkpoints in Auto Loader","text":"Auto Loader has TWO state locations: `checkpointLocation` (progress — which files are done) and `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` (the inferred schema and its history). Chapter 12 adds the incident playbooks: checkpoint deleted, shared between streams, or kept after changing the source path. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Open the box: a checkpoint holds **offsets/** (what each micro-batch reads), **commits/** (what reached the sink), **state/** (stateful operators) and **metadata** (query identity) — not just 'the last offset'. Why new code can make it incompatible, and why deleting it means a brand-new query: see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 9.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch02-s06","title":"CDC: Change Data Capture","hook":"Don't copy 2 billion rows every hour to learn that one customer moved city.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Operational **PostgreSQL** has a `customers` table:"},{"t":"table","head":["id","name","city"],"rows":[["1","Maria","Athens"],["2","John","Patras"]]},{"t":"p","text":"At 10:00 Maria moves from Athens to Volos:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"UPDATE customers\nSET city = 'Volos'\nWHERE id = 1;"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Solution 1: re-read the whole table","points":["10:00 SELECT * FROM customers","11:00 SELECT * FROM customers","12:00 SELECT * FROM customers","With 2 billion rows → tragically expensive"]},{"title":"Solution 2: send only the changes","points":["Source emits `UPDATE id=1 city=Volos`","Lakehouse applies just that change","This is **Change Data Capture (CDC)**"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["op","id","name","city","seq"],"rows":[["I","1","Maria","Athens","10"],["I","2","John","Patras","11"],["U","1","Maria","Volos","12"],["D","2","John","null","13"]],"caption":"A conceptual CDC stream: I = INSERT, U = UPDATE, D = DELETE, seq = order of the changes"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why do we need the `seq` column if events arrive in a queue anyway?","text":"Because **arrival order ≠ the order the source made the changes**. In a distributed network, seq 18 can arrive before seq 17."},{"t":"diagram","text":"source changes:   seq 17: city = Volos\n                  seq 18: city = Larisa   (latest truth)\n\narrival order:    18 ......... then 17\n\n\"last event received wins\" → Volos   ✗ WRONG\norder by seq               → Larisa  ✓ CORRECT","caption":"Out-of-order arrival"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Arrival order is not source order","text":"If you apply 'last event received wins', late-arriving older events overwrite newer truth. CDC needs **ordering/sequencing** (a seq, version or event time from the source)."},{"t":"list","items":["**Ordering / sequencing** — to know which change is newest (seq, version, event ordering).","**A primary/business key** — to know WHICH entity each change applies to (e.g. customer `id`)."],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Deleted at source, alive forever in Silver","text":"A customer was deleted from PostgreSQL but still exists in Silver forever. Suspect **CDC/delete handling**: the delete event may not be captured, or it is captured but not applied."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when the CDC result looks wrong","questions":["Am I applying changes by the source sequence (seq/version), or by arrival order?","Is the key I match on really the primary/business key of the entity?","Are DELETE events captured at the source at all?","If they are captured, does my apply logic actually execute deletes?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Streaming FROM a Delta table is append-oriented: if the source gets UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE, read its **Change Data Feed** (`readChangeFeed`) to propagate row-level changes, or deliberately skip them (`skipChangeCommits`). see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 12.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch02-s07","title":"SCD Type 1 vs Type 2 (and AUTO CDC)","hook":"Do you need only today's truth — or the full history of truths?","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**SCD Type 1** (Slowly Changing Dimension Type 1): keep only the **current state**. Old values are overwritten."},{"t":"diagram","text":"customer_id = 1\nMaria | Athens\n      │  UPDATE\n      ▼\nMaria | Volos        ← Athens is gone","caption":"SCD Type 1"},{"t":"p","text":"**SCD Type 2**: keep **history**. Each change closes the old version and opens a new one."},{"t":"table","head":["customer","city","from","to"],"rows":[["Maria","Athens","2024-01-01","2026-08-10"],["Maria","Volos","2026-08-10","NULL (current)"]],"caption":"SCD Type 2"},{"t":"p","text":"Now you can ask: *Where did Maria live on 1 July 2025?* → **Athens** (2024-01-01 ≤ date < 2026-08-10)."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"SCD Type 1","points":["Overwrite in place","Only current state","1 row per key","Question it answers: 'Where does she live now?'"]},{"title":"SCD Type 2","points":["Close old version, add new one","Full history with validity ranges","1 row per version","Question it answers: 'Where did she live on date X?'"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["seq","event"],"rows":[["20","INSERT id=4 salary=1000"],["21","UPDATE id=4 salary=1300"],["22","UPDATE id=4 salary=1500"]],"caption":"Exercise input"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"SCD1 result:\n  id=4  salary=1500\n\nSCD2 result (version history):\n  id  salary  valid_from  valid_to\n  4   1000    ...         ...\n  4   1300    ...         ...\n  4   1500    ...         current","caption":"Same events, two outcomes"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"AUTO CDC vs APPLY CHANGES","text":"Today Databricks provides **AUTO CDC** and **AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT** in Lakeflow pipelines; they can manage **SCD Type 1 and Type 2**. The older **APPLY CHANGES** APIs still exist, but current docs recommend AUTO CDC. Older courses and exam questions may still use the old name — it's the same concept."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: in the SCD2 table, what does `to = NULL` mean?","text":"That row is the **current** version — it has not been closed by a newer change yet."}]},{"id":"ch02-s08","title":"Schema as a Contract: Enforcement","hook":"A bad upstream value should be stopped at the door, not discovered on the CEO's dashboard.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A **schema** = column names + column types. Treat it as a **contract** between producer and consumers."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"orders\n  order_id     BIGINT\n  customer_id  BIGINT\n  amount       DECIMAL\n  country      STRING","caption":"The orders contract"},{"t":"p","text":"Someone tries to write `amount = {\"foo\": 12}` or a column `unknown_column`. You want to say: *I don't accept arbitrary data that doesn't match the contract.* That is **schema enforcement**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Delta enforces on write","text":"For Delta tables, Databricks validates the schema **at write time** and rejects incompatible data — unless you have enabled an allowed schema-evolution procedure."},{"t":"p","text":"Why it matters: Power BI expects `amount: DECIMAL`. Suddenly the upstream producer sends `amount: STRING` with the value `\"unknown\"`."},{"t":"flow","items":["upstream schema bug","Silver corrupted","Gold wrong","dashboard broken"],"caption":"What happens if you accept anything"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Fail early, fail cheap","text":"Schema enforcement can stop the problem at the first write, before bad data spreads to Silver, Gold and BI."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Schema-on-write","def":"Structure is checked when data is written (Delta's schema enforcement)."},{"term":"Schema-on-read","def":"Data is stored as-is and structure is applied when it's read/parsed later (the raw ELT/Bronze idea)."},{"term":"Schema drift","def":"Informal name for the source schema changing over time — sometimes intentionally, sometimes by bug."}]}]},{"id":"ch02-s09","title":"Schema Evolution & Debugging a Schema Mismatch","hook":"Schemas change legitimately. The skill is evolving on purpose — not by accident.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Today `orders` has `id, amount`. Tomorrow the source adds `discount_code`. You don't always want **FAIL FOREVER** — you may want the schema to change in a **controlled** way. That is **schema evolution**."},{"t":"list","items":["New columns","Renaming / dropping columns","Type widening or other type changes"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Enforcement and evolution are NOT opposites","text":"It's not *enforcement OR evolution*. It's usually **enforcement + an explicit evolution policy**: by default I protect the schema, but I allow specific changes under specific rules."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Schema enforcement","points":["Protects the existing contract","Rejects incompatible writes","Default behaviour"]},{"title":"Schema evolution","points":["Allows a controlled change of the contract","E.g. add `discount_code`","Explicitly enabled, with rules"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"AnalysisException: schema mismatch","text":"Yesterday the pipeline worked. Today: `AnalysisException ... schema mismatch`. Do **not** immediately write 'enable schema evolution everywhere'. First ask what actually changed."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Did the source schema really change?\n   │\n   ├── yes → was it intentional?\n   │          ├── yes → evolve carefully\n   │          └── no  → source bug (fix upstream)\n   │\n   └── no  → parsing / type-inference bug on our side","caption":"Schema-mismatch decision tree — this is production thinking"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when you hit a schema mismatch","questions":["Did the source schema REALLY change, or did my parsing/type inference change?","If it changed, was the change intentional (announced, documented)?","If intentional, which exact evolution (add column, rename, widen type) do I need — and only where?","If unintentional, who owns the source bug upstream?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: target is `customer_id BIGINT, age INT, country STRING`; the source suddenly also has `loyalty_points INT`. What happened, and do you accept it?","text":"It's a **schema-evolution candidate: add column**. That does NOT automatically mean you accept it — first confirm the change is expected/intentional, then evolve in a controlled way."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Blind evolution","text":"Turning on every possible schema evolution everywhere is the WORST option: real upstream bugs then flow silently into Silver and Gold."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → schema evolution at ingestion time","text":"Auto Loader makes the 'evolve vs enforce' decision concrete with `cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode` (addNewColumns / rescue / failOnNewColumns / none) and a `_rescued_data` column that keeps values that don't fit. Its default deliberately stops the stream on a new column, then continues with the new schema after restart. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch02-s10","title":"Medallion Architecture: Bronze, Silver, Gold","hook":"Everything so far snaps together here: raw truth, cleaning, CDC and business-ready data.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Databricks widely recommends **Bronze → Silver → Gold**. It is **not mandatory** — it's a design pattern of **progressively increasing data quality**."},{"t":"flow","items":["Bronze (raw)","Silver (clean, validated)","Gold (business-ready)"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Bronze** — keep as faithfully as possible what came from the source (e.g. Kafka → Bronze orders):"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"{\n  \"id\": \"00192\",\n  \"amount\": \"25.98\",\n  \"customer\": \"  MARIA \",\n  \"event_time\": \"...\"\n}","caption":"A Bronze record — messy on purpose"},{"t":"list","items":["Duplicates","Nulls","Bad formatting","Incorrect cases","Unexpected fields"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"That's not necessarily a problem: Bronze is very close to the **source truth**."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why on earth keep 'dirty' data?","text":"Suppose today's rule was wrong: `amount = amount * 100` and you built a broken Silver. With Bronze: Bronze → correct code → **rebuild Silver**. Without raw data you may have to re-request it from the source — and the source may no longer have it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Silver** — cleaned, validated, usable data. Typical work here:"},{"t":"list","items":["parsing","casting","deduplication","null handling","quality checks","CDC application","joins","normalization","business-key validation"],"ordered":false},{"t":"table","head":["Field","Bronze","Silver"],"rows":[["id","\"00192\" (string)","192 (BIGINT)"],["customer","\"  MARIA \" (string)","\"MARIA\" (STRING)"],["amount","\"25.98\" (string)","25.98 (DECIMAL)"]],"caption":"Same record, Bronze → Silver"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks describes Silver as the cleansed/validated/refined layer that data analysts and data scientists can use."},{"t":"p","text":"**Gold** — data shaped for a **specific business use**: `gold.daily_sales`, `customer_lifetime_value`, `monthly_revenue_by_product`."},{"t":"table","head":["date","country","revenue"],"rows":[["2026-09-14","GR","190000"],["2026-09-14","DE","310000"]],"caption":"gold.daily_sales"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Gold ≠ 'Silver but cleaner'","text":"Gold is usually **consumer/business oriented**: aggregates, metrics, models for a specific audience — not just an extra cleaning pass."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Bronze","points":["Source-faithful raw data","Duplicates/nulls allowed","Purpose: audit, reprocess, debug"]},{"title":"Silver","points":["Clean, validated, deduplicated","CDC applied, types cast","Purpose: reusable datasets for analysts/DS"]},{"title":"Gold","points":["Business-shaped aggregates/models","e.g. daily_sales, lifetime value","Purpose: specific consumers (BI, CEO)"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → a production Bronze table","text":"A robust Bronze row = source fields (often kept as STRING) + `_source_file` + `_ingested_at` + `_rescued_data`; rows with rescued content go to a quarantine table, and business-key dedup/MERGE happens in Silver. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch02-s11","title":"Medallion in Practice: The E-shop End to End","hook":"One picture that ties ingestion, CDC, cleaning and aggregation together.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"PostgreSQL ───┐\nKafka ────────┼──►  ┌──────────┐\nJSON files ───┘     │  BRONZE  │\n                    └────┬─────┘\n                         │ parse / validate\n                         │ deduplicate\n                         │ apply CDC\n                         │ clean types\n                         ▼\n                    ┌──────────┐\n                    │  SILVER  │\n                    │ customers│\n                    │ orders   │\n                    │ products │\n                    └────┬─────┘\n                         │ aggregate / model\n                         ▼\n                    ┌──────────┐\n                    │   GOLD   │\n                    └──────────┘","caption":"The full e-shop example"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Layers are not file formats","text":"Don't think *Bronze = CSV, Silver = Parquet, Gold = database*. All three can be **Delta tables**. The difference is **semantic / quality / purpose**, not necessarily file format."},{"t":"table","head":["Layer","Typical Lakeflow dataset type (current Databricks recommendation)"],"rows":[["Bronze","Streaming tables"],["Silver","Streaming tables or materialized views, depending on the transformation"],["Gold","Materialized views for aggregations / metrics"]]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: where do these go? A raw JSON exactly as received · B orders with validated types, duplicates removed · C monthly revenue by country · D customer table after applying CDC · E malformed records captured for investigation","text":"A → Bronze · B → Silver · C → Gold · D → Silver · E → usually Bronze / a quarantine layer. E depends on the architecture — but you **never make bad records silently disappear**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Never silently drop malformed records","text":"Capture them (Bronze or a quarantine table) so they can be investigated and replayed."},{"t":"diagram","text":"SOURCE\n  │  extract / ingest\n  ▼\n┌────────┐\n│ BRONZE │\n└───┬────┘\n    │  validate · clean · CDC · evolve schema\n    ▼\n┌────────┐\n│ SILVER │\n└───┬────┘\n    │  model · aggregate\n    ▼\n┌────────┐\n│  GOLD  │\n└────────┘","caption":"The mental model to keep: which work happens at which boundary"}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch02-d01","title":"Write fails: AnalysisException — schema mismatch","section":"ch02-s09","symptom":"A pipeline that worked yesterday fails today with `AnalysisException ... schema mismatch` when writing to a Delta table.","askYourself":["Did the source schema REALLY change, or only what my code inferred?","Can I diff yesterday's input schema against today's (columns + types)?","If it changed: was it intentional — announced, documented, agreed with the producer?","If intentional: what is the narrowest evolution I need (add column, rename, widen type) and on which table?","If not intentional: who owns the source, and how do I stop bad data meanwhile?","If it did NOT change: did my parsing or type inference produce a different type today?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the incoming schema with the table schema","why":"Identify exactly which column/type differs.","code":"df.printSchema()\nDESCRIBE TABLE silver.orders;"},{"do":"Check with the source owner / release notes whether the change was planned","why":"Only intentional changes should be evolved."},{"do":"If unchanged at source, inspect parsing / type inference (e.g. a stray header row turning INT into STRING)","why":"The bug may be on our side."},{"do":"Apply a narrow, explicit evolution — or fix the bug — and re-run","why":"Never 'enable schema evolution everywhere'."}],"rootCauses":["Intentional upstream change (e.g. new column) not yet allowed by the table","Unintentional upstream bug (e.g. amount sent as STRING 'unknown')","Parsing / type-inference bug in our own ingestion"],"fix":"Changed + intentional → evolve carefully; changed + unintentional → fix the source; unchanged → fix parsing/inference. Keep enforcement on.","mnemonic":"Changed? → Intended? → Evolve narrowly. (C-I-E)"},{"id":"ch02-d02","title":"Deleted at source, still alive in Silver","section":"ch02-s06","symptom":"A customer was deleted in PostgreSQL, but the row is still present in Silver — forever.","askYourself":["Is the customer really deleted in the source?","Does the raw CDC feed in Bronze contain a D (DELETE) event for that key?","If not: is my CDC capture configured to emit deletes at all?","If yes: does my Silver apply logic handle DELETE operations?","Am I matching the delete on the real primary/business key?","Is the delete being applied in source sequence order (not overwritten by an older late event)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Query Bronze for change events of that key","why":"Splits the problem: captured vs not captured."},{"do":"Inspect the Silver CDC apply logic (AUTO CDC config / MERGE)","why":"Look for ignored D events or a wrong key."},{"do":"Check sequencing for that key","why":"An older out-of-order UPDATE could re-create the row."}],"rootCauses":["Delete events not captured by CDC","Apply logic ignores deletes","Wrong key in the apply step","Out-of-order events applied by arrival order"],"fix":"Capture deletes at the source, apply them by key in sequence order (e.g. with AUTO CDC), then rebuild/repair Silver.","mnemonic":"Captured? Applied? Right key? Right order?"},{"id":"ch02-d03","title":"Wrong 'latest' state after CDC","section":"ch02-s06","symptom":"The Silver customer table shows Volos, but the source says the latest city is Larisa.","askYourself":["Am I applying changes by arrival order or by the source sequence?","Does every CDC event carry a seq / version / event-time from the source?","Did events for the same key arrive out of order?","Is my key the true primary/business key?"],"steps":[{"do":"List all CDC events for the key with their seq and arrival time","why":"Reveals out-of-order arrival (e.g. 18 before 17)."},{"do":"Check the apply logic's ordering column","why":"'Last received wins' is the bug."},{"do":"Re-apply ordered by seq","why":"The highest seq per key is the truth."}],"rootCauses":["'Last event received wins' logic","Missing or ignored sequence column","Wrong key"],"fix":"Always order and resolve CDC changes per key by the source sequence (seq/version), never by arrival.","mnemonic":"Arrival ≠ truth; seq = truth."},{"id":"ch02-d04","title":"Stream restarted: reprocessed or skipped data","section":"ch02-s05","symptom":"After a crash or redeploy, the stream either re-ingests old events (duplicates) or seems to have skipped events.","askYourself":["Did the stream restart with the SAME checkpoint location?","Was the checkpoint deleted, reset or moved during redeploy?","What offset does the checkpoint say was processed last?","Do the first events after restart start right after that offset?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the checkpoint path in the old and new job config","why":"A new path = a brand-new stream with no memory."},{"do":"Inspect the last committed offset","why":"Tells you where the engine believes it stopped."},{"do":"Compare Bronze row counts per source offset/file before and after restart","why":"Shows duplicates or gaps."}],"rootCauses":["Checkpoint reset/deleted","Checkpoint location changed in a redeploy","Restart without any checkpoint"],"fix":"Keep a stable checkpoint per stream; if it was reset, deduplicate the replayed range (e.g. in Silver) and restore the correct checkpoint setup.","mnemonic":"Same path? Same progress? Next offset?"},{"id":"ch02-d05","title":"Silver is wrong because a transformation rule was wrong","section":"ch02-s10","symptom":"Gold metrics are off (e.g. revenue 100× too high) and the cause is a bad rule like `amount = amount * 100` in Silver.","askYourself":["Is the error already present in Silver, or only in Gold?","Which transformation rule produced it, and since when?","Do I still have the raw data in Bronze for that period?","Which downstream tables depend on the wrong Silver table?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare a few Bronze vs Silver records","why":"Proves the rule, not the source, is wrong."},{"do":"Fix the transformation code","why":"Fix the cause, not the symptom."},{"do":"Rebuild Silver from Bronze, then Gold from Silver","why":"Recompute in dependency order."}],"rootCauses":["Wrong transformation logic","No raw copy kept (makes recovery hard)"],"fix":"Correct the code and rebuild downstream from Bronze. This is why Bronze exists.","mnemonic":"Fix code → rebuild from Bronze → cascade down."}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Fighting over the ETL/ELT label","text":"Databricks calls most pipelines 'ETL' even when they're ELT-like.","fix":"Focus on where the transformation happens and whether raw is kept."},{"title":"Not keeping raw data","text":"Pure ETL stores only the transformed output; a wrong rule (USD vs EUR, amount*100) can't be fixed without re-asking the source.","fix":"Load raw first (Bronze) and transform downstream."},{"title":"Streaming = one row at a time","text":"Structured Streaming often processes micro-batches through normal Spark execution.","fix":"Think micro-batch → job → stages → tasks → partitions."},{"title":"Streaming = incremental (and batch ≠ incremental)","text":"They are independent axes; a nightly job can be batch + incremental.","fix":"Ask separately: how is it triggered? how much is reprocessed?"},{"title":"Full reload of a huge table every day","text":"Reprocessing 5 TB to pick up 20 MB of changes wastes time and money.","fix":"Process only new/changed data (incremental, CDC)."},{"title":"Resetting or moving the checkpoint","text":"The stream forgets its progress and replays or skips data.","fix":"Keep a stable checkpoint location per stream."},{"title":"Last-received-wins CDC","text":"Arrival order ≠ source order; late old events overwrite newer truth.","fix":"Order changes per key by seq/version."},{"title":"Ignoring DELETE events","text":"Customers deleted at source live forever in Silver.","fix":"Capture and apply deletes by key."},{"title":"Thinking APPLY CHANGES and AUTO CDC are unrelated","text":"Old material uses APPLY CHANGES; current docs recommend AUTO CDC.","fix":"Treat them as old/new names for the CDC API."},{"title":"Enforcement vs evolution as opposites","text":"Production uses both together.","fix":"Enforce by default + explicit, narrow evolution policy."},{"title":"Enabling schema evolution everywhere","text":"Real upstream bugs flow silently into Silver and Gold.","fix":"Investigate: changed? intended? then evolve narrowly."},{"title":"Treating every new field as an error","text":"New fields can be legitimate evolution.","fix":"Confirm intent, then evolve in a controlled way."},{"title":"Calling Bronze duplicates a bug","text":"Bronze intentionally preserves raw source behaviour.","fix":"Deduplicate in Silver."},{"title":"Gold = cleaner Silver","text":"Gold is shaped for specific business consumers.","fix":"Build Gold as aggregates/models for a use case."},{"title":"Bronze = CSV, Silver = Parquet, Gold = database","text":"All layers can be Delta tables.","fix":"Distinguish layers by semantics, quality, purpose."},{"title":"Silently dropping malformed records","text":"Data disappears without trace.","fix":"Capture them in Bronze or a quarantine table."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"Storage model in five lines?","a":"Spark = compute · Parquet = file format · Delta Lake = transactional table layer · Unity Catalog = metadata + governance · Cloud storage = durable storage","section":"ch02-s01"},{"q":"ETL vs ELT in one line?","a":"ETL transforms before loading; ELT loads raw first and transforms inside the platform.","section":"ch02-s02"},{"q":"Why does ELT fit a lakehouse?","a":"Cheap storage for huge raw data + distributed compute on demand → keep raw, transform later, rebuild anytime.","section":"ch02-s02"},{"q":"What should you focus on in exam ETL/ELT questions?","a":"Where the transformation happens — Databricks uses 'ETL' broadly.","section":"ch02-s02"},{"q":"Bounded vs unbounded?","a":"Bounded = has an END (a file); unbounded = no natural end (Kafka events).","section":"ch02-s03"},{"q":"What is a micro-batch?","a":"A small group of incoming streaming records processed together as a normal Spark job.","section":"ch02-s03"},{"q":"Does streaming replace Spark's execution model?","a":"No — each micro-batch runs as job → stages → tasks → partitions (possibly shuffle).","section":"ch02-s03"},{"q":"Incremental processing formula?","a":"previous state + new/changed input = new state (not recompute from zero).","section":"ch02-s04"},{"q":"Can batch be incremental?","a":"Yes — e.g. a 02:00 job reading only yesterday's rows is batch + incremental.","section":"ch02-s04"},{"q":"1 TB history, 10 MB nightly, job processes only 10 MB → ?","a":"Batch + incremental.","section":"ch02-s04"},{"q":"What does a checkpoint remember (conceptually)?","a":"How far the stream has processed, e.g. 'processed until offset 900' → resume at 901.","section":"ch02-s05"},{"q":"What happens if the checkpoint is reset?","a":"The stream forgets its progress → replays (duplicates) or wrong resume point.","section":"ch02-s05"},{"q":"What is CDC?","a":"Capturing only inserts/updates/deletes from a source instead of reloading full snapshots.","section":"ch02-s06"},{"q":"CDC op codes?","a":"I = INSERT, U = UPDATE, D = DELETE (+ seq for order, key for entity).","section":"ch02-s06"},{"q":"Why not trust arrival order in CDC?","a":"Arrival order ≠ source modification order; use seq/version.","section":"ch02-s06"},{"q":"Two things every CDC apply needs?","a":"Ordering/sequencing and a primary/business key.","section":"ch02-s06"},{"q":"Deleted at source, alive in Silver → suspect?","a":"CDC/delete handling: delete not captured or not applied.","section":"ch02-s06"},{"q":"SCD Type 1?","a":"Overwrite — keep only the current state.","section":"ch02-s07"},{"q":"SCD Type 2?","a":"Keep history: one row per version with valid_from / valid_to (NULL/current = latest).","section":"ch02-s07"},{"q":"Current Databricks CDC API name? Old name?","a":"AUTO CDC (and AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT); older name APPLY CHANGES.","section":"ch02-s07"},{"q":"Schema enforcement?","a":"Validate on write and reject data that breaks the schema contract (Delta does this by default).","section":"ch02-s08"},{"q":"Schema evolution?","a":"A controlled, explicit change of the schema — e.g. add column, rename/drop, type widening.","section":"ch02-s09"},{"q":"Enforcement vs evolution — opposites?","a":"No: enforcement + explicit evolution policy.","section":"ch02-s09"},{"q":"Schema mismatch decision tree?","a":"Source changed? yes → intentional? yes evolve carefully / no source bug; no → parsing/type-inference bug.","section":"ch02-s09"},{"q":"Worst reaction to a schema change?","a":"Blindly enabling every schema evolution everywhere.","section":"ch02-s09"},{"q":"Bronze purpose?","a":"Keep source data as faithfully as possible — for audit, reprocessing, debugging, rebuilds.","section":"ch02-s10"},{"q":"Silver purpose?","a":"Cleaned, validated, deduplicated, CDC-applied, reusable data.","section":"ch02-s10"},{"q":"Gold purpose?","a":"Business-shaped aggregates/models for specific consumers (daily_sales, lifetime value).","section":"ch02-s10"},{"q":"What distinguishes the medallion layers?","a":"Semantics / quality / purpose — not file format (all can be Delta).","section":"ch02-s11"},{"q":"Typical Lakeflow types per layer?","a":"Bronze: streaming tables · Silver: streaming tables or MVs · Gold: materialized views.","section":"ch02-s11"},{"q":"Malformed records go where?","a":"Usually Bronze or a quarantine layer — never silently dropped.","section":"ch02-s11"},{"q":"Plain spark.read over the whole folder every day — incremental?","a":"No: it re-reads every file each run (duplicates on append). Use COPY INTO or Auto Loader for file-level incremental loads.","section":"ch02-s04","src":"part2"},{"q":"checkpointLocation vs cloudFiles.schemaLocation?","a":"Checkpoint = how far the stream got (processed files/state). schemaLocation = what shape the data has (schema + history).","section":"ch02-s05","src":"part2"},{"q":"Trigger 5 s, micro-batch needs 12 s — what happens?","a":"Batches stay sequential → backlog builds, latency grows (ingest > capacity).","section":"ch02-s03","src":"part2"},{"q":"Checkpoint folders?","a":"offsets/, commits/, state/ (stateful), metadata — the query's identity and memory.","section":"ch02-s05","src":"part2"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch02-e001","type":"match","section":"ch02-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each Round-1 component to its role.","pairs":[["Spark","compute"],["Parquet","file format"],["Delta Lake","transactional table layer"],["Unity Catalog","metadata + governance"],["Cloud storage","durable storage"]],"explain":"These five roles are the storage model. Keep them separate: Spark computes but stores nothing permanently; Parquet is just a file layout; Delta adds transactions on top of Parquet; Unity Catalog governs names and permissions; cloud storage holds the bytes."},{"id":"ch02-e002","type":"order","section":"ch02-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Put the Round-2 concept chain in the order the tutor builds it.","items":["Data source","ETL / ELT","Batch, streaming, incremental processing","CDC","Schema enforcement / evolution","Bronze → Silver → Gold","Identities / permissions","Dev → test → prod and CI/CD"],"explain":"Data first has to be moved (ETL/ELT), then you decide how often and how much to process, how to capture changes, how to protect the structure, how to organise quality layers — and only then who may access it and how code is shipped safely."},{"id":"ch02-e003","type":"odd","section":"ch02-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT part of the storage layer of a lakehouse?","options":["Parquet","Delta Lake","Cloud storage","Spark"],"answer":3,"explain":"Spark is the compute engine; it reads and writes data but is not where data lives. Parquet (file format), Delta Lake (table layer over files) and cloud storage (the bytes) all belong to the storage side."},{"id":"ch02-e004","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Delta Lake and Parquet are two names for the same thing.","answer":false,"explain":"Parquet is a columnar *file format*. Delta Lake is a *transactional table layer* built on Parquet files plus a transaction log. Delta ≠ Parquet is one of the Round-1 must-know distinctions."},{"id":"ch02-e005","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which component answers the question 'who may read `company.finance.payroll`?'","options":["Spark","Parquet","Unity Catalog","Cloud storage"],"answer":2,"why":["Spark executes queries; it does not decide permissions.","Parquet is a file format with no permission model.","Correct: governance is Unity Catalog's job.","Storage holds files; table-level access is governed above it."],"explain":"Unity Catalog is the metadata + governance layer: names (catalog.schema.object) and permissions live there. Spark only executes, Parquet only stores, and cloud storage only holds bytes."},{"id":"ch02-e006","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"An API returns JSON every night. You must never lose the original response, be able to change transformation rules later, and rebuild downstream tables. Which flow fits best?","options":["Extract → Transform → Load","Extract → Load raw → Transform","Transform → Extract → Load","Load → Extract → Transform"],"answer":1,"why":["Only the transformed result is stored, so the original is lost.","Correct: raw is kept immutable and is the rebuild source.","You cannot transform data you haven't extracted yet.","You cannot load data you haven't extracted."],"explain":"Keeping the untouched response first (load raw) and transforming afterwards satisfies all three needs: the original is preserved, rules can change, and downstream tables can be rebuilt from raw. With classic ETL only the transformed output survives."},{"id":"ch02-e007","type":"cloze","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Fill in the letter order.","text":"ETL = Extract → [[Transform]] → [[Load]]\nELT = Extract → [[Load]] → [[Transform]]","bank":["Validate","Join"],"explain":"Both start with Extract. ETL transforms on the way (e.g. on an ETL server) and loads only the result; ELT loads raw data into the platform and transforms it there."},{"id":"ch02-e008","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Sort each statement into ETL or ELT.","buckets":["ETL","ELT"],"items":[{"text":"Transformation happens on a separate ETL server before the warehouse","bucket":0},{"text":"Raw source data lands in the lakehouse first","bucket":1},{"text":"Only the cleaned result is stored","bucket":0},{"text":"Transformations run later with Spark / SQL inside the platform","bucket":1},{"text":"Lets you rebuild clean tables after fixing a rule","bucket":1},{"text":"Traditional warehouse pattern","bucket":0}],"explain":"ETL = transform before load (classic warehouses, ETL servers, only clean output). ELT = load raw first and transform with the platform's distributed compute, which naturally keeps raw data for rebuilds."},{"id":"ch02-e009","type":"match","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each raw value to the transformation it needs.","pairs":[["\" Athens \"","Trim spaces and upper-case → \"ATHENS\""],["\"19.95\"","Cast to DECIMAL(10,2)"],["NULL age","Validation / quarantine"],["customer_id + orders","JOIN"]],"explain":"These are the four typical Transform examples: string cleaning, type casting, validation (bad rows are not silently accepted — they are rejected or quarantined) and combining datasets with joins."},{"id":"ch02-e010","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Because Databricks calls its pipelines 'ETL', the transformation in a Databricks pipeline always happens before the data is stored.","answer":false,"explain":"Databricks uses 'ETL' loosely for any data-engineering pipeline. Many Databricks pipelines are physically ELT-like: raw data lands in Bronze first and is transformed later. Exams test understanding of where the transformation happens, not the label."},{"id":"ch02-e011","type":"scenario","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"You loaded `amount` as EUR into your clean `orders` table for 6 months. Today finance says the source always sent **USD**.","steps":[{"prompt":"Your pipeline was ETL-only (no raw copy). What is your realistic situation?","options":[{"text":"Re-request the historical data from the source system — and hope it still has it","ok":true,"fb":"Right. Without a raw copy, the only way to recompute is to go back to the source, which may no longer keep 6 months of history."},{"text":"Run `RESTORE` on the clean table","ok":false,"fb":"Restoring an older version still gives you EUR-labelled values; the bug was in the transformation itself."},{"text":"Nothing to do, DECIMAL values are correct","ok":false,"fb":"The numbers were interpreted with the wrong currency; downstream totals are wrong."}]},{"prompt":"Now imagine the same pipeline as ELT with a Bronze copy of every raw file. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Fix the conversion rule and rebuild the clean table from Bronze","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly the ELT benefit: raw is still there, so you just reprocess with the corrected rule."},{"text":"Delete Bronze to save storage, then fix the rule","ok":false,"fb":"Bronze is the rebuild source — deleting it destroys your recovery path."},{"text":"Ask every customer to re-place their orders","ok":false,"fb":"Not needed: the raw truth is already stored in Bronze."}]}],"explain":"Keeping raw data turns a 'call the source and pray' situation into a simple reprocess."},{"id":"ch02-e012","type":"free","section":"ch02-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In 3–4 sentences: why is ELT such a natural fit for a lakehouse like Databricks?","model":"A lakehouse can store huge volumes of raw data cheaply in cloud storage and bring distributed compute (Spark) only when needed. So it is cheap to land everything exactly as it came from the source first and transform later inside the platform. Keeping the raw copy means you can change transformation rules and rebuild downstream tables without asking the source again. That raw landing zone is what we call Bronze.","rubric":["cheap storage of large raw data","distributed compute on demand","keep exactly what came from source","rebuild / change rules later without re-asking the source","links to Bronze"],"explain":"The core argument is economic + recoverability: cheap storage + elastic compute make 'load raw, transform later' affordable, and the raw copy makes every downstream table reproducible."},{"id":"ch02-e013","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"In Spark Structured Streaming, each incoming event is immediately processed by its own Spark task.","answer":false,"explain":"Structured Streaming typically collects incoming records into micro-batches; each micro-batch becomes a Spark job with stages and tasks over partitions. 'One event → one task' is the classic misconception."},{"id":"ch02-e014","type":"calc","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Five events arrive (1, 2, 3, 4, 5) and the engine groups them two at a time, exactly as in the lesson's example. How many micro-batches run?","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"micro-batches","explain":"[1 2], [3 4], [5] → 3 micro-batches. The last batch can be smaller; micro-batches are not required to be full. Each one is a separate distributed Spark execution."},{"id":"ch02-e015","type":"free","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q2: Why does 'streaming' not necessarily mean 'one event → one Spark task immediately'?","model":"Because engines like Spark Structured Streaming collect incoming records into micro-batches and execute each micro-batch through the normal Spark execution model (job → stages → tasks over partitions, possibly with a shuffle). So many events are processed together, in parallel, rather than each event triggering its own task.","rubric":["micro-batches","many records grouped together","normal Spark execution model (job/stages/tasks/partitions)"],"explain":"Official answer: engines like Spark Structured Streaming can gather incoming records into micro-batches and run them through the usual Spark execution model."},{"id":"ch02-e016","type":"order","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the execution chain of ONE micro-batch.","items":["micro-batch","Spark job","stages","tasks","partitions processed (possible shuffle)"],"explain":"A micro-batch is submitted as a Spark job, the job is split into stages (at shuffle boundaries), each stage into tasks, and each task processes one partition. Streaming reuses the whole batch execution model."},{"id":"ch02-e017","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Bounded (batch-style) or unbounded (streaming-style) input?","buckets":["Bounded","Unbounded"],"items":[{"text":"orders.csv, 1 GB, 1,000,000 rows","bucket":0},{"text":"Kafka topic receiving orders all day","bucket":1},{"text":"Last month's exported invoices","bucket":0},{"text":"Click events from the web shop, continuously","bucket":1},{"text":"A one-off historical backfill file","bucket":0},{"text":"Payment events from a message queue","bucket":1}],"explain":"Ask: does this input have a natural END? A file or export does; a topic or queue that keeps receiving events does not."},{"id":"ch02-e018","type":"spotbug","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A teammate wrote these notes on streaming. Click the WRONG lines.","lines":["1. Streaming input is logically unbounded: there is no natural END OF FILE.","2. In Structured Streaming each event becomes its own Spark task immediately.","3. Structured Streaming often groups records into micro-batches.","4. Streaming replaces Spark's jobs/stages/tasks model with a different engine.","5. You often write streaming transformations almost like batch DataFrames."],"bugs":[1,3],"fix":"2. Records are typically grouped into micro-batches, and each micro-batch runs as a Spark job.\n4. Streaming builds ON the Spark execution model: each micro-batch → job → stages → tasks → partitions.","explain":"Lines 2 and 4 are the two classic myths. Micro-batches are processed by normal distributed Spark execution, so streaming builds on (doesn't replace) jobs, stages and tasks."},{"id":"ch02-e019","type":"write","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the PySpark batch job from the lesson: read `/data/orders.csv`, count orders per `country`, and save the result as table `sales_per_country`.","solution":"df = spark.read.csv(\"/data/orders.csv\")\nresult = df.groupBy(\"country\").count()\nresult.write.saveAsTable(\"sales_per_country\")","keywords":["spark.read.csv","groupBy","count()","saveAsTable"],"lang":"python","explain":"This is a bounded job: it reads all of the file, aggregates, writes, and stops. (In practice you'd add `header=True` so `country` is a real column name — the lesson keeps it minimal.)"},{"id":"ch02-e020","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"1 TB historical orders. Every night 10 MB of new orders arrive. A job at 03:00 processes only those 10 MB. What is it?","options":["Streaming only","Batch only","Batch + incremental","Streaming + full refresh"],"answer":2,"why":["Nothing runs continuously; it's a scheduled run.","True but incomplete: it also processes only new data.","Correct: scheduled + only-new-data.","It neither streams nor refreshes the full 1 TB."],"explain":"It runs once per night on a finite chunk (batch) and processes only the new 10 MB (incremental). 'Batch only' misses the incremental part; nothing here runs continuously, so it isn't streaming."},{"id":"ch02-e021","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q1: A job runs at 02:00 once a day and reads only the rows created since the previous run. Select ALL that apply.","options":["batch","streaming","incremental"],"answer":[0,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Once-a-day scheduled execution over a finite input = batch; reading only rows since the previous run = incremental. It is not streaming because it does not keep running as data arrives."},{"id":"ch02-e022","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A pipeline that runs only once a day cannot be incremental.","answer":false,"explain":"Frequency and amount are independent. A once-a-day batch job that filters `WHERE transaction_date = CURRENT_DATE - 1` is batch AND incremental."},{"id":"ch02-e023","type":"calc","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Using the lesson's numbers (Day 1: 100 GB, Day 2: +2 GB, Day 3: +3 GB): how many GB in total does the NAIVE pipeline process over the three days, minus what the INCREMENTAL pipeline processes? (Saved GB)","answer":202,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB","explain":"Naive: 100 + 102 + 105 = 307 GB. Incremental: 100 + 2 + 3 = 105 GB. Saved: 307 − 105 = 202 GB — and the gap keeps growing every day."},{"id":"ch02-e024","type":"calc","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q3 numbers: a 5 TB table where only 20 MB changes per day. Roughly how many times more data does a daily full reload read than an incremental load? (Use 1 TB = 1,000,000 MB.)","answer":250000,"tolerance":1000,"unit":"×","explain":"5 TB = 5,000,000 MB; 5,000,000 / 20 = 250,000×. You'd reprocess a huge historical dataset even though only a tiny subset changed."},{"id":"ch02-e025","type":"free","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q3: You have a 5 TB table and 20 MB changes per day. Why is it probably a bad idea to reload the whole table daily?","model":"Because you would reprocess (and pay compute and time for) a huge historical dataset even though only a tiny subset changed. Incremental processing handles only the new or changed data — previous state + changes = new state — so it is far cheaper and faster.","rubric":["reprocesses huge historical data","only a tiny subset changed","incremental processes only changes","cost / time"],"explain":"Official answer: you reprocess an enormous historical dataset although only a minimal subset changed; incremental processing can process just the changes."},{"id":"ch02-e026","type":"free","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q30 (interview): Explain the difference between batch, streaming and incremental processing WITHOUT buzzwords.","model":"Batch: I process a finite set of data in one run and then stop. Streaming: the input keeps arriving and the computation keeps updating as it arrives. Incremental: instead of recomputing the whole history, I process only what is new or changed. So one workload can be batch and incremental at the same time.","rubric":["batch = finite set, one run","streaming = input keeps arriving, result keeps updating","incremental = only new/changed data, not full history","batch and incremental can coexist"],"explain":"The key insight interviewers look for is the last sentence: incremental is an independent axis, so a nightly job can be both batch and incremental."},{"id":"ch02-e027","type":"cloze","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the nightly incremental batch query.","text":"SELECT *\nFROM transactions\nWHERE [[transaction_date]] = [[CURRENT_DATE]] - 1;","bank":["created_by","NOW()","MAX_DATE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Filtering on yesterday's date makes a once-a-day batch job read only new rows — incremental. Without the WHERE clause the same job would be a full recompute."},{"id":"ch02-e028","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Batch? Streaming? Classify the TIMING axis only.","buckets":["Batch (runs and stops)","Streaming (keeps running as data arrives)"],"items":[{"text":"Nightly 02:00 job reading yesterday's rows","bucket":0},{"text":"Query that continuously updates as Kafka events arrive","bucket":1},{"text":"Monthly full rebuild of a report table","bucket":0},{"text":"Pipeline that resumes after checkpoint offset 100000 and keeps consuming","bucket":1},{"text":"03:00 job that processes the 10 MB of new orders","bucket":0}],"explain":"Timing axis: does it run and stop (batch) or keep consuming as data arrives (streaming)? Whether it's incremental is a separate question — e.g. the 02:00 and 03:00 jobs are batch AND incremental."},{"id":"ch02-e029","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"A stream processed events 1–900 and stored `900` in its checkpoint, then crashed. After restart with the same checkpoint, what happens?","options":["It starts again from event 1","It continues from event 901","It skips to the newest event only","It fails because state is lost"],"answer":1,"why":["That's what happens WITHOUT (or with a reset) checkpoint.","Correct.","That would silently skip events 901..newest-1.","State is exactly what the checkpoint preserved."],"explain":"The checkpoint records committed progress, so the engine resumes right after it: 901, 902, … Starting at 1 would reprocess (duplicates); jumping to the newest would skip data."},{"id":"ch02-e030","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"One role of a streaming checkpoint is to remember how far the stream has already processed the source (e.g. up to offset 900).","answer":true,"explain":"Yes. Conceptually the checkpoint says 'processed until offset 900', which tells the engine to continue from 901 after a restart."},{"id":"ch02-e031","type":"cloze","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the restart logic.","text":"Checkpoint says: processed until offset [[900]]. After the restart the stream continues from offset [[901]]. Without a checkpoint the engine can't easily know whether to start from [[1]] or continue.","explain":"The checkpoint stores committed progress. Resume point = last committed offset + 1."},{"id":"ch02-e032","type":"order","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the crash-recovery story of a stream with a checkpoint.","items":["Process events 1–900","Record progress '900' in the checkpoint","Server crashes","Stream restarts with the same checkpoint location","Engine reads checkpoint → resume at 901"],"explain":"Progress must be recorded BEFORE the crash for recovery to work; on restart the engine reads that stored progress and continues right after it."},{"id":"ch02-e033","type":"scenario","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After an overnight incident, your streaming Bronze table suddenly contains every event from the last month **twice**. The source itself has no duplicates.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you ask yourself first?","options":[{"text":"Did the stream restart with the same checkpoint, or was the checkpoint deleted/reset/moved?","ok":true,"fb":"Right: a reset checkpoint makes the engine forget its progress and re-read old offsets."},{"text":"Is the Spark UI showing a big shuffle?","ok":false,"fb":"Shuffles explain performance, not replayed data."},{"text":"Should I enable schema evolution?","ok":false,"fb":"Schema has nothing to do with replayed events."}]},{"prompt":"You find the job was redeployed with a new checkpoint path. What is the mechanism?","options":[{"text":"The new, empty checkpoint had no progress, so the stream started reading old data again","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly — with no stored offset the engine re-ingested data it had already written."},{"text":"Delta Lake duplicated the files during OPTIMIZE","ok":false,"fb":"Compaction rewrites files but doesn't duplicate rows logically."},{"text":"Kafka sent the events twice","ok":false,"fb":"You verified the source has no duplicates."}]}],"explain":"Checkpoint reset is one of the seven duplicate causes listed in Chapter 3. The checkpoint location is part of the stream's identity — changing it is like starting a brand-new stream."},{"id":"ch02-e034","type":"odd","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT something the checkpoint helps with?","options":["Knowing where to resume after a crash","Avoiding re-reading events already processed","Fault-tolerant streaming","Converting amount strings to DECIMAL"],"answer":3,"explain":"Checkpoints are about progress/state and fault tolerance. Type conversion is a transformation rule (Silver logic), unrelated to where the stream resumes."},{"id":"ch02-e035","type":"free","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q4: What problem does CDC solve?","model":"CDC carries inserts, updates and deletes from a source system to the target without needing a full snapshot/reload every time. Instead of re-reading e.g. a 2-billion-row table every hour, only the changes are sent and applied.","rubric":["transfers inserts/updates/deletes","no full snapshot / reload each time","only changes are moved"],"explain":"Official answer: it transfers inserts/updates/deletes from the source system without needing a full snapshot/reload every time."},{"id":"ch02-e036","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall","calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q5: `seq=100 UPDATE balance=50` and `seq=101 UPDATE balance=80` — but event 101 arrives BEFORE event 100. If you apply arrival order, what final balance do you get, and what's correct?","options":["Final 80; correct 80","Final 50; correct 80","Final 80; correct 50","Final 50; correct 50"],"answer":1,"why":["Arrival order ends with event 100, not 101.","Correct: late older event overwrote the newer truth.","Seq 101 is newer, so 80 is correct.","50 is the older value."],"explain":"Arrival order applies 101 (80) and then 100 (50), so you end at 50. The source's latest change is seq 101 → 80. Arrival order ≠ source modification order; you need sequence/version/event ordering."},{"id":"ch02-e037","type":"calc","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Apply this CDC stream to an empty current-state table: I(1 Maria Athens, seq 10), I(2 John Patras, seq 11), U(1 Maria Volos, seq 12), D(2, seq 13). How many rows remain?","answer":1,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"After seq 10–11 there are 2 rows; seq 12 updates Maria to Volos; seq 13 deletes John. Result: 1 row — Maria | Volos."},{"id":"ch02-e038","type":"match","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each CDC element to its meaning.","pairs":[["I","INSERT"],["U","UPDATE"],["D","DELETE"],["seq","order of the changes at the source"],["id (primary/business key)","which entity the change applies to"]],"explain":"A CDC event = operation + key + new values + ordering. Without the key you don't know which row to change; without seq you don't know which change is newest."},{"id":"ch02-e039","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"CDC means re-reading the full source table on a schedule and comparing it with the target.","answer":false,"explain":"Re-reading the whole table every hour is the expensive 'Solution 1'. CDC is 'Solution 2': the source emits only the changes (I/U/D) and the target applies them."},{"id":"ch02-e040","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Updates `seq 17: city=Volos` and `seq 18: city=Larisa` arrive in the order 18, 17. You use 'last event received wins'. Final city?","options":["Larisa","Volos","NULL","Athens"],"answer":1,"explain":"Last received is seq 17 (Volos), so you wrongly end up with Volos although the latest real state is Larisa. Ordering by seq fixes it."},{"id":"ch02-e041","type":"scenario","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q27: A customer was deleted from PostgreSQL. In Silver the customer still exists — forever.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which category of problem do you suspect?","options":[{"text":"CDC / delete handling","ok":true,"fb":"Right. Inserts and updates flow, but the delete is lost somewhere between capture and apply."},{"text":"Schema evolution","ok":false,"fb":"A schema change would usually fail writes or add columns, not keep a specific row alive."},{"text":"Checkpoint reset","ok":false,"fb":"A reset causes replays/duplicates, not a missing delete."}]},{"prompt":"Where do you look first?","options":[{"text":"Bronze: does the raw CDC feed contain a D event for this customer?","ok":true,"fb":"Good — this splits the problem in two: not captured vs captured-but-not-applied."},{"text":"The Gold dashboard","ok":false,"fb":"Gold is downstream; first prove where the delete disappears."},{"text":"Cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"Compute size doesn't drop events."}]},{"prompt":"The D event IS in Bronze. What's the likely bug now?","options":[{"text":"The Silver apply logic ignores DELETE operations (or matches on the wrong key)","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly — the event was captured but not applied correctly."},{"text":"PostgreSQL never emitted it","ok":false,"fb":"You just saw it in Bronze, so capture worked."},{"text":"Bronze should not contain deletes","ok":false,"fb":"Bronze keeps raw source behaviour, including deletes."}]}],"explain":"Official answer: CDC/delete handling — the delete event is probably not captured or not applied correctly."},{"id":"ch02-e042","type":"order","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the steps to apply a CDC feed correctly to a current-state table.","items":["Read the new change events (I/U/D)","Identify each event's entity by its primary/business key","Order events per key by the source sequence (not arrival)","Apply INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE in that order","Result: the latest correct state per key"],"explain":"Key + ordering are the two things CDC needs. Ordering by arrival or forgetting deletes are the two most common bugs."},{"id":"ch02-e043","type":"order","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A deleted customer is still in Silver. Order your investigation.","items":["Confirm the customer is really deleted in the source (PostgreSQL)","Check Bronze: is there a D event for that key?","If no D event → capture problem (deletes not collected)","If D event exists → check the Silver apply logic handles deletes","Check the key used to match the delete is the real business key"],"explain":"Walk the pipeline in order and find the first point where reality diverges: source → captured in Bronze? → applied in Silver? The key check catches deletes that 'apply' to no row."},{"id":"ch02-e044","type":"spotbug","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"This pseudo-code applies CDC events to `silver.customers`. Click the buggy lines.","lines":["events = read_new_cdc_events()","events = sort(events, by='arrival_time')","for e in events:","    if e.op == 'I': insert(key=e.id, row=e)","    if e.op == 'U': update(key=e.id, row=e)","    if e.op == 'D': pass   # ignore deletes"],"bugs":[1,5],"fix":"events = sort(events, by='seq')          # source order, not arrival\n...\n    if e.op == 'D': delete(key=e.id)","explain":"Sorting by arrival time reproduces the Volos/Larisa bug; ignoring D events produces the 'deleted at source, alive forever in Silver' bug. Both are textbook CDC errors."},{"id":"ch02-e045","type":"write","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the SQL that Maria's move produces in the operational PostgreSQL table (customer id 1 moves to Volos).","solution":"UPDATE customers\nSET city = 'Volos'\nWHERE id = 1;","keywords":["update customers","set city","'volos'","where id = 1"],"lang":"sql","explain":"This single UPDATE is what CDC captures as `U | 1 | Maria | Volos | seq`. Without CDC the lakehouse would only notice it by re-reading the whole table."},{"id":"ch02-e046","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q6: You only need to know a customer's CURRENT address. SCD1 or SCD2?","options":["SCD Type 1","SCD Type 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the current state matters, so overwriting in place (Type 1) is enough. Type 2 would store history you don't need."},{"id":"ch02-e047","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q7: You need to know where a customer lived in 2019, 2022 and 2025. SCD1 or SCD2?","options":["SCD Type 1","SCD Type 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Point-in-time questions need history with validity ranges — Type 2. Type 1 overwrote 2019 and 2022 long ago."},{"id":"ch02-e048","type":"cloze","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Events: seq 20 INSERT id=4 salary=1000; seq 21 UPDATE salary=1300; seq 22 UPDATE salary=1500.","text":"SCD1 keeps only id=4 salary=[[1500]]. SCD2 keeps [[3]] versions for id=4, and the current one has salary [[1500]].","explain":"Type 1 overwrites, so only the latest value survives. Type 2 keeps one row per version (1000, 1300, 1500), with the latest marked current (open valid_to)."},{"id":"ch02-e049","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Using the SCD2 table (Athens 2024-01-01 → 2026-08-10, Volos 2026-08-10 → NULL), where did Maria live on 1 July 2025?","options":["Athens","Volos","Unknown — SCD2 only stores the current city","Both"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 July 2025 lies inside the Athens validity range (2024-01-01 to 2026-08-10). This kind of question is exactly what Type 2 exists for."},{"id":"ch02-e050","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"APPLY CHANGES and AUTO CDC are completely different features, so old exam questions about APPLY CHANGES no longer apply.","answer":false,"explain":"APPLY CHANGES is the older API name for the same CDC idea; the current docs recommend AUTO CDC (and AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT). Old courses/exams may still use the old terminology."},{"id":"ch02-e051","type":"match","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the term to its description.","pairs":[["SCD Type 1","Overwrite: keep only the current state"],["SCD Type 2","Keep a version history with validity ranges"],["AUTO CDC","Current Lakeflow API for applying CDC (SCD1/SCD2)"],["APPLY CHANGES","Older name of the CDC API, still found in old material"],["AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT","CDC variant that works from successive source snapshots"]],"explain":"AUTO CDC is the recommended way in Lakeflow pipelines; APPLY CHANGES is legacy naming; both can produce SCD1 or SCD2 tables."},{"id":"ch02-e052","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"SCD Type 1 or Type 2?","buckets":["SCD Type 1","SCD Type 2"],"items":[{"text":"Shipping label needs the customer's current address","bucket":0},{"text":"Auditor asks what salary employee 4 had last March","bucket":1},{"text":"Report of revenue by the region the customer lived in AT ORDER TIME","bucket":1},{"text":"Fix a typo in a product name everywhere","bucket":0},{"text":"Track every price change of a product for analysis","bucket":1}],"explain":"Need only 'now' → Type 1. Need 'what was true at time X' → Type 2."},{"id":"ch02-e053","type":"odd","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one does NOT belong to SCD Type 2?","options":["valid_from column","valid_to column","one row per version","overwrite the old value in place"],"answer":3,"explain":"Overwriting in place is the definition of Type 1. Type 2 closes the old row (valid_to) and inserts a new version (valid_from)."},{"id":"ch02-e054","type":"calc","section":"ch02-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"A customer moves 4 times after the initial insert. How many rows does an SCD Type 2 table hold for that customer (one row per version)?","answer":5,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"Initial version + 4 changes = 5 versions, exactly one of them current (open valid_to). An SCD Type 1 table would still hold 1 row."},{"id":"ch02-e055","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Target Delta column `amount DECIMAL`. A producer writes a batch where `amount` is the STRING `\"unknown\"`. No schema evolution is configured. What happens?","options":["The write is rejected with a schema error","Delta silently converts it to NULL","Delta changes the column type to STRING","The row is written and fixed later by OPTIMIZE"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: enforcement on write.","No silent coercion — that would hide the bug.","Type changes don't happen implicitly.","OPTIMIZE compacts files; it never fixes data."],"explain":"Delta validates the schema on write and rejects incompatible data. It does not silently coerce values or change column types unless an evolution path is explicitly enabled — and even then, changing DECIMAL to STRING is not an automatic evolution."},{"id":"ch02-e056","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"By default, writing a DataFrame with an extra column `unknown_column` into a Delta table just ignores the extra column.","answer":false,"explain":"Schema enforcement rejects the write because the incoming schema doesn't match the table contract. Adding columns requires explicit, controlled schema evolution."},{"id":"ch02-e057","type":"order","section":"ch02-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the damage chain when bad data is accepted without enforcement.","items":["Upstream schema bug","Silver corrupted","Gold wrong","Dashboard broken"],"explain":"Each layer consumes the previous one, so an unchecked bug propagates. Enforcement cuts the chain at the first step."},{"id":"ch02-e058","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Without schema evolution enabled: does a Delta write into `orders(order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL, country STRING)` pass or get rejected?","buckets":["Passes","Rejected"],"items":[{"text":"A batch with exactly those four columns and valid values","bucket":0},{"text":"amount = {\"foo\": 12}","bucket":1},{"text":"An extra column `unknown_column`","bucket":1},{"text":"amount arriving as the string \"unknown\"","bucket":1},{"text":"New orders with the correct types for all columns","bucket":0}],"explain":"Enforcement accepts data that fits the contract and rejects incompatible types or unexpected columns until you explicitly allow an evolution."},{"id":"ch02-e059","type":"odd","section":"ch02-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A schema is defined by…","options":["column names","column types","the contract consumers rely on","the number of files in the table"],"answer":3,"explain":"Schema = names + types (the contract). File count is a physical storage detail, unrelated to the schema."},{"id":"ch02-e060","type":"free","section":"ch02-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain to a junior why schema enforcement is necessary, using a Power BI dashboard example.","model":"The dashboard expects amount as DECIMAL. If an upstream producer suddenly sends amount as the string 'unknown' and we accept anything, the bug corrupts Silver, then Gold aggregates are wrong, and finally the dashboard breaks or shows wrong numbers. Enforcement rejects the incompatible write at the first table, so the problem is caught early and close to its cause.","rubric":["contract: dashboard expects DECIMAL","upstream sends STRING 'unknown'","propagates Silver → Gold → dashboard","enforcement stops it early"],"explain":"Good answers name the propagation chain and the benefit of failing early at the boundary."},{"id":"ch02-e061","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q28: The source schema changed. Which is the WORST practice?","options":["A. Fail and investigate","B. Confirm the change is intentional, then apply evolution","C. Blindly enable every possible schema evolution everywhere"],"answer":2,"why":["Safe: you lose time, not correctness.","Best practice: enforce + controlled evolution.","Correct (worst): blind evolution hides bugs."],"explain":"C removes the safety net: unintended upstream bugs would be silently absorbed. A is safe (maybe slow); B is the ideal production behaviour."},{"id":"ch02-e062","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q9: When an upstream API adds a new field, that is necessarily an error.","answer":false,"explain":"Not necessarily — it may be completely legitimate schema evolution. Confirm it's intentional, then evolve in a controlled way."},{"id":"ch02-e063","type":"free","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q8: What is the difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution?","model":"Enforcement protects the existing schema contract by rejecting incompatible writes. Evolution allows a controlled change of that contract, such as adding a column. They are not opposites: in production you usually combine enforcement with an explicit evolution policy.","rubric":["enforcement protects existing contract / rejects incompatible writes","evolution = controlled change of the schema","not opposites — used together"],"explain":"Official answer: enforcement protects the existing schema contract; evolution allows a controlled change of that schema."},{"id":"ch02-e064","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Target: `customer_id BIGINT, age INT, country STRING`. Source now also sends `loyalty_points INT`. Best description?","options":["Data corruption — drop the source","Schema-evolution candidate (add column); confirm it's expected before accepting","Type widening","Must always be accepted automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adding a column is the classic evolution candidate, but 'candidate' is the key word: verify the change is intended before evolving the table."},{"id":"ch02-e065","type":"scenario","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Yesterday the pipeline worked. Today the write fails with `AnalysisException: schema mismatch`.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you do FIRST?","options":[{"text":"Check whether the source schema really changed","ok":true,"fb":"Right — the first branch of the decision tree."},{"text":"Enable schema evolution on all tables","ok":false,"fb":"That's the anti-pattern: it may silently accept a real bug."},{"text":"Restart the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"A schema mismatch is about data shape, not compute health."}]},{"prompt":"The source schema did NOT change. What do you suspect?","options":[{"text":"A parsing / type-inference bug on our side","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — e.g. inference read a column as STRING today because of an odd value."},{"text":"A source bug","ok":false,"fb":"The source didn't change, so the source isn't the culprit."},{"text":"Intentional evolution","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing evolved upstream."}]},{"prompt":"In a different incident the source DID change, but nobody upstream intended it. Conclusion?","options":[{"text":"It's a source bug — report/fix upstream, don't evolve","ok":true,"fb":"Correct: evolving would bake the bug into your contract."},{"text":"Evolve carefully","ok":false,"fb":"Only intentional changes should be evolved."},{"text":"Parsing bug","ok":false,"fb":"The source really changed, so it's not our parser."}]}],"explain":"Decision tree: changed? → intentional? → evolve carefully / source bug; not changed → our parsing/type-inference bug."},{"id":"ch02-e066","type":"order","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the schema-mismatch decision process for the INTENTIONAL-change path.","items":["See AnalysisException: schema mismatch","Check whether the source schema really changed","Confirm the change is intentional","Choose the specific evolution needed (e.g. add column)","Apply the evolution carefully, only where needed"],"explain":"Production thinking: prove what changed, prove it's intended, then evolve narrowly. Skipping straight to evolution is the classic mistake."},{"id":"ch02-e067","type":"match","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each decision-tree outcome to its conclusion.","pairs":[["Source changed + intentional","Evolve carefully"],["Source changed + NOT intentional","Source bug"],["Source did NOT change","Parsing / type-inference bug"]],"explain":"Only the first branch leads to schema evolution; the other two are bugs to fix, not changes to accept."},{"id":"ch02-e068","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Likely legitimate evolution (after confirming) or likely a bug?","buckets":["Likely evolution candidate","Likely bug"],"items":[{"text":"New `discount_code` column announced in the source release notes","bucket":0},{"text":"`amount` sometimes arrives as the string \"unknown\"","bucket":1},{"text":"New `loyalty_points INT` column after a planned loyalty launch","bucket":0},{"text":"`age` inferred as STRING today because one file had a stray header row","bucket":1},{"text":"A planned widening of `quantity` from INT to BIGINT","bucket":0}],"explain":"Intentional, announced changes are evolution candidates; random type changes or inference glitches are bugs — evolving them would corrupt the contract."},{"id":"ch02-e069","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Enforcement and evolution are mutually exclusive: a table either enforces its schema or allows evolution.","answer":false,"explain":"They work together: enforcement by default, plus an explicit policy that allows specific, controlled changes."},{"id":"ch02-e070","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q10: If Bronze contains duplicates, that is necessarily a bug.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Bronze may deliberately preserve raw source behaviour, duplicates included. Deduplication is a Silver responsibility."},{"id":"ch02-e071","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q12: A table with `customer_id, lifetime_value` per customer usually belongs to…","options":["Bronze","Silver","Gold"],"answer":2,"explain":"Lifetime value is a business metric computed for a specific use — Gold. Silver holds the clean customers/orders it is computed from."},{"id":"ch02-e072","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q13: A deduplicated, validated customer table usually belongs to…","options":["Bronze","Silver","Gold"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dedup + validation = Silver. It's clean and reusable but not yet shaped for one business question."},{"id":"ch02-e073","type":"free","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q11: Why keep Bronze instead of writing straight to Gold?","model":"For auditability, reprocessing, debugging and the ability to rebuild downstream layers when the transformation logic changes. If a rule was wrong (e.g. amount * 100), we fix the code and rebuild Silver/Gold from Bronze; without raw data we might have to ask the source again, and it may no longer have the data.","rubric":["auditability","reprocessing / rebuild downstream","debugging","transformation logic may change","source may no longer have the data"],"explain":"Official answer: auditability, reprocessing, debugging and the ability to recreate downstream layers when transformation logic changes."},{"id":"ch02-e074","type":"free","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q29 (interview): Explain the medallion architecture in 30 seconds.","model":"The medallion architecture organizes lakehouse data into progressively higher-quality layers: Bronze for raw, source-faithful data; Silver for validated, cleaned, reusable datasets; and Gold for business-oriented aggregates or models that serve specific consumers. It's a design pattern, not a requirement, and all three layers can be Delta tables.","rubric":["progressively increasing quality","Bronze = raw / source-faithful","Silver = validated, cleaned, reusable","Gold = business-oriented for specific consumers"],"explain":"A strong answer names all three layers by purpose (not by file format) and the idea of progressive quality."},{"id":"ch02-e075","type":"match","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each Bronze value to its Silver form.","pairs":[["\"id\": \"00192\"","192 as BIGINT"],["\"customer\": \"  MARIA \"","\"MARIA\" as STRING"],["\"amount\": \"25.98\"","25.98 as DECIMAL"]],"explain":"Silver parses and casts strings into proper types and cleans formatting (trimming). Bronze keeps the original strings so this can always be redone."},{"id":"ch02-e076","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The medallion architecture is a mandatory Databricks feature you must enable.","answer":false,"explain":"It's a widely recommended design pattern (progressively increasing quality), not a required feature or setting."},{"id":"ch02-e077","type":"scenario","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A bug in your Silver job multiplied every amount by 100 for the past week (`amount = amount * 100`). Gold revenue is 100× too high.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your recovery plan, given you have Bronze?","options":[{"text":"Fix the transformation code and rebuild Silver (and then Gold) from Bronze","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly what Bronze is for: raw → corrected code → rebuild."},{"text":"Divide Gold revenue by 100 by hand","ok":false,"fb":"Patching the symptom leaves Silver wrong and every other consumer still broken."},{"text":"Re-request a week of data from the source","ok":false,"fb":"Unnecessary — Bronze already holds the source-faithful data."}]},{"prompt":"Your colleague asks: what if we had NOT kept Bronze?","options":[{"text":"We would need the source to resend the data — and it may no longer have it","ok":true,"fb":"That's the main argument for keeping raw data."},{"text":"No difference, Silver can fix itself","ok":false,"fb":"Silver only contains the already-wrong values."},{"text":"Delta's transaction log would contain the raw JSON","ok":false,"fb":"The log tracks table versions of Silver, which were already wrong."}]}],"explain":"Bronze turns a transformation bug into a reprocessing job instead of a data-loss incident."},{"id":"ch02-e078","type":"order","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the recovery after a wrong Silver transformation rule.","items":["Notice wrong numbers in Gold","Trace the error to the Silver transformation rule","Fix the transformation code","Rebuild Silver from Bronze","Rebuild Gold from the corrected Silver"],"explain":"Fix the cause, then recompute downstream in dependency order: Bronze is the trusted starting point, Gold comes last."},{"id":"ch02-e079","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which layer typically does this work?","buckets":["Bronze","Silver","Gold"],"items":[{"text":"Land raw Kafka events exactly as received","bucket":0},{"text":"Deduplicate orders","bucket":1},{"text":"Apply CDC to build the current customer table","bucket":1},{"text":"Compute monthly revenue by product","bucket":2},{"text":"Cast amount strings to DECIMAL","bucket":1},{"text":"Customer lifetime value for marketing","bucket":2},{"text":"Keep duplicates and unexpected fields as they came","bucket":0}],"explain":"Bronze = keep raw; Silver = parse, cast, dedup, CDC, quality; Gold = business-shaped aggregates and metrics."},{"id":"ch02-e080","type":"bucket","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Where would you put each dataset? (the lesson's exercise)","buckets":["Bronze","Silver","Gold","Bronze / quarantine"],"items":[{"text":"A. Raw JSON exactly as received","bucket":0},{"text":"B. Orders with validated types and duplicates removed","bucket":1},{"text":"C. Monthly revenue by country","bucket":2},{"text":"D. Customer table after applying CDC","bucket":1},{"text":"E. Malformed records captured for investigation","bucket":3}],"explain":"A is source-faithful (Bronze); B and D are cleaned/CDC-applied reusable data (Silver); C is a business aggregate (Gold); E is usually kept in Bronze or a dedicated quarantine area — architecture-dependent, but never silently discarded."},{"id":"ch02-e081","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A correct medallion design uses CSV for Bronze, Parquet for Silver and a database for Gold.","answer":false,"explain":"All three layers can be Delta tables. The layers differ in semantics, quality and purpose — not necessarily in file format."},{"id":"ch02-e082","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"In current Lakeflow pipelines, which dataset type does Databricks typically recommend for Gold aggregations/metrics?","options":["Streaming tables only","Materialized views","Raw JSON files in a volume","Temporary views"],"answer":1,"explain":"The recommended pattern: Bronze often streaming tables, Silver streaming tables or materialized views depending on the transformation, Gold materialized views for aggregations/metrics."},{"id":"ch02-e083","type":"odd","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT what distinguishes Bronze, Silver and Gold?","options":["Semantics","Data quality","Purpose / consumer","File format"],"answer":3,"explain":"All layers can be Delta tables; the distinction is semantic/quality/purpose. File format is the misconception."},{"id":"ch02-e084","type":"match","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each transformation to the boundary where it usually happens.","pairs":[["Extract / ingest raw events","Source → Bronze"],["Validate, clean, apply CDC, evolve schema","Bronze → Silver"],["Model and aggregate for a business use","Silver → Gold"]],"explain":"Knowing which work happens at which boundary is also how you debug: if data is in Bronze but not in Silver, the Bronze → Silver work is your suspect."},{"id":"ch02-e085","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Your Silver validation finds 300 malformed order records. What is the right default behaviour?","options":["Drop them silently so Silver stays clean","Capture them in Bronze / a quarantine table for investigation","Write them to Gold with a warning flag","Stop all pipelines permanently"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bad records should be visible and investigable, not vanish. Where exactly they live depends on the architecture (Bronze or a quarantine layer), but silently discarding them hides data loss."},{"id":"ch02-e086","type":"order","section":"ch02-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the e-shop data flow.","items":["Sources: PostgreSQL, Kafka, JSON files","Bronze (raw)","Parse / validate, deduplicate, apply CDC, clean types","Silver: customers, orders, products","Aggregate / model","Gold"],"explain":"Raw lands first, cleaning/CDC produce Silver entities, and aggregation produces Gold. This is the same flow you'll build with Lakeflow pipelines later."},{"id":"ch02-e801","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A scheduled Auto Loader job using `.trigger(availableNow=True)` is an example of batch-like scheduling with incremental processing.","answer":true,"explain":"AvailableNow processes all new files available at start, then stops — run hourly from a Job it behaves like batch, but the checkpoint makes each run touch only new files. Batch vs streaming describes timing; incremental describes how much work is redone.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e802","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In an Auto Loader stream, which location remembers **which files have already been processed**?","options":["checkpointLocation","cloudFiles.schemaLocation","The target table's _delta_log only","/tmp on the driver"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: progress/state.","That's schema history, not progress.","Auto Loader's file progress is in the checkpoint.","Ephemeral — lost on termination."],"explain":"Progress lives in the streaming checkpoint (`checkpointLocation` on the write). `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` stores the inferred schema and its evolution — not progress. A `/tmp` path is ephemeral and would lose everything when compute terminates.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e803","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"With Auto Loader's default schema evolution (schema inferred, addNewColumns), a new column in a source file stops the stream; after a restart the column is part of the schema.","answer":true,"explain":"Stopping is a deliberate control point: the new schema is recorded in schemaLocation, and a restart (e.g. via Job retries) continues with it. It's 'enforcement + an explicit evolution policy', not silent mutation.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e804","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Auto Loader infers JSON/CSV columns as STRING by default. Why is that often a *good* fit for Bronze?","options":["It preserves odd values like \"unknown\" so Silver can validate and cast","Delta cannot store DOUBLE in Bronze","Strings make aggregations faster","Unity Catalog forbids typed Bronze tables"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Delta stores all common types.","Strings are usually slower for math.","No such rule."],"explain":"Bronze should preserve the source. If tomorrow `amount` arrives as \"unknown\", a STRING column keeps it; a DOUBLE column would fail or push it to rescued data. Silver then validates and casts. The other options are false claims.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e901","type":"tf","section":"ch02-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"If a micro-batch takes 12 s but the trigger is every 5 s, Spark runs the overdue micro-batches in parallel to keep up.","answer":false,"explain":"No. In the normal micro-batch model batches run **sequentially**: the next starts after the current one finishes, so a processing backlog builds and latency grows. That's a signal that ingestion rate > processing capacity.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e902","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"An hourly job uses `readStream` + `writeStream.trigger(availableNow=True)` with a checkpoint. How do you classify it?","options":["Always-on streaming, full recompute","Scheduled (batch-like) + incremental, using the streaming engine","Batch + full recompute","Real-time mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"It runs on a schedule and stops (batch-like execution) but touches only new input thanks to the checkpoint (incremental). AvailableNow = scheduled incremental processing with streaming semantics, not an always-on stream.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e903","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Certification trap: what does a Structured Streaming checkpoint store?","options":["Only the last processed timestamp","Offsets, commits, query metadata and state (where applicable)","A full copy of the sink table","The notebook code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Offsets/progress, commit information, query metadata and — for stateful queries — state. 'Just the last timestamp' is the classic wrong answer; that's why deleting it loses progress **and** state and turns the next run into a new query.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch02-e904","type":"mcq","section":"ch02-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A Silver Delta table receives MERGE updates. A downstream stream must see those row-level changes. What should it read?","options":["spark.readStream.table(\"silver.customers\")","spark.readStream.option(\"readChangeFeed\", \"true\").table(\"silver.customers\")","spark.readStream.option(\"skipChangeCommits\", \"true\").table(\"silver.customers\")","spark.read.table(\"silver.customers\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"A plain `readStream.table(...)` on Delta is append-oriented and fails (or, with skipChangeCommits, ignores) commits that change existing rows. The Change Data Feed exposes inserts/updates/deletes as a stream.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch03","num":3,"title":"Identity, Governance & Shipping Code","subtitle":"Principals, securables, privileges, dev→staging→prod, Git, CI/CD, IaC, Bundles — and the three classic debugging cases","emoji":"🔐","sourcePages":"52–76","mantra":"Know WHO is running, WHAT it may touch and HOW the code reached prod — then debug by finding the first layer where reality diverges from what you expected.","objectives":["You can separate authentication from authorization and name the three principal types.","You can read a GRANT statement word by word and list every privilege needed to SELECT from catalog.schema.table.","You can debug PERMISSION_DENIED in the right order — ending with 'which identity is ACTUALLY running?'.","You can explain least privilege and why ALL PRIVILEGES for everybody is a bad fix.","You can describe dev → test/staging → prod, Git flow, CI, CD and Infrastructure as Code (Declarative Automation Bundles, formerly Asset Bundles).","You can run the three classic debugging playbooks: no new data, duplicate records, works in dev / fails in prod.","You can map the whole platform: Lakeflow/Spark, Unity Catalog, Git/CI/CD, Bundles, Delta + cloud storage."],"sections":[{"id":"ch03-s01","title":"Authentication vs Authorization","hook":"'Who are you?' and 'What may you do?' are two different questions — and two different failure modes.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Authentication","points":["Question: **Who are you?**","`nikos@company.com` logs in","System proves: this really is Nikos"]},{"title":"Authorization","points":["Question: **What are you allowed to do?**","Checked after identity is known","E.g. SELECT yes, DELETE no"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["Action by Nikos","Allowed?"],"rows":[["SELECT customers","YES"],["DELETE customers","NO"],["CREATE TABLE","NO"]],"caption":"Authorization = a list of allowed actions for a known identity"},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Hotel analogy","text":"Reception checks your passport — **authentication**. Your key card then opens only your room and the gym, not the kitchen — **authorization**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Order matters","text":"Authorization only makes sense after authentication: first the system knows WHO, then it decides WHAT that identity may do."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Nikos logs in successfully but gets an error when running DELETE. Authentication or authorization problem?","text":"**Authorization.** Login worked, so his identity was proven; the system simply doesn't allow that identity to delete."}]},{"id":"ch03-s02","title":"Principals: User, Group, Service Principal","hook":"Every grant goes TO a principal — pick the right kind and half of your security problems disappear.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A **principal** is an identity to which permissions can be given. Unity Catalog grants privileges to principals on securable objects."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"User","def":"A human, e.g. `nikos@company.com`."},{"term":"Group","def":"A set of principals, e.g. `data_engineers` = Nikos, Maria, John."},{"term":"Service principal","def":"A machine identity for automation, e.g. `orders-etl-prod`."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- painful: one grant per person\nGRANT ... TO Nikos;\nGRANT ... TO Maria;\nGRANT ... TO John;\n\n-- better: grant to the group, manage membership\nGRANT ... TO `data_engineers`;","caption":"Grant to groups, not individuals"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Groups over individuals","text":"Databricks recommends managing access through **groups** instead of individual users wherever possible. Then onboarding/offboarding = changing group membership."},{"t":"p","text":"A production job runs **every night at 02:00**. Ideally it should **not** 'run as Nikos'. What if Nikos leaves the company and his account is disabled? The job breaks."},{"t":"diagram","text":"human       ──►  user identity       (nikos@company.com)\nautomation  ──►  service principal   (orders-etl-prod)","caption":"Humans use users; automation uses service principals"},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Why service principals for production?","text":"A production workload needs a **stable machine identity** independent of the lifecycle and permissions of one specific employee. We'll use this again with Jobs and CI/CD."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → principals in production","text":"New: provision principals at the **account level**, and give **production ownership and write access** to groups / service principals, never personal users — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch03-s03","title":"Securable Objects, Privileges & GRANT Grammar","hook":"Read GRANT like a sentence: give WHAT right, ON which object, TO whom.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A **securable object** is anything whose permissions you can control: catalog, schema, table, volume, function, …"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"catalog:  company\nschema:   company.finance\ntable:    company.finance.payroll","caption":"Securables at three levels"},{"t":"p","text":"A **privilege** is the action a principal is allowed to perform on a securable object."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT SELECT\nON TABLE company.finance.transactions\nTO `analysts`;"},{"t":"table","head":["Word","Meaning"],"rows":[["GRANT","give"],["SELECT","the read permission (the privilege)"],["ON TABLE …","on this securable object"],["TO analysts","to this principal (here a group)"]],"caption":"Parse it grammatically instead of memorizing it"},{"t":"p","text":"To SELECT from `company.finance.transactions` you conceptually walk the path **company → finance → transactions**, so permissions usually include the containers too:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG company TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA  company.finance TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT      ON TABLE   company.finance.transactions TO `analysts`;","caption":"The full path for a read"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"SELECT alone is not enough","text":"Having SELECT on a table but **not USE CATALOG** (or USE SCHEMA) can still produce a permission problem: access passes through the catalog/schema hierarchy."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Coming later","text":"Privilege inheritance and the exact Unity Catalog semantics come in the Unity Catalog chapter."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Securable object","def":"A governed object on which permissions can be granted (catalog, schema, table, volume, function…)."},{"term":"Privilege","def":"An action a principal may perform on a securable (SELECT, USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA…)."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → inheritance, REVOKE, ownership, MANAGE, BROWSE","text":"Grants on a schema/catalog **flow down** to children (including future tables), so a table-level `REVOKE` can leave access in place. Ownership, MANAGE, BROWSE and SHOW GRANTS are covered too — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch03-s04","title":"Debugging PERMISSION_DENIED","hook":"'Give me admin' is not debugging. Walk the path — and check WHO is really running.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *\nFROM prod.finance.transactions;\n\n-- Error: PERMISSION_DENIED"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Bad debugging","points":["\"Just give me admin\"","Fixes the symptom, breaks least privilege","You learn nothing about the cause"]},{"title":"Good debugging","points":["Walk the access path step by step","Check the identity that actually runs","Grant only the missing privilege"]}]},{"t":"flow","items":["Workspace access?","USE CATALOG prod?","USE SCHEMA finance?","SELECT on table?","Row filters / masks / policies?","Which identity is ACTUALLY running?"],"caption":"The permission-debugging chain"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The last question is the critical one","text":"**You** may have SELECT. But the Job may run as `orders-prod-service-principal` — and that identity may not."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when you see PERMISSION_DENIED","questions":["Can this principal access the workspace at all?","Does it have USE CATALOG on the catalog (prod)?","Does it have USE SCHEMA on the schema (finance)?","Does it have SELECT on the table itself?","Are row filters, column masks or other policies limiting it?","Which identity is ACTUALLY running this — me, or a job's service principal?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"'It works for me' proves nothing","text":"Your own successful query only proves YOUR identity has the privileges. Always check the run identity of the job."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → the 8-layer PERMISSION_DENIED algorithm","text":"The full algorithm puts **identity first** and adds three layers this checklist lacks: **workspace binding**, **external storage** (location / credential / cloud IAM) and **UC-compatible compute**. Mnemonic: *I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions* — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch03-s05","title":"Least Privilege","hook":"Give exactly what's needed — because every extra privilege is an extra way to break production.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Least privilege**: give only the permissions that are necessary."},{"t":"p","text":"If an analyst needs `SELECT`, you do **not** give `ALL PRIVILEGES` 'so they can work more easily'."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Powerful privileges","text":"Databricks specifically warns to use powerful privileges such as **ALL PRIVILEGES**, **MANAGE** and **ownership** sparingly."},{"t":"list","items":["Violates least privilege","Increases the **blast radius** (how much damage one mistake or compromised identity can do)","Risk of accidental or destructive actions","Security exposure"],"ordered":false},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Blast radius","def":"How much can be damaged if one identity makes a mistake or is compromised."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"'Give ALL PRIVILEGES to everybody'","text":"It makes the error disappear today and creates a much bigger incident tomorrow."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: an analyst needs to read gold.daily_sales. What exactly do they need?","text":"USE CATALOG on the catalog, USE SCHEMA on the schema, SELECT on the table — nothing more (no MODIFY, no ALL PRIVILEGES, no ownership)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → ALL PRIVILEGES is not everything","text":"`ALL PRIVILEGES` **excludes MANAGE, EXTERNAL USE LOCATION and EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA**, and it is **evaluated dynamically** (new privileges are added automatically) — one more reason for restraint — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch03-s06","title":"Dev → Test/Staging → Prod","hook":"Never debug on the CEO's dashboard. Environments let you break things safely.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You have `prod.gold.monthly_revenue` feeding the **CEO dashboard**. You want to try `.filter(col(\"country\") != \"GR\")` for debugging. Do you edit the production notebook and press **Run All**? **No.** You want **environment isolation**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"DEV\n │  code changes\n ▼\nTEST / STAGING\n │  validation\n ▼\nPROD"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"DEV","points":["Developer works here","May break things, experiment, debug","Sample data","e.g. `dev_nikos.silver.orders`"]},{"title":"TEST / STAGING","points":["Closer to production","Checks permissions, dependencies, configuration","Integration, data quality, deployment","No impact on real consumers"]},{"title":"PROD","points":["Real workloads run","Controlled deployments, stable configs","Monitoring, restricted permissions","Reproducibility"]}]},{"t":"flow","items":["Notebook in browser","edit production code manually","Run All"],"caption":"The big mistake — not a serious deployment model"},{"t":"ask","title":"Questions you CAN'T answer after a manual prod edit","questions":["Which version is running?","Who changed it?","When?","What changed?","How do I go back to the previous version?","Did this same code pass tests?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Manual production edits","text":"You lose reproducibility, controlled review/testing, reliable version history and repeatable deployment."}]},{"id":"ch03-s07","title":"Source Control with Git","hook":"The source of truth is the repository — not 'that notebook Nikos has in his workspace'.","blocks":[{"t":"flow","items":["developer","branch","commit","pull request","review","main"],"caption":"Typical Git flow"},{"t":"p","text":"The **source of truth** for code becomes the **Git repository** — not *\"that notebook Nikos has in the workspace\"*."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Branch","def":"An isolated line of work for one change."},{"term":"Commit","def":"A recorded snapshot of changes with author and time."},{"term":"Pull request (PR)","def":"A request to merge a branch, where the change is reviewed (and tested)."},{"term":"main","def":"The shared branch that represents accepted code."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"What Git gives you","text":"Version history, who/what/when for every change, review before merge, and an easy way back to previous versions — the answers to the 'manual edit' questions."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: which of the six 'manual edit' questions does Git answer?","text":"Which version, who, when, what changed and how to go back. 'Did it pass tests?' needs CI on top of Git."}]},{"id":"ch03-s08","title":"CI and CD","hook":"Machines check every change (CI) and ship validated changes the same way every time (CD).","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**CI — Continuous Integration.** You `git push`; the system automatically runs:"},{"t":"flow","items":["lint","unit tests","integration tests","configuration validation","build"],"caption":"A CI pipeline"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"CI gate","text":"If anything fails: **don't merge / don't deploy.**"},{"t":"p","text":"**CD** can mean **Continuous Delivery** or **Continuous Deployment** depending on the process. The core idea: validated artifact/code → automated, controlled deployment → target environment."},{"t":"flow","items":["main branch","tests","deploy staging","integration tests","deploy production"],"caption":"A CD pipeline"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"CI","points":["Automatic integration / validation / testing of code changes","Runs on push / PR","Output: pass/fail + build"]},{"title":"CD","points":["Controlled delivery/deployment of validated changes","To staging, then production","Output: deployed environment"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Two meanings of CD","text":"Continuous **Delivery** (ready to deploy, often with an approval) vs Continuous **Deployment** (deployed automatically). Either way: validated code → automated, controlled deployment."}]},{"id":"ch03-s09","title":"Infrastructure as Code & Declarative Automation Bundles","hook":"If a Job exists only as UI clicks, nobody can review it, version it or recreate it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Job needs: task A, task B, schedule, compute, parameters, permissions. You *can* create all of it manually in the UI — but in production it's often better to **declare it as code/configuration**."},{"t":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"job:\n  name: orders_pipeline\n  schedule:\n    ...\n  tasks:\n    - ingest\n    - transform","caption":"Conceptual job definition (not real syntax yet)"},{"t":"list","items":["Can live in Git","Can be reviewed","Has version history","Can be reproduced","Can be deployed automatically"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"This is the philosophy of **Infrastructure as Code / configuration as code**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Naming: Declarative Automation Bundles = Databricks Asset Bundles","text":"The tool you'll learn later is called **Declarative Automation Bundles**; it was formerly called **Databricks Asset Bundles**. Tutorials saying 'Asset Bundles' mean the same concept."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks recommends bundles as the main approach for CI/CD and IaC-style deployment of Databricks projects. A bundle can describe **jobs, pipelines, source files, tests and target environments**. The central file is usually **`databricks.yml`**."},{"t":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"targets:\n  dev:\n    mode: development\n  prod:\n    mode: production","caption":"databricks.yml targets with the two deployment modes"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Don't memorize yet","text":"We'll learn bundle syntax line by line in the Bundles chapter. For now: bundle = project + resources + targets, as code."}]},{"id":"ch03-s10","title":"The Whole Platform at a Glance (and 2026 Naming)","hook":"One mental model for the entire data-engineering platform — plus a decoder ring for old names.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"PostgreSQL\n    │  CDC\n    ▼\n┌──────────────┐\n│    BRONZE    │  raw changes\n└──────┬───────┘\n       │  AUTO CDC · validation · cleaning\n       ▼\n┌──────────────┐\n│    SILVER    │  customers · orders · products\n└──────┬───────┘\n       │  aggregation\n       ▼\n┌──────────────┐\n│     GOLD     │\n└──────────────┘","caption":"The e-commerce data flow"},{"t":"table","head":["Concern","What handles it"],"rows":[["Execution","Lakeflow / Spark → processing"],["Governance","Unity Catalog → who? what object? what privilege?"],["Development","Git → DEV → tests → STAGING → PROD"],["Deployment","Declarative Automation Bundle + CI/CD system"],["Storage","Delta tables → cloud object storage"]],"caption":"Add these layers around the data flow"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"A correct mental model","text":"This is already a fairly accurate mental model of the entire Databricks data-engineering platform."},{"t":"p","text":"**2026 naming:** you'll meet tutorials using different names for the pipeline product:"},{"t":"list","items":["Delta Live Tables","DLT","Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Spark Declarative Pipelines","Lakeflow pipelines"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"In current docs, **Lakeflow pipelines** build on **Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines (SDP)** and support **batch and streaming** pipelines in **SQL/Python**, with **automatic dependency orchestration** and **incremental processing**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Old names still matter","text":"You'll learn today's syntax, but old names remain important for older tutorials, Stack Overflow, older exams and existing production systems."}]},{"id":"ch03-s11","title":"Debugging Case 1: No New Data Appears","hook":"Source has today's data, Gold doesn't. Walk the pipeline — don't blame the platform.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pipeline: **source files → bronze → silver → gold**. The source has today's data. **Gold doesn't.**"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Don't say 'Databricks bug'","text":"Think in pipeline terms and check each layer in order."},{"t":"flow","items":["Source: new files exist?","Bronze: ingested?","Checkpoint: advanced?","Silver: rows rejected?","Schema: changed?","CDC: sequence/key correct?","Gold: refresh/job ran?"],"caption":"The layer-by-layer check"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when new data doesn't show up","questions":["Source: do the new files/events really exist?","Bronze: were they ingested?","Checkpoint: did it advance?","Silver: did the transformation reject (filter/quarantine) the rows?","Schema: did it change and block the write?","CDC: are sequence and key correct?","Gold: did the refresh/job actually run?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The golden rule of this course","text":"**Find the first layer at which reality diverges from what you expected.** Everything upstream of it is fine; the bug lives at that boundary."},{"t":"table","head":["Observation","Where to debug"],"rows":[["Kafka has event 500, Bronze doesn't","Source → Bronze (ingestion, checkpoint)"],["Bronze has event 500, Silver doesn't","Bronze → Silver (validation, schema, CDC, dedup)"],["Silver has it, Gold doesn't","Silver → Gold (aggregation, refresh / job run)"]]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first (test Q25): Kafka → Bronze → Silver → Gold. Kafka has event 500. Bronze has event 500. Silver doesn't. Where do you start?","text":"At the **Bronze → Silver boundary** — you've already proven the event reached Bronze, so source and ingestion are fine."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → the 8-step 'new files, no rows' chain","text":"For file ingestion the source→Bronze boundary splits into eight checks: files exist → path matches → extension/format → discovered → checkpoint already processed → parsing → schema → sink. Chapter 12 turns it into a playbook with scenarios. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"For streams, 'no new data' has a 9-point checklist: query active? source producing? new offsets? checkpoint corrupted? sink blocked? state batch stuck? executor failure? permission expired? source retention violated? — and **don't restart blindly**. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 15 (playbook 'Stream stopped making progress').","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch03-s12","title":"Debugging Case 2: Duplicate Records","hook":"'I have duplicates' is a symptom, not a root cause — there are at least seven very different causes.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"order 123\norder 123","caption":"The symptom"},{"t":"table","head":["Possible cause","Where it lives / what to check"],"rows":[["Source produced a duplicate","Source: does the source itself contain order 123 twice?"],["File ingested twice","Source → Bronze: was the same file loaded twice?"],["Checkpoint reset","Streaming: was the checkpoint deleted/reset/moved?"],["Retry semantics","Job: did a retry re-write data that was already written?"],["CDC key incorrect","Silver: is the key really unique per entity?"],["Deduplication missing","Silver: is there a dedup step at all?"],["MERGE condition incorrect","Silver/Gold: does the MERGE ON clause match existing rows?"]],"caption":"Seven very different root causes"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Symptom ≠ root cause","text":"\"I have duplicates\" is **not** a root cause. It's a symptom. Your job is to find which mechanism created the second copy."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when you see duplicate records","questions":["Does the source itself contain the duplicate?","Was the same file/batch ingested twice into Bronze?","Was a streaming checkpoint reset or moved?","Did a job retry re-write data it had already written?","Is the CDC/business key I use really unique?","Does my Silver logic deduplicate at all?","Is my MERGE condition matching on the right key?"]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- BUG: matching on a changing column means updates never match\nMERGE INTO silver.orders t\nUSING updates s\nON t.order_id = s.order_id AND t.updated_at = s.updated_at\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","caption":"An incorrect MERGE condition that inserts a second order 123"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Deduplicating blindly","text":"Adding `DISTINCT` everywhere may hide the symptom while the real cause (e.g. a reset checkpoint or wrong MERGE key) keeps producing bad data."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → file idempotency vs business-key idempotency","text":"Exactly-once file ingestion (Auto Loader/COPY INTO) never guarantees one row per order_id: two different files can carry the same key. First ask *same file twice or two different files?* — the answer tells you whether to fix ingestion state or Silver's MERGE. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Exactly-once has edges: Delta sinks are exactly-once, but a **non-idempotent foreachBatch** (emails, `balance += x`) replays on retry, and **source duplicates** pass straight through (processing guarantee ≠ business dedup). Bounded streaming dedup: `withWatermark(...).dropDuplicates([\"event_id\", \"event_time\"])`. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, sections 10 & 13.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch03-s13","title":"Debugging Case 3: Works in Dev, Fails in Prod","hook":"Perfect code can still fail — because prod is a different execution environment.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"DEV   → success\nPROD  → PERMISSION_DENIED"},{"t":"p","text":"The code may be perfect. What can differ between environments:"},{"t":"table","head":["Group","What may differ"],"rows":[["WHO runs it","identity"],["WHAT it may touch","permissions · catalog · schema · storage credential"],["HOW it's configured","configuration · secret"],["WHERE it runs","compute · runtime"]],"caption":"Nine differences, grouped WHO → WHAT → HOW → WHERE"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Not just the traceback","text":"Production debugging isn't just reading the Python traceback. You must understand the **whole execution environment**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Works when I run it, fails when scheduled (test Q26)","text":"One of the first things to check: the **run identity**. You may have the permissions while the scheduled execution identity does not."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when it works in dev but fails in prod","questions":["Which identity runs it in prod — and is it the same one that ran it in dev?","Does that identity have the needed permissions in prod?","Am I pointing at the right catalog and schema for prod (not dev_…)?","Does prod use a storage credential this identity can use?","Is the prod configuration (parameters, paths) different?","Do the secrets exist in prod and can this identity read them?","Is the compute different (type, access mode, size)?","Is the runtime version different?"]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"DEV run","points":["You (user identity)","dev_ catalog/schema","Your personal config & secrets","Your interactive compute/runtime"]},{"title":"PROD run","points":["Service principal / run-as identity","prod catalog/schema","Prod config & secrets","Job compute, possibly another runtime"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Assuming the code is the problem","text":"When only the environment changed, the code is the least likely suspect. Compare environments before rewriting code."}]},{"id":"ch03-s14","title":"The Debugging Mindset & What Comes Next","hook":"One rule to carry into every Databricks topic, and the map of where we go next.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"One rule for all debugging","text":"Find the **first layer where reality diverges** from what you expected. Symptoms (missing data, duplicates, PERMISSION_DENIED) are not root causes."},{"t":"table","head":["Debugging case","First move"],"rows":[["No new data","Walk Source → Bronze → checkpoint → Silver → schema → CDC → Gold"],["Duplicates","Treat as symptom; find where the second copy first appears"],["Works in dev, fails in prod","Compare environments, starting with identity"],["PERMISSION_DENIED","Walk workspace → catalog → schema → table → policies → run identity"]]},{"t":"p","text":"With these prerequisites you can start Databricks itself without memorizing product names you don't understand."},{"t":"diagram","text":"DATABRICKS — PART 1\n├── what Databricks really is\n├── account vs workspace\n├── control plane vs compute plane\n├── workspace UI\n├── serverless vs classic compute\n├── clusters / compute\n├── SQL warehouses\n├── Databricks Runtime\n├── Photon\n├── notebooks\n├── catalogs / schemas / tables in the UI\n├── how a notebook really executes\n├── first real exercise\n└── debugging failing compute/notebooks/queries","caption":"Next: Databricks Part 1"},{"t":"flow","items":["Delta Lake","Unity Catalog","Ingestion / Auto Loader","Lakeflow","Jobs","Databricks SQL","Performance / Spark UI","Bundles / CI-CD","Security & projects"],"caption":"The learning path after Part 1 (then production projects and certification drills)"}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch03-d01","title":"PERMISSION_DENIED on SELECT","section":"ch03-s04","symptom":"`SELECT * FROM prod.finance.transactions` fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED`.","askYourself":["Can this principal access the workspace at all?","Does it have USE CATALOG on prod?","Does it have USE SCHEMA on prod.finance?","Does it have SELECT on the table?","Are row filters, column masks or other policies in play?","Which identity is ACTUALLY running this — me or a job's service principal?"],"steps":[{"do":"Identify the executing identity (user vs job run-as / service principal)","why":"Your privileges don't transfer to other identities."},{"do":"Check the privilege path for that identity","why":"USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → SELECT.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG prod;\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod.finance;\nSHOW GRANTS ON TABLE prod.finance.transactions;"},{"do":"Check fine-grained policies (row filters / masks)","why":"They can restrict even with SELECT."},{"do":"Grant only the missing privilege, ideally to a group or the service principal","why":"Least privilege — never 'give me admin'."}],"rootCauses":["Missing USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA","Missing SELECT","Job runs as a different identity without grants","Policies (row filters/masks)"],"fix":"Grant exactly the missing privilege to the identity that actually runs the workload.","mnemonic":"Workspace → Catalog → Schema → Table → Policies → WHO? (W-C-S-T-P-Who)"},{"id":"ch03-d02","title":"No new data appears in Gold","section":"ch03-s11","symptom":"The source has today's data, but Gold doesn't.","askYourself":["Source: do the new files/events really exist?","Bronze: were they ingested?","Checkpoint: did it advance?","Silver: did the transformation reject the rows?","Schema: did it change?","CDC: are sequence and key correct?","Gold: did the refresh/job run?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm new data at the source","why":"Rules out 'nothing arrived'."},{"do":"Count today's rows in Bronze","why":"Splits ingestion problems from transformation problems."},{"do":"Inspect the stream checkpoint progress","why":"A stuck checkpoint means nothing new is read."},{"do":"Look for rejected/quarantined rows and schema errors in Silver","why":"Validation or schema mismatch can drop the rows."},{"do":"Verify CDC sequence/key logic","why":"Wrong ordering or keys can hide changes."},{"do":"Check the Gold job/refresh run history","why":"Maybe Gold simply didn't refresh."}],"rootCauses":["Files never arrived","Ingestion didn't run","Checkpoint stuck","Rows rejected by validation","Schema change blocked writes","CDC key/sequence wrong","Gold refresh/job didn't run"],"fix":"Fix the first layer where reality diverges, then let the downstream layers catch up (or rerun them).","mnemonic":"Source · Bronze · Checkpoint · Silver · Schema · CDC · Gold — 'Some Big Cats Sleep Soundly Chasing Geese'"},{"id":"ch03-d03","title":"Duplicate records","section":"ch03-s12","symptom":"The same record appears twice (e.g. order 123 twice) in Silver or Gold.","askYourself":["Does the source itself contain the duplicate?","Was the same file ingested twice?","Was a checkpoint reset?","Did a retry re-write data already written?","Is the CDC key correct and unique?","Is deduplication missing?","Is the MERGE condition correct?"],"steps":[{"do":"Find the first layer where the duplicate appears (source, Bronze, Silver)","why":"Separates upstream from logic causes."},{"do":"For Bronze duplicates: check ingestion history and checkpoint","why":"File-twice or reset checkpoint."},{"do":"Check job run history for retries","why":"Non-idempotent writes duplicate data on retry."},{"do":"Review CDC key, dedup step and MERGE ON clause","why":"Logic causes in Silver.","code":"MERGE INTO silver.orders t\nUSING updates s\nON t.order_id = s.order_id\n..."}],"rootCauses":["Source produced duplicate","File ingested twice","Checkpoint reset","Retry semantics","CDC key incorrect","Deduplication missing","MERGE condition incorrect"],"fix":"Fix the mechanism that created the second copy, then clean existing duplicates (e.g. dedup in Silver). Never just hide them with DISTINCT.","mnemonic":"'Some Files Can Repeat; Keys, Dedup, MERGE' (S-F-C-R-K-D-M)"},{"id":"ch03-d04","title":"Works in dev, fails in prod","section":"ch03-s13","symptom":"DEV: success. PROD: PERMISSION_DENIED (or another failure) with identical code.","askYourself":["Which identity runs it in prod, and is it the same as in dev?","Does that identity have the needed permissions in prod?","Am I using the right prod catalog?","Am I using the right prod schema?","Is there a storage credential this identity can use?","Is the prod configuration different?","Do the needed secrets exist in prod?","Is the compute different?","Is the runtime different?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare run identity between dev and prod","why":"Most common difference."},{"do":"Check privileges and object names in prod","why":"Catalog/schema names and grants differ per environment."},{"do":"Diff configuration and secrets between targets","why":"Parameters, paths, credentials."},{"do":"Compare compute type and runtime version","why":"Behaviour can differ by environment."}],"rootCauses":["Different identity","Missing permissions","Wrong catalog/schema","Missing storage credential","Different configuration","Missing secret","Different compute","Different runtime"],"fix":"Make environments differ only in deliberate, declared ways (bundle targets), and grant the prod identity exactly what it needs.","mnemonic":"WHO → WHAT → HOW → WHERE (identity → permissions/catalog/schema/credential → config/secret → compute/runtime)"},{"id":"ch03-d05","title":"Works when I run it, fails when scheduled","section":"ch03-s13","symptom":"A pipeline succeeds when run manually but fails when the scheduled job runs it.","askYourself":["Which identity does the scheduled run use?","Is it the same identity as my manual run?","Does the scheduled identity have USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT/MODIFY where needed?","Does it differ in configuration, secrets or compute from my manual run?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the job settings and read the run-as identity","why":"Manual runs use you; scheduled runs may not."},{"do":"Check that identity's grants along the path","why":"Find the specific missing privilege."},{"do":"Grant the minimal privilege and re-run","why":"Least privilege."}],"rootCauses":["Scheduled identity lacks privileges","Job configured with different parameters/secrets"],"fix":"Check run identity first; grant the scheduled identity exactly what it needs.","mnemonic":"Me ≠ the job."},{"id":"ch03-d06","title":"Nobody knows what is running in production","section":"ch03-s06","symptom":"Prod output changed overnight; someone edited the production notebook manually and pressed Run All.","askYourself":["Which version is running?","Who changed it?","When?","What changed?","How do I return to the previous version?","Did this same code pass tests?"],"steps":[{"do":"Try to reconstruct the change from notebook history / colleagues","why":"Without Git, evidence is weak."},{"do":"Restore known-good code","why":"Stop the damage."},{"do":"Move the code into Git with review, CI and CD via bundles","why":"Make the six questions answerable next time."}],"rootCauses":["Manual edits in production","No source control","No CI/CD or environment isolation"],"fix":"Dev → staging → prod with Git, CI and CD (bundles). Never edit production by hand.","mnemonic":"Which · Who · When · What · Way back · Tested? (6 Ws)"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Confusing authentication with authorization","text":"PERMISSION_DENIED after login is about permissions, not identity proof.","fix":"Ask: who are you? vs what may you do?"},{"title":"Granting to individuals","text":"Per-user grants become unmanageable.","fix":"Grant to groups and manage membership."},{"title":"Production jobs running as a person","text":"When the person leaves or changes role, prod breaks.","fix":"Use a service principal."},{"title":"SELECT without USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA","text":"Access passes through the catalog/schema hierarchy.","fix":"Grant the whole path."},{"title":"'Give me admin' debugging","text":"Hides the cause and violates least privilege.","fix":"Walk the privilege path and check run identity."},{"title":"'It works for me'","text":"Your identity's privileges don't apply to the job's identity.","fix":"Check which identity is ACTUALLY running."},{"title":"ALL PRIVILEGES for everybody","text":"Larger blast radius, accidental/destructive actions, security exposure.","fix":"Least privilege; use ALL PRIVILEGES/MANAGE/ownership sparingly."},{"title":"Editing production notebooks manually","text":"No reproducibility, review, version history or repeatable deployment.","fix":"Dev → staging → prod via Git + CI/CD."},{"title":"Treating a workspace notebook as source of truth","text":"No reliable history or review.","fix":"The Git repository is the source of truth."},{"title":"Merging despite failing CI","text":"Untested code reaches prod.","fix":"CI failure blocks merge/deploy."},{"title":"Thinking Asset Bundles and Declarative Automation Bundles differ","text":"It's a rename.","fix":"Same tool, new name."},{"title":"Thinking DLT is unrelated to Lakeflow pipelines","text":"Old names persist in tutorials, exams and prod systems.","fix":"Map DLT → Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines → Lakeflow pipelines (SDP)."},{"title":"Blaming 'a Databricks bug'","text":"Skips systematic debugging.","fix":"Find the first layer where reality diverges."},{"title":"Treating duplicates as a root cause","text":"DISTINCT hides the mechanism.","fix":"Trace the second copy to one of the seven causes."},{"title":"Assuming dev success means prod code bug","text":"Environments differ in nine ways.","fix":"Compare WHO → WHAT → HOW → WHERE."},{"title":"REVOKE on a table ≠ access gone","text":"Inherited SELECT on the schema or catalog keeps the table readable after a table-level REVOKE.","fix":"SHOW GRANTS on schema and catalog; restructure where SELECT is granted.","src":"part2"}],"flashcards":[{"q":"Authentication vs authorization?","a":"Authentication: who are you? Authorization: what are you allowed to do?","section":"ch03-s01"},{"q":"What is a principal?","a":"An identity privileges can be granted to: user, group or service principal.","section":"ch03-s02"},{"q":"User / group / service principal examples?","a":"nikos@company.com / data_engineers / orders-etl-prod","section":"ch03-s02"},{"q":"Why grant to groups?","a":"Manage access via membership instead of many per-user grants (Databricks recommendation).","section":"ch03-s02"},{"q":"Why service principals for prod jobs?","a":"Stable machine identity independent of an employee's lifecycle and permissions.","section":"ch03-s02"},{"q":"Securable object?","a":"A governed object permissions can be granted on (catalog, schema, table, volume, function…).","section":"ch03-s03"},{"q":"Privilege?","a":"An action a principal may perform on a securable object.","section":"ch03-s03"},{"q":"Parse GRANT SELECT ON TABLE t TO `analysts`.","a":"GRANT = give · SELECT = read privilege · ON TABLE t = securable · TO analysts = principal.","section":"ch03-s03"},{"q":"Privileges needed to read company.finance.transactions?","a":"USE CATALOG on company, USE SCHEMA on company.finance, SELECT on the table.","section":"ch03-s03"},{"q":"SELECT on table but no USE CATALOG — problem?","a":"Yes, access passes through the catalog/schema hierarchy.","section":"ch03-s03"},{"q":"PERMISSION_DENIED checklist?","a":"Workspace → USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → SELECT → row filters/masks/policies → which identity is ACTUALLY running?","section":"ch03-s04"},{"q":"Least privilege?","a":"Give only the permissions that are necessary.","section":"ch03-s05"},{"q":"Privileges to use sparingly?","a":"ALL PRIVILEGES, MANAGE, ownership.","section":"ch03-s05"},{"q":"Why not ALL PRIVILEGES for everyone?","a":"Violates least privilege, bigger blast radius, accidental/destructive actions, security exposure.","section":"ch03-s05"},{"q":"Role of TEST/STAGING?","a":"Production-like validation of permissions, dependencies, config, integration, data quality, deployment — without real consumers.","section":"ch03-s06"},{"q":"Six questions a manual prod edit can't answer?","a":"Which version? Who? When? What changed? How to go back? Did it pass tests?","section":"ch03-s06"},{"q":"Git flow?","a":"developer → branch → commit → pull request → review → main.","section":"ch03-s07"},{"q":"Source of truth for code?","a":"The Git repository — not a notebook in someone's workspace.","section":"ch03-s07"},{"q":"CI?","a":"Automatic integration/validation/testing on push: lint → unit → integration → config validation → build; fail = no merge/deploy.","section":"ch03-s08"},{"q":"CD?","a":"Controlled delivery/deployment of validated changes: main → tests → staging → integration tests → prod.","section":"ch03-s08"},{"q":"Benefits of Job config in YAML/Git?","a":"Version control, review, reproducibility, automation, repeatable deployments, env-specific config.","section":"ch03-s09"},{"q":"Declarative Automation Bundles — old name?","a":"Databricks Asset Bundles. Central file: databricks.yml.","section":"ch03-s09"},{"q":"Bundle deployment modes?","a":"development and production (e.g. targets dev / prod).","section":"ch03-s09"},{"q":"Old names of Lakeflow pipelines?","a":"Delta Live Tables (DLT), Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines; built on Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines (SDP).","section":"ch03-s10"},{"q":"No new data in Gold — check order?","a":"Source → Bronze → checkpoint → Silver rejects → schema → CDC → Gold refresh.","section":"ch03-s11"},{"q":"Course-wide debugging rule?","a":"Find the first layer where reality diverges from expectation.","section":"ch03-s11"},{"q":"Kafka & Bronze have event 500, Silver doesn't → ?","a":"Debug the Bronze → Silver boundary.","section":"ch03-s11"},{"q":"Seven duplicate causes?","a":"Source duplicate, file ingested twice, checkpoint reset, retry semantics, CDC key incorrect, dedup missing, MERGE condition incorrect.","section":"ch03-s12"},{"q":"Nine dev-vs-prod differences?","a":"identity, permissions, catalog, schema, storage credential, configuration, secret, compute, runtime.","section":"ch03-s13"},{"q":"Works manually, fails scheduled → first check?","a":"The scheduled run identity.","section":"ch03-s13"},{"q":"Why can a user still read a table after REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE?","a":"An inherited SELECT on the parent schema or catalog.","section":"ch03-s03","src":"part2"},{"q":"Full PERMISSION_DENIED mnemonic?","a":"I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions — Identity, Binding, Catalog, Schema, Object, Fine-grained, Storage, Compute.","section":"ch03-s04","src":"part2"},{"q":"What does ALL PRIVILEGES exclude?","a":"MANAGE, EXTERNAL USE LOCATION, EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA.","section":"ch03-s05","src":"part2"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch03-e001","type":"free","section":"ch03-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q14: What is the difference between authentication and authorization?","model":"Authentication answers 'who are you?' — it proves an identity, e.g. that the login really is nikos@company.com. Authorization answers 'what are you allowed to do?' — e.g. Nikos may SELECT customers but not DELETE or CREATE TABLE.","rubric":["authentication = who are you / prove identity","authorization = what are you allowed to do","example"],"explain":"Official answer: Authentication — who are you? Authorization — what are you allowed to do?"},{"id":"ch03-e002","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Authentication or authorization?","buckets":["Authentication","Authorization"],"items":[{"text":"Logging in as nikos@company.com","bucket":0},{"text":"Checking whether Nikos may DELETE customers","bucket":1},{"text":"Proving the caller really is the service principal orders-etl-prod","bucket":0},{"text":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE ... TO analysts","bucket":1},{"text":"A login fails because of a wrong password/token","bucket":0},{"text":"PERMISSION_DENIED on a SELECT after a successful login","bucket":1}],"explain":"Authentication = establishing identity (login, token). Authorization = permissions of that identity (grants, PERMISSION_DENIED)."},{"id":"ch03-e003","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"PERMISSION_DENIED after you have successfully logged in is an authentication problem.","answer":false,"explain":"You are already authenticated (the system knows who you are). PERMISSION_DENIED means that identity lacks a privilege — an authorization problem."},{"id":"ch03-e004","type":"cloze","section":"ch03-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the two questions.","text":"Authentication asks: [[who are you|who are you?]] — Authorization asks: [[what are you allowed to do|what are you allowed to do?|what may you do]]","bank":["where are you","how fast are you"],"explain":"Two separate checks: identity first, then permissions for that identity."},{"id":"ch03-e005","type":"odd","section":"ch03-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT an authorization decision?","options":["Nikos may SELECT customers","Nikos may not DELETE customers","Nikos may not CREATE TABLE","Nikos proves his identity at login"],"answer":3,"explain":"Proving identity at login is authentication; the other three are permissions granted (or not) to that identity."},{"id":"ch03-e006","type":"free","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q15: What is a principal?","model":"An identity to which privileges can be assigned — for example a user, a group or a service principal.","rubric":["identity","privileges/permissions can be granted to it","user / group / service principal"],"explain":"Official answer: an identity to which privileges can be assigned, e.g. user, group or service principal."},{"id":"ch03-e007","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q16: Why should a production job usually run as a service principal instead of a developer's personal account?","options":["Service principals run queries faster","Production needs a stable machine identity independent of one employee's lifecycle and permissions","Personal accounts cannot run jobs on a schedule","Service principals automatically get ALL PRIVILEGES"],"answer":1,"why":["Identity doesn't affect query speed.","Correct.","They can — that's exactly the risk.","No! Service principals should also follow least privilege."],"explain":"If the job runs as Nikos and Nikos leaves (account disabled) or his permissions change, production breaks. A service principal like `orders-etl-prod` is owned by the system, not a person."},{"id":"ch03-e008","type":"match","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each example to its principal type.","pairs":[["nikos@company.com","User"],["data_engineers","Group"],["orders-etl-prod","Service principal"]],"explain":"Users = humans, groups = sets of principals, service principals = machine identities for automation."},{"id":"ch03-e009","type":"odd","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a principal?","options":["nikos@company.com","data_engineers","orders-etl-prod","company.finance.payroll"],"answer":3,"explain":"`company.finance.payroll` is a table — a securable object you grant ON. The other three are identities you grant TO."},{"id":"ch03-e010","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The nightly `orders` job has run fine for a year. Today it fails at 02:00. Yesterday Nikos — the engineer who created it — left the company.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is your first hypothesis?","options":[{"text":"The job runs as Nikos's user identity, which was just disabled","ok":true,"fb":"Right — a classic reason to use service principals for production."},{"text":"The data volume suddenly grew","ok":false,"fb":"Possible in general, but the timing with Nikos leaving is the strong clue."},{"text":"Spark has a bug","ok":false,"fb":"Don't blame the platform before checking the identity."}]},{"prompt":"Confirmed: 'Run as' = nikos@company.com. What is the durable fix?","options":[{"text":"Run the job as a service principal (e.g. orders-etl-prod) with only the privileges it needs","ok":true,"fb":"Stable machine identity + least privilege."},{"text":"Run it as another engineer's account","ok":false,"fb":"Same problem again when that person leaves or changes role."},{"text":"Re-enable Nikos's account forever","ok":false,"fb":"Keeps a departed employee's identity alive — a security risk."}]}],"explain":"Human → user identity; automation → service principal."},{"id":"ch03-e011","type":"write","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Grant the group `data_engineers` SELECT on table `company.silver.orders` (one statement).","solution":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE company.silver.orders TO `data_engineers`;","keywords":["grant select","on table company.silver.orders","to `data_engineers`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Granting to a group means you manage access by membership; backticks are used around principal names in Databricks SQL."},{"id":"ch03-e012","type":"calc","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Nikos, Maria and John each need the same 4 privileges. How many GRANT statements do you write if you grant per user (instead of 4 grants to `data_engineers`)?","answer":12,"tolerance":0,"unit":"statements","explain":"3 users × 4 privileges = 12 statements, versus 4 when granting to the group — and with the group, onboarding a 4th engineer needs 0 new grants, just a membership change."},{"id":"ch03-e013","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Databricks recommends granting privileges to individual users rather than groups wherever possible.","answer":false,"explain":"The recommendation is the opposite: manage access through groups where possible, and manage membership."},{"id":"ch03-e014","type":"free","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q17: What is a securable object?","model":"A governed object on which permissions can be granted — e.g. a catalog, schema, table, volume or function.","rubric":["governed object","permissions can be granted on it","examples: catalog/schema/table/volume/function"],"explain":"Official answer: a governed object on which permissions can be granted."},{"id":"ch03-e015","type":"match","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q18: Explain `GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.finance.transactions TO analysts;` word by word — match each part to its meaning.","pairs":[["GRANT","give"],["SELECT","the read permission"],["ON TABLE prod.finance.transactions","on this table (securable)"],["TO `analysts`","to this principal / group"]],"explain":"GRANT = give, SELECT = privilege, ON TABLE … = securable, TO … = principal. Reading it as a sentence prevents mechanical memorization."},{"id":"ch03-e016","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q19: A group has SELECT on a table but NOT USE CATALOG on its catalog. Can there be a permission problem?","options":["No — SELECT on the table is all you need","Yes — access passes through the catalog/schema hierarchy","Only if the table is a view","Only for service principals"],"answer":1,"explain":"To reach `catalog.schema.table` the principal also needs USE CATALOG on the catalog and USE SCHEMA on the schema. Missing either blocks the read."},{"id":"ch03-e017","type":"write","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the three GRANT statements that let group `analysts` read `company.finance.transactions`.","solution":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG company TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA company.finance TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE company.finance.transactions TO `analysts`;","keywords":["use catalog on catalog company","use schema on schema company.finance","select on table company.finance.transactions","to `analysts`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Walk the path: catalog → schema → table. Each level needs its own privilege."},{"id":"ch03-e018","type":"calc","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A brand-new group has no privileges at all. What is the minimum number of GRANT statements so it can SELECT from one table `prod.finance.transactions` (as taught in the lesson)?","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"grants","explain":"USE CATALOG on prod, USE SCHEMA on prod.finance, SELECT on the table — one per level of the three-level name."},{"id":"ch03-e019","type":"order","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the grants along the access path (outer container first).","items":["USE CATALOG on company","USE SCHEMA on company.finance","SELECT on company.finance.transactions"],"explain":"The path goes company → finance → transactions; the outer containers must be usable before the table can be read."},{"id":"ch03-e020","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Principal, securable object or privilege?","buckets":["Principal","Securable object","Privilege"],"items":[{"text":"`analysts`","bucket":0},{"text":"orders-etl-prod","bucket":0},{"text":"company.finance","bucket":1},{"text":"a volume","bucket":1},{"text":"SELECT","bucket":2},{"text":"USE CATALOG","bucket":2},{"text":"company.finance.payroll","bucket":1},{"text":"USE SCHEMA","bucket":2}],"explain":"Principal = WHO (TO …); securable = WHAT object (ON …); privilege = WHICH action (GRANT …)."},{"id":"ch03-e021","type":"spotbug","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This script should let `analysts` read `company.finance.transactions`. Click the buggy lines.","lines":["GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG company TO `analysts`;","GRANT USE SCHEMA ON TABLE company.finance TO `analysts`;","GRANT SELECT ON TABLE company.finance TO `analysts`;"],"bugs":[1,2],"fix":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA company.finance TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE company.finance.transactions TO `analysts`;","explain":"USE SCHEMA is granted ON SCHEMA (not ON TABLE), and SELECT needs the full three-level table name — `company.finance` is a schema, not a table."},{"id":"ch03-e022","type":"order","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the PERMISSION_DENIED checks exactly as taught.","items":["Can the principal access the workspace?","Can it USE CATALOG prod?","Can it USE SCHEMA finance?","Does it have SELECT on the table?","Are there row filters / masks / policies?","Which identity is ACTUALLY running?"],"explain":"Walk from the outside in (workspace → catalog → schema → table → fine-grained policies), and never forget the run identity, which is often the real cause."},{"id":"ch03-e023","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"If my own SELECT on a table works, a scheduled job that reads the same table cannot get PERMISSION_DENIED.","answer":false,"explain":"The job may run as a different identity (e.g. a service principal) with different privileges. 'Which identity is actually running?' is the critical check."},{"id":"ch03-e024","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`SELECT * FROM prod.finance.transactions` works in your notebook, but the nightly Job fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED`.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Which identity the Job actually runs as","ok":true,"fb":"Your success proves only your privileges; the job's identity is the prime suspect."},{"text":"Ask an admin for ALL PRIVILEGES on prod","ok":false,"fb":"That's the 'give me admin' anti-pattern."},{"text":"Rewrite the query in Python","ok":false,"fb":"Language doesn't change permissions."}]},{"prompt":"It runs as `orders-prod-service-principal`. Which privileges must you verify for it?","options":[{"text":"USE CATALOG on prod, USE SCHEMA on prod.finance, SELECT on the table (plus any row filters/masks)","ok":true,"fb":"That's the full path for a read."},{"text":"Only SELECT on the table","ok":false,"fb":"Without USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA the read still fails."},{"text":"MANAGE on the catalog","ok":false,"fb":"Far more than needed — violates least privilege."}]},{"prompt":"It lacks USE SCHEMA on prod.finance. Fix?","options":[{"text":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.finance TO the service principal","ok":true,"fb":"Grant exactly the missing privilege."},{"text":"Make the service principal a workspace admin","ok":false,"fb":"Huge blast radius for a one-privilege problem."},{"text":"Run the job as your own user","ok":false,"fb":"Ties production to a person — the exact thing service principals avoid."}]}],"explain":"Path + identity: workspace → catalog → schema → table → policies → who is ACTUALLY running."},{"id":"ch03-e025","type":"cloze","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the permission-debugging chain.","text":"workspace access → USE [[CATALOG]] → USE [[SCHEMA]] → [[SELECT]] on table → row filters / masks / policies → which [[identity]] is ACTUALLY running?","bank":["ADMIN","CLUSTER","MANAGE"],"explain":"Outer containers first, then the table, then fine-grained policies, and finally the run identity — the check that most often explains 'works for me, fails in the job'."},{"id":"ch03-e026","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which response to PERMISSION_DENIED is the 'bad debugging' the lesson warns about?","options":["Checking USE CATALOG on the catalog","Checking which identity runs the job","Asking for admin rights so it 'just works'","Checking row filters and masks"],"answer":2,"explain":"Requesting admin hides the cause and breaks least privilege. The other three are steps of the correct debugging chain."},{"id":"ch03-e027","type":"free","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain why 'Which identity is ACTUALLY running?' is the most important PERMISSION_DENIED question.","model":"Because permissions belong to identities. When I test interactively my user identity runs the query, but a scheduled job usually runs as another identity, often a service principal like orders-prod-service-principal. My grants don't apply to it, so the job can fail even though the same query works for me.","rubric":["permissions are per identity","interactive = my user; job = service principal / run-as","my privileges don't transfer"],"explain":"This single check explains most 'works for me, fails in the job' permission incidents."},{"id":"ch03-e028","type":"odd","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT part of the PERMISSION_DENIED checklist?","options":["USE CATALOG on the catalog","SELECT on the table","Run identity","Number of shuffle partitions"],"answer":3,"explain":"Shuffle partitions affect performance, never permissions. The other three are steps of the permission chain."},{"id":"ch03-e029","type":"free","section":"ch03-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q20: Why is 'give ALL PRIVILEGES to everybody' a bad solution to permission errors?","model":"It violates least privilege, increases the blast radius, raises the risk of accidental or destructive actions, and increases security exposure. It also hides the real missing privilege instead of fixing it.","rubric":["violates least privilege","bigger blast radius","accidental/destructive actions","security exposure"],"explain":"Official answer: violates least privilege, increases blast radius, risk of accidental/destructive actions and security exposure."},{"id":"ch03-e030","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"An analyst only needs to read one table. Which grant set follows least privilege?","options":["ALL PRIVILEGES on the catalog","USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT on the table","Ownership of the table","MANAGE on the schema"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grant exactly the read path. ALL PRIVILEGES, MANAGE and ownership are powerful privileges Databricks says to use sparingly."},{"id":"ch03-e031","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Select ALL the consequences of over-granting privileges.","options":["Larger blast radius","Risk of accidental destructive actions","Higher security exposure","Faster queries"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Over-granting has only downsides for safety; privileges don't change performance."},{"id":"ch03-e032","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"ALL PRIVILEGES, MANAGE and ownership should be used sparingly.","answer":true,"explain":"Databricks explicitly warns about these powerful privileges; they should be reserved for the few identities that truly administer objects."},{"id":"ch03-e033","type":"odd","section":"ch03-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which grant does NOT fit 'least privilege for a read-only analyst'?","options":["USE CATALOG","USE SCHEMA","SELECT","ALL PRIVILEGES"],"answer":3,"explain":"ALL PRIVILEGES includes writes and more — far beyond read-only needs."},{"id":"ch03-e034","type":"free","section":"ch03-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q21: Why shouldn't you edit a production notebook manually?","model":"Because you lose reproducibility, controlled review and testing, reliable version history and repeatable deployment. Afterwards nobody can reliably say which version runs, who changed what and when, how to roll back, or whether that code passed tests.","rubric":["reproducibility","review / testing","version history","repeatable deployment / rollback"],"explain":"Official answer: you lose reproducibility, controlled review/testing, reliable version history and repeatable deployment."},{"id":"ch03-e035","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which environment is this typical of?","buckets":["DEV","TEST / STAGING","PROD"],"items":[{"text":"Experimenting with sample data","bucket":0},{"text":"Validating permissions, dependencies and configuration before release","bucket":1},{"text":"CEO dashboard reads from here","bucket":2},{"text":"`dev_nikos.silver.orders`","bucket":0},{"text":"Integration and data-quality checks without affecting real consumers","bucket":1},{"text":"Restricted permissions, monitoring, stable configs","bucket":2},{"text":"It's fine to break things here","bucket":0}],"explain":"DEV = freedom to break; STAGING = production-like validation without real consumers; PROD = controlled, monitored, restricted."},{"id":"ch03-e036","type":"order","section":"ch03-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the path a code change should take.","items":["DEV (code changes)","TEST / STAGING (validation)","PROD"],"explain":"Changes are developed in DEV, validated in a production-like STAGING environment, and only then reach PROD."},{"id":"ch03-e037","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Test/staging is where you validate permissions, dependencies, configuration, integration, data quality and deployment without affecting real consumers.","answer":true,"explain":"That is exactly the role of staging: be close to prod, but isolated from real consumers."},{"id":"ch03-e038","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The CEO's monthly revenue numbers changed overnight. A colleague says: 'I just tested a filter in the prod notebook and pressed Run All.'","steps":[{"prompt":"What's the first problem you face?","options":[{"text":"You can't reliably tell which version ran, what changed, or how to roll back","ok":true,"fb":"Manual edits leave no reliable version history or review trail."},{"text":"The cluster is too small","ok":false,"fb":"Compute size is not the issue here."},{"text":"Unity Catalog is down","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing points to governance."}]},{"prompt":"What should the process have been?","options":[{"text":"Change code in DEV, commit to Git, review, test in STAGING, deploy to PROD automatically","ok":true,"fb":"Environment isolation + source control + CI/CD."},{"text":"Edit prod but warn the CEO first","ok":false,"fb":"Still no review, tests or reproducibility."},{"text":"Copy the prod notebook and edit the copy in prod","ok":false,"fb":"Still running untested code against prod data."}]}],"explain":"Production changes must be reproducible, reviewed, tested and reversible."},{"id":"ch03-e039","type":"order","section":"ch03-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A manual prod edit broke a dashboard. Order the questions you'd need answered (the lesson's list).","items":["Which version is running?","Who changed it?","When?","What changed?","How do I return to the previous version?","Did the same code pass tests?"],"explain":"These six questions are exactly what Git + CI/CD answer reliably — and what manual edits make unanswerable."},{"id":"ch03-e040","type":"order","section":"ch03-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the Git flow.","items":["developer","branch","commit","pull request","review","main"],"explain":"Work happens on a branch, is recorded in commits, proposed via a pull request, reviewed, and only then merged into main."},{"id":"ch03-e041","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is the source of truth for production code in a mature setup?","options":["The notebook in Nikos's workspace","The Git repository","The latest cluster log","The CEO dashboard"],"answer":1,"explain":"Code lives and is versioned in Git; workspaces get their code from it. A personal workspace notebook has no reliable history or review."},{"id":"ch03-e042","type":"match","section":"ch03-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each Git term to its role.","pairs":[["branch","isolated line of work for a change"],["commit","recorded snapshot of changes"],["pull request","proposal to merge, where review happens"],["main","shared branch of accepted code"]],"explain":"Together these give versioning, traceability and review before code reaches production."},{"id":"ch03-e043","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Using Git alone already guarantees that the code passed tests.","answer":false,"explain":"Git gives history and review. Automatic testing of each change is CI (Continuous Integration), built on top of Git."},{"id":"ch03-e044","type":"free","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q22: What is CI?","model":"Automatic integration, validation and testing of code changes — e.g. lint, unit tests, integration tests, configuration validation and build on every push; if anything fails, you don't merge or deploy.","rubric":["automatic","integration / validation / testing of code changes","fail → no merge/deploy"],"explain":"Official answer: automatic integration/validation/testing of code changes."},{"id":"ch03-e045","type":"free","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q23: What is CD?","model":"Controlled delivery or deployment of validated changes to the target environments, e.g. main → tests → deploy staging → integration tests → deploy production.","rubric":["controlled delivery/deployment","validated changes","target environments (staging, prod)"],"explain":"Official answer: controlled delivery/deployment of the validated changes to the target environments."},{"id":"ch03-e046","type":"order","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the CI pipeline from the lesson.","items":["lint","unit tests","integration tests","configuration validation","build"],"explain":"Cheap, fast checks first (lint, unit tests), slower ones later (integration, config validation), and build at the end."},{"id":"ch03-e047","type":"order","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the CD pipeline from the lesson.","items":["main branch","tests","deploy staging","integration tests","deploy production"],"explain":"Validated code moves to staging, is integration-tested there, and only then is deployed to production."},{"id":"ch03-e048","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"CI fails on the integration tests for your pull request. What should happen?","options":["Merge anyway and fix in prod","Do not merge / deploy","Deploy to prod but not staging","Disable the failing test"],"answer":1,"explain":"CI is a gate: a failing check blocks merge and deploy. Disabling tests or 'fixing in prod' defeats its purpose."},{"id":"ch03-e049","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"CD always means fully automatic deployment to production with no human approval.","answer":false,"explain":"CD can mean Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment depending on the process; the shared idea is automated, controlled deployment of validated code."},{"id":"ch03-e050","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"CI or CD?","buckets":["CI","CD"],"items":[{"text":"Run lint and unit tests on every push","bucket":0},{"text":"Deploy to staging after merge to main","bucket":1},{"text":"Validate configuration before merge","bucket":0},{"text":"Promote the validated version to production","bucket":1},{"text":"Build the artifact","bucket":0}],"explain":"CI validates the change; CD ships the validated change to environments."},{"id":"ch03-e051","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q24: What do we gain when a Job's definition lives in YAML/Git instead of only UI clicks? Select ALL that apply.","options":["Version control","Review","Reproducibility","Automation / repeatable deployments","Environment-specific configuration","Faster Spark execution"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"multi":true,"explain":"Official answer: version control, review, reproducibility, automation, repeatable deployments, environment-specific configuration. Configuration as code doesn't make Spark itself faster."},{"id":"ch03-e052","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"'Databricks Asset Bundles' and 'Declarative Automation Bundles' are two completely different concepts.","answer":false,"explain":"Declarative Automation Bundles is the current name; Databricks Asset Bundles was the earlier name of the same tool."},{"id":"ch03-e053","type":"cloze","section":"ch03-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the bundle targets in `databricks.yml`.","text":"targets:\n  dev:\n    mode: [[development]]\n  prod:\n    mode: [[production]]","bank":["staging","debug","release"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Databricks explicitly supports `development` and `production` deployment modes for bundle targets."},{"id":"ch03-e054","type":"write","section":"ch03-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the `targets` section of a `databricks.yml` with a `dev` target in development mode and a `prod` target in production mode.","solution":"targets:\n  dev:\n    mode: development\n  prod:\n    mode: production","keywords":["targets:","dev:","mode: development","prod:","mode: production"],"lang":"yaml","explain":"Targets are the environments a bundle deploys to; each declares its mode. Exact further syntax comes in the Bundles chapter."},{"id":"ch03-e055","type":"spotbug","section":"ch03-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A teammate summarized bundles. Click the WRONG statements.","lines":["A bundle can describe jobs, pipelines, source files, tests and target environments.","The central configuration file is usually databricks.yml.","Bundles are a different technology from the old Databricks Asset Bundles.","Targets can use the modes development and production.","Bundles are meant only for one-off manual deployments from the UI."],"bugs":[2,4],"fix":"Declarative Automation Bundles are the renamed Databricks Asset Bundles (same concept).\nBundles are the recommended basis for CI/CD and IaC-style, automated, repeatable deployments.","explain":"Bundles = renamed Asset Bundles, designed for automated CI/CD deployment — the opposite of manual UI clicks."},{"id":"ch03-e056","type":"free","section":"ch03-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain Infrastructure as Code to a teammate who builds all Jobs by clicking in the UI.","model":"Instead of creating the job's tasks, schedule, compute, parameters and permissions by hand, you declare them in a configuration file (e.g. YAML in a bundle). That file lives in Git, gets reviewed, has version history, can recreate the same job anywhere, and can be deployed automatically to dev, staging and prod by CI/CD.","rubric":["declare job/infrastructure as code/config","in Git: review + version history","reproducible","automated deployment per environment"],"explain":"The point isn't YAML itself — it's that configuration gets the same safety net as code."},{"id":"ch03-e057","type":"match","section":"ch03-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each platform concern to what handles it.","pairs":[["Execution","Lakeflow / Spark"],["Governance","Unity Catalog"],["Deployment","Bundles + CI/CD"],["Storage","Delta tables on cloud object storage"],["Development","Git → DEV → tests → STAGING → PROD"]],"explain":"Keeping these five concerns separate is what makes debugging possible: each failure usually lives in one of them."},{"id":"ch03-e058","type":"odd","section":"ch03-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Which name is NOT a (past or present) name for Databricks' declarative pipeline product?","options":["Delta Live Tables (DLT)","Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Spark Declarative Pipelines","Declarative Automation Bundles"],"answer":3,"explain":"Bundles are the deployment/IaC tool (formerly Asset Bundles). The other three are names along the DLT → Lakeflow pipelines evolution."},{"id":"ch03-e059","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"According to current docs, what do Lakeflow pipelines build on?","options":["Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines (SDP)","Apache Kafka Streams","Databricks Asset Bundles","Unity Catalog lineage"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lakeflow pipelines build on Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines and support batch + streaming in SQL/Python with automatic dependency orchestration and incremental processing."},{"id":"ch03-e060","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which capabilities does the lesson attribute to Lakeflow pipelines? Select ALL.","options":["Batch and streaming pipelines","SQL and Python","Automatic dependency orchestration","Incremental processing","Granting Unity Catalog privileges"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"Governance (grants) is Unity Catalog's job, not the pipeline engine's."},{"id":"ch03-e061","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A tutorial about 'Delta Live Tables' is about a totally unrelated product from Lakeflow pipelines.","answer":false,"explain":"DLT is an older name in the same lineage: DLT → Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines → Lakeflow pipelines on Spark Declarative Pipelines."},{"id":"ch03-e062","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which layer of the platform does each item belong to?","buckets":["Execution","Governance","Deployment","Storage"],"items":[{"text":"Spark / Lakeflow processing","bucket":0},{"text":"GRANT SELECT to analysts","bucket":1},{"text":"databricks.yml targets","bucket":2},{"text":"Delta tables on cloud object storage","bucket":3},{"text":"CI/CD system deploying to prod","bucket":2},{"text":"'who / what object / what privilege'","bucket":1}],"explain":"Execution computes, governance controls access, deployment ships code/config, storage keeps the data."},{"id":"ch03-e063","type":"order","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the 'no new data' checks exactly as taught.","items":["Source: do the new files exist?","Bronze: ingested?","Checkpoint: advanced?","Silver: rows rejected by transformation?","Schema: changed?","CDC: sequence/key correct?","Gold: refresh/job ran?"],"explain":"Follow the data from source to Gold. The first check that fails is the layer where reality diverges — that's where the bug lives."},{"id":"ch03-e064","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q25: Kafka has event 500, Bronze has event 500, Silver does not. Where do you start debugging?","options":["Kafka producer","Source → Bronze ingestion","The Bronze → Silver boundary","The Gold dashboard"],"answer":2,"why":["Kafka has the event — the producer is fine.","Bronze has the event — ingestion is fine.","Correct: first layer where reality diverges.","Gold is downstream of the problem."],"explain":"The event is proven to be in Bronze, so everything up to Bronze works. The first divergence is Bronze → Silver (validation, schema, CDC, dedup, filters)."},{"id":"ch03-e065","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Source has today's files; Gold shows nothing for today.","steps":[{"prompt":"Where do you start?","options":[{"text":"At the source: confirm the new files really exist","ok":true,"fb":"Start at the beginning and walk forward."},{"text":"Open a ticket: Databricks bug","ok":false,"fb":"Never the first hypothesis."},{"text":"Rebuild Gold from scratch","ok":false,"fb":"You don't know yet where data stops."}]},{"prompt":"Files exist, but Bronze has no rows for today. What next?","options":[{"text":"Check whether ingestion ran and whether the checkpoint advanced","ok":true,"fb":"Data stops at Source → Bronze: ingestion + checkpoint are the suspects."},{"text":"Check Silver's dedup logic","ok":false,"fb":"Silver can't process rows Bronze never received."},{"text":"Check Gold refresh","ok":false,"fb":"Too far downstream."}]},{"prompt":"Alternate timeline: Bronze AND Silver have today's rows, Gold doesn't. Suspect?","options":[{"text":"Gold refresh/job didn't run (or its aggregation filtered the rows)","ok":true,"fb":"First divergence is Silver → Gold."},{"text":"The checkpoint","ok":false,"fb":"Ingestion is proven fine — Bronze and Silver have the data."},{"text":"Schema at source","ok":false,"fb":"Rows already flowed through Silver."}]}],"explain":"Find the first layer where reality diverges from expectation."},{"id":"ch03-e066","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Bronze has today's rows; Silver doesn't. The Silver job ran successfully.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which group of causes lives at the Bronze → Silver boundary?","options":[{"text":"Rows rejected by validation, a schema change, wrong CDC sequence/key","ok":true,"fb":"These are exactly the Silver, Schema and CDC checks."},{"text":"Source files missing","ok":false,"fb":"Bronze already has them."},{"text":"Gold refresh schedule","ok":false,"fb":"Gold is after Silver."}]},{"prompt":"You find a new quarantine table full of today's rows with 'amount is not DECIMAL'. Meaning?","options":[{"text":"The Silver transformation rejected them — investigate the schema/type change upstream","ok":true,"fb":"Rejected rows + type complaint → follow the schema-mismatch decision tree."},{"text":"Delete the quarantine table and rerun","ok":false,"fb":"That hides the evidence and loses data."},{"text":"The checkpoint is broken","ok":false,"fb":"Rows were ingested — the checkpoint did its job."}]}],"explain":"'Silver: rejected rows?' and 'Schema: changed?' are consecutive checks for a reason."},{"id":"ch03-e067","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"When Gold lacks today's data, the efficient approach is to start checking at Gold and work backwards randomly.","answer":false,"explain":"Walk the pipeline systematically and find the FIRST layer where data diverges from expectation. Starting at the source (or bisecting with evidence like 'Bronze has event 500') prevents guessing."},{"id":"ch03-e068","type":"free","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"State the course's general debugging principle and apply it to: 'Kafka has event 500, Bronze has it, Silver doesn't'.","model":"Find the first layer at which reality diverges from what you expected. Kafka and Bronze both have event 500, so source and ingestion work; Silver doesn't, so the first divergence is Bronze → Silver — I investigate validation/rejected rows, schema changes and CDC key/sequence there.","rubric":["first layer where reality diverges","Bronze has it → ingestion fine","debug the Bronze → Silver boundary"],"explain":"The principle turns a vague 'data is missing' into one specific boundary to inspect."},{"id":"ch03-e069","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"'We have duplicates' is a root cause.","answer":false,"explain":"It's a symptom. Causes range from the source producing duplicates to a wrong MERGE condition — each needs a different fix."},{"id":"ch03-e070","type":"cloze","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the key sentence.","text":"\"I have duplicates\" is not a [[root cause]]. It is a [[symptom]].","bank":["feature","solution"],"explain":"Seven different mechanisms can produce the same duplicate symptom; each needs a different fix."},{"id":"ch03-e071","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the seven duplicate causes listed in the lesson?","options":["Checkpoint reset","MERGE condition incorrect","Too few shuffle partitions","File ingested twice"],"answer":2,"explain":"Shuffle partition count affects performance, not row multiplicity. The listed causes: source duplicate, file ingested twice, checkpoint reset, retry semantics, CDC key incorrect, dedup missing, MERGE condition incorrect."},{"id":"ch03-e072","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Where does each duplicate cause live?","buckets":["Source / ingestion","Streaming / job execution","Silver logic"],"items":[{"text":"Source produced the duplicate","bucket":0},{"text":"Same file ingested twice","bucket":0},{"text":"Checkpoint reset","bucket":1},{"text":"Retry re-wrote already written data","bucket":1},{"text":"CDC key not unique","bucket":2},{"text":"No deduplication step","bucket":2},{"text":"Wrong MERGE condition","bucket":2}],"explain":"Locating the cause by layer tells you where to look first and which fix applies."},{"id":"ch03-e073","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Silver `orders` contains order 123 twice.","steps":[{"prompt":"First question?","options":[{"text":"Does Bronze (the raw source data) already contain order 123 twice?","ok":true,"fb":"This splits upstream causes from Silver-logic causes."},{"text":"Add SELECT DISTINCT to Gold","ok":false,"fb":"Hides the symptom without finding the mechanism."},{"text":"Increase cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"Duplicates aren't a capacity issue."}]},{"prompt":"Bronze has order 123 twice, from two different files with the same content. Next?","options":[{"text":"Check whether the same file was ingested twice or the checkpoint was reset","ok":true,"fb":"Same content in two loads → ingestion/checkpoint suspects."},{"text":"Check the Gold refresh","ok":false,"fb":"Gold is downstream."},{"text":"Check schema evolution","ok":false,"fb":"Schema doesn't duplicate rows."}]},{"prompt":"Other timeline: Bronze has order 123 once (an insert) and once more as an update; Silver has 2 rows. Suspect?","options":[{"text":"The MERGE condition or CDC key is wrong, so the update was inserted instead of matched","ok":true,"fb":"Update not matched → WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT → duplicate."},{"text":"Source produced a duplicate","ok":false,"fb":"The source sent an insert + an update, which is valid."},{"text":"Checkpoint reset","ok":false,"fb":"Bronze shows no replay."}]}],"explain":"Duplicates are a symptom; trace them to the mechanism."},{"id":"ch03-e074","type":"order","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order a sensible duplicate investigation (upstream first).","items":["Does the source itself contain the duplicate?","Was the same file ingested twice into Bronze?","Was the checkpoint reset, or did a retry re-write data?","Is the CDC/business key correct and unique?","Does Silver deduplicate, and is the MERGE condition right?"],"explain":"Upstream checks first: if Bronze already holds the duplicate, Silver logic isn't the origin (though missing dedup may fail to remove it)."},{"id":"ch03-e075","type":"spotbug","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"This MERGE keeps creating a second row for order 123 whenever the order is updated. Click the buggy line.","lines":["MERGE INTO silver.orders t","USING updates s","ON t.order_id = s.order_id AND t.updated_at = s.updated_at","WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *","WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"ON t.order_id = s.order_id","explain":"An update carries a new `updated_at`, so `t.updated_at = s.updated_at` never matches the existing row; it falls into WHEN NOT MATCHED and gets inserted again. Match on the business key only."},{"id":"ch03-e076","type":"free","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Your manager says: 'Just add DISTINCT and close the duplicates ticket.' Respond professionally.","model":"Duplicates are a symptom, not a root cause. DISTINCT might hide them in one query but the mechanism — e.g. a file ingested twice, a reset checkpoint, a retry, a wrong CDC key or MERGE condition, or missing dedup — will keep producing bad data and affect other consumers. Let me first find where the second copy appears (source, Bronze, Silver) and fix that mechanism; then we add proper dedup in Silver if needed.","rubric":["symptom not root cause","DISTINCT hides the problem","name possible mechanisms","find where the duplicate first appears","fix mechanism / proper Silver dedup"],"explain":"Good answers redirect from the symptom to the first layer where the duplicate appears."},{"id":"ch03-e077","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q26: A pipeline runs correctly when YOU run it but fails when it runs scheduled. One of the first things to check?","options":["The Python traceback line numbers","The run identity of the scheduled execution","Whether Photon is enabled","The number of files in Bronze"],"answer":1,"explain":"You may have the permissions, but the scheduled execution identity (often a service principal) may not. Identity is the classic difference between manual and scheduled runs."},{"id":"ch03-e078","type":"bucket","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Group the nine dev-vs-prod differences.","buckets":["WHO","WHAT it may touch","HOW it's configured","WHERE it runs"],"items":[{"text":"identity","bucket":0},{"text":"permissions","bucket":1},{"text":"catalog","bucket":1},{"text":"schema","bucket":1},{"text":"storage credential","bucket":1},{"text":"configuration","bucket":2},{"text":"secret","bucket":2},{"text":"compute","bucket":3},{"text":"runtime","bucket":3}],"explain":"WHO → WHAT → HOW → WHERE is a compact way to remember all nine items from the lesson."},{"id":"ch03-e079","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A job works in DEV. In PROD it fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED`. The code is identical.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"The identity running in prod vs dev","ok":true,"fb":"WHO first — permissions belong to identities."},{"text":"Rewrite the code","ok":false,"fb":"Identical code succeeded in dev; the environment differs."},{"text":"Upgrade the runtime","ok":false,"fb":"PERMISSION_DENIED points at identity/permissions, not runtime."}]},{"prompt":"Prod runs as a service principal. What next?","options":[{"text":"Verify its privileges along the path in the PROD catalog/schema (and storage credential if it touches external storage)","ok":true,"fb":"WHAT it may touch in prod."},{"text":"Give it ALL PRIVILEGES","ok":false,"fb":"Violates least privilege."},{"text":"Switch prod to run as your user","ok":false,"fb":"Ties production to a person."}]},{"prompt":"Privileges look right. The code reads a table name from a parameter. What else could differ?","options":[{"text":"Configuration: the prod parameter may point at a different catalog/schema (e.g. still dev_…)","ok":true,"fb":"HOW it's configured — config, then secrets."},{"text":"Shuffle partitions","ok":false,"fb":"Performance setting, not a permission cause."},{"text":"Nothing else can differ","ok":false,"fb":"Config, secrets, compute and runtime can all differ."}]}],"explain":"Dev and prod differ in identity, permissions, catalog, schema, storage credential, configuration, secret, compute and runtime."},{"id":"ch03-e080","type":"order","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the dev-vs-prod checks using the WHO → WHAT → HOW → WHERE grouping.","items":["Identity (who runs it)","Permissions, catalog, schema, storage credential","Configuration and secrets","Compute and runtime"],"explain":"Start with identity because it changes everything downstream; then what that identity may access; then configuration; finally the compute environment."},{"id":"ch03-e081","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A notebook succeeds when you click Run, but the same notebook as a scheduled Job fails every night.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's one of the first things to check?","options":[{"text":"Under which identity the scheduled run executes","ok":true,"fb":"Official answer: run identity."},{"text":"Whether the notebook has too many cells","ok":false,"fb":"Cell count isn't a failure cause."},{"text":"Whether the schedule time is correct","ok":false,"fb":"It runs — and fails — so scheduling works."}]},{"prompt":"The job runs as a service principal that lacks SELECT on one table. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Grant that specific privilege (and the USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA path) to the service principal","ok":true,"fb":"Least-privilege fix for the actual gap."},{"text":"Change 'Run as' to your own user","ok":false,"fb":"Works until you leave or your permissions change."},{"text":"Give the service principal MANAGE on the catalog","ok":false,"fb":"Far too broad."}]}],"explain":"Manual vs scheduled differ mainly in identity."},{"id":"ch03-e082","type":"order","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your notebook works manually but fails as a scheduled job. Order the checks.","items":["Find the job's run identity","Compare it with the identity used in the manual run","Check that identity's privileges along the catalog → schema → table path","Grant only the missing privilege","Re-run the scheduled job"],"explain":"Identity first, then the privilege path, then a least-privilege fix, then verify."},{"id":"ch03-e083","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"If code works in dev, a failure in prod must be caused by a bug in the code.","answer":false,"explain":"The code may be perfect; identity, permissions, catalog, schema, storage credential, configuration, secrets, compute or runtime may differ."},{"id":"ch03-e084","type":"odd","section":"ch03-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT among the dev-vs-prod differences listed in the lesson?","options":["storage credential","secret","runtime","the developer's keyboard layout"],"answer":3,"explain":"The nine: identity, permissions, catalog, schema, storage credential, configuration, secret, compute, runtime."},{"id":"ch03-e085","type":"match","section":"ch03-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each symptom to its first move.","pairs":[["No new data in Gold","Walk the layers from the source"],["Duplicate records","Find where the second copy first appears"],["Works in dev, fails in prod","Compare environments, starting with identity"],["PERMISSION_DENIED","Walk the privilege path, then check run identity"]],"explain":"All four apply the same principle: find the first point where reality diverges from expectation."},{"id":"ch03-e086","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Every symptom (missing data, duplicates, PERMISSION_DENIED) is itself a root cause you can fix directly.","answer":false,"explain":"They are symptoms. The course's rule: find the first layer where reality diverges, and the mechanism there is the root cause."},{"id":"ch03-e087","type":"order","section":"ch03-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the learning path that follows the prerequisites.","items":["Databricks Part 1 (platform, compute, notebooks)","Delta Lake","Unity Catalog","Ingestion / Auto Loader","Lakeflow","Jobs","Databricks SQL","Performance / Spark UI"],"explain":"The roadmap goes from the platform itself to storage (Delta), governance (UC), ingestion, pipelines, orchestration, SQL, performance and then Bundles/CI-CD, security, production projects and certification drills."},{"id":"ch03-e088","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which topic is part of 'Databricks — Part 1' in the roadmap?","options":["Control plane vs compute plane","AUTO CDC syntax","Bundle targets","Row filters and masks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Part 1 covers the platform basics: account vs workspace, control vs compute plane, compute types, SQL warehouses, Runtime, Photon, notebooks and first debugging."},{"id":"ch03-e703","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"After `REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE company.finance.transactions FROM analysts`, analysts are guaranteed to lose read access to that table.","answer":false,"explain":"False. If `SELECT` was also granted on the parent **schema** or **catalog**, the table inherits it and access remains. Always run SHOW GRANTS on the schema and catalog too (Chapter 11, Case 3).","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e701","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The Phase-3 permission algorithm extends this section's checklist. Which layers does it ADD? (choose all)","options":["Workspace / catalog binding","External-storage permissions (external location, credential, cloud IAM)","UC-compatible compute","USE SCHEMA","Row filters / column masks"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"why":["Added — grants can't override a missing binding.","Added — matters for external tables/volumes.","Added — correct grants on legacy compute still fail.","Already in the checklist.","Already in the checklist."],"explain":"Full order: Identity → Binding → USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → Object → Fine-grained → Storage → Compute. Binding, storage and compute are the new layers; identity also moves to the front, because every grant is evaluated for the executing principal.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e702","type":"order","section":"ch03-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Put the 8 layers of the full PERMISSION_DENIED algorithm in order.","items":["Which principal actually executes?","Workspace / catalog binding","USE CATALOG","USE SCHEMA","Object privilege (SELECT …)","Row filter / column mask / ABAC","External storage (if relevant)","UC-compatible compute"],"explain":"I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions: Identity, Binding, Catalog, Schema, Object, Fine-grained, Storage, Compute.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e704","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"`ALL PRIVILEGES` is a fixed list that includes MANAGE.","answer":false,"explain":"False twice: MANAGE (plus EXTERNAL USE LOCATION / EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA) is excluded, and ALL PRIVILEGES is evaluated dynamically, so it silently grows when new applicable privileges appear.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e801","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"If the Auto Loader query progress shows the new files were read but 0 rows reached Bronze, the stream's load path is the most likely problem.","answer":false,"explain":"If files were read, discovery (and therefore the path) worked. The break is later in the chain: parsing (format/extension mismatch, rescued or corrupt records), a schema-change stop, or the sink write.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e802","type":"scenario","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Silver shows order_id 100 twice. A colleague blames Auto Loader.","steps":[{"prompt":"The two Bronze rows have different `_source_file` values. What does that tell you?","options":[{"text":"Auto Loader processed two distinct files once each; the duplicate comes from the source","ok":true,"fb":"Right: file-level exactly-once ≠ business-key dedup."},{"text":"Auto Loader ingested the same file twice","ok":false,"fb":"Then `_source_file` would be identical."},{"text":"The checkpoint was deleted","ok":false,"fb":"A reset re-ingests the SAME files — same `_source_file`, new `_ingested_at`."}]},{"prompt":"Where do you fix it?","options":[{"text":"Silver: dedupe by order_id and MERGE on the business key","ok":true,"fb":"Business-key idempotency belongs downstream."},{"text":"Delete one of the source files","ok":false,"fb":"Never destroy source evidence."},{"text":"Reset the Auto Loader checkpoint","ok":false,"fb":"That creates real duplicates."}]}],"src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e901","type":"tf","section":"ch03-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"When a streaming query stops making progress, the safest first move is to restart it with a fresh checkpoint location.","answer":false,"explain":"No. First inspect `query.status`/`lastProgress` and walk the checklist. A new checkpoint makes the query brand new → reprocessing and duplicates, and a restart can destroy the evidence.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch03-e902","type":"mcq","section":"ch03-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A streaming job writes to Delta (exactly-once sink) AND posts each order to a CRM inside foreachBatch. After a crash, the CRM shows order 123 twice; Delta doesn't. Most likely cause?","options":["Delta's exactly-once guarantee failed","The retried batch re-ran a non-idempotent external side effect","The watermark was too short","Complete output mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"The retried micro-batch re-invoked foreachBatch, and the CRM call isn't covered by Delta/checkpoint transactions. Exactly-once stops at non-transactional edges — make the side effect idempotent (business key/version).","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch04","num":4,"title":"Databricks Platform Tour","subtitle":"Account, workspace, planes, compute, runtime, Photon, notebooks, widgets, catalogs — and first debugging","emoji":"🧭","sourcePages":"76–131","mantra":"Workspace is where you work, compute is what works, DBR is the software on it, Photon makes it fast, Unity Catalog names and guards the data — debug by finding the layer first.","objectives":["You can explain Databricks as a platform (not 'managed Spark') and separate account vs workspace and control vs compute plane.","You can choose between serverless, classic all-purpose, jobs compute and SQL warehouses for a given workload.","You can explain Databricks Runtime, LTS, Runtime ML and Photon — and why Photon needs no code changes.","You can write notebook code mixing Python and SQL: spark.table, spark.sql, display, magic commands and _sqldf.","You can parameterize notebooks with dbutils.widgets and modern :param / IDENTIFIER() SQL syntax.","You can explore and create catalogs, schemas and Delta tables with SHOW/DESCRIBE/CREATE/INSERT and fully qualified names.","You can debug by layer: not attached, TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND, INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES, ModuleNotFoundError, slow queries."],"sections":[{"id":"ch04-s01","title":"What Databricks Actually Is","hook":"If you think \"Databricks = managed Spark\", every product name will feel random.","blocks":[{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Workspace, Serverless, Compute, SQL Warehouse, Databricks Runtime, Photon, Notebook, Unity Catalog — 8 unrelated products?","text":"No. They are **layers of one model**: where you work (workspace, notebook), what executes (compute: serverless/classic/SQL warehouse), the software on the machines (Runtime), the fast engine (Photon), and who may touch what (Unity Catalog). This chapter builds that model."},{"t":"p","text":"The most dangerous oversimplification is **Databricks = managed Spark**. Historically that was close to the truth. Today it is not."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks is a **unified data/AI platform**: data engineering, analytics, AI/ML, governance and deployment in one environment. **Apache Spark** is still the central *execution technology* inside it."},{"t":"list","items":["**Delta Lake** — the table format","**Unity Catalog** — governance","**SQL warehouses** — SQL compute","**Lakeflow** — pipelines/ingestion","**Serverless compute**","**Photon** — vectorized engine","ML/AI services, orchestration (Jobs), DevOps tooling"],"ordered":false},{"t":"diagram","text":"┌──────────────────── DATABRICKS ────────────────────┐\n│   Notebooks        SQL Editor        Jobs          │\n│       └────────────────┼────────────────┘          │\n│                        ▼                           │\n│              COMPUTE  (Spark / Photon)             │\n│                        ▼                           │\n│              Delta / other data                    │\n│                        ▼                           │\n│              Cloud storage                         │\n│                                                    │\n│   Unity Catalog governs all of these assets        │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘","caption":"Interfaces on top, compute in the middle, data at the bottom, governance across everything."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Spark","points":["Mainly an **execution engine**","Runs transformations, SQL, joins, aggregations","One technology inside Databricks"]},{"title":"Databricks","points":["A whole **data/AI platform**","UI, governance, compute management, orchestration, SQL, ML","Uses Spark (and Photon) to execute work"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Engine vs car","text":"Spark is the **engine**. Databricks is the whole car: engine, dashboard, keys and locks (governance), navigation (orchestration) and the garage that maintains it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Classic trap","text":"Spark is **not** a cloud provider and **not** a file format. Databricks and Spark are **not** synonyms: Spark is one of the core execution technologies *inside* Databricks."}]},{"id":"ch04-s02","title":"Account vs Workspace","hook":"The first boundary every exam and every admin conversation starts with.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a company, **ACME Corporation**, buys Databricks. At the very top sits the **Databricks account** — the organization-level boundary."},{"t":"p","text":"At **account level** you manage users, groups and service principals, workspaces, Unity Catalog metastores, billing and account-level policies. One account can contain **many workspaces**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"DATABRICKS ACCOUNT  (organization level)\n│\n├── identities (users, groups, service principals)\n├── billing\n├── account policies\n├── Unity Catalog configuration (metastores)\n│\n├── Workspace A\n├── Workspace B\n└── Workspace C","caption":"One account → many workspaces."},{"t":"p","text":"A **workspace** is the operational/collaboration environment where users actually work. Databricks describes it as the environment where workloads such as ingestion, interactive analysis, scheduled jobs and ML training run."},{"t":"list","items":["notebooks","files","Git folders","jobs","pipelines","SQL queries","dashboards","compute","Catalog Explorer"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"ACME might have one account with three workspaces: `analytics-prod`, `engineering-dev`, `research`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Azure tenant mental model","text":"**Azure tenant** ≈ organization-wide level; **Databricks account** ≈ organization-wide Databricks level; **workspace** ≈ a specific environment where teams and workloads operate. Not a perfect 1:1 mapping, but a useful mental model."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Data engineer (daily life)","points":["Opens `https://....databricks....`","Sees Workspace, Catalog, Jobs & Pipelines, Compute, SQL…","Lives mostly in the **workspace**"]},{"title":"Admin (account level)","points":["Create workspace","Configure SSO","Add account group","Manage billing"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["Operation","Where it most likely happens"],"rows":[["Create a notebook","Workspace"],["Create an organization-wide identity","Account-level administration"],["Run a Spark query","Workspace workload, executed on compute"],["Create a second workspace","Account level"]],"caption":"Source exercise 2 — memorize the pattern, not the rows."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Which is higher?","text":"The **account** is the higher organizational object. A workspace never contains an account; an account contains workspaces."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → workspace ≠ metastore","text":"Usually **one metastore per region**; many workspaces attach to it and share governance. A notebook lives in a workspace, but `prod.sales.orders` is registered in the metastore — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch04-s03","title":"Control Plane vs Compute Plane","hook":"Knowing which plane does what tells you where to look when something breaks.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Databricks architecture explicitly separates two planes: the **control plane** and the **compute plane**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"CONTROL PLANE — \"manages and coordinates\"","points":["Databricks-managed backend services","Web UI (the web app lives here)","APIs","Workspace metadata","Job orchestration metadata","Configuration, platform coordination"]},{"title":"COMPUTE PLANE — \"does the work\"","points":["Actual data processing","Spark transformations","SQL execution","Joins, aggregations","ML computation"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Restaurant","text":"The **control plane** is the front desk and manager: takes the order, schedules the kitchen, keeps the books. The **compute plane** is the kitchen where the food (your data) is actually cooked."},{"t":"p","text":"Follow one command. In a notebook you write:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\").show()"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Browser\n   │\n   ▼\nDatabricks control services   (control plane)\n   │  schedule / coordinate execution\n   ▼\nCompute plane\n   ├── Driver / Spark session\n   ├── Workers\n   └── Photon / Spark execution\n   │\n   ▼\nData storage","caption":"The path of one notebook command."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Who actually runs groupBy?","text":"Your **browser does not** run the groupBy. The **control plane is not** where the Spark workload runs. The computation happens in the **compute plane**."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Browser → Databricks UI → Spark query → ADLS. Which two planes are involved?","text":"The UI / platform coordination belongs to the **control plane**; the Spark computation happens in the **compute plane** (which then reads the data in ADLS storage)."}]},{"id":"ch04-s04","title":"Classic vs Serverless Compute","hook":"Two compute planes, one fundamental trade-off: control vs convenience.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Today there are two main kinds of compute plane: the **classic compute plane** and the **serverless compute plane**. Databricks documentation separates them explicitly."},{"t":"p","text":"With **classic compute** you create and configure the resources yourself — a driver VM plus worker VMs — and choose many infrastructure characteristics."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Your cloud environment  (e.g. YOUR AWS account)\n│\n├── Driver VM\n├── Worker VM\n├── Worker VM\n└── Worker VM","caption":"Classic: resources live in the customer's cloud account (AWS; similar on Azure/GCP)."},{"t":"p","text":"Classic = **more control**, but also **more operational responsibility**."},{"t":"p","text":"With **serverless** you say \"I want compute to run this workload\" — not \"give me 1 driver with X RAM and 8 workers of this VM type\". Databricks takes over most of the provisioning and scaling."},{"t":"diagram","text":"YOU: run notebook\n        │\n        ▼\nDatabricks\n  ├── allocates compute\n  ├── scales it\n  ├── manages the runtime\n  └── tears down / scales resources as appropriate"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Current recommendation","text":"Databricks today recommends **serverless compute for most interactive and automated workloads**, when it is available and supports your requirements."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Serverless ≠ \"no servers\"","text":"Of course there are servers, CPUs and RAM. **Serverless** means *you* don't manage the underlying server infrastructure the same way — the **abstraction boundary moved**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"SERVERLESS","points":["Less infrastructure to manage","Fast provisioning","Automatic scaling","Platform optimizations"]},{"title":"CLASSIC","points":["More control","Custom configuration","Special API / runtime / network needs"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Not \"serverless good, classic useless\"","text":"Some workloads **still need classic**. Serverless is a default recommendation, not a verdict that classic is obsolete."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Serverless limitations (current)","text":"Serverless compute **does not support R**, **does not support Spark RDD APIs**, and is built on **Spark Connect** APIs. Old code like `spark.sparkContext.parallelize([1,2,3])` should not be assumed to work in a serverless notebook."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"rdd = spark.sparkContext.parallelize([1, 2, 3])   # RDD API → needs classic compute","caption":"Don't assume this runs on serverless."},{"t":"p","text":"**\"Cluster\" vs \"compute\"**: older tutorials say *cluster* everywhere; today's UI/docs prefer the broader word **compute**. Classic Spark compute still has the familiar shape:"},{"t":"diagram","text":"            DRIVER\n              │\n     ┌────────┼────────┐\n     ▼        ▼        ▼\n  worker   worker   worker\n\njob → stage → task → partition","caption":"Everything you know from Spark still applies on classic compute."}]},{"id":"ch04-s05","title":"Compute Types at a Glance: All-Purpose, Jobs, SQL Warehouse","hook":"There is no \"the Databricks cluster\" — there are compute types for different workload shapes.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**All-purpose compute** (classic) is used mostly for **interactive development**: several notebooks can attach to the same compute."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Notebook A ─┐\nNotebook B ─┼──► All-purpose compute\nNotebook C ─┘","caption":"Good for development, interactive exploration, debugging."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"All-purpose for automated jobs?","text":"All-purpose compute is **not** Databricks' first recommendation for automated Jobs. For jobs it recommends **serverless** in most cases, or **classic jobs compute** when you need custom cluster characteristics."},{"t":"p","text":"**Jobs compute**: a pipeline runs at 02:00 (ingest → transform → aggregate). You don't need an interactive cluster open all day — compute exists for the job."},{"t":"flow","items":["Job starts","Compute available / provisioned","Tasks run","Workload finishes"],"caption":"Jobs compute lifecycle"},{"t":"p","text":"Deeper later (Jobs chapters): serverless jobs, classic jobs compute, shared job compute, task-level compute."},{"t":"p","text":"A **SQL warehouse** is a compute resource **optimized for SQL analytics**, used by the SQL editor, dashboards, BI tools and other SQL workloads."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT country, SUM(revenue)\nFROM prod.gold.sales\nGROUP BY country;","caption":"A typical query a SQL warehouse executes."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Power BI ─────┐\n              │\nSQL Editor ───┼──► SQL Warehouse ───► tables\n              │\nDashboard ────┘"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"SQL warehouse ≠ database","text":"Here **warehouse** means a **compute resource**, not the place where tables are stored. Tables keep their own data/storage layer; the warehouse only **executes queries**."},{"t":"p","text":"SQL warehouse types today: **Serverless**, **Pro**, **Classic**. There is also **Lakehouse Real-Time** (Beta) for specific low-latency/high-concurrency cases. For most SQL analytics, Databricks recommends a **serverless SQL warehouse** when available."},{"t":"table","head":["Workload","Likely compute"],"rows":[["Notebook: `spark.read.table(\"prod.sales.orders\")`","Serverless notebook compute or classic all-purpose compute"],["SQL dashboard: `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM prod.sales.orders`","SQL warehouse"],["Scheduled Python ETL","Serverless jobs compute or classic jobs compute"]],"caption":"Different compute types for different workload shapes."},{"t":"flow","items":["Interactive Python/DataFrames → Serverless notebook compute","Interactive SQL / BI → Serverless SQL warehouse","Automated Python/SQL job → Serverless Jobs","Custom cluster config / unsupported feature → Classic","RDD / R requirement → Classic"],"caption":"Compute-selection mental model (interview/exam)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Why a SQL warehouse instead of general Spark compute?","text":"*A SQL warehouse is purpose-built for SQL analytics and BI workloads and provides SQL-oriented performance, concurrency and scaling capabilities, whereas general notebook or classic compute is suited to broader Spark and programmatic workloads.*"}]},{"id":"ch04-s06","title":"Databricks Runtime (DBR), LTS and Runtime ML","hook":"Hardware alone runs nothing: the Runtime is the software that makes compute behave a certain way.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You have a compute machine. Hardware alone is not enough — you need a **software environment**: Spark, Python, Scala, JVM, Delta libraries, Databricks integrations, optimized libraries, system dependencies."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks packages a **tested software stack** called the **Databricks Runtime (DBR)**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Compute VM            (hardware / infrastructure)\n│\n└── Databricks Runtime  (software)\n      ├── Apache Spark\n      ├── Delta support\n      ├── Python environment\n      ├── libraries\n      ├── Databricks optimizations\n      └── integrations"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"VM ≠ DBR","text":"**VM / compute resources** = hardware/infrastructure abstraction. **DBR** = software runtime environment. Same VM type + different DBR can behave differently."},{"t":"p","text":"You will see versions like `17.3 LTS`, `18.x`, `19 Beta`. (In June 2026 the source reports DBR 19 Beta on Spark 4.2.0, with LTS lines like 17.3 and 16.4 still getting maintenance.) **Don't memorize numbers** — understand the consequence:"},{"t":"flow","items":["Different DBR","different Spark version","different Python / packages","different capabilities & bug fixes","different behavior"]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"LTS","def":"**Long-Term Support** runtime. Preferred in production when you want stability, predictable maintenance and less frequent migration instead of chasing every new release."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Runtime debugging story**: DEV runs DBR 18.x, PROD runs DBR 17.3 LTS. `some_new_api(...)` works in dev but raises `AttributeError` in prod. Likely root cause: **runtime version mismatch**."},{"t":"flow","items":["code","+ runtime","+ libraries","+ configuration"],"caption":"Production debugging is never just \"does the Python code have a bug?\""},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when code works in DEV but fails in PROD","questions":["Which DBR version runs in DEV, and which in PROD?","Does the failing API/library exist in the older runtime?","Are the Python libraries/versions the same in both environments?","Is the configuration (compute, settings) the same?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning (DBR ML)** adds prebuilt ML/DL infrastructure and common ML libraries so you don't build the whole environment yourself."},{"t":"flow","items":["Standard DBR","+ ML libraries","+ ML/DL infrastructure","= DBR ML"]}]},{"id":"ch04-s07","title":"Photon: The Vectorized Engine Under Your Code","hook":"Faster SQL/DataFrames without rewriting a single line.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Spark** is an execution framework / engine ecosystem. **Photon** is the **Databricks-native vectorized query engine**, designed to accelerate SQL and DataFrame workloads."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"What Photon is NOT","text":"Photon is **not** another programming language, **not** a database and **not** a storage format."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without Photon","points":["SQL / DataFrame","↓ Spark plan","↓ Spark/JVM execution"]},{"title":"With Photon (supported ops)","points":["SQL / DataFrame","↓ query plan","↓ Photon-native execution"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"The key point: you write the **same SQL/DataFrame code**. The optimization happens **beneath the API abstraction**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"result = (\n    spark.table(\"prod.sales.orders\")\n    .groupBy(\"country\")\n    .sum(\"amount\")\n)\n# NOT: photon.groupBy(...)  ← no such API"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why would processing a batch of values be faster than one row at a time?","text":"Row-at-a-time pays per-row overhead (row 1 → process, row 2 → process…). **Vectorized** processing works on a batch `[v1 v2 v3 v4 …]` at once, exploiting CPU characteristics — less overhead, better CPU efficiency. (C++/SIMD internals come in the performance chapter.)"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Row-at-a-time:   row1 → process\n                 row2 → process\n                 row3 → process\n\nVectorized:      [v1 v2 v3 v4 ...] → operate on the whole batch"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Where Photon is on","text":"Photon is **enabled on serverless compute and SQL warehouses**. On **classic** compute it is also available and usually enabled by default through the corresponding configuration."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What is Photon?","text":"*Photon is Databricks' native vectorized execution engine that accelerates supported SQL and DataFrame operations underneath the existing APIs, so applications generally do not need a separate Photon-specific programming model.*"}]},{"id":"ch04-s08","title":"Notebooks: Cells, Compute Attachment, display() vs show()","hook":"A notebook is a document — it does nothing until it is attached to compute.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Databricks **notebook** is an interactive document made of **cells** — e.g. Cell 1 Python, Cell 2 SQL, Cell 3 Markdown, Cell 4 Python. Notebooks support **Python, SQL, Scala and R**, depending on the compute/runtime."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The notebook alone executes nothing","text":"A notebook is a code/document **artifact**. Even `print(\"hello\")` needs an **active execution session** on compute."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Notebook\n   │  attach / connect\n   ▼\nCompute\n   │\n   ▼\nexecution"},{"t":"p","text":"In **Unity Catalog-enabled workspaces**, Databricks can **auto-attach** a new notebook to **serverless compute** when you run a cell without having chosen other compute."},{"t":"p","text":"Your first real notebook cell:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"data = [\n    (1, \"Athens\", 120.0),\n    (2, \"Volos\", 80.0),\n    (3, \"Athens\", 50.0),\n    (4, \"Patras\", 200.0),\n    (5, \"Athens\", 30.0),\n]\n\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    data,\n    [\"order_id\", \"city\", \"amount\"]\n)\n\ndisplay(df)"},{"t":"p","text":"You never created `spark` — in a Databricks notebook a ready-made **Spark session** exists on supported compute."},{"t":"flow","items":["Python list","spark.createDataFrame()","Spark DataFrame"],"caption":"Inferred schema: order_id: long · city: string · amount: double"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"df.show()","points":["**Spark API** (core PySpark)","Text-oriented output","Works anywhere Spark runs"]},{"title":"display(df)","points":["**Databricks notebook/platform helper**","Richer rendering, easy table/visualizations","Not a core PySpark DataFrame method"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Porting code out of Databricks","text":"`display()` is a notebook convenience. If you move code outside Databricks (plain PySpark, a library, a test), `display` won't exist — use Spark APIs such as `df.show()`."}]},{"id":"ch04-s09","title":"Transformations, Physical Plans and Lazy Evaluation","hook":"Databricks doesn't hide your Spark knowledge — it leans on it.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ncity_sales = (\n    df\n    .groupBy(\"city\")\n    .agg(\n        F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total_sales\"),\n        F.count(\"*\").alias(\"orders\")\n    )\n    .orderBy(F.desc(\"total_sales\"))\n)\n\ndisplay(city_sales)"},{"t":"table","head":["city","total_sales","orders"],"rows":[["Athens","200.0","3"],["Patras","200.0","1"],["Volos","80.0","1"]],"caption":"Athens = 120 + 50 + 30. (Athens and Patras tie at 200.0, so their relative order is not guaranteed.)"},{"t":"p","text":"`groupBy(\"city\")` most likely needs a **shuffle**, so that all rows with the same city key end up in the same downstream partition."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"city_sales.explain(\"formatted\")\n# or\ncity_sales.explain(\"extended\")","caption":"See the physical plan."},{"t":"diagram","text":"HashAggregate   (final)\nExchange        ← redistribution / shuffle boundary\nHashAggregate   (partial, per partition)\nSort","caption":"Operators you will typically see for groupBy + orderBy."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Exchange = shuffle","text":"In a physical plan, **Exchange** is usually where you see a redistribution/shuffle boundary."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why does `df.filter(F.col(\"amount\") > 100)` usually need no shuffle, while `df.groupBy(\"city\").sum(\"amount\")` probably does?","text":"A **filter** can be decided independently inside each existing partition (partition 1 → filter locally, partition 2 → filter locally…). For **groupBy(\"city\")**, all rows with `city = Athens` must be combined logically even if they started in different partitions → **redistribution/shuffle**."},{"t":"p","text":"**What really happens when you press Run** (interview level):"},{"t":"flow","items":["Notebook cell","Python runs in active compute session","Spark builds logical transformations","Action/display needs a result","Catalyst analysis & optimization","Physical execution plan","Spark / Photon execution","Tasks process partitions (scan, filter, shuffle for groupBy)"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Lazy evaluation remains**: `orders = spark.table(...)`, `x = orders.filter(...)`, `y = x.groupBy(...).sum(...)` only **describe** computation."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"display(y)      # action\ny.count()       # action\ny.write...      # action","caption":"These force evaluation. Databricks doesn't change this basic Spark logic."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"filter() ≠ processed","text":"After `df.filter(...)` no data has necessarily been processed yet: the transformation only extends the plan. Execution happens when an action/result is requested."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Lazy evaluation applies to streams too: `spark.readStream...load()` only builds a plan; the query starts at `writeStream ... toTable()/start()`. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 2.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch04-s10","title":"Magic Commands, spark.table(), spark.sql() and _sqldf","hook":"One notebook, several languages — and they all meet at the same tables.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Say the notebook's **default language is Python**. A cell starting with **`%sql`** means: *interpret this cell as SQL*."},{"t":"table","head":["Magic","What the cell becomes"],"rows":[["`%python`","Python cell"],["`%sql`","SQL cell"],["`%scala`","Scala cell"],["`%r`","R cell"],["`%md`","Markdown (documentation) cell"],["`%pip`","Install notebook-scoped Python libraries"]],"caption":"Magic commands listed in the source."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"%python\nx = 5\nprint(x * 2)"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"%sql\nSELECT current_timestamp() AS now;"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"%md\n# Sales Analysis\nThis is the daily sales notebook."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"%pip install requests"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"%pip is notebook-scoped","text":"`%pip` installs **notebook-scoped** Python libraries (scoped to the notebook/session context). Don't assume it is a permanent installation for the whole workspace."},{"t":"p","text":"**SQL and Python in the same flow**: both of these can point to the same logical table `main.sales.orders`."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Python","points":["`orders = spark.table(\"main.sales.orders\")`","`display(orders)`"]},{"title":"SQL","points":["`%sql`","`SELECT * FROM main.sales.orders;`"]}]},{"t":"flow","items":["UC name prod.silver.orders","resolve metadata","table","Spark DataFrame"],"caption":"spark.table(\"catalog.schema.table\")"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.sql(\"\"\"\n    SELECT country, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n    FROM prod.silver.orders\n    GROUP BY country\n\"\"\")\ndisplay(df)","caption":"spark.sql() returns a DataFrame: SQL statement → Spark SQL → DataFrame."},{"t":"p","text":"**`_sqldf`**: in supported notebook environments, the result of a `%sql` cell becomes available to **subsequent Python** code as `_sqldf`."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"%sql\nSELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders WHERE amount > 100;"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"display(_sqldf)   # next Python cell: result of the SQL cell above"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Interactive only","text":"`_sqldf` is a handy interactive feature. **Don't build production architecture on it** — use `spark.sql()` / `spark.table()` and named DataFrames."}]},{"id":"ch04-s11","title":"Widgets and SQL Parameters (:param, IDENTIFIER)","hook":"Stop editing code to change a country — parameterize the notebook.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You want the notebook to run for different countries. **Bad**: `country = \"GR\"` and edit the code by hand each time."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"dbutils.widgets.dropdown(\n    \"country\",          # name\n    \"GR\",               # default value\n    [\"GR\", \"DE\", \"FR\"], # choices\n    \"Country\"           # label shown in the UI\n)\n\ncountry = dbutils.widgets.get(\"country\")\n\nfiltered = (\n    spark.table(\"prod.silver.orders\")\n    .filter(F.col(\"country\") == country)\n)\ndisplay(filtered)","caption":"Create → read → use."},{"t":"flow","items":["Create widget (dbutils.widgets.dropdown/text)","Read it (dbutils.widgets.get)","Use the value in filter/SQL","display()"]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Widgets","def":"Notebook input controls for **parameterized notebooks/dashboards**. Types include **text**, **dropdown**, **combobox** (and others, e.g. multiselect)."},{"term":"dbutils.widgets.get(name)","def":"Returns the widget's current value (as a string) to Python code."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Modern SQL parameter markers** use **`:parameter`** — not the older **`${parameter}`**, which is **deprecated for DBR 15.2+**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"dbutils.widgets.text(\"country\", \"GR\")","caption":"Python cell"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"%sql\nSELECT *\nFROM prod.silver.orders\nWHERE country = :country;","caption":"SQL cell — modern parameter marker"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":":country, not ${country}","text":"Learn today's syntax, not old tutorials: `:country` is current; `${country}` is legacy/deprecated."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a widget holds `table = orders`. Why can't you just write `SELECT * FROM :table`?","text":"A **parameter value** and a **SQL identifier** are different semantic categories. `WHERE country = :country` inserts a *value*; `FROM ...` needs an *identifier* (an object name). Use **`IDENTIFIER(:table_name)`** for parameterized object names."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *\nFROM IDENTIFIER(:table_name);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Value vs identifier","text":"Parameter markers stand for **values**. For table/column/schema names use **`IDENTIFIER()`**. Literal vs identifier vs parameter vs expression is a frequent source of SQL bugs (covered in depth later)."}]},{"id":"ch04-s12","title":"Catalog Explorer, SHOW/DESCRIBE and Your First Delta Table","hook":"Find data by clicking, confirm it by SQL — then create a real table.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On the left of the workspace you'll find **Catalog / Catalog Explorer**: discovery of Unity Catalog objects."},{"t":"diagram","text":"catalog\n  └── schema\n        ├── tables\n        ├── views\n        ├── volumes\n        ├── functions\n        └── models ...\n\nprod\n├── bronze\n│   └── raw_orders\n├── silver\n│   └── orders\n└── gold\n    └── daily_sales"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Discovery needs privileges","text":"Catalog Explorer requires suitable privileges such as **BROWSE** and/or the corresponding **data access privileges** to see objects."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SHOW CATALOGS;\nSHOW SCHEMAS IN prod;\nSHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;\nDESCRIBE TABLE prod.silver.orders;\nDESCRIBE DETAIL prod.silver.orders;","caption":"Drill down level by level. These become your basic debugging tools."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"DESCRIBE TABLE","def":"Shows the table's columns and data types."},{"term":"DESCRIBE DETAIL","def":"Shows table-level details (format, location, size, number of files…)."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS dev_training.sales;","caption":"Catalog dev_training, schema sales."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE dev_training.sales.orders (\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      DECIMAL(10,2),\n  country     STRING,\n  order_ts    TIMESTAMP\n)\nUSING DELTA;"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Unity Catalog\n   │  name / metadata / permissions\n   ▼\ndev_training.sales.orders\n   │  table format\n   ▼\nDelta\n   │  files + transaction state\n   ▼\nstorage","caption":"Connect the layers: UC names & governs, Delta formats, storage holds bytes."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT INTO dev_training.sales.orders\nVALUES\n  (1, 101, 20.50, 'GR', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()),\n  (2, 102, 90.00, 'DE', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()),\n  (3, 103, 15.25, 'GR', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP());\n\nSELECT * FROM dev_training.sales.orders;","caption":"A real table workflow — not a toy DataFrame living only in RAM."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"orders = spark.table(\"dev_training.sales.orders\")\n\ngreek_orders = orders.filter(F.col(\"country\") == \"GR\")\ndisplay(greek_orders)\n\n(orders\n  .groupBy(\"country\")\n  .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n  .display())          # if .display() isn't supported, use display(...)","caption":"The same table from Python."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Compute lifetime ≠ data lifetime","text":"If compute stops, your Delta table is **not** lost — it lives in persistent storage. Compute can come and go; data persists."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Catalog Explorer as governance UI","text":"Per object you can see schema, details, **permissions, history, lineage, tags, dependencies**; BROWSE lets users discover objects without data access — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch04-s13","title":"Namespaces and Fully Qualified Names","hook":"`orders` might silently mean dev — `prod.silver.orders` can't.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"`prod.silver.orders` is a **Unity Catalog three-level namespace**: **catalog.schema.table**."},{"t":"table","head":["Part","Level"],"rows":[["`prod`","catalog"],["`silver`","schema"],["`orders`","object / table"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Good production habit: write **`catalog.schema.table`** instead of just `orders`."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: your current context is `dev.sales` and you run `SELECT * FROM orders;`. What do you read?","text":"You read **`dev.sales.orders`** — while you may have believed you were reading `prod.sales.orders`. No error, just the wrong data. Very dangerous."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT current_catalog();\nSELECT current_schema();","caption":"Check the current namespace."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"USE CATALOG prod;\nUSE SCHEMA silver;\n\nSELECT * FROM orders;   -- now resolves to prod.silver.orders"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Unqualified names resolve against the current context","text":"A short name is resolved using the **current catalog and schema**. For production transformations prefer the fully qualified `prod.silver.orders`."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before running a query with a short table name","questions":["What are current_catalog() and current_schema() right now?","Could the same table name exist in dev and prod?","Would a fully qualified name remove any doubt?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → catalog.schema.object","text":"The third name can be a table, view, **volume, function, model**, materialized view or streaming table — so the general form is `catalog.schema.object`. Catalog/schema design and creation privileges are covered — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch04-s14","title":"Debugging by Layer: Cases A–D","hook":"Find the failing layer first — don't jump to shuffles when the notebook isn't even attached.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A **notebook cell is stuck**. Don't start by inspecting Spark shuffle. First decide **which layer** the failure is in."},{"t":"flow","items":["Compute attached?","Compute running?","Session healthy?","Code parsed?","Table exists?","Permission?","Query plan?","Runtime execution?"],"caption":"The layer ladder — top to bottom."},{"t":"table","head":["Case","Symptom","Layer","First move"],"rows":[["A","notebook not attached","Execution environment (not Spark code)","Attach / start compute"],["B","`TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND`","Naming / namespace","`SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;`"],["C","`INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES`","Governance / authorization","Which principal? USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT?"],["D","`ModuleNotFoundError`","Runtime / environment / dependency","`%pip install` (notebook) or managed deps (prod)"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"spark.table(\"prod.silver.ordres\")   # → TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND","caption":"Case B: typo 'ordres' instead of 'orders' — or wrong catalog/schema."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself on TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND","questions":["Does SHOW TABLES IN <catalog>.<schema> list the table?","Did I spell the table name exactly (ordres vs orders)?","Am I in the catalog/schema I think I am?","Did I use a fully qualified name?"]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT * FROM prod.finance.transactions;  -- → INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES","caption":"Case C"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself on INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES / PERMISSION_DENIED","questions":["Which principal am I (or is the job running as)?","Do I have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Do I have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Do I have SELECT on the table?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Wrong layer, wrong tool","text":"A permission error is a **governance** problem — you don't look at Spark partitions. A `ModuleNotFoundError` is an **environment** problem — not a data problem."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"import some_library        # ModuleNotFoundError\n%pip install some-library   # notebook fix","caption":"Case D. In production, manage dependencies systematically, not with ad-hoc %pip."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Works when I run it, fails as a scheduled job","text":"Notebook works for you, but the scheduled job gets `PERMISSION_DENIED`. First serious hypothesis: a **different execution identity/principal** runs the job."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Works in DEV, ModuleNotFoundError in PROD","text":"Check early: (1) the **Databricks Runtime version**, (2) the **Python/library environment**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → six permission-debugging cases","text":"Wrong identity, SELECT without USE SCHEMA, revoked-but-inherited, external-storage failures, volume READ/WRITE VOLUME and masked values each get a playbook — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch04-s15","title":"Slow Queries: Case E and the Debugging Tree","hook":"\"It's slow, get a bigger cluster\" is a guess, not a diagnosis.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Case E — the query runs but is slow.** *Now* you start looking at Spark."},{"t":"list","items":["Physical plan","Spark UI","tasks","shuffle","skew","spill","input bytes","partition counts","join strategy"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Slow ≠ small cluster","text":"A slow query does **not** automatically mean the cluster is too small. That habit should disappear from day one."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"result = (\n    spark.table(\"prod.silver.orders\")\n    .join(spark.table(\"prod.silver.customers\"), \"customer_id\")\n    .groupBy(\"country\")\n    .agg(F.sum(\"amount\"))\n)\ndisplay(result)     # runs for 40 minutes","caption":"A real debugging case."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Is compute actually healthy?\n            │\n            ▼\nHow much input data?\n            │\n            ▼\nWhat physical join?\n     ┌──────┴──────┐\n     ▼             ▼\n   BHJ?          SMJ?\n     │             │\n     ▼             ▼\n reasonable?   huge shuffle?\n                   │\n                   ▼\n                 skew?\n                   │\n                   ▼\n             spill to disk?\n                   │\n                   ▼\n         tiny files / bad layout?","caption":"BHJ = broadcast hash join, SMJ = sort-merge join. Real Spark UI examples come later."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a junior says \"we need a bigger cluster\". What's wrong with that reasoning?","text":"No **root cause** has been identified. It could be skew, a wrong join, a huge shuffle, spill, poor table layout, small files, an unnecessary scan, a bad query, insufficient statistics — or a genuine compute bottleneck. Cluster sizing is only **one** possibility."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a query is slow","questions":["Is the compute actually healthy?","How much input data am I really reading?","Which physical join did Spark pick (BHJ or SMJ)?","Is there a huge shuffle (Exchange)?","Is the data skewed?","Are tasks spilling to disk?","Are there tiny files or a bad table layout?"]}]},{"id":"ch04-s16","title":"Mini-Project, Mental Buckets and Recap","hook":"Put it all together in one parameterized notebook — then sort every term into the right bucket.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dev_training.sales.orders (\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  city        STRING,\n  amount      DECIMAL(10,2),\n  order_date  DATE\n) USING DELTA;\n\nINSERT INTO dev_training.sales.orders VALUES\n (1, 101, 'Athens', 100.00, DATE'2026-09-10'),\n (2, 102, 'Volos',   80.00, DATE'2026-09-10'),\n (3, 101, 'Athens',  30.00, DATE'2026-09-11'),\n (4, 103, 'Patras', 150.00, DATE'2026-09-11'),\n (5, 104, 'Athens', 200.00, DATE'2026-09-12'),\n (6, 102, 'Volos',   40.00, DATE'2026-09-12');","caption":"Setup (works on Free / normal workspaces, depending on your access)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- Task A: revenue per city\nSELECT city, SUM(amount) AS revenue\nFROM dev_training.sales.orders\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY revenue DESC;"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"# Task B: same in PySpark\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\norders = spark.table(\"dev_training.sales.orders\")\ncity_revenue = (\n    orders.groupBy(\"city\")\n    .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n    .orderBy(F.desc(\"revenue\"))\n)\ndisplay(city_revenue)\n\n# Task C: physical plan → look for Exchange and Aggregate\ncity_revenue.explain(\"formatted\")"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"# Task D: parameter\ndbutils.widgets.dropdown(\"city\", \"Athens\", [\"Athens\", \"Volos\", \"Patras\"])\ncity = dbutils.widgets.get(\"city\")\nresult = (\n    spark.table(\"dev_training.sales.orders\")\n    .filter(F.col(\"city\") == city)\n)\ndisplay(result)"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"%sql\n-- Task E: SQL version with the modern marker\nSELECT *\nFROM dev_training.sales.orders\nWHERE city = :city;"},{"t":"table","head":["When you see…","Think…"],"rows":[["`spark.table(...)`","Spark API"],["`display(...)`","Databricks notebook convenience"],["`prod.silver.orders`","Unity Catalog namespace"],["Serverless","Databricks-managed compute infrastructure"],["SQL Warehouse","SQL-optimized compute"],["Photon","Vectorized native execution engine"],["DBR 17.3","Software/runtime environment"]],"caption":"Not everything goes into one mental bucket called \"Databricks stuff\"."},{"t":"diagram","text":"ACCOUNT\n  └── WORKSPACE\n        ├── Notebook\n        ├── SQL Editor\n        ├── Jobs\n        └── Pipelines\n              │\n              ▼\n           COMPUTE\n        ┌─────┴─────┐\n   Serverless    Classic\n        └─────┬─────┘\n       Spark / Photon\n              │\n              ▼\n        Delta Tables\n              │\n              ▼\n      Persistent Storage","caption":"Your first Databricks execution model."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Python you now know","points":["`spark.table(...)`","`spark.sql(...)`","`df.explain(...)`","`display(...)`","`dbutils.widgets...`"]},{"title":"SQL you now know","points":["`SHOW CATALOGS / SCHEMAS / TABLES`","`CREATE SCHEMA`","`CREATE TABLE ... USING DELTA`","`INSERT INTO`, `SELECT`","`USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`"]},{"title":"Notebook constructs","points":["`%python`","`%sql`","`%md`","`%pip`"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Workspace vs compute","text":"*A workspace is the collaborative and operational environment that contains development, orchestration, governance and analytics interfaces, whereas compute is the execution infrastructure used to actually process workloads submitted from those interfaces.*"}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch04-d01","title":"Notebook cell stuck or won't run","section":"ch04-s14","symptom":"You press Run and the cell hangs, does nothing, or the notebook says it is not attached.","askYourself":["Is the notebook attached to any compute at all?","Is that compute actually running?","Is the session healthy?","Did my code even parse?","Does the table I reference exist?","Do I have permission on it?","What does the query plan look like?","Is the problem in runtime execution itself?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the compute selector at the top of the notebook; attach to serverless or start the compute.","why":"A notebook is only an artifact — without a session nothing executes (Case A)."},{"do":"Re-run a trivial cell such as print(\"hello\").","why":"Separates environment problems from code problems.","code":"print(\"hello\")"},{"do":"Only if the environment is fine, check names (SHOW TABLES) and permissions.","why":"Next layers on the ladder: data/naming, then governance."},{"do":"Only then inspect the physical plan / Spark UI.","why":"Shuffle and skew analysis is the last layer, not the first."}],"rootCauses":["Notebook not attached to compute","Compute stopped / starting","Unhealthy session","Errors in later layers (names, permissions, plan)"],"fix":"Locate the failing layer top-down; for 'not attached' simply attach/start compute and re-run.","mnemonic":"Outside-in: Attach → Run → Session → Parse → Table → Permission → Plan → Execution"},{"id":"ch04-d02","title":"TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND","section":"ch04-s14","symptom":"`spark.table(\"prod.silver.ordres\")` or a SQL query fails with TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND.","askYourself":["Does SHOW TABLES IN <catalog>.<schema> list this table?","Did I spell the name exactly right (ordres vs orders)?","Am I in the catalog/schema I think I am?","Did I use a fully qualified catalog.schema.table name?","Can I even see the catalog/schema (privileges)?"],"steps":[{"do":"List the tables in the schema.","why":"Confirms what actually exists.","code":"SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;"},{"do":"Compare the spelling character by character.","why":"Typos are the most common cause."},{"do":"Check the current namespace.","why":"Short names resolve against the current catalog/schema.","code":"SELECT current_catalog();\nSELECT current_schema();"},{"do":"Walk up: SHOW SCHEMAS IN prod; SHOW CATALOGS;","why":"The table may live in another schema/catalog."},{"do":"Rewrite with the fully qualified name and re-run.","why":"Removes namespace ambiguity.","code":"spark.table(\"prod.silver.orders\")"}],"rootCauses":["Typo in table name","Wrong catalog or schema","Unqualified name resolved in the wrong context"],"fix":"Correct the name and use catalog.schema.table.","mnemonic":"SHOW → Spell → Scope → Qualify"},{"id":"ch04-d03","title":"INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES / PERMISSION_DENIED","section":"ch04-s14","symptom":"`SELECT * FROM prod.finance.transactions` fails with INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES — or a notebook that works for you fails as a scheduled job with PERMISSION_DENIED.","askYourself":["Which principal am I — or which identity is the job running as?","Is the job running as a different identity than me?","Do I (does it) have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Do I (does it) have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Do I (does it) have SELECT on the table?"],"steps":[{"do":"Identify the executing principal (you vs the job's identity).","why":"Works-for-me / fails-as-job almost always means a different principal."},{"do":"Check USE CATALOG on the catalog.","why":"Outer privilege in the chain."},{"do":"Check USE SCHEMA on the schema.","why":"Middle privilege in the chain."},{"do":"Check SELECT on the table.","why":"The actual data access privilege."}],"rootCauses":["Different execution identity/principal","Missing USE CATALOG","Missing USE SCHEMA","Missing SELECT"],"fix":"Grant the missing privileges to the right principal (or run the job as an identity that has them). Do not look at Spark partitions — this is the governance layer.","mnemonic":"WHO, then Catalog → Schema → Select"},{"id":"ch04-d04","title":"Works in DEV, fails in PROD (AttributeError / ModuleNotFoundError)","section":"ch04-s06","symptom":"Code runs on DEV (e.g. DBR 18.x) but PROD (e.g. DBR 17.3 LTS) raises AttributeError or ModuleNotFoundError.","askYourself":["Which DBR version runs in DEV and which in PROD?","Does this API exist in the older runtime?","Are the Python libraries and their versions the same?","Was the library installed only notebook-scoped with %pip in DEV?","Is the compute configuration the same?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read the exact error and the failing call/import.","why":"AttributeError → API missing; ModuleNotFoundError → library missing."},{"do":"Compare the DBR versions of both environments.","why":"Different DBR = different Spark/Python/packages/capabilities."},{"do":"Compare installed libraries.","why":"`%pip` is notebook-scoped; PROD may not have the package.","code":"%pip install some-library   # notebook only"},{"do":"Compare configuration, then align runtime/dependencies.","why":"Production needs systematic dependency management, not ad-hoc installs."}],"rootCauses":["Runtime version mismatch","Missing/different Python libraries","Configuration differences"],"fix":"Align DBR versions and manage dependencies systematically (or avoid APIs not present in the PROD runtime).","mnemonic":"Code + Runtime + Libraries + Config"},{"id":"ch04-d05","title":"Query runs but is slow","section":"ch04-s15","symptom":"A join + groupBy query (orders ⋈ customers → groupBy country) runs for 40 minutes.","askYourself":["Is the compute actually healthy?","How much input data am I reading?","Which physical join did Spark choose — BHJ or SMJ?","Is there a huge shuffle (Exchange)?","Is the data skewed?","Are tasks spilling to disk?","Are there tiny files or a bad table layout?"],"steps":[{"do":"Rule out compute health problems.","why":"Environment first."},{"do":"Check input bytes and scans.","why":"Unnecessary scans are a common hidden cost."},{"do":"Read the physical plan for the join strategy.","why":"BHJ is usually reasonable; SMJ implies a shuffle.","code":"result.explain(\"formatted\")"},{"do":"In the Spark UI look at shuffle size, task durations (skew) and spill.","why":"One very slow task = skew; spill = memory pressure."},{"do":"Check file sizes/layout.","why":"Tiny files or poor layout slow scans."}],"rootCauses":["Skew","Wrong join strategy","Huge shuffle","Spill","Poor table layout / small files","Unnecessary scan","Bad query","Insufficient statistics","Genuine compute bottleneck"],"fix":"Fix the identified root cause; resize compute only if evidence shows a compute bottleneck.","mnemonic":"Slow ≠ small: Health → Input → Join → Shuffle → Skew → Spill → Files"},{"id":"ch04-d06","title":"Query succeeds but returns the wrong data (wrong namespace)","section":"ch04-s13","symptom":"`SELECT * FROM orders` runs without error, but the numbers don't match production.","askYourself":["What are current_catalog() and current_schema() right now?","Could a table with the same name exist in dev and prod?","Did I use a fully qualified name?","Did a previous USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA change my context?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the current namespace.","why":"Short names resolve against it.","code":"SELECT current_catalog();\nSELECT current_schema();"},{"do":"Compare it with the intended environment.","why":"dev.sales.orders vs prod.sales.orders."},{"do":"Rewrite with catalog.schema.table.","why":"Eliminates ambiguity.","code":"SELECT * FROM prod.sales.orders;"}],"rootCauses":["Unqualified name resolved in the wrong catalog/schema"],"fix":"Use fully qualified names in production transformations (or set USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA explicitly).","mnemonic":"No error ≠ right table"},{"id":"ch04-d07","title":"Legacy RDD / R code fails on serverless","section":"ch04-s04","symptom":"`spark.sparkContext.parallelize([1,2,3])` (or R code) fails in a serverless notebook.","askYourself":["Is this notebook running on serverless compute?","Does the failing line use RDD APIs / sparkContext or R?","Can I express it with the DataFrame API instead?","Do I truly need classic compute for this workload?"],"steps":[{"do":"Identify the failing construct.","why":"Serverless doesn't support RDD APIs or R; it's built on Spark Connect."},{"do":"Confirm the compute type.","why":"The same code may work on classic compute."},{"do":"Rewrite with DataFrames, or move the workload to classic compute.","why":"Both are valid; DataFrames keep you on the recommended path.","code":"df = spark.createDataFrame([(1,), (2,), (3,)], [\"n\"])"}],"rootCauses":["Serverless limitation: no RDD APIs","Serverless limitation: no R"],"fix":"Modernize to DataFrame API or choose classic compute when RDD/R is required.","mnemonic":"RDD or R → classic"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Databricks = managed Spark","text":"Treating Databricks as just hosted Spark hides Delta, Unity Catalog, SQL warehouses, Lakeflow, Photon, serverless and more.","fix":"Think platform (interfaces, compute, runtime, engine, governance) with Spark as the core engine."},{"title":"Confusing account and workspace","text":"The workspace is what you see daily, so people think it's the top level.","fix":"Account = org level (identities, billing, policies, metastores, workspaces); workspace = collaboration environment."},{"title":"Thinking the control plane runs your Spark job","text":"The UI and coordination live in the control plane, but the browser/control plane don't do the groupBy.","fix":"Data processing happens in the compute plane."},{"title":"Serverless = no servers","text":"There are still machines, CPUs and RAM.","fix":"Serverless means Databricks manages provisioning and scaling — the abstraction boundary moved."},{"title":"Serverless good, classic useless","text":"Some workloads still need classic compute.","fix":"Pick classic for R, RDD APIs or custom infra/network/runtime needs."},{"title":"Assuming RDD code runs on serverless","text":"`spark.sparkContext.parallelize` and R aren't supported on serverless (Spark Connect).","fix":"Use the DataFrame API or classic compute."},{"title":"Using all-purpose compute for automated jobs","text":"All-purpose compute is meant for interactive development.","fix":"Use serverless jobs (default) or classic jobs compute for custom needs."},{"title":"SQL warehouse = database","text":"A SQL warehouse doesn't store tables.","fix":"It's SQL-optimized compute; tables live in storage."},{"title":"\"The Databricks cluster\"","text":"There isn't one cluster for everything.","fix":"Choose compute by workload shape: notebook, SQL/BI, jobs, classic special cases."},{"title":"DBR = VM","text":"Mixing hardware and software layers.","fix":"VM = infrastructure; DBR = software runtime stack."},{"title":"Ignoring runtime mismatches","text":"Code works in DEV (DBR 18.x) but fails in PROD (17.3 LTS) with AttributeError.","fix":"Debug code + runtime + libraries + configuration together."},{"title":"Photon misconceptions","text":"Believing Photon is a language, database or storage format, or needs special code.","fix":"Photon is a vectorized engine under the existing SQL/DataFrame APIs."},{"title":"Notebook runs without compute","text":"A notebook is just a document/artifact.","fix":"Attach it to compute (UC workspaces can auto-attach serverless)."},{"title":"display() in portable code","text":"`display()` is a Databricks notebook helper, not PySpark.","fix":"Use `df.show()` or other Spark APIs in code that runs outside Databricks."},{"title":"%pip is a permanent install","text":"`%pip` installs notebook-scoped libraries only.","fix":"Manage production dependencies systematically."},{"title":"Building production on _sqldf","text":"`_sqldf` is an interactive convenience.","fix":"Use `spark.sql()`/named DataFrames in production code."},{"title":"Using ${param}","text":"Legacy parameter syntax, deprecated for DBR 15.2+.","fix":"Use `:param` markers."},{"title":"FROM :table","text":"Parameter markers stand for values, not identifiers.","fix":"Use `IDENTIFIER(:table_name)` for dynamic object names."},{"title":"Unqualified table names","text":"`orders` resolves against the current catalog/schema and can silently read dev.","fix":"Use catalog.schema.table in production; check current_catalog()/current_schema()."},{"title":"Debugging the wrong layer","text":"Jumping to shuffles when the notebook isn't attached, or to partitions on a permission error.","fix":"Walk the layer ladder top-down."},{"title":"Slow query → bigger cluster","text":"Scaling without a root cause.","fix":"Check plan, Spark UI, join strategy, shuffle, skew, spill, file layout first."},{"title":"Compute stopped = data lost","text":"Confusing compute lifetime with data lifetime.","fix":"Delta tables live in persistent storage."},{"title":"Transformation = processed","text":"Lazy evaluation: `filter` only extends the plan.","fix":"Execution happens on actions (display, count, write)."},{"title":"Works for me, fails as job","text":"The job may run as a different principal.","fix":"Check the job identity's USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"Is Databricks just managed Spark?","a":"No — a unified data/AI platform; Spark is its core execution engine.","section":"ch04-s01"},{"q":"Account vs workspace: which is higher?","a":"Account (org-level); it contains many workspaces.","section":"ch04-s02"},{"q":"What is managed at account level?","a":"Users/groups/service principals, workspaces, UC metastores, billing, account policies.","section":"ch04-s02"},{"q":"What lives in a workspace?","a":"Notebooks, files, Git folders, jobs, pipelines, SQL queries, dashboards, compute, Catalog Explorer.","section":"ch04-s02"},{"q":"Control plane does…?","a":"Manages & coordinates: web UI, APIs, workspace metadata, job orchestration metadata, configuration.","section":"ch04-s03"},{"q":"Compute plane does…?","a":"The actual data processing: Spark transformations, SQL, joins, aggregations, ML.","section":"ch04-s03"},{"q":"Classic compute resources live where (AWS)?","a":"In the customer's cloud (AWS) account.","section":"ch04-s04"},{"q":"Serverless means…?","a":"Databricks manages provisioning/scaling — servers still exist; the abstraction boundary moved.","section":"ch04-s04"},{"q":"Two things serverless doesn't support today","a":"R and Spark RDD APIs (it's built on Spark Connect).","section":"ch04-s04"},{"q":"Default recommendation for most interactive/automated workloads?","a":"Serverless compute, if available and supported.","section":"ch04-s04"},{"q":"All-purpose compute is for…?","a":"Interactive development, exploration, debugging — not the first choice for automated jobs.","section":"ch04-s05"},{"q":"SQL warehouse is…?","a":"Compute optimized for SQL analytics/BI — NOT a database/storage.","section":"ch04-s05"},{"q":"SQL warehouse types","a":"Serverless, Pro, Classic (+ Lakehouse Real-Time in Beta).","section":"ch04-s05"},{"q":"Power BI queries → which compute?","a":"Usually a serverless SQL warehouse.","section":"ch04-s05"},{"q":"Need RDD or R → which compute?","a":"Classic compute.","section":"ch04-s05"},{"q":"What is DBR?","a":"Databricks Runtime: tested software stack (Spark, Python, Delta, libs, optimizations) on the compute.","section":"ch04-s06"},{"q":"LTS means?","a":"Long-Term Support — stable, predictable maintenance, less frequent migration.","section":"ch04-s06"},{"q":"DBR ML =","a":"Standard DBR + ML libraries + ML/DL infrastructure.","section":"ch04-s06"},{"q":"AttributeError only in PROD — first suspect?","a":"Runtime version mismatch (e.g. 18.x dev vs 17.3 LTS prod).","section":"ch04-s06"},{"q":"What is Photon?","a":"Databricks-native vectorized query engine accelerating SQL/DataFrame workloads under existing APIs.","section":"ch04-s07"},{"q":"Do you write Photon-specific code?","a":"No — same SQL/DataFrame code.","section":"ch04-s07"},{"q":"Where is Photon enabled?","a":"Serverless & SQL warehouses; available (usually default) on classic.","section":"ch04-s07"},{"q":"Can a notebook run without compute?","a":"No — it's an artifact; it needs an execution session.","section":"ch04-s08"},{"q":"display(df) vs df.show()","a":"display = Databricks notebook helper (rich); show = portable Spark API (text).","section":"ch04-s08"},{"q":"What does Exchange in a plan mean?","a":"A shuffle / redistribution boundary.","section":"ch04-s09"},{"q":"Why does groupBy shuffle but filter not?","a":"Filter works per partition; groupBy must bring same keys together.","section":"ch04-s09"},{"q":"Name 3 actions","a":"display(), count(), write.","section":"ch04-s09"},{"q":"Magic commands in the source","a":"%python, %sql, %scala, %r, %md, %pip.","section":"ch04-s10"},{"q":"Scope of %pip install?","a":"Notebook-scoped (notebook/session), not workspace-wide.","section":"ch04-s10"},{"q":"spark.sql(...) returns?","a":"A DataFrame.","section":"ch04-s10"},{"q":"What is _sqldf?","a":"The previous %sql cell's result exposed to later Python — interactive use only.","section":"ch04-s10"},{"q":"Read a widget value","a":"dbutils.widgets.get(\"name\")","section":"ch04-s11"},{"q":"dropdown widget argument order","a":"name, default value, choices, label.","section":"ch04-s11"},{"q":"Modern SQL parameter marker","a":":param (not ${param}, deprecated for DBR 15.2+).","section":"ch04-s11"},{"q":"Dynamic table name in SQL","a":"FROM IDENTIFIER(:table_name)","section":"ch04-s11"},{"q":"Catalog Explorer needs which privilege to discover objects?","a":"BROWSE and/or data access privileges.","section":"ch04-s12"},{"q":"DESCRIBE TABLE vs DESCRIBE DETAIL","a":"Columns/types vs table-level details (format, location, size, files).","section":"ch04-s12"},{"q":"Compute stops — is the Delta table lost?","a":"No: compute lifetime ≠ data lifetime.","section":"ch04-s12"},{"q":"prod.silver.orders parts","a":"catalog.schema.table.","section":"ch04-s13"},{"q":"How to check the current namespace?","a":"SELECT current_catalog(); SELECT current_schema();","section":"ch04-s13"},{"q":"TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND — first command?","a":"SHOW TABLES IN <catalog>.<schema>;","section":"ch04-s14"},{"q":"INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES — 4 questions","a":"Which principal? USE CATALOG? USE SCHEMA? SELECT?","section":"ch04-s14"},{"q":"Works for me, job gets PERMISSION_DENIED","a":"Different execution identity/principal.","section":"ch04-s14"},{"q":"ModuleNotFoundError DEV vs PROD — check?","a":"DBR version and Python/library environment.","section":"ch04-s14"},{"q":"Slow query — first rule","a":"Slow ≠ small cluster; find the root cause (plan, Spark UI, skew, spill, join, layout).","section":"ch04-s15"},{"q":"BHJ / SMJ","a":"Broadcast hash join / sort-merge join.","section":"ch04-s15"},{"q":"Typical number of UC metastores per region?","a":"One, shared by the region's workspaces.","section":"ch04-s02","src":"part2"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch04-e001","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Source Exercise 1 — which statement is correct?","options":["Databricks and Spark are synonyms.","Spark is one of the core execution technologies inside Databricks.","Databricks is a file format.","Spark is a cloud provider."],"answer":1,"why":["Databricks is a whole platform (UI, governance, SQL, orchestration…); Spark is one engine inside it.","Correct: Spark remains the central execution technology inside the platform.","Delta is the table format; Databricks is a platform.","AWS/Azure/GCP are cloud providers; Spark is an execution engine."],"explain":"Spark is an **execution engine**; Databricks is a **data/AI platform** that uses Spark (and Photon) to execute work. The tempting option A is the historical 'managed Spark' view, which is now an oversimplification."},{"id":"ch04-e002","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"\"Databricks = managed Spark\" is an accurate description of today's Databricks platform.","answer":false,"explain":"Historically this was close; today it is the **most dangerous oversimplification**. Databricks also includes Delta Lake, Unity Catalog, SQL warehouses, Lakeflow, serverless compute, Photon, ML/AI services, orchestration and DevOps tooling — Spark is just the core execution technology."},{"id":"ch04-e003","type":"match","section":"ch04-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each platform component to its role.","pairs":[["Delta Lake","Table format"],["Unity Catalog","Governance of data & AI assets"],["SQL warehouse","Compute for SQL analytics"],["Photon","Vectorized query engine"],["Lakeflow","Pipelines / ingestion"]],"explain":"These are the pieces that make Databricks a **platform** rather than 'just Spark'. Keeping each in its own bucket (format, governance, compute, engine, pipelines) is the whole point of this chapter."},{"id":"ch04-e004","type":"odd","section":"ch04-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong?","options":["Apache Spark","Photon","SQL warehouse","Unity Catalog"],"answer":3,"explain":"Spark and Photon are execution engines and a SQL warehouse is compute — all three **execute** work. **Unity Catalog** governs assets (names, metadata, permissions); it doesn't run queries."},{"id":"ch04-e005","type":"free","section":"ch04-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In your own words: why is \"Databricks is just managed Spark\" an oversimplification, and what role does Spark still play?","model":"Spark is still the central execution engine, but Databricks today is a unified data/AI platform: it adds Delta Lake (table format), Unity Catalog (governance), SQL warehouses (SQL compute), Lakeflow (pipelines), serverless compute, Photon (vectorized engine), ML/AI services, orchestration and DevOps tooling. So Spark is one technology inside the platform, not the platform itself.","rubric":["Spark = execution engine still central","Databricks = unified platform (engineering, analytics, AI, governance, deployment)","Names at least 3 non-Spark components (Delta, UC, SQL warehouses, Photon, Lakeflow, serverless…)"],"explain":"A strong answer contrasts **engine** vs **platform** and lists concrete components beyond Spark."},{"id":"ch04-e006","type":"bucket","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Source Exercise 2 (extended) — where does each operation most likely happen?","buckets":["Account level","Workspace"],"items":[{"text":"Create a notebook","bucket":1},{"text":"Create an organization-wide identity","bucket":0},{"text":"Run a Spark query","bucket":1},{"text":"Create a second workspace","bucket":0},{"text":"Configure SSO","bucket":0},{"text":"Manage billing","bucket":0},{"text":"Open Catalog Explorer","bucket":1},{"text":"Add an account group","bucket":0},{"text":"Schedule a job","bucket":1}],"explain":"The **account** is the org-level boundary: identities, billing, policies, UC metastores, workspace creation. The **workspace** is where daily work happens: notebooks, jobs, queries, Catalog Explorer — and a Spark query is a workspace workload executed on compute."},{"id":"ch04-e007","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q1 — which is the higher organizational object?","options":["Workspace","Account","Notebook","Compute"],"answer":1,"why":["A workspace lives inside an account.","Correct: one account can contain many workspaces.","A notebook lives inside a workspace.","Compute is execution infrastructure used from a workspace."],"explain":"The **account** sits at the top; it contains workspaces. The trap is thinking the workspace (what you see every day) is the top level."},{"id":"ch04-e008","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A single Databricks account can contain multiple workspaces.","answer":true,"explain":"Yes — e.g. ACME's one account can hold `analytics-prod`, `engineering-dev` and `research`. The reverse (one workspace containing accounts) is never true."},{"id":"ch04-e009","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"ACME's Databricks setup shows `analytics-prod`, `engineering-dev` and `research` under one account. What are they?","options":["Three accounts","Three workspaces in one account","Three catalogs in one metastore","Three clusters"],"answer":1,"why":["There is one account (the org boundary).","Correct: environments where teams and workloads operate.","Catalogs are data namespaces, not team environments.","Clusters are compute, not environments."],"explain":"Separate team/environment spaces like prod/dev/research are typically **workspaces** under a single **account**. Catalogs are data namespaces and clusters are compute — different layers."},{"id":"ch04-e010","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the levels.","text":"The [[account]] is the organization-level boundary where you manage identities, billing and workspaces. The [[workspace]] is the collaboration environment containing notebooks, jobs, dashboards and Catalog Explorer.","bank":["catalog","cluster","metastore"],"explain":"Account = org-wide administration; workspace = where users work. Catalog, cluster and metastore belong to other layers (data namespace, compute, UC configuration)."},{"id":"ch04-e011","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The analogy \"Azure tenant ≈ Databricks account, workspace ≈ a specific team environment\" is an exact one-to-one mapping.","answer":false,"explain":"The source explicitly says it is **not** an exact mapping — just a useful mental model for the org-wide vs environment-level distinction."},{"id":"ch04-e012","type":"bucket","section":"ch04-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Sort each item into the plane it belongs to.","buckets":["Control plane","Compute plane"],"items":[{"text":"Web UI","bucket":0},{"text":"APIs","bucket":0},{"text":"Workspace metadata","bucket":0},{"text":"Job orchestration metadata","bucket":0},{"text":"Spark transformations","bucket":1},{"text":"SQL execution","bucket":1},{"text":"Joins and aggregations","bucket":1},{"text":"ML computation","bucket":1},{"text":"Platform configuration","bucket":0}],"explain":"The **control plane** 'manages and coordinates' (UI, APIs, metadata, configuration). The **compute plane** does the actual data processing (Spark, SQL, joins, ML)."},{"id":"ch04-e013","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q2 — where does the real data processing happen?","options":["In the control plane","In the compute plane","In your browser","In Unity Catalog"],"answer":1,"why":["The control plane coordinates; it doesn't run the Spark workload.","Correct.","The browser only shows the UI.","UC governs; it doesn't process data."],"explain":"Data processing (transformations, SQL, joins, aggregations, ML) runs on compute resources in the **compute plane**."},{"id":"ch04-e014","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q3 — the Databricks web UI conceptually belongs to…","options":["the control plane","the compute plane","the storage layer"],"answer":0,"explain":"The web application lives in the **control plane**, together with APIs, metadata and orchestration. The compute plane is where data is processed."},{"id":"ch04-e015","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"When you run `df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\").show()` in a notebook, your browser performs the aggregation.","answer":false,"explain":"The browser only sends the command. The control plane coordinates, and the **compute plane** (driver + workers, Spark/Photon) executes the groupBy against storage."},{"id":"ch04-e016","type":"order","section":"ch04-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the path of a notebook command from your keyboard to the data.","items":["Browser","Databricks control services (schedule/coordinate)","Compute plane: driver / Spark session","Workers run Spark/Photon execution","Data storage"],"explain":"Browser → control plane coordinates → compute plane executes (driver plans, workers run tasks) → data is read from/written to storage."},{"id":"ch04-e017","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s03","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q21 (hard) — `Browser → Databricks UI → Spark query → ADLS`. Which two planes take part, and how?","options":["Control plane for UI/coordination; compute plane for the Spark computation","Compute plane for the UI; control plane for the Spark computation","Only the control plane — ADLS is part of it","Only the compute plane — the UI runs on the driver"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Exactly reversed.","ADLS is storage; the Spark computation still needs the compute plane.","The UI is a control-plane web app, not something running on the driver."],"explain":"UI and platform coordination = **control plane**; Spark computation = **compute plane**, which then reads data in ADLS storage."},{"id":"ch04-e018","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Source Exercise 3 — interactive Python notebook with `spark.read.table(...)`, DataFrame transformations and SQL; no special infrastructure requirement. What does Databricks generally recommend today?","options":["Classic all-purpose compute","Serverless compute (if available and the workload is supported)","A SQL warehouse","Classic jobs compute"],"answer":1,"why":["Fine but not the default recommendation without special needs.","Correct.","SQL warehouses serve SQL/BI, not Python notebooks.","Jobs compute is for automated jobs, not interactive work."],"explain":"Databricks recommends **serverless** for most interactive and automated workloads when available and supported. Classic is for special requirements (R, RDDs, custom config)."},{"id":"ch04-e019","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q4 — \"Serverless\" means there are no servers involved.","answer":false,"explain":"There are still CPUs, RAM and machines. Serverless means **Databricks manages** the provisioning/infrastructure for you — the abstraction boundary moved."},{"id":"ch04-e020","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q5 — valid reasons to choose classic instead of serverless (select all).","options":["You need R","You use Spark RDD APIs","Custom infrastructure/network/runtime requirements","Serverless cannot run SQL","Classic is always faster"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"why":["Correct: serverless doesn't support R today.","Correct: no RDD APIs on serverless (Spark Connect based).","Correct: classic gives custom configuration.","False: serverless runs SQL fine.","False: no such rule."],"explain":"Choose classic when you need capabilities/config serverless lacks: **R**, **RDD APIs**, or custom infra/network/runtime needs. 'Serverless = good, classic = useless' is wrong both ways."},{"id":"ch04-e021","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This notebook runs on **serverless** compute. Which line should you not expect to work?","lines":["df = spark.read.table(\"prod.sales.orders\")","rdd = spark.sparkContext.parallelize([1, 2, 3])","big = df.filter(df.amount > 100)","display(big)"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"Use the DataFrame API instead (e.g. `spark.createDataFrame([(1,),(2,),(3,)], [\"n\"])`) or run the notebook on classic compute if RDDs are truly required.","explain":"Serverless is built on **Spark Connect** and does **not support RDD APIs** (`sparkContext`). DataFrame reads, filters and `display` are fine."},{"id":"ch04-e022","type":"bucket","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless or classic? Sort each characteristic.","buckets":["Serverless","Classic"],"items":[{"text":"Less infrastructure to manage","bucket":0},{"text":"Fast provisioning","bucket":0},{"text":"Automatic scaling","bucket":0},{"text":"You pick driver/worker VM types","bucket":1},{"text":"Custom network/runtime needs","bucket":1},{"text":"Supports RDD APIs and R","bucket":1},{"text":"More operational responsibility","bucket":1},{"text":"Platform optimizations handled for you","bucket":0}],"explain":"The trade-off: serverless = less to manage, fast, auto-scaling; classic = more control and custom config, but more responsibility."},{"id":"ch04-e023","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"On AWS, classic compute resources (driver and worker VMs) live in the customer's AWS account.","answer":true,"explain":"Yes — with classic compute the VMs run in **your** cloud environment (similar architectures exist on Azure and GCP). With serverless, Databricks manages the compute."},{"id":"ch04-e024","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague moves an old Spark notebook to a new serverless notebook. A cell fails at `spark.sparkContext.parallelize(...)`.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you suspect first?","options":[{"text":"The serverless limitation: no RDD APIs","ok":true,"fb":"Right — serverless is Spark Connect-based and doesn't support RDD APIs."},{"text":"The cluster is too small","ok":false,"fb":"Size doesn't make an unsupported API appear."},{"text":"The table doesn't exist","ok":false,"fb":"There is no table read on this line."},{"text":"Photon is disabled","ok":false,"fb":"Photon has nothing to do with RDD API availability."}]},{"prompt":"What's the best fix?","options":[{"text":"Rewrite with the DataFrame API, or run on classic compute if RDDs are truly needed","ok":true,"fb":"Both are valid; DataFrames keep you on the recommended serverless path."},{"text":"Add %pip install pyspark","ok":false,"fb":"The library is there; the API isn't supported on serverless."},{"text":"Switch to a SQL warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"SQL warehouses run SQL, not Python RDD code."},{"text":"Retry the cell a few times","ok":false,"fb":"It's not transient — it's an unsupported API."}]}],"explain":"Serverless doesn't support R or RDD APIs. Either modernize to DataFrames or pick classic compute."},{"id":"ch04-e025","type":"odd","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a current serverless limitation?","options":["R language","Spark RDD APIs","`spark.sparkContext.parallelize(...)`","DataFrame `groupBy().agg()`"],"answer":3,"explain":"R and RDD APIs (including `sparkContext.parallelize`) aren't supported on serverless. **DataFrame** operations are exactly what serverless (Spark Connect) supports."},{"id":"ch04-e026","type":"order","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the steps to handle legacy code failing on serverless.","items":["Identify the failing line (e.g. uses sparkContext / RDD or R)","Confirm the notebook is running on serverless compute","Decide: rewrite with DataFrame API, or move to classic compute","Re-run and verify results"],"explain":"Locate the failing construct, confirm the compute type, then choose between modernizing the code and choosing classic compute."},{"id":"ch04-e027","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Today's UI and docs mostly say \"compute\" where older tutorials said \"cluster\", but classic Spark compute still has a driver and workers.","answer":true,"explain":"'Compute' is the broader modern term. Classic compute still follows driver → workers, and job → stage → task → partition logic applies."},{"id":"ch04-e028","type":"match","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Compute-selection mental model — match the workload to the generally preferred compute.","pairs":[["Interactive Python/DataFrames","Serverless notebook compute"],["Interactive SQL / BI (Power BI)","Serverless SQL warehouse"],["Automated Python/SQL job","Serverless jobs compute"],["Needs RDD APIs or R","Classic compute"]],"explain":"This is the current Databricks recommendation pattern: serverless where possible, the SQL warehouse for SQL/BI, and classic when you need unsupported features or custom config."},{"id":"ch04-e029","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A SQL warehouse is where your tables' data is physically stored.","answer":false,"explain":"Classic exam/interview trap. A SQL warehouse is **compute** that executes queries; tables keep their own storage layer (Delta files in cloud storage)."},{"id":"ch04-e030","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q7 — which compute would you typically use for Power BI SQL queries?","options":["Serverless SQL warehouse","Classic all-purpose compute","Serverless notebook compute","Classic jobs compute"],"answer":0,"explain":"BI tools send SQL to a **SQL warehouse** — usually **serverless** when available. Notebook/all-purpose compute is for programmatic work."},{"id":"ch04-e031","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q8 — what is a SQL warehouse?","options":["Compute optimized for SQL workloads","The physical storage of tables","A schema inside a catalog","A BI dashboard"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Storage is a separate layer.","That's a schema (namespace).","Dashboards *use* a warehouse."],"explain":"A SQL warehouse is **SQL-optimized compute** used by the SQL editor, dashboards and BI tools — not storage."},{"id":"ch04-e032","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT a SQL warehouse type?","options":["Serverless","Pro","Classic","Photon"],"answer":3,"explain":"Warehouse types are **Serverless, Pro, Classic** (plus Lakehouse Real-Time in Beta for low-latency/high-concurrency). **Photon** is an execution engine that runs inside warehouses."},{"id":"ch04-e033","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Databricks' first recommendation for automated jobs is classic all-purpose compute.","answer":false,"explain":"All-purpose compute is for interactive development. For jobs Databricks recommends **serverless** in most cases, or **classic jobs compute** when you need custom cluster characteristics."},{"id":"ch04-e034","type":"order","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A nightly 02:00 pipeline runs on jobs compute. Order its lifecycle.","items":["Job starts","Compute available / provisioned","Tasks run (ingest → transform → aggregate)","Workload finishes"],"explain":"Jobs compute exists for the job — no interactive cluster sitting open all day."},{"id":"ch04-e035","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q6 — default recommendation for an interactive Python/DataFrame notebook?","options":["Serverless notebook compute (if supported)","SQL warehouse","Classic jobs compute","Lakehouse Real-Time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Interactive Python/DataFrames → **serverless notebook compute** generally preferred. SQL warehouses serve SQL/BI; jobs compute serves automation."},{"id":"ch04-e036","type":"free","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview Q28: \"Why would you choose a SQL warehouse instead of general-purpose Spark compute?\"","model":"A SQL warehouse is purpose-built for SQL analytics and BI workloads and provides SQL-oriented performance, concurrency and scaling capabilities, whereas general notebook or classic compute is suited to broader Spark and programmatic workloads.","rubric":["Purpose-built for SQL analytics / BI","SQL-oriented performance, concurrency, scaling","General compute suits broader Spark/programmatic workloads"],"explain":"Hit the three axes: purpose (SQL/BI), properties (performance, concurrency, scaling) and contrast (programmatic Spark work)."},{"id":"ch04-e037","type":"odd","section":"ch04-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong with the others?","options":["Serverless notebook compute","SQL warehouse","Classic jobs compute","Databricks Runtime 17.3 LTS"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first three are **compute** types. **DBR 17.3 LTS** is the software/runtime environment that runs *on* compute."},{"id":"ch04-e038","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q9 — what is the Databricks Runtime?","options":["The software/runtime stack (Spark + Databricks-integrated libraries and dependencies) the workload runs on","The VM type of the driver","A SQL warehouse size","The Unity Catalog metastore"],"answer":0,"explain":"DBR is a **tested software stack**: Spark, Python, JVM, Delta support, libraries, optimizations, integrations. The VM is hardware; DBR is software."},{"id":"ch04-e039","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q10 — DBR and the VM are the same thing.","answer":false,"explain":"**VM/compute resources** = hardware/infrastructure abstraction; **DBR** = software runtime environment installed on it."},{"id":"ch04-e040","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"DEV compute runs DBR 18.x, PROD runs DBR 17.3 LTS. `some_new_api(...)` works in DEV but PROD raises `AttributeError`.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your first hypothesis?","options":[{"text":"Runtime version mismatch: the API doesn't exist in 17.3","ok":true,"fb":"Different DBR = different Spark/Python/packages = different capabilities."},{"text":"The PROD cluster is too small","ok":false,"fb":"Size doesn't remove attributes from an API."},{"text":"A typo in the table name","ok":false,"fb":"That would be TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND, not AttributeError."},{"text":"Missing SELECT permission","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions give INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES, not AttributeError."}]},{"prompt":"Which comparison do you make?","options":[{"text":"Compare DBR versions, libraries and configuration across DEV and PROD","ok":true,"fb":"Production debugging = code + runtime + libraries + configuration."},{"text":"Compare row counts of the tables","ok":false,"fb":"This is an environment failure, not a data issue."},{"text":"Compare Photon settings","ok":false,"fb":"Photon doesn't add or remove Python attributes."}]}],"explain":"When code behaves differently across environments, align the **runtime, libraries and configuration** before suspecting the code."},{"id":"ch04-e041","type":"order","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the investigation of \"works in DEV, AttributeError in PROD\".","items":["Read the exact error and the failing call","Compare DBR versions in DEV vs PROD","Compare Python/library versions","Compare compute configuration","Align the runtime (or avoid the newer API) and re-test"],"explain":"Start from the error, then walk the environment layers: runtime → libraries → configuration, and fix by aligning environments."},{"id":"ch04-e042","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the runtime terms.","text":"LTS stands for [[Long-Term Support]]. For production, LTS is often preferred for stability, predictable maintenance and less frequent [[migration|migrations]]. DBR ML = standard DBR + [[ML libraries|machine learning libraries]] + ML/DL infrastructure.","explain":"LTS runtimes trade newest features for stability; DBR ML saves you from building an ML environment yourself."},{"id":"ch04-e043","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What can change when you switch DBR version? (select all)","options":["Spark version","Python packages","Capabilities and bug fixes","Behavior of the same code","The data stored in your Delta tables"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"Different DBR → different Spark, packages, capabilities, bug fixes and behavior. Your **stored data** doesn't change just because the runtime changed."},{"id":"ch04-e044","type":"match","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the term to what it is.","pairs":[["VM","Hardware / infrastructure"],["DBR","Tested software stack incl. Spark"],["LTS","Long-Term Support runtime line"],["DBR ML","DBR + ML libraries + ML/DL infrastructure"]],"explain":"Keep hardware (VM) separate from software (DBR), and know the two DBR flavors that matter now: LTS for stability, ML for prebuilt ML tooling."},{"id":"ch04-e045","type":"free","section":"ch04-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is production debugging \"code + runtime + libraries + configuration\" and not just \"does the Python code have a bug?\"","model":"The same code can behave differently depending on the Databricks Runtime (Spark/Python versions, available APIs), the installed libraries and the configuration. A failure in PROD may come from a runtime mismatch (e.g. DBR 18.x in dev vs 17.3 LTS in prod) or a missing library, even if the code is correct.","rubric":["Same code, different environment → different behavior","Mentions runtime version differences","Mentions libraries/configuration","Example such as AttributeError from version mismatch"],"explain":"The point is to widen your search space from 'my code' to the whole execution environment."},{"id":"ch04-e046","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Source Exercise 4 — to use Photon you must rewrite `df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").sum(\"amount\")` with a Photon-specific API.","answer":false,"explain":"No. The same DataFrame/SQL abstraction can execute through Photon for supported operations — optimization happens **beneath** the API."},{"id":"ch04-e047","type":"odd","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Photon IS one of these — which one?","options":["A programming language","A database","A storage format","A vectorized query engine"],"answer":3,"explain":"Photon is a **Databricks-native vectorized query engine**. It is not a language, not a database and not a storage format — the three classic misconceptions."},{"id":"ch04-e048","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Where is Photon available?","options":["Only on SQL warehouses","Enabled on serverless compute and SQL warehouses; also available (usually on by default) on classic compute","Only on classic compute with a special Photon library","Only when you import the photon module"],"answer":1,"explain":"Photon is **enabled on serverless and SQL warehouses**, and on classic compute it's available and usually enabled by default via configuration. There is no module to import."},{"id":"ch04-e049","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Someone tried to 'turn on Photon' in code. Which line is wrong?","lines":["from pyspark.sql import functions as F","orders = spark.table(\"prod.sales.orders\")","result = photon.groupBy(orders, \"country\").sum(\"amount\")","display(result)"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"result = orders.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")","explain":"There is no `photon.groupBy` API. You write normal DataFrame/SQL code and Photon accelerates supported operations underneath."},{"id":"ch04-e050","type":"free","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview Q29: \"What is Photon?\"","model":"Photon is Databricks' native vectorized execution engine that accelerates supported SQL and DataFrame operations underneath the existing APIs, so applications generally do not need a separate Photon-specific programming model.","rubric":["Native/Databricks vectorized engine","Accelerates supported SQL & DataFrame ops","Works under existing APIs — no special code"],"explain":"Nail the three points: what (vectorized native engine), for what (SQL/DataFrames), how you use it (you don't change code)."},{"id":"ch04-e051","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why can vectorized processing be faster than row-at-a-time?","options":["It processes batches of values at once, reducing per-row overhead and using CPU characteristics better","It skips rows that don't match","It stores data in a different file format","It uses more workers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vectorized execution operates on **batches** (`[v1 v2 v3 …]`) instead of one row at a time, cutting overhead and improving CPU efficiency. It is not a storage format and doesn't change cluster size."},{"id":"ch04-e052","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the definition.","text":"Photon is a Databricks-native [[vectorized]] query engine that accelerates [[SQL]] and [[DataFrame]] workloads, while Spark is the broader execution framework.","bank":["row-based","RDD","storage"],"explain":"Vectorized + SQL/DataFrame is the core of the definition. RDD workloads are not what Photon targets."},{"id":"ch04-e053","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q19 — a notebook that exists but is not attached to any compute can still execute a Spark workload.","answer":false,"explain":"A notebook is an artifact/interface. It needs an **execution compute/session** — even `print(\"hello\")` needs one."},{"id":"ch04-e054","type":"bucket","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","compare","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Spark API or Databricks notebook/platform helper?","buckets":["Spark API (portable)","Databricks notebook helper"],"items":[{"text":"df.show()","bucket":0},{"text":"display(df)","bucket":1},{"text":"spark.createDataFrame(...)","bucket":0},{"text":"df.groupBy(...).agg(...)","bucket":0},{"text":"dbutils.widgets.get(...)","bucket":1},{"text":"df.explain(\"formatted\")","bucket":0},{"text":"_sqldf","bucket":1},{"text":"spark.table(...)","bucket":0}],"explain":"Spark APIs work anywhere Spark runs. `display`, `dbutils` and `_sqldf` are **notebook/platform conveniences** — they break if you port code outside Databricks."},{"id":"ch04-e055","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"`spark.createDataFrame([(1, \"Athens\", 120.0), …], [\"order_id\", \"city\", \"amount\"])` — which schema is inferred?","options":["order_id: long, city: string, amount: double","order_id: int, city: string, amount: float","order_id: string, city: string, amount: string","order_id: long, city: string, amount: decimal(10,2)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Python ints become **long** and Python floats become **double**. DECIMAL only appears when you declare it (as in a SQL table definition)."},{"id":"ch04-e056","type":"write","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Create a Spark DataFrame `df` from the list `data` with columns `order_id`, `city`, `amount`, and render it with the Databricks notebook helper.","solution":"df = spark.createDataFrame(\n    data,\n    [\"order_id\", \"city\", \"amount\"]\n)\ndisplay(df)","keywords":["spark.createDataFrame","order_id","city","amount","display(df)"],"lang":"python","explain":"In a Databricks notebook `spark` already exists. `display(df)` gives rich rendering; `df.show()` would be the portable Spark alternative."},{"id":"ch04-e057","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This script will run as a **plain PySpark job outside Databricks**. Which line will fail?","lines":["from pyspark.sql import SparkSession","spark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()","df = spark.createDataFrame([(1, \"Athens\", 120.0)], [\"order_id\", \"city\", \"amount\"])","display(df)"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"df.show()","explain":"`display()` is a **Databricks notebook helper**, not a PySpark method. Outside Databricks use `df.show()`. (Outside Databricks you *do* need to build the SparkSession, as lines 0–1 do.)"},{"id":"ch04-e058","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In a Unity Catalog-enabled workspace you open a brand-new notebook and run a cell without choosing compute. What can happen?","options":["Databricks can automatically attach it to serverless compute","The cell runs in your browser","The cell silently does nothing forever","A SQL warehouse is created for it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Current Databricks can **auto-attach** new notebooks to **serverless compute** in UC-enabled workspaces. Code never runs in the browser."},{"id":"ch04-e059","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In a Databricks notebook you must create `spark` yourself with `SparkSession.builder` before using it.","answer":false,"explain":"A ready-made Spark session (`spark`) is provided on supported compute. You only build it yourself outside Databricks."},{"id":"ch04-e060","type":"calc","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Orders: (1, Athens, 120.0), (2, Volos, 80.0), (3, Athens, 50.0), (4, Patras, 200.0), (5, Athens, 30.0). What is `total_sales` for Athens?","answer":200,"tolerance":0,"unit":"","explain":"120 + 50 + 30 = **200.0**, from 3 orders. Note Patras also has 200.0, so in `orderBy(desc(\"total_sales\"))` Athens and Patras tie and their order isn't guaranteed."},{"id":"ch04-e061","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Source Exercise 5 — why does `df.filter(F.col(\"amount\") > 100)` usually need no shuffle, while `df.groupBy(\"city\").sum(\"amount\")` probably does?","options":["Filter is decided independently within each partition; groupBy must bring all rows of the same city together across partitions","Filter runs on the driver; groupBy runs on workers","Filter uses Photon; groupBy doesn't","groupBy writes to disk; filter doesn't"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each partition can filter **locally**. But rows with `city = Athens` may sit in several partitions and must be combined logically → **redistribution/shuffle**."},{"id":"ch04-e062","type":"order","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What happens when you press Run on a cell with transformations + `display(result)`? Put the steps in order.","items":["Python code executes in the active compute session","Spark builds logical DataFrame transformations","display() requires a result (action)","Catalyst analysis and optimization","Physical execution plan","Spark/Photon execution: tasks process partitions (scan, filter, shuffle)"],"explain":"Not just 'Python runs': code builds a lazy plan, an action triggers Catalyst optimization, a physical plan is chosen, and Spark/Photon tasks process partitions."},{"id":"ch04-e063","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which of these trigger actual evaluation? (select all)","options":["display(y)","y.count()","y.write...","x = orders.filter(...)","y = x.groupBy(...).sum(...)"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"`display`, `count` and `write` are **actions** needing a result. `filter` and `groupBy().sum()` are **transformations** that only describe computation (lazy evaluation)."},{"id":"ch04-e064","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the code to print the physical plan in the readable format, and name the shuffle operator.","text":"city_sales.[[explain]](\"[[formatted|extended]]\")\n# shuffle boundary operator in the plan: [[Exchange]]","asCode":true,"explain":"`explain(\"formatted\")` (or `\"extended\"`) shows the plan. **Exchange** marks redistribution/shuffle; you'll also see HashAggregate and Sort."},{"id":"ch04-e065","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q26 — `spark.table(\"orders\").groupBy(\"customer_id\").sum(\"amount\")` shows **Exchange** in the plan. What does that most likely mean?","options":["Data redistribution/shuffle is required for the aggregation","Data is being exchanged with the control plane","Photon is disabled","The table is being copied to a new location"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exchange = **shuffle**: rows with the same `customer_id` must meet in the same partition. This is your Spark knowledge applied directly in Databricks."},{"id":"ch04-e066","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q20 — once `df.filter(...)` has run in a cell, the data has already been filtered.","answer":false,"explain":"Because of **lazy evaluation**, the filter only adds to the computation plan. Execution happens when an action/result is requested (display, count, write)."},{"id":"ch04-e067","type":"calc","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"How many rows will `city_sales` (groupBy city over the 5 orders in Athens/Volos/Patras) contain?","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"One row per distinct city: Athens, Patras, Volos → **3** rows. groupBy collapses the 5 orders into 3 groups."},{"id":"ch04-e068","type":"write","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write `city_sales`: group `df` by `city`, compute `total_sales` (sum of amount) and `orders` (count of rows), sorted by `total_sales` descending.","solution":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ncity_sales = (\n    df\n    .groupBy(\"city\")\n    .agg(\n        F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total_sales\"),\n        F.count(\"*\").alias(\"orders\")\n    )\n    .orderBy(F.desc(\"total_sales\"))\n)","keywords":["groupBy(\"city\")",".agg(","F.sum(\"amount\")","alias(\"total_sales\")","F.count(\"*\")","orderBy","F.desc"],"lang":"python","explain":"`agg` with aliased `F.sum`/`F.count`, then `orderBy(F.desc(...))`. Expect HashAggregate + Exchange in the plan."},{"id":"ch04-e069","type":"free","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview: explain what really happens between pressing Run and seeing a table from `display(result)`.","model":"The cell's Python runs in the active compute session. The DataFrame calls build a lazy logical plan; nothing is processed yet. display() is an action, so Spark runs Catalyst analysis/optimization, picks a physical plan, and Spark/Photon executes it as tasks over partitions — scanning, filtering, and shuffling for the groupBy — and returns the result to the notebook.","rubric":["Runs in compute session, not browser","Transformations are lazy","Action triggers Catalyst optimization","Physical plan executed by Spark/Photon tasks over partitions","Mentions shuffle for groupBy"],"explain":"The interview-level answer connects notebook → lazy plan → action → Catalyst → physical plan → distributed tasks."},{"id":"ch04-e070","type":"match","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q15/Q16 (extended) — match each magic command to what it does.","pairs":[["%sql","Run this cell as SQL"],["%md","Render this cell as Markdown"],["%pip","Manage notebook-scoped Python packages"],["%python","Run this cell as Python"],["%scala","Run this cell as Scala"]],"explain":"Magic commands switch how **one cell** is interpreted (`%python`, `%sql`, `%scala`, `%r`, `%md`) or manage notebook-scoped libraries (`%pip`)."},{"id":"ch04-e071","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`%pip install requests` permanently installs `requests` for every notebook in the workspace.","answer":false,"explain":"`%pip` installs **notebook-scoped** libraries tied to the notebook/session context — not a permanent workspace-wide installation."},{"id":"ch04-e072","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A cell in a Python-default notebook should run this query as SQL, but it fails with a Python SyntaxError. Which line is wrong?","lines":["%python","SELECT city, SUM(amount) AS revenue","FROM prod.silver.orders","GROUP BY city;"],"bugs":[0],"fix":"%sql\nSELECT city, SUM(amount) AS revenue\nFROM prod.silver.orders\nGROUP BY city;","explain":"The magic `%python` tells the notebook to parse SQL text as Python. Start the cell with **`%sql`** so it's interpreted as SQL."},{"id":"ch04-e073","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q13 — what does `spark.table(\"prod.silver.orders\")` do?","options":["Creates a Spark DataFrame that refers to that table object","Copies the whole table into driver memory","Creates a new empty table","Opens the table in Catalog Explorer"],"answer":0,"explain":"It resolves the UC name's metadata and returns a **DataFrame** pointing at the table. Thanks to lazy evaluation, nothing is read until an action runs."},{"id":"ch04-e074","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What does `spark.sql(\"SELECT country, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM prod.silver.orders GROUP BY country\")` return?","options":["A DataFrame","A Python list of rows","A printed text table","Nothing — it only runs the query"],"answer":0,"explain":"SQL statement → Spark SQL → **DataFrame**. You can keep transforming it or `display()` it."},{"id":"ch04-e075","type":"write","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"From Python, run SQL that computes revenue per country from `prod.silver.orders` into DataFrame `df`, then display it.","solution":"df = spark.sql(\"\"\"\n    SELECT country, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n    FROM prod.silver.orders\n    GROUP BY country\n\"\"\")\ndisplay(df)","keywords":["spark.sql(","SUM(amount) AS revenue","FROM prod.silver.orders","GROUP BY country","display(df)"],"lang":"python","explain":"`spark.sql` lets you write SQL while staying in Python and getting a DataFrame back."},{"id":"ch04-e076","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What is `_sqldf`, and how should you use it?","options":["The result of the previous SQL cell exposed to subsequent Python; a handy interactive feature, not a basis for production design","A built-in table listing all SQL queries; ideal for production auditing","A function that converts a DataFrame to SQL","The SQL warehouse's default DataFrame"],"answer":0,"explain":"In supported notebook environments, a `%sql` cell's result becomes `_sqldf` for later Python cells. Great for exploration — but use `spark.sql()`/named DataFrames in production."},{"id":"ch04-e077","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the two cells so a SQL result is reused in Python.","text":"%[[sql]]\nSELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders WHERE amount > 100;\n\n# next cell (Python)\ndisplay([[_sqldf]])","asCode":true,"explain":"`%sql` makes the cell SQL; its result is exposed to later Python as `_sqldf` in supported environments."},{"id":"ch04-e078","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"`spark.table(\"main.sales.orders\")` in Python and `%sql SELECT * FROM main.sales.orders` can lead to the same logical table.","answer":true,"explain":"Both resolve the same Unity Catalog name. Python and SQL are just two front doors to the same table."},{"id":"ch04-e079","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the dropdown widget and read its value.","text":"dbutils.widgets.[[dropdown]](\n    \"country\",\n    \"GR\",\n    [\"GR\", \"DE\", \"FR\"],\n    \"Country\"\n)\ncountry = dbutils.widgets.[[get]](\"country\")","asCode":true,"bank":["text","set","read"],"explain":"`dropdown(name, default, choices, label)` creates the control; `dbutils.widgets.get(name)` returns its current value."},{"id":"ch04-e080","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q17 — which is today's SQL parameter marker syntax for a widget named `country`?","options":[":country","${country}","@country","{{country}}"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: modern parameter marker.","Legacy; deprecated for DBR 15.2+.","Not Databricks parameter syntax here.","Not this syntax."],"explain":"Use **`:country`**. `${country}` is the older syntax, deprecated for DBR 15.2+ — learn today's syntax, not old tutorials."},{"id":"ch04-e081","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which line uses deprecated syntax?","lines":["# Python cell: dbutils.widgets.text(\"country\", \"GR\")","%sql","SELECT *","FROM prod.silver.orders","WHERE country = '${country}';"],"bugs":[4],"fix":"WHERE country = :country;","explain":"`${country}` is legacy/deprecated (DBR 15.2+). The modern parameter marker is `:country`."},{"id":"ch04-e082","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A widget holds a table name. Which line is wrong?","lines":["dbutils.widgets.text(\"table_name\", \"prod.silver.orders\")","%sql","SELECT *","FROM :table_name;"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"FROM IDENTIFIER(:table_name);","explain":"A parameter marker stands for a **value**; after `FROM` SQL needs an **identifier**. Wrap it: `IDENTIFIER(:table_name)`."},{"id":"ch04-e083","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q18 — why isn't `FROM :table` treated like `WHERE country = :country`?","options":["`FROM` needs a SQL identifier (object name), while `WHERE country = :country` takes a parameter value","Parameters only work in Python cells","`:table` is reserved syntax for temporary views","Widgets can't hold strings"],"answer":0,"explain":"Identifier vs value are different semantic categories. For dynamic object names use `IDENTIFIER()`."},{"id":"ch04-e084","type":"write","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Create a text widget `country` with default `GR` (Python), then write the SQL cell that filters `prod.silver.orders` with the modern parameter marker.","solution":"dbutils.widgets.text(\"country\", \"GR\")\n\n%sql\nSELECT *\nFROM prod.silver.orders\nWHERE country = :country;","keywords":["dbutils.widgets.text","\"country\"","%sql","where country = :country"],"lang":"python","explain":"Create the widget once, then reference it in SQL with `:country` (not `${country}`)."},{"id":"ch04-e085","type":"order","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the parameterized-notebook pattern.","items":["Create the widget (dbutils.widgets.dropdown)","Read the value (dbutils.widgets.get)","Use the value in a filter","Render with display()"],"explain":"Create → get → use → display. Users then change the widget instead of editing code."},{"id":"ch04-e086","type":"bucket","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["pitfall","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Can a plain parameter marker (`:p`) go there, or do you need `IDENTIFIER(:p)`?","buckets":["Plain value marker :p","Needs IDENTIFIER(:p)"],"items":[{"text":"WHERE country = ?","bucket":0},{"text":"FROM ? (table name)","bucket":1},{"text":"WHERE amount > ?","bucket":0},{"text":"SELECT ? FROM t (column name)","bucket":1},{"text":"WHERE order_date = ?","bucket":0},{"text":"Catalog/schema name in a qualified name","bucket":1}],"explain":"Values (strings, numbers, dates) use plain markers. Anything that is an **object name** (table, column, schema) is an identifier → `IDENTIFIER()`."},{"id":"ch04-e087","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The `${param}` syntax is deprecated for DBR 15.2 and above.","answer":true,"explain":"Correct. Modern SQL uses parameter markers like `:param`."},{"id":"ch04-e088","type":"free","section":"ch04-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why are widgets better than writing `country = \"GR\"` at the top of the notebook?","model":"Hardcoding means editing code every time you need another country, which is error-prone and not reusable. Widgets turn the notebook into a parameterized notebook/dashboard: the value is chosen in the UI (or passed in), read with dbutils.widgets.get, and the code stays unchanged.","rubric":["Avoids editing code for each run","Parameterized/reusable notebook","Value read via dbutils.widgets.get"],"explain":"Widgets separate **inputs** from **logic** — the first step toward reusable, schedulable notebooks."},{"id":"ch04-e089","type":"order","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the SQL drill-down from the top of the namespace to one table's details.","items":["SHOW CATALOGS;","SHOW SCHEMAS IN prod;","SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;","DESCRIBE TABLE prod.silver.orders;","DESCRIBE DETAIL prod.silver.orders;"],"explain":"Catalog → schema → table → columns → table-level details. This drill-down is your basic debugging toolkit."},{"id":"ch04-e090","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the exploration commands.","text":"SHOW [[CATALOGS]];\nSHOW [[SCHEMAS]] IN prod;\nSHOW [[TABLES]] IN prod.silver;\nDESCRIBE [[DETAIL]] prod.silver.orders;","asCode":true,"bank":["VIEWS","COLUMNS"],"explain":"SHOW CATALOGS / SCHEMAS IN catalog / TABLES IN catalog.schema, and DESCRIBE DETAIL for table-level info (format, location, size)."},{"id":"ch04-e091","type":"write","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Create the schema `dev_training.sales` only if missing, then a Delta table `orders` with order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(10,2), country STRING, order_ts TIMESTAMP.","solution":"CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS dev_training.sales;\n\nCREATE TABLE dev_training.sales.orders (\n  order_id BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount DECIMAL(10,2),\n  country STRING,\n  order_ts TIMESTAMP\n)\nUSING DELTA;","keywords":["create schema if not exists dev_training.sales","create table dev_training.sales.orders","decimal(10,2)","timestamp","using delta"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Schema first (catalog.schema), then the table with a fully qualified name and `USING DELTA`."},{"id":"ch04-e092","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The table has 5 columns (order_id, customer_id, amount, country, order_ts). Which line breaks the INSERT?","lines":["INSERT INTO dev_training.sales.orders","VALUES","  (1, 101, 20.50, 'GR', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()),","  (2, 102, 90.00, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()),","  (3, 103, 15.25, 'GR', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP());"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"  (2, 102, 90.00, 'DE', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()),","explain":"Row 2 has only 4 values — the `country` value is missing. Every VALUES row must match the table's column count and order."},{"id":"ch04-e093","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q22 — the compute stopped, so your Delta table is gone.","answer":false,"explain":"**Compute lifetime ≠ data lifetime.** The Delta table lives in persistent storage; compute can stop and start without affecting it."},{"id":"ch04-e094","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague can't see the `prod` catalog in Catalog Explorer, but you can. Most likely reason?","options":["They lack privileges such as BROWSE or the corresponding data access privileges","Their compute is too small","Catalog Explorer only shows the creator's objects","Photon is disabled for them"],"answer":0,"explain":"Catalog Explorer discovery is **governed**: you need privileges like **BROWSE** and/or data access privileges. Compute size and Photon are irrelevant to visibility."},{"id":"ch04-e095","type":"match","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each layer to its job for `dev_training.sales.orders`.","pairs":[["Unity Catalog","Name, metadata, permissions"],["Delta","Table format: files + transaction state"],["Storage","Where the bytes persist"],["Compute","Executes the queries"]],"explain":"UC names and governs, Delta formats, storage persists, compute executes — four separate layers."},{"id":"ch04-e096","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"You want table-level information such as format, location and number of files. Which command?","options":["DESCRIBE DETAIL prod.silver.orders;","DESCRIBE TABLE prod.silver.orders;","SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;","SHOW SCHEMAS IN prod;"],"answer":0,"explain":"`DESCRIBE DETAIL` gives table-level details; `DESCRIBE TABLE` gives columns and types; SHOW commands list objects."},{"id":"ch04-e097","type":"match","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q14 — what is each part of `prod.silver.orders`?","pairs":[["prod","catalog"],["silver","schema"],["orders","table / object"]],"explain":"Unity Catalog's three-level namespace: **catalog.schema.table**."},{"id":"ch04-e098","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Current context is `dev.sales`. You run `SELECT * FROM orders;` believing you read production. What do you actually read?","options":["dev.sales.orders","prod.sales.orders","An error: the name is ambiguous","main.default.orders"],"answer":0,"explain":"Short names resolve against the **current catalog and schema** → `dev.sales.orders`. No error — just silently wrong data. Use fully qualified names."},{"id":"ch04-e099","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Your notebook's revenue numbers are far lower than finance's report. The query is `SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders;` and it runs without errors.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"SELECT current_catalog(), current_schema()","ok":true,"fb":"The unqualified name may be resolving to a different catalog/schema."},{"text":"Increase the cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"Wrong numbers aren't a performance problem."},{"text":"Check Photon is enabled","ok":false,"fb":"Photon changes speed, not results."},{"text":"Reinstall libraries with %pip","ok":false,"fb":"No dependency error here."}]},{"prompt":"It returns `dev` / `sales`. What now?","options":[{"text":"Rewrite with the fully qualified name prod.sales.orders (or USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA explicitly)","ok":true,"fb":"Fully qualified names remove the ambiguity."},{"text":"Delete dev.sales.orders","ok":false,"fb":"Destructive and doesn't fix the habit."},{"text":"Run the same query again","ok":false,"fb":"Same context → same wrong table."}]}],"explain":"Silent wrong-namespace reads are dangerous because nothing errors. Check the current namespace and fully qualify production queries."},{"id":"ch04-e100","type":"order","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the steps when you suspect a short table name hit the wrong environment.","items":["Notice results look wrong (no error raised)","Run SELECT current_catalog() and current_schema()","Compare with the intended catalog.schema","Rewrite using catalog.schema.table","Re-run and compare results"],"explain":"Confirm the current namespace, compare with intent, and fix by fully qualifying."},{"id":"ch04-e101","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Check and set the namespace.","text":"SELECT [[current_catalog]]();\nSELECT [[current_schema]]();\nUSE [[CATALOG]] prod;\nUSE [[SCHEMA]] silver;","asCode":true,"explain":"`current_catalog()`/`current_schema()` reveal the context; `USE CATALOG`/`USE SCHEMA` set it."},{"id":"ch04-e102","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"An unqualified table name like `orders` is always resolved in the `prod` catalog.","answer":false,"explain":"It's resolved against the **current** catalog and schema, whatever they are. That's why production code should use `catalog.schema.table`."},{"id":"ch04-e103","type":"write","section":"ch04-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Set the context to catalog `prod` and schema `silver`, then select all rows from `orders` using the short name.","solution":"USE CATALOG prod;\nUSE SCHEMA silver;\n\nSELECT *\nFROM orders;","keywords":["use catalog prod","use schema silver","from orders"],"lang":"sql","explain":"After `USE CATALOG`/`USE SCHEMA`, `orders` resolves to `prod.silver.orders`. For production transformations, the fully qualified name is still safer."},{"id":"ch04-e104","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`spark.table(\"prod.silver.ordres\")` fails with `TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND`.","code":"spark.table(\"prod.silver.ordres\")","steps":[{"prompt":"First thing to run?","options":[{"text":"SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;","ok":true,"fb":"List what actually exists in that schema."},{"text":"Open the Spark UI and inspect shuffles","ok":false,"fb":"Wrong layer — the plan never ran; the name didn't resolve."},{"text":"Increase cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"Compute size can't create a missing name."},{"text":"%pip install the table","ok":false,"fb":"Tables aren't packages."}]},{"prompt":"The list shows `orders`. Conclusion?","options":[{"text":"Typo: ordres → orders","ok":true,"fb":"Fix the name and re-run."},{"text":"Permission problem","ok":false,"fb":"Permission errors look like INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES."},{"text":"Runtime mismatch","ok":false,"fb":"Runtime issues show up as AttributeError/ModuleNotFoundError."}]},{"prompt":"If `orders` had NOT been listed there, what would you check next?","options":[{"text":"Whether I'm in the wrong catalog/schema (current_catalog(), current_schema(), SHOW SCHEMAS)","ok":true,"fb":"The table may live elsewhere."},{"text":"Restart the compute","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't change where tables live."},{"text":"Enable Photon","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to name resolution."}]}],"explain":"TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND is a **naming/namespace** problem: typo or wrong catalog/schema. Use SHOW TABLES/SCHEMAS before touching Spark internals."},{"id":"ch04-e105","type":"order","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A notebook cell is stuck/failing. Order the layer ladder (top to bottom).","items":["Compute attached?","Compute running?","Session healthy?","Code parsed?","Table exists?","Permission?","Query plan?","Runtime execution?"],"explain":"Find the failing **layer** first: environment → code → data/naming → governance → plan → execution. Don't jump to shuffle analysis when the notebook isn't even attached."},{"id":"ch04-e106","type":"bucket","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which layer does each symptom point to?","buckets":["Execution environment","Naming / namespace","Governance / authorization","Runtime / dependencies"],"items":[{"text":"notebook not attached","bucket":0},{"text":"TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND","bucket":1},{"text":"INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES","bucket":2},{"text":"ModuleNotFoundError","bucket":3},{"text":"Typo 'ordres' in a table name","bucket":1},{"text":"Scheduled job gets PERMISSION_DENIED","bucket":2},{"text":"AttributeError only on the older DBR","bucket":3},{"text":"Compute not running","bucket":0}],"explain":"Classify before you fix: Case A environment, Case B naming, Case C governance, Case D runtime/dependency."},{"id":"ch04-e107","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q25 — the notebook works when **you** run it, but the scheduled job fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED`.","steps":[{"prompt":"First serious hypothesis?","options":[{"text":"The job runs as a different execution identity/principal","ok":true,"fb":"Same code, different principal → different privileges."},{"text":"The job's cluster is too small","ok":false,"fb":"Size doesn't cause permission errors."},{"text":"The table was deleted","ok":false,"fb":"Then you'd see TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND, and it works for you."},{"text":"Photon is off on job compute","ok":false,"fb":"Photon doesn't affect authorization."}]},{"prompt":"What do you verify for that principal?","options":[{"text":"USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT on the objects","ok":true,"fb":"The full privilege chain must exist for the job's principal."},{"text":"Spark partition counts","ok":false,"fb":"Wrong layer — this is governance."},{"text":"The DBR version","ok":false,"fb":"Runtime doesn't grant permissions."}]}],"explain":"When it works for you but not for the job, compare **who** runs it. Then check the privilege chain for that principal."},{"id":"ch04-e108","type":"order","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case C — order the questions for `INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES` on `SELECT * FROM prod.finance.transactions`.","items":["Which principal am I (or is the job running as)?","Do I have USE CATALOG on prod?","Do I have USE SCHEMA on prod.finance?","Do I have SELECT on the table?"],"explain":"Identity first, then the chain from outer to inner: catalog → schema → table. This is the governance layer — not Spark partitions."},{"id":"ch04-e109","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q24 — notebook works in DEV but PROD shows `ModuleNotFoundError`. Which two categories do you check early? (select all that apply)","options":["Databricks Runtime version","Python/library environment","Unity Catalog grants","Spark shuffle partitions"],"answer":[0,1],"multi":true,"explain":"ModuleNotFoundError is a **runtime/environment/dependency** issue: compare DBR versions and the installed Python libraries. Grants and shuffles belong to other layers."},{"id":"ch04-e110","type":"spotbug","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"This cell raises `TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND`. Which line?","lines":["from pyspark.sql import functions as F","orders = spark.table(\"prod.silver.ordres\")","big = orders.filter(F.col(\"amount\") > 100)","display(big)"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"orders = spark.table(\"prod.silver.orders\")","explain":"`ordres` is a typo for `orders`. `SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;` would have revealed it immediately."},{"id":"ch04-e111","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case A — you click Run and nothing executes; the notebook header says it's not attached.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which layer is this?","options":[{"text":"Execution environment","ok":true,"fb":"No compute session → nothing can run."},{"text":"Spark code bug","ok":false,"fb":"The code never even reached Spark."},{"text":"Governance","ok":false,"fb":"No permission error was raised."},{"text":"Data layout","ok":false,"fb":"No data has been read."}]},{"prompt":"What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Attach to compute (e.g. serverless) / start the compute, then re-run","ok":true,"fb":"Give the notebook an execution session."},{"text":"Rewrite the groupBy","ok":false,"fb":"Code isn't the problem."},{"text":"Run OPTIMIZE on the table","ok":false,"fb":"Table layout is irrelevant here."}]}],"explain":"A notebook is just an artifact. 'Not attached' is an environment problem — fix the compute, not the code."},{"id":"ch04-e112","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You get `INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES`. Which of these should you NOT spend time on?","options":["Spark partition counts","Which principal you are","USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA grants","SELECT on the table"],"answer":0,"explain":"Permission errors live in the **governance/authorization** layer. Spark partitions are an execution-layer concern and won't explain a denied access."},{"id":"ch04-e113","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`ModuleNotFoundError` most likely indicates a data problem in your tables.","answer":false,"explain":"It's a **runtime/environment/dependency** problem. In a notebook `%pip install some-library` may help; in production manage dependencies systematically."},{"id":"ch04-e114","type":"free","section":"ch04-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q23 — a query is slow and a junior engineer says \"we need a bigger cluster\". What's wrong with that reasoning?","model":"No root cause has been identified. The slowness could come from skew, a wrong join strategy, a huge shuffle, spill to disk, poor table layout, small files, an unnecessary scan, a badly written query or insufficient statistics — or a real compute bottleneck. Cluster sizing is only one possibility, so you first inspect the physical plan and Spark UI.","rubric":["Root cause not identified","Lists several alternatives (skew, join, shuffle, spill, layout/small files, scan, statistics)","Cluster size is only one possibility","Investigate plan/Spark UI first"],"explain":"Slow ≠ small cluster. Diagnose before you scale."},{"id":"ch04-e115","type":"order","section":"ch04-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the debugging tree for a 40-minute join + groupBy query.","items":["Is compute actually healthy?","How much input data?","What physical join (BHJ or SMJ)?","Huge shuffle?","Skew?","Spill to disk?","Tiny files / bad layout?"],"explain":"Health → input size → join strategy → shuffle → skew → spill → file layout. Each step narrows the cause before you touch cluster size."},{"id":"ch04-e116","type":"scenario","section":"ch04-s15","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`orders JOIN customers ON customer_id`, then `groupBy(\"country\")`, runs for 40 minutes.","steps":[{"prompt":"Before anything else?","options":[{"text":"Confirm the compute is actually healthy","ok":true,"fb":"Rule out the environment layer first."},{"text":"Double the workers","ok":false,"fb":"That's guessing without a root cause."},{"text":"Rewrite in Scala","ok":false,"fb":"Language isn't the issue."}]},{"prompt":"Compute is fine. Next?","options":[{"text":"Check input size and which physical join was chosen (BHJ vs SMJ) in the plan","ok":true,"fb":"Input volume and join strategy are the biggest levers."},{"text":"Check UC permissions","ok":false,"fb":"The query runs — permissions are fine."},{"text":"Reinstall libraries","ok":false,"fb":"No dependency error."}]},{"prompt":"It's a sort-merge join with a huge shuffle. One task runs far longer than the rest. Suspect?","options":[{"text":"Skew — some customer_id/country keys are much bigger","ok":true,"fb":"One slow task among many fast ones is the classic skew signal."},{"text":"Typo in the table name","ok":false,"fb":"Then it wouldn't run at all."},{"text":"Photon is a database","ok":false,"fb":"Photon is an engine, and this doesn't explain one slow task."}]}],"explain":"Walk the tree: healthy compute → input size → join type → shuffle → skew → spill → file layout. Evidence before resources."},{"id":"ch04-e117","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case E (query runs but is slow) — what do you inspect? (select all)","options":["Physical plan","Spark UI tasks","Skew and spill","Join strategy and partition counts","USE CATALOG privileges","%pip packages"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"Only now do you open Spark: plan, Spark UI, tasks, shuffle, skew, spill, input bytes, partition counts, join strategy. Privileges and packages belong to other layers — the query already runs."},{"id":"ch04-e118","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A slow query automatically means the cluster is too small.","answer":false,"explain":"Slow ≠ small cluster. Skew, joins, shuffles, spill, small files or bad queries are just as likely. Find the root cause first."},{"id":"ch04-e119","type":"odd","section":"ch04-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a typical cause of a slow (but running) query?","options":["Huge shuffle","Data skew","Spill to disk","INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES"],"answer":3,"explain":"Shuffle, skew and spill are performance causes. `INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES` stops the query from running at all — a governance error, not slowness."},{"id":"ch04-e120","type":"calc","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Mini-project data: Athens 100.00, Volos 80.00, Athens 30.00, Patras 150.00, Athens 200.00, Volos 40.00. What revenue does Task A return for Athens?","answer":330,"tolerance":0,"unit":"","explain":"100 + 30 + 200 = **330.00**. Full Task A result (ORDER BY revenue DESC): Athens 330, Patras 150, Volos 120."},{"id":"ch04-e121","type":"match","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"When you see it, think it — match each term to its mental bucket.","pairs":[["spark.table(...)","Spark API"],["display(...)","Databricks notebook convenience"],["prod.silver.orders","Unity Catalog namespace"],["Serverless","Databricks-managed compute infrastructure"],["Photon","Vectorized native execution engine"],["DBR 17.3","Software/runtime environment"]],"explain":"Not everything belongs in a bucket called 'Databricks stuff'. Each term has a precise layer: API, helper, namespace, infrastructure, engine, runtime. (SQL Warehouse → SQL-optimized compute.)"},{"id":"ch04-e122","type":"write","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Task A — SQL: revenue per city from `dev_training.sales.orders`, highest first.","solution":"SELECT\n  city,\n  SUM(amount) AS revenue\nFROM dev_training.sales.orders\nGROUP BY city\nORDER BY revenue DESC;","keywords":["sum(amount) as revenue","from dev_training.sales.orders","group by city","order by revenue desc"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Aggregate with SUM, group by city, sort descending by the alias."},{"id":"ch04-e123","type":"write","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Task B — PySpark: the same result as Task A in a DataFrame `city_revenue`, displayed.","solution":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\norders = spark.table(\"dev_training.sales.orders\")\ncity_revenue = (\n    orders\n    .groupBy(\"city\")\n    .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n    .orderBy(F.desc(\"revenue\"))\n)\ndisplay(city_revenue)","keywords":["spark.table(\"dev_training.sales.orders\")","groupBy(\"city\")","F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\")","orderBy(F.desc(\"revenue\"))","display(city_revenue)"],"lang":"python","explain":"Same logic, DataFrame API: `spark.table` → `groupBy` → `agg(F.sum().alias())` → `orderBy(F.desc())`."},{"id":"ch04-e124","type":"cloze","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Task D — complete the parameterized filter.","text":"dbutils.widgets.[[dropdown]](\"city\", \"Athens\", [\"Athens\", \"Volos\", \"Patras\"])\ncity = dbutils.widgets.[[get]](\"city\")\nresult = (\n    spark.[[table]](\"dev_training.sales.orders\")\n    .filter(F.col(\"city\") == [[city]])\n)\ndisplay(result)","asCode":true,"explain":"Create the dropdown, read it with `get`, read the table with `spark.table`, and compare the column to the Python variable `city`."},{"id":"ch04-e125","type":"mcq","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Task C — after `city_revenue.explain(\"formatted\")`, which operators should you look for, and why?","options":["Exchange and Aggregate — GROUP BY city must bring rows with the same city together","Filter and Project — they cause the shuffle","BroadcastHashJoin — every groupBy is a join","Photon and Delta — they show the table format"],"answer":0,"explain":"GROUP BY needs a **shuffle** (Exchange) so same-city rows meet, and **(Hash)Aggregate** operators compute partial and final sums."},{"id":"ch04-e126","type":"free","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview Q27: \"What is the difference between a Databricks workspace and compute?\"","model":"A workspace is the collaborative and operational environment that contains development, orchestration, governance and analytics interfaces, whereas compute is the execution infrastructure used to actually process workloads submitted from those interfaces.","rubric":["Workspace = collaborative/operational environment with interfaces","Compute = execution infrastructure","Workloads are submitted from the workspace and processed on compute"],"explain":"Interfaces vs execution: the workspace is where you work; compute is what does the work."},{"id":"ch04-e127","type":"calc","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Task D with the default widget value (`Athens`): how many rows does `display(result)` show?","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"Athens appears in orders 1, 3 and 5 → **3** rows. The widget default is the second argument of `dropdown`."},{"id":"ch04-e128","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Task E — the recommended SQL version is `SELECT * FROM dev_training.sales.orders WHERE city = '${city}';`","answer":false,"explain":"Use the modern marker: `WHERE city = :city`. `${city}` is legacy/deprecated syntax."},{"id":"ch04-e129","type":"odd","section":"ch04-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong?","options":["display(df)","dbutils.widgets.get(\"city\")","_sqldf","df.groupBy(\"city\")"],"answer":3,"explain":"`display`, `dbutils` and `_sqldf` are **Databricks notebook conveniences**. `df.groupBy` is a portable **Spark API**."},{"id":"ch04-e130","type":"order","section":"ch04-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Order your moves for `TABLE_OR_VIEW_NOT_FOUND` on `spark.table(\"prod.silver.ordres\")`.","items":["Run SHOW TABLES IN prod.silver;","Compare the spelling (ordres vs orders)","Check current_catalog() / current_schema() and SHOW SCHEMAS if the table isn't there","Rewrite with the correct fully qualified name and re-run"],"explain":"SHOW → spell → scope → qualify. All of this is the naming layer — no Spark UI needed."},{"id":"ch04-e701","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A table created from workspace A can be queried from workspace B in the same region, if both use the same metastore, the principal has the grants and no workspace binding blocks it.","answer":true,"explain":"True. Tables are registered in the regional **metastore**, not in a workspace. Same metastore + grants + no blocking binding = access from any attached workspace.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch04-e901","type":"tf","section":"ch04-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`events = spark.readStream.format(\"json\").schema(s).load(path)` immediately starts reading files.","answer":false,"explain":"No — like any transformation it defines a (streaming) plan. Execution begins when a streaming sink is started with `writeStream ... toTable()` or `.start()`.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch05","num":5,"title":"Delta Lake Internals","subtitle":"How SQL commands become files + transaction-log commits","emoji":"🔺","sourcePages":"132–179, 358–402 (internals parts)","mantra":"Every Delta write = read a snapshot, write new files (or deletion vectors), validate optimistically, and commit one atomic log version.","objectives":["You can replay a Delta log in your head and say which files form any snapshot.","You can explain how UPDATE/DELETE become file rewrites or deletion vectors.","You can use DESCRIBE HISTORY, time travel and RESTORE to investigate and repair bad data.","You can explain why time travel is not a backup and how VACUUM retention affects it.","You can write correct MERGE statements (SQL + Python), including NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE, and deduplicate CDC sources.","You can explain optimistic concurrency, isolation levels and row-level concurrency.","You can debug MERGE failures, concurrency exceptions, 'storage didn't shrink' and 'time travel broke'."],"sections":[{"id":"ch05-s01","title":"Anatomy of a Delta Table","hook":"A Delta table is not 'a folder of Parquet files' — it is whatever the transaction log says it is.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Zoom in on the bottom of the Databricks stack. Today we open the **Delta table** box and look at files and commits."},{"t":"flow","items":["Notebook / SQL / Job","Compute","Spark / Photon","Delta tables","Cloud storage"],"caption":"The stack from the previous chapter — we now open 'Delta tables'"},{"t":"p","text":"Goal: when you see a `MERGE`, an `UPDATE`, a concurrency error or a `VACUUM`, you can **predict** what happens to the data files and to `_delta_log`. Not just recite 'Delta = Parquet + log'."},{"t":"p","text":"**Delta Lake** is the default table format in Databricks. It extends Parquet data files with a **versioned transaction log**, which gives ACID semantics, metadata management, batch/streaming interoperability and table versioning."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE dev_training.sales.orders (\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      DECIMAL(10,2),\n  status      STRING\n)\nUSING DELTA;","caption":"A plain e-shop orders table"},{"t":"diagram","text":"orders/\n├── part-00000-....parquet      <- columnar data\n├── part-00001-....parquet\n├── part-00002-....parquet\n└── _delta_log/                 <- versioned table state\n    ├── 00000000000000000000.json   (version 0)\n    ├── 00000000000000000001.json   (version 1)\n    ├── 00000000000000000002.json   (version 2)\n    └── ...","caption":"What the storage layer conceptually holds"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"part-*.parquet","def":"The **data files**: real rows, stored column by column (Parquet)."},{"term":"_delta_log/","def":"Folder holding the **versioned state** of the table: one JSON commit file per version."},{"term":"Commit file","def":"A JSON file named with a **20-digit zero-padded** version number, e.g. `00000000000000000003.json`."},{"term":"Version","def":"Each normal commit creates a new **atomic** version with a monotonically increasing number: 0, 1, 2, 3…"},{"term":"Action","def":"One entry inside a commit (e.g. `add`, `remove`). A commit = an atomic set of actions that turns the previous snapshot into the next."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Example: `CREATE TABLE` was version 0. Now you insert two orders:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT INTO dev_training.sales.orders VALUES\n  (1, 101, 20.00, 'NEW'),\n  (2, 102, 30.00, 'NEW');"},{"t":"diagram","text":"part-A.parquet\norder_id | customer_id | amount | status\n---------+-------------+--------+-------\n       1 |         101 |  20.00 | NEW\n       2 |         102 |  30.00 | NEW\n\n_delta_log/00000000000000000001.json\n  version 1:  ADD part-A.parquet","caption":"One INSERT -> one new data file + one commit"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"{\n  \"add\": {\n    \"path\": \"part-A.parquet\",\n    \"size\": 12345,\n    \"dataChange\": true\n  }\n}","caption":"Very simplified add action (real JSON is richer)"},{"t":"p","text":"A real `add` action can also carry **partition values**, **modification time** and **file-level statistics** (record count, min/max per column). Those stats power data skipping (Chapter 6)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The first big idea","text":"The table is **the set of logical files that the transaction log says belong to the current snapshot** — not every `.parquet` file sitting in the folder.\n`physical storage ≠ logical current table state`"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: storage has A.parquet, B.parquet, C.parquet but the current snapshot lists only A and C. Is B part of the table?","text":"**No.** B may be an old file still needed for time travel, or one waiting to be cleaned up by `VACUUM`. A `SELECT` on the current version never reads it."},{"t":"diagram","text":"  Parquet files\n+ optional deletion vectors\n+ _delta_log versions (JSON commits)\n+ checkpoints\n---------------------------------\n= Delta table state","caption":"Storage-side formula to make instinctive"},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"\"Parquet already stores data, so why do I need Delta?\"","text":"Parquet defines a **columnar file format**, but it does not define **transactional table state across many files**. Delta adds a versioned transaction log, atomic commits, snapshot isolation, schema enforcement/evolution, DML (`MERGE`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE`), concurrency control, history/time travel and physical optimization features over those files."}]},{"id":"ch05-s02","title":"Snapshots, ADD/REMOVE & Checkpoints","hook":"A snapshot is just the result of replaying the log — once you can replay it in your head, Delta stops being magic.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A **snapshot** is the table as of one version. You get it by **replaying** the log actions from the start (or from a checkpoint)."},{"t":"diagram","text":"v0:  ADD A, ADD B        -> {A, B}\nv1:  REMOVE A, ADD C     -> {B, C}\nv2:  ADD D               -> {B, C, D}","caption":"Replay the log to compute each snapshot"},{"t":"p","text":"So `SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 2` must read exactly **{B, C, D}** — the files of that snapshot, not whatever is current today."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"MVCC","text":"Delta uses **MVCC (multi-version concurrency control)**: every reader picks one consistent snapshot through the transaction log and reads only its files."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"ADD file","points":["This logical file now **participates** in the snapshot.","Written by INSERT, UPDATE, MERGE, OPTIMIZE…"]},{"title":"REMOVE file","points":["This file **no longer participates** in the current snapshot.","Is **NOT** `rm file` — the bytes stay on storage.","Kept as a **tombstone** for a while."]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Why keep removed files around? So that **older snapshots** and **concurrent readers** that started earlier keep working. Physical deletion happens later, typically via **VACUUM**."},{"t":"list","items":["Snapshot isolation for readers that started before the change","Concurrent readers still mid-query","Time travel to older versions","Recovery / history semantics — until retention expires and cleanup runs"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Library catalogue","text":"The data files are books on the shelves; the log is the **catalogue**. 'REMOVE' crosses a book out of the catalogue — the book stays on the shelf until the cleaner (VACUUM) comes."},{"t":"p","text":"**Problem:** a table with 1,000,000 commits. If every reader replayed v0 → v1 → … → v1,000,000, query planning would be painfully slow."},{"t":"diagram","text":"v0.json  v1.json ... v100.checkpoint  v101.json  v102.json  v103.json\n                          │              │          │          │\nsnapshot v103 = load checkpoint v100 + replay 101 + 102 + 103\n(NOT replay 0 → 103)","caption":"Checkpoints summarize the log up to a version"},{"t":"p","text":"A **checkpoint** stores a summary of table state up to some version in **Parquet-based** structures. **Checkpoint V2** is designed for better scalability and concurrency on large / frequently updated tables."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Don't rely on checkpoint frequency","text":"The exact checkpoint interval is an **implementation/performance detail** that Databricks may adjust. Application code must never assume 'a checkpoint every exactly N commits'."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Never hand-edit `_delta_log`","text":"Do not edit `00000000000000000152.json` or delete transaction files to 'fix' a table. The log **is** the transactional protocol. Use supported commands: `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE`, `RESTORE`, `ALTER TABLE`, `VACUUM`, `OPTIMIZE` and the supported APIs."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a file exists but a query doesn't read it","questions":["Is this file referenced by the current snapshot, or only by older versions?","Was it removed by an UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE/OPTIMIZE commit (tombstone)?","Is it just waiting for VACUUM after the retention window?"]}]},{"id":"ch05-s03","title":"UPDATE & DELETE: Copy-on-Write","hook":"Object storage has no 'database pages' — so how can a row change? By swapping files atomically.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Delta supports SQL DML. Logically an `UPDATE` means:"},{"t":"flow","items":["Find matching rows","Change current table state","Commit a new Delta version"]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"UPDATE dev_training.sales.orders\nSET status = 'SHIPPED'\nWHERE order_id = 2;"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Before you read on: why can't Delta just open the Parquet file and change the bytes of row 2?","text":"Parquet files are **immutable**, compressed, columnar blobs on object storage. Changing one value in place is not supported — and readers using the old snapshot still need the old file."},{"t":"p","text":"The classic Delta mechanism is **copy-on-write**: write a new file containing the changed row plus the untouched rows, then swap files in one commit."},{"t":"diagram","text":"A.parquet (old)          B.parquet (new)\n1 | 20 | NEW             1 | 20 | NEW\n2 | 30 | NEW      ==>    2 | 30 | SHIPPED\n3 | 40 | NEW             3 | 40 | NEW\n\ncommit:  REMOVE A  +  ADD B\nbefore: snapshot -> A      after: snapshot -> B","caption":"UPDATE via file rewrite (the first mental model)"},{"t":"p","text":"A more realistic **conditional update** from the e-shop: late shipments get a 5% discount."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"UPDATE ecommerce.silver.orders\nSET\n  status = 'LATE',\n  amount = amount * 0.95\nWHERE status = 'SHIPPING'\n  AND order_ts < current_timestamp() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS;"},{"t":"p","text":"**DELETE** is just as simple. With a predicate, or without one — which logically deletes **all rows** (the table itself stays)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"DELETE FROM dev_training.sales.orders\nWHERE status = 'CANCELLED';\n\n-- no WHERE: logically removes every row\nDELETE FROM dev_training.sales.orders;"},{"t":"p","text":"`DELETE` on Delta supports fairly complex predicates and subqueries (with some limitations)."},{"t":"diagram","text":"before            after\n1 NEW             1 NEW\n2 CANCELLED  ==>  3 SHIPPED\n3 SHIPPED","caption":"State changes atomically"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Atomic = no half states","text":"No reader may ever see 'half old + half new'. A reader sees the whole old snapshot or the whole new one."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Logical removal ≠ physical removal","text":"After `DELETE`, the old files can stay on storage (history/time travel) until `VACUUM` runs."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Why UPDATE/DELETE matter in a lakehouse","text":"On plain Parquet folders, mutation semantics are hard. With Delta, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` and `MERGE` are **transactional** operations — that's why we say Delta gives **database-like table semantics on object storage**."}]},{"id":"ch05-s04","title":"Deletion Vectors","hook":"Rewriting a 1 GB file to delete one row is absurd — deletion vectors fix that.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Picture a huge Parquet file: **1 GB, 2,000,000 rows**. You want to delete **1 row**. The naïve copy-on-write model does:"},{"t":"flow","items":["Read huge file","Rewrite almost all of it","REMOVE old file","ADD replacement file"],"caption":"Very expensive for one row"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks has **deletion vectors (DV)**: instead of immediately rewriting the file, Delta records *which rows* are logically deleted."},{"t":"diagram","text":"A.parquet  +  DV: \"row 72832 is logically deleted\"\n\nread A  ->  apply deletion vector  ->  don't return row 72832","caption":"Merge-on-read: the file stays, a small marker hides rows"},{"t":"p","text":"DVs can speed up **DELETE, UPDATE and MERGE**. For an `UPDATE`, the old row version is marked deleted in the DV and the new row version is written to a small **supplemental data file**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without DV","points":["delete one row","→ rewrite the whole Parquet file","expensive write, simple read"]},{"title":"With DV","points":["data file remains","+ metadata marks the row as deleted","cheap write; reads apply the DV"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"So we correct our mental model. 'UPDATE one row → always rewrite the whole file' is **no longer generally true**:"},{"t":"diagram","text":"logical table change\n        │\nDelta chooses an implementation\n        ├── file rewrite (copy-on-write)\n        └── deletion vector + supplemental data\n(depends on table features / runtime / engine)"},{"t":"p","text":"DVs must be **enabled on the table**. Databricks recommends them unless you have a compatibility constraint with older clients."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE dev_training.sales.orders\nSET TBLPROPERTIES (\n  'delta.enableDeletionVectors' = true\n);"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Table features upgrade the protocol","text":"Enabling table features (like DVs) can **upgrade the Delta protocol** and make the table incompatible with older Delta clients/readers."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a 3 GB Parquet file, you delete 2 rows. Why is a DV much cheaper than an immediate rewrite?","text":"The engine just records that those 2 rows are logically deleted. It does **not** rewrite the other millions of rows of the 3 GB file right now."},{"t":"p","text":"Physically purging DV-soft-deleted data needs two steps: rewrite the affected files, then clean up the old ones after retention."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"REORG TABLE dev_training.sales.orders APPLY (PURGE);\n-- ...after the required retention period:\nVACUUM dev_training.sales.orders;","caption":"Physical purge of soft-deleted rows"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"DVs also help concurrency","text":"Because a row change doesn't always need a full file rewrite, two writers touching **different rows of the same file** can avoid conflicts (row-level concurrency, section 11)."}]},{"id":"ch05-s05","title":"DESCRIBE HISTORY & Time Travel","hook":"'The data broke around noon' — history + time travel turn that panic into a 3-query investigation.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**DESCRIBE HISTORY** is your first Delta debugging tool. It lists table versions in **reverse chronological order** with operation metadata."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY dev_training.sales.orders;"},{"t":"table","head":["version","timestamp","operation"],"rows":[["8","12:30","MERGE"],["7","12:10","UPDATE"],["6","11:00","WRITE"],["5","10:00","DELETE"]],"caption":"Conceptual output (newest first)"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"version / timestamp","def":"Which commit, and when."},{"term":"user","def":"Who (or which identity) ran it."},{"term":"operation","def":"WRITE, MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE, RESTORE, OPTIMIZE…"},{"term":"operationParameters","def":"e.g. the MERGE predicate, write mode."},{"term":"operationMetrics","def":"e.g. rows inserted/updated/deleted, files added/removed."}]},{"t":"p","text":"History retention (the log) is **30 days by default** (`delta.logRetentionDuration`)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Don't start by guessing","text":"Someone says 'data broke around 12'. Run `DESCRIBE HISTORY` and look for: **which operation** changed the table, **who**, **when**, **what metrics** it had."},{"t":"p","text":"**Time travel** reads an older snapshot. It never changes the current table."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT * FROM dev_training.sales.orders VERSION AS OF 5;\n\nSELECT * FROM dev_training.sales.orders\nTIMESTAMP AS OF '2026-09-15 10:00:00';"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"old_df = (\n    spark.read\n         .option(\"versionAsOf\", 121)\n         .table(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\")\n)\n# by time instead: .option(\"timestampAsOf\", \"2026-09-26 10:00:00\")\n# path-based equivalents exist where appropriate","caption":"Time travel from PySpark"},{"t":"p","text":"Real debugging workflow — 'today `customer_id` became null':"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 124;  -- looks right\nSELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 125;  -- broken!\nDESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders;  -- which operation made v125?"},{"t":"flow","items":["DESCRIBE HISTORY","Compare good vs bad version","Find the operation that created the bad version","Fix or RESTORE"],"caption":"History-driven debugging"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when 'someone broke the data'","questions":["When was the data last known to be correct?","Which version(s) were committed between 'good' and 'bad' in DESCRIBE HISTORY?","What operation, by which user/job, created the first bad version — and what do its operationMetrics say?","If I time-travel to the previous version, is the data correct there?","Do I fix forward, or RESTORE to the last good version?"]}]},{"id":"ch05-s06","title":"RESTORE & Why Time Travel Is Not a Backup","hook":"RESTORE rolls the table forward to an old state — and history has an expiry date.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Time travel","points":["`SELECT … VERSION AS OF n`","**Reads** an old snapshot","Current table unchanged","No new version"]},{"title":"RESTORE","points":["`RESTORE TABLE … TO VERSION AS OF n`","Makes an old state **current again**","Creates a **new** version","Logged in history as RESTORE"]}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"RESTORE TABLE prod.silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 124;\n\nRESTORE TABLE prod.silver.orders\nTO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2026-09-15 10:00:00';"},{"t":"diagram","text":"v124  good\nv125  bad\nv126  RESTORE  (state equivalent to v124)","caption":"RESTORE does not delete history — it appends"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"History stays history","text":"Restoring v124 while current is v125 does **not** delete v125. A new transaction (v126) is committed whose state equals v124. Restore to v7 from v10 → you get **v11**."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why is it GOOD that RESTORE appends a version instead of deleting the bad ones?","text":"You keep a full audit trail (who restored, when) and you can still inspect — or even return to — the 'bad' versions while investigating."},{"t":"p","text":"Now the classic exam/interview trap: **time travel is not a backup**. Databricks says explicitly not to use table history as a long-term backup strategy."},{"t":"table","head":["Retention","Default","Controls"],"rows":[["Deleted data files (VACUUM threshold)","7 days","`delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`"],["Transaction-log history","30 days","`delta.logRetentionDuration`"]],"caption":"Two different clocks"},{"t":"p","text":"To time travel to version N you need **both** the log entries of N **and** the data files of N. VACUUM can delete the files even while the history entry is still visible."},{"t":"diagram","text":"history metadata exists\n         ≠\nall old data files still exist"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: 'Delta history = backup'","text":"False. Retention and VACUUM limit history/time travel. For real backups use a proper backup/replication strategy (e.g. deep copies to another location)."}]},{"id":"ch05-s07","title":"VACUUM: Physical Garbage Collection","hook":"DELETE makes rows disappear from queries; VACUUM makes bytes disappear from storage.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"v0 -> {A, B}\nv1 -> {B, C}\nv2 -> {C, D}       current snapshot = {C, D}\nphysically on storage: A  B  C  D","caption":"Why old files pile up"},{"t":"p","text":"A and B may still be needed for older snapshots. After the retention interval, **VACUUM** removes files that are no longer referenced by any version inside the retention window."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"VACUUM dev_training.sales.orders;                  -- default 7 days\nVACUUM dev_training.sales.orders RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- 168 h = 7 days"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"VACUUM","points":["garbage collection","physically deletes old **unreferenced** files","no new data layout"]},{"title":"NOT VACUUM's job","points":["compacting small files → that is **OPTIMIZE** (Chapter 6)","making queries faster","changing the current snapshot"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Never `VACUUM … RETAIN 0 HOURS` blindly","text":"Deleting old/uncommitted files too early can break **time travel**, **long-running readers**, **long-running writers** (whose not-yet-committed files look 'unreferenced') and **recovery**. Databricks recommends at least 7 days and has a safety check (`spark.databricks.delta.retentionDurationCheck.enabled`) that blocks dangerously short retention."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Red flag answer","text":"\"I want to save storage, so `VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS` every hour.\" → always wrong in an exam."},{"t":"p","text":"**Production bug: DELETE worked, storage didn't shrink.** A 10 TB table; you delete everything before 2020. Queries no longer return those rows, yet storage stays near 10 TB."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"DELETE FROM orders WHERE event_date < DATE'2020-01-01';"},{"t":"list","items":["Logical delete ≠ immediate physical file deletion.","Old files are kept for snapshots/time travel.","With DVs, the **original file may still be the current physical input**, with the deleted rows merely masked.","Physical purge: `REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE)`, then `VACUUM` once the retention has passed."],"ordered":false},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when storage didn't shrink after DELETE","questions":["Is this actually a bug, or just logical vs physical deletion?","Are deletion vectors enabled, so rows are only masked inside still-current files?","Has VACUUM run since the delete — and has the retention window passed?","Do I need REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE) before VACUUM?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Production bug: time travel stopped working.** Yesterday `SELECT * FROM orders VERSION AS OF 80` worked; today it fails."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when an old version is no longer queryable","questions":["What does DESCRIBE HISTORY show — did a VACUUM run?","With which retention?","Do the data files of v80 still exist, or only its history entry?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"History entry ≠ queryable version","text":"VACUUM can remove physical files required by older versions, so a visible history entry doesn't guarantee that version is still readable."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → VACUUM breaks stopped streams","text":"VACUUM vs **streaming readers**: a stream reading this table that stays stopped longer than retention may find its needed files vacuumed and fail with a missing-file error. Never 'fix' it with `spark.sql.files.ignoreMissingFiles=true` (silent wrong results) — increase retention or rebuild the stream. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 12.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch05-s08","title":"MERGE Fundamentals","hook":"MERGE is the most important Delta command for a data engineer: one atomic statement for insert + update + delete.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Target `prod.silver.customers` holds Maria/Athens, John/Patras, Anna/Volos. A source batch arrives: John moved to Athens, George (id 4) is new."},{"t":"table","head":["","id","name","city","→ action"],"rows":[["target","1","Maria","Athens","untouched"],["target","2","John","Patras","UPDATE to Athens"],["target","3","Anna","Volos","untouched"],["source","2","John","Athens","matches id 2"],["source","4","George","Larisa","INSERT"]],"caption":"UPDATE + INSERT = upsert"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE INTO prod.silver.customers AS target\nUSING staging.customer_updates AS source\nON target.id = source.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN\n  UPDATE SET\n    target.name = source.name,\n    target.city = source.city\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN\n  INSERT (id, name, city)\n  VALUES (source.id, source.name, source.city);","caption":"Basic upsert"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"TARGET","def":"The real table I want to change."},{"term":"SOURCE","def":"The new data."},{"term":"ON","def":"When a source row and a target row are considered **the same entity**."}]},{"t":"diagram","text":"              source row\n                  │\n   does target contain a matching key?\n        ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n       YES                  NO\n        │                   │\n  WHEN MATCHED        WHEN NOT MATCHED\n(UPDATE / DELETE)        (INSERT)\n\ntarget row with NO matching source row\n                  │\n      WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE\n          (UPDATE / DELETE)","caption":"MERGE as a Venn diagram: 3 cases"},{"t":"p","text":"**WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE**: the target row exists but the source doesn't have it. Target {1,2,3,4}, source {1,2,4} → row 3. Allowed actions: **UPDATE or DELETE** (never INSERT — there's no source row to insert)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE INTO target t\nUSING source s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE;","caption":"Full sync against an authoritative snapshot"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE + incremental source = disaster","text":"If the source is only **'today's changed records'** (not a full authoritative snapshot), `WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE` deletes almost the **entire** target. Know the **semantics of your source**, not just the syntax."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when rows vanished after a MERGE","questions":["Does the MERGE contain WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE?","Is the source a full authoritative snapshot, or only today's changes?","What do the MERGE's operationMetrics in DESCRIBE HISTORY say about deleted rows?","Which version is the last good one to RESTORE?"]},{"t":"p","text":"`UPDATE SET *` / `INSERT *` are handy when source and target schemas are compatible:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE INTO target t\nUSING source s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;"},{"t":"p","text":"In production you often prefer **explicit mapping**, so you know exactly which columns change (and can add audit columns):"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"UPDATE SET\n  t.name       = s.name,\n  t.city       = s.city,\n  t.updated_at = current_timestamp()"},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Why MERGE matters for CDC","text":"A change feed usually contains inserts, updates and deletes for existing entities. `MERGE` applies all of them to the target **atomically** in one transaction — a Delta-only DML for insert/update/delete based on matching conditions."}]},{"id":"ch05-s09","title":"MERGE in Practice: Duplicates, Dedup, Python","hook":"The #1 MERGE failure in production is a duplicated key in the source — learn to spot and fix it in seconds.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hands-on first. Build this and run it — then look at `DESCRIBE HISTORY` and you'll see a **MERGE** version."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dev_training.silver.customers (\n  customer_id BIGINT, name STRING, city STRING, updated_at TIMESTAMP\n) USING DELTA;\n\nINSERT INTO dev_training.silver.customers VALUES\n  (1, 'Maria', 'Athens', current_timestamp()),\n  (2, 'John',  'Patras', current_timestamp()),\n  (3, 'Anna',  'Volos',  current_timestamp());\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE dev_training.staging.customer_updates AS\nSELECT * FROM VALUES\n  (2L, 'John',   'Athens'),\n  (4L, 'George', 'Larisa')\nAS t(customer_id, name, city);\n\nMERGE INTO dev_training.silver.customers AS t\nUSING dev_training.staging.customer_updates AS s\nON t.customer_id = s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET\n  t.name = s.name, t.city = s.city, t.updated_at = current_timestamp()\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, city, updated_at)\n  VALUES (s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, current_timestamp());\n\nDESCRIBE HISTORY dev_training.silver.customers;","caption":"Result: 1 Maria Athens · 2 John Athens · 3 Anna Volos · 4 George Larisa"},{"t":"p","text":"**The duplicate-match problem.** Target has id=7. The source has id=7/Athens **and** id=7/Volos. Which city should win?"},{"t":"diagram","text":"source: id=7 city=Athens seq=10\n        id=7 city=Volos  seq=11      target: id=7\nMERGE ... ON target.id = source.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE\n-> Athens? Volos?  ambiguous -> FAIL"},{"t":"p","text":"There is no single answer, so the MERGE fails with a **multiple source rows matched** error (`DELTA_MULTIPLE_SOURCE_ROW_MATCHING_TARGET_ROW_IN_MERGE`). Databricks requires you to **preprocess the source** so no ambiguous matches exist."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Runtime detail","text":"From **DBR 16.0+**, duplicate-match detection considers the `ON` condition **and** the relevant `WHEN MATCHED` conditions. **15.4 LTS and below** used only the `ON` condition."},{"t":"p","text":"Fix: keep only the **latest** change per key with a window function (CDC + window functions + MERGE in one real pattern)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT *,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n           PARTITION BY id\n           ORDER BY seq DESC\n         ) AS rn\n  FROM staging.customer_changes\n)\nSELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rn = 1;   -- then MERGE using this","caption":"SQL dedup: latest event per key"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nfrom pyspark.sql.window import Window\n\nw = Window.partitionBy(\"customer_id\").orderBy(F.col(\"sequence_number\").desc())\n\nlatest = (\n    source_df\n      .withColumn(\"rn\", F.row_number().over(w))\n      .filter(F.col(\"rn\") == 1)\n      .drop(\"rn\")\n)","caption":"PySpark dedup"},{"t":"flow","items":["Is target key unique?","Does source contain duplicates?","Which source row is latest?","Is there a sequence / updated_at?","Deduplicate","MERGE"],"caption":"Debug sequence for a failing MERGE"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT order_id, COUNT(*)\nFROM ecommerce.staging.orders_updates\nGROUP BY order_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;","caption":"Find the duplicated keys"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when MERGE says 'multiple source rows matched'","questions":["Is the target key really unique?","Does my source contain duplicate keys?","Which source row is the latest for each key?","Do I have a deterministic ordering field (event_sequence / updated_at / version)?","Did I deduplicate before the MERGE?"]},{"t":"p","text":"Typical ordering fields: `event_sequence`, `updated_at`, `version`."},{"t":"p","text":"**MERGE in Python** — the classic Delta API (same matching model as SQL):"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\ntarget = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"prod.silver.customers\")\nsource = spark.table(\"staging.customer_updates\")\n\n(\n    target.alias(\"t\")\n      .merge(source.alias(\"s\"), \"t.id = s.id\")\n      .whenMatchedUpdate(set={\"name\": \"s.name\", \"city\": \"s.city\"})\n      .whenNotMatchedInsert(values={\n          \"id\": \"s.id\", \"name\": \"s.name\", \"city\": \"s.city\"})\n      .execute()\n)"},{"t":"p","text":"Modern PySpark/Databricks also offers a DataFrame `mergeInto()` API — but master SQL `MERGE` and `DeltaTable` first: they're everywhere in existing production code."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"No `UPDATE … FROM … JOIN`","text":"Coming from PostgreSQL/SQL Server you may write `UPDATE target SET … FROM source WHERE …`. **Databricks SQL does not support this pattern.** To update from another relation, use `MERGE INTO`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"MERGE in a stream: `writeStream.foreachBatch(upsert)` where `upsert(batch_df, batch_id)` runs `DeltaTable.forName(...).merge(...).whenMatchedUpdateAll().whenNotMatchedInsertAll().execute()` — the classic streaming upsert, and the way to get update-like results into Delta (the Delta sink has no update mode). Keep it idempotent: a retried batch may run twice. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 13.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch05-s10","title":"Optimistic Concurrency Control","hook":"Two jobs, one table, no locks — and yet no corruption. Here's the trick.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jobs A and B start together on a table at **v50**. A wants to update customer 10, B wants to update customer 20. Both read snapshot v50."},{"t":"p","text":"Delta does **not** necessarily take a pessimistic lock ('lock the entire table, everyone wait'). It uses **optimistic concurrency control**:"},{"t":"flow","items":["READ: consistent snapshot","WRITE: prepare candidate files","VALIDATE: did a conflicting commit happen?","COMMIT: new atomic version, or fail/retry"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why 'optimistic'?** The system assumes most concurrent transactions probably won't conflict. It lets A and B both work, and only at commit time asks: *can both results coexist consistently?* Yes → commit. No → conflict."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Google Docs vs a library book","text":"Pessimistic = only one person can borrow the book. Optimistic = everyone edits their copy; at 'save' time the system checks whether edits collide."},{"t":"p","text":"**Atomic version creation**: current = v50. A commits first → **v51**. B (based on v50) must now check: *what changed from v50 → v51, and does it affect rows/files I read or change?*"},{"t":"diagram","text":"v50 ──► A commits ──► v51\n │\n └─ B (read v50) validates v50→v51\n        ├─ no conflict ──► commits v52\n        └─ conflict    ──► transaction FAILS\n                           (table not corrupted)"},{"t":"p","text":"Put together, every Delta write follows the same skeleton:"},{"t":"diagram","text":"      CURRENT SNAPSHOT\n             │\n       READ / ANALYZE\n             │\nprepare data-file changes (files / DVs)\n             │\n   optimistic validation\n      ┌──────┴──────┐\n  conflict?        safe?\n      │              │\n    FAIL       ATOMIC COMMIT\n                     │\n                NEW VERSION\n                     │\n                NEW SNAPSHOT","caption":"The mental model for every Delta write"},{"t":"p","text":"When you read `MERGE INTO prod.silver.customers …` don't think only 'update/insert'. See the whole pipeline:"},{"t":"flow","items":["Source rows","Matching (ON)","Read target snapshot","Determine changed rows/files","Write new data / DVs","Optimistic conflict validation","Atomic log commit","New version","New consistent snapshot"],"caption":"A MERGE, end to end"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Data engineer level","text":"Once this pipeline is instinctive, most Delta concepts stop being memorization."}]},{"id":"ch05-s11","title":"Isolation Levels & Row-Level Concurrency","hook":"Which conflicts are real and which are just 'same file, different row'?","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Delta on Databricks supports two **isolation levels** for writes:"},{"t":"table","head":["Level","Meaning"],"rows":[["WriteSerializable (default)","Writes are serializable; weaker than Serializable but allows more concurrency."],["Serializable","Strongest: the outcome of concurrent reads **and** writes must be explainable by a fully serial order."]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Reads** use **snapshot isolation** — every reader sees one consistent version."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE my_table\nSET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.isolationLevel' = 'Serializable');"},{"t":"diagram","text":"readers         -> consistent snapshots\nwriters         -> optimistic validation\ndefault writes  -> WriteSerializable","caption":"Memorize this trio (not the full conflict matrix)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Row-level concurrency.** Older behaviour: A updates row 1 in file X, B updates row 9000 in the same file X → they could **conflict** because both touched file X."},{"t":"p","text":"With **row-level concurrency + deletion vectors**, Databricks detects conflicts at **row** granularity, so A and B can both succeed even though their rows share a physical file."},{"t":"list","items":["Supported Databricks Runtime","Deletion vectors enabled","Unpartitioned tables — liquid-clustered tables also use this architecture"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Same row still conflicts","text":"Job A: `UPDATE customers SET status='A' WHERE id=10`; Job B: `… status='B' WHERE id=10`. That's a **real logical conflict**: one commits, the other fails or is serialized safely — never random corruption."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Different rows, same file","points":["Old: possible conflict (file-level)","Now: can coexist with DVs + row-level concurrency"]},{"title":"Same row","points":["Always a real conflict","One commit wins, the other fails/retries"]}]}]},{"id":"ch05-s12","title":"Concurrency Errors & Debugging","hook":"A concurrency exception means Delta protected you — your job is to find out from whom.","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Error","What it usually means"],"rows":[["DELTA_CONCURRENT_WRITE","A concurrent transaction wrote new data after your transaction read the table."],["ConcurrentAppendException (DELTA_CONCURRENT_APPEND)","Another operation added files to the data/partition your operation read."],["ConcurrentDeleteReadException (DELTA_CONCURRENT_DELETE_READ)","Another operation deleted/rewrote a file your operation read."],["MetadataChangedException","A concurrent operation changed table metadata (e.g. `ALTER TABLE`, schema-evolving write)."],["ProtocolChangedException","The table protocol changed concurrently (e.g. table feature upgrade, concurrent create/replace)."],["ConcurrentTransactionException","Two writers with the same transaction identity (e.g. two streams sharing one checkpoint) collided."]],"caption":"Concurrency errors you will meet"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Not corruption","text":"These errors do **not** mean 'the Delta table is corrupted'. They usually mean **the optimistic concurrency system prevented an unsafe commit**. Databricks suggests **retry logic** for legitimate transient conflicts — after **re-reading fresh state**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Bad response","points":["'Run it again until it works.'"]},{"title":"Engineer response","points":["Who else wrote?","What did both read?","Can I narrow the scope?","Is a schema change running?","Then decide on retry."]}]},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a MERGE fails with DELTA_CONCURRENT_WRITE","questions":["What other writer touched this table at the same time (DESCRIBE HISTORY)?","What rows/files/partitions did both operations read?","Are my predicates too broad?","Can I narrow the MERGE condition (e.g. add a date bound)?","Is a schema ALTER happening concurrently?","Are deletion vectors / row-level concurrency available on this table?","Should the application retry after re-reading fresh state?"]},{"t":"p","text":"`MERGE … ON t.id = s.id` on a huge table can have a **broad read scope**. If it is semantically correct, add a physical/time bound:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE INTO prod.silver.orders t\nUSING daily_updates s\nON t.id = s.id\n   AND t.event_date = DATE'2026-09-16'\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","caption":"Narrower ON = smaller scan AND smaller conflict domain"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Only if semantically correct","text":"Adding `t.event_date = …` is valid only if matching target rows really live on that date. Otherwise you'd insert duplicates for rows from other dates."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Schema changes are metadata changes","text":"An `ALTER TABLE` at 12:00 while a pipeline writes can fail that writer with `MetadataChangedException` — expected semantics, not a bug. Full story in Chapter 6."},{"t":"p","text":"**What's next:** Unity Catalog in depth — `catalog.schema.object`, managed vs external tables, volumes, external locations, grants, and real `PERMISSION_DENIED` debugging."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Unity Catalog in depth is here","text":"The promised deep dive (catalog.schema.object, managed vs external, volumes, external locations, grants, PERMISSION_DENIED debugging) is now written — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch05-d01","title":"MERGE fails: multiple source rows matched","section":"ch05-s09","symptom":"MERGE aborts with `DELTA_MULTIPLE_SOURCE_ROW_MATCHING_TARGET_ROW_IN_MERGE` (\"multiple source rows matched target row\").","askYourself":["Is the target key really unique?","Does my source contain duplicate keys (GROUP BY … HAVING COUNT(*) > 1)?","Which source row is the latest for each key?","Do I have a deterministic ordering field (event_sequence / updated_at / version)?","Did I deduplicate before the MERGE, and does my ON (+ WHEN MATCHED conditions on DBR 16+) still allow multiple matches?"],"steps":[{"do":"Count duplicate keys in the source","why":"Confirms the ambiguity and shows which keys","code":"SELECT order_id, COUNT(*)\nFROM ecommerce.staging.orders_updates\nGROUP BY order_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;"},{"do":"Pick the ordering field","why":"Makes the choice deterministic and retry-safe"},{"do":"Deduplicate with ROW_NUMBER","why":"Keep exactly one latest row per key","code":"ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY event_sequence DESC) AS rn  -- keep rn = 1"},{"do":"Re-run MERGE using the deduplicated source","why":"No ambiguity → MERGE succeeds"}],"rootCauses":["CDC feed delivers several events per key in one batch","Source join fan-out created duplicates","Target key not unique"],"fix":"Deduplicate the source by business key with a deterministic ordering field (ROW_NUMBER … rn = 1), then MERGE.","mnemonic":"U-D-L-O-D-M: Unique? Dupes? Latest? Ordering field? Dedup! Merge!"},{"id":"ch05-d02","title":"Someone broke the data","section":"ch05-s05","symptom":"Data was correct at 09:00, wrong at 11:00 (e.g. `customer_id` suddenly NULL); corruption shows up in some version vN.","askYourself":["When was the data last known to be correct?","Which versions were committed between good and bad (DESCRIBE HISTORY)?","If I time-travel to the previous version, is it correct?","Which operation, user/job and operationMetrics created the first bad version?","Should I fix forward, or RESTORE to the last good version — and are its files still there?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE HISTORY","why":"Lists versions, operations, users, metrics newest first","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Compare good vs bad with time travel","why":"Pinpoints the exact version that broke","code":"SELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 124;\nSELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 125;"},{"do":"Inspect the guilty operation","why":"Root cause: which job/user, which predicate"},{"do":"Repair","why":"RESTORE creates a new version equal to the good one","code":"RESTORE TABLE prod.silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 124;"}],"rootCauses":["Buggy job/UPDATE/MERGE","Wrong source data merged","Manual change by a user"],"fix":"Fix the job, then fix forward or `RESTORE TABLE … TO VERSION AS OF <good>` (new version, history kept).","mnemonic":"H-C-B-R: History, Compare, Blame, Restore"},{"id":"ch05-d03","title":"DELETE worked but storage didn't shrink","section":"ch05-s07","symptom":"`DELETE` succeeded, queries don't return the rows, but cloud storage usage is unchanged (e.g. still ~10 TB).","askYourself":["Is this really a bug, or logical vs physical deletion?","Are old files simply retained for snapshots/time travel?","Are deletion vectors enabled, so rows are only masked inside still-current files?","Has VACUUM run since, and has the retention window passed?","Do I need REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE) first?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check history and table properties","why":"See DELETE/VACUUM operations and whether DVs are on","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY orders;"},{"do":"Purge DV-masked rows","why":"Rewrites files without soft-deleted rows","code":"REORG TABLE orders APPLY (PURGE);"},{"do":"VACUUM after retention","why":"Physically removes unreferenced old files","code":"VACUUM orders;"}],"rootCauses":["Logical delete ≠ physical deletion","Files retained for time travel","DV soft-deletes keep original files current"],"fix":"REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE), then VACUUM once retention has passed. Never shortcut with RETAIN 0 HOURS.","mnemonic":"Logical ≠ Physical: PURGE then VACUUM"},{"id":"ch05-d04","title":"Time travel to an old version stopped working","section":"ch05-s07","symptom":"Yesterday `SELECT * FROM orders VERSION AS OF 80` worked; today it errors (files not found).","askYourself":["What does DESCRIBE HISTORY show — did a VACUUM run?","With which retention?","Do the data files of v80 still exist, or only its history entry?","Is v80 still inside the 30-day log retention?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE HISTORY orders","why":"Find a VACUUM operation and its parameters","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY orders;"},{"do":"Compare v80's age with retention settings","why":"7-day file retention vs 30-day log retention"}],"rootCauses":["VACUUM removed files needed by v80","Version older than retention","Short custom retention"],"fix":"Accept that time travel isn't a backup; keep ≥7 days retention and use a real backup strategy for long-term recovery.","mnemonic":"History entry ≠ files exist"},{"id":"ch05-d05","title":"Concurrent write conflict (DELTA_CONCURRENT_WRITE)","section":"ch05-s12","symptom":"MERGE/UPDATE fails with `DELTA_CONCURRENT_WRITE`, `ConcurrentAppendException`, `ConcurrentDeleteReadException`, `MetadataChangedException`…","askYourself":["What other writer touched this table at the same time?","What rows/files/partitions did both operations read?","Are my predicates too broad?","Can I narrow the MERGE condition (e.g. a date bound)?","Is a schema ALTER happening concurrently?","Are deletion vectors / row-level concurrency available?","Should the application retry after re-reading fresh state?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE HISTORY around the failure time","why":"Identify the competing transaction","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Narrow the read scope if semantically correct","why":"Smaller scan, smaller conflict domain","code":"ON t.id = s.id AND t.event_date = DATE'2026-09-16'"},{"do":"Enable DVs / row-level concurrency where possible","why":"Different-row changes stop conflicting","code":"ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableDeletionVectors' = true);"},{"do":"Add retry-on-fresh-state for transient conflicts","why":"Legitimate conflicts resolve on retry"}],"rootCauses":["Two jobs writing overlapping data","Over-broad MERGE predicates","Concurrent schema change","File-level conflicts without DVs"],"fix":"Coordinate writers, narrow scopes, enable DV/RLC, retry after re-reading — never blind 'run until it works'.","mnemonic":"Who-What-Wide-Schema-Rows-Retry"},{"id":"ch05-d06","title":"Rows vanished after a MERGE","section":"ch05-s08","symptom":"After a routine MERGE, most of the target table is gone; DESCRIBE HISTORY shows a MERGE with huge `numTargetRowsDeleted`.","askYourself":["Does the MERGE have WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE?","Is the source a full authoritative snapshot or just today's changes?","What do the MERGE's operationMetrics show (rows deleted)?","Which version was the last good one to RESTORE?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE HISTORY","why":"Find the MERGE and its delete metrics","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.customers;"},{"do":"RESTORE the last good version","why":"New version equal to pre-MERGE state","code":"RESTORE TABLE prod.silver.customers TO VERSION AS OF 41;"},{"do":"Remove/condition the clause","why":"Only use NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE with authoritative snapshots"}],"rootCauses":["NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE DELETE with an incremental source"],"fix":"Restore, then drop the clause (or bound it with a condition) unless the source is a complete snapshot.","mnemonic":"Absent ≠ deleted"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Physical files ≠ table","text":"Counting/reading .parquet files in the folder includes removed files.","fix":"Trust the snapshot in `_delta_log`; query through the table."},{"title":"REMOVE is not rm","text":"A remove action only drops the file from the snapshot.","fix":"Expect files to remain until VACUUM after retention."},{"title":"Relying on checkpoint frequency","text":"Checkpoint interval is an implementation detail.","fix":"Never code against 'every N commits'."},{"title":"Hand-editing _delta_log","text":"Editing/deleting commit JSONs breaks the protocol.","fix":"Use RESTORE, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, ALTER, VACUUM, OPTIMIZE."},{"title":"'UPDATE always rewrites the whole file'","text":"Outdated with deletion vectors.","fix":"Delta chooses rewrite or DV + supplemental data."},{"title":"Table features break old clients","text":"Enabling DVs etc. can upgrade the protocol.","fix":"Check reader compatibility before enabling."},{"title":"Time travel ≠ backup","text":"7-day file retention and 30-day log retention limit history.","fix":"Use a real backup strategy."},{"title":"RESTORE deletes history?","text":"No — it appends a new version.","fix":"Expect vN+1 equal to the restored state."},{"title":"VACUUM ≠ OPTIMIZE","text":"VACUUM deletes old unreferenced files; it doesn't compact.","fix":"Use OPTIMIZE for layout, VACUUM for cleanup."},{"title":"VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS","text":"Breaks time travel, long-running readers/writers, recovery.","fix":"Keep ≥ 7 days and the safety check enabled."},{"title":"Storage didn't shrink after DELETE","text":"Logical delete ≠ physical deletion (DVs mask rows).","fix":"REORG … APPLY (PURGE) then VACUUM."},{"title":"NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE DELETE on incremental source","text":"Deletes everything not in today's batch.","fix":"Only with authoritative full snapshots."},{"title":"Duplicate keys in MERGE source","text":"Ambiguous match → MERGE fails.","fix":"Dedup with ROW_NUMBER by ordering field."},{"title":"UPDATE … FROM … JOIN","text":"Not supported in Databricks SQL.","fix":"Use MERGE INTO."},{"title":"'Run it again until it works'","text":"Hides real concurrency causes.","fix":"Investigate writers/scope; retry only after fresh read."},{"title":"Concurrency error = corruption?","text":"No — it's protection against an unsafe commit.","fix":"Find the competing writer."},{"title":"VACUUM vs stopped streams","text":"A stream stopped longer than the source retention can't catch up once its files are vacuumed.","fix":"Run streams at least once per retention window; increase retention; never use ignoreMissingFiles as a workaround.","src":"part2"}],"flashcards":[{"q":"Delta = ?","a":"Parquet data files + versioned transaction log (`_delta_log`) → ACID table semantics.","section":"ch05-s01"},{"q":"Commit file name format?","a":"20-digit zero-padded version + .json, e.g. `00000000000000000003.json`.","section":"ch05-s01"},{"q":"What is the 'table' in Delta?","a":"The set of logical files the log says belong to the current snapshot.","section":"ch05-s01"},{"q":"50 physical files, snapshot lists 37 → current files?","a":"37.","section":"ch05-s01"},{"q":"What can an `add` action contain?","a":"path, size, dataChange, partition values, modification time, file stats (numRecords, min/max).","section":"ch05-s01"},{"q":"What is a snapshot?","a":"Table state at a version = replay of log actions (from a checkpoint).","section":"ch05-s02"},{"q":"MVCC in Delta?","a":"Readers pick one consistent snapshot via the log; writers create new versions.","section":"ch05-s02"},{"q":"Does REMOVE delete the file?","a":"No — tombstone; physical deletion later via VACUUM.","section":"ch05-s02"},{"q":"Checkpoint purpose?","a":"Parquet-based summary of state so readers don't replay from v0.","section":"ch05-s02"},{"q":"Checkpoint V2?","a":"Newer checkpoint design for scalability/concurrency on large, frequently updated tables.","section":"ch05-s02"},{"q":"Classic UPDATE mechanism?","a":"Copy-on-write: write new file B, commit REMOVE A + ADD B.","section":"ch05-s03"},{"q":"DELETE without WHERE?","a":"Logically deletes all rows; table object stays.","section":"ch05-s03"},{"q":"Deletion vector?","a":"Metadata marking specific rows of a file as deleted, avoiding immediate rewrite.","section":"ch05-s04"},{"q":"Which ops do DVs speed up?","a":"DELETE, UPDATE, MERGE.","section":"ch05-s04"},{"q":"Enable DVs?","a":"`ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableDeletionVectors' = true)`.","section":"ch05-s04"},{"q":"Physically purge DV-deleted rows?","a":"`REORG TABLE t APPLY (PURGE)` then `VACUUM` after retention.","section":"ch05-s04"},{"q":"DESCRIBE HISTORY order & content?","a":"Newest first: version, timestamp, user, operation, operationParameters, operationMetrics.","section":"ch05-s05"},{"q":"Time travel SQL?","a":"`SELECT … FROM t VERSION AS OF n` / `TIMESTAMP AS OF '…'`.","section":"ch05-s05"},{"q":"Time travel PySpark?","a":"`spark.read.option('versionAsOf', n).table('t')` (or timestampAsOf).","section":"ch05-s05"},{"q":"Default log retention?","a":"30 days (`delta.logRetentionDuration`).","section":"ch05-s05"},{"q":"RESTORE vs time travel?","a":"Time travel reads old snapshot; RESTORE makes it current via a NEW version.","section":"ch05-s06"},{"q":"Current v10, RESTORE to v7 → ?","a":"v11 (state = v7); v8–v10 remain in history.","section":"ch05-s06"},{"q":"Is time travel a backup?","a":"No — finite retention + VACUUM.","section":"ch05-s06"},{"q":"VACUUM default retention?","a":"7 days = RETAIN 168 HOURS.","section":"ch05-s07"},{"q":"VACUUM does what?","a":"Physically deletes old unreferenced files beyond retention (garbage collection).","section":"ch05-s07"},{"q":"Why not RETAIN 0 HOURS?","a":"Breaks time travel, long-running readers/writers, recovery; safety check blocks it.","section":"ch05-s07"},{"q":"DELETE done, storage same — why?","a":"Logical ≠ physical; files kept for history; DVs mask rows in current files.","section":"ch05-s07"},{"q":"WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE allows?","a":"UPDATE or DELETE (not INSERT).","section":"ch05-s08"},{"q":"Danger of NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE DELETE?","a":"With an incremental source it deletes legit rows missing from the batch.","section":"ch05-s08"},{"q":"MERGE's three cases?","a":"MATCHED (both), NOT MATCHED (source only), NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE (target only).","section":"ch05-s08"},{"q":"MERGE error with duplicate source keys?","a":"Multiple source rows matched the same target row → ambiguous → fail.","section":"ch05-s09"},{"q":"Dedup pattern?","a":"ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY key ORDER BY seq DESC), keep rn = 1.","section":"ch05-s09"},{"q":"DBR 16.0+ duplicate-match rule?","a":"Considers ON + relevant WHEN MATCHED conditions (≤15.4 LTS: ON only).","section":"ch05-s09"},{"q":"UPDATE … FROM … JOIN on Databricks?","a":"Not supported → use MERGE INTO.","section":"ch05-s09"},{"q":"Python MERGE entry point?","a":"`DeltaTable.forName(spark, name).alias('t').merge(src.alias('s'), cond)…execute()`.","section":"ch05-s09"},{"q":"OCC phases?","a":"READ → WRITE → VALIDATE → COMMIT.","section":"ch05-s10"},{"q":"Why 'optimistic'?","a":"Assumes no conflict; checks only at commit.","section":"ch05-s10"},{"q":"Default write isolation?","a":"WriteSerializable (reads: snapshot isolation).","section":"ch05-s11"},{"q":"Different rows, same file — conflict?","a":"Not necessarily: DVs + row-level concurrency let them coexist.","section":"ch05-s11"},{"q":"Same row by two writers?","a":"Real conflict: one commits, other fails/serializes.","section":"ch05-s11"},{"q":"MetadataChangedException cause?","a":"Concurrent metadata change: ALTER TABLE or schema-evolving write.","section":"ch05-s12"},{"q":"Concurrency exception = corrupted table?","a":"No — OCC prevented an unsafe commit.","section":"ch05-s12"},{"q":"How to shrink conflict domain of a MERGE?","a":"Narrow ON with a semantically correct bound (e.g. event_date).","section":"ch05-s12"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch05-e001","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A Delta table consists of every `.parquet` file that physically exists in the table's folder.","answer":false,"explain":"The table is the set of **logical** files that the transaction log references in the current snapshot. Old files (removed by UPDATE/DELETE/OPTIMIZE) can still sit in the folder for time travel or until VACUUM — they are not part of the current table."},{"id":"ch05-e002","type":"calc","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A Delta table folder physically holds **50** Parquet files. The current snapshot in `_delta_log` references **37** of them. How many files are logically part of the current table?","answer":37,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"**37.** Physical existence does not imply current logical membership. The other 13 are typically tombstoned files kept for older snapshots/time travel or waiting for VACUUM."},{"id":"ch05-e003","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Which filename is a valid Delta commit file for **version 3**?","options":["`3.json`","`00000000000000000003.json`","`v3.parquet`","`commit-0003.log`"],"answer":1,"why":["Not zero-padded.","Correct: 20 digits, zero-padded, JSON.","Commits are JSON; Parquet is used for data files and checkpoints.","Invented naming."],"explain":"Commit files use **20-digit zero-padded** version numbers with a `.json` extension. Zero padding keeps lexical order = version order when listing the folder."},{"id":"ch05-e004","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Storage contains `A.parquet`, `B.parquet`, `C.parquet`; the current snapshot lists only A and C. What is the **most likely** reason B still exists?","options":["B is corrupted and Delta skips it","B is an old file kept for time travel / not yet removed by VACUUM","B is a checkpoint file","B is a staging file Spark forgot to delete, so the table is broken"],"answer":1,"why":["Delta doesn't skip files because of corruption; it reads exactly what the snapshot lists.","Correct.","Checkpoints live inside `_delta_log`, not as data files.","Unreferenced files are expected; the table is fine."],"explain":"B was probably removed logically by an earlier commit (REMOVE action) and is retained for older snapshots until VACUUM cleans it up after the retention window. This is normal, not corruption."},{"id":"ch05-e005","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which information can a real `add` action in the transaction log carry? (choose all)","options":["File path and size","`dataChange` flag","Partition values and modification time","File-level statistics (record count, min/max)","The full row data of the file"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"An `add` action describes a file: path, size, `dataChange`, partition values, modification time and per-file statistics. The rows themselves live only in the Parquet file — the log stores metadata, not data."},{"id":"ch05-e006","type":"bucket","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Where does each thing live?","buckets":["Parquet data files","_delta_log/"],"items":[{"text":"Actual column values of orders","bucket":0},{"text":"JSON commit per version","bucket":1},{"text":"Columnar compressed rows","bucket":0},{"text":"ADD / REMOVE actions","bucket":1},{"text":"Checkpoint summaries of table state","bucket":1},{"text":"Table version history","bucket":1}],"explain":"Parquet files hold the **data**; `_delta_log` holds the **versioned state**: commit JSONs with actions, plus checkpoints. Checkpoints are Parquet-based but they live in the log folder and summarize metadata, not business rows."},{"id":"ch05-e007","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the storage-side formula of a Delta table.","text":"Parquet files + optional [[deletion vectors|DVs|deletion vector]] + _delta_log [[versions|commits]] + [[checkpoints|checkpoint]] = Delta table state","bank":["indexes","locks","partitions"],"explain":"The table state is assembled from the data files, any deletion vectors that mask rows, the versioned commits and the checkpoints that summarize them. If you can rebuild this in your head, MERGE/VACUUM/concurrency become predictable."},{"id":"ch05-e008","type":"free","section":"ch05-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview: **\"Parquet already stores data, so why do I need Delta?\"** Answer in 3–4 sentences.","model":"Parquet defines how columnar data is stored in individual files, but it doesn't define transactional table state across many files. Delta adds a versioned transaction log on top of those files, giving atomic (ACID) commits and snapshot isolation. That enables DML such as MERGE/UPDATE/DELETE, schema enforcement and evolution, concurrency control, history and time travel, plus physical optimization features like OPTIMIZE, data skipping and clustering.","rubric":["Parquet = file format, not table state across files","Versioned transaction log","ACID / atomic commits + snapshot isolation","DML: MERGE/UPDATE/DELETE","Schema enforcement/evolution, time travel, concurrency control, optimization"],"explain":"Strong answers contrast **file format** vs **table semantics**. Mentioning only 'Delta is faster' misses the point: the core value is the transaction log and everything it enables."},{"id":"ch05-e009","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Replay this log. Which files form the snapshot of **version 2**?","code":"v0: ADD A, ADD B\nv1: REMOVE A, ADD C\nv2: ADD D","options":["{A, B, C, D}","{B, C, D}","{C, D}","{A, C, D}"],"answer":1,"why":["Includes A, which was removed in v1.","Correct replay.","B was never removed.","A was removed; B was not."],"explain":"v0 → {A, B}; v1 removes A and adds C → {B, C}; v2 adds D → {B, C, D}. A is still on storage but not in the snapshot."},{"id":"ch05-e010","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A `remove` action in the Delta log means the file is immediately deleted from cloud storage (`rm`).","answer":false,"explain":"`remove` only means the file **no longer participates** in the snapshot. It is kept as a tombstone so older snapshots and concurrent readers still work; physical deletion happens later via VACUUM."},{"id":"ch05-e011","type":"order","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the steps a reader uses to build the snapshot of **v103** when a checkpoint exists at v100.","items":["Load checkpoint v100","Replay v101.json","Replay v102.json","Replay v103.json"],"explain":"Readers start from the latest checkpoint at or before the target version and replay only the JSON commits after it. Replaying from v0 would make planning expensive on tables with thousands of commits."},{"id":"ch05-e012","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why does Delta keep old (removed) data files around for a while? (choose all)","options":["Snapshot isolation for readers that started earlier","Concurrent readers mid-query","Time travel to older versions","To make current queries faster","Recovery/history semantics until retention + cleanup"],"answer":[0,1,2,4],"multi":true,"explain":"Old files serve older snapshots: in-flight readers, time travel and recovery. They do **not** speed up current queries — current queries never read them."},{"id":"ch05-e013","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What does a Delta **checkpoint** do?","options":["Physically deletes old files","Stores a summarized table state so readers don't replay the log from version 0","Locks the table during writes","Compacts small data files"],"answer":1,"why":["That's VACUUM.","Correct.","Delta uses optimistic concurrency, no table lock.","That's OPTIMIZE."],"explain":"A checkpoint summarizes the log up to a version in Parquet-based structures. Deleting files is VACUUM; compaction is OPTIMIZE; Delta doesn't lock the table for writes."},{"id":"ch05-e014","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Your application can safely assume Delta writes a checkpoint every exactly N commits.","answer":false,"explain":"Checkpoint frequency is an implementation/performance detail and Databricks may adjust it (Checkpoint V2 even changes the structure). Code that depends on a fixed interval is fragile."},{"id":"ch05-e015","type":"spotbug","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A runbook to 'undo' a bad commit. Which line is the dangerous one?","lines":["-- bad data appeared in version 152","DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders;","%sh rm /mnt/lake/orders/_delta_log/00000000000000000152.json","SELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 151;"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"RESTORE TABLE prod.silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 151;","explain":"Never edit or delete files in `_delta_log` — the log is the transactional protocol and hand edits can corrupt the table for every reader/writer. Use the supported `RESTORE` operation, which appends a new version equivalent to v151."},{"id":"ch05-e016","type":"odd","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT a supported way to change a Delta table's state?","options":["`RESTORE TABLE … TO VERSION AS OF 5`","`VACUUM t`","Editing `00000000000000000152.json` by hand","`ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES (…)`"],"answer":2,"explain":"UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, RESTORE, ALTER TABLE, VACUUM, OPTIMIZE and supported APIs are the legit tools. Hand-editing the log bypasses the protocol."},{"id":"ch05-e017","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A Delta `UPDATE` opens the existing Parquet file and edits the changed bytes in place.","answer":false,"explain":"Parquet files are immutable. Delta writes new data (rewritten file or supplemental file + DV) and commits the swap atomically. Old snapshots still point at the untouched old file."},{"id":"ch05-e018","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"File A holds 3 rows; you update row 2 using classic copy-on-write. Which commit is written?","options":["`UPDATE A row 2`","`REMOVE A` + `ADD B` (B = all 3 rows, row 2 changed)","`ADD B` (B = only row 2)","`REMOVE A` only"],"answer":1,"why":["There's no in-place row action in the log.","Correct.","Old row 2 would still be visible in A → duplicate.","Rows 1 and 3 would vanish."],"explain":"Copy-on-write rewrites the file: B contains rows 1 and 3 unchanged plus the new row 2, and the commit atomically removes A and adds B. Adding only row 2 would leave the old row 2 visible in A."},{"id":"ch05-e019","type":"write","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the conditional update: orders in status `SHIPPING` older than 7 days become `LATE` and get a 5% discount on `amount`. Table: `ecommerce.silver.orders` (cols `status`, `amount`, `order_ts`).","solution":"UPDATE ecommerce.silver.orders\nSET status = 'LATE',\n    amount = amount * 0.95\nWHERE status = 'SHIPPING'\n  AND order_ts < current_timestamp() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS;","keywords":["update ecommerce.silver.orders","set","amount * 0.95","where","interval 7 days"],"lang":"sql","explain":"One `UPDATE` can set several columns. The time predicate uses `current_timestamp() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS`. Delta then decides the physical implementation (rewrite or DV) and commits a new version."},{"id":"ch05-e020","type":"write","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Delete all cancelled orders from `dev_training.sales.orders`.","solution":"DELETE FROM dev_training.sales.orders\nWHERE status = 'CANCELLED';","keywords":["delete from dev_training.sales.orders","where status = 'cancelled'"],"lang":"sql","explain":"`DELETE FROM … WHERE …` removes rows logically and commits a new version. The files may remain physically until VACUUM."},{"id":"ch05-e021","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"`DELETE FROM dev_training.sales.orders;` (no WHERE) drops the table object.","answer":false,"explain":"Without WHERE it logically deletes **all rows**, but the table object, schema and history remain. Dropping the object is `DROP TABLE`."},{"id":"ch05-e022","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A long `SELECT` is running while a `DELETE` commits on the same Delta table. What can the reader see?","options":["A mix: some deleted rows, some not","Either the full old snapshot or the full new snapshot — never half","An error, because the table is locked","Random results until VACUUM runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Commits are atomic and readers use snapshot isolation: a reader is pinned to one version for its whole query. 'Half old + half new' is exactly what Delta guarantees never happens."},{"id":"ch05-e023","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the UPDATE.","text":"[[UPDATE]] dev_training.sales.orders\n[[SET]] status = 'SHIPPED'\n[[WHERE]] order_id = 2;","bank":["MERGE","VALUES","FROM"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Standard SQL DML. On Delta it results in a new table version; the old version stays readable via time travel."},{"id":"ch05-e024","type":"free","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why do we say Delta gives 'database-like table semantics on object storage'? Use UPDATE/DELETE as your example.","model":"On a plain Parquet folder you can't safely change a row: you'd have to rewrite files by hand and readers could see half-written states. Delta turns UPDATE, DELETE and MERGE into transactional operations: it writes new files (or deletion vectors) and atomically commits a new version in the log, so readers always see a consistent snapshot. That's the kind of mutation guarantee you'd expect from a database, on top of cheap object storage.","rubric":["Plain Parquet: mutation is hard/unsafe","Delta DML is transactional","New files/DVs + atomic log commit","Readers see consistent snapshots"],"explain":"The key is **transactional mutation**. Answers that just list commands miss why they're safe: the atomic commit to the log."},{"id":"ch05-e025","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"A 3 GB Parquet file; you delete 2 rows. Why can a deletion vector be much cheaper than an immediate file rewrite?","options":["DVs compress the file","Delta records that 2 rows are logically deleted without rewriting the millions of remaining rows now","DVs delete the rows from cloud storage directly","DVs skip writing to the transaction log"],"answer":1,"why":["DVs don't compress.","Correct.","Nothing is physically deleted — rows are masked.","Every change is still a log commit."],"explain":"A DV is a small marker of deleted row positions. The expensive rewrite of the 3 GB file is avoided (or deferred, e.g. to OPTIMIZE/REORG). The commit still goes through the log."},{"id":"ch05-e026","type":"order","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order what a reader does on a table whose file has a deletion vector.","items":["Read the snapshot from the log","Read data file A.parquet","Apply the deletion vector","Return rows not marked deleted"],"explain":"The reader combines the underlying data file with the DV state and never returns logically deleted rows. That's why reads pay a small cost while writes become cheap."},{"id":"ch05-e027","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"With deletion vectors, an `UPDATE` can mark the old row version as deleted and write the new row version into a small supplemental data file.","answer":true,"explain":"That's exactly how DVs accelerate UPDATE (and MERGE): mark old row in the DV, write only the new row version, instead of rewriting the full file."},{"id":"ch05-e028","type":"write","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Enable deletion vectors on `dev_training.sales.orders`.","solution":"ALTER TABLE dev_training.sales.orders\nSET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableDeletionVectors' = true);","keywords":["alter table dev_training.sales.orders","set tblproperties","delta.enableDeletionVectors"],"lang":"sql","explain":"DVs are a table feature set via a table property. Databricks recommends them unless older clients must still read the table."},{"id":"ch05-e029","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"What is the main risk when enabling a table feature such as deletion vectors?","options":["It deletes table history","It can upgrade the Delta protocol, making the table unreadable by older Delta clients","It disables time travel","It turns the table into a Parquet table"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table features may raise the minimum reader/writer protocol versions. Old engines/clients that don't support the feature can no longer read/write the table — check compatibility first."},{"id":"ch05-e030","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"In modern Databricks, updating one row **always** rewrites the whole Parquet file.","answer":false,"explain":"That was the first mental model, but with deletion vectors Delta can choose between file rewrite and DV + supplemental data, depending on table features/runtime/engine."},{"id":"ch05-e031","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Physically purge rows soft-deleted via deletion vectors.","text":"[[REORG]] TABLE dev_training.sales.orders APPLY ([[PURGE]]);\n-- after the retention period has passed:\n[[VACUUM]] dev_training.sales.orders;","bank":["OPTIMIZE","RESTORE","TRUNCATE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"REORG … APPLY (PURGE) rewrites files so soft-deleted rows are physically gone from current files; VACUUM later removes the old files once they're beyond retention. Either step alone isn't enough."},{"id":"ch05-e032","type":"match","section":"ch05-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each DV situation to what happens.","pairs":[["DELETE 1 row with DVs on","Row marked deleted in a DV; file kept"],["Reading a file with a DV","File + DV combined; masked rows skipped"],["UPDATE 1 row with DVs on","Old row masked + new row in supplemental file"],["REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE)","Files rewritten without soft-deleted rows"]],"explain":"DVs make writes cheap by deferring rewrites; reads apply the DV; PURGE finally materializes the deletion physically."},{"id":"ch05-e033","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Read two old snapshots.","text":"SELECT * FROM dev_training.sales.orders [[VERSION AS OF]] 5;\nSELECT * FROM dev_training.sales.orders [[TIMESTAMP AS OF]] '2026-09-15 10:00:00';","bank":["RESTORE TO","AS OF VERSION","SNAPSHOT"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Time travel by version number or by timestamp. Neither changes the current table."},{"id":"ch05-e034","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What does `DESCRIBE HISTORY t` return?","options":["The current schema","Table versions in reverse chronological order with timestamp, user, operation, parameters and metrics","The list of Parquet files","Only the last 10 queries run on the table"],"answer":1,"why":["That's DESCRIBE TABLE.","Correct.","File-level info isn't listed; DESCRIBE DETAIL gives counts.","It lists writes (commits), not SELECT queries."],"explain":"History is versioned provenance per write: version, timestamp, user, operation, operationParameters, operationMetrics… newest first. Schema → DESCRIBE TABLE; files/size → DESCRIBE DETAIL."},{"id":"ch05-e035","type":"write","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"PySpark: load `ecommerce.silver.orders` as it was at version 121 into `old_df`.","solution":"old_df = (\n    spark.read\n         .option(\"versionAsOf\", 121)\n         .table(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\")\n)","keywords":["spark.read","versionAsOf","121",".table("],"lang":"python","explain":"The reader option `versionAsOf` (or `timestampAsOf`) selects the snapshot. It's the DataFrame equivalent of `VERSION AS OF`."},{"id":"ch05-e036","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`SELECT * FROM orders VERSION AS OF 5` makes version 5 the current version of the table.","answer":false,"explain":"Time travel is read-only: it reads an older snapshot. Making an old state current again is `RESTORE`, which creates a new version."},{"id":"ch05-e037","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What is the default transaction-log history retention?","options":["7 days","30 days","90 days","Forever"],"answer":1,"why":["That's the VACUUM data-file threshold.","Correct.","Not a Delta default.","History is finite — that's why it isn't a backup."],"explain":"Log history defaults to **30 days** (`delta.logRetentionDuration`). Don't confuse with the **7-day** deleted-file retention used by VACUUM."},{"id":"ch05-e038","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A stakeholder: \"Today `customer_id` in `prod.silver.orders` became NULL for many rows.\"","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Run DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders to see recent operations","ok":true,"fb":"Right: don't guess — history shows which operations ran, when and by whom."},{"text":"Re-run yesterday's pipeline to overwrite the table","ok":false,"fb":"You'd change the table before understanding what broke it, and maybe re-break it."},{"text":"Run VACUUM to clean up bad files","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM can destroy the old files you need for comparison and recovery!"}]},{"prompt":"History shows v124 (MERGE 09:00) and v125 (UPDATE 11:00). How do you pinpoint the bad version?","options":[{"text":"Time-travel: compare VERSION AS OF 124 vs VERSION AS OF 125","ok":true,"fb":"Comparing snapshots shows v124 correct and v125 broken."},{"text":"Read the Parquet files directly from storage","ok":false,"fb":"Physical files include removed ones; you'd mix snapshots."},{"text":"Look at DESCRIBE DETAIL","ok":false,"fb":"DETAIL gives current table-level metadata, not per-version contents."}],"code":"SELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 124;\nSELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 125;"},{"prompt":"v125 is the culprit (an UPDATE from a buggy job). Next?","options":[{"text":"Inspect v125's operation, user and operationMetrics, then fix the job and decide fix-forward vs RESTORE TABLE … TO VERSION AS OF 124","ok":true,"fb":"Correct: find root cause, then repair data safely."},{"text":"Delete 00000000000000000125.json from _delta_log","ok":false,"fb":"Never hand-edit the log."},{"text":"Do nothing; time travel is a backup anyway","ok":false,"fb":"History expires and VACUUM can remove files."}]}],"explain":"History → compare versions with time travel → identify the operation → fix or RESTORE. That is the real Delta debugging workflow."},{"id":"ch05-e039","type":"order","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A pipeline corrupted data and it showed up in v101. Order your actions.","items":["Run DESCRIBE HISTORY","Time-travel and compare v100 vs v101","Identify the operation/job that created v101","Decide: fix forward or RESTORE to v100"],"explain":"This is the test answer for 'corrupted pipeline at v101': history, compare, locate operation, then repair. Restoring first without understanding risks repeating the bug on the next run."},{"id":"ch05-e040","type":"spotbug","section":"ch05-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Which line has invalid time-travel syntax?","lines":["SELECT *","FROM ecommerce.silver.orders","AS OF VERSION 121","WHERE status = 'PAID';"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"SELECT *\nFROM ecommerce.silver.orders VERSION AS OF 121\nWHERE status = 'PAID';","explain":"The syntax is `table_name VERSION AS OF n` (or `TIMESTAMP AS OF '…'`), placed right after the table name. `AS OF VERSION` is not valid Databricks SQL."},{"id":"ch05-e041","type":"calc","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Current version is **v10**. You run `RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF 7`. What version number does the table have afterwards?","answer":11,"tolerance":0,"unit":"version","explain":"RESTORE is a new transaction appended to history: v11 with state equivalent to v7. Versions 8–10 are not deleted."},{"id":"ch05-e042","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Restoring to v7 while current is v10 deletes versions 8–10 from the history.","answer":false,"explain":"History stays history. RESTORE commits a new version (v11) whose state equals v7; you can still inspect v8–v10 (as long as retention allows)."},{"id":"ch05-e043","type":"bucket","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Time travel or RESTORE?","buckets":["Time travel (VERSION AS OF)","RESTORE"],"items":[{"text":"Reads an old snapshot","bucket":0},{"text":"Creates a new table version","bucket":1},{"text":"Current table state unchanged","bucket":0},{"text":"Old state becomes current again","bucket":1},{"text":"Shows up as an operation in DESCRIBE HISTORY","bucket":1},{"text":"Works in a plain SELECT","bucket":0}],"explain":"Time travel = read only. RESTORE = a write that makes an old state current, committed as a new version and visible in history."},{"id":"ch05-e044","type":"write","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Restore `prod.silver.orders` to how it was at `2026-09-15 10:00:00`.","solution":"RESTORE TABLE prod.silver.orders\nTO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2026-09-15 10:00:00';","keywords":["restore table prod.silver.orders","to timestamp as of"],"lang":"sql","explain":"RESTORE accepts `TO VERSION AS OF n` or `TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '…'`. It fails if the files for that version were vacuumed."},{"id":"ch05-e045","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Delta table history is a long-term backup system.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Default 7-day deleted-file retention (VACUUM) and 30-day log retention limit time travel. Databricks explicitly says not to use history as a long-term backup strategy."},{"id":"ch05-e046","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"To successfully time travel to version N, what must still exist?","options":["Only the history entry for N","Only the data files for N","Both the log history for N and the data files of N","Only the latest checkpoint"],"answer":2,"why":["Metadata alone can't return rows.","Without the log you don't know which files form N.","Correct.","A checkpoint summarizes state but not the old data files."],"explain":"You need the log (to know which files form N) **and** the files themselves. VACUUM may delete files while the history entry is still listed — the classic 'history exists but query fails' case."},{"id":"ch05-e047","type":"match","section":"ch05-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each retention fact.","pairs":[["VACUUM deleted-file threshold (default)","7 days"],["Transaction-log history (default)","30 days"],["RETAIN 168 HOURS","Same as the 7-day default"],["Time travel to a vacuumed version","Fails: data files gone"]],"explain":"Two different clocks: 7 days for data files, 30 days for log history. 168 h = 7 × 24."},{"id":"ch05-e048","type":"calc","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"`VACUUM t RETAIN 168 HOURS` keeps how many **days** of removed files?","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"days","explain":"168 / 24 = 7 days — exactly the Databricks default and recommended minimum."},{"id":"ch05-e049","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"VACUUM speeds up queries by compacting small files into bigger ones.","answer":false,"explain":"VACUUM = garbage collection of old unreferenced files. Compaction/layout is OPTIMIZE. Confusing them is a very common exam trap."},{"id":"ch05-e050","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague proposes: \"To save storage, run `VACUUM t RETAIN 0 HOURS` every hour.\" What can break? (choose all)","options":["Time travel","Long-running readers","Long-running writers whose uncommitted files look unreferenced","Recovery assumptions","Schema enforcement"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"Zero retention deletes files that older snapshots, in-flight queries and in-flight writers still need. Databricks recommends ≥7 days and has a safety check that blocks short retention. Schema enforcement is unrelated."},{"id":"ch05-e051","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Log: v0 → {A,B}, v1 → {B,C}, v2 → {C,D}. After the retention window passes, which files can VACUUM eventually remove?","options":["C and D","A and B","A only","None — VACUUM never deletes data files"],"answer":1,"explain":"Current snapshot is {C, D}. A and B are referenced only by older versions; once those versions fall outside retention, VACUUM can delete them."},{"id":"ch05-e052","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A 10 TB `orders` table. You ran `DELETE FROM orders WHERE event_date < DATE'2020-01-01'`. Queries no longer return those rows, but storage is still ~10 TB.","steps":[{"prompt":"First reaction?","options":[{"text":"Recognize logical delete ≠ physical deletion; this is not necessarily a bug","ok":true,"fb":"Correct: old files are kept for snapshots/time travel."},{"text":"Open a P1: Delta failed to delete","ok":false,"fb":"The DELETE worked logically — check the physical side first."},{"text":"Re-run the DELETE until storage drops","ok":false,"fb":"Re-running deletes nothing new and adds versions."}]},{"prompt":"DVs are enabled on the table. What does that imply?","options":[{"text":"Original files may still be current physical inputs, with deleted rows only masked","ok":true,"fb":"Right: soft-deleted rows sit inside still-referenced files."},{"text":"DVs physically delete rows immediately","ok":false,"fb":"DVs mask rows; they don't purge."},{"text":"DVs mean VACUUM alone will free the space","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM only removes unreferenced files; masked rows are inside referenced files."}]},{"prompt":"How do you actually free the space?","options":[{"text":"REORG TABLE orders APPLY (PURGE), then VACUUM after retention","ok":true,"fb":"PURGE rewrites files without masked rows; VACUUM later deletes the old ones."},{"text":"VACUUM orders RETAIN 0 HOURS","ok":false,"fb":"Dangerous and still doesn't purge DV-masked rows."},{"text":"OPTIMIZE orders and stop","ok":false,"fb":"Rewritten files help, but old files stay until VACUUM."}]}],"explain":"Two layers: logical state (DELETE) vs physical garbage collection (REORG PURGE + VACUUM). Not a contradiction."},{"id":"ch05-e053","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Yesterday `SELECT * FROM orders VERSION AS OF 80` worked. Today it fails.","steps":[{"prompt":"First thing you check?","options":[{"text":"DESCRIBE HISTORY orders — did a VACUUM run?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes: history reveals maintenance operations and their timing."},{"text":"Increase cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"Not a compute problem."},{"text":"Re-create the table from scratch","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys evidence and history."}]},{"prompt":"History shows `VACUUM` ran last night. What next?","options":[{"text":"Check its retention and whether v80's data files still exist","ok":true,"fb":"A history entry for v80 doesn't guarantee its files survived."},{"text":"Assume the log is corrupted and delete it","ok":false,"fb":"Never touch the log by hand."},{"text":"Run RESTORE TO VERSION AS OF 80","ok":false,"fb":"RESTORE needs the same files — it would fail too."}]}],"explain":"VACUUM can remove the physical files older versions need. History entries alone don't make a version queryable — another reason time travel isn't a backup."},{"id":"ch05-e054","type":"order","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the steps to physically get rid of rows soft-deleted with deletion vectors.","items":["DELETE FROM … WHERE … (rows masked by DV)","REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE)","Wait until the retention period has passed","VACUUM …"],"explain":"DELETE is logical; PURGE rewrites files so the rows are physically gone from current files; after retention, VACUUM deletes the old files from storage."},{"id":"ch05-e055","type":"spotbug","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A nightly 'storage saver' job. Which lines are dangerous?","lines":["# nightly maintenance","spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.retentionDurationCheck.enabled', 'false')","spark.sql('VACUUM prod.silver.orders RETAIN 0 HOURS')","spark.sql('DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders').show()"],"bugs":[1,2],"fix":"spark.sql('VACUUM prod.silver.orders')  # default 7 days\n# or RETAIN 168 HOURS; keep the safety check enabled","explain":"Line 1 disables the safety check that exists precisely to block short retention, and line 2 deletes every unreferenced file immediately — breaking time travel, long-running readers/writers and recovery. Keep ≥7 days."},{"id":"ch05-e056","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Default retention values.","text":"VACUUM keeps deleted data files for [[7]] days by default, while transaction-log history is kept for [[30]] days.","bank":["1","14","90","365"],"explain":"Two different clocks. You need both the log entry and the files to time travel, so the effective window is usually the 7-day file retention."},{"id":"ch05-e057","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"In `MERGE`, what does **WHEN MATCHED** mean?","options":["A target row has no source row","A source row found a target row that satisfies the ON condition","The source and target schemas match","The row was already merged earlier"],"answer":1,"explain":"WHEN MATCHED = source row ↔ target row pair via ON. Typically UPDATE (or DELETE). 'Target row with no source row' is NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE."},{"id":"ch05-e058","type":"match","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each MERGE clause to its meaning.","pairs":[["ON","When source and target rows are the same entity"],["WHEN MATCHED","Source row has a matching target row"],["WHEN NOT MATCHED","Source row has no target row (usually INSERT)"],["WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE","Target row has no source row (UPDATE/DELETE)"]],"explain":"Three cases of the Venn diagram plus the ON condition that defines 'same entity'."},{"id":"ch05-e059","type":"write","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write an upsert: target `prod.silver.customers` (id, name, city), source `staging.customer_updates`. Update name/city on match, insert otherwise. Use explicit columns.","solution":"MERGE INTO prod.silver.customers AS t\nUSING staging.customer_updates AS s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN\n  UPDATE SET t.name = s.name, t.city = s.city\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN\n  INSERT (id, name, city) VALUES (s.id, s.name, s.city);","keywords":["merge into prod.silver.customers","using staging.customer_updates","on t.id = s.id","when matched then","update set","when not matched then","insert"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Read it like a compiler: TARGET, SOURCE, ON (entity identity), then the action per case. This is the canonical upsert."},{"id":"ch05-e060","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN INSERT *` is valid MERGE syntax.","answer":false,"explain":"NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE supports only **UPDATE or DELETE** — there is no source row whose values could be inserted."},{"id":"ch05-e061","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Your source contains only **today's changed records**. You add `WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE`. What happens?","options":["Only today's deleted records are removed","Every target row not in today's batch is deleted — possibly almost the entire table","Nothing; the clause is ignored for incremental sources","The MERGE fails with a syntax error"],"answer":1,"why":["Delta can't know which rows are 'deleted today' — absence = delete.","Correct.","Delta doesn't know your source semantics.","The syntax is valid."],"explain":"The clause treats the source as **authoritative**: anything missing from it is deleted. Use it only when the source is a complete snapshot."},{"id":"ch05-e062","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete a full sync against an authoritative snapshot.","text":"MERGE INTO target t\nUSING source s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET [[*]]\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN [[INSERT]] *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED BY [[SOURCE]] THEN [[DELETE]];","bank":["TARGET","VALUES","ALL"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`UPDATE SET *` and `INSERT *` map columns by name when schemas are compatible; NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE … DELETE removes target rows absent from the snapshot."},{"id":"ch05-e063","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why might production code prefer explicit `UPDATE SET t.name = s.name, …` over `UPDATE SET *`?","options":["`SET *` is not supported on Delta","Explicit mapping shows exactly which columns change and lets you add audit columns like `updated_at`","Explicit mapping is always faster","`SET *` deletes unmapped columns"],"answer":1,"explain":"Explicit mapping is about control and readability: you know precisely what's touched, and you can add `t.updated_at = current_timestamp()`. `SET *` works when schemas are compatible."},{"id":"ch05-e064","type":"free","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is MERGE especially important for CDC (change data capture)?","model":"A change feed typically contains inserts, updates and deletes for entities that already exist in the target. MERGE matches each change to the target by key and applies insert/update/delete in one atomic transaction, so the target stays a consistent mirror of the source state. Without it you'd need several separate statements with intermediate inconsistent states.","rubric":["CDC = inserts + updates + deletes for existing entities","MERGE matches by key","Applies all actions atomically in one commit"],"explain":"The answer combines **mixed change types** and **atomicity**."},{"id":"ch05-e065","type":"bucket","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Target ids {1,2,3,4}; source ids {1,2,5}. Which MERGE case does each id fall into?","buckets":["WHEN MATCHED","WHEN NOT MATCHED","WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE"],"items":[{"text":"id 1","bucket":0},{"text":"id 2","bucket":0},{"text":"id 5","bucket":1},{"text":"id 3","bucket":2},{"text":"id 4","bucket":2}],"explain":"In both → MATCHED; only in source → NOT MATCHED (insert); only in target → NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE (update/delete)."},{"id":"ch05-e066","type":"calc","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Target ids {1,2,3,4}; source ids {1,2,4,5}. You run MERGE with WHEN MATCHED UPDATE, WHEN NOT MATCHED INSERT, WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE DELETE. How many rows does the target have afterwards?","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"1,2,4 matched → updated; 5 inserted; 3 deleted (not in source). Result {1,2,4,5} = 4 rows — the target now mirrors the source exactly."},{"id":"ch05-e067","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"The source has **two rows with the same business key** that both match the same target row in an UPDATE. What do you do?","options":["Nothing; Delta picks the last one","Deduplicate/rank the source first so the choice is deterministic","Switch to INSERT OVERWRITE","Disable the duplicate check"],"answer":1,"why":["Delta does not pick silently — it errors.","Correct.","Overwrite changes semantics completely.","There's no such switch; ambiguity must be resolved."],"explain":"Multiple matches make the intended final value ambiguous, so MERGE fails. Rank by a deterministic ordering field (sequence/updated_at/version) and keep one row per key."},{"id":"ch05-e068","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Keep only the latest CDC event per key.","text":"WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT *,\n         [[ROW_NUMBER]]() OVER (\n           [[PARTITION BY]] id\n           ORDER BY seq [[DESC]]\n         ) AS rn\n  FROM staging.customer_changes\n)\nSELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rn = [[1]];","bank":["RANK","GROUP BY","ASC","0"],"asCode":true,"explain":"PARTITION BY the business key, ORDER BY the sequence descending, keep rn = 1 → one latest row per key. RANK() could keep ties; ROW_NUMBER() guarantees one."},{"id":"ch05-e069","type":"write","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"PySpark: from `source_df`, keep only the latest row per `customer_id` by `sequence_number`, result in `latest` (drop helper column).","solution":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nfrom pyspark.sql.window import Window\n\nw = Window.partitionBy(\"customer_id\").orderBy(F.col(\"sequence_number\").desc())\nlatest = (source_df\n          .withColumn(\"rn\", F.row_number().over(w))\n          .filter(F.col(\"rn\") == 1)\n          .drop(\"rn\"))","keywords":["Window.partitionBy(\"customer_id\")",".desc()","row_number()","filter","== 1","drop(\"rn\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Same pattern as SQL: a window per key ordered newest first, row_number, keep 1. This is a real data-engineering pattern, not a toy window exercise."},{"id":"ch05-e070","type":"order","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"MERGE on `customer_id` fails. Order the debug sequence.","items":["Is the target key unique?","Does the source contain duplicates?","Which source row is the latest?","Is there an event sequence / updated_at?","Deduplicate","Run the MERGE"],"explain":"Check uniqueness on both sides, then find a deterministic ordering field, dedup, and only then merge. Skipping the ordering-field question leads to arbitrary picks."},{"id":"ch05-e071","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"On DBR 16.0+, MERGE duplicate-match detection considers both the ON condition and relevant WHEN MATCHED conditions, while 15.4 LTS and below used only ON.","answer":true,"explain":"Correct runtime detail: newer runtimes can avoid false 'multiple matches' errors when WHEN MATCHED conditions make only one source row applicable."},{"id":"ch05-e072","type":"spotbug","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"A PostgreSQL developer wrote this for Databricks SQL. Which line is the problem?","lines":["UPDATE prod.silver.customers c","SET city = u.city","FROM staging.customer_updates u","WHERE c.id = u.id;"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"MERGE INTO prod.silver.customers c\nUSING staging.customer_updates u\nON c.id = u.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET c.city = u.city;","explain":"Databricks SQL does not support the `UPDATE … FROM …` (join-update) pattern. To update from another relation, use `MERGE INTO … WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE`."},{"id":"ch05-e073","type":"write","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Python: upsert `staging.customer_updates` into `prod.silver.customers` on `id` using the `DeltaTable` API (update name, city; insert id, name, city).","solution":"from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\ntarget = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"prod.silver.customers\")\nsource = spark.table(\"staging.customer_updates\")\n\n(target.alias(\"t\")\n   .merge(source.alias(\"s\"), \"t.id = s.id\")\n   .whenMatchedUpdate(set={\"name\": \"s.name\", \"city\": \"s.city\"})\n   .whenNotMatchedInsert(values={\"id\": \"s.id\", \"name\": \"s.name\", \"city\": \"s.city\"})\n   .execute())","keywords":["DeltaTable.forName",".merge(","t.id = s.id","whenMatchedUpdate","whenNotMatchedInsert",".execute()"],"lang":"python","explain":"The DeltaTable builder mirrors SQL MERGE: alias target/source, merge condition, clause builders, then `.execute()` — forgetting `execute()` means nothing runs."},{"id":"ch05-e074","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Nightly MERGE into `ecommerce.silver.orders` fails: *multiple source rows matched target row*.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you query first?","options":[{"text":"GROUP BY order_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 on the staging table","ok":true,"fb":"Finds which keys are duplicated in the source."},{"text":"DESCRIBE DETAIL on the target","ok":false,"fb":"File counts won't reveal duplicate keys."},{"text":"VACUUM the target","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant and risky."}],"code":"SELECT order_id, COUNT(*)\nFROM ecommerce.staging.orders_updates\nGROUP BY order_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;"},{"prompt":"order_id 1001 appears twice (two CDC events). What do you need to decide which wins?","options":[{"text":"A deterministic ordering field such as event_sequence, updated_at or version","ok":true,"fb":"That's what makes the pick reproducible."},{"text":"Pick whichever Spark returns first","ok":false,"fb":"Non-deterministic — results may change on retry."},{"text":"Average the two amounts","ok":false,"fb":"Invents data that never existed."}]},{"prompt":"Final fix?","options":[{"text":"ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY event_sequence DESC), keep rn = 1, then MERGE","ok":true,"fb":"Dedup, then merge the clean source."},{"text":"Add WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated, and dangerous for an incremental source."},{"text":"Switch to append mode","ok":false,"fb":"Creates duplicates in the target instead."}]}],"explain":"Find duplicates → find ordering field → dedup → merge."},{"id":"ch05-e075","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Source has `id=10 version=1 amount=100` and `id=10 version=2 amount=120`. Before MERGE you…","options":["Keep both; MERGE handles it","Rank by business key ordered by `version DESC` and keep rn = 1 (amount 120)","Keep the lower version","SUM the amounts → 220"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deduplicate by business key with a deterministic ordering field. The newest version (2, amount 120) represents the latest state."},{"id":"ch05-e076","type":"write","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the query that finds duplicated `order_id`s in `ecommerce.staging.orders_updates`.","solution":"SELECT order_id, COUNT(*)\nFROM ecommerce.staging.orders_updates\nGROUP BY order_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;","keywords":["group by order_id","having count(*) > 1"],"lang":"sql","explain":"The first query in the 'merge fails' workflow. `HAVING` filters groups after aggregation — WHERE can't reference COUNT(*)."},{"id":"ch05-e077","type":"order","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the phases of optimistic concurrency control.","items":["READ: consistent snapshot","WRITE: prepare candidate output","VALIDATE: check for conflicting commits","COMMIT: new atomic version (or fail)"],"explain":"Databricks describes exactly read → write → validate-and-commit. Validation happens at the end, which is what makes it 'optimistic'."},{"id":"ch05-e078","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Delta locks the entire table pessimistically so that concurrent writers wait for each other.","answer":false,"explain":"Delta uses optimistic concurrency: writers work in parallel on a snapshot and conflicts are checked at commit time."},{"id":"ch05-e079","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is it called **optimistic** concurrency control?","options":["It always succeeds","It assumes most concurrent transactions won't conflict and checks only at validation/commit","It retries automatically forever","It optimistically skips validation for small writes"],"answer":1,"explain":"It bets on 'no conflict', lets everyone work, then validates. If the bet fails, one transaction fails — the table stays consistent."},{"id":"ch05-e080","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Table at v50. A and B both read v50. A commits v51. B's changes don't conflict with A's. What happens to B?","options":["B fails because it read an old version","B validates v50→v51, finds no conflict and commits v52","B overwrites v51","B waits for a lock"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reading an older version isn't itself a failure: B checks what changed since its snapshot. No conflict → it commits as the next version, v52."},{"id":"ch05-e081","type":"order","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the life of a MERGE as you should picture it.","items":["Source rows matched via ON","Read target snapshot","Determine changed rows/files","Write new data / deletion vectors","Optimistic conflict validation","Atomic transaction-log commit","New version → new consistent snapshot"],"explain":"This is the 'data engineer' level view of MERGE: matching, file-level changes, validation, atomic commit. Seeing only 'update/insert' hides where failures and costs come from."},{"id":"ch05-e082","type":"free","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain optimistic concurrency control in Delta in your own words.","model":"Each transaction reads a consistent snapshot of the table and prepares its new files without locking the whole table. At commit time Delta checks which commits happened since that snapshot and whether they conflict with what this transaction read or changed. If it's safe, the commit becomes a new atomic version; if not, the transaction fails (and can retry on fresh state) instead of corrupting the table.","rubric":["Works on a snapshot, no pessimistic table lock","Validation at commit against concurrent commits","Safe → new atomic version","Conflict → fail/retry, no corruption"],"explain":"Hit the four phases and the 'fail instead of corrupt' guarantee."},{"id":"ch05-e083","type":"calc","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Table at v50. Jobs A, B, C all read v50. A commits first. B validates — no conflict — and commits. C validates — conflicts with A — and fails. What is the latest table version?","answer":52,"tolerance":0,"unit":"version","explain":"A → v51, B → v52, C fails and creates no version. Failed transactions never produce versions."},{"id":"ch05-e084","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is the default isolation level for Delta writes on Databricks?","options":["Serializable","WriteSerializable","Read Committed","Snapshot"],"answer":1,"why":["Stronger, opt-in.","Correct.","Not a Delta level.","Snapshot isolation describes reads, not the write level."],"explain":"Default writes: **WriteSerializable**. Reads use snapshot isolation. Serializable is the stronger, opt-in level."},{"id":"ch05-e085","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"If jobs A and B change **different rows** of the **same** Parquet file, they must conflict.","answer":false,"explain":"Not anymore: with deletion vectors and row-level concurrency they can coexist, as long as they don't change the same row and the requirements are met."},{"id":"ch05-e086","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A: `UPDATE customers SET status='A' WHERE id=10`; B concurrently: `… status='B' WHERE id=10`. What do you expect?","options":["Both succeed; last writer wins silently","A real write conflict: one commits, the other fails or is serialized safely","Random corruption of row 10","Both fail"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same-row modifications still conflict even with row-level concurrency. Optimistic validation guarantees one wins and the other fails — never inconsistent state."},{"id":"ch05-e087","type":"write","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Set the isolation level of `my_table` to Serializable.","solution":"ALTER TABLE my_table\nSET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.isolationLevel' = 'Serializable');","keywords":["alter table my_table","set tblproperties","delta.isolationLevel","serializable"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Isolation level is a table property. Serializable is stricter (reads + writes explainable by a serial order) and may cause more conflicts."},{"id":"ch05-e088","type":"match","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the concurrency concept.","pairs":[["Readers","Consistent snapshots (snapshot isolation)"],["Writers","Optimistic validation at commit"],["Default write isolation","WriteSerializable"],["Strongest isolation","Serializable"],["Different rows, same file","Can coexist with DVs + row-level concurrency"]],"explain":"The memorization trio plus the row-level concurrency rule."},{"id":"ch05-e089","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which conditions enable row-level concurrency on Databricks? (choose all)","options":["Deletion vectors enabled","Supported Databricks Runtime","Unpartitioned table (or liquid-clustered table)","Isolation level set to Serializable","ZORDER applied"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Row-level concurrency relies on DVs and works for unpartitioned tables on supported runtimes; liquid-clustered tables also use this architecture. Isolation level and ZORDER are not prerequisites."},{"id":"ch05-e090","type":"odd","section":"ch05-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one does NOT belong to Delta's isolation vocabulary on Databricks?","options":["WriteSerializable","Serializable","Snapshot isolation for reads","Read Uncommitted"],"answer":3,"explain":"Delta writes use WriteSerializable (default) or Serializable; reads use snapshot isolation. Delta never exposes uncommitted data — that's the whole point of atomic commits."},{"id":"ch05-e091","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Your write fails with `ConcurrentAppendException`. What does this usually mean?","options":["The Delta table is corrupted","The optimistic concurrency system prevented an unsafe commit because another operation added files your operation depended on","Cluster ran out of memory","Schema mismatch"],"answer":1,"explain":"Concurrency exceptions signal protection, not corruption. Investigate the competing writer; retry after re-reading fresh state if the conflict is transient."},{"id":"ch05-e092","type":"match","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each exception to its likely cause.","pairs":[["MetadataChangedException","Concurrent ALTER TABLE / schema-evolving write"],["ConcurrentAppendException","Another writer added files in the data you read"],["ConcurrentDeleteReadException","Another writer deleted/rewrote a file you read"],["ProtocolChangedException","Table protocol/features upgraded concurrently"],["ConcurrentTransactionException","Two writers share one transaction identity (e.g. stream checkpoint)"]],"explain":"Learn the cause behind each name: it tells you which *other* operation to look for in DESCRIBE HISTORY."},{"id":"ch05-e093","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your daily `MERGE INTO prod.silver.orders … ON t.id = s.id` fails with `DELTA_CONCURRENT_WRITE`.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your first move?","options":[{"text":"Find what other writer touched the table at the same time (DESCRIBE HISTORY)","ok":true,"fb":"Identify the competing transaction first."},{"text":"Wrap it in a loop: run again until it works","ok":false,"fb":"Hides the cause; may hammer the table and never converge."},{"text":"Drop and recreate the table","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys data/history; the table isn't corrupted."}]},{"prompt":"History shows a backfill job running MERGE on the same table. Both MERGEs scan the whole table. What helps?","options":[{"text":"Narrow the MERGE condition if semantically correct, e.g. AND t.event_date = DATE'2026-09-16'","ok":true,"fb":"Smaller read scope = smaller scan and smaller conflict domain."},{"text":"Switch both to INSERT OVERWRITE","ok":false,"fb":"Changes semantics and still conflicts."},{"text":"Lower the cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"Concurrency conflicts aren't a compute problem."}]},{"prompt":"Anything else to check?","options":[{"text":"Whether DVs/row-level concurrency are enabled, whether a schema ALTER runs concurrently, and add retry-after-fresh-read for transient conflicts","ok":true,"fb":"Covers the remaining causes and a safe retry policy."},{"text":"Set VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated and dangerous."},{"text":"Disable the transaction log","ok":false,"fb":"Impossible and nonsensical."}]}],"explain":"Concurrency debugging = who else wrote, what did both read, can scope be narrowed, schema changes?, DV/RLC available?, then retry policy."},{"id":"ch05-e094","type":"order","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the concurrency debugging questions (playbook order).","items":["What other writer touched this table?","What rows/files/partitions did both operations read?","Are predicates too broad / can I narrow the MERGE condition?","Is a schema ALTER happening concurrently?","Are deletion vectors / row-level concurrency available?","Should the application retry after re-reading fresh state?"],"explain":"From cause (who/what) to scope (predicates) to special causes (schema) to platform features (DV/RLC) and finally the retry policy. Retrying first is the 'bad response'."},{"id":"ch05-e095","type":"write","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Rewrite the MERGE so its ON condition is bounded to `event_date = DATE'2026-09-16'` (target `prod.silver.orders` t, source `daily_updates` s, key `id`, use `SET *`/`INSERT *`).","solution":"MERGE INTO prod.silver.orders t\nUSING daily_updates s\nON t.id = s.id\n   AND t.event_date = DATE'2026-09-16'\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","keywords":["merge into prod.silver.orders","on t.id = s.id","and t.event_date = date'2026-09-16'","update set *","insert *"],"lang":"sql","explain":"The extra bound reduces both the scan and the conflict domain — valid only if matching target rows really live on that date, otherwise rows from other dates would be inserted as duplicates."},{"id":"ch05-e096","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The right response to a concurrency exception is to re-run the job until it succeeds.","answer":false,"explain":"That's the 'bad response'. First understand who else wrote and why the scopes overlap. Retry logic is fine for legitimate transient conflicts — after re-reading fresh state."},{"id":"ch05-e097","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`MetadataChangedException` during a write. What probably happened?","options":["Disk full on a worker","A concurrent operation changed the table metadata, e.g. ALTER TABLE or a schema-evolving write","Your SELECT used the wrong version","VACUUM deleted a data file"],"answer":1,"explain":"Schema changes are metadata changes and conflict with concurrent writes (they can also stop streams reading the table until restart). Coordinate schema updates."},{"id":"ch05-e098","type":"bucket","section":"ch05-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Will these two concurrent operations necessarily conflict (assume DVs + row-level concurrency enabled, unpartitioned table)?","buckets":["Real conflict expected","Can usually coexist"],"items":[{"text":"Both UPDATE row id=10","bucket":0},{"text":"A updates row 1, B updates row 9000 of the same file","bucket":1},{"text":"A runs ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMNS while B writes","bucket":0},{"text":"A deletes a file that B's MERGE read","bucket":0},{"text":"Two blind INSERT appends","bucket":1}],"explain":"Same-row changes, metadata changes and deletes of files you read are real conflicts. Different rows (with RLC) and blind appends usually coexist."},{"id":"ch05-e099","type":"mcq","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What is `MERGE`?","options":["A command that combines two tables into a new table","An atomic source-to-target operation that can update, insert and delete target rows based on matching conditions","A way to merge small files","A schema-evolution command"],"answer":1,"why":["It changes an existing target, it doesn't build a new table.","Correct.","That's OPTIMIZE.","That's mergeSchema / WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION."],"explain":"MERGE matches source rows to target rows with an ON condition and applies UPDATE/INSERT/DELETE per case, all in one transaction (one new version). File compaction is OPTIMIZE; schema merging is mergeSchema."},{"id":"ch05-e100","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s02","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Checkpoint V2 is a newer checkpoint design aimed at better scalability and concurrency for large or frequently updated tables.","answer":true,"explain":"Checkpoints summarize log state in Parquet-based structures; V2 improves how that works at scale. You still shouldn't depend on when checkpoints are written."},{"id":"ch05-e101","type":"tf","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Besides SQL MERGE and `DeltaTable.merge`, modern PySpark/Databricks also has a DataFrame `mergeInto()` API — all implement the same source-target matching model.","answer":true,"explain":"Different APIs, same semantics: ON condition + matched / not matched / not matched by source clauses. Learn SQL MERGE and DeltaTable first — they dominate existing production code."},{"id":"ch05-e102","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the Python MERGE.","text":"from delta.tables import [[DeltaTable]]\n\ntarget = DeltaTable.[[forName]](spark, \"prod.silver.customers\")\n(target.alias(\"t\")\n   .[[merge]](source.alias(\"s\"), \"t.id = s.id\")\n   .[[whenMatchedUpdate]](set={\"name\": \"s.name\", \"city\": \"s.city\"})\n   .[[whenNotMatchedInsert]](values={\"id\": \"s.id\", \"name\": \"s.name\", \"city\": \"s.city\"})\n   .[[execute]]())","bank":["forPath","join","whenMatchedInsert","collect"],"asCode":true,"explain":"forName loads the table by catalog name; merge(source, condition) starts the builder; whenMatchedUpdate/whenNotMatchedInsert mirror WHEN MATCHED / WHEN NOT MATCHED; execute() runs it."},{"id":"ch05-e103","type":"spotbug","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The source `staging.customer_changes_today` holds only today's changed customers. Which line will cause data loss?","lines":["MERGE INTO prod.silver.customers t","USING staging.customer_changes_today s","ON t.id = s.id","WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *","WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE;"],"bugs":[5],"fix":"MERGE INTO prod.silver.customers t\nUSING staging.customer_changes_today s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","explain":"With an incremental source, every customer not changed today is 'not matched by source' and gets deleted. Only use that clause when the source is a full authoritative snapshot."},{"id":"ch05-e104","type":"spotbug","section":"ch05-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague's 'mental model' notes for an UPDATE of row 2 in file A (rows 1–3). Which line is wrong?","lines":["1. Find files containing order_id = 2 (file A)","2. Open A.parquet and overwrite row 2's bytes in place","3. Commit to _delta_log as a new version","4. Old snapshots still see the old row 2"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"2. Write a new file B (rows 1, 3 unchanged + new row 2) — or a DV + supplemental file — then commit REMOVE A + ADD B","explain":"Parquet files are immutable. Delta writes new data (copy-on-write rewrite or deletion vector + supplemental file) and swaps it in atomically; that's also why old snapshots still work."},{"id":"ch05-e105","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Optimistic concurrency in one sentence.","text":"Each writer [[reads]] a consistent snapshot, [[writes]] candidate files without locking, then [[validates]] against commits made since its snapshot and either [[commits]] a new atomic version or fails.","bank":["locks","vacuums","merges"],"explain":"READ → WRITE → VALIDATE → COMMIT. Conflicts are detected at the end, not prevented with locks up front."},{"id":"ch05-e106","type":"order","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`VERSION AS OF 80` worked yesterday and fails today. Order your investigation.","items":["Run DESCRIBE HISTORY on the table","Check whether a VACUUM ran and with which retention","Check whether v80's data files still exist (not just its history entry)","Conclude: time travel isn't a backup — rely on a real backup for older states"],"explain":"History first, then the VACUUM and its retention, then the physical files. A history entry alone never proves a version is still queryable."},{"id":"ch05-e107","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After the nightly MERGE into `prod.silver.customers`, the table dropped from 2 million rows to 3,000.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you look at first?","options":[{"text":"DESCRIBE HISTORY: the MERGE's operationMetrics (numTargetRowsDeleted)","ok":true,"fb":"Confirms the MERGE deleted rows and how many."},{"text":"DESCRIBE DETAIL numFiles","ok":false,"fb":"File counts don't explain logical row loss."},{"text":"Cluster event log","ok":false,"fb":"Data loss is a logic issue, visible in history."}]},{"prompt":"The MERGE has WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE. The source is 'today's changed customers'. Diagnosis?","options":[{"text":"The clause treated the incremental batch as authoritative and deleted every customer not in it","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly the documented danger."},{"text":"A concurrency conflict deleted rows","ok":false,"fb":"Conflicts fail transactions, they don't delete rows."},{"text":"VACUUM removed the rows","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM never changes the current snapshot."}]},{"prompt":"Fix?","options":[{"text":"RESTORE to the pre-MERGE version, then remove the clause (keep it only for full snapshots)","ok":true,"fb":"Repair data, then fix semantics."},{"text":"Re-run the MERGE twice","ok":false,"fb":"Same result again."},{"text":"Switch the source to INSERT OVERWRITE","ok":false,"fb":"Another way to lose data."}]}],"explain":"NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE DELETE is only safe with a full authoritative snapshot as source."},{"id":"ch05-e108","type":"order","section":"ch05-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Rows vanished after a MERGE. Order the response.","items":["Check the MERGE for WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE","Determine whether the source is a full snapshot or incremental","Read the MERGE's operationMetrics in DESCRIBE HISTORY","RESTORE the last good version","Remove or condition the clause"],"explain":"Find the clause, confirm the source semantics, quantify via metrics, restore, then fix the code so it can't recur."},{"id":"ch05-e901","type":"scenario","section":"ch05-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A stream reading `ecommerce.bronze.events` (VACUUM retention 7 days, VACUUM runs daily) was stopped for 2 weeks. On restart it fails: files for a version the checkpoint needs are missing.","steps":[{"prompt":"What happened?","options":[{"text":"VACUUM removed files the stopped stream still needed","ok":true,"fb":"Right — the checkpoint points at version X, whose files are gone."},{"text":"The checkpoint expired after 7 days","ok":false,"fb":"Checkpoints don't expire; the source files did."},{"text":"The watermark deleted old rows","ok":false,"fb":"Watermarks never delete table data."}]},{"prompt":"Best response?","options":[{"text":"Recover/rebuild the stream and increase retention for the future","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — fix properly, then prevent."},{"text":"Set spark.sql.files.ignoreMissingFiles=true","ok":false,"fb":"Databricks warns it can silently produce incorrect results."},{"text":"Run VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS to clean up","ok":false,"fb":"Makes things worse."}]}],"explain":"Streams must run at least once within the source retention window. Design retention and stream operations together.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch05-e902","type":"cloze","section":"ch05-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the streaming upsert.","text":"def upsert(batch_df, batch_id):\n    (DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"ecommerce.silver.customers\").alias(\"t\")\n        .[[merge]](batch_df.alias(\"s\"), \"t.customer_id = s.customer_id\")\n        .whenMatchedUpdateAll()\n        .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n        .execute())\n\nchanges.writeStream.[[foreachBatch]](upsert).option(\"[[checkpointLocation]]\", ckpt).start()","asCode":true,"explain":"foreachBatch hands each micro-batch to a function as a batch DataFrame, so batch-only MERGE becomes possible; MERGE by key keeps a replayed batch idempotent.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch06","num":6,"title":"Delta Table Engineering & Optimization","subtitle":"Writes, overwrite semantics, schemas and physical layout in production","emoji":"🛠️","sourcePages":"358–402 (+ 157–168 schema & OPTIMIZE parts)","mantra":"Write explicitly (create, append, replace a slice), evolve schemas locally, and keep files few, big and clustered on what you filter.","objectives":["You can create Delta tables with CREATE, CREATE OR REPLACE, CTAS, LIKE and the PySpark writeTo()/saveAsTable() APIs.","You can choose between append, full overwrite, REPLACE WHERE and REPLACE USING, and design idempotent writes.","You can explain schema enforcement, per-write evolution (mergeSchema / WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION), overwriteSchema and type widening.","You can inspect tables with DESCRIBE TABLE / DETAIL / HISTORY and diagnose small files and failed data skipping.","You can apply OPTIMIZE, liquid clustering (CLUSTER BY, OPTIMIZE FULL) and predictive optimization correctly.","You can run the 5 Delta debugging workflows and follow the 13 best practices."],"sections":[{"id":"ch06-s01","title":"Creating Delta Tables in SQL","hook":"CREATE vs CREATE OR REPLACE is the difference between 'safe' and 'I just wiped prod'.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Phase 2 goal: move from 'I understand Delta internals' to '**I can use Delta correctly in production**' — syntax, APIs, patterns, optimization."},{"t":"diagram","text":"PHASE 2 — DELTA LAKE IN PRACTICE\n1. Table creation + writes\n2. UPDATE / DELETE / MERGE\n3. Write modes + overwrite semantics\n4. Schema enforcement / evolution / type widening\n5. History / time travel / restore / vacuum\n6. Physical optimization: small files, OPTIMIZE,\n   data skipping, file stats, deletion vectors,\n   liquid clustering, predictive optimization","caption":"Roadmap (internals of DML/MERGE/history live in Chapter 5)"},{"t":"p","text":"In Databricks, tables are **Delta by default** unless you ask for another format. Databricks recommends **Unity Catalog managed Delta tables** for most workloads."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw (\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      DECIMAL(10,2),\n  status      STRING,\n  order_ts    TIMESTAMP\n);            -- Delta by default","caption":"Project: catalog ecommerce · schema bronze · table orders_raw"},{"t":"p","text":"`USING DELTA` is optional today, but useful when teaching or when you want to be **absolutely explicit**."},{"t":"table","head":["Statement","If table exists","Typical use"],"rows":[["`CREATE TABLE t (...)`","Fails (table already exists)","Safe default"],["`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS t (...)`","Does nothing","Idempotent setup scripts"],["`CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE t (...)`","**Replaces** definition/state","Dev; deliberate rebuilds"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"CREATE OR REPLACE in production","text":"Handy in development, dangerous in production if not used consciously: it replaces the existing table definition and its current state. (On Delta the replace is a new version, so history survives — but downstream consumers instantly see the new state.)"},{"t":"p","text":"**CTAS — CREATE TABLE AS SELECT.** You don't declare a schema; it is **derived from the query**. Databricks uses this pattern even in its official Delta tutorials."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders AS\nSELECT\n  CAST(order_id AS BIGINT)       AS order_id,\n  customer_id,\n  CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2))  AS amount,\n  UPPER(status)                  AS status,\n  order_ts\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw;","caption":"CTAS: Bronze → Silver with casts and cleanup"},{"t":"p","text":"**CREATE TABLE LIKE** copies the table **definition/metadata, not the data** — an empty table with the same structure."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders_test\nLIKE ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"t":"list","items":["dev → test copies","prod-like staging tables","schema cloning","migration testing"],"ordered":false},{"t":"reveal","label":"Exercise 1 — think first: you want an empty table with the same schema as `prod.silver.customers`, no data. What do you write?","text":"`CREATE TABLE dev.silver.customers LIKE prod.silver.customers;`"}]},{"id":"ch06-s02","title":"Creating & Writing from PySpark","hook":"Two writer APIs, one positional trap: know writeTo(), saveAsTable() and why insertInto() bites.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The modern recommended API is **DataFrameWriterV2**: `df.writeTo(...)`."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.writeTo(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\").create()\ndf.writeTo(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\").createOrReplace()\ndf.writeTo(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\").append()\n\n(df.writeTo(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n   .using(\"delta\")\n   .createOrReplace())","caption":"writeTo() — V2 API"},{"t":"p","text":"The classic API is still everywhere: `df.write…saveAsTable(...)`."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.write.saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n\n(df.write\n   .mode(\"overwrite\")\n   .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\"))","caption":"saveAsTable() — classic DataFrameWriter"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"saveAsTable() (classic)","points":["Simple, very widespread","Behaviour driven by `.mode(...)`","You'll see it in most existing code"]},{"title":"writeTo() (V2)","points":["Clear create / replace / append semantics","Better partitioning support","Table properties & clustering support","Conditional overwrite features"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Know both","text":"For new code prefer `writeTo()` where practical — but read and maintain `saveAsTable()` code fluently."},{"t":"p","text":"**insertInto() — important trap.** `df.write.insertInto(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\")` requires a compatible schema, but it matches columns **by position, not by name**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"target:     id BIGINT | name STRING\nDataFrame:  name      | id\n              │          │\ninsertInto -> col1 -> id,  col2 -> name   (positional!)\nif types can be coerced -> silently WRONG mapping"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Exercise 2 — think first: target (id BIGINT, name STRING), DataFrame columns (name, id). Which API is more dangerous?","text":"`insertInto(...)` — it uses **position-based** resolution, so `name` lands in `id` (or fails only if the cast is impossible). Name-based APIs (`saveAsTable` append, `writeTo().append()`) avoid this."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Positional resolution","text":"If types allow coercion, `insertInto` can write values into the wrong columns without any error. Reorder with `df.select(target_cols)` or use a name-based API."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when values land in the wrong columns","questions":["Did I write with insertInto (positional) instead of a name-based API?","Does the DataFrame column order match the target column order?","Could the types be coerced silently (e.g. numbers into strings)?","Which version introduced the bad rows, so I can RESTORE or fix them?"]}]},{"id":"ch06-s03","title":"INSERT, INSERT OVERWRITE & TRUNCATE","hook":"INTO adds, OVERWRITE replaces, TRUNCATE empties, DROP deletes the object — four verbs, four outcomes.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT INTO ecommerce.silver.orders VALUES\n  (1001, 51, 29.90, 'NEW', current_timestamp()),\n  (1002, 92, 55.00, 'NEW', current_timestamp());","caption":"INSERT INTO = add rows"},{"t":"p","text":"**INSERT INTO** is **additive**: it adds rows and never replaces existing ones (the docs say so explicitly)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT INTO ecommerce.silver.orders\nSELECT order_id, customer_id, amount, status, order_ts\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nWHERE ingestion_date = current_date();","caption":"INSERT INTO … SELECT — the everyday batch ETL pattern"},{"t":"p","text":"**INSERT OVERWRITE** does not append: it **overwrites the target scope**. Without a partition/predicate restriction it can replace the **entire** table contents."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT OVERWRITE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\nSELECT * FROM staging.orders;"},{"t":"diagram","text":"INSERT INTO       = additive    (existing + new)\nINSERT OVERWRITE  = replacement (target scope := new)"},{"t":"p","text":"**TRUNCATE TABLE** empties a table: schema and table object remain, rows are removed."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"TRUNCATE TABLE ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw;"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"TRUNCATE TABLE","points":["schema remains","table object remains","rows removed"]},{"title":"DROP TABLE","points":["removes the table object itself","schema/definition gone from the catalog"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"INTO vs OVERWRITE","text":"Q: difference between INSERT INTO and INSERT OVERWRITE? A: INTO **adds** rows; OVERWRITE **replaces the target scope**."}]},{"id":"ch06-s04","title":"Append vs Overwrite & Idempotency","hook":"A retry should never double your revenue — design writes that are safe to run twice.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.write.mode(\"append\").saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\")\ndf.writeTo(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\").append()","caption":"append: existing rows + new rows"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\")","caption":"overwrite: can replace the WHOLE table"},{"t":"table","head":["Mode","Good when…"],"rows":[["append","Data are **genuinely new**, no overlap with existing rows"],["full overwrite","The target should **exactly equal** this new DataFrame"]],"caption":"Daily file 2026-09-27 orders: which mode?"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"The overwrite trap","text":"Table holds **3 years** of data. Your DataFrame holds **only yesterday**. `df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").saveAsTable(...)` → the other 3 years vanish from the logical state. One of the most classic production mistakes."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a table suddenly lost most of its rows after a write","questions":["Was the last write mode overwrite (DESCRIBE HISTORY → operationParameters)?","Did the DataFrame contain only a subset (e.g. only yesterday)?","Which version was the last good one, and are its files still within retention?","Should this write have been append, MERGE or REPLACE WHERE instead?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: when exactly is a full overwrite dangerous?","text":"When the DataFrame contains only a **subset** of what the table should contain."},{"t":"p","text":"**Idempotent** = running the same operation a second time with the same input does **not change** the final state again. Critical when a job **retries**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"source: id=5, status='PAID'\nMERGE run 1: updates id=5 -> PAID\nMERGE run 2: updates id=5 -> PAID   (same final state)\n=> idempotent"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"UPDATE orders\nSET amount = amount + 10\nWHERE id = 5;\n-- run twice: +10, +10  => +20 total  => NOT idempotent"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Idempotent","points":["MERGE that sets values from source (`t.status = s.status`)","Full overwrite with the same complete DataFrame","REPLACE WHERE for one date with the same data"]},{"title":"Not idempotent","points":["`amount = amount + 10`","Blind append of the same batch twice (duplicates)","Counters/increments"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Use MERGE for idempotent upserts","text":"MERGE that **sets** target values from a deduplicated source converges to the same state on every retry."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → idempotent file loads","text":"COPY INTO is idempotent per *file*: re-runs skip already-loaded files — even if a file's content was later changed. Only `COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')` reloads, and then you own the duplicates. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch06-s05","title":"Selective Overwrite: REPLACE WHERE & REPLACE USING","hook":"Replace exactly one day (or one country) and leave everything else untouched.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You want to replace only `date = 2026-09-27`. Today there are better options than the legacy `partitionOverwriteMode`: Databricks recommends **REPLACE WHERE** or **REPLACE USING** for new workloads."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT INTO ecommerce.silver.orders\nREPLACE WHERE order_date = DATE'2026-09-27'\nSELECT * FROM ecommerce.staging.orders_daily;","caption":"REPLACE WHERE: explicit predicate"},{"t":"diagram","text":"replace rows matching the predicate\nleave everything else untouched"},{"t":"p","text":"REPLACE WHERE is supported on **all compute types**."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"INSERT INTO ecommerce.silver.orders\nREPLACE USING (order_date)\nSELECT * FROM ecommerce.staging.orders_daily;","caption":"REPLACE USING: dynamic overwrite by key columns"},{"t":"p","text":"**REPLACE USING** replaces target rows whose values in the listed key columns match those in the **incoming** dataset. It's the recommended replacement for legacy dynamic partition overwrite. (The source also lists `REPLACE ON` as a further per-use-case variant.)"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"(df.write\n   .mode(\"overwrite\")\n   .option(\"partitionOverwriteMode\", \"dynamic\")\n   .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\"))","caption":"Legacy: dynamic partition overwrite"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Legacy","text":"`partitionOverwriteMode = dynamic` is still supported in some contexts but is legacy, not preferred for new workloads, and has compute limitations."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Dynamic overwrite with a stray key","text":"Incoming data meant for `2026-09-27` accidentally contains one row with `order_date = 2024-01-01`. Dynamic overwrite (and REPLACE USING) will replace the **whole 2024-01-01 slice** with that single row. Always **validate the incoming key/partition domain** before writing."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"REPLACE WHERE","points":["You state the predicate explicitly","Replaces exactly that slice","Stray rows outside the predicate → write fails instead of silently replacing"]},{"title":"REPLACE USING / dynamic","points":["Slices derived from incoming data","Convenient for many keys","Stray keys replace unintended slices"]}]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Exercise 4 — think first: replace only `country='GR'`, keep DE/FR unchanged. Better than full overwrite?","text":"Selective overwrite: `INSERT INTO t REPLACE WHERE country = 'GR' SELECT …`."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before any overwrite","questions":["Does my DataFrame contain the whole table, or only a subset?","Which slice (date/country) am I really trying to replace?","Have I validated the distinct key values in the incoming data?","Would REPLACE WHERE with an explicit predicate be safer here?"]}]},{"id":"ch06-s06","title":"Schema Enforcement & Safe Casting","hook":"Schema enforcement is a seatbelt, not a nuisance — it stops bad producers before the CEO dashboard.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Target has `id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(10,2)`. A DataFrame arrives with `amount STRUCT<foo:INT>`. Default expectation: **the write fails**."},{"t":"flow","items":["Bad producer","Silent corruption","Silver","Gold","CEO dashboard"],"caption":"What schema enforcement prevents"},{"t":"p","text":"Delta performs **schema validation on write**, using the schema stored in the transaction-log metadata."},{"t":"list","items":["Columns must exist in the target","Types must match or be **safely castable**"],"ordered":false},{"t":"table","head":["Target","Incoming","Result"],"rows":[["amount BIGINT","amount INT","OK — safe cast"],["amount BIGINT","amount STRUCT<…>","Fails — no safe cast"],["amount DECIMAL(10,2)","amount STRUCT<foo:INT>","Fails"],["id, name","id, name, email","Fails unless evolution is allowed (next section)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Enforcement vs evolution","text":"**Enforcement** rejects incompatible data. **Evolution** allows specific, controlled schema changes."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Best practice","text":"Use schema enforcement as **protection**, not as a nuisance to disable."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a write fails with a schema mismatch","questions":["What does DESCRIBE TABLE say vs df.printSchema()?","Is it a new column, a different type, or a renamed column?","Is the change intentional evolution — or a bug in the source?","If intentional: can I evolve explicitly and locally (mergeSchema / WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION / ALTER TABLE)?"]}]},{"id":"ch06-s07","title":"Schema Evolution: mergeSchema, autoMerge, overwriteSchema, ALTER","hook":"Let intentional changes in, keep accidental ones out — and make it explicit which write may change the schema.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yesterday the source had `customer_id, name, city`. Today it also has `segment`. `source_df.write.mode(\"append\").saveAsTable(...)` **fails**."},{"t":"p","text":"Don't immediately enable a global setting. **First ask: is `segment` an intentional addition?** If yes, allow evolution **for this write only**:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"(source_df.write\n   .option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")\n   .mode(\"append\")\n   .saveAsTable(\"prod.silver.customers\"))","caption":"Per-write schema evolution"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"spark.conf.set(\n    \"spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled\", \"true\"\n)   # session-wide: every later write may evolve schemas!","caption":"Global autoMerge — not preferred for production"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"mergeSchema (per write)","points":["Localized and explicit","Clear which write may change the schema","Recommended"]},{"title":"autoMerge (session-wide)","points":["Many unrelated writes may evolve schemas","Hard to tell which operation was allowed to","Avoid in production"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Modern **SQL syntax** for evolution scoped to one statement:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO target t\nUSING source s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\n\n-- DBR 18.1+\nINSERT WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO target\nSELECT * FROM source;"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Know both","text":"For 2026 certification/real projects, know the new `WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION` syntax **and** `.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")` — you'll see the latter everywhere."},{"t":"p","text":"**overwriteSchema is different.** It means: overwrite the data **and replace the schema/partitioning**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"(df.write\n   .mode(\"overwrite\")\n   .option(\"overwriteSchema\", \"true\")\n   .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\"))"},{"t":"diagram","text":"mergeSchema      -> evolve / add compatible schema changes\noverwriteSchema  -> replace table schema during overwrite"},{"t":"p","text":"**Manual evolution** — when the change is intentional, change the schema explicitly with `ALTER TABLE`:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE prod.silver.customers ADD COLUMNS (email STRING);\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders ADD COLUMN source_system STRING;\nALTER TABLE prod.silver.customers RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name;\nALTER TABLE prod.silver.customers DROP COLUMN obsolete_field;\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\n  SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableDeletionVectors' = 'true');","caption":"ALTER TABLE: metadata, schema and properties"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Column mapping","text":"Metadata-only **RENAME COLUMN** and **DROP COLUMN** require **column mapping** support on the table."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → mergeSchema in COPY INTO","text":"COPY INTO has two different mergeSchema switches: `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')` merges the schemas of the source files, `COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')` evolves the target Delta table. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch06-s08","title":"Type Widening & Schema Changes Under Concurrency","hook":"INT → BIGINT is growth; INT → STRING is a different meaning — and every schema change is a metadata commit that can collide.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"`quantity INT` grows so much you need `quantity BIGINT`. That is **type widening** — a move to a compatible, wider type. Delta supports it as a **table feature** (now GA)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\nSET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableTypeWidening' = 'true');\n\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\nALTER COLUMN quantity TYPE BIGINT;"},{"t":"table","head":["From","Can widen to"],"rows":[["BYTE","SHORT / INT / BIGINT / DECIMAL / DOUBLE"],["SHORT","INT / BIGINT / DECIMAL / DOUBLE"],["INT","BIGINT / DECIMAL / DOUBLE"],["FLOAT","DOUBLE"]],"caption":"Supported widenings (no rewrite of underlying data files)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Widening ≠ arbitrary cast","text":"`INT → BIGINT` is widening. `INT → STRING` or `STRING → STRUCT` are **not** — they change meaning. Type evolution does not mean 'any type to any other type'."},{"t":"p","text":"Supported widening changes happen **without rewriting** the underlying data files."},{"t":"p","text":"Now the production caveat: **schema changes are metadata changes**, and metadata changes **conflict with concurrent writes**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"11:59  production streaming pipeline running\n12:00  ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMNS ...\n12:00  writer fails  (MetadataChangedException)","caption":"Not necessarily a bug — expected concurrency semantics"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks recommends **coordinating schema updates**: they can make concurrent writes fail and can also **stop streams** reading the table until they are restarted."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a writer fails right after a schema change","questions":["Did someone run ALTER TABLE or a schema-evolving write at that time (DESCRIBE HISTORY)?","Is the error MetadataChangedException?","Are streaming readers of this table now stopped and needing a restart?","Can schema changes be scheduled/coordinated with the pipelines that write this table?"]}]},{"id":"ch06-s09","title":"DESCRIBE DETAIL & Inspecting Tables","hook":"Three DESCRIBEs, three questions: what columns? what versions? what files?","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"DESCRIBE DETAIL ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"t":"p","text":"Returns **one row** of table-level metadata — a top debugging command."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"format","def":"e.g. delta"},{"term":"location","def":"Where the files live"},{"term":"sizeInBytes","def":"Current table size (current snapshot)"},{"term":"numFiles","def":"Number of files in the current snapshot"},{"term":"partitionColumns / clusteringColumns","def":"Layout keys"},{"term":"properties / table features","def":"e.g. deletion vectors, type widening"}]},{"t":"table","head":["Command","Answers"],"rows":[["DESCRIBE TABLE t","Which columns and types? (compare with `df.printSchema()`)"],["DESCRIBE HISTORY t","Which versions/operations, by whom, with what metrics? (Chapter 5)"],["DESCRIBE DETAIL t","Format, location, size, number of files, partitioning, properties/features"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Average file size","text":"`sizeInBytes / numFiles` = average file size. Hundreds of thousands of files with a tiny average size → small-file pressure."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: storage costs doubled but DESCRIBE DETAIL `sizeInBytes` didn't. Why?","text":"DETAIL reports the **current snapshot**. Old unreferenced files kept for history/time travel aren't counted — check VACUUM/retention (Chapter 5)."}]},{"id":"ch06-s10","title":"OPTIMIZE & the Small-File Problem","hook":"10 GB in 10 files flies; 10 GB in 100,000 files crawls — same bytes, different file count.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Writes often produce files of 10 MB, 12 MB, 8 MB… thousands of **small Parquet files**."},{"t":"table","head":["Option","Layout","Effect"],"rows":[["A","10 files × 1 GB","Few file opens, healthy tasks"],["B","100,000 files × 100 KB","Huge metadata/file-open overhead, many tiny Spark tasks, inefficient scheduling, poor scan efficiency"]],"caption":"Same 10 GB dataset"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Look at file distribution, not just total bytes","text":"Health = **number and size distribution of files**, not only total size."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"OPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"t":"p","text":"**OPTIMIZE** improves physical layout. Without clustering/Z-order semantics it does **bin packing**: compacting small files into more balanced sizes (e.g. 50,000 × 1 MB → far fewer, larger files)."},{"t":"diagram","text":"A B C D E F      many small files\n     │  OPTIMIZE\n     ▼\n   X   Y         fewer, larger files\n\nlog: REMOVE A..F, ADD X, ADD Y   (dataChange = false)"},{"t":"p","text":"The logical data doesn't change. The `dataChange` flag in the protocol marks the commit as **rearrangement**, not a logical record change (e.g. streaming readers don't treat it as new data)."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"OPTIMIZE","points":["Rewrites/reorganizes **active** data files","Layout & performance","Creates new files + a new version"]},{"title":"VACUUM","points":["Physically **deletes old unreferenced** files","Garbage collection / storage","Doesn't change the current snapshot"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Classic confusion","text":"OPTIMIZE ≠ VACUUM. After OPTIMIZE, the old small files are still on storage until VACUUM removes them past retention."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when storage 'exploded'","questions":["What do numFiles and sizeInBytes say in DESCRIBE DETAIL?","Is the average file size tiny (hundreds of thousands of files)?","Which writes produce so many small files (frequent small appends, streaming)?","Has OPTIMIZE run — and has VACUUM cleaned up the replaced files after retention?","Is predictive optimization enabled for this UC managed table?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Streaming is a small-file factory: every tiny micro-batch commits small files (1–3 MB each). OPTIMIZE / predictive optimization can compact later, but pick a sensible trigger latency. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, section 14.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch06-s11","title":"Data Skipping & File Statistics","hook":"The fastest file to read is the one you never open.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Delta stores **file-level statistics** in the log's `add` actions:"},{"t":"list","items":["min values per column","max values per column","null counts","row count"],"ordered":false},{"t":"diagram","text":"file A: customer_id min=1    max=1000\nfile B: customer_id min=1001 max=2000\nfile C: customer_id min=2001 max=3000\n\nWHERE customer_id = 2500\n  A -> cannot contain 2500 -> skip\n  B -> cannot contain 2500 -> skip\n  C -> may contain 2500    -> read","caption":"Data skipping"},{"t":"p","text":"**Data skipping** = using file statistics so files that cannot contain matching rows are not read. Stats also help pruning and query planning."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Skipping needs a good layout","text":"If every file holds customer_ids 1…3000 (min=1, max=3000), no file can be skipped. Stats only help when values are **clustered** into narrow ranges per file."},{"t":"p","text":"Predictive optimization also **collects statistics** on Unity Catalog managed tables."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: `WHERE customer_id = 1234` still scans most of the table. What could be wrong?","text":"Files hold wide, overlapping customer_id ranges (bad layout/no clustering), stats missing for that column, or the filter isn't selective. Consider liquid clustering on `customer_id`."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a selective query scans too much","questions":["How is the table laid out — partitioned, clustered, or neither?","Is the table clustered on the column I filter by?","Are statistics collected for that column?","Is the filter actually selective?","Is data skipping kicking in (files read vs total files)?"]}]},{"id":"ch06-s12","title":"Liquid Clustering","hook":"Partition + ZORDER locked you in; liquid clustering lets the layout follow your queries.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Old way","points":["`partitionBy(date)`","+ maybe `ZORDER BY customer_id`","Static physical design decision"]},{"title":"Liquid clustering","points":["`CLUSTER BY (customer_id)`","Keys can change as query patterns change","Applied incrementally"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks describes **liquid clustering** as the layout technique that replaces traditional partitioning and ZORDER for many use cases, and recommends it for new Delta tables."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\nCLUSTER BY (customer_id);\n\nOPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders;        -- incremental clustering\nOPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders FULL;   -- recluster ALL existing data"},{"t":"p","text":"Setting `CLUSTER BY` doesn't rewrite old data on its own. Plain `OPTIMIZE` clusters incrementally; to apply clustering to **existing** data, run `OPTIMIZE … FULL`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Why 'liquid'?","text":"A static `partitionBy(country)` is concrete poured into the table's design. Liquid clustering is a shape you can change: new keys without an immediate rewrite of all historical data."},{"t":"p","text":"**When does it make sense?** Columns used often in **filters**, **joins** and **selective lookups**, with enough **cardinality** that physical ordering helps — e.g. `CLUSTER BY (customer_id)` when you often run `WHERE customer_id = ?`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Bonus: concurrency","text":"Liquid-clustered tables also benefit from row-level concurrency (Chapter 5)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Not combined with partitioning/ZORDER","text":"Liquid clustering replaces partitioning and ZORDER on a table — you don't stack them."}]},{"id":"ch06-s13","title":"Predictive Optimization","hook":"Let Databricks schedule maintenance — but you still need to know why a table is slow.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For **Unity Catalog managed tables**, **predictive optimization** lets Databricks run maintenance automatically and decide which tables benefit."},{"t":"list","items":["OPTIMIZE","VACUUM","ANALYZE (statistics)"],"ordered":false},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without","points":["You schedule OPTIMIZE every Sunday","VACUUM every Monday","ANALYZE every…"]},{"title":"With predictive optimization","points":["Databricks monitors tables","Decides if maintenance is needed","Runs it when useful"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Automation ≠ understanding","text":"Predictive optimization does **not** mean you don't need to understand optimization. In debugging you must still know why files are small, why skipping doesn't work, why a clustering key is bad, why a scan is huge."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Best practice","text":"Enable predictive optimization for Unity Catalog managed tables where supported."}]},{"id":"ch06-s14","title":"Production Project, Debug Workflows & Best Practices","hook":"Glue it all together: the e-shop pipeline, the 5 debug workflows, 13 rules and a 20-step drill.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw (\n  order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(10,2),\n  status STRING, order_ts TIMESTAMP, ingestion_ts TIMESTAMP\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders (\n  order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(10,2),\n  status STRING, order_ts TIMESTAMP, updated_at TIMESTAMP\n);\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ecommerce.staging.orders_updates AS\nSELECT * FROM VALUES\n  (1001L, 51L, 29.90D, 'PAID'),\n  (1003L, 92L, 44.50D, 'NEW')\nAS t(order_id, customer_id, amount, status);","caption":"Bronze, Silver, staging"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE INTO ecommerce.silver.orders AS t\nUSING ecommerce.staging.orders_updates AS s\nON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET\n  t.customer_id = s.customer_id,\n  t.amount      = s.amount,\n  t.status      = s.status,\n  t.updated_at  = current_timestamp()\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT\n  (order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at)\nVALUES\n  (s.order_id, s.customer_id, s.amount, s.status, current_timestamp());","caption":"Idempotent MERGE: re-running gives the same business state"},{"t":"table","head":["#","Symptom","First command","Look for"],"rows":[["1","MERGE fails (multiple source rows matched)","`GROUP BY key HAVING COUNT(*) > 1`","Ordering field → dedup (Ch.5)"],["2","Write fails: schema mismatch","`DESCRIBE TABLE` vs `df.printSchema()`","New column? type? rename? intentional?"],["3","Storage exploded","`DESCRIBE DETAIL`","numFiles, sizeInBytes → small files"],["4","Query scans too much","Inspect layout/stats","Clustering, stats, selectivity, skipping"],["5","Someone broke data","`DESCRIBE HISTORY`","Bad version → time travel → RESTORE (Ch.5)"]],"caption":"The 5 debugging workflows at a glance"},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Workflow 2 rule","text":"Do **not** blindly enable `mergeSchema` — first decide whether the change is intentional evolution or a source bug."},{"t":"list","items":["Prefer Unity Catalog managed Delta tables unless you have a reason not to.","Prefer `writeTo()`/V2 semantics for modern table creation where practical.","Use MERGE for idempotent upserts.","Deduplicate the source before MERGE.","Avoid blind full overwrite.","Prefer REPLACE WHERE / REPLACE USING over legacy dynamic partition overwrite for new workloads.","Use schema enforcement as protection, not nuisance.","Enable schema evolution explicitly and locally when possible.","Use DESCRIBE HISTORY and time travel for debugging.","Do not treat time travel as long-term backup.","Understand OPTIMIZE vs VACUUM.","Prefer liquid clustering for many modern layout cases instead of static partitions.","Enable predictive optimization for UC managed tables where supported."],"ordered":true},{"t":"p","text":"**Practical assignment** — build `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`, `ecommerce.staging.orders_updates`, `ecommerce.silver.orders` and do, in order:"},{"t":"list","items":["CREATE TABLE","INSERT initial rows","Append new rows","UPDATE one row","DELETE one row","MERGE updates + inserts","Intentionally duplicate a source key","Deduplicate with ROW_NUMBER","Run MERGE again to prove idempotency","Add a new column with mergeSchema","DESCRIBE DETAIL","DESCRIBE HISTORY","Query VERSION AS OF","RESTORE","Create many small files","OPTIMIZE","Inspect numFiles before/after","Enable clustering","OPTIMIZE FULL","Inspect behaviour/history"],"ordered":true},{"t":"p","text":"**What's next:** Phase 3 — Unity Catalog mastery: metastore → catalog → schema → managed/external tables → volumes → credentials/external locations → grants → service principals → row filters/masks → lineage → `PERMISSION_DENIED` debugging."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Phase 3 is now a full chapter","text":"Every item of this \"what's next\" list — metastore to PERMISSION_DENIED debugging — is taught and tested — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch06-d01","title":"Write fails: schema mismatch","section":"ch06-s07","symptom":"Append/MERGE that worked yesterday fails today with a schema mismatch error (e.g. new column `segment`).","askYourself":["What does DESCRIBE TABLE show vs df.printSchema()?","Is it a new column, a different type, or a renamed column?","Is this intentional evolution or a source bug?","If intentional, can I evolve explicitly and locally (mergeSchema / WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION / ALTER TABLE)?","Is it a widening (INT → BIGINT) that needs type widening rather than a cast?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare schemas","why":"Find the exact difference","code":"DESCRIBE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders;\n-- vs df.printSchema()"},{"do":"Classify the change","why":"New column / type / rename lead to different fixes"},{"do":"Confirm intent with the producer","why":"Don't bake source bugs into Silver"},{"do":"Evolve explicitly","why":"Local evolution keeps other writes protected","code":"(source_df.write.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")\n   .mode(\"append\").saveAsTable(\"prod.silver.customers\"))"}],"rootCauses":["Producer added a column","Producer changed a type","Column renamed upstream","Upstream bug"],"fix":"Never blindly enable mergeSchema or global autoMerge; evolve per write or via ALTER TABLE once the change is confirmed intentional.","mnemonic":"3C + E: Compare, Classify, Confirm — then Evolve"},{"id":"ch06-d02","title":"Storage exploded","section":"ch06-s10","symptom":"Storage and/or query time grew sharply; listing the table is slow.","askYourself":["What do numFiles and sizeInBytes say in DESCRIBE DETAIL?","Is the average file size tiny (small-file pressure)?","Which writes produce many small files?","Has OPTIMIZE run — and VACUUM afterwards, past retention?","Is predictive optimization enabled for this UC managed table?","Is the growth actually old history files (not counted in DETAIL)?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE DETAIL","why":"numFiles, sizeInBytes → average file size","code":"DESCRIBE DETAIL ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Compact","why":"Bin-pack small files","code":"OPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Clean up after retention","why":"Remove replaced files","code":"VACUUM ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Automate","why":"Let PO maintain UC managed tables"}],"rootCauses":["Frequent tiny appends/streaming micro-batches","No compaction","No VACUUM after rewrites"],"fix":"OPTIMIZE (or predictive optimization), VACUUM after retention, and fix writers that produce tiny files.","mnemonic":"DETAIL → Divide (size/files) → OPTIMIZE → VACUUM"},{"id":"ch06-d03","title":"Query scans too much","section":"ch06-s11","symptom":"`WHERE customer_id = 1234` still reads most of the table's files.","askYourself":["How is the table laid out (partitioned, clustered, neither)?","Is it clustered on the column I filter by?","Are statistics collected for that column?","Is the filter actually selective?","Is data skipping pruning files at all?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check DESCRIBE DETAIL partition/clustering columns","why":"Know the current layout","code":"DESCRIBE DETAIL ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Cluster on the filter column","why":"Narrow per-file ranges enable skipping","code":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders CLUSTER BY (customer_id);\nOPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders FULL;"}],"rootCauses":["No clustering on filter column","Overlapping min/max ranges","Missing stats","Unselective filter"],"fix":"Liquid clustering on the frequently filtered/joined column, then OPTIMIZE FULL; keep stats current (predictive optimization/ANALYZE).","mnemonic":"L-C-S-S-S: Layout, Clustering, Stats, Selectivity, Skipping"},{"id":"ch06-d04","title":"Writer fails right after a schema change (MetadataChangedException)","section":"ch06-s08","symptom":"A streaming/batch writer fails with `MetadataChangedException` at the moment someone ran ALTER TABLE; streams reading the table stop.","askYourself":["Did someone run ALTER TABLE or a schema-evolving write at that time (DESCRIBE HISTORY)?","Is the error MetadataChangedException?","Are streaming readers now stopped and needing a restart?","Can schema changes be coordinated with the pipelines that write this table?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE HISTORY","why":"Find the concurrent metadata change","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY prod.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Restart affected writers/streams","why":"They must pick up the new metadata"},{"do":"Agree on a change process","why":"Coordinate schema updates"}],"rootCauses":["Concurrent ALTER TABLE","Schema-evolving write in another job"],"fix":"Treat it as expected concurrency semantics: retry/restart, and coordinate future schema changes.","mnemonic":"Schema change = metadata commit = conflict"},{"id":"ch06-d05","title":"Table lost most of its rows after a write","section":"ch06-s04","symptom":"A table with years of data now only contains the latest batch.","askYourself":["Was the last write mode overwrite (DESCRIBE HISTORY → operationParameters)?","Did the DataFrame contain only a subset?","Which version was the last good one, and are its files still within retention?","Should this job use append, MERGE or REPLACE WHERE instead?"],"steps":[{"do":"DESCRIBE HISTORY","why":"Find the overwrite","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"do":"RESTORE","why":"Bring back the last good state as a new version","code":"RESTORE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 309;"},{"do":"Fix the job's write semantics","why":"Prevent recurrence"}],"rootCauses":["Full overwrite with a subset DataFrame","INSERT OVERWRITE without predicate"],"fix":"RESTORE, then switch the job to append / MERGE / REPLACE WHERE.","mnemonic":"Subset + overwrite = loss"},{"id":"ch06-d06","title":"Dynamic overwrite replaced an unexpected slice","section":"ch06-s05","symptom":"After a 'one-day' dynamic overwrite, a different date's data shrank to a few rows.","askYourself":["Which keys/partitions did the incoming data actually contain (SELECT DISTINCT)?","Did a stray row carry an unexpected date?","Which version changed the slice (history + time travel)?","Would REPLACE WHERE with an explicit predicate have failed safely?"],"steps":[{"do":"Inspect incoming key domain","why":"Find stray keys","code":"SELECT DISTINCT order_date FROM ecommerce.staging.orders_daily;"},{"do":"Compare versions for the damaged slice","why":"Confirm the culprit write"},{"do":"Restore/repair and switch to REPLACE WHERE","why":"Explicit predicate validates rows"}],"rootCauses":["Data-derived overwrite with stray keys","No validation of incoming domain"],"fix":"Validate the incoming domain; prefer explicit REPLACE WHERE; repair with RESTORE or re-load of the slice.","mnemonic":"Validate the domain before you replace"},{"id":"ch06-d07","title":"Values landed in the wrong columns","section":"ch06-s02","symptom":"After a load, `id` contains names (or numbers appear in a text column); no error was raised.","askYourself":["Did I write with insertInto (positional)?","Does the DataFrame column order match the target?","Could types be coerced silently?","Which version introduced the bad rows?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the writing code","why":"Spot positional APIs"},{"do":"Compare DataFrame column order vs DESCRIBE TABLE","why":"Confirm mismatch"},{"do":"Fix code: select columns in target order or use a name-based API","why":"Prevent recurrence","code":"df.writeTo('ecommerce.silver.customers').append()"},{"do":"RESTORE or repair affected version","why":"Clean data"}],"rootCauses":["insertInto positional resolution"],"fix":"Use name-based writers (writeTo().append(), saveAsTable append) or reorder columns explicitly.","mnemonic":"insertInto = position, not name"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"CREATE OR REPLACE in production","text":"Replaces definition/state of an existing table.","fix":"Use CREATE TABLE / IF NOT EXISTS unless a rebuild is intended."},{"title":"insertInto is positional","text":"Columns matched by position; silent wrong mapping if coercible.","fix":"Use writeTo().append()/saveAsTable or reorder columns."},{"title":"INSERT OVERWRITE without predicate","text":"Replaces the entire table.","fix":"Use INSERT INTO or REPLACE WHERE."},{"title":"Full overwrite with a subset","text":"3 years → 1 day.","fix":"Append / MERGE / REPLACE WHERE."},{"title":"Non-idempotent writes","text":"amount = amount + 10 or blind appends double-apply on retry.","fix":"MERGE that sets values from a deduplicated source."},{"title":"Legacy dynamic partition overwrite","text":"Legacy, compute limitations, stray keys replace slices.","fix":"REPLACE WHERE / REPLACE USING; validate the key domain."},{"title":"Disabling enforcement mentally","text":"Treating schema errors as a nuisance.","fix":"Investigate: intentional evolution or source bug?"},{"title":"Global autoMerge","text":"Every write in the session can evolve schemas.","fix":"Per-write mergeSchema / WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION."},{"title":"mergeSchema ≠ overwriteSchema","text":"overwriteSchema replaces schema/partitioning on overwrite.","fix":"Pick the tool by intent."},{"title":"Rename/drop without column mapping","text":"Metadata-only rename/drop needs column mapping.","fix":"Enable column mapping first."},{"title":"Widening ≠ arbitrary cast","text":"INT → STRING / STRING → STRUCT aren't widening.","fix":"Use type widening only for supported wider types."},{"title":"Uncoordinated schema changes","text":"ALTER during writes → MetadataChangedException; streams stop.","fix":"Coordinate schema changes and restart streams."},{"title":"OPTIMIZE ≠ VACUUM","text":"OPTIMIZE rewrites active files; VACUUM deletes old ones.","fix":"Run both for their own purpose."},{"title":"Judging health by total bytes","text":"File count/size distribution matters.","fix":"Check numFiles and average size in DESCRIBE DETAIL."},{"title":"Expecting immediate rewrite from CLUSTER BY","text":"Changing keys doesn't recluster existing data.","fix":"OPTIMIZE FULL to recluster everything."},{"title":"Automation replaces understanding","text":"Predictive optimization doesn't debug layouts for you.","fix":"Still learn small files, skipping, clustering."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"Default table format on Databricks?","a":"Delta (USING DELTA optional).","section":"ch06-s01"},{"q":"Recommended table type for most workloads?","a":"Unity Catalog managed Delta tables.","section":"ch06-s01"},{"q":"CREATE TABLE when table exists?","a":"Fails (use IF NOT EXISTS to no-op, OR REPLACE to replace).","section":"ch06-s01"},{"q":"CTAS schema source?","a":"Derived from the SELECT query.","section":"ch06-s01"},{"q":"CREATE TABLE b LIKE a copies?","a":"Definition/metadata only, no data.","section":"ch06-s01"},{"q":"V2 writer API?","a":"`df.writeTo(t).create() / createOrReplace() / append()`.","section":"ch06-s02"},{"q":"writeTo advantages?","a":"Clear create/replace semantics, partitioning, table properties, clustering, conditional overwrite.","section":"ch06-s02"},{"q":"insertInto resolves columns by?","a":"Position, not name.","section":"ch06-s02"},{"q":"INSERT INTO vs INSERT OVERWRITE?","a":"INTO adds rows; OVERWRITE replaces the target scope.","section":"ch06-s03"},{"q":"TRUNCATE vs DROP?","a":"TRUNCATE removes rows (table + schema stay); DROP removes the table object.","section":"ch06-s03"},{"q":"When append?","a":"Rows are genuinely new, no overlap.","section":"ch06-s04"},{"q":"When full overwrite?","a":"Target should exactly equal the new DataFrame.","section":"ch06-s04"},{"q":"Idempotent operation?","a":"Re-running with same input doesn't change final state again.","section":"ch06-s04"},{"q":"Non-idempotent example?","a":"`UPDATE … SET amount = amount + 10` (twice → +20).","section":"ch06-s04"},{"q":"REPLACE WHERE syntax?","a":"`INSERT INTO t REPLACE WHERE pred SELECT …`.","section":"ch06-s05"},{"q":"REPLACE USING does?","a":"Replaces target rows matching incoming values of the listed key columns (dynamic overwrite).","section":"ch06-s05"},{"q":"Legacy dynamic overwrite option?","a":"`.option('partitionOverwriteMode','dynamic')` with mode overwrite.","section":"ch06-s05"},{"q":"Dynamic overwrite risk?","a":"A stray key in the data replaces that whole slice — validate the domain.","section":"ch06-s05"},{"q":"Schema enforcement rules?","a":"Columns must exist; types must match or be safely castable.","section":"ch06-s06"},{"q":"INT into BIGINT column?","a":"OK — safe cast.","section":"ch06-s06"},{"q":"Per-write evolution option?","a":"`.option('mergeSchema','true')`.","section":"ch06-s07"},{"q":"Global evolution config?","a":"`spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled` — avoid in production.","section":"ch06-s07"},{"q":"SQL evolution syntax?","a":"`MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO …`; `INSERT WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO …` (DBR 18.1+).","section":"ch06-s07"},{"q":"overwriteSchema does?","a":"Replaces schema/partitioning during overwrite.","section":"ch06-s07"},{"q":"Rename/drop column requires?","a":"Column mapping (for metadata-only changes).","section":"ch06-s07"},{"q":"Enable type widening?","a":"`'delta.enableTypeWidening' = 'true'` then `ALTER COLUMN c TYPE BIGINT`.","section":"ch06-s08"},{"q":"INT can widen to?","a":"BIGINT, DECIMAL, DOUBLE.","section":"ch06-s08"},{"q":"Does supported widening rewrite files?","a":"No.","section":"ch06-s08"},{"q":"Schema change during writes →?","a":"MetadataChangedException for concurrent writers; streams may stop.","section":"ch06-s08"},{"q":"DESCRIBE DETAIL key fields?","a":"format, location, sizeInBytes, numFiles, partition/clustering cols, properties/features.","section":"ch06-s09"},{"q":"Average file size formula?","a":"sizeInBytes / numFiles.","section":"ch06-s09"},{"q":"Small-file problem costs?","a":"Metadata, file listing/open, task scheduling, poor scan efficiency.","section":"ch06-s10"},{"q":"Plain OPTIMIZE does?","a":"Bin packing: compacts small files into balanced larger files.","section":"ch06-s10"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE vs VACUUM?","a":"OPTIMIZE reorganizes active files; VACUUM deletes expired unreferenced files.","section":"ch06-s10"},{"q":"dataChange=false means?","a":"Commit rearranges data without logical record change.","section":"ch06-s10"},{"q":"Data skipping?","a":"Using file stats to skip files that can't contain matching rows.","section":"ch06-s11"},{"q":"File stats stored?","a":"min, max, null counts, row count.","section":"ch06-s11"},{"q":"Liquid clustering syntax?","a":"`ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (col)`; `OPTIMIZE t FULL` for existing data.","section":"ch06-s12"},{"q":"Good clustering key?","a":"Frequently filtered/joined, selective lookups, enough cardinality.","section":"ch06-s12"},{"q":"Why 'liquid'?","a":"Keys can change without immediate rewrite of historical layout; incremental.","section":"ch06-s12"},{"q":"Predictive optimization runs?","a":"OPTIMIZE, VACUUM, ANALYZE on UC managed tables when useful.","section":"ch06-s13"},{"q":"5 debug workflows?","a":"MERGE fails; schema mismatch; storage exploded; scans too much; someone broke data.","section":"ch06-s14"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch06-e001","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`CREATE TABLE ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw (...)` silently replaces the table if it already exists.","answer":false,"explain":"Plain `CREATE TABLE` usually **fails** when the table exists. Replacement needs `CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE`; no-op-if-exists needs `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`."},{"id":"ch06-e002","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"In a CTAS (`CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT`), where does the table's schema come from?","options":["You must declare it in parentheses first","It is derived from the SELECT query","It is copied from the source table's DDL","Delta infers it later on first read"],"answer":1,"why":["Not in CTAS.","Correct.","Only CREATE TABLE LIKE copies a definition.","Schema is fixed at creation."],"explain":"CTAS derives column names and types from the query — that's why you use CAST/aliases in the SELECT to control the resulting schema."},{"id":"ch06-e003","type":"write","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Exercise 1: create an **empty** table `dev.silver.customers` with the same schema as `prod.silver.customers` — no data.","solution":"CREATE TABLE dev.silver.customers\nLIKE prod.silver.customers;","keywords":["create table dev.silver.customers","like prod.silver.customers"],"lang":"sql","explain":"`CREATE TABLE … LIKE …` copies the definition/metadata, not the rows. Great for dev/test, prod-like staging, schema cloning and migration testing."},{"id":"ch06-e004","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"In Databricks you must write `USING DELTA` or the table will be created as Parquet.","answer":false,"explain":"Delta is the default table format on Databricks. `USING DELTA` is optional — useful only for explicitness."},{"id":"ch06-e005","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The table already exists. What does each statement do?","buckets":["Fails","No-op","Replaces definition/state"],"items":[{"text":"CREATE TABLE t (...)","bucket":0},{"text":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS t (...)","bucket":1},{"text":"CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE t (...)","bucket":2},{"text":"CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE t AS SELECT …","bucket":2},{"text":"CREATE TABLE t AS SELECT … (CTAS without OR REPLACE)","bucket":0}],"explain":"Only OR REPLACE replaces; IF NOT EXISTS skips; plain CREATE (including plain CTAS) fails on an existing table. In production, OR REPLACE must be a conscious choice."},{"id":"ch06-e006","type":"write","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"CTAS: create/replace `ecommerce.silver.orders` from `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`, casting `order_id` to BIGINT and `amount` to DECIMAL(10,2), upper-casing `status`, keeping `customer_id` and `order_ts`.","solution":"CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders AS\nSELECT\n  CAST(order_id AS BIGINT) AS order_id,\n  customer_id,\n  CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2)) AS amount,\n  UPPER(status) AS status,\n  order_ts\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw;","keywords":["create or replace table ecommerce.silver.orders as","cast(order_id as bigint)","decimal(10,2)","upper(status)","from ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw"],"lang":"sql","explain":"The SELECT defines the Silver schema; casts make types explicit instead of relying on whatever Bronze had."},{"id":"ch06-e007","type":"odd","section":"ch06-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement copies data rows into the new table?","options":["`CREATE TABLE b LIKE a`","`CREATE TABLE b (id BIGINT)`","`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS b (id BIGINT)`","`CREATE TABLE b AS SELECT * FROM a`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Only CTAS materializes query results. LIKE copies structure only; the others define an empty schema."},{"id":"ch06-e008","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Exercise 2: target `(id BIGINT, name STRING)`; DataFrame columns `(name, id)`. Which API is the most dangerous here?","options":["`df.writeTo(t).append()`","`df.write.mode('append').saveAsTable(t)`","`df.write.insertInto(t)`","`MERGE INTO` with explicit columns"],"answer":2,"why":["Name-based.","Name-based for Delta tables.","Correct: position-based.","Explicit mapping by name."],"explain":"`insertInto` resolves columns **by position**, so `name` goes into `id`. If types can be coerced it silently writes wrong data; otherwise it fails."},{"id":"ch06-e009","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"`df.writeTo(...)` is the DataFrameWriterV2 API, which Databricks recommends for many modern table writes.","answer":true,"explain":"writeTo() = V2: clearer create/replace semantics, better partitioning, table properties, clustering and conditional overwrite support."},{"id":"ch06-e010","type":"match","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each call to its effect.","pairs":[["df.writeTo(t).create()","Create new table; fail if it exists"],["df.writeTo(t).createOrReplace()","Create or replace the table"],["df.writeTo(t).append()","Add rows to existing table"],["df.write.mode('overwrite').saveAsTable(t)","Classic API: replace table contents"],["df.write.insertInto(t)","Insert by column position"]],"explain":"V2 methods name the intent directly; the classic API encodes it in `.mode()`; insertInto is positional."},{"id":"ch06-e011","type":"spotbug","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Target `ecommerce.silver.customers` has columns `(id, name)`. Which line causes silently swapped values?","lines":["df = spark.table('staging.customers_raw').select('name', 'id')","df = df.dropDuplicates(['id'])","df.write.insertInto('ecommerce.silver.customers')","spark.table('ecommerce.silver.customers').show()"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"df.select('id', 'name').write.insertInto('ecommerce.silver.customers')\n# or: df.writeTo('ecommerce.silver.customers').append()","explain":"insertInto maps by position: DataFrame column 1 (`name`) → target column 1 (`id`). Either reorder to match the target, or use a name-based API."},{"id":"ch06-e012","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Create or replace a Delta table with the V2 API.","text":"(df.[[writeTo]](\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n   .[[using]](\"delta\")\n   .[[createOrReplace]]())","bank":["saveAsTable","mode","insertInto","format"],"asCode":true,"explain":"writeTo(name) → using(format) → createOrReplace(). `.create()` would fail if the table existed; `.append()` adds rows."},{"id":"ch06-e013","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Difference between `saveAsTable(...)` and `writeTo(...)`?","options":["They are identical aliases","saveAsTable is the older DataFrameWriter API; writeTo is DataFrameWriterV2 with more explicit create/replace/partitioning/table semantics","writeTo only works for Parquet","saveAsTable can't overwrite"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both write tables. V2 makes intent explicit (create, createOrReplace, append, overwrite…) and supports properties/clustering better; the classic API relies on `.mode()`."},{"id":"ch06-e014","type":"write","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Append `df` to `ecommerce.silver.orders` using the V2 API.","solution":"df.writeTo(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\").append()","keywords":["writeto(","ecommerce.silver.orders",".append()"],"lang":"python","explain":"V2 append is name-based and explicit. Classic equivalent: `df.write.mode('append').saveAsTable(...)`."},{"id":"ch06-e015","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Difference between `INSERT INTO` and `INSERT OVERWRITE`?","options":["None on Delta","INTO adds rows; OVERWRITE replaces the target scope","INTO replaces; OVERWRITE appends","OVERWRITE only changes the schema"],"answer":1,"explain":"INTO is additive. OVERWRITE replaces the target scope — without restriction, the entire table contents."},{"id":"ch06-e016","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`TRUNCATE TABLE` removes the table object from the catalog.","answer":false,"explain":"TRUNCATE removes rows; schema and table object remain. `DROP TABLE` removes the table object."},{"id":"ch06-e017","type":"calc","section":"ch06-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"`ecommerce.silver.orders` has 1,000 rows. You run `INSERT OVERWRITE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders SELECT * FROM staging.orders` where staging has 200 rows (no partition restriction). How many rows does the table have now?","answer":200,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"INSERT OVERWRITE replaced the whole target scope with the 200 staging rows. With `INSERT INTO` it would have been 1,200."},{"id":"ch06-e018","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Daily batch ETL: copy today's Bronze rows into Silver.","text":"INSERT [[INTO]] ecommerce.silver.orders\n[[SELECT]] order_id, customer_id, amount, status, order_ts\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nWHERE ingestion_date = [[current_date()]];","bank":["OVERWRITE","VALUES","now"],"asCode":true,"explain":"INSERT INTO … SELECT is the bread-and-butter additive load. Using OVERWRITE here would wipe all previous days."},{"id":"ch06-e019","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"TRUNCATE TABLE or DROP TABLE?","buckets":["TRUNCATE TABLE","DROP TABLE"],"items":[{"text":"Schema remains","bucket":0},{"text":"Table object removed","bucket":1},{"text":"All rows removed, table still queryable","bucket":0},{"text":"Table no longer appears in SHOW TABLES","bucket":1},{"text":"You can INSERT into it immediately afterwards","bucket":0}],"explain":"TRUNCATE empties; DROP deletes the object (and its definition)."},{"id":"ch06-e020","type":"odd","section":"ch06-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement does NOT keep existing rows?","options":["`INSERT INTO t VALUES (...)`","`INSERT INTO t SELECT …`","`df.writeTo(t).append()`","`INSERT OVERWRITE TABLE t SELECT …`"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first three are additive; INSERT OVERWRITE replaces the target scope."},{"id":"ch06-e021","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"When do you use append?","options":["When the incoming rows are genuinely additive and don't replace existing state","Whenever a job might retry","When the target should exactly equal the DataFrame","When fixing yesterday's wrong data"],"answer":0,"explain":"Append = existing + new rows, no overlap. If rows overlap or correct existing state, you need MERGE/REPLACE WHERE; 'exactly equal' is full overwrite."},{"id":"ch06-e022","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A full overwrite is dangerous when the DataFrame contains only a subset of the table (e.g. only yesterday).","answer":true,"explain":"Overwrite replaces the logical state with the DataFrame: 3 years of data become 1 day. One of the most classic production mistakes."},{"id":"ch06-e023","type":"calc","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Order id=5 has `amount = 100`. A job runs `UPDATE orders SET amount = amount + 10 WHERE id = 5`, fails after committing, and is retried once. What is the amount now?","answer":120,"tolerance":0,"unit":"","explain":"+10 and +10 → 120. The statement is **not idempotent**: repeating it keeps changing the state. A MERGE that sets `amount = s.amount` would give the same result on every retry."},{"id":"ch06-e024","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Idempotent (safe to re-run) or not?","buckets":["Idempotent","Not idempotent"],"items":[{"text":"MERGE setting t.status = s.status from a deduplicated source","bucket":0},{"text":"UPDATE … SET amount = amount + 10","bucket":1},{"text":"Appending the same daily batch twice","bucket":1},{"text":"REPLACE WHERE order_date = '2026-09-27' with the same data","bucket":0},{"text":"Full overwrite with the same complete DataFrame","bucket":0},{"text":"Incrementing a counter column","bucket":1}],"explain":"Idempotent writes **set** a final state; non-idempotent ones **add to** the current state (increments, blind appends)."},{"id":"ch06-e025","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Monday morning: `ecommerce.silver.orders` (3 years of data) now holds only Sunday's orders.","steps":[{"prompt":"First move?","options":[{"text":"DESCRIBE HISTORY to find the last write and its operationParameters (mode)","ok":true,"fb":"History reveals a WRITE with mode Overwrite — the smoking gun."},{"text":"Re-run all ingestion jobs for 3 years","ok":false,"fb":"Slow and unnecessary while history still has the good version."},{"text":"VACUUM to clean up","ok":false,"fb":"Would destroy the old files you need to recover!"}]},{"prompt":"History: v310 = WRITE mode=Overwrite by the daily job. Recovery?","options":[{"text":"RESTORE TABLE … TO VERSION AS OF 309 (files still within retention)","ok":true,"fb":"Creates v311 equal to the good state."},{"text":"Time travel only — SELECT VERSION AS OF 309 fixes it","ok":false,"fb":"Time travel reads; it doesn't make the state current."},{"text":"Delete the v310 commit file","ok":false,"fb":"Never hand-edit the log."}]},{"prompt":"Prevention?","options":[{"text":"Change the daily job to append / MERGE / REPLACE WHERE order_date = <day>","ok":true,"fb":"Never full-overwrite with a subset."},{"text":"Keep overwrite but run it at night","ok":false,"fb":"Timing doesn't change semantics."},{"text":"Enable autoMerge","ok":false,"fb":"Schema setting, unrelated."}]}],"explain":"Overwrite with a subset = data loss in the current state. Recover with RESTORE, prevent with append/MERGE/selective overwrite."},{"id":"ch06-e026","type":"free","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"What does it mean that a MERGE is idempotent, and why does it matter for production jobs?","model":"Idempotent means that running the same operation again with the same input does not change the final logical state any further. A MERGE that sets target columns from a deduplicated source (e.g. id=5 → status 'PAID') gives the same table no matter how many times it runs. That matters because jobs retry after failures: a retry must not double-apply changes like `amount = amount + 10` or duplicate appended rows.","rubric":["Same input re-run → same final state","MERGE sets values (not increments)","Retries are common in production","Contrast with non-idempotent increments/appends"],"explain":"Mention **retries** and give a non-idempotent counter-example."},{"id":"ch06-e027","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Daily file for 2026-09-27 may be re-delivered with corrections later that day. Which write is the best fit?","options":["Append","Full overwrite of the table","Selective overwrite of that day (REPLACE WHERE) or MERGE on the key","INSERT OVERWRITE without predicate"],"answer":2,"explain":"Data overlap with existing rows, so plain append would duplicate; full overwrite would wipe other days. Replace that day's slice or MERGE by key — both idempotent."},{"id":"ch06-e028","type":"write","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Exercise 4: replace only `country = 'GR'` rows of `sales.orders` with `staging.orders_gr`, keeping DE/FR unchanged.","solution":"INSERT INTO sales.orders\nREPLACE WHERE country = 'GR'\nSELECT * FROM staging.orders_gr;","keywords":["insert into sales.orders","replace where country = 'gr'","select"],"lang":"sql","explain":"REPLACE WHERE replaces exactly the rows matching the predicate and leaves everything else untouched — far safer than a full overwrite."},{"id":"ch06-e029","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Dynamic overwrite, modern style.","text":"INSERT INTO ecommerce.silver.orders\n[[REPLACE USING]] (order_date)\nSELECT * FROM ecommerce.staging.orders_daily;","bank":["REPLACE WHERE","OVERWRITE PARTITION","MERGE USING"],"asCode":true,"explain":"REPLACE USING (cols) replaces target rows whose key-column values appear in the incoming data. Recommended over legacy partitionOverwriteMode=dynamic."},{"id":"ch06-e030","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which pattern does Databricks recommend for selective overwrite in new workloads?","options":["`partitionOverwriteMode = dynamic`","`REPLACE WHERE` / `REPLACE USING` (or `REPLACE ON`) depending on use case","Full overwrite then re-append old data","DELETE then INSERT in two jobs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The modern INSERT … REPLACE variants are atomic and explicit. Dynamic partition overwrite is legacy with compute limitations; DELETE + INSERT in two jobs isn't atomic."},{"id":"ch06-e031","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`.option(\"partitionOverwriteMode\", \"dynamic\")` is the preferred choice for new workloads.","answer":false,"explain":"It's legacy: still supported in some contexts, has compute limitations, and Databricks recommends REPLACE WHERE / REPLACE USING instead."},{"id":"ch06-e032","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"You ran a dynamic overwrite for 2026-09-27. Next day, analysts report that all of **2024-01-01**'s orders are gone except one row.","steps":[{"prompt":"What likely happened?","options":[{"text":"The incoming data contained a stray row with order_date = 2024-01-01, so that whole slice was replaced","ok":true,"fb":"Dynamic overwrite replaces every key present in the incoming data."},{"text":"VACUUM deleted 2024 files","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM doesn't change the current snapshot."},{"text":"A concurrency conflict dropped rows","ok":false,"fb":"Conflicts fail transactions; they don't drop rows."}]},{"prompt":"How do you confirm?","options":[{"text":"DESCRIBE HISTORY + time-travel compare the 2024-01-01 slice before/after the write","ok":true,"fb":"Shows the slice shrank in that exact version."},{"text":"DESCRIBE DETAIL","ok":false,"fb":"Gives current size/files, not per-slice changes."},{"text":"Check cluster logs only","ok":false,"fb":"The data evidence is in history."}]},{"prompt":"Prevention for next time?","options":[{"text":"Validate the incoming key domain (e.g. SELECT DISTINCT order_date) before writing, or use REPLACE WHERE with an explicit predicate","ok":true,"fb":"REPLACE WHERE fails if rows violate the predicate."},{"text":"Switch to full overwrite","ok":false,"fb":"Even worse."},{"text":"Increase VACUUM retention","ok":false,"fb":"Helps recovery, not prevention."}]}],"explain":"Always validate the incoming key/partition domain; explicit predicates are safer than data-derived slices."},{"id":"ch06-e033","type":"spotbug","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A job should only replace 2026-09-27 in a 3-year table. Which line is the bug?","lines":["daily = spark.table('ecommerce.staging.orders_daily')","daily = daily.filter(\"order_date = '2026-09-27'\")","daily.write.mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('ecommerce.silver.orders')","print('done')"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"(daily.write.mode(\"overwrite\")\n     .option(\"replaceWhere\", \"order_date = '2026-09-27'\")\n     .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\"))\n# or SQL: INSERT INTO ecommerce.silver.orders REPLACE WHERE order_date = DATE'2026-09-27' SELECT ...","explain":"A plain overwrite replaces the **whole** table with one day. Restrict it with a replaceWhere predicate (the DataFrame form of REPLACE WHERE)."},{"id":"ch06-e034","type":"order","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Order a safe selective-overwrite routine.","items":["Decide which slice to replace (e.g. order_date = 2026-09-27)","Validate the incoming key domain (SELECT DISTINCT order_date)","INSERT INTO … REPLACE WHERE <slice predicate> SELECT …","Check DESCRIBE HISTORY metrics for the new version"],"explain":"Decide → validate → replace explicitly → verify. Validation catches stray keys before they replace unintended slices."},{"id":"ch06-e035","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":3,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Incoming data accidentally includes one row outside the intended date. Which option protects you best?","options":["REPLACE USING (order_date)","partitionOverwriteMode = dynamic","REPLACE WHERE order_date = DATE'2026-09-27'","Full overwrite"],"answer":2,"explain":"REPLACE WHERE states the slice explicitly and validates that written rows match the predicate, so the stray row makes the write fail instead of wiping another slice. Data-derived dynamic options replace whatever keys arrive."},{"id":"ch06-e036","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is schema enforcement?","options":["Automatically adding new columns","Validation on write that incoming columns/types are compatible with the target schema","Casting everything to STRING","Locking the schema forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Enforcement rejects incompatible writes (missing/unknown columns, unsafe types) using the schema stored in the log metadata."},{"id":"ch06-e037","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Writing `amount INT` into a target column `amount BIGINT` can succeed through a safe cast.","answer":true,"explain":"INT → BIGINT is safely castable. A complex STRUCT into BIGINT is not."},{"id":"ch06-e038","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Default behaviour (no evolution enabled): accepted or rejected?","buckets":["Accepted","Rejected"],"items":[{"text":"INT into BIGINT column","bucket":0},{"text":"STRUCT<foo:INT> into DECIMAL(10,2) column","bucket":1},{"text":"Extra column `email` not in target","bucket":1},{"text":"Exactly matching columns and types","bucket":0},{"text":"STRUCT into BIGINT column","bucket":1}],"explain":"Columns must exist and types must match or be safely castable. New columns need explicit evolution."},{"id":"ch06-e039","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which rules does Delta check on write? (choose all)","options":["Columns must exist in the target","Types must match or be safely castable","Column order must match exactly for saveAsTable","Row count must not decrease"],"answer":[0,1],"multi":true,"explain":"Schema validation is about column existence and type compatibility. Column order matters only for positional APIs like insertInto; row counts aren't validated."},{"id":"ch06-e040","type":"free","section":"ch06-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution?","model":"Schema enforcement validates every write against the target schema and rejects incompatible data, protecting downstream tables from silent corruption. Schema evolution is a controlled, explicit change of the target schema so it can accept intentional structural changes, such as a new column, via mergeSchema, WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION or ALTER TABLE.","rubric":["Enforcement rejects incompatible data","Evolution = controlled schema change","Evolution must be explicit/intentional","Examples of mechanisms"],"explain":"One protects, the other permits — explicitly."},{"id":"ch06-e041","type":"odd","section":"ch06-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a goal of schema enforcement?","options":["Stop a bad producer before it corrupts Silver/Gold","Reject a STRUCT arriving in a DECIMAL column","Automatically add every new column a source sends","Use the schema stored in the transaction log for validation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Adding columns is evolution — opt-in and explicit. Enforcement's job is to reject."},{"id":"ch06-e042","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Why is a global `spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true` not the preferred production approach?","options":["It's slower","It lets many unrelated writes evolve schemas and hides which operation was allowed to","It's deprecated and removed","It only works with Parquet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Session-wide evolution affects every subsequent write. Per-write `mergeSchema` or `WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION` keeps the permission local and explicit."},{"id":"ch06-e043","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`mergeSchema` and `overwriteSchema` do the same thing.","answer":false,"explain":"mergeSchema evolves/adds compatible changes during a write; overwriteSchema replaces the table schema (and partitioning) during an overwrite."},{"id":"ch06-e044","type":"write","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Append `source_df` to `prod.silver.customers`, allowing schema evolution for this write only.","solution":"(source_df.write\n   .option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")\n   .mode(\"append\")\n   .saveAsTable(\"prod.silver.customers\"))","keywords":["option(\"mergeschema\", \"true\")","mode(\"append\")","saveastable(\"prod.silver.customers\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Per-write evolution: only this write may add `segment`. The rest of the session stays protected by enforcement."},{"id":"ch06-e045","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"MERGE that may evolve the schema for this operation only.","text":"MERGE [[WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION]] INTO target t\nUSING source s\nON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","bank":["WITH MERGESCHEMA","AUTO MERGE","OVERWRITE SCHEMA"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO` scopes evolution to that statement — the SQL counterpart of per-write mergeSchema."},{"id":"ch06-e046","type":"spotbug","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A notebook fixing a failing append. Which line is the anti-pattern?","lines":["# source added column 'segment'","spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled', 'true')","source_df.write.mode('append').saveAsTable('prod.silver.customers')","other_df.write.mode('append').saveAsTable('prod.gold.kpis')"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"source_df.write.option('mergeSchema', 'true').mode('append').saveAsTable('prod.silver.customers')","explain":"The global setting also lets `other_df`'s write (and every later one) evolve schemas silently. Scope evolution to the one write that needs it — after confirming the new column is intentional."},{"id":"ch06-e047","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Today's append to `prod.silver.customers` fails with a schema mismatch. Yesterday it worked.","steps":[{"prompt":"First step?","options":[{"text":"Compare DESCRIBE TABLE with df.printSchema()","ok":true,"fb":"See exactly which column/type differs."},{"text":"Enable mergeSchema and re-run","ok":false,"fb":"Blindly evolving can bake a source bug into Silver."},{"text":"Drop the target and recreate it from the DataFrame","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys data and history."}],"code":"DESCRIBE TABLE prod.silver.customers;\n-- vs\ndf.printSchema()"},{"prompt":"The DataFrame has a new column `segment STRING`. Next question?","options":[{"text":"Is `segment` an intentional addition, or a source bug?","ok":true,"fb":"Intentional evolution vs bug is the key decision."},{"text":"Which cluster ran the job?","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to schema."},{"text":"How many files does the table have?","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant here."}]},{"prompt":"Product confirms `segment` is intentional. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Per-write .option('mergeSchema','true') (or ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMNS segment STRING)","ok":true,"fb":"Local, explicit evolution."},{"text":"Set autoMerge globally for the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Too broad: unrelated writes could evolve too."},{"text":"Use overwriteSchema with append","ok":false,"fb":"overwriteSchema replaces schema on overwrite — wrong tool."}]}],"explain":"DESCRIBE TABLE vs printSchema → new column/type/rename? → intentional? → explicit local evolution. Never blind mergeSchema."},{"id":"ch06-e048","type":"match","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each tool to its effect.","pairs":[["option('mergeSchema','true')","Evolve schema for this write"],["autoMerge.enabled = true","Evolve schema for all writes in the session"],["option('overwriteSchema','true')","Replace schema/partitioning during overwrite"],["ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMNS","Manual, explicit schema change"],["INSERT WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO","SQL insert that may evolve schema (DBR 18.1+)"]],"explain":"Know the scope of each: per write, per session, replace, manual DDL, per SQL statement."},{"id":"ch06-e049","type":"write","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Manual evolution on `prod.silver.customers`: add `email STRING`, then rename `name` to `full_name`.","solution":"ALTER TABLE prod.silver.customers ADD COLUMNS (email STRING);\nALTER TABLE prod.silver.customers RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name;","keywords":["add columns (email string)","rename column name to full_name"],"lang":"sql","explain":"ALTER TABLE makes intentional changes explicit. Metadata-only rename (and drop) requires column mapping on the table."},{"id":"ch06-e050","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Metadata-only `RENAME COLUMN` and `DROP COLUMN` on Delta require column mapping support.","answer":true,"explain":"Without column mapping, Delta identifies columns by name in the Parquet files, so renames/drops can't be metadata-only."},{"id":"ch06-e051","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement needs DBR 18.1+ according to the course?","options":["`MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO …`","`INSERT WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO … SELECT …`","`.option('mergeSchema','true')`","`ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMNS`"],"answer":1,"explain":"INSERT WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION is the newest form (DBR 18.1+). MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION, mergeSchema and ALTER have been around longer."},{"id":"ch06-e052","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is type widening?","options":["Any type change, e.g. INT → STRING","A compatible move to a wider type, e.g. INT → BIGINT or FLOAT → DOUBLE, without rewriting data files for supported cases","Adding new columns","Increasing the number of partitions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Widening keeps meaning and only expands range/precision. INT → STRING changes semantics and isn't widening."},{"id":"ch06-e053","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`STRING → STRUCT` is a supported type widening.","answer":false,"explain":"Widening ≠ arbitrary cast. STRING → STRUCT changes the data's meaning entirely; type evolution doesn't mean 'any type to any type'."},{"id":"ch06-e054","type":"write","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Enable type widening on `ecommerce.silver.orders` and widen `quantity` to BIGINT.","solution":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\nSET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableTypeWidening' = 'true');\n\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\nALTER COLUMN quantity TYPE BIGINT;","keywords":["delta.enableTypeWidening","alter column quantity type bigint"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Type widening is a table feature: enable it, then widen. Supported changes don't rewrite the underlying Parquet files."},{"id":"ch06-e055","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Supported widening or not?","buckets":["Supported widening","Not widening"],"items":[{"text":"INT → BIGINT","bucket":0},{"text":"FLOAT → DOUBLE","bucket":0},{"text":"SHORT → INT","bucket":0},{"text":"BYTE → DECIMAL","bucket":0},{"text":"INT → STRING","bucket":1},{"text":"BIGINT → INT","bucket":1},{"text":"STRING → STRUCT","bucket":1}],"explain":"Widening goes to a wider compatible numeric type. Narrowing (BIGINT → INT) loses range; INT → STRING / STRING → STRUCT change meaning."},{"id":"ch06-e056","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"12:00 — a streaming pipeline writing to `prod.silver.orders` fails with `MetadataChangedException`. At 12:00 a colleague ran `ALTER TABLE prod.silver.orders ADD COLUMNS (channel STRING)`.","steps":[{"prompt":"Is this a Delta bug?","options":[{"text":"No — schema changes are metadata commits that conflict with concurrent writes","ok":true,"fb":"Expected optimistic-concurrency semantics."},{"text":"Yes — the table is corrupted","ok":false,"fb":"Concurrency exceptions prevent corruption."},{"text":"Yes — streaming doesn't support Delta","ok":false,"fb":"It does; it just reacts to schema changes."}]},{"prompt":"What else should you expect?","options":[{"text":"Streams reading the table may stop until restarted","ok":true,"fb":"Schema changes can halt streaming readers too."},{"text":"All old versions are deleted","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing like that happens."},{"text":"The column is silently dropped","ok":false,"fb":"The ALTER committed fine."}]},{"prompt":"Process fix?","options":[{"text":"Coordinate schema changes with pipeline owners (maintenance window), then restart affected streams","ok":true,"fb":"Databricks recommends coordinating schema updates."},{"text":"Disable the transaction log during ALTERs","ok":false,"fb":"Not a thing."},{"text":"Enable autoMerge globally so ALTERs aren't needed","ok":false,"fb":"Trades a coordination problem for uncontrolled evolution."}]}],"explain":"Schema change = metadata change → conflicts with concurrent writers and can stop streams. Coordinate it."},{"id":"ch06-e057","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Supported type-widening changes rewrite all the table's Parquet files.","answer":false,"explain":"Supported widening is metadata-level: the underlying data files are not rewritten — readers upcast values on read."},{"id":"ch06-e058","type":"order","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the steps to widen `quantity` from INT to BIGINT safely in production.","items":["Coordinate the change with pipelines writing the table","ALTER TABLE … SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableTypeWidening' = 'true')","ALTER TABLE … ALTER COLUMN quantity TYPE BIGINT","Restart streams that read the table if they stopped"],"explain":"Schema changes are metadata commits that conflict with concurrent writes — coordinate first, enable the feature, widen, then restart affected streams."},{"id":"ch06-e059","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What does `DESCRIBE DETAIL` show?","options":["Every version with its operation","Current table-level metadata: format, location, numFiles, sizeInBytes, partitioning, properties/features","Column names only","Running queries"],"answer":1,"explain":"DETAIL = one row of current table metadata. Versions → DESCRIBE HISTORY; columns → DESCRIBE TABLE."},{"id":"ch06-e060","type":"match","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Which command answers which question?","pairs":[["DESCRIBE TABLE","Which columns and types?"],["DESCRIBE HISTORY","Who changed what, when?"],["DESCRIBE DETAIL","How many files and bytes?"],["df.printSchema()","What schema does my DataFrame have?"]],"explain":"Schema-mismatch debugging pairs DESCRIBE TABLE with printSchema; storage debugging uses DETAIL; data-change debugging uses HISTORY."},{"id":"ch06-e061","type":"calc","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"DESCRIBE DETAIL shows `sizeInBytes` = 20 GB and `numFiles` = 200,000. What's the average file size in **KB**? (use 1 GB = 1,000,000 KB)","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"KB","explain":"20,000,000 KB / 200,000 = 100 KB per file — classic small-file pressure. Healthy Delta files are typically in the hundreds of MB to ~1 GB range."},{"id":"ch06-e062","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"`DESCRIBE DETAIL` returns one row with table-level metadata.","answer":true,"explain":"One row: format, location, size, numFiles, partition/clustering columns, properties, features…"},{"id":"ch06-e063","type":"odd","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which field does NOT come from DESCRIBE DETAIL?","options":["numFiles","sizeInBytes","operationMetrics","location"],"answer":2,"explain":"operationMetrics is per-version info from DESCRIBE HISTORY."},{"id":"ch06-e064","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Cloud bill for the table's storage doubled, but `DESCRIBE DETAIL` `sizeInBytes` stayed flat. Most likely explanation?","options":["DETAIL is broken","DETAIL measures the current snapshot; old unreferenced files (history) aren't counted — check VACUUM/retention","The table switched to Parquet","Deletion vectors doubled the size"],"answer":1,"explain":"Removed files still occupy storage until VACUUM. DETAIL reports the current snapshot only, so history-driven storage growth is invisible there."},{"id":"ch06-e065","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is the small-file problem?","options":["Files under 1 GB can't be read","Too many small physical files create metadata, file-open and task-scheduling overhead","Parquet compresses small files badly","Small files break ACID"],"answer":1,"explain":"Total bytes may be fine; the number of files kills performance: listing, metadata, many tiny tasks, poor scan efficiency."},{"id":"ch06-e066","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"OPTIMIZE physically deletes old unreferenced files from storage.","answer":false,"explain":"OPTIMIZE rewrites active files into a better layout (new version). Deleting old unreferenced files is VACUUM's job, after retention."},{"id":"ch06-e067","type":"calc","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"A 10 GB dataset is stored as files of 100 KB each. How many files is that? (1 GB = 1,000,000 KB)","answer":100000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"10,000,000 KB / 100 KB = 100,000 files — option B from the lesson, versus 10 × 1 GB in option A. Same bytes, vastly more overhead."},{"id":"ch06-e068","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Without clustering/Z-order, what does `OPTIMIZE` do?","options":["Bin packing: compacts small files into more balanced, larger files","Sorts all data by primary key","Deletes duplicates","Collects statistics only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plain OPTIMIZE = bin packing. With liquid clustering it also organizes data by the clustering keys."},{"id":"ch06-e069","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"OPTIMIZE or VACUUM?","buckets":["OPTIMIZE","VACUUM"],"items":[{"text":"Rewrites active data files","bucket":0},{"text":"Deletes old unreferenced files","bucket":1},{"text":"Fixes small-file pressure","bucket":0},{"text":"Reduces storage after DELETEs (after retention)","bucket":1},{"text":"Commits REMOVE/ADD with dataChange=false","bucket":0},{"text":"Garbage collection","bucket":1}],"explain":"OPTIMIZE = layout/performance; VACUUM = physical cleanup. Often run together: OPTIMIZE, then later VACUUM removes the replaced small files."},{"id":"ch06-e070","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`ecommerce.silver.orders` storage and query times exploded after a streaming job went live.","steps":[{"prompt":"First command?","options":[{"text":"DESCRIBE DETAIL ecommerce.silver.orders","ok":true,"fb":"numFiles and sizeInBytes reveal file count and average size."},{"text":"DESCRIBE HISTORY only","ok":false,"fb":"Useful later, but file counts come from DETAIL."},{"text":"VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS","ok":false,"fb":"Dangerous and doesn't fix layout."}],"code":"DESCRIBE DETAIL ecommerce.silver.orders;"},{"prompt":"numFiles = 450,000, average ~50 KB. Diagnosis?","options":[{"text":"Small-file pressure from frequent tiny writes","ok":true,"fb":"Hundreds of thousands of tiny files = classic small-file problem."},{"text":"Data skew","ok":false,"fb":"Skew is uneven partition sizes, not tiny files."},{"text":"Schema mismatch","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated."}]},{"prompt":"Fix?","options":[{"text":"Run OPTIMIZE (or enable predictive optimization), then VACUUM after retention to drop replaced files","ok":true,"fb":"Compaction + later cleanup."},{"text":"Only VACUUM","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM doesn't compact active files."},{"text":"Increase driver memory","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't change file layout."}]}],"explain":"DETAIL → numFiles/sizeInBytes → small files → OPTIMIZE (+ predictive optimization) and VACUUM later."},{"id":"ch06-e071","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"After OPTIMIZE, the commit can use `dataChange = false` because the data was rearranged, not logically changed.","answer":true,"explain":"The protocol's dataChange flag distinguishes rearrangement (compaction) from real record changes — e.g. so streaming readers don't reprocess compacted files as new data."},{"id":"ch06-e072","type":"calc","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Stats: file A customer_id 1–1000, file B 1001–2000, file C 2001–3000. Query: `WHERE customer_id = 2500`. How many files must be read?","answer":1,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"Only C's min/max range can contain 2500. A and B are skipped without being opened — that's data skipping."},{"id":"ch06-e073","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is data skipping?","options":["Skipping corrupted files","Using file statistics so files that cannot contain matching rows are not read","Sampling 10% of the data","Skipping VACUUM"],"answer":1,"explain":"Min/max (and null count) stats per file let the engine prove a file can't match the predicate."},{"id":"ch06-e074","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which file-level statistics does Delta typically store? (choose all)","options":["min values","max values","null counts","row count","average query latency"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"These stats live in the add actions and drive pruning, skipping and planning. Query latency is not file metadata."},{"id":"ch06-e075","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"If every file contains customer_ids from 1 to 3000, min/max statistics can still skip most files for `customer_id = 2500`.","answer":false,"explain":"Each file's range [1, 3000] contains 2500, so no file can be skipped. Skipping needs a layout where values are clustered into narrow per-file ranges."},{"id":"ch06-e076","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`SELECT * FROM ecommerce.silver.orders WHERE customer_id = 1234` still scans most of the table.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you investigate first?","options":[{"text":"Table layout and clustering: is data organized by customer_id?","ok":true,"fb":"Without clustering, every file spans many customers."},{"text":"Driver memory","ok":false,"fb":"Scan volume isn't a memory setting."},{"text":"Schema enforcement","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated."}]},{"prompt":"Layout: partitioned by order_date, no clustering. Stats exist but every file spans the full customer_id range. Also check?","options":[{"text":"Filter selectivity and whether data skipping actually prunes files","ok":true,"fb":"Confirms skipping is ineffective due to layout."},{"text":"Whether VACUUM ran","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM doesn't affect current file ranges."},{"text":"insertInto usage","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated."}]},{"prompt":"Fix?","options":[{"text":"Consider liquid clustering on customer_id, then OPTIMIZE (FULL for existing data)","ok":true,"fb":"Clusters values into narrow ranges so skipping works."},{"text":"Add more workers","ok":false,"fb":"Scans the same data faster but more expensively."},{"text":"Partition by customer_id","ok":false,"fb":"High-cardinality partitioning creates huge numbers of tiny partitions — liquid clustering is the modern answer."}]}],"explain":"Layout → clustering → stats → selectivity → skipping. Liquid clustering on the filter column is often the fix."},{"id":"ch06-e077","type":"free","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain why data skipping can fail even though Delta collects min/max statistics.","model":"Statistics only help if each file holds a narrow range of the filtered column. If data was written in arrival order, every file may contain nearly the full range of customer_id, so for any value every file's min/max range overlaps and nothing can be skipped. Missing statistics for that column or a non-selective filter also defeat skipping. Clustering the data on the filter column fixes the ranges.","rubric":["Stats help only with narrow per-file ranges","Overlapping ranges → nothing skipped","Missing stats / low selectivity","Clustering as fix"],"explain":"Link stats to physical layout."},{"id":"ch06-e078","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Why is liquid clustering different from static partitioning?","options":["It stores data in JSON","Clustering keys can evolve without immediately redesigning/rewriting the historical layout, and clustering is applied incrementally","It requires ZORDER on every column","It only works on external tables"],"answer":1,"explain":"Partitioning is a fixed physical design; liquid clustering is adaptable and incremental."},{"id":"ch06-e079","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Cluster the orders table by customer and recluster existing data.","text":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders\n[[CLUSTER BY]] (customer_id);\n\nOPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders [[FULL]];","bank":["PARTITIONED BY","ZORDER BY","ALL"],"asCode":true,"explain":"ALTER … CLUSTER BY sets the keys; OPTIMIZE … FULL applies clustering to all existing data (plain OPTIMIZE clusters incrementally)."},{"id":"ch06-e080","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Running `ALTER TABLE … CLUSTER BY (customer_id)` immediately rewrites all historical data.","answer":false,"explain":"Changing keys doesn't force an immediate rewrite. New writes/OPTIMIZE apply clustering incrementally; OPTIMIZE FULL reclusters everything."},{"id":"ch06-e081","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which column is the best liquid-clustering candidate for an orders table queried mostly with `WHERE customer_id = ?` and joined to customers?","options":["`status` (4 distinct values)","`customer_id`","`amount`","`ingestion_ts` (never filtered)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Good keys are used in filters/joins/selective lookups and have enough cardinality for layout to help. status has too few values; never-filtered columns don't help."},{"id":"ch06-e082","type":"odd","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT a typical reason to choose a clustering key?","options":["Frequently used in filters","Used in joins","Selective lookups on it","It has only 2 distinct values"],"answer":3,"explain":"Very low cardinality gives physical ordering little to work with."},{"id":"ch06-e083","type":"write","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Assignment steps 18–19: enable clustering on `ecommerce.silver.orders` by `customer_id`, then cluster all existing data.","solution":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders CLUSTER BY (customer_id);\nOPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders FULL;","keywords":["cluster by (customer_id)","optimize ecommerce.silver.orders full"],"lang":"sql","explain":"CLUSTER BY sets keys; OPTIMIZE FULL rewrites existing data accordingly. Afterwards compare DESCRIBE HISTORY/DETAIL."},{"id":"ch06-e084","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statements about liquid clustering are true? (choose all)","options":["Databricks recommends it for many new Delta tables instead of partitioning + ZORDER","Clustering keys can change as query patterns change","Liquid-clustered tables also use row-level concurrency","You should combine it with partitionBy on the same keys for best results"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Liquid clustering replaces partitioning/ZORDER rather than stacking on top of them."},{"id":"ch06-e085","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which maintenance operations can predictive optimization run automatically? (choose all)","options":["OPTIMIZE","VACUUM","ANALYZE","RESTORE","MERGE"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"PO handles maintenance: compaction/clustering, cleanup, statistics. RESTORE and MERGE are data operations you decide."},{"id":"ch06-e086","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"With predictive optimization enabled you no longer need to understand optimization.","answer":false,"explain":"Automation doesn't replace engineering understanding: you still debug small files, failed skipping, bad clustering keys and huge scans."},{"id":"ch06-e087","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Predictive optimization is recommended for which tables?","options":["Any Parquet folder","Unity Catalog managed tables","Only external tables","Only streaming tables"],"answer":1,"explain":"Databricks recommends PO for UC managed tables — it monitors and maintains them, and also collects statistics."},{"id":"ch06-e088","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Manual maintenance or predictive optimization?","buckets":["Manual","Predictive optimization"],"items":[{"text":"You schedule OPTIMIZE every Sunday","bucket":0},{"text":"Databricks decides which tables benefit","bucket":1},{"text":"You schedule VACUUM every Monday","bucket":0},{"text":"Maintenance runs when useful","bucket":1},{"text":"Collects statistics on UC managed tables","bucket":1}],"explain":"PO turns calendar-driven maintenance into need-driven maintenance."},{"id":"ch06-e089","type":"free","section":"ch06-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"A teammate says: \"We enabled predictive optimization, so performance tuning is solved.\" Respond.","model":"Predictive optimization automates maintenance on UC managed tables — it runs OPTIMIZE, VACUUM and ANALYZE when it judges they help. But it doesn't choose the right clustering keys for our queries, doesn't fix a pipeline that writes millions of tiny files, and doesn't explain why a query scans everything. We still need to understand small files, data skipping, statistics and layout to debug and design tables.","rubric":["PO = automated OPTIMIZE/VACUUM/ANALYZE on UC managed tables","Doesn't replace understanding","Examples: clustering keys, small files, skipping, huge scans"],"explain":"Balance: appreciate automation, keep ownership."},{"id":"ch06-e090","type":"match","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each debugging workflow to its first command.","pairs":[["MERGE fails: multiple source rows matched","GROUP BY key HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"],["Write fails: schema mismatch","DESCRIBE TABLE vs df.printSchema()"],["Storage exploded","DESCRIBE DETAIL (numFiles, sizeInBytes)"],["Query scans too much","Inspect layout, clustering, stats, skipping"],["Someone broke data","DESCRIBE HISTORY"]],"explain":"Five workflows, five entry points. Memorize the pairs — they are the first 30 seconds of every Delta incident."},{"id":"ch06-e091","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which is NOT one of the Phase 2 best practices?","options":["Deduplicate source before MERGE","Enable schema evolution explicitly and locally","Use time travel as long-term backup","Prefer liquid clustering over static partitions for many cases"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rule 10 says the opposite: do NOT treat time travel as long-term backup."},{"id":"ch06-e092","type":"order","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the first part of the practical assignment.","items":["CREATE TABLE","INSERT initial rows","Append new rows","UPDATE one row","DELETE one row","MERGE updates + inserts","Intentionally duplicate a source key","Deduplicate with ROW_NUMBER","Run MERGE again to prove idempotency"],"explain":"Steps 1–9: build up writes, DML, then break and fix MERGE and prove idempotency."},{"id":"ch06-e093","type":"order","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the second part of the practical assignment.","items":["Add a new column with mergeSchema","DESCRIBE DETAIL / DESCRIBE HISTORY","Query VERSION AS OF","RESTORE","Create many small files","OPTIMIZE and inspect numFiles before/after","Enable clustering","OPTIMIZE FULL","Inspect behaviour/history"],"explain":"Steps 10–20: schema evolution, inspection, time travel/restore, then small files → OPTIMIZE → clustering → OPTIMIZE FULL."},{"id":"ch06-e094","type":"bucket","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Best practice or anti-pattern?","buckets":["Best practice","Anti-pattern"],"items":[{"text":"Prefer UC managed Delta tables","bucket":0},{"text":"Blind full overwrite with a daily DataFrame","bucket":1},{"text":"Prefer REPLACE WHERE / REPLACE USING over dynamic partition overwrite","bucket":0},{"text":"Global autoMerge for the whole cluster","bucket":1},{"text":"Use MERGE for idempotent upserts","bucket":0},{"text":"Treat time travel as backup","bucket":1},{"text":"Enable predictive optimization on UC managed tables","bucket":0},{"text":"Design everything around static partitions","bucket":1}],"explain":"The 13 rules reduce to: explicit, local, idempotent, and let the platform maintain layout — while you understand it."},{"id":"ch06-e095","type":"write","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the project's idempotent MERGE: target `ecommerce.silver.orders`, source `ecommerce.staging.orders_updates` on `order_id`; on match set customer_id, amount, status, `updated_at = current_timestamp()`; otherwise insert those columns.","solution":"MERGE INTO ecommerce.silver.orders AS t\nUSING ecommerce.staging.orders_updates AS s\nON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET\n  t.customer_id = s.customer_id,\n  t.amount = s.amount,\n  t.status = s.status,\n  t.updated_at = current_timestamp()\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at)\nVALUES (s.order_id, s.customer_id, s.amount, s.status, current_timestamp());","keywords":["merge into ecommerce.silver.orders","on t.order_id = s.order_id","when matched then update set","t.updated_at = current_timestamp()","when not matched then insert"],"lang":"sql","explain":"It **sets** values from the source, so re-running yields the same business state (only `updated_at` refreshes). That's what makes retries safe."},{"id":"ch06-e096","type":"order","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the schema-mismatch workflow.","items":["Write fails","DESCRIBE TABLE on the target","Compare with df.printSchema()","Classify: new column / different type / rename","Decide: intentional evolution or source bug?","Evolve explicitly (mergeSchema / ALTER) or fix the source"],"explain":"Workflow 2. The decision point is 'intentional or bug?' — never blindly enable mergeSchema."},{"id":"ch06-e097","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"In the 'someone broke data' workflow, RESTORE comes before DESCRIBE HISTORY.","answer":false,"explain":"History first (find the bad version), time travel to confirm the last good one, then RESTORE if needed."},{"id":"ch06-e098","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why does the project MERGE count as idempotent even though it sets `updated_at = current_timestamp()`?","options":["It isn't idempotent at all","Business columns are set from the source, so re-runs converge to the same logical state; only the audit timestamp refreshes","current_timestamp() is constant forever","MERGE is always idempotent regardless of SET expressions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Idempotency is about the final logical/business state. An audit timestamp changing is acceptable; `amount = amount + 10` would not be. A MERGE with increments is not idempotent."},{"id":"ch06-e099","type":"tf","section":"ch06-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"According to the course, `REPLACE WHERE` is supported on all compute types, while legacy dynamic partition overwrite has compute limitations.","answer":true,"explain":"One more reason to prefer REPLACE WHERE / REPLACE USING for new workloads: no compute-type restrictions, explicit semantics, atomic replacement."},{"id":"ch06-e100","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Overwrite the table AND replace its schema (deliberately).","text":"(df.write\n   .mode(\"[[overwrite]]\")\n   .option(\"[[overwriteSchema]]\", \"true\")\n   .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders\"))","bank":["append","mergeSchema","replaceWhere"],"asCode":true,"explain":"overwriteSchema only makes sense with overwrite mode: it replaces data and schema/partitioning. To just add a column during append, use mergeSchema instead."},{"id":"ch06-e101","type":"spotbug","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Goal: append today's rows and accept the new `segment` column. Which line is wrong?","lines":["(source_df.write","   .mode('overwrite')","   .option('overwriteSchema', 'true')","   .saveAsTable('prod.silver.customers'))"],"bugs":[1,2],"fix":"(source_df.write\n   .mode('append')\n   .option('mergeSchema', 'true')\n   .saveAsTable('prod.silver.customers'))","explain":"overwrite + overwriteSchema replaces the whole table (data and schema) with today's rows. Appending with an extra column needs mode append + mergeSchema."},{"id":"ch06-e102","type":"spotbug","section":"ch06-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Goal: cluster the existing orders by customer_id. Which line won't do what the comment claims?","lines":["ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders CLUSTER BY (customer_id);","-- now ALL historical data is clustered by customer_id","SELECT * FROM ecommerce.silver.orders WHERE customer_id = 1234;"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders CLUSTER BY (customer_id);\nOPTIMIZE ecommerce.silver.orders FULL;  -- recluster existing data","explain":"Setting CLUSTER BY doesn't rewrite historical data. Run OPTIMIZE … FULL to apply clustering to all existing records (plain OPTIMIZE is incremental)."},{"id":"ch06-e103","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Storage debugging: fill in the command and the two fields you read first.","text":"DESCRIBE [[DETAIL]] ecommerce.silver.orders;\n-- look at: [[numFiles]] and [[sizeInBytes]]","bank":["HISTORY","TABLE","operationMetrics","version"],"asCode":true,"explain":"DESCRIBE DETAIL gives one row of table-level metadata; numFiles and sizeInBytes reveal small-file pressure (average file size)."},{"id":"ch06-e104","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Compact small files.","text":"[[OPTIMIZE]] ecommerce.silver.orders;","bank":["VACUUM","ANALYZE","REORG"],"asCode":true,"explain":"OPTIMIZE bin-packs small files into larger ones (and clusters if keys are set). VACUUM would only delete old unreferenced files."},{"id":"ch06-e105","type":"scenario","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After yesterday's load, `ecommerce.silver.customers.id` contains values like 'Maria' cast weirdly / NULLs, and `name` contains numbers. No job failed.","steps":[{"prompt":"First suspicion?","options":[{"text":"The load used a positional writer (insertInto) with a different column order","ok":true,"fb":"Silent swaps without errors are the signature of positional resolution."},{"text":"Schema enforcement is broken","ok":false,"fb":"Enforcement checks types/columns, not intended mapping by position."},{"text":"VACUUM corrupted the data","ok":false,"fb":"VACUUM doesn't change current data."}]},{"prompt":"The code: df.select('name','id').write.insertInto(target). Fix?","options":[{"text":"Use df.writeTo(target).append() (name-based) or select columns in target order","ok":true,"fb":"Removes the positional dependency."},{"text":"Add mergeSchema","ok":false,"fb":"Schema evolution doesn't fix mapping."},{"text":"Switch to overwrite mode","ok":false,"fb":"Would wipe the table and still swap columns."}]},{"prompt":"And the bad rows already written?","options":[{"text":"Find the bad version in DESCRIBE HISTORY and RESTORE (or repair) it","ok":true,"fb":"History pinpoints the load; RESTORE repairs."},{"text":"Ignore them","ok":false,"fb":"Silent corruption propagates to Gold."},{"text":"Delete the _delta_log commit","ok":false,"fb":"Never hand-edit the log."}]}],"explain":"Positional writes swap columns silently when types can be coerced. Use name-based APIs."},{"id":"ch06-e106","type":"order","section":"ch06-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Values landed in the wrong columns. Order the investigation.","items":["Check whether the writer used insertInto (positional)","Compare DataFrame column order with DESCRIBE TABLE","Fix code: name-based API or reorder columns","RESTORE or repair the version that introduced bad rows"],"explain":"Identify the positional API, prove the order mismatch, fix the code, then repair the data."},{"id":"ch06-e107","type":"order","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A table lost years of rows after a daily job. Order your response.","items":["DESCRIBE HISTORY: find the WRITE with mode Overwrite","Confirm the DataFrame contained only a subset","RESTORE the last good version (within retention)","Change the job to append / MERGE / REPLACE WHERE"],"explain":"Locate, confirm, recover, prevent."},{"id":"ch06-e108","type":"order","section":"ch06-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A writer fails with MetadataChangedException right after an ALTER TABLE. Order the response.","items":["DESCRIBE HISTORY: find the concurrent ALTER / schema-evolving write","Confirm the error is MetadataChangedException","Restart the failed writer and any stopped streams","Agree a coordination process for future schema changes"],"explain":"It's expected concurrency semantics: identify the change, restart, and coordinate next time."},{"id":"ch06-e109","type":"order","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Storage exploded. Order the workflow.","items":["DESCRIBE DETAIL: read numFiles and sizeInBytes","Compute average file size","Identify writers producing tiny files","OPTIMIZE (or enable predictive optimization)","VACUUM after retention to remove replaced files"],"explain":"Measure, diagnose small-file pressure, find the cause, compact, then clean up."},{"id":"ch06-e110","type":"order","section":"ch06-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A selective query scans too much. Order the checks.","items":["Table layout (partitioned / clustered?)","Clustering on the filter column?","Statistics collected for that column?","Filter selectivity","Is data skipping pruning files?"],"explain":"Workflow 4: layout → clustering → stats → selectivity → skipping; often the fix is liquid clustering on the filter column."},{"id":"ch06-e801","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Upstream overwrote an already-loaded `orders_0928.csv` with corrected amounts. What does the next normal COPY INTO run do with it?","options":["Skips it — it was already loaded","Reloads it because the content changed","Fails with a conflict error","Replaces the old rows automatically"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Content changes don't trigger reloads.","It succeeds silently.","COPY INTO never deletes old rows."],"explain":"COPY INTO tracks loaded file identities; a modified file with an already-loaded identity is skipped. Corrections should arrive as new files; a deliberate reload needs FILES + force and a cleanup of the old rows.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch06-e802","type":"cloze","section":"ch06-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete COPY INTO so that new source columns are merged across files AND added to the target table.","asCode":true,"text":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/path/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\n[[FORMAT_OPTIONS]] ('header' = 'true', 'mergeSchema' = 'true')\n[[COPY_OPTIONS]] ('mergeSchema' = 'true');","bank":["OPTIONS","TBLPROPERTIES","WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION"],"explain":"FORMAT_OPTIONS = how the source files are read (merge their schemas); COPY_OPTIONS = how the target is loaded (evolve its schema). With only one of them the new column may not reach the table.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch06-e901","type":"mcq","section":"ch06-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A stream writes to Delta with `trigger(processingTime=\"1 second\")` although the dashboard refreshes every 15 minutes. DESCRIBE DETAIL shows 400,000 files of ~1 MB. Best first change?","options":["Add more workers","Lengthen the trigger to match the real latency need, then OPTIMIZE","Run VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS","Switch to complete output mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tiny micro-batches mean more scheduling overhead, more commits, more storage API calls and smaller files. Matching the trigger to the real latency need (or AvailableNow on a schedule) attacks the cause; OPTIMIZE then cleans up the existing layout.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch07","num":7,"title":"Compute Landscape: Every Compute & Every Axis","subtitle":"Serverless vs classic, all-purpose vs jobs, access modes, SQL warehouses — as axes, not a flat list","emoji":"🧭","sourcePages":"179–216","mantra":"First ask WHAT you run, then WHO manages the machines, and only then turn the knobs.","objectives":["You can explain compute as a configuration vector and name the axis behind any setting.","You can draw the Workload × Infrastructure matrix and name all 10 compute types.","You can distinguish all-purpose vs jobs lifecycles and Standard vs Dedicated access mode.","You can tell Standard access mode from Standard performance mode and explain serverless performance modes.","You can compare Serverless / Pro / Classic SQL warehouses and know a notebook on a warehouse runs only SQL/Markdown.","You can choose the right compute for a workload with the one decision diagram.","You can debug 'missing field on serverless', 'unpredictable prod job' and 'unsupported language' situations."],"sections":[{"id":"ch07-s01","title":"Compute = a Configuration Vector","hook":"Serverless, Classic, All-purpose, Photon… are not 20 rival products — they are settings of ONE thing.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your code is just text. `spark.table(\"prod.sales.orders\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` computes nothing until it gets real resources."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.table(\"prod.sales.orders\")\ndf.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\").display()","caption":"Code alone does no computation"},{"t":"p","text":"Those resources are **CPU, RAM, network, possibly GPU, a Spark driver and Spark executors**. Databricks calls the whole bundle **compute**."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Serverless, Classic, All-purpose, Jobs, Standard, Dedicated, Single-node, Autoscaling, GPU, Photon… how many *compute types* is that?","text":"Almost none of them are types. Most are **different characteristics of the same compute** — like colour, engine and gearbox are characteristics of one car, not competing cars."},{"t":"p","text":"You will meet all these words: Serverless, Classic, All-purpose, Jobs, Pipeline, Standard, Dedicated, Single-node, Multi-node, Fixed-size, Autoscaling, CPU, GPU, Photon, non-Photon, SQL Serverless, SQL Pro, SQL Classic."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"One compute, many axes","text":"A single compute can be **Classic + All-purpose + Standard access + Multi-node + Autoscaling + CPU + Photon + DBR 17.3 LTS**. That whole list is ONE compute configuration."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Compute = (\n  workload purpose,      e.g. interactive\n  infrastructure model,  e.g. classic\n  access mode,           e.g. standard\n  topology,              e.g. multi-node\n  scaling,               e.g. autoscaling\n  hardware,              e.g. CPU\n  runtime,               e.g. DBR 17.3\n  execution engine       e.g. Photon\n)","caption":"Think of compute as a vector of 8 axes"},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Ordering a car","text":"\"Diesel\", \"automatic\" and \"red\" are not three cars. They are three answers to three different questions about ONE car. Same with \"classic\", \"all-purpose\" and \"Photon\"."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Flat-list thinking","text":"Reading the compute page as a flat list of 12+ competing products is the root of most confusion. Always ask: *which axis does this word belong to?*"}]},{"id":"ch07-s02","title":"Axis 1 & 2: Purpose vs Infrastructure","hook":"\"What do I want to do?\" and \"Who manages the machines?\" are two completely different questions.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Axis 1 — workload purpose** answers: *what do I want to do?* There are four basic categories."},{"t":"table","head":["Workload","What it means"],"rows":[["Interactive","I sit in a notebook and run cells"],["Job","Code runs automatically / on a schedule"],["Pipeline","A Lakeflow declarative data pipeline"],["SQL","SQL analytics, dashboards, BI"]],"caption":"Axis 1 — purpose"},{"t":"p","text":"**Axis 2 — infrastructure model** answers: *who manages the machines?* Two options: **Serverless** or **Classic**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Serverless","points":["You say: \"run the workload\"","Databricks manages machines, instance types, scaling, server-side runtime, infrastructure lifecycle","You do NOT define 1 driver, 8 workers, m5.4xlarge, DBR X, min 2 / max 10","Infrastructure is Databricks-managed"]},{"title":"Classic","points":["You (or a compute policy) define much more","VM type, driver, workers, runtime, autoscaling, access mode, Photon, spot instances…","Classic all-purpose, jobs and pipeline compute are resources YOU create and configure","They run in the customer's cloud account/environment"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Different axes","text":"Purpose (interactive/job/pipeline/SQL) is a completely different axis from serverless/classic. Every purpose has to be combined with an infrastructure model."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Taxi vs your own car","text":"Serverless = taxi: you give the destination, the company handles the vehicle. Classic = your own car: you choose the model, engine, tyres and you pay for parking while it sits idle."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a cluster with `m5.4xlarge` workers, 2–10 autoscaling and DBR 17.3 LTS — serverless or classic?","text":"**Classic.** Picking VM types, worker counts and a DBR version are exactly the decisions serverless takes away from you."}]},{"id":"ch07-s03","title":"The Workload × Infrastructure Matrix","hook":"The first map to memorise: every workload has a serverless and a classic flavour — with different product names.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**All-purpose does NOT mean non-serverless.** It roughly means: *reusable interactive classic Spark compute* — the thing you attach notebooks to while experimenting."},{"t":"diagram","text":"09:00 Nikos opens notebook\n  ↓ attaches notebook to cluster\n09:10 run cell\n09:30 another cell\n10:20 debugging\n11:00 another notebook attaches","caption":"All-purpose = a reusable resource that lives across sessions"},{"t":"p","text":"Is there a *serverless all-purpose*? Conceptually yes (interactive + serverless), but Databricks does not call it that. It is named **Serverless compute for notebooks**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"INTERACTIVE COMPUTE\n├── Serverless → Serverless notebook compute\n└── Classic    → Classic all-purpose compute\n\nAUTOMATED WORKLOAD\n├── Serverless → Serverless Jobs compute\n└── Classic    → Classic Jobs compute\n\nLAKEFLOW PIPELINE\n├── Serverless → Serverless pipeline compute\n└── Classic    → Classic pipeline compute","caption":"Same split, three times"},{"t":"table","head":["Purpose","Serverless","Classic"],"rows":[["Interactive notebook","Serverless notebook compute","All-purpose compute"],["Automated job","Serverless Jobs compute","Jobs compute"],["Lakeflow pipeline","Serverless pipeline","Classic pipeline"],["SQL / BI","Serverless SQL warehouse","Pro / Classic SQL warehouse"]],"caption":"THE matrix to memorise: Workload × Infrastructure"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks today recommends **serverless** for most new notebook, job and pipeline workloads."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Different axes — but a constrained matrix","text":"There is no UI with \"Purpose: ☑ all-purpose\" + \"Infrastructure: ☑ serverless\" that produces a \"serverless all-purpose cluster\". interactive + classic is *named* classic all-purpose compute; interactive + serverless is *named* serverless notebook compute. Think **constrained matrix**, not a free Cartesian product."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"\"Serverless is the opposite of all-purpose\"","text":"Wrong level. The opposite of serverless is **classic**. All-purpose describes the interactive/reusable *lifecycle* of a classic compute."}]},{"id":"ch07-s04","title":"The Full Compute Family Tree","hook":"Ten compute types to master — six Spark/general, four SQL — arranged as a tree, not a flat list.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For data engineering and analytics you need these **Spark/general compute** types:"},{"t":"list","items":["Serverless notebook compute","Classic all-purpose compute","Serverless Jobs compute","Classic Jobs compute","Serverless Lakeflow pipeline compute","Classic Lakeflow pipeline compute"],"ordered":true},{"t":"p","text":"The official docs split serverless into **notebooks, jobs and pipelines**, while classic covers **all-purpose, jobs and pipeline** resources."},{"t":"p","text":"**SQL compute is a separate family** — SQL Warehouses: (7) Serverless SQL Warehouse, (8) Pro SQL Warehouse, (9) Classic SQL Warehouse, and now (10) **Lakehouse Real-Time SQL Warehouse [Beta]** for very low-latency / high-concurrency read workloads."},{"t":"diagram","text":"DATABRICKS COMPUTE\n├── General Spark compute\n│   ├── Interactive\n│   │   ├── Serverless notebook\n│   │   └── Classic all-purpose\n│   ├── Automated\n│   │   ├── Serverless Jobs\n│   │   └── Classic Jobs\n│   └── Lakeflow pipelines\n│       ├── Serverless pipeline\n│       └── Classic pipeline\n└── SQL Compute\n    ├── Serverless SQL Warehouse\n    ├── Pro SQL Warehouse\n    ├── Classic SQL Warehouse\n    └── Lakehouse Real-Time [Beta]","caption":"A far better mental model than a flat list of 10"},{"t":"p","text":"Other products also run on serverless infrastructure — **model serving, data-quality monitoring, predictive optimization** — but they are managed from separate product surfaces. Don't put them in the same bucket yet."},{"t":"p","text":"There is also **AI Runtime** (Preview) for serverless GPU / deep-learning workloads."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"What to master first","text":"As a data engineer, master the 9–10 compute types above first. Model serving, AI Runtime etc. come later."}]},{"id":"ch07-s05","title":"All-purpose vs Jobs: Two Lifecycles","hook":"All-purpose lives on between your cells; Jobs compute is born with the run and dies with it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**All-purpose, concretely:** you create `my-dev-cluster` with Runtime 17.3 LTS, 2–8 workers, autoscaling ON, Standard access."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Notebook A ─┐\nNotebook B ─┼──► my-dev-cluster\nNotebook C ─┘","caption":"Many notebooks share one reusable cluster"},{"t":"p","text":"The cluster exists independently of any specific job. You run `spark.read.table(...)`, pause 10 minutes, run another command, debug… That is **all-purpose**: a reusable classic resource not created for one automated run."},{"t":"p","text":"**Jobs compute:** a job runs Bronze → Silver at 02:00. You don't need a cluster sitting open at 08:00, 12:00, 18:00."},{"t":"flow","items":["JOB START","compute starts","run pipeline","job ends","compute goes away"],"caption":"Jobs compute lifecycle"},{"t":"p","text":"In **Classic Jobs Compute** you define the cluster configuration; in **Serverless Jobs** Databricks manages it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Job on all-purpose? Technically yes","text":"Many job task types *can* run on a classic all-purpose cluster, but Databricks does NOT recommend it for production — it prefers serverless jobs or classic jobs compute."},{"t":"diagram","text":"my-dev-cluster (all-purpose)\n├── Developer A: running a query\n├── Developer B: debugging\n└── Job C: production ETL\n      → all compete for CPU, RAM,\n        executors, shuffle bandwidth","caption":"Why production jobs suffer on shared all-purpose"},{"t":"p","text":"One developer can destroy the **performance predictability** of the production job."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"The name is misleading","text":"**All-purpose ≠ \"can do / best for all workloads\".** It means *generic reusable interactive compute*."}]},{"id":"ch07-s06","title":"Access Mode: Standard vs Dedicated","hook":"Who may use this classic compute, and how are users isolated?","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Access mode** is the next axis. It applies mainly to **classic all-purpose and jobs** compute: **Standard** vs **Dedicated**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Classic cluster (Standard access)\n┌────────────────────────┐\n│  Nikos workload        │\n│  Maria workload        │\n│  John workload         │\n└────────────────────────┘\nmany users, workload isolation","caption":"Standard = shared with isolation"},{"t":"p","text":"**Standard**: many users can use the same compute with workload isolation. Databricks recommends Standard for **most classic workloads**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Dedicated**: the compute is assigned to **one user or a specific group** and is not general shared compute. Used when workloads/features need a more direct environment — e.g. some **ML / GPU / R** scenarios."},{"t":"table","head":["Lifecycle","Standard","Dedicated"],"rows":[["All-purpose","✅","✅"],["Jobs","✅","✅"],["Pipeline","own pipeline configuration model","own pipeline configuration model"]],"caption":"Classic compute matrix — a real second axis"},{"t":"p","text":"So valid combos include Classic + All-purpose + Standard, Classic + All-purpose + Dedicated, Classic + Jobs + Standard, Classic + Jobs + Dedicated."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: does serverless have the Standard/Dedicated dropdown?","text":"**No, not in the same way.** You don't create a cluster, so you never write `Access mode = Dedicated`, `Workers = 8`. Those are platform-managed choices. E.g. **serverless Jobs uses Standard access-mode semantics** and requires a workload compatible with Standard compute."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Terminology trap: \"standard compute\"","text":"\"Standard compute\" does NOT mean normal/basic compute. Today it means **classic compute configured with Standard access mode**. Likewise **\"Dedicated compute\" = classic compute configured with Dedicated access mode**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Standard/Dedicated is not universal","text":"SQL warehouses and serverless don't expose this selector. It is a **classic compute** concept."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → access mode = UC compatibility","text":"Classic compute in **Standard or Dedicated** access mode, serverless and Databricks SQL can access Unity Catalog. **Correct permissions + wrong legacy compute = still failure** — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch07-s07","title":"Topology, Scaling & Hardware","hook":"Single vs multi-node, fixed vs autoscaling, CPU vs GPU — three more independent knobs.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Single-node vs Multi-node** is a classic compute setting."},{"t":"diagram","text":"MULTI-NODE                 SINGLE-NODE\n      DRIVER              ┌───────────────────┐\n        │                 │ DRIVER + executor │\n  ┌─────┼─────┐           │ same machine      │\n  ▼     ▼     ▼           └───────────────────┘\nWORKER WORKER WORKER      no separate worker VMs\ndistributed Spark","caption":"Topology axis"},{"t":"p","text":"Single-node is for **small data, non-distributed libraries, certain ML workloads, development** — not large distributed Spark processing."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Fixed size","points":["`workers = 4`","4 workers all the time"]},{"title":"Autoscaling","points":["`min workers = 2`, `max workers = 10`","low load 2 → medium 5 → heavy 10 → later 3","Databricks adds/removes workers within YOUR bounds"]}]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Classic autoscaling","points":["YOU: min = 2, max = 10, worker type = X","Databricks: chooses a number between 2 and 10"]},{"title":"Serverless","points":["YOU: run my workload","Databricks: handles resource selection AND scaling"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Autoscaling ≠ serverless","text":"Classic compute can perfectly well autoscale. Autoscaling is a scaling axis, serverless is an infrastructure axis."},{"t":"p","text":"**CPU vs GPU:** classic compute can use CPU instances or **GPU instances** (deep learning). GPU classic compute uses the **ML runtime**, and **Photon is not supported on GPU instance types**."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"Classic + All-purpose + Dedicated + GPU + DBR ML","caption":"A perfectly sensible configuration"},{"t":"p","text":"Since 2026 **AI Runtime** (Public Preview) brings **serverless GPU** infrastructure for deep learning. So GPU and serverless are not opposites — again, different axes."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Constraint between axes","text":"GPU + Photon on classic GPU compute ❌. Not every combination of axis values is allowed."}]},{"id":"ch07-s08","title":"Runtime & Photon as Axes","hook":"DBR is software, Photon is an execution engine — neither is a compute family.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On classic compute you pick a **Databricks Runtime (DBR)** such as `DBR 17.3 LTS` or a `DBR ML` variant. That is a **software stack**, not hardware."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"AWS VM:  m5.xlarge      <- hardware\nsoftware: DBR 17.3 LTS  <- runtime","caption":"Hardware and runtime are separate choices"},{"t":"p","text":"The runtime includes **Spark plus Databricks software/libraries**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Classic","points":["\"I choose the DBR\"","e.g. DBR 17.3 LTS"]},{"title":"Serverless","points":["\"Databricks manages the server runtime\"","No traditional DBR version picker","Databricks upgrades the server-side runtime; **serverless environment versions** give application compatibility"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Photon** on classic CPU compute: **ON or OFF** — your choice. On **serverless**: Photon enabled, managed by Databricks. **SQL warehouses** also use Photon."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Photon is an axis, not a family","text":"There is no \"Photon compute\" product. Photon is a setting (engine) that sits on top of a compute."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"\"Which DBR for my serverless job?\"","text":"Usually the wrong question: on serverless you don't pin a traditional DBR version."}]},{"id":"ch07-s09","title":"The Classic Matrix & What Serverless Removes","hook":"Classic = a dozen knobs; serverless = \"here is my workload, run it\".","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Axis","Options (classic all-purpose / jobs)"],"rows":[["Purpose","All-purpose / Jobs"],["Infrastructure","Classic"],["Access","Standard / Dedicated"],["Nodes","Single / Multi"],["Scale","Fixed / Autoscaling"],["Hardware","CPU / GPU"],["Worker type","memory / compute / general purpose etc."],["Driver type","same as worker or different"],["Runtime","DBR version / DBR ML"],["Photon","On / Off, where supported"],["Instances","On-demand / Spot workers"],["Pool","Pool / direct provisioning"],["Termination","manual / auto-terminate"]],"caption":"The big classic matrix"},{"t":"p","text":"**Example 1 — interactive ETL development:** All-purpose · Classic · Standard · DBR 17.3 LTS · Photon ON · Multi-node · general-purpose worker · 2 → 8 workers · general-purpose driver. That is **one classic all-purpose compute with 9 configuration choices**, not 10 computes."},{"t":"p","text":"**Example 2 — production scheduled ETL:** Jobs · Classic · Standard · DBR 17.3 LTS · Photon ON · Multi-node · autoscaling 4 → 20 workers → a **Classic Jobs Compute** with specific parameters."},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless equivalent:** Purpose = Jobs, Infrastructure = Serverless. Now you do NOT choose driver VM, worker VM, 2–20 workers, DBR 17.3, spot or pool. **Autoscaling and Photon are enabled automatically** in serverless Jobs."},{"t":"p","text":"Order of decisions: first decide *Classic all-purpose* or *Classic Jobs*; **only then** go into Standard/Dedicated, Single/Multi, DBR, CPU/GPU, worker type, driver type, Fixed/Autoscaling, Photon, Spot, Pools, auto termination."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Classic — you answer","points":["Which VMs? How many?","Which driver? Which workers?","Which runtime?","Autoscale from where to where?","Photon? Spot? Pool?"]},{"title":"Serverless — you answer","points":["\"Here is my workload. Run it.\"","Far fewer infrastructure choices"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Not supported on serverless","text":"Serverless does NOT support **compute policies, instance pools, compute-scoped init scripts, compute-scoped libraries**, or most **Spark cluster configurations** — these infrastructure concerns are platform-managed."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Classic = 'large matrix', Serverless = 'matrix disappears'","text":"That disappearance is the whole point of serverless."}]},{"id":"ch07-s10","title":"Serverless Performance Modes","hook":"Serverless still has a knob — speed vs cost — and its name collides with an access mode.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For serverless **Jobs and Pipelines** there is a **performance mode**: **Performance Optimized** vs **Standard**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Performance Optimized","points":["faster startup","more latency-sensitive"]},{"title":"Standard (performance)","points":["slower startup","lower cost","batch-friendly"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["Serverless workload","Performance optimized","Standard"],"rows":[["Notebook","✅","❌"],["Job","✅","✅"],["Pipeline","✅","✅"]],"caption":"Serverless matrix — notebooks use a performance-oriented interactive mode"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Standard access ≠ Standard performance","text":"**Standard access mode** = security/isolation concept for classic compute (who may use it, how users are isolated). **Standard performance mode** = serverless cost/latency concept (lower cost or faster startup?). Nothing in common except the English word."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Standard ACCESS mode","points":["Classic compute","Who may use this compute?","How are users isolated?"]},{"title":"Standard PERFORMANCE mode","points":["Serverless Jobs / Pipelines","Lower cost or faster startup?","Not available for notebooks"]}]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a nightly 02:00 batch ETL with no SLA on start time — which serverless performance mode?","text":"**Standard** performance mode: slower startup doesn't matter at 02:00, and it costs less."}]},{"id":"ch07-s11","title":"SQL Warehouses: Serverless, Pro, Classic","hook":"The SQL family is its own branch — with its own feature ladder.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"SQL WAREHOUSE\n├── Serverless\n├── Pro\n└── Classic","caption":"A different branch from Spark/general compute"},{"t":"p","text":"**All** current warehouse types support **Photon**. **Serverless** additionally has **Predictive IO + Intelligent Workload Management**. **Pro** has Photon + Predictive IO. **Classic** has Photon only."},{"t":"table","head":["Feature","Serverless","Pro","Classic"],"rows":[["Photon","✅","✅","✅"],["Predictive IO","✅","✅","❌"],["Intelligent Workload Management","✅","❌","❌"],["Databricks-managed serverless infra","✅","❌","❌"],["Custom networking flexibility","limited / different","✅","✅"],["Recommended default","✅","special cases","fallback / basic"]],"caption":"SQL warehouse matrix"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Feature ladder mnemonic","text":"**Classic = P**, **Pro = P + P**, **Serverless = P + P + I**: Photon → + Predictive IO → + Intelligent Workload Management."},{"t":"p","text":"Can a **notebook attach to a SQL warehouse**? Yes — but it can only run **SQL and Markdown** cells, not Python/R."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- works in a notebook attached to a SQL warehouse\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM prod.sales.orders;","caption":"✅ SQL cell"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"# fails in a notebook attached ONLY to a SQL warehouse\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F","caption":"❌ Python cell"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Python on a SQL warehouse","text":"A notebook attached exclusively to a SQL warehouse cannot run Python cells. Attach to serverless notebook compute or all-purpose for PySpark."}]},{"id":"ch07-s12","title":"Choosing Compute: The One Decision Diagram","hook":"First question: what is my workload? Second: do I need custom infra? Only then: parameters.","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["I want to…","Best default","Other options"],"rows":[["Interactive Python/PySpark notebook","Serverless notebook","Classic all-purpose"],["Interactive SQL notebook","Serverless compute or serverless SQL warehouse","Pro/classic warehouse, all-purpose"],["Scheduled Python notebook","Serverless Jobs","Classic Jobs"],["Python script job","Serverless Jobs","Classic Jobs"],["Python wheel","Serverless Jobs","Classic Jobs"],["JAR","Classic Jobs in some cases / serverless support per task","Classic"],["spark-submit","Classic Jobs","—"],["SQL Job task","Serverless SQL Warehouse","Pro SQL Warehouse"],["dbt","Serverless SQL Warehouse","Pro"],["Lakeflow pipeline","Serverless pipeline","Classic pipeline"],["BI / dashboard","Serverless SQL Warehouse","Pro / Classic warehouse"]],"caption":"Usage matrix (mirrors Databricks' task-support matrix)"},{"t":"table","head":["Family","SL/Classic","Access cfg?","Nodes cfg?","DBR cfg?","Autoscale cfg?"],"rows":[["Serverless notebook","Serverless","❌","❌","❌ (no traditional DBR)","❌ infra managed"],["All-purpose","Classic","✅","✅","✅","✅"],["Serverless Jobs","Serverless","❌","❌","❌ (no traditional DBR)","❌ infra managed"],["Classic Jobs","Classic","✅","✅","✅","✅"],["Serverless pipeline","Serverless","managed","❌","managed","managed"],["Classic pipeline","Classic","pipeline-specific","✅","✅","✅"],["Serverless SQL WH","Serverless","SQL security model","managed","managed","managed"],["Pro SQL WH","non-serverless","warehouse model","abstracted","Databricks SQL runtime","warehouse autoscale"],["Classic SQL WH","non-serverless","warehouse model","abstracted","Databricks SQL runtime","warehouse autoscale"]],"caption":"\"All possible combinations\" in one useful matrix"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Constraints between axes","text":"If you choose serverless, many axes are managed by Databricks (no worker type, no pool…). And on classic GPU compute, GPU + Photon is not possible. The axes are real but **constrained**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"              DATABRICKS COMPUTE\n                     │\n     FIRST QUESTION: What is my workload?\n                     │\n  ┌────────────┬─────┴──────┬────────────┐\nINTERACTIVE   JOB        PIPELINE       SQL\n  │            │            │            │\nchoose infra choose infra choose infra  SQL WH\n ┌─┴─┐        ┌─┴─┐        ┌─┴─┐      ┌──┼───┐\n S   C        S   C        S   C      S Pro  C\n     │\n ALL-PURPOSE → configure: Standard/Dedicated,\n   Single/Multi, Fixed/Autoscale, CPU/GPU,\n   DBR, Photon\nS = Serverless   C = Classic","caption":"The ONE diagram to remember"},{"t":"p","text":"**Example A:** \"Explore 500 GB of customer transactions.\" Step 1: interactive (I'll try commands). Step 2: custom infra? No → **Serverless notebook compute**. Done — no worker VMs to decide."},{"t":"p","text":"**Example B:** \"Run ETL every night at 2 a.m.\" automated + no special infra → **Serverless Jobs**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Example C:** nightly ETL using a **custom Spark JAR and low-level Spark functionality** not supported on serverless → automated + classic required → **Classic Jobs Compute**, then choose DBR, workers, autoscaling, access mode, Photon…"},{"t":"p","text":"**Example D:** 100 analysts run SQL from **Power BI**. Not an all-purpose cluster! SQL/BI → SQL Warehouse → usually **Serverless SQL Warehouse** (recommended today for BI, SQL ETL and exploratory analytics)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Example E:** \"I want an **R** notebook.\" Serverless notebooks don't support **R** (the source also lists Scala and **RDD APIs** as unsupported). Interactive + unsupported serverless feature → **Classic all-purpose**, possibly **Dedicated**."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when choosing compute","questions":["Am I sitting and trying commands (interactive), scheduling something (job), building a declarative pipeline, or serving SQL/BI?","Do I need anything serverless doesn't support (R, RDD APIs, custom JAR/low-level Spark, init scripts, pools, specific Spark configs)?","If not — why wouldn't I use serverless?","If classic: have I decided all-purpose vs jobs BEFORE touching the parameters?","Is this SQL/BI? Then why am I looking at Spark clusters instead of a SQL warehouse?"]}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch07-d01","title":"My R / RDD notebook fails on serverless","section":"ch07-s12","symptom":"A notebook using R (or RDD APIs) fails or the language isn't available when attached to serverless notebook compute.","askYourself":["Is this notebook running on serverless notebook compute?","Does my code use something serverless notebooks don't support (R, RDD APIs; the source also lists Scala)?","Can I rewrite it with DataFrame/SQL APIs that serverless supports?","If not, do I need classic all-purpose — and does the workload need Dedicated access?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check which compute the notebook is attached to.","why":"Serverless notebook compute has language/API limits."},{"do":"Identify the unsupported feature (R, RDD).","why":"Decides whether a rewrite or a compute change is needed."},{"do":"Attach to a classic all-purpose cluster (Dedicated if required).","why":"Interactive + unsupported serverless feature → classic all-purpose."}],"rootCauses":["Serverless notebooks don't support R or RDD APIs.","Workload needs a direct/dedicated environment."],"fix":"Use classic all-purpose compute (possibly Dedicated access) for R/RDD development, or rewrite to DataFrame/SQL to stay on serverless.","mnemonic":"Serverless says 'R? Arrr, no.'"},{"id":"ch07-d02","title":"I can't find worker type / pool / init script fields on my serverless job","section":"ch07-s09","symptom":"You try to set worker type, min/max workers, an instance pool, a compute policy or a compute-scoped init script on serverless Jobs and the fields don't exist.","askYourself":["Am I on serverless or classic?","Is the setting I want an infrastructure concern (VMs, counts, pools, init scripts, policies, Spark cluster configs)?","Does my workload truly need that setting, or was I copying a classic habit?","If it truly needs it, should this be Classic Jobs compute?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm the job's compute is serverless.","why":"Serverless hides the infrastructure envelope by design."},{"do":"Check whether the requirement is real (e.g. custom JAR/low-level Spark).","why":"Most workloads don't need these knobs."},{"do":"If required, switch the task to Classic Jobs compute and configure there.","why":"Classic exposes VM type, workers, pools, init scripts, policies."}],"rootCauses":["Serverless doesn't support compute policies, instance pools, compute-scoped init scripts, compute-scoped libraries or most Spark cluster configs.","Applying classic thinking to serverless."],"fix":"Let serverless manage infrastructure; if a classic-only feature is required, use Classic Jobs compute.","mnemonic":"No PIPS on serverless: Policies, Init scripts, Pools, Spark confs."},{"id":"ch07-d03","title":"Production job runtime is unpredictable","section":"ch07-s05","symptom":"A scheduled production ETL takes 20 min some nights and 90 min others; it runs on a shared all-purpose cluster.","askYourself":["Which compute does this job run on — all-purpose, classic jobs or serverless jobs?","Who else is attached to that cluster at the same time?","Are developers' notebooks competing for CPU, RAM, executors, shuffle bandwidth?","Is this one of the limited exceptions (iterative job testing, very short frequent jobs) — or real production?","Can I move it to serverless Jobs or classic Jobs compute?"],"steps":[{"do":"Look at the job's compute setting.","why":"All-purpose is shared and interactive by design."},{"do":"Check other activity on that cluster during the run.","why":"Contention explains run-to-run variance."},{"do":"Switch the job to serverless Jobs (or classic Jobs compute).","why":"Jobs compute's lifecycle is tied to the run — isolated and predictable."}],"rootCauses":["Resource contention on shared all-purpose compute.","Using all-purpose for production against Databricks' recommendation."],"fix":"Run production jobs on serverless Jobs or classic Jobs compute.","mnemonic":"Prod gets its own room."},{"id":"ch07-d04","title":"Python cell fails in a notebook attached to a SQL warehouse","section":"ch07-s11","symptom":"SQL cells run fine, but `from pyspark.sql import functions as F` fails in the same notebook.","askYourself":["What is this notebook attached to — a SQL warehouse or Spark compute?","Which cell languages does a SQL warehouse run (SQL + Markdown only)?","Can this logic be expressed in SQL instead?","If I need Python, should I attach serverless notebook compute or all-purpose?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the attached compute in the notebook header.","why":"Warehouses only run SQL and Markdown."},{"do":"Rewrite in SQL or re-attach to Spark compute.","why":"PySpark needs Spark/general compute."}],"rootCauses":["Notebook attached exclusively to a SQL warehouse."],"fix":"Attach the notebook to serverless notebook compute (or classic all-purpose) for Python, or keep SQL-only cells on the warehouse."},{"id":"ch07-d05","title":"Serverless job/pipeline starts too slowly (or costs more than expected)","section":"ch07-s10","symptom":"A serverless job or pipeline has slow startup that breaks an SLA — or a batch job is more expensive than needed.","askYourself":["Is this a serverless Job/pipeline or a notebook (notebooks have no Standard performance mode)?","Which performance mode is set: Standard or Performance Optimized?","Does this workload need fast startup (latency-sensitive) or is it batch with slack?","Am I confusing performance mode with Standard ACCESS mode?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the job/pipeline settings and read the performance mode.","why":"It's the serverless cost/startup knob."},{"do":"Latency-sensitive → Performance Optimized; batch with slack → Standard.","why":"Standard = slower startup, lower cost."}],"rootCauses":["Standard performance mode on a latency-sensitive workload.","Performance Optimized on a relaxed batch workload (paying for speed you don't need)."],"fix":"Match the performance mode to the SLA: Performance Optimized for fast startup, Standard for cheaper batch.","mnemonic":"Fast or cheap — pick the mode."},{"id":"ch07-d06","title":"Photon option unavailable on my GPU cluster","section":"ch07-s07","symptom":"On a classic GPU cluster with DBR ML, Photon can't be enabled.","askYourself":["Is this classic compute on GPU instance types?","Do I actually need Photon (SQL/DataFrame engine) or the GPU (deep learning)?","Should SQL/ETL parts run on a separate CPU compute with Photon?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm the instance type is GPU.","why":"Photon isn't supported on GPU instance types."},{"do":"Split workloads: GPU compute for DL, CPU + Photon for ETL.","why":"Different axes, different needs."}],"rootCauses":["Axis constraint: GPU + Photon is not allowed on classic GPU compute."],"fix":"Accept no Photon on GPU compute, or run SQL/ETL on CPU compute with Photon."},{"id":"ch07-d07","title":"Workload fails on serverless Jobs but worked on my Dedicated cluster","section":"ch07-s06","symptom":"Code that ran on a classic Dedicated cluster fails after moving the task to serverless Jobs.","askYourself":["Does serverless Jobs use Standard access-mode semantics?","Was my code relying on something only Dedicated (single-user/direct) environments allow?","Is the failing feature in the serverless-unsupported list (R, RDD, init scripts, Spark configs)?","Should this task stay on classic Jobs compute with Dedicated access?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read the error and identify the feature used.","why":"Serverless Jobs requires Standard-compatible workloads."},{"do":"Refactor to Standard-compatible APIs, or keep classic Jobs + Dedicated.","why":"Only classic exposes Dedicated."}],"rootCauses":["Serverless Jobs = Standard access semantics; Dedicated-only features fail."],"fix":"Make the workload Standard-compatible or run it on classic Jobs compute with Dedicated access."}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Serverless is the opposite of all-purpose","text":"They live on different axes: serverless/classic is infrastructure, all-purpose/jobs is lifecycle/purpose.","fix":"Opposite of serverless = classic. Classic interactive = all-purpose."},{"title":"Looking for a 'serverless all-purpose cluster'","text":"Interactive + serverless is named serverless notebook compute; the matrix is constrained.","fix":"Use the Workload × Infrastructure matrix names."},{"title":"All-purpose = best for everything","text":"The name is misleading: it means generic reusable interactive compute.","fix":"Use Jobs compute (serverless or classic) for production jobs."},{"title":"Production jobs on shared all-purpose","text":"Developers compete with the job for CPU, RAM, executors, shuffle bandwidth — no predictability.","fix":"Serverless Jobs or classic Jobs compute."},{"title":"Autoscaling = serverless","text":"Classic compute autoscales between min and max that YOU set.","fix":"Autoscaling is a scaling axis; serverless is an infrastructure axis."},{"title":"Standard access vs Standard performance","text":"Same word, unrelated concepts: security/isolation vs serverless cost/startup.","fix":"Ask: classic security question or serverless speed/cost question?"},{"title":"'Standard compute' = basic compute","text":"It means classic compute with Standard access mode.","fix":"Read 'standard/dedicated compute' as access-mode names."},{"title":"Expecting a Standard/Dedicated dropdown on serverless","text":"Serverless hides it; serverless Jobs uses Standard semantics.","fix":"Need Dedicated → classic compute."},{"title":"Setting worker type / min-max / pools on serverless","text":"Serverless manages infrastructure and doesn't support pools, policies, compute-scoped init scripts/libraries, most Spark configs.","fix":"Let it go, or use classic if truly required."},{"title":"Photon on GPU classic compute","text":"Photon isn't supported on GPU instance types.","fix":"GPU for DL; CPU + Photon for ETL/SQL."},{"title":"Python cells on a SQL warehouse","text":"Notebooks on a SQL warehouse run only SQL and Markdown.","fix":"Attach to serverless notebook or all-purpose for Python."},{"title":"R on serverless notebooks","text":"Serverless notebooks don't support R (nor RDD APIs).","fix":"Classic all-purpose, possibly Dedicated."},{"title":"Standard performance mode for notebooks","text":"Notebooks only use the performance-oriented interactive mode.","fix":"Standard performance mode is for serverless Jobs/pipelines."},{"title":"All-purpose cluster for BI","text":"Power BI / dashboards are a SQL/BI workload.","fix":"Serverless SQL Warehouse."},{"title":"Pinning a DBR on serverless","text":"Serverless manages the server runtime; you choose environment versions instead.","fix":"Pin DBR only on classic."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"What is 'compute' in Databricks?","a":"The resources used to execute a workload: CPU, RAM, network, possibly GPU, Spark driver and executors.","section":"ch07-s01"},{"q":"Name the 8 axes of the compute configuration vector.","a":"Purpose, infrastructure, access mode, topology, scaling, hardware, runtime, execution engine.","section":"ch07-s01"},{"q":"Axis 1 values?","a":"Interactive, Job, Pipeline, SQL.","section":"ch07-s02"},{"q":"Axis 2 values?","a":"Serverless vs Classic — who manages the machines.","section":"ch07-s02"},{"q":"Where do classic computes run?","a":"In the customer's cloud account/environment.","section":"ch07-s02"},{"q":"Interactive + serverless is called…","a":"Serverless compute for notebooks (serverless notebook compute).","section":"ch07-s03"},{"q":"Interactive + classic is called…","a":"Classic all-purpose compute.","section":"ch07-s03"},{"q":"Opposite of serverless?","a":"Classic (not all-purpose).","section":"ch07-s03"},{"q":"Why 'constrained matrix'?","a":"Axes are conceptually independent, but only certain named products exist (no 'serverless all-purpose').","section":"ch07-s03"},{"q":"The 6 Spark/general compute types?","a":"Serverless notebook, classic all-purpose, serverless Jobs, classic Jobs, serverless pipeline, classic pipeline.","section":"ch07-s04"},{"q":"The 4 SQL warehouse types?","a":"Serverless, Pro, Classic, Lakehouse Real-Time [Beta].","section":"ch07-s04"},{"q":"Lakehouse Real-Time SQL Warehouse is for…","a":"Very low-latency / high-concurrency read workloads (Beta).","section":"ch07-s04"},{"q":"AI Runtime?","a":"Serverless GPU infrastructure for deep learning (Preview).","section":"ch07-s04"},{"q":"Jobs compute lifecycle?","a":"Job start → compute starts → run → job ends → compute goes away.","section":"ch07-s05"},{"q":"Can a job run on all-purpose?","a":"Technically yes for many task types, but not recommended for production.","section":"ch07-s05"},{"q":"Standard access mode?","a":"Many users share classic compute with workload isolation; recommended for most classic workloads.","section":"ch07-s06"},{"q":"Dedicated access mode?","a":"Compute assigned to one user or group; for some ML/GPU/R scenarios.","section":"ch07-s06"},{"q":"'Standard compute' means…","a":"Classic compute configured with Standard access mode.","section":"ch07-s06"},{"q":"Serverless Jobs access semantics?","a":"Standard access-mode semantics; workload must be Standard-compatible.","section":"ch07-s06"},{"q":"Single-node is for…","a":"Small data, non-distributed libraries, certain ML, development.","section":"ch07-s07"},{"q":"Autoscaling vs serverless?","a":"Different axes; classic can autoscale within your min/max.","section":"ch07-s07"},{"q":"Photon on GPU classic compute?","a":"Not supported.","section":"ch07-s07"},{"q":"Classic vs serverless runtime?","a":"Classic: you choose the DBR. Serverless: Databricks manages the runtime; you get environment versions.","section":"ch07-s08"},{"q":"Photon on serverless?","a":"Enabled and managed by Databricks.","section":"ch07-s08"},{"q":"4 things serverless doesn't support (infra)?","a":"Compute policies, instance pools, compute-scoped init scripts, compute-scoped libraries (and most Spark cluster configs).","section":"ch07-s09"},{"q":"What's automatic on serverless Jobs?","a":"Autoscaling and Photon.","section":"ch07-s09"},{"q":"Serverless performance modes?","a":"Performance Optimized (fast startup) vs Standard (slower startup, lower cost, batch).","section":"ch07-s10"},{"q":"Which serverless workload lacks Standard performance mode?","a":"Notebooks.","section":"ch07-s10"},{"q":"Standard access vs Standard performance?","a":"Security/isolation (classic) vs cost/startup trade-off (serverless). Unrelated.","section":"ch07-s10"},{"q":"SQL warehouse features ladder?","a":"Classic: Photon. Pro: + Predictive IO. Serverless: + Intelligent Workload Management.","section":"ch07-s11"},{"q":"Notebook on a SQL warehouse can run…","a":"SQL and Markdown only.","section":"ch07-s11"},{"q":"Best default for Power BI?","a":"Serverless SQL Warehouse.","section":"ch07-s12"},{"q":"Best default for spark-submit?","a":"Classic Jobs.","section":"ch07-s12"},{"q":"Best default for dbt?","a":"Serverless SQL Warehouse (alt: Pro).","section":"ch07-s12"},{"q":"First question in the decision diagram?","a":"What is my workload?","section":"ch07-s12"},{"q":"Which compute can access Unity Catalog?","a":"Classic Standard/Dedicated access mode, serverless, Databricks SQL.","section":"ch07-s06","src":"part2"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch07-e001","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which list best describes what **compute** means in Databricks?","options":["The notebook and its code","CPU, RAM, network, possibly GPU, a Spark driver and Spark executors used to run the workload","The Delta tables your query reads","The Unity Catalog metastore"],"answer":1,"why":["Code needs compute to run; it isn't compute.","Correct — the bundle of execution resources.","Tables are data, stored outside compute.","The metastore holds metadata, not execution resources."],"explain":"Compute is the set of real resources Databricks uses to execute a workload. Code alone is just text and does no computation; tables and metastores are data/metadata, not the execution resources."},{"id":"ch07-e002","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Serverless, Classic, All-purpose, Jobs, Standard, Dedicated, Photon and GPU are twelve-ish competing compute types you pick one of.","answer":false,"explain":"Most of these words are **characteristics (axes) of the same compute**. One compute can be Classic + All-purpose + Standard + Multi-node + Autoscaling + CPU + Photon + DBR 17.3 LTS at the same time."},{"id":"ch07-e003","type":"match","section":"ch07-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each value to the axis it belongs to.","pairs":[["Interactive","Workload purpose"],["Classic","Infrastructure model"],["Standard","Access mode"],["Multi-node","Topology"],["DBR 17.3","Runtime"],["Photon","Execution engine"]],"explain":"This is the configuration vector: purpose, infrastructure, access, topology, scaling, hardware, runtime, engine. Each word answers a different question about the SAME compute."},{"id":"ch07-e004","type":"cloze","section":"ch07-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the configuration vector.","text":"Compute = (workload [[purpose]], [[infrastructure]] model, [[access]] mode, topology, [[scaling]], hardware, [[runtime]], execution [[engine]])","bank":["cluster","catalog","storage"],"explain":"The eight axes: purpose, infrastructure, access mode, topology, scaling, hardware, runtime, execution engine. Learning which axis each UI option belongs to removes most confusion."},{"id":"ch07-e005","type":"free","section":"ch07-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"In your own words: why is it wrong to think of the Databricks compute page as a flat list of compute types?","model":"Because most terms are values on different axes of one compute configuration. Serverless/classic is about who manages infrastructure, all-purpose/jobs about lifecycle/purpose, Standard/Dedicated about access, Photon about the execution engine, DBR about software. One compute combines one value from each axis, so 'Classic + All-purpose + Standard + Photon' is one compute, not four.","rubric":["Most terms are axes/characteristics, not types","Names at least 3 axes correctly","One compute = one value per axis (configuration vector)"],"explain":"A flat list hides that the words answer different questions. Thinking in axes lets you read any configuration as a vector of choices."},{"id":"ch07-e006","type":"odd","section":"ch07-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a characteristic/axis value of a single compute configuration?","options":["Autoscaling","Photon","prod.sales.orders","Standard access"],"answer":2,"explain":"`prod.sales.orders` is a table (data), not a compute setting. Autoscaling (scaling), Photon (engine) and Standard (access mode) are all axis values of one compute."},{"id":"ch07-e007","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Sort: which axis does each phrase answer?","buckets":["Axis 1: What do I want to do?","Axis 2: Who manages the machines?"],"items":[{"text":"Interactive development","bucket":0},{"text":"Automated job","bucket":0},{"text":"Lakeflow pipeline","bucket":0},{"text":"SQL / BI","bucket":0},{"text":"Serverless","bucket":1},{"text":"Classic","bucket":1}],"explain":"Purpose (interactive / job / pipeline / SQL) is one axis; infrastructure model (serverless / classic) is a completely different axis."},{"id":"ch07-e008","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"On **serverless** compute, which of these do you still decide yourself?","options":["Worker VM type m5.4xlarge","min workers 2 / max workers 10","The workload to run","The DBR version 17.3"],"answer":2,"why":["Instance types are managed by Databricks.","Scaling is managed by Databricks.","Correct — the workload is yours.","Server-side runtime is managed by Databricks."],"explain":"With serverless you say 'run the workload' and Databricks manages machines, instance types, scaling, server-side runtime and lifecycle. VM types, worker counts and DBR pinning are classic decisions."},{"id":"ch07-e009","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Classic all-purpose, jobs and pipeline compute run in the customer's cloud account/environment and are resources you create and configure.","answer":true,"explain":"That is the classic model: you (or a compute policy) define VM type, driver, workers, runtime, autoscaling, access mode, Photon, spot instances, etc., and the VMs live in your cloud account."},{"id":"ch07-e010","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Who decides it? Sort each item.","buckets":["You decide (classic)","Databricks manages (serverless)"],"items":[{"text":"VM type for workers","bucket":0},{"text":"Spot instances","bucket":0},{"text":"Access mode","bucket":0},{"text":"Infrastructure lifecycle (serverless)","bucket":1},{"text":"Instance types (serverless)","bucket":1},{"text":"Scaling (serverless)","bucket":1},{"text":"Number of workers (classic)","bucket":0}],"explain":"In classic you configure the infrastructure envelope; in serverless the infrastructure (machines, instance types, scaling, runtime server side, lifecycle) is Databricks-managed."},{"id":"ch07-e011","type":"free","section":"ch07-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain serverless vs classic with an everyday analogy.","model":"Serverless is like a taxi: you give the destination (your workload) and the company handles the vehicle, maintenance and scaling. Classic is like owning a car: you choose the model (VM type), how many (workers), the engine (runtime/Photon), and you pay while it sits idle.","rubric":["Serverless: you specify the workload only","Classic: you choose infrastructure details","Mentions who manages lifecycle/cost of idle"],"explain":"Any analogy works if it separates 'what I want done' from 'who owns and configures the machines'."},{"id":"ch07-e012","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What is the **classic** equivalent of **serverless notebook compute**?","options":["Classic Jobs compute","Classic all-purpose compute","Pro SQL warehouse","Classic pipeline compute"],"answer":1,"explain":"Interactive + serverless = serverless notebook compute; interactive + classic = classic all-purpose compute. Jobs compute is for automated runs, warehouses for SQL."},{"id":"ch07-e013","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"What is the classic equivalent of **serverless Jobs compute**?","options":["Classic all-purpose compute","Classic Jobs compute","Serverless pipeline","Classic SQL warehouse"],"answer":1,"explain":"Automated workload: serverless → Serverless Jobs compute, classic → Classic Jobs compute. All-purpose is the interactive classic flavour."},{"id":"ch07-e014","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Serverless is the opposite of all-purpose.","answer":false,"explain":"Different levels. The infrastructure opposite of serverless is **classic**. All-purpose describes an interactive/reusable classic workload lifecycle."},{"id":"ch07-e015","type":"match","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the Workload × Infrastructure matrix: match each (purpose + infra) to its product name.","pairs":[["Interactive + Serverless","Serverless notebook compute"],["Interactive + Classic","All-purpose compute"],["Automated + Serverless","Serverless Jobs compute"],["Automated + Classic","Jobs compute"],["Lakeflow pipeline + Classic","Classic pipeline"],["SQL/BI + Serverless","Serverless SQL warehouse"]],"explain":"This matrix is the first map to memorise. Notice that the interactive/serverless product is NOT called 'serverless all-purpose'."},{"id":"ch07-e016","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"In the UI you can tick 'Purpose: all-purpose' and 'Infrastructure: serverless' to create a serverless all-purpose cluster.","answer":false,"explain":"There is no such pair of dropdowns. Interactive + serverless is the product **serverless notebook compute**. Purpose and infrastructure are different axes conceptually, but the matrix is constrained, not a free Cartesian product."},{"id":"ch07-e017","type":"cloze","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the product names.","text":"INTERACTIVE → Serverless: [[Serverless notebook compute|serverless compute for notebooks|serverless notebook]] / Classic: [[Classic all-purpose compute|all-purpose compute|all-purpose]]","explain":"Databricks names the serverless interactive option 'Serverless compute for notebooks'; the classic one is all-purpose compute."},{"id":"ch07-e018","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What does **all-purpose** actually mean?","options":["Non-serverless","Best for every workload","Reusable interactive classic Spark compute you attach notebooks to","A cluster that can run SQL warehouses"],"answer":2,"why":["Classic is the opposite of serverless; all-purpose is a lifecycle.","The name misleads — it's not the best choice for all.","Correct.","Warehouses are a separate family."],"explain":"All-purpose is a lifecycle concept: a reusable classic resource that lives across interactive sessions. It is not a synonym of 'non-serverless' and not 'best for everything'."},{"id":"ch07-e019","type":"calc","section":"ch07-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"How many **Spark/general** compute types (serverless + classic flavours of interactive, automated and pipeline) are in the list?","answer":6,"tolerance":0,"unit":"types","explain":"Interactive, automated and pipeline × serverless/classic = 3 × 2 = 6: serverless notebook, classic all-purpose, serverless jobs, classic jobs, serverless pipeline, classic pipeline."},{"id":"ch07-e020","type":"calc","section":"ch07-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"How many SQL warehouse types are listed, counting the Beta one?","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"warehouse types","explain":"Serverless, Pro, Classic, plus Lakehouse Real-Time SQL Warehouse [Beta] for very low-latency/high-concurrency reads = 4. Together with the 6 Spark types that's the 10 compute types to master."},{"id":"ch07-e021","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Put each compute type into its family.","buckets":["General Spark compute","SQL compute"],"items":[{"text":"Serverless notebook","bucket":0},{"text":"Classic Jobs","bucket":0},{"text":"Serverless pipeline","bucket":0},{"text":"Pro SQL Warehouse","bucket":1},{"text":"Lakehouse Real-Time [Beta]","bucket":1},{"text":"Classic all-purpose","bucket":0},{"text":"Classic SQL Warehouse","bucket":1}],"explain":"SQL warehouses are a separate family from the six Spark/general compute types."},{"id":"ch07-e022","type":"odd","section":"ch07-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one should NOT be put in the same bucket as the core data-engineering compute types (yet)?","options":["Serverless Jobs","Model serving","Classic pipeline","Serverless SQL Warehouse"],"answer":1,"explain":"Model serving (like data-quality monitoring and predictive optimization) uses serverless infrastructure but is managed from a separate product surface. The other three are core compute types."},{"id":"ch07-e023","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What is the **Lakehouse Real-Time SQL Warehouse [Beta]** for?","options":["GPU deep learning","Very low-latency / high-concurrency read workloads","Running R notebooks","Streaming ingestion with Auto Loader"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is a new SQL warehouse type for particularly low-latency, high-concurrency reads. Serverless GPU deep learning is AI Runtime."},{"id":"ch07-e024","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"AI Runtime (Preview) provides serverless GPU infrastructure for deep-learning workloads.","answer":true,"explain":"AI Runtime brings serverless GPU — which proves GPU and serverless are not opposite concepts."},{"id":"ch07-e025","type":"order","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the **Jobs compute** lifecycle.","items":["Job start","Compute starts","Run pipeline","Job ends","Compute goes away"],"explain":"Jobs compute is born with the run and terminates with it — no cluster sitting open at 08:00, 12:00, 18:00 for a 02:00 job."},{"id":"ch07-e026","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"A job task can never run on a classic all-purpose cluster.","answer":false,"explain":"Technically many job task types can use all-purpose compute. Databricks just doesn't recommend it for production — it prefers serverless jobs or classic jobs compute."},{"id":"ch07-e027","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Why does Databricks discourage running production ETL on a shared all-purpose cluster?","options":["All-purpose can't read Delta tables","Developers' queries compete with the job for CPU, RAM, executors and shuffle bandwidth, killing predictability","All-purpose clusters can't autoscale","Jobs can't be scheduled on all-purpose"],"answer":1,"why":["False — it reads Delta fine.","Correct.","False — classic all-purpose can autoscale.","Technically jobs can use it."],"explain":"Shared resources mean a developer's heavy query can destroy the performance predictability of the production job. All-purpose can read Delta and can autoscale — those distractors are false."},{"id":"ch07-e028","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"'All-purpose' means it is the best choice for all workloads.","answer":false,"explain":"The name is misleading. It means generic reusable interactive compute, not 'optimal for everything'."},{"id":"ch07-e029","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"All-purpose or Jobs compute?","buckets":["Classic all-purpose","Classic Jobs compute"],"items":[{"text":"Exists independently of any specific job","bucket":0},{"text":"Starts when the job starts","bucket":1},{"text":"Several notebooks attach to it","bucket":0},{"text":"Terminates when the run completes","bucket":1},{"text":"Pause 10 min, then run another cell","bucket":0},{"text":"02:00 Bronze → Silver scheduled run","bucket":1}],"explain":"All-purpose is a reusable interactive resource; Jobs compute's lifecycle is tied to the run."},{"id":"ch07-e030","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your nightly production ETL runs on `my-dev-cluster` (shared all-purpose). Run times vary wildly: 20 minutes some nights, 90 minutes others.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you suspect first?","options":[{"text":"Resource contention: other users/notebooks share the cluster","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — on all-purpose, developers and the job compete for CPU, RAM, executors and shuffle bandwidth."},{"text":"The Delta table is corrupt","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing points to corruption; variable runtime on a shared cluster points to contention."},{"text":"Photon is broken","ok":false,"fb":"Photon doesn't cause night-to-night variance tied to other users."}]},{"prompt":"What's the recommended fix?","options":[{"text":"Move the job to serverless Jobs (or classic Jobs compute)","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — jobs compute gives the run its own lifecycle and isolated resources."},{"text":"Ask developers to stop working at night forever","ok":false,"fb":"Fragile and doesn't fix billing/idle issues; use the intended compute."},{"text":"Make the all-purpose cluster Dedicated to the job","ok":false,"fb":"That still keeps an always-on interactive cluster; Jobs compute is the operational model."}]}],"explain":"All-purpose = interactive by design. Production jobs belong on serverless Jobs or classic Jobs compute for predictable performance."},{"id":"ch07-e031","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which access mode does Databricks recommend for **most classic workloads**?","options":["Dedicated","Standard","No isolation shared","Single user legacy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Standard: many users share the compute with workload isolation. Dedicated is for one user/group when features need a more direct environment (some ML/GPU/R)."},{"id":"ch07-e032","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"When someone says \"standard compute\" in today's Databricks terminology, they mean a basic/normal cluster.","answer":false,"explain":"\"Standard compute\" = classic compute configured with **Standard access mode**. \"Dedicated compute\" = classic compute configured with Dedicated access mode."},{"id":"ch07-e033","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A classic all-purpose compute can be either Standard or Dedicated.","answer":true,"explain":"Yes — Standard/Dedicated are explicit access-mode options for both classic all-purpose and classic jobs compute."},{"id":"ch07-e034","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Does **serverless Jobs** have a Standard/Dedicated dropdown?","options":["Yes, Dedicated by default","Yes, Standard by default and you can switch","No — the platform manages it; serverless Jobs uses Standard access-mode semantics and needs Standard-compatible workloads","No — serverless has no security at all"],"answer":2,"why":["There's no selector.","There's no selector to switch.","Correct.","Serverless has access management, just not this selector."],"explain":"In serverless you don't create a cluster or set access mode/workers. Serverless Jobs uses Standard access semantics, so workloads must be compatible with Standard compute."},{"id":"ch07-e035","type":"match","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the classic-compute matrix cell to its value.","pairs":[["All-purpose × Standard","✅ supported"],["Jobs × Dedicated","✅ also supported"],["Pipeline × Standard/Dedicated","own pipeline configuration model"],["Serverless Jobs × access dropdown","not exposed — Standard semantics"]],"explain":"Standard/Dedicated is explicit for all-purpose and jobs compute; pipelines have their own configuration model; serverless hides the selector."},{"id":"ch07-e036","type":"odd","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which scenario is the odd one out (doesn't point to Dedicated access)?","options":["R development needing a direct environment","Some GPU ML workloads","A compute assigned to a single data scientist group","20 analysts sharing a cluster with workload isolation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Many users sharing with isolation is **Standard**. Dedicated is assigned to one user/group and used for some ML/GPU/R scenarios."},{"id":"ch07-e037","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Autoscaling means serverless.","answer":false,"explain":"Classic compute can autoscale perfectly well (you set min/max; Databricks picks a number between). Autoscaling ≠ serverless."},{"id":"ch07-e038","type":"calc","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"A **fixed-size** classic cluster has `workers = 4`. During a heavy spike, how many workers does it have?","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"workers","explain":"Fixed means fixed: 4 workers all the time. Only autoscaling clusters move between min and max."},{"id":"ch07-e039","type":"calc","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"An autoscaling classic cluster has min 2, max 10 workers. What is the most workers it can reach under the heaviest load?","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"workers","explain":"Databricks adds/removes workers but only inside the bounds YOU set: 2–10. It will never go to 11 on its own."},{"id":"ch07-e040","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Single-node or multi-node?","buckets":["Single-node","Multi-node"],"items":[{"text":"Small data","bucket":0},{"text":"Non-distributed libraries","bucket":0},{"text":"Large distributed Spark processing","bucket":1},{"text":"Driver + separate worker VMs","bucket":1},{"text":"Driver acts as executor on the same machine","bucket":0},{"text":"Development / certain ML workloads","bucket":0}],"explain":"Single-node has no separate worker VMs — fine for small data, dev, non-distributed libraries and some ML, not for large distributed Spark."},{"id":"ch07-e041","type":"spotbug","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"This classic compute spec has an invalid combination. Click the bad line.","lines":["Infrastructure: Classic","Purpose: All-purpose","Access mode: Dedicated","Hardware: GPU instances","Runtime: DBR ML","Photon: ON"],"bugs":[5],"fix":"Photon: OFF (Photon is not supported on GPU instance types)","explain":"Classic GPU compute uses the ML runtime and Photon is not supported on GPU instance types. Classic + All-purpose + Dedicated + GPU + DBR ML is otherwise a perfectly sensible config."},{"id":"ch07-e042","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"GPU and serverless are opposite concepts: if you need GPU you must use classic.","answer":false,"explain":"AI Runtime (Public Preview, 2026) brings serverless GPU infrastructure for deep learning. GPU (hardware) and serverless (infrastructure model) are different axes."},{"id":"ch07-e043","type":"cloze","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the comparison.","text":"Classic autoscaling: YOU set [[min]] = 2 and [[max]] = 10 and the worker type; Databricks chooses a number between them. Serverless: YOU say \"run my [[workload]]\" and Databricks handles resource selection and [[scaling]].","bank":["driver","pool","Photon"],"explain":"The difference is who owns the bounds and resource choice. Classic autoscaling is still you configuring infrastructure."},{"id":"ch07-e044","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"On classic compute, choosing `DBR 17.3 LTS` selects…","options":["The VM hardware","The software stack (Spark + Databricks software/libraries)","The access mode","The number of workers"],"answer":1,"explain":"The runtime is software, not hardware. `m5.xlarge` would be the hardware choice."},{"id":"ch07-e045","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"On serverless compute you pick a traditional DBR version like `DBR 17.3` from a dropdown.","answer":false,"explain":"Databricks upgrades the server-side runtime itself and uses **serverless environment versions** for application compatibility. 'Classic: I choose the DBR; serverless: Databricks manages the server runtime.'"},{"id":"ch07-e046","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Who decides Photon?","buckets":["You choose ON/OFF","Always on / managed"],"items":[{"text":"Classic all-purpose (CPU)","bucket":0},{"text":"Classic Jobs (CPU)","bucket":0},{"text":"Serverless notebook","bucket":1},{"text":"Serverless Jobs","bucket":1},{"text":"SQL warehouses","bucket":1}],"explain":"On classic CPU compute Photon is a toggle; on serverless it is enabled and managed; SQL warehouses also use Photon."},{"id":"ch07-e047","type":"odd","section":"ch07-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is a compute *family* rather than an axis value?","options":["Photon","DBR 17.3 LTS","SQL Warehouse","Autoscaling"],"answer":2,"explain":"SQL Warehouse is a compute family. Photon (engine), DBR (runtime) and autoscaling (scaling) are axes/settings on a compute."},{"id":"ch07-e048","type":"free","section":"ch07-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain why 'AWS VM m5.xlarge' and 'DBR 17.3 LTS' are not alternatives to each other.","model":"They live on different axes: m5.xlarge is hardware (the machine), DBR 17.3 LTS is the software stack installed on it (Spark + Databricks software/libraries). A classic compute needs both: a VM type and a runtime.","rubric":["Hardware vs software","Both are chosen together on classic","Runtime includes Spark + Databricks libraries"],"explain":"Every classic config answers 'which machine?' and 'which software on it?' separately."},{"id":"ch07-e049","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Which of these is NOT supported on serverless compute?","options":["Running a scheduled Python notebook","Instance pools and compute-scoped init scripts","Photon","Autoscaling"],"answer":1,"why":["Supported — serverless Jobs.","Correct.","Photon is enabled automatically.","Autoscaling is automatic/managed."],"explain":"Serverless doesn't support compute policies, instance pools, compute-scoped init scripts, compute-scoped libraries or most Spark cluster configs — they're platform-managed. Photon and autoscaling are automatic on serverless Jobs."},{"id":"ch07-e050","type":"spotbug","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A colleague wrote this **serverless Jobs** spec. Which lines are impossible?","lines":["Purpose: Jobs","Infrastructure: Serverless","Worker type: m5.4xlarge","Workers: 2-20 (autoscaling)","Performance mode: Standard"],"bugs":[2,3],"fix":"Purpose: Jobs\nInfrastructure: Serverless\nPerformance mode: Standard","explain":"Worker type and min/max workers are infrastructure configuration that serverless manages for you. Performance mode (Standard vs Performance Optimized) IS a valid serverless Jobs setting."},{"id":"ch07-e051","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Classic + Jobs + Standard + Multi-node + Autoscaling + Photon is a valid configuration.","answer":true,"explain":"Completely sensible: a classic jobs compute with Standard access, several workers, autoscaling and Photon."},{"id":"ch07-e052","type":"calc","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Example 1 (interactive ETL development) lists: Purpose, Infrastructure, Access mode, Runtime, Photon, Topology, Worker, Scaling, Driver. How many **computes** does it describe?","answer":1,"tolerance":0,"unit":"compute","explain":"One classic all-purpose compute with 9 configuration choices — not 9 or 10 computes."},{"id":"ch07-e053","type":"order","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the decision process for a classic compute.","items":["Decide workload purpose (interactive / job / pipeline / SQL)","Decide infrastructure (serverless vs classic)","Land on the product (e.g. Classic Jobs)","Then set parameters: access mode, nodes, DBR, worker/driver type, scaling, Photon, spot, pools, termination"],"explain":"Parameters come LAST. Only after you've chosen 'classic all-purpose' or 'classic Jobs' do Standard/Dedicated, DBR, workers etc. become relevant."},{"id":"ch07-e054","type":"match","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the classic matrix axis to its options.","pairs":[["Instances","On-demand / Spot workers"],["Pool","Pool / direct provisioning"],["Termination","manual / auto-terminate"],["Driver type","same as worker or different"],["Worker type","memory / compute / general purpose"],["Scale","Fixed / Autoscaling"]],"explain":"These are classic all-purpose/jobs configuration settings — the ones serverless removes."},{"id":"ch07-e055","type":"write","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the axis-by-axis spec (one `Axis: value` per line) for a **production scheduled ETL on classic jobs compute**: Standard access, DBR 17.3 LTS, Photon on, multi-node, autoscaling 4 → 20 workers.","solution":"Purpose: Jobs\nInfrastructure: Classic\nAccess: Standard\nRuntime: DBR 17.3 LTS\nPhoton: ON\nTopology: Multi-node\nAutoscaling: 4 -> 20 workers","keywords":["Jobs","Classic","Standard","17.3 LTS","Photon","Multi-node","4 -> 20"],"lang":"text","explain":"This is exactly the source's example 2 — one Classic Jobs Compute with specific parameters. LTS suits production; autoscaling bounds are yours on classic."},{"id":"ch07-e056","type":"write","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Now write the **serverless equivalent** of that ETL spec (only the lines you still choose; one `Axis: value` per line).","solution":"Purpose: Jobs\nInfrastructure: Serverless","keywords":["Purpose: Jobs","Infrastructure: Serverless"],"lang":"text","explain":"Driver VM, worker VM, 2–20 workers, DBR 17.3, spot and pool all disappear. Autoscaling and Photon are enabled automatically on serverless Jobs."},{"id":"ch07-e057","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which serverless workload does NOT offer **Standard performance mode**?","options":["Jobs","Lakeflow pipelines","Notebooks","All of them offer it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Standard performance mode exists for serverless Jobs and pipelines; notebooks use a performance-oriented interactive mode."},{"id":"ch07-e058","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Standard access mode and Standard performance mode are the same setting seen from two screens.","answer":false,"explain":"No relation besides the English word. Standard **access** = security/isolation on classic compute. Standard **performance** = serverless cost vs startup trade-off."},{"id":"ch07-e059","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which 'Standard' is this about?","buckets":["Standard ACCESS mode","Standard PERFORMANCE mode"],"items":[{"text":"Who may use this classic compute?","bucket":0},{"text":"How are users isolated?","bucket":0},{"text":"Lower cost vs faster startup","bucket":1},{"text":"Serverless Jobs / pipelines","bucket":1},{"text":"Classic all-purpose/jobs","bucket":0},{"text":"Batch-friendly, slower startup","bucket":1}],"explain":"Same word, two unrelated concepts — a classic source of confusion and a likely exam trap."},{"id":"ch07-e060","type":"match","section":"ch07-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each serverless performance mode to its trait.","pairs":[["Performance Optimized","faster startup, latency-sensitive"],["Standard","slower startup, lower cost, batch-friendly"],["Notebook mode","performance-oriented interactive only"]],"explain":"For nightly batch with no start-time SLA, Standard performance mode saves money."},{"id":"ch07-e061","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your serverless nightly job is cheap but finance's dashboard SLA was just moved earlier, and the job now starts too slowly to finish in time.","steps":[{"prompt":"What setting do you look at first?","options":[{"text":"The serverless performance mode (Standard vs Performance Optimized)","ok":true,"fb":"Right — Standard mode trades slower startup for lower cost."},{"text":"The access mode (Standard vs Dedicated)","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode is security/isolation, and serverless doesn't expose it."},{"text":"The worker type","ok":false,"fb":"Serverless doesn't let you pick worker types."}]},{"prompt":"What change fits the new SLA?","options":[{"text":"Switch the job to Performance Optimized","ok":true,"fb":"Faster startup, more latency-sensitive — at higher cost."},{"text":"Move the job to a notebook on Standard performance","ok":false,"fb":"Notebooks don't even have Standard performance mode."},{"text":"Add an instance pool","ok":false,"fb":"Pools aren't supported on serverless."}]}],"explain":"Performance mode is THE serverless knob for start-up latency vs cost. Don't confuse it with Standard access mode."},{"id":"ch07-e062","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which SQL warehouse type has Photon + Predictive IO but NOT Intelligent Workload Management?","options":["Serverless","Pro","Classic","Lakehouse Real-Time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Classic = Photon; Pro = Photon + Predictive IO; Serverless = Photon + Predictive IO + Intelligent Workload Management."},{"id":"ch07-e063","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A notebook attached only to a SQL warehouse can run `from pyspark.sql import functions as F`.","answer":false,"explain":"A notebook on a SQL warehouse can run SQL and Markdown cells only — not Python/R. Use serverless notebook compute or all-purpose for PySpark."},{"id":"ch07-e064","type":"match","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the SQL warehouse matrix row to its correct 'Classic' column value.","pairs":[["Photon","✅"],["Predictive IO","❌"],["Recommended default","fallback/basic"],["Custom networking flexibility","✅ (flexible)"]],"explain":"Classic warehouses have Photon but neither Predictive IO nor IWM, keep custom networking flexibility, and are the fallback/basic option. Serverless is the recommended default."},{"id":"ch07-e065","type":"spotbug","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Notebook attached to `analytics-wh` (a SQL warehouse). Which cell will fail?","lines":["%md ## Orders report","SELECT COUNT(*) FROM prod.sales.orders;","%python","from pyspark.sql import functions as F","SELECT country, SUM(amount) FROM prod.sales.orders GROUP BY country;"],"bugs":[2,3],"fix":"Remove the Python cell, or attach the notebook to serverless notebook compute / all-purpose compute to run PySpark.","explain":"SQL warehouses only execute SQL and Markdown in notebooks. The Python cell fails; the Markdown and SQL cells work."},{"id":"ch07-e066","type":"write","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a query you CAN run in a notebook attached to a SQL warehouse: total `amount` per `country` from `prod.sales.orders`.","solution":"SELECT country, SUM(amount) AS total_amount\nFROM prod.sales.orders\nGROUP BY country;","keywords":["select","sum(amount)","from prod.sales.orders","group by country"],"lang":"sql","explain":"SQL cells run on a SQL warehouse; the same logic in PySpark (`groupBy(...).sum(...)`) would need Spark compute."},{"id":"ch07-e067","type":"order","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the warehouse types from FEWEST to MOST features (Photon / Predictive IO / IWM).","items":["Classic (Photon)","Pro (Photon + Predictive IO)","Serverless (Photon + Predictive IO + IWM)"],"explain":"Each step up adds one: Predictive IO at Pro, Intelligent Workload Management at Serverless."},{"id":"ch07-e068","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"All current SQL warehouse types support Photon.","answer":true,"explain":"Serverless, Pro and Classic all have Photon; they differ in Predictive IO and Intelligent Workload Management."},{"id":"ch07-e069","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"100 analysts run SQL from **Power BI**. What do you start with?","options":["A big classic all-purpose cluster","Serverless SQL Warehouse","Serverless Jobs","Classic pipeline"],"answer":1,"explain":"SQL/BI workload → SQL warehouse → usually Serverless SQL Warehouse, recommended today for BI, SQL ETL and exploratory analytics."},{"id":"ch07-e070","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"You want to develop in **R** interactively. Which compute?","options":["Serverless notebook compute","Classic all-purpose (possibly Dedicated)","Serverless SQL warehouse","Serverless Jobs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serverless notebooks don't support R. Interactive + unsupported serverless feature → classic all-purpose, possibly Dedicated depending on the workload."},{"id":"ch07-e071","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Interactive PySpark exploration of 500 GB, no special requirements?","options":["Serverless notebook compute","Classic Jobs","Pro SQL warehouse","Classic pipeline"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: interactive. Step 2: no custom infra → serverless notebook compute. No worker VMs to decide."},{"id":"ch07-e072","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Nightly ETL uses a **custom Spark JAR and low-level Spark functionality** that serverless doesn't support. Which compute?","options":["Serverless Jobs","Classic Jobs compute","Serverless notebook","Serverless SQL warehouse"],"answer":1,"explain":"Automated + classic required → Classic Jobs Compute, then choose DBR, workers, autoscaling, access mode, Photon. Without that constraint, serverless Jobs would be the default."},{"id":"ch07-e073","type":"match","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the task to its best default compute (usage matrix).","pairs":[["Scheduled Python notebook","Serverless Jobs"],["spark-submit","Classic Jobs"],["dbt","Serverless SQL Warehouse"],["Lakeflow pipeline","Serverless pipeline"],["Interactive PySpark notebook","Serverless notebook"]],"explain":"The usage matrix mirrors Databricks' task-support matrix: serverless defaults everywhere except where a classic-only feature (spark-submit) is needed; SQL-shaped tasks go to warehouses."},{"id":"ch07-e074","type":"order","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the questions in the decision diagram.","items":["What is my workload? (interactive / job / pipeline / SQL)","Do I need custom infra or an unsupported serverless feature?","Pick the product (e.g. serverless notebook vs classic all-purpose)","If classic: configure Standard/Dedicated, Single/Multi, Fixed/Autoscale, CPU/GPU, DBR, Photon"],"explain":"Workload first, infrastructure second, parameters last. Once this is clear, Compute → Create compute stops looking like dozens of unrelated checkboxes."},{"id":"ch07-e075","type":"bucket","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless or classic required?","buckets":["Serverless is fine","Classic required"],"items":[{"text":"Nightly 2 a.m. ETL in Python, nothing special","bucket":0},{"text":"Interactive R notebook","bucket":1},{"text":"RDD API code","bucket":1},{"text":"Custom JAR with low-level Spark features","bucket":1},{"text":"Exploring a 500 GB table in PySpark","bucket":0},{"text":"spark-submit task","bucket":1}],"explain":"Default to serverless; fall back to classic only for unsupported features (R, RDD APIs, spark-submit, custom low-level JARs…)."},{"id":"ch07-e076","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A teammate says: \"I need interactive compute, so I'll create a serverless all-purpose cluster with Dedicated access, 2–8 workers and DBR 17.3.\"","steps":[{"prompt":"What's wrong in the first part?","options":[{"text":"There is no 'serverless all-purpose cluster' — interactive + serverless is serverless notebook compute","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — interactive + classic is all-purpose; interactive + serverless is a differently named product."},{"text":"Nothing — that product exists","ok":false,"fb":"It doesn't exist under that name; the matrix is constrained."},{"text":"All-purpose can't be interactive","ok":false,"fb":"All-purpose is interactive by design."}]},{"prompt":"If they really need Dedicated, 2–8 workers and DBR 17.3, what do they need?","options":[{"text":"Classic all-purpose compute","ok":true,"fb":"Those are all classic infrastructure knobs."},{"text":"Serverless notebook with a Dedicated toggle","ok":false,"fb":"Serverless has no access-mode, worker or DBR selector."},{"text":"Serverless SQL warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"Warehouses don't have Standard/Dedicated or DBR selectors either."}]}],"explain":"Purpose and infrastructure are different axes, but the matrix is constrained: classic exposes the knobs; serverless hides them."},{"id":"ch07-e077","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In the 'all combinations' matrix, Classic Jobs lets you configure access mode, nodes, DBR and autoscaling, while Serverless Jobs exposes none of them.","answer":true,"explain":"Classic Jobs: ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅. Serverless Jobs: ❌ access, ❌ nodes, ❌ traditional DBR, ❌ autoscaling (infra managed)."},{"id":"ch07-e078","type":"mcq","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"In the 'all combinations' matrix, how are DBR and autoscaling described for **Pro/Classic SQL warehouses**?","options":["You pick a DBR; min/max workers","Databricks SQL runtime; warehouse autoscale","Serverless managed; no scaling","Pipeline channel; vertical scaling"],"answer":1,"explain":"Non-serverless warehouses use the Databricks SQL runtime (no DBR selector) and scale as warehouses (warehouse autoscale), with access via the warehouse model. Nodes are abstracted."},{"id":"ch07-e079","type":"free","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview question: \"Walk me through how you would choose compute for a new workload in Databricks.\"","model":"First I identify the workload: interactive development, an automated job, a Lakeflow pipeline or SQL/BI. SQL/BI goes to a SQL warehouse, usually serverless. For the others I ask whether I need anything serverless doesn't support (R, RDD APIs, custom JAR/low-level Spark, init scripts, pools, specific Spark configs). If not, I pick serverless notebook / serverless Jobs / serverless pipeline. If I do, I choose the classic equivalent (all-purpose, Jobs compute, classic pipeline) and only then configure access mode, nodes, DBR, worker/driver types, scaling, Photon, spot, pools and termination.","rubric":["Workload first","SQL/BI → SQL warehouse","Serverless default unless unsupported feature","Classic equivalents named correctly","Parameters last"],"explain":"This is the decision diagram in prose: workload → infrastructure → parameters."},{"id":"ch07-e080","type":"cloze","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the quiz answers.","text":"Interactive PySpark without special needs → [[serverless notebook compute|serverless notebook]]. Scheduled PySpark production ETL → [[serverless jobs]] if supported, otherwise [[classic jobs|classic jobs compute]]. Power BI → usually [[serverless sql warehouse]].","explain":"These four answers cover most real-life compute choices."},{"id":"ch07-e081","type":"cloze","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the access-mode definitions.","text":"Access mode is an axis mainly for classic [[all-purpose]] and [[jobs]] compute. [[Standard]]: many users share the compute with workload isolation. [[Dedicated]]: assigned to one user or group.","bank":["serverless","SQL warehouse","Photon"],"explain":"Standard/Dedicated is the classic access-mode axis; serverless and SQL warehouses don't expose it."},{"id":"ch07-e082","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A task that ran fine on a classic **Dedicated** cluster fails after you move it to **serverless Jobs**.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you ask yourself first?","options":[{"text":"Does serverless Jobs use Standard access semantics that my code isn't compatible with?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — serverless Jobs requires Standard-compatible workloads."},{"text":"Did serverless forget to enable Photon?","ok":false,"fb":"Photon is automatic on serverless Jobs and wouldn't break compatibility."},{"text":"Is the serverless worker type too small?","ok":false,"fb":"You can't choose worker types on serverless; that isn't the lever."}]},{"prompt":"The code relies on a Dedicated-only feature and can't be refactored now. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Run it on classic Jobs compute with Dedicated access","ok":true,"fb":"Only classic exposes Dedicated access mode."},{"text":"Set Access mode = Dedicated in the serverless job","ok":false,"fb":"Serverless has no such dropdown."},{"text":"Run it on a SQL warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"Warehouses have no Dedicated mode and don't run arbitrary Spark code."}]}],"explain":"Serverless Jobs = Standard access semantics. Dedicated-only workloads stay on classic."},{"id":"ch07-e083","type":"order","section":"ch07-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the debug steps for an unpredictable production job.","items":["Check which compute the job runs on","Check who else is attached to that cluster during the run","Confirm contention for CPU/RAM/executors/shuffle","Move the job to serverless Jobs or classic Jobs compute"],"explain":"Compute first, then contention evidence, then move to the intended operational compute."},{"id":"ch07-e084","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You migrate a classic job to serverless Jobs and can't find where to set the instance pool, the init script and `min_workers`.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is the most likely explanation?","options":[{"text":"Serverless doesn't support pools, compute-scoped init scripts or worker counts — they're platform-managed","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly; the infrastructure matrix disappears on serverless."},{"text":"You lack CAN MANAGE on the job","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions don't hide these fields; they simply don't exist on serverless."},{"text":"The fields are under Advanced → Spark config","ok":false,"fb":"Most Spark cluster configs are also not supported on serverless."}]},{"prompt":"The init script installs a native library the job truly needs. Next step?","options":[{"text":"Run this task on Classic Jobs compute","ok":true,"fb":"Classic exposes init scripts, pools and worker settings."},{"text":"Add the init script to the serverless notebook instead","ok":false,"fb":"Compute-scoped init scripts aren't supported on serverless anywhere."},{"text":"Use a bigger serverless performance mode","ok":false,"fb":"Performance mode affects startup/cost, not native installs."}]}],"explain":"Serverless removes policies, pools, compute-scoped init scripts/libraries and most Spark configs. Truly required → classic."},{"id":"ch07-e085","type":"order","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the 'missing field on serverless' playbook.","items":["Am I on serverless or classic?","Is the setting an infrastructure concern (VMs, counts, pools, init scripts, policies)?","Do I truly need it or is it a classic habit?","If truly needed, switch the task to Classic Jobs compute"],"explain":"Identify the compute, classify the setting, challenge the need, then switch only if required."},{"id":"ch07-e086","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"In a notebook, `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM prod.sales.orders` works but the next cell `from pyspark.sql import functions as F` errors.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"What compute the notebook is attached to","ok":true,"fb":"If it's a SQL warehouse, only SQL and Markdown run."},{"text":"Whether `prod.sales.orders` exists","ok":false,"fb":"The SQL cell already worked; the table is fine."},{"text":"Whether Photon is enabled","ok":false,"fb":"Photon doesn't control which languages run."}]},{"prompt":"It's attached to `analytics-wh`. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Attach to serverless notebook compute (or all-purpose) for the Python cells","ok":true,"fb":"Python/PySpark needs Spark compute."},{"text":"Upgrade the warehouse from Pro to Serverless","ok":false,"fb":"No warehouse type runs Python notebook cells."},{"text":"Grant CAN MANAGE on the warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"It's not a permission problem."}]}],"explain":"Notebooks on SQL warehouses run SQL + Markdown only."},{"id":"ch07-e087","type":"order","section":"ch07-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the questions for a serverless job with a startup-latency problem.","items":["Is it a serverless Job/pipeline (not a notebook)?","Which performance mode is set?","Does the workload need fast startup or is it relaxed batch?","Switch to Performance Optimized (or Standard for cheaper batch)"],"explain":"Identify, read the mode, match it to the SLA, then switch."},{"id":"ch07-e088","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A data scientist's R notebook won't run on serverless notebook compute.","steps":[{"prompt":"First question?","options":[{"text":"Does serverless notebook compute support R?","ok":true,"fb":"It doesn't — R (and RDD APIs) are unsupported on serverless notebooks."},{"text":"Is the cluster's DBR too old?","ok":false,"fb":"Serverless has no user-chosen DBR."},{"text":"Is the SQL warehouse stopped?","ok":false,"fb":"Not relevant to a serverless notebook."}]},{"prompt":"Best compute for this R development?","options":[{"text":"Classic all-purpose, possibly Dedicated","ok":true,"fb":"Interactive + unsupported serverless feature → classic all-purpose."},{"text":"Serverless Jobs","ok":false,"fb":"Still serverless, and not interactive."},{"text":"Pro SQL warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"Warehouses run SQL only."}]}],"explain":"Interactive + unsupported serverless feature → classic all-purpose (maybe Dedicated)."},{"id":"ch07-e089","type":"scenario","section":"ch07-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You configure a classic all-purpose cluster with GPU instances and DBR ML and notice the Photon toggle can't be turned on.","steps":[{"prompt":"Why?","options":[{"text":"Photon isn't supported on GPU instance types","ok":true,"fb":"Axis constraint: GPU + Photon ❌ on classic."},{"text":"You need Dedicated access for Photon","ok":false,"fb":"Photon has nothing to do with access mode."},{"text":"DBR ML is too old","ok":false,"fb":"It's the GPU instance type, not the runtime version."}]},{"prompt":"The team also runs heavy SQL ETL in the same notebook. Best approach?","options":[{"text":"Run the SQL/ETL on a CPU compute with Photon; keep GPU compute for deep learning","ok":true,"fb":"Different axes, different needs."},{"text":"Force Photon via Spark config","ok":false,"fb":"Not supported — don't fight the constraint."},{"text":"Switch to single-node","ok":false,"fb":"Topology doesn't unlock Photon on GPU."}]}],"explain":"Not all axis combinations are valid: GPU + Photon on classic GPU compute is the classic example."},{"id":"ch07-e090","type":"cloze","section":"ch07-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the list of things serverless does NOT support.","text":"Serverless does not support compute [[policies]], instance [[pools]], compute-scoped [[init scripts]], compute-scoped [[libraries]] or most Spark cluster [[configurations|configs]].","bank":["Photon","autoscaling","notebooks"],"explain":"These are infrastructure-level concerns that the platform manages on serverless."},{"id":"ch07-e701","type":"tf","section":"ch07-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"If a user's Unity Catalog grants are correct, the query works on any cluster, whatever its configuration.","answer":false,"explain":"False. The compute must be UC-compatible (Standard/Dedicated classic, serverless, SQL warehouse). A legacy configuration fails even with perfect grants — layer 8 of the permission algorithm.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch08","num":8,"title":"Compute Anatomy & Access Control","subtitle":"DBR, Photon, driver/workers, scaling, pools, termination — and who may use compute vs data","emoji":"⚙️","sourcePages":"216–271","mantra":"Every knob lives on one layer — and permission to use compute is never permission to use data.","objectives":["You can explain what a runtime is, choose between current, LTS and ML DBRs, and know how runtime works on serverless, pipelines and warehouses.","You can explain Photon (vectorized, Catalyst still plans, fallback) and separate it from DBR and GPU.","You can size driver vs workers, compute aggregate cores/RAM and diagnose driver-OOM vs worker bottlenecks.","You can compare autoscaling on classic, serverless, pipelines and SQL warehouses, and pools vs autoscaling.","You can explain termination, auto termination and Auto Stop and what survives them.","You can debug SQL warehouse PERMISSION_DENIED (two gates) and failing pipeline runs (three layers).","You can read a full classic configuration and narrate what happens when you press Start."],"sections":[{"id":"ch08-s01","title":"The Layered Stack & What 'Runtime' Means","hook":"DBR, Photon, driver type, pool, autoscaling and serverless sit on DIFFERENT layers — the UI just puts them side by side.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"┌────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ YOUR WORKLOAD                      │\n│ Python / SQL / Spark / ML/Pipeline │\n└─────────────────┬──────────────────┘\n                  ▼\nEXECUTION ENGINE   Spark JVM and/or Photon\n                  ▼\nRUNTIME            Databricks Runtime\n                   Spark + Python + JVM + libs\n                  ▼\nCOMPUTE NODES      Driver + Worker(s)\n              ┌───┴────┐\n          Driver type  Worker type\n          (VM shape)   (VM shape)\n                  ▼\nINFRASTRUCTURE     Classic / Serverless","caption":"Each setting belongs to ONE layer"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Not alternatives on one level","text":"DBR, Photon, driver type, worker type, pool, autoscaling, all-purpose and serverless are **not** alternative choices on the same level. Place each on its layer."},{"t":"p","text":"**Runtime in general:** source code is just text. To execute it you need an environment: Python interpreter, JVM, Apache Spark, PySpark, Java/Scala libraries, native and system libraries, configuration and dependency versions."},{"t":"p","text":"That environment is the **runtime environment**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"my_program.py\n     ▼\nPython 3.12 runtime\n├── Python interpreter\n├── standard library\n└── installed packages","caption":"A plain Python runtime"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: the same program moves from Python 3.12 + numpy 2.x to Python 3.8 + numpy 1.20. What can happen?","text":"It may **behave differently or fail**. Code behaviour depends on the runtime it runs in — that's why runtime versions matter."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Recipe vs kitchen","text":"Your code is the recipe; the runtime is the stocked kitchen (tools, ingredients, versions). The same recipe in a different kitchen can turn out differently."}]},{"id":"ch08-s02","title":"Databricks Runtime (DBR), Versions & LTS","hook":"DBR is the software stack loaded on every VM — and its version decides which Spark you get.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Databricks the packaged runtime is the **Databricks Runtime (DBR)**: the set of core components that run on the compute. **Every DBR version includes Apache Spark.**"},{"t":"diagram","text":"VM (CPU, RAM, disk, network)   ← can't run spark.read alone\n└── Databricks Runtime\n    ├── Apache Spark   ├── JVM\n    ├── Python         ├── PySpark\n    ├── Scala          ├── Delta Lake integrations\n    ├── Databricks optimizations\n    ├── built-in libraries\n    └── Databricks services/integrations\n\nyour code → DBR → VM hardware","caption":"DBR loaded on top of a bare VM"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Worker type: m5.4xlarge","points":["\"What kind of machine do I want?\"","NODE TYPE = hardware/infrastructure"]},{"title":"Databricks Runtime: 18 LTS","points":["\"What software environment goes on those machines?\"","RUNTIME = software environment"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Laptop","text":"Ryzen 9, 32 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD ≈ **worker node type**. Ubuntu + Python + Java + Spark + libraries ≈ **runtime**. (Not exactly an OS, but a good start.)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Why versions?** Spark, Python, Scala, Java, Delta support, Databricks APIs, libraries, security fixes and performance optimizations all evolve. DBR 16 and DBR 19 differ in Spark version, APIs, bug fixes, library versions, optimizer behaviour, Python versions and features."},{"t":"table","head":["DBR","Spark"],"rows":[["19","Spark 4.2.0"],["18 LTS","Spark 4.1.0"],["17.3 LTS","Spark 4.0.0"],["16.4 LTS","Spark 3.5.2"],["15.4 LTS","Spark 3.5.0"],["14.3 LTS","Spark 3.5.0"]],"caption":"Versions per the source's release notes snapshot; 19 and all these LTS have ML variants"},{"t":"p","text":"**LTS = Long-Term Support.** For a production chain PostgreSQL → Databricks → bronze → silver → gold → financial reporting you don't want a new runtime / new Spark / new library every few weeks where *something subtly changes*."},{"t":"p","text":"Pinning **DBR 18 LTS** gives a longer support/stability horizon — supported until **June 2029** (per the source)."},{"t":"table","head":["Use","Databricks' general advice"],"rows":[["All-purpose development","newest / current runtime"],["Operational jobs","consider LTS"],["Machine learning","consider DBR ML"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"LTS for production","text":"Production jobs → LTS DBR; learning/interactive dev → current DBR."}]},{"id":"ch08-s03","title":"DBR vs DBR ML, and Runtime Beyond Classic","hook":"Classic picks a DBR; serverless, pipelines and warehouses each manage runtime their own way.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Databricks Runtime","points":["For Spark, SQL, ETL, data engineering","e.g. Databricks Runtime 18 LTS"]},{"title":"Databricks Runtime for ML","points":["Normal DBR + preconfigured ML/DL stack","e.g. 18 LTS ML: PyTorch, transformers, XGBoost, MLflow, GPU components","DBR + ML/DL libs + ML integrations + GPU software"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"DBR ML is not 'better'","text":"You don't pick DBR ML because it sounds better. For ordinary ETL the normal DBR is usually more appropriate."},{"t":"table","head":["Use case","Usual choice"],"rows":[["Learning / interactive development","Current DBR"],["Production Spark job","LTS DBR"],["ML / PyTorch / XGBoost","DBR ML"],["GPU ML","DBR ML + GPU node"],["Legacy application","DBR version compatible with the application"],["Serverless","You do NOT choose a traditional DBR"]],"caption":"Beginner's runtime decision table"},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless:** Databricks explicitly says that for serverless Jobs it **auto-upgrades the runtime** and manages runtime version, instance types, memory and processing engines. So *\"which DBR for my serverless job?\"* is usually the wrong question."},{"t":"p","text":"But **serverless still has a runtime** — you just don't choose the exact traditional DBR. Serverless notebooks have an **Environment** configuration: base environment / **environment version** plus dependencies."},{"t":"diagram","text":"SERVERLESS\n├── Databricks-managed underlying runtime\n└── user-configurable application environment\n    ├── libraries\n    ├── dependencies\n    └── environment version","caption":"Serverless runtime model"},{"t":"p","text":"**Lakeflow pipelines** use **runtime channels**: `current` and `preview`. Databricks upgrades the pipeline runtime. Per the source: current ≈ DBR 17.3, preview ≈ DBR 18, rolling out per region."},{"t":"table","head":["Compute","Who decides the runtime?"],"rows":[["Classic all-purpose / job","You choose the DBR"],["Serverless","Databricks manages DBR/runtime"],["Lakeflow pipeline","Pipeline runtime channel (current / preview)"],["SQL warehouse","Databricks SQL-managed runtime"]],"caption":"The runtime mental map"}]},{"id":"ch08-s04","title":"Photon: The Vectorized Engine","hook":"Same code, same Catalyst plan — a faster C++ engine executes it underneath.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Photon** is the Databricks-native **vectorized execution engine**."},{"t":"list","items":["NOT a runtime version","NOT a VM","NOT a replacement of Spark as a programming model","NOT a database","NOT a file format"],"ordered":false},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"(spark.table(\"orders\")\n   .filter(\"amount > 100\")\n   .groupBy(\"country\")\n   .sum(\"amount\"))"},{"t":"flow","items":["DataFrame API","logical plan","Catalyst optimizer","physical plan","execution"],"caption":"Catalyst still plans — Photon doesn't replace it"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without Photon","points":["Catalyst physical plan","↓ Spark JVM execution","↓ CPU"]},{"title":"With Photon (supported operators)","points":["Catalyst physical plan","↓ Photon native engine (C++)","↓ vectorized columnar batches","↓ CPU / SIMD"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Vectorized:** row-at-a-time loads row 1, calculates, loads row 2… Vectorized processes a whole batch `[100, 200, 300, 400, …]` of `amount * 1.24` at once. CPUs use **SIMD** — *Single Instruction, Multiple Data*. Photon processes batches of **thousands of rows**."},{"t":"p","text":"**No code change:** you still write `df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` — never `photon.groupBy(...)`. Spark API → Catalyst → Photon."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Scan        → Photon\nFilter      → Photon\nSomeUnsupportedOperation → Spark/JVM (fallback)\nAggregate","caption":"Unsupported operator? Fallback, not failure"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Fallback","text":"If Photon doesn't support an operator, the query does NOT fail — that part falls back to Spark/JVM. Photon and regular Spark can participate in the same workload."},{"t":"p","text":"The \"other option\" to Photon is simply **Photon OFF → traditional Spark JVM execution**. There is no third general engine."}]},{"id":"ch08-s05","title":"Photon per Compute, vs GPU, vs Runtime","hook":"Where is Photon on by default, and why it is neither a GPU nor a DBR.","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Compute","Photon"],"rows":[["Serverless compute","Enabled"],["SQL warehouse","Enabled"],["Serverless Lakeflow pipeline","Enabled"],["Classic all-purpose","On by default, configurable"],["Classic jobs","On by default, configurable"],["Classic pipeline","Configurable via the Photon setting"]],"caption":"Photon configuration per compute type"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Photon ≠ GPU acceleration","text":"Photon = **CPU-native vectorized SQL/DataFrame engine**. GPU = **hardware accelerator** for deep learning, matrix operations, model training. Classic GPU compute does **not** support Photon."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Execution engine axis","points":["Photon / JVM"]},{"title":"Hardware axis","points":["CPU / GPU"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Runtime vs Photon:** `DBR 18 LTS + Photon ON` coexist. DBR = software environment; Photon = query execution engine inside the execution stack."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Your PySpark\n   ↓\nDBR environment\n├── Catalyst planner\n└── execution\n    ├── Photon\n    └── JVM Spark","caption":"Photon lives INSIDE the runtime's execution layer"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Not competitors","text":"DBR and Photon are not competing options — one compute can use DBR 18 LTS **and** Photon."}]},{"id":"ch08-s06","title":"The Driver Node & Driver Type","hook":"The driver coordinates — and everything you collect() lands in its RAM.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"              DRIVER\n                │\n      ┌─────────┼─────────┐\n      ▼         ▼         ▼\n   WORKER    WORKER    WORKER\n   executor  executor  executor","caption":"Classic multi-node Spark compute"},{"t":"p","text":"The **driver** is the coordinator. It holds the **state of attached notebooks**, the **SparkContext**, interprets commands and runs the Spark master/coordinator for the executors."},{"t":"flow","items":["receive notebook command","build logical plan","analyze/optimize","build stages/tasks","schedule tasks on workers","monitor execution","receive results","send output to notebook"],"caption":"What the driver does for display(result)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Driver type** is not a different software. It's **which cloud VM instance type the driver uses** — e.g. AWS `m5.xlarge`, `m5.2xlarge`, `r5.4xlarge` (names differ per cloud)."},{"t":"diagram","text":"distributed rows on workers\n   ↓      ↓      ↓\n        DRIVER        ← df.collect()\ndata = 50 GB, driver RAM = 16 GB → driver OOM","caption":"collect() brings EVERYTHING to the driver"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks recommends a **larger driver type** when you expect lots of collected data. Other driver-memory stressors: **large plans, too much task metadata, many attached notebook sessions, local Python objects**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"\"But I have 640 GB of RAM!\"","text":"Driver 8 GB, workers 10 × 64 GB = 640 GB. `huge_df.collect()` crashes the driver with OOM. collect() moves data workers → driver, and the driver has only 8 GB. That's why driver type and worker type are separate axes."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when the driver crashes with OOM","questions":["Did I call collect() (or toPandas / similar) on a large result?","How big is the data I'm bringing back vs the driver's RAM?","Is the driver type much smaller than the workers?","Are many notebooks attached / large local Python objects living on the driver?","Do I really need the data on the driver, or can workers finish the job?"]}]},{"id":"ch08-s07","title":"Workers, Worker Types & Scaling Up vs Out","hook":"Workers do the heavy lifting; the right worker shape depends on the workload's bottleneck.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"WORKER 1: partition 1, partition 2\nWORKER 2: partition 3, partition 4\nWORKER 3: partition 5, partition 6","caption":"Workers do the distributed work"},{"t":"p","text":"On Databricks multi-node classic compute each worker runs a **Spark executor**; docs often use worker/executor almost interchangeably."},{"t":"p","text":"**Worker type** = which cloud VM type each worker uses. 4 workers × (8 cores, 32 GB) = **32 worker cores** and **128 GB aggregate worker RAM** — distributed, not one 128 GB machine."},{"t":"table","head":["Workload","Pressure on"],"rows":[["CPU-heavy","CPU → compute-optimized machines"],["Large join, cache, wide aggregation","RAM"],["Huge shuffle","network, local disk, memory"]],"caption":"There is no 'best' worker type — it's workload-dependent"},{"t":"p","text":"**Driver type and worker type can differ** (e.g. driver 16 cores/64 GB, workers 8 cores/32 GB, or the reverse). By **default the driver type = worker type**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Scale UP","points":["Bigger machine","worker 8 CPU → 32 CPU","32 GB → 128 GB"]},{"title":"Scale OUT","points":["More machines","4 workers → 16 workers"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Spark is designed mainly for distributed **scale-out**, but node sizing still matters enormously."},{"t":"table","head":["Spark UI shows","Consider","NOT"],"rows":[["All workers CPU-saturated, little/no spill, memory OK, huge CPU time","More workers, larger/CPU-oriented workers, Photon, query optimization","A bigger driver"],["Massive spill to disk, executor OOMs","More worker memory, better partitioning, less skew, different join, more/larger workers","Necessarily a bigger driver"]],"caption":"Two classic diagnosis scenarios"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Upsizing the driver for worker problems","text":"CPU saturation, spill and executor OOM are **worker-side** bottlenecks. A bigger driver doesn't help."}]},{"id":"ch08-s08","title":"Autoscaling Across Compute Types","hook":"Four different things are all called 'autoscaling'.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Classic autoscaling:** worker type 8 CPU/32 GB, **Min workers = 2, Max workers = 10**. Databricks may go 2 → 4 → 8 → 10 → 6 → 2 with load. The **type** of each worker stays; the **number** changes = **horizontal scaling**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless notebooks/jobs:** autoscaling exists but is not a parameter you manage. You submit the workload; Databricks selects, scales, adjusts memory and optimizes. In serverless Jobs, **autoscaling and Photon are automatic**. No `min_workers = 4`, `max_workers = 20`, `worker_type = r5.4xlarge`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Is autoscaling a classic-only axis?","text":"Conceptually autoscaling exists on BOTH. As a **manual configuration axis** (fixed 8 vs autoscale 2–10) it's mainly classic. On serverless, scaling is inherent — no \"Enable autoscaling?\" checkbox."},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless Lakeflow pipelines** have **horizontal** (more/fewer workers/executors) **and vertical** autoscaling (larger/smaller machine types). E.g. Databricks may see an OOM and use a larger worker/driver instance in the next update. You don't pick the worker type, but you **can set a Max workers upper bound** for cost/capacity."},{"t":"p","text":"**SQL warehouse autoscaling** is about **concurrency**: a Medium warehouse gets more **warehouse clusters** (1 → 2 → 3…) as 10, 50, 100 queries arrive. Serverless warehouses use **Intelligent Workload Management**; you can set min/max cluster counts."},{"t":"table","head":["Kind","What scales"],"rows":[["Classic Spark autoscaling","workers"],["SQL warehouse concurrency scaling","warehouse clusters"],["Serverless compute scaling","managed infrastructure"],["Serverless pipeline","horizontal + vertical"]],"caption":"Same word, four meanings"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Serverless still scales","text":"\"Is there scaling on serverless?\" Yes — intrinsic and platform-managed, just not a user-selected fixed/autoscaling axis."}]},{"id":"ch08-s09","title":"All-purpose Is Not Interactive-only","hook":"Interactive by design — not by technical restriction.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"All-purpose is designed and recommended for **interactive development, ad-hoc analysis, debugging, exploration** — but you **can** use an existing all-purpose classic compute for certain Job tasks. Databricks supports it but explicitly **does not recommend it for production jobs**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"All-purpose lifecycle","points":["start cluster → Nikos runs → thinks 10 min → runs again","Maria attaches → Nikos debugs → more notebooks","lives independently of one task"]},{"title":"Jobs compute lifecycle","points":["job starts → compute starts","job executes → job completes","compute terminates — the operational model"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Why not for production jobs?** Developer A, Developer B, Notebook C and a production job share CPU, RAM, executors, shuffle bandwidth. A random `df.crossJoin(other_huge_df).count()` can hurt production ETL."},{"t":"list","items":["Different billing model","Can stay idle (still running)","Job-specific tuning doesn't always fit a shared interactive cluster","Less predictable production behaviour"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"**Limited exceptions:** iterative development/testing of a Job (avoid repeated startup) and **very short, frequent jobs** where an already-running all-purpose cluster cuts startup latency."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"One-liner","text":"**all-purpose = interactive by design, not an interactive-only technical restriction.**"}]},{"id":"ch08-s10","title":"Instance Pools","hook":"A parking lot of warm VMs, so classic clusters start fast.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Full name: **Instance Pool**. NOT a thread pool, connection pool, data pool or Spark executor pool. It's **a reservoir of already-provisioned cloud VM instances** that classic compute can use quickly."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without pool","points":["start cluster","Databricks asks AWS for 8 VMs","AWS provisions VMs → VM boot","DBR install/start → cluster ready","can take a while"]},{"title":"With pool","points":["idle VMs already provisioned/warm","cluster grabs them","much faster startup"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Pool settings:** **minimum idle**, **maximum capacity**, **idle instance auto-termination**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Lifecycle:** min idle = 3, max capacity = 20 → initially 3 idle VMs. Cluster A needs 2 → gets 2 idle VMs, pool tries to restore min idle. Cluster B needs 5 more → takes from the pool, or the pool requests new cloud machines."},{"t":"p","text":"**Preloaded DBR:** a pool can have `Preloaded DBR = 18 LTS`, so idle VMs already have the runtime cached → cluster wants DBR 18 → VM ready → very fast startup."},{"t":"diagram","text":"cluster: min 2, max 10   pool: max capacity 20\nnow: 2 workers → autoscaler: \"I need 4 more\"\n   pool has idle? → give 4 immediately\n   otherwise      → pool → cloud → provision more\n\nPOOL        = WHERE machines come from\nAUTOSCALING = HOW MANY machines compute wants","caption":"Pools + autoscaling work together"},{"t":"p","text":"**Pools and serverless:** you don't pick a pool on serverless — prewarming, capacity, machine choice and idle infrastructure are Databricks' problem. Current docs recommend **serverless instead of pools** when the workload supports it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Cluster takes 4 minutes to start","text":"Query performance is excellent once running, but startup is 4 min. More worker RAM won't fix it. A **pool** reduces VM provisioning/runtime startup latency — or better, if compatible, **serverless** removes the provisioning problem entirely."}]},{"id":"ch08-s11","title":"Termination, Auto Termination & Auto Stop","hook":"Terminating compute is not deleting it — and your Delta tables don't care either way.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Terminate compute","points":["running VMs terminated/released","configuration (name, runtime, worker type, autoscaling, settings) STAYS","press Start later → new VMs"]},{"title":"Delete compute configuration","points":["the configuration itself is removed"]}]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Lost on termination (ephemeral)","points":["executor memory","cached DataFrames","shuffle files","local temporary files","Python process state","in-memory variables / session state"]},{"title":"Survives termination (persistent)","points":["Delta tables in ADLS/S3/GCS","Unity Catalog metadata","notebook source code","job definition"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"compute lifetime ≠ persistent data lifetime","text":"Back to the basic principle: clusters come and go; tables and code persist."},{"t":"flow","items":["active","idle","30 minutes","terminate"],"caption":"All-purpose auto termination (inactivity-based)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Classic Jobs compute** doesn't wait 30 idle minutes: job starts → compute starts → job completes → compute terminates."},{"t":"table","head":["Compute","Termination model"],"rows":[["All-purpose","Auto termination = idle-time based"],["Jobs compute","Job-lifecycle based"],["SQL warehouse","Auto Stop — default ~10 min serverless, ~45 min Pro/Classic (different minimums)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Similar concept, different product setting","text":"Classic auto termination and SQL warehouse **Auto Stop** are similar lifecycle ideas but separate configurations."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when compute keeps costing money","questions":["Is this an all-purpose cluster (it stays up while idle)?","Is auto termination configured, and to what idle time?","Is something keeping it 'active' (attached notebook running, a job using it)?","Is a production job running on all-purpose instead of Jobs compute?","For SQL warehouses: what's the Auto Stop value?","Could serverless remove idle infrastructure altogether?"]}]},{"id":"ch08-s12","title":"SQL Warehouse Access: Two Gates","hook":"Permission to use the compute ≠ permission to read the data.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *\nFROM prod.finance.payroll;","caption":"Run through a SQL warehouse"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Gate 1: May Nikos use THIS COMPUTE?  → warehouse ACL\nGate 2: May Nikos read THIS TABLE?   → Unity Catalog\n        both open → query possible","caption":"Two separate questions"},{"t":"table","head":["Permission","What it allows"],"rows":[["CAN VIEW","see the warehouse / details"],["CAN MONITOR","view/monitor (query history, profiles) and run queries"],["CAN USE","use the warehouse to run queries"],["CAN MANAGE","change configuration / permissions"],["IS OWNER","ownership / admin-like control"]],"caption":"Gate 1 — warehouse ACL (the warehouse is a workspace object)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"CAN USE ≠ read all data","text":"Nikos has CAN USE on the warehouse but no SELECT on `prod.finance.payroll` → **permission denied**. CAN USE = you may use this query engine; it does NOT grant every table the engine can reach."},{"t":"p","text":"**Gate 2 — Unity Catalog:** e.g. `USE CATALOG` on `prod`, `USE SCHEMA` on `finance`, `SELECT` on `payroll`. **Warehouse ACL + UC permissions = query possible.**"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `nikos@company.com`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.finance TO `nikos@company.com`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.finance.payroll TO `nikos@company.com`;","caption":"Gate 2 grants (UC syntax)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Example:** `analysts` have CAN USE on `analytics-wh` and SELECT on `prod.sales.*` but not `prod.hr.*`. `SELECT * FROM prod.sales.orders` ✅, `SELECT * FROM prod.hr.salary` ❌ — on exactly the same warehouse."},{"t":"p","text":"**CAN MONITOR** fits a power user / performance engineer who needs query history, query profiles and warehouse monitoring without changing size, scaling or permissions."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"No Standard/Dedicated on warehouses","text":"SQL warehouses don't use the classic Standard/Dedicated selector. They have warehouse ACL + Unity Catalog governance + their own security architecture. \"Serverless SQL Warehouse + Dedicated access mode\" is not a valid axis."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a warehouse query gets PERMISSION_DENIED","questions":["Can I start/use the warehouse at all (Gate 1: CAN USE or higher)?","Is the error about the warehouse or about a table/schema/catalog?","Do I have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Do I have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Do I have SELECT on the table itself?","Am I assuming CAN USE grants data access?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → what Gate 2 really contains","text":"\"Unity Catalog\" in Gate 2 is itself a chain: workspace binding → USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → object privilege → row filters / masks / ABAC → external storage — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch08-s13","title":"Pipeline & Serverless Access: Three Layers","hook":"Who may press Start, whose identity runs the pipeline, and what data that identity can touch.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"Layer 1: Can you access/control the PIPELINE OBJECT?\n         → pipeline ACL\nLayer 2: Under WHOSE IDENTITY does it execute?\n         → run-as (user or service principal)\nLayer 3: What DATA can that identity access?\n         → Unity Catalog privileges","caption":"Pipeline access = 3 layers"},{"t":"table","head":["Permission","Capabilities"],"rows":[["CAN VIEW","see pipeline / details"],["CAN RUN","start/stop a pipeline update"],["CAN MANAGE","edit settings + permissions + run"],["IS OWNER","owner"]],"caption":"Layer 1 — pipeline ACL (workspace object)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Layer 2 — run-as:** Nikos has CAN RUN and presses **Start**. When the pipeline reads `prod.raw.orders`, it uses the **run-as identity**, e.g. `etl-service-principal` — not necessarily Nikos' permissions."},{"t":"p","text":"By default **run-as = the creator**, but it can be changed to another user or a **service principal**. Databricks recommends a **service principal for production**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Nikos:            CAN RUN pipeline, no SELECT on prod.raw.orders\npipeline-prod-sp: SELECT prod.raw.orders, CREATE TABLE prod.silver\n→ Nikos can trigger it (CAN RUN)\n→ it executes fine (run-as SP has the data privileges)","caption":"Same pattern as Jobs"},{"t":"p","text":"**Layer 3 — UC privileges** for the pipeline service principal: `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, `SELECT` on sources, `CREATE TABLE` or `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW` on the target schema. Grant **only what's needed**."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.raw TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.raw.orders TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA, CREATE TABLE, CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW\n  ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;","caption":"Least-privilege grants for the run-as SP"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Pipeline permission ≠ output data permission","text":"Maria has CAN VIEW on the pipeline. That is NOT `SELECT` on `prod.gold.revenue` — the output table is a UC object with its own permissions. To open the backing pipeline from a streaming table/materialized view, non-admins may also need **REFRESH** on that object."},{"t":"p","text":"**Pipeline compute permissions:** a **serverless pipeline** runs on Databricks-managed compute — no cluster, no `CAN ATTACH TO`, no user-managed sizing/configuration/security. A **classic pipeline** has more infra options, but its compute lifecycle is pipeline-managed (you can't freely pin a Spark version; the pipeline runtime manages it)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless access management exists** — just not Standard/Dedicated. Built-in workspace objects: **Default Interactive Compute** (serverless notebooks / Databricks Connect) and **Default Automated Compute** (serverless Jobs / Lakeflow pipelines), with **CAN USE / CAN MANAGE**; admins can restrict which users/groups use serverless."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Standard/Dedicated is not universal","text":"It's mainly a classic compute access-mode concept."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a pipeline update fails although I could start it","questions":["Layer 1: do I (still) have CAN RUN / CAN MANAGE — and did the update actually start?","Layer 2: whose identity does the pipeline run as — me, the creator, or a service principal?","Layer 3: does THAT identity have USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA on sources and target?","Does it have SELECT on every source table?","Does it have CREATE TABLE / CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW on the target schema?","Am I testing with MY permissions instead of the run-as identity's?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → run-as = Case 1","text":"\"Notebook works, job/pipeline fails\" is Debugging Case 1 (wrong identity). The production example `gold_pipeline_sp` gets read-Silver + write-Gold grants only — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch08-s14","title":"Putting It Together: Matrices, Start-up & the Full Stack","hook":"Read any configuration field by field and know what happens when you press Start.","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Axis","Classic All-purpose","Classic Jobs","Serverless Notebook","Serverless Jobs"],"rows":[["Purpose","interactive-oriented","automated","interactive","automated"],["DBR","you choose","you choose","managed","managed"],["Photon","configurable","configurable","enabled","enabled"],["Driver type","you choose","you choose","managed","managed"],["Worker type","you choose","you choose","managed","managed"],["Fixed workers","✅","✅","❌","❌"],["Min/max autoscale","✅","✅","❌","❌"],["Automatic scaling","optional","optional","✅ managed","✅ managed"],["Pool","✅","✅","❌","❌"],["Standard/Dedicated","✅","✅","not a selector","not a selector"],["Auto termination","configurable","lifecycle-based","managed","lifecycle-based"],["GPU","possible (classic config)","possible","AI Runtime separately","AI Runtime separately"],["Job use","supported, discouraged for prod","intended use","N/A","intended use"]],"caption":"Spark compute matrix"},{"t":"table","head":["Axis","Classic Pipeline","Serverless Pipeline"],"rows":[["Compute ownership","customer/classic","Databricks managed"],["Runtime","pipeline-managed channel","pipeline-managed"],["Instance types","configurable","managed"],["Worker autoscaling","enhanced, configurable","enhanced automatic"],["Vertical scaling","limited / manual node choice","automatic"],["Photon","configurable","enabled"],["Unity Catalog","supported","required"],["Legacy Hive","possible","no"],["Pool / custom cluster settings","some classic controls","no"],["Pipeline ACL","CAN VIEW/RUN/MANAGE/OWNER","same"],["Run-as","user / service principal","same"]],"caption":"Lakeflow pipeline matrix"},{"t":"table","head":["Parameter","Serverless SQL WH","Pro","Classic"],"rows":[["DBR selector","❌","❌","❌"],["Photon","✅","✅","✅"],["Warehouse size","✅","✅","✅"],["Concurrent cluster scaling","✅","✅","✅"],["Intelligent workload management","✅","❌","❌"],["Predictive IO","✅","✅","❌"],["Standard/Dedicated","❌","❌","❌"],["Warehouse ACL","✅","✅","✅"],["Unity Catalog data ACL","✅","✅","✅"],["Auto Stop","✅","✅","✅"]],"caption":"SQL warehouse matrix"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"Name: etl-development\nPurpose: All-purpose        → lifecycle/purpose\nInfrastructure: Classic     → infrastructure management\nAccess mode: Standard       → access/security mode\nRuntime: DBR 18 LTS         → software environment\nPhoton: Enabled             → execution engine\nDriver: r6i.2xlarge         → driver hardware\nWorker: r6i.xlarge          → worker hardware\nAutoscaling: min 2, max 8   → horizontal scaling policy\nPool: (optional)            → VM provisioning source","caption":"Reading a complete classic configuration"},{"t":"flow","items":["Need compute","Driver VM (maybe from pool)","Min worker VMs","Start/install DBR 18 LTS","Start Spark driver","Start Spark executors","Photon available","Spark session ready","Notebook attaches"],"caption":"What happens when you press Start"},{"t":"flow","items":["Notebook","Driver","Catalyst","physical plan","Photon","tasks","workers","partitions"],"caption":"Then df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\") runs"},{"t":"diagram","text":"WORKLOAD          SQL / Python / Spark / ML\n   ▼\nAPI / PLANNER     Spark Catalyst\n   ▼\nEXECUTION ENGINE  Photon / Spark JVM\n   ▼\nRUNTIME           DBR / DBR ML\n   ▼\nCLASSIC COMPUTE   Driver + Worker node(s)\n   ├── Driver type\n   └── Worker type → scaling policy\n\nSERVERLESS collapses the bottom half:\nmanaged runtime, Photon, machine types,\nscaling, provisioning, lifecycle","caption":"The full stack"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Classic","points":["You manage/configure the infrastructure envelope."]},{"title":"Serverless","points":["You specify the workload; Databricks manages the infrastructure envelope."]}]}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch08-d01","title":"I can use the warehouse but get PERMISSION_DENIED","section":"ch08-s12","symptom":"`SELECT * FROM prod.finance.payroll` on a SQL warehouse fails with PERMISSION_DENIED, although the user has CAN USE on the warehouse.","askYourself":["Could I start/use the warehouse at all — is Gate 1 (warehouse ACL) open?","Is the error about the warehouse or about a data object?","Do I have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Do I have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Do I have SELECT on the table itself?","Am I wrongly assuming CAN USE (or even CAN MANAGE) grants data access?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm warehouse permission level (CAN USE/MONITOR/MANAGE).","why":"Gate 1 = may I use this compute."},{"do":"Check UC privileges top-down.","why":"Gate 2 = may I read this data.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE prod.finance.payroll;"},{"do":"Ask the data owner to grant the missing privilege (if appropriate).","why":"Data access is governed in Unity Catalog.","code":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.finance.payroll TO `nikos@company.com`;"}],"rootCauses":["Missing SELECT on the table.","Missing USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA on parents.","Belief that compute permission = data permission."],"fix":"Grant the needed UC privileges (USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT). Warehouse ACL + UC permissions = query possible.","mnemonic":"Two gates: Machine, then Data."},{"id":"ch08-d02","title":"Pipeline runs fail although I can run it","section":"ch08-s13","symptom":"You have CAN RUN, the update starts, but fails with permission errors reading sources or creating target tables — even though you personally can read the source.","askYourself":["Layer 1: did the update actually start (so my CAN RUN is fine)?","Layer 2: whose identity does the pipeline run as — creator, another user, a service principal?","Am I testing with MY permissions instead of the run-as identity's?","Layer 3: does the run-as identity have USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA on source and target?","Does it have SELECT on every source table?","Does it have CREATE TABLE / CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW on the target schema?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the pipeline's permissions page.","why":"Layer 1: object ACL."},{"do":"Read the 'Run as' setting.","why":"Layer 2: updates execute as this identity."},{"do":"Inspect grants for the run-as identity on sources and target.","why":"Layer 3: UC privileges of THAT identity.","code":"SHOW GRANTS `pipeline-prod-sp` ON SCHEMA prod.silver;"},{"do":"Grant least privilege to the run-as SP.","why":"Only what the pipeline needs.","code":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.raw.orders TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;"}],"rootCauses":["Run-as identity lacks UC privileges.","Grants were given to a person instead of the run-as SP.","Run-as still the creator, whose access changed."],"fix":"Run production pipelines as a service principal and give that SP USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT on sources and CREATE TABLE/MATERIALIZED VIEW on the target.","mnemonic":"ORD — Object (ACL), Run-as (identity), Data (UC)."},{"id":"ch08-d03","title":"I can see the pipeline but can't read its output table","section":"ch08-s13","symptom":"Maria has CAN VIEW on the pipeline, but `SELECT * FROM prod.gold.revenue` fails with a permission error.","askYourself":["Is my permission on the pipeline OBJECT or on the DATA?","Do I have USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT on the output table?","Am I a non-admin trying to open the backing pipeline from a streaming table/MV (REFRESH needed)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Separate pipeline ACL from UC ACL.","why":"Output tables are UC objects with their own permissions."},{"do":"Request SELECT (and parent USE privileges) on the output table.","why":"Gate to the data is in Unity Catalog."}],"rootCauses":["Pipeline permission ≠ output data permission."],"fix":"Grant UC privileges on the output table (and REFRESH if she must open the backing pipeline from the ST/MV)."},{"id":"ch08-d04","title":"Driver OOM on collect() despite huge worker RAM","section":"ch08-s06","symptom":"Driver 8 GB, workers 10 × 64 GB. `huge_df.collect()` crashes the driver with OOM.","askYourself":["Am I bringing data back to the driver (collect / similar)?","How big is the result vs the DRIVER's RAM (not the workers')?","Is the driver type much smaller than the worker type?","Can the work stay on the workers (aggregate, write, display a sample)?","If I truly need data on the driver, is a larger driver type justified?","Are other driver stressors present (huge plans, task metadata, many sessions, big local Python objects)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Find the collect()-style action.","why":"collect() moves all rows workers → driver."},{"do":"Compare result size with driver RAM.","why":"Worker RAM is irrelevant for driver-side data."},{"do":"Rewrite to keep work distributed, or size up the driver.","why":"Databricks recommends a larger driver when much data is collected."}],"rootCauses":["Collecting a large result into a small driver.","Confusing aggregate worker RAM with driver RAM."],"fix":"Avoid collecting big data; aggregate or write on workers. If unavoidable, choose a larger driver type.","mnemonic":"collect() = Everything Comes Home to the driver."},{"id":"ch08-d05","title":"Cluster is slow to start","section":"ch08-s10","symptom":"Queries are excellent once the classic cluster runs, but startup takes ~4 minutes.","askYourself":["Is the problem startup time or query time?","Is the cluster provisioning fresh VMs from the cloud every time?","Is the runtime downloaded/started from scratch on each VM?","Could an instance pool (with preloaded DBR) provide warm VMs?","Is the workload compatible with serverless, removing provisioning entirely?"],"steps":[{"do":"Separate startup from execution time.","why":"More worker RAM doesn't fix startup."},{"do":"Attach the cluster to an instance pool with preloaded DBR.","why":"Warm VMs + cached runtime = fast launch."},{"do":"Consider serverless.","why":"Databricks owns prewarming and capacity."}],"rootCauses":["VM provisioning + boot + DBR start latency."],"fix":"Use a pool (min idle, preloaded DBR) or move to serverless if compatible.","mnemonic":"Slow start? Pool it or go serverless."},{"id":"ch08-d06","title":"Why is my cluster still costing money?","section":"ch08-s11","symptom":"An all-purpose cluster (or a warehouse) kept billing overnight/at the weekend though nobody was working.","askYourself":["Is this all-purpose compute (it stays up while idle)?","Is auto termination configured, and to what idle time?","Is something keeping it active — a running notebook or a job pointed at it?","Should that scheduled work run on Jobs compute (lifecycle-based termination) instead?","For a SQL warehouse: what is Auto Stop set to (serverless ~10 min, Pro/Classic ~45 min defaults)?","Is a pool keeping minimum idle VMs provisioned?","Could serverless remove idle infrastructure altogether?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the compute type and its auto-termination setting.","why":"All-purpose termination is idle-time based."},{"do":"Look for attached notebooks/jobs keeping it active.","why":"Activity resets the idle clock."},{"do":"Move scheduled work to Jobs compute; set auto termination / Auto Stop.","why":"Jobs compute terminates when the run ends."}],"rootCauses":["Auto termination off or too long.","Jobs running on all-purpose.","Long Auto Stop on warehouses.","Pools keeping idle VMs."],"fix":"Enable auto termination (e.g. 30 min), use Jobs compute for scheduled work, tune warehouse Auto Stop and pool min idle.","mnemonic":"IDLE: Is it all-purpose? Did auto-termination get set? Lingering jobs/notebooks? Elsewhere (warehouse Auto Stop, pools)?"},{"id":"ch08-d07","title":"Workers are struggling — should I upsize the driver?","section":"ch08-s07","symptom":"Spark UI shows saturated worker CPUs, or massive spill and executor OOMs. Someone proposes a bigger driver.","askYourself":["Which layer shows the symptom — driver or workers?","CPU saturated with little spill and healthy memory → is this a worker CPU bottleneck?","Massive spill / executor OOM → is worker memory, partitioning, skew or join strategy the issue?","Would more workers (scale out) or larger workers (scale up) help?","Would Photon or query optimization reduce CPU time?","Is the driver actually doing anything heavy (collect)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read Spark UI symptoms.","why":"Diagnose before resizing."},{"do":"Apply worker-side fixes.","why":"The bottleneck is on workers."}],"rootCauses":["Worker CPU bottleneck.","Insufficient worker memory / bad partitioning / skew / join choice."],"fix":"CPU-bound: more/CPU-oriented workers, Photon, query optimization. Spill/OOM: more worker memory, better partitioning, less skew, different join, more/larger workers. Not a bigger driver.","mnemonic":"Fix the layer that hurts."},{"id":"ch08-d08","title":"My variables/cached data vanished after the cluster restarted","section":"ch08-s11","symptom":"After auto termination, re-running a cell gives NameError or cached DataFrames are gone; the user fears data loss.","askYourself":["Did the compute terminate (auto termination / manual)?","Is the missing thing ephemeral (variables, cache, shuffle, temp files) or persistent (Delta tables, UC metadata, notebooks, jobs)?","Can I recreate the state by re-running cells from persistent data?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm termination in the compute event log/state.","why":"Termination wipes ephemeral state."},{"do":"Re-run the defining cells.","why":"Persistent tables are untouched."}],"rootCauses":["Compute lifetime ≠ persistent data lifetime."],"fix":"Restart and recreate session state from tables; nothing persistent was lost."},{"id":"ch08-d09","title":"A user can't use serverless compute at all","section":"ch08-s13","symptom":"A user's notebooks or jobs can't use serverless compute while colleagues' can.","askYourself":["Is this interactive (notebooks / Databricks Connect) or automated (jobs / pipelines)?","Does the user (or their group) have CAN USE on Default Interactive Compute?","For jobs/pipelines: CAN USE on Default Automated Compute?","Am I looking for a Standard/Dedicated setting that doesn't exist on serverless?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the permissions of the built-in serverless compute objects.","why":"Admins can restrict serverless per user/group."},{"do":"Grant CAN USE to the right group.","why":"Restores access."}],"rootCauses":["Missing CAN USE on Default Interactive / Default Automated Compute."],"fix":"Grant CAN USE on the relevant serverless compute object."},{"id":"ch08-d10","title":"Production code changed behaviour after a runtime change","section":"ch08-s02","symptom":"A production job that ran for months suddenly produces different results or fails after its runtime changed.","askYourself":["Did the DBR version (and with it Spark/Python/library versions) change?","Is the job pinned to an LTS runtime?","Is it on serverless, where Databricks upgrades the runtime automatically?","Is the job on a pipeline channel (current/preview) that moved forward?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare runtime/Spark versions before and after.","why":"DBRs differ in Spark version, APIs, libraries, optimizer behaviour."},{"do":"Pin classic production jobs to an LTS DBR.","why":"Longer stability horizon."}],"rootCauses":["Runtime upgrade changed Spark/library behaviour."],"fix":"Use LTS DBR for classic production jobs; test on new runtimes before switching; on serverless use environment versions for application compatibility."}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Runtime = machine","text":"DBR 18 LTS and r5.4xlarge are not versions of the same thing.","fix":"Runtime = software; node type = hardware."},{"title":"DBR ML for everything","text":"DBR ML adds ML/DL libraries; it's not 'better' for ETL.","fix":"Normal DBR for ETL; DBR ML for ML."},{"title":"Pinning DBR on serverless","text":"Serverless auto-upgrades its runtime.","fix":"Use environment versions; pin DBR only on classic."},{"title":"Photon replaces Catalyst","text":"Catalyst still plans; Photon executes supported operators.","fix":"Photon = engine below the physical plan."},{"title":"Photon = GPU","text":"Photon is a CPU vectorized engine; GPU compute doesn't support Photon.","fix":"Engine axis vs hardware axis."},{"title":"DBR vs Photon as competitors","text":"They coexist: DBR 18 LTS + Photon ON.","fix":"Photon lives inside the runtime's execution layer."},{"title":"Unsupported operator = failure","text":"Unsupported operations fall back to Spark/JVM.","fix":"Expect mixed Photon + JVM plans."},{"title":"Big workers protect collect()","text":"collect() lands on the driver; worker RAM doesn't help.","fix":"Avoid collect on big data or size the driver."},{"title":"Bigger driver for worker bottlenecks","text":"CPU saturation, spill and executor OOM are worker-side.","fix":"Fix workers, partitioning, skew, join, Photon."},{"title":"Autoscaling = serverless","text":"Classic autoscales between your min/max.","fix":"Separate scaling axis from infrastructure axis."},{"title":"Serverless doesn't scale","text":"Scaling is intrinsic and managed.","fix":"Pipelines even scale vertically; you may cap max workers."},{"title":"Warehouse scaling = worker scaling","text":"Warehouses add clusters for concurrency.","fix":"Know which thing each 'autoscaling' changes."},{"title":"All-purpose is interactive-only","text":"Jobs can use it; it's discouraged for production.","fix":"Interactive by design, not by restriction."},{"title":"Pools = autoscaling","text":"Pool = where VMs come from; autoscaling = how many.","fix":"Use both together."},{"title":"Pools on serverless","text":"Not available; serverless handles prewarming.","fix":"Prefer serverless over pools when compatible."},{"title":"Terminate = delete","text":"Termination releases VMs, keeps config.","fix":"Delete only to remove the configuration."},{"title":"Termination deletes tables","text":"Only ephemeral state is lost.","fix":"compute lifetime ≠ persistent data lifetime."},{"title":"CAN USE = SELECT everywhere","text":"Warehouse ACL is about compute only.","fix":"Also grant USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT."},{"title":"Standard/Dedicated on warehouses","text":"Not a valid axis for SQL warehouses.","fix":"Warehouse ACL + UC governance."},{"title":"CAN RUN means my permissions are used","text":"Pipelines execute as the run-as identity.","fix":"Grant data privileges to the run-as SP."},{"title":"CAN VIEW pipeline = SELECT on outputs","text":"Output tables are UC objects with their own ACL.","fix":"Grant UC privileges (and REFRESH if needed)."},{"title":"Personal run-as in production","text":"Default run-as is the creator.","fix":"Use a service principal for production pipelines."},{"title":"Standard/Dedicated is universal","text":"Serverless uses Default Interactive/Automated Compute with CAN USE/CAN MANAGE.","fix":"Know each compute's access model."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"What is a runtime environment?","a":"Everything code needs to execute: interpreter, JVM, Spark, libraries, native/system libs, config, dependency versions.","section":"ch08-s01"},{"q":"What does every DBR include?","a":"Apache Spark.","section":"ch08-s02"},{"q":"Runtime vs node type?","a":"Runtime = software environment; node type = hardware/VM.","section":"ch08-s02"},{"q":"LTS?","a":"Long-Term Support — longer stability horizon; DBR 18 LTS supported until June 2029 (source).","section":"ch08-s02"},{"q":"DBR 17.3 LTS ships which Spark?","a":"Spark 4.0.0.","section":"ch08-s02"},{"q":"DBR 18 LTS ships which Spark?","a":"Spark 4.1.0.","section":"ch08-s02"},{"q":"DBR ML?","a":"DBR + ML/DL stack (PyTorch, transformers, XGBoost, MLflow, GPU components).","section":"ch08-s03"},{"q":"Runtime on serverless?","a":"Databricks-managed & auto-upgraded; you configure environment version + dependencies.","section":"ch08-s03"},{"q":"Lakeflow pipeline runtime?","a":"Channels: current (≈DBR 17.3) and preview (≈DBR 18).","section":"ch08-s03"},{"q":"SQL warehouse runtime?","a":"Databricks SQL-managed runtime; no DBR selector.","section":"ch08-s03"},{"q":"Photon in one line?","a":"Databricks-native C++ vectorized execution engine for supported Spark SQL/DataFrame ops.","section":"ch08-s04"},{"q":"Does Photon replace Catalyst?","a":"No — Catalyst plans; Photon executes supported operators.","section":"ch08-s04"},{"q":"SIMD?","a":"Single Instruction, Multiple Data.","section":"ch08-s04"},{"q":"Unsupported operator in Photon?","a":"Falls back to Spark/JVM; query doesn't fail.","section":"ch08-s04"},{"q":"Where is Photon always enabled?","a":"Serverless compute, SQL warehouses, serverless pipelines.","section":"ch08-s05"},{"q":"Photon on GPU classic compute?","a":"Not supported.","section":"ch08-s05"},{"q":"Driver's job?","a":"Coordinator: notebook state, SparkContext, planning, tasks, scheduling, monitoring, results.","section":"ch08-s06"},{"q":"Driver type?","a":"The cloud VM instance type of the driver.","section":"ch08-s06"},{"q":"collect() danger?","a":"Brings all rows to the driver → driver OOM if larger than driver RAM.","section":"ch08-s06"},{"q":"Default driver type?","a":"Same as the worker type.","section":"ch08-s07"},{"q":"4 workers × 8 cores × 32 GB?","a":"32 cores, 128 GB aggregate (distributed).","section":"ch08-s07"},{"q":"Scale up vs out?","a":"Bigger machines vs more machines.","section":"ch08-s07"},{"q":"Classic autoscaling changes…","a":"The number of workers (type stays) — horizontal.","section":"ch08-s08"},{"q":"Serverless pipeline scaling?","a":"Horizontal + vertical, automatic; optional Max workers cap.","section":"ch08-s08"},{"q":"SQL warehouse scaling?","a":"Adds warehouse clusters for concurrency (min/max cluster counts).","section":"ch08-s08"},{"q":"All-purpose for jobs?","a":"Supported, discouraged for production; exceptions: iterative job testing, very short frequent jobs.","section":"ch08-s09"},{"q":"Instance pool?","a":"Reservoir of pre-provisioned cloud VMs for faster classic cluster starts.","section":"ch08-s10"},{"q":"Pool settings?","a":"Minimum idle, maximum capacity, idle instance auto-termination (+ preloaded DBR).","section":"ch08-s10"},{"q":"Pool vs autoscaling?","a":"Pool = where machines come from; autoscaling = how many.","section":"ch08-s10"},{"q":"Lost on termination?","a":"Executor memory, cached DFs, shuffle/temp files, Python/session state.","section":"ch08-s11"},{"q":"Auto Stop defaults?","a":"Serverless ~10 min; Pro/Classic ~45 min.","section":"ch08-s11"},{"q":"SQL warehouse 2 gates?","a":"Gate 1 warehouse ACL; Gate 2 Unity Catalog privileges.","section":"ch08-s12"},{"q":"CAN MONITOR on warehouse?","a":"Run queries + query history/profiles/monitoring, no config changes.","section":"ch08-s12"},{"q":"Pipeline 3 layers?","a":"Pipeline ACL → run-as identity → UC privileges of that identity.","section":"ch08-s13"},{"q":"Pipeline ACL levels?","a":"CAN VIEW, CAN RUN, CAN MANAGE, IS OWNER.","section":"ch08-s13"},{"q":"Default run-as?","a":"The creator; recommended: service principal for production.","section":"ch08-s13"},{"q":"Serverless access objects?","a":"Default Interactive Compute (notebooks/Databricks Connect), Default Automated Compute (Jobs/pipelines); CAN USE/CAN MANAGE.","section":"ch08-s13"},{"q":"Start sequence?","a":"Need compute → driver VM → min workers → DBR → Spark driver → executors → Photon → session → notebook attaches.","section":"ch08-s14"},{"q":"Classic vs serverless in one line?","a":"Classic: you configure the infrastructure envelope. Serverless: you specify the workload; Databricks manages it.","section":"ch08-s14"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch08-e001","type":"order","section":"ch08-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the layers from TOP (your code) to BOTTOM.","items":["Your workload (Python/SQL/Spark/ML/Pipeline)","Execution engine (Spark JVM and/or Photon)","Runtime (Databricks Runtime)","Compute nodes (driver + workers, with driver/worker types)","Infrastructure (classic / serverless)"],"explain":"Each setting lives on one layer. DBR is software under the engine; driver/worker types are VM shapes; classic/serverless is who runs the infrastructure."},{"id":"ch08-e002","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"DBR, Photon, driver type, pool, autoscaling and serverless are alternative choices on the same level.","answer":false,"explain":"They belong to different layers: engine, runtime, nodes, provisioning, scaling, infrastructure. The UI just shows them side by side."},{"id":"ch08-e003","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT part of a runtime environment?","options":["Python interpreter","JVM and Apache Spark","Dependency versions and configuration","The rows of your orders table"],"answer":3,"explain":"A runtime is the software environment the program executes in (interpreter, JVM, Spark, libraries, configs). Your table's rows are data."},{"id":"ch08-e004","type":"free","section":"ch08-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain what 'runtime' means and why the same program can behave differently in a different runtime.","model":"A runtime environment is everything the code needs to execute: interpreter, JVM, Spark, libraries, native/system libs, configuration and dependency versions. The source is only text; behaviour depends on the versions in the runtime, so moving from Python 3.12/numpy 2.x to Python 3.8/numpy 1.20 can change results or make the code fail.","rubric":["Runtime = execution environment (interpreter, libs, config, versions)","Code is just text","Different versions → different behaviour or failure"],"explain":"Understanding runtime as 'the stocked kitchen' explains why DBR versions and LTS matter."},{"id":"ch08-e005","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A program written for Python 3.12 + numpy 2.x is guaranteed to run identically on Python 3.8 + numpy 1.20.","answer":false,"explain":"Different runtime versions can change behaviour or make code fail — the motivation for pinned/LTS runtimes."},{"id":"ch08-e006","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What does every Databricks Runtime version include?","options":["A GPU driver","Apache Spark","Photon is always on","An instance pool"],"answer":1,"explain":"DBR is the set of core components on the compute; all DBR versions include Apache Spark. GPU software is in DBR ML; Photon and pools are separate settings."},{"id":"ch08-e007","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"DBR 18 LTS and r5.4xlarge are two versions of the same thing.","answer":false,"explain":"DBR = software stack; r5.4xlarge = cloud VM/hardware instance type. Runtime ≠ machine. (Final test Q1.)"},{"id":"ch08-e008","type":"match","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Laptop analogy: match the laptop part to its Databricks equivalent.","pairs":[["Ryzen 9, 32 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD","Worker node type"],["Ubuntu + Python + Java + Spark + libraries","Runtime (DBR)"],["The program you run","Your workload / code"]],"explain":"Hardware ≈ node type; installed software stack ≈ runtime. Not exactly an OS, but a good starting analogy."},{"id":"ch08-e009","type":"match","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each DBR to its Spark version (source snapshot).","pairs":[["19","Spark 4.2.0"],["18 LTS","Spark 4.1.0"],["17.3 LTS","Spark 4.0.0"],["16.4 LTS","Spark 3.5.2"],["15.4 LTS","Spark 3.5.0"]],"explain":"Different DBRs ship different Spark versions — one reason why changing DBR can change behaviour. (14.3 LTS also ships Spark 3.5.0.)"},{"id":"ch08-e010","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"What does **LTS** give a production pipeline?","options":["Faster queries","A longer support/stability horizon so things don't subtly change every few weeks","GPU support","Automatic Photon"],"answer":1,"explain":"LTS = Long-Term Support. For PostgreSQL → bronze → silver → gold → financial reporting you want stability; DBR 18 LTS is supported until June 2029 per the source."},{"id":"ch08-e011","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which runtime does Databricks generally advise?","buckets":["Newest / current DBR","LTS DBR","DBR ML"],"items":[{"text":"All-purpose development","bucket":0},{"text":"Learning / interactive work","bucket":0},{"text":"Operational production jobs","bucket":1},{"text":"Nightly financial reporting ETL","bucket":1},{"text":"XGBoost model training","bucket":2},{"text":"PyTorch deep learning","bucket":2}],"explain":"Dev → current; operational jobs → LTS; ML → DBR ML."},{"id":"ch08-e012","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Per the source, until which year is DBR 18 LTS supported?","answer":2029,"tolerance":0,"unit":"year","explain":"June 2029 — that long horizon is why LTS suits production."},{"id":"ch08-e013","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete.","text":"NODE TYPE = [[hardware|infrastructure|hardware/infrastructure]] configuration, while RUNTIME = [[software]] environment. LTS stands for [[Long-Term Support|long term support]].","explain":"Choosing m5.4xlarge answers 'what machine?'; choosing DBR 18 LTS answers 'what software on it?'."},{"id":"ch08-e014","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"You should use DBR ML for ordinary ETL because it includes more libraries and is therefore better.","answer":false,"explain":"DBR ML = DBR + ML/DL libraries (PyTorch, transformers, XGBoost, MLflow, GPU components). For ordinary ETL the normal DBR is usually more appropriate."},{"id":"ch08-e015","type":"match","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the use case to the usual runtime choice.","pairs":[["Learning / interactive development","Current DBR"],["Production Spark job","LTS DBR"],["GPU ML","DBR ML + GPU node"],["Legacy application","DBR compatible with the app"],["Serverless","No traditional DBR choice"]],"explain":"The beginner's runtime decision table. The last row is the most important: on serverless you don't choose a DBR."},{"id":"ch08-e016","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A colleague asks: \"Which DBR should I pin for my serverless job?\" Best answer?","options":["DBR 18 LTS","The newest DBR","Usually the wrong question — Databricks auto-upgrades and manages the runtime on serverless","DBR ML"],"answer":2,"explain":"For serverless Jobs, Databricks manages runtime version, instance types, memory and processing engines and upgrades automatically. You don't pin DBR like on classic."},{"id":"ch08-e017","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless means there is no runtime at all.","answer":false,"explain":"A runtime exists — the user just doesn't choose the exact traditional DBR. Serverless notebooks expose an Environment (environment version + dependencies) on top of a Databricks-managed runtime."},{"id":"ch08-e018","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Who decides the runtime?","buckets":["You choose a DBR","Databricks manages","Pipeline runtime channel"],"items":[{"text":"Classic all-purpose","bucket":0},{"text":"Classic jobs","bucket":0},{"text":"Serverless Jobs","bucket":1},{"text":"SQL warehouse (Databricks SQL runtime)","bucket":1},{"text":"Lakeflow pipeline (current/preview)","bucket":2}],"explain":"Classic → you pick DBR; serverless & warehouses → managed; pipelines → current/preview channels upgraded by Databricks."},{"id":"ch08-e019","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the pipeline runtime facts.","text":"Lakeflow pipelines use runtime [[channels]]: [[current]] and [[preview]]. Per the source, current is based on DBR [[17.3]] and preview on DBR [[18]].","bank":["LTS","stable","19"],"explain":"You don't pick 'DBR 18.0' for a pipeline like on an all-purpose cluster; Databricks upgrades the pipeline runtime with a rolling per-region rollout."},{"id":"ch08-e020","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What can you configure on a **serverless notebook's** Environment panel?","options":["The exact DBR version","Base environment / environment version and dependencies","Worker instance type","Access mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serverless gives a user-configurable application environment (libraries, dependencies, environment version) on top of a Databricks-managed runtime."},{"id":"ch08-e021","type":"odd","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Photon is a vectorized execution engine. Which statement about it is TRUE (the odd one out among the false ones)?","options":["Photon is a runtime version","Photon is a VM type","Photon executes supported operators natively in C++","Photon is a file format"],"answer":2,"explain":"Photon is not a runtime version, VM, database, file format or Spark-replacing programming model. It executes supported physical-plan operators natively in C++ on columnar batches."},{"id":"ch08-e022","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"When Photon is used, it replaces the Catalyst optimizer.","answer":false,"explain":"Catalyst still plans and optimizes. Photon takes over execution of supported operators. (Final test Q5.)"},{"id":"ch08-e023","type":"order","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the path of a DataFrame query with Photon ON.","items":["DataFrame API","Logical plan","Catalyst optimizer","Physical plan","Photon native engine (C++)","Vectorized batches on CPU/SIMD"],"explain":"Photon only enters after the physical plan; it replaces the JVM execution layer for supported operations."},{"id":"ch08-e024","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What does SIMD stand for, and why does Photon care?","options":["Single Instruction, Multiple Data — process many values per instruction on batches","Spark In-Memory Distribution — caching","Serverless Instance Management Daemon","Sequential Instruction, Many Disks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vectorized engines process batches (thousands of rows) and modern CPUs apply one instruction to many values at once."},{"id":"ch08-e025","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"To use Photon you must rewrite code as `photon.groupBy(...)`.","answer":false,"explain":"No code change: the same DataFrame/SQL code goes Spark API → Catalyst → Photon."},{"id":"ch08-e026","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A physical plan contains an operator Photon doesn't support. What happens?","options":["The whole query fails","That part falls back to Spark/JVM execution","Databricks switches to GPU","The cluster restarts without Photon"],"answer":1,"explain":"Photon and regular Spark/JVM execution can participate in the same workload — unsupported operations fall back."},{"id":"ch08-e027","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What is the alternative when Photon is OFF or unsupported?","options":["X-engine","Traditional Spark/JVM execution","Pandas","SQL warehouse"],"answer":1,"explain":"The main comparison is Spark traditional JVM execution vs Photon native execution. There's no third general engine. (Final test Q4.)"},{"id":"ch08-e028","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"The vectorized example computes `amount * 1.24`. For amount = 300, what's the result?","answer":372,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"","explain":"300 × 1.24 = 372. Row-at-a-time loads and computes each row; vectorized applies the multiplication to a whole batch at once."},{"id":"ch08-e029","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Photon: always on, or configurable?","buckets":["Enabled (managed)","On by default / configurable"],"items":[{"text":"Serverless compute","bucket":0},{"text":"SQL warehouse","bucket":0},{"text":"Serverless Lakeflow pipeline","bucket":0},{"text":"Classic all-purpose","bucket":1},{"text":"Classic jobs","bucket":1},{"text":"Classic pipeline","bucket":1}],"explain":"Serverless and warehouses: enabled. Classic all-purpose/jobs/pipelines: toggled via the Photon setting."},{"id":"ch08-e030","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Photon = GPU acceleration.","answer":false,"explain":"Photon is a CPU-native vectorized SQL/DataFrame engine. GPU is a hardware accelerator for deep learning/matrix ops/training. Classic GPU compute doesn't even support Photon."},{"id":"ch08-e031","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A compute can use DBR 18 LTS and Photon at the same time.","answer":true,"explain":"DBR = software environment; Photon = execution engine inside it. Not competing options. (Final test Q2.)"},{"id":"ch08-e032","type":"match","section":"ch08-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each term to its axis.","pairs":[["Photon / JVM","Execution engine"],["CPU / GPU","Hardware"],["DBR 18 LTS","Software environment"],["Catalyst","Planner"]],"explain":"Keeping axes apart prevents 'Photon = GPU' and 'DBR vs Photon' confusions."},{"id":"ch08-e033","type":"free","section":"ch08-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview: \"What is Photon and how does it relate to DBR?\"","model":"Photon is Databricks' native C++ vectorized execution engine for supported Spark SQL/DataFrame operations. Catalyst still plans the query; Photon executes supported operators on columnar batches using SIMD, falling back to JVM Spark for unsupported ones. It lives inside the DBR's execution layer — DBR is the software environment, Photon the engine — so a compute can run DBR 18 LTS with Photon on. It needs no code changes and is not GPU acceleration.","rubric":["Native C++ vectorized engine","Catalyst still plans","Fallback to JVM","Inside DBR — not competing","No code change; not GPU"],"explain":"A complete answer separates engine, planner, runtime and hardware."},{"id":"ch08-e034","type":"spotbug","section":"ch08-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which line in this classic compute plan is invalid?","lines":["Purpose: All-purpose","Hardware: GPU (deep learning)","Runtime: DBR 18 LTS ML","Photon: Enabled","Access: Dedicated"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"Photon: Disabled (not supported on classic GPU compute)","explain":"Classic GPU compute doesn't support Photon. Everything else is a sensible DL configuration."},{"id":"ch08-e035","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What is the driver's role?","options":["Stores the Delta files","Coordinator: holds notebook state & SparkContext, builds plans/tasks, schedules them on workers","Runs only the Photon engine","Is a backup worker"],"answer":1,"explain":"The driver interprets commands, plans, builds stages/tasks, schedules and monitors them, and returns results to the notebook. Workers do the bulk distributed processing."},{"id":"ch08-e036","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Driver 8 GB, workers 10 × 64 GB. What's the total **worker** RAM (GB)?","answer":640,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB","explain":"10 × 64 = 640 GB — but collect() needs the result to fit in the 8 GB driver, not in 640 GB."},{"id":"ch08-e037","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"With 20 workers × 64 GB and an 8 GB driver, you can safely `collect()` a 100 GB result.","answer":false,"explain":"collect() gathers the result on the driver, which has 8 GB → driver OOM. (Final test Q8.)"},{"id":"ch08-e038","type":"order","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order what the driver does for `display(result)`.","items":["Receive the notebook command","Build the logical plan","Analyze/optimize the plan","Construct stages/tasks","Schedule tasks on workers","Monitor execution","Send output to the notebook"],"explain":"Plan → tasks → schedule → monitor → return. The driver coordinates; workers execute."},{"id":"ch08-e039","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What is **driver type**?","options":["A different Spark software for the driver","The cloud VM instance type used for the driver (e.g. m5.2xlarge)","Standard vs Dedicated","Python vs Scala driver"],"answer":1,"explain":"Driver type = VM shape of the Spark driver/coordinator. Names differ per cloud. (Final test Q7.)"},{"id":"ch08-e040","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT listed as a driver-memory stressor?","options":["Large collect() results","Large plans / too much task metadata","Many attached notebook sessions and local Python objects","Many partitions spilled by executors to local disk"],"answer":3,"explain":"Executor spill is a worker-side issue. Driver stressors: collected data, large plans, task metadata, many sessions, local Python objects."},{"id":"ch08-e041","type":"spotbug","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Driver: 8 GB. Workers: 10 × 64 GB. Which line will most likely crash the driver?","lines":["huge_df = spark.table(\"prod.sales.events\")","agg = huge_df.groupBy(\"country\").count()","rows = huge_df.collect()","display(agg)"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"display(agg)  # or aggregate/write on workers instead of collecting the huge DataFrame","explain":"collect() moves every row from the workers to the 8 GB driver. The aggregation runs distributed and returns a small result."},{"id":"ch08-e042","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"A junior says: \"The driver crashed with OOM on `huge_df.collect()`, but we have 640 GB of RAM!\" (driver 8 GB, 10 × 64 GB workers)","steps":[{"prompt":"What's the flaw in the reasoning?","options":[{"text":"collect() brings data to the driver, which has only 8 GB; worker RAM doesn't help","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly — driver and worker RAM are different axes."},{"text":"The workers must be too small","ok":false,"fb":"640 GB of worker RAM is irrelevant to what lands on the driver."},{"text":"Photon should have been off","ok":false,"fb":"Photon has nothing to do with driver memory for collect()."}]},{"prompt":"If the team truly needs lots of data on the driver, which setting changes?","options":[{"text":"A larger driver type","ok":true,"fb":"Databricks recommends a larger driver when you expect much collected data."},{"text":"More workers","ok":false,"fb":"Scale-out adds worker RAM, not driver RAM."},{"text":"An instance pool","ok":false,"fb":"Pools speed up provisioning, not memory."}]}],"explain":"Driver type and worker type are separate parameters for a reason. Avoid collecting big data; if unavoidable, size the driver."},{"id":"ch08-e043","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"4 workers, each 8 cores / 32 GB. Total worker CPU cores?","answer":32,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cores","explain":"4 × 8 = 32 worker cores."},{"id":"ch08-e044","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Same cluster: aggregate worker RAM in GB?","answer":128,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB","explain":"4 × 32 = 128 GB — distributed across 4 machines, not one 128 GB box."},{"id":"ch08-e045","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"4 workers × 32 GB behave like a single 128 GB machine.","answer":false,"explain":"The memory is aggregate but distributed; each executor still has only its own node's RAM."},{"id":"ch08-e046","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which resource is the workload most likely to pressure?","buckets":["CPU","RAM","Network / local disk"],"items":[{"text":"CPU-heavy transformations","bucket":0},{"text":"Large join","bucket":1},{"text":"Caching a big DataFrame","bucket":1},{"text":"Wide aggregation","bucket":1},{"text":"Huge shuffle","bucket":2}],"explain":"Different node families give different CPU/RAM/disk/network ratios; there's no best worker type — it's workload-dependent. (A huge shuffle also pressures memory.)"},{"id":"ch08-e047","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"By default, the driver type is the same as the worker type, but they can differ.","answer":true,"explain":"Driver and worker type are two separate classic parameters; default is the same VM shape."},{"id":"ch08-e048","type":"match","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Scale up or scale out?","pairs":[["Worker 8 CPU → 32 CPU","Scale up"],["4 workers → 16 workers","Scale out"],["Spark's primary design","Distributed scale-out"],["Autoscaling min/max workers","Horizontal scaling"]],"explain":"Scale up = bigger machines; scale out = more machines. Spark favours scale-out but node sizing still matters."},{"id":"ch08-e049","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Spark UI: all workers CPU-saturated, little/no spill, memory OK, huge CPU processing time.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's the bottleneck?","options":[{"text":"Worker CPU","ok":true,"fb":"Saturated workers with healthy memory = CPU-bound."},{"text":"The driver","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing points to the driver."},{"text":"Network shuffle","ok":false,"fb":"No spill or shuffle symptoms are described."}]},{"prompt":"Which option is NOT a good response?","options":[{"text":"Get a bigger driver","ok":true,"fb":"Correct to reject — the driver isn't the bottleneck."},{"text":"More workers or larger/CPU-oriented workers","ok":false,"fb":"That's a valid response, so it's not the 'NOT good' option."},{"text":"Enable Photon / optimize the query","ok":false,"fb":"Also valid for CPU-heavy SQL/DataFrame work."}]}],"explain":"Match the fix to the layer: worker CPU problems → more/stronger workers, Photon, query optimization — never 'bigger driver' by reflex."},{"id":"ch08-e050","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Spark UI shows massive spill to disk and executor OOMs. Which response is LEAST relevant?","options":["More worker memory / larger workers","Better partitioning, less skew, different join","More workers","A bigger driver"],"answer":3,"explain":"Spill and executor OOM are worker-side. Driver size isn't necessarily the answer."},{"id":"ch08-e051","type":"order","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order your reasoning when Spark UI shows a performance problem.","items":["Read the symptom in Spark UI (CPU? spill? executor OOM?)","Decide which layer is the bottleneck (driver vs workers)","Pick the fix for that layer (workers/memory/partitioning/Photon/query)","Only upsize the driver if the driver is the bottleneck (e.g. collect)"],"explain":"Symptom → layer → targeted fix. 'Bigger driver' is a reflex that rarely helps worker bottlenecks."},{"id":"ch08-e052","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Classic cluster min 2 / max 10, currently 2 workers. Autoscaler wants 4 more. How many workers after scaling?","answer":6,"tolerance":0,"unit":"workers","explain":"2 + 4 = 6, within the 2–10 bounds. The worker type stays the same; only the count changes."},{"id":"ch08-e053","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Autoscaling means serverless.","answer":false,"explain":"Classic clusters can autoscale between min and max. (Final test Q9.)"},{"id":"ch08-e054","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Can you set min=2 / max=20 workers on an ordinary **serverless Job**?","options":["Yes, under Advanced","No — not with the classic cluster model; Databricks manages scaling/resources automatically","Only with Dedicated access","Only with Photon off"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serverless Jobs have autoscaling and Photon automatically, but no classic min/max worker settings. (Final test Q10.)"},{"id":"ch08-e055","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless compute has no scaling at all.","answer":false,"explain":"Scaling is intrinsic/platform-managed on serverless — just not a user-selected fixed/autoscaling axis. (Final test Q11.)"},{"id":"ch08-e056","type":"match","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each autoscaling kind to what it scales.","pairs":[["Classic Spark autoscaling","Number of workers"],["SQL warehouse scaling","Number of warehouse clusters (concurrency)"],["Serverless compute","Managed infrastructure"],["Serverless pipeline","Horizontal + vertical (machine size)"]],"explain":"Same word, four meanings. Don't mix 'warehouse clusters for concurrency' with 'Spark workers 2–10'."},{"id":"ch08-e057","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless Lakeflow pipeline: what can you still set regarding scaling?","options":["Worker instance type","A Max workers upper bound for cost/capacity","Min/max driver size","Nothing — and it can't scale"],"answer":1,"explain":"Databricks does horizontal + vertical autoscaling (e.g. after OOM, a bigger instance next update). You may cap Max workers, but you don't do the full classic worker configuration. (Final test Q22–23.)"},{"id":"ch08-e058","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless pipelines can scale vertically: after an OOM Databricks may pick a larger worker/driver instance for the next update.","answer":true,"explain":"Vertical autoscaling = larger/smaller machine types, chosen automatically by Databricks."},{"id":"ch08-e059","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete.","text":"Classic autoscaling is [[horizontal]] scaling: the [[number|count]] of workers changes, the worker [[type]] stays. A SQL warehouse scales the number of warehouse [[clusters]] for concurrency; serverless SQL warehouses use [[Intelligent Workload Management|IWM]].","bank":["vertical","driver","Photon"],"explain":"Know which thing each 'autoscaling' changes."},{"id":"ch08-e060","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"All-purpose compute is technically restricted to notebooks.","answer":false,"explain":"It can be used by Jobs in supported cases, but Databricks doesn't recommend it for production jobs. Interactive by design, not by restriction. (Final test Q15.)"},{"id":"ch08-e061","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which reasons explain why all-purpose isn't recommended for production jobs? (choose all)","options":["Different billing model","Resource contention with other users","Can stay idle while running","Less predictable production behaviour","It cannot read Unity Catalog tables"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"Billing, contention, idle lifecycle and predictability (Final test Q16). Reading UC tables is not the issue."},{"id":"ch08-e062","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Is using all-purpose for a job reasonable here?","buckets":["Reasonable exception","Not recommended"],"items":[{"text":"Iterative development/testing of a job","bucket":0},{"text":"Very short, very frequent jobs where startup latency hurts","bucket":0},{"text":"Nightly production revenue ETL","bucket":1},{"text":"Monthly finance close pipeline","bucket":1},{"text":"Production job on the team's shared dev cluster","bucket":1}],"explain":"Databricks lists limited exceptions: iterative job dev/testing (avoid repeated startup) and very short frequent jobs. Production → jobs compute."},{"id":"ch08-e063","type":"free","section":"ch08-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"\"Isn't all-purpose restricted to interactive mode?\" Answer like in an interview.","model":"No. All-purpose is interactive by design — it lives independently of tasks while people attach notebooks, think, rerun and debug — but it's not a technical restriction. Jobs can run on existing all-purpose compute in supported cases. Databricks discourages it for production because of billing, resource contention (a developer's crossJoin can hurt prod ETL), idle lifecycle and unpredictability. Exceptions: iterative job testing and very short frequent jobs where startup latency matters.","rubric":["Not a technical restriction","Supported but discouraged for production","Reasons: billing, contention, idle, predictability","Exceptions: iterative testing, short frequent jobs"],"explain":"The key phrase is 'interactive by design, not interactive-only'."},{"id":"ch08-e064","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which code line in a developer's notebook is the source's example of something that can hurt a production job sharing the cluster?","options":["df.limit(10).show()","df.crossJoin(other_huge_df).count()","spark.sql(\"SELECT 1\")","dbutils.fs.ls(\"/\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"A huge cross join eats shared CPU, RAM, executors and shuffle bandwidth — exactly why shared all-purpose is risky for production."},{"id":"ch08-e065","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What is an **instance pool**?","options":["A thread pool in the driver","A reservoir of already-provisioned cloud VMs that classic compute can grab quickly","A pool of Spark executors shared across clusters","A connection pool to SQL warehouses"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pools are about VM provisioning speed. Not thread, connection, data or executor pools. (Final test Q12.)"},{"id":"ch08-e066","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Pools and autoscaling are the same thing.","answer":false,"explain":"Autoscaling decides HOW MANY nodes the cluster wants; the pool decides WHERE they come from (and provides them faster). (Final test Q13.)"},{"id":"ch08-e067","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Pool: minimum idle = 3, max capacity = 20. Cluster A takes 2 idle VMs. How many idle VMs remain immediately, before the pool replenishes?","answer":1,"tolerance":0,"unit":"VMs","explain":"3 − 2 = 1. Then the pool tries to restore its minimum idle capacity back to 3."},{"id":"ch08-e068","type":"order","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order cluster startup WITHOUT a pool.","items":["User starts cluster","Databricks asks the cloud for VMs","Cloud provisions VMs","VMs boot","DBR install/start","Cluster ready"],"explain":"Every step costs time; pools skip provisioning/boot (and preloaded DBR skips runtime loading)."},{"id":"ch08-e069","type":"match","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each pool setting/feature to its effect.","pairs":[["Minimum idle","How many warm VMs the pool tries to keep ready"],["Maximum capacity","Upper limit on instances in the pool"],["Idle instance auto-termination","Releases idle VMs after a while"],["Preloaded DBR","Runtime already cached on idle VMs"]],"explain":"These are the pool settings Databricks defines; preloaded DBR makes startup very fast."},{"id":"ch08-e070","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"On serverless you choose an instance pool to speed up startup.","answer":false,"explain":"No pools on serverless — prewarming and capacity are Databricks' problem. Docs recommend serverless instead of pools when supported."},{"id":"ch08-e071","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Queries are excellent once the classic cluster is running, but every start takes ~4 minutes and users complain.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you suspect?","options":[{"text":"VM provisioning + runtime startup latency","ok":true,"fb":"Startup, not query performance, is the problem."},{"text":"Too little worker RAM","ok":false,"fb":"Query performance is already excellent — RAM isn't it."},{"text":"Missing SELECT privileges","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions would cause errors, not slow startup."}]},{"prompt":"Which classic option reduces it?","options":[{"text":"An instance pool (ideally with preloaded DBR)","ok":true,"fb":"Warm VMs + cached runtime = fast launch."},{"text":"Bigger worker type","ok":false,"fb":"Bigger VMs don't provision faster."},{"text":"Photon","ok":false,"fb":"Photon speeds execution, not startup."}]},{"prompt":"And the even better option if the workload is compatible?","options":[{"text":"Serverless","ok":true,"fb":"You stop managing the provisioning problem at all."},{"text":"Dedicated access mode","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode doesn't affect startup."},{"text":"DBR ML","ok":false,"fb":"A heavier runtime doesn't help startup."}]}],"explain":"Slow start ≠ slow queries. Pools (with preloaded DBR) or serverless attack provisioning latency."},{"id":"ch08-e072","type":"order","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the 'cluster slow to start' playbook.","items":["Is the slowness at startup or during queries?","Is this classic compute provisioning VMs from the cloud each time?","Could a pool with preloaded DBR serve warm VMs?","Is the workload serverless-compatible, so provisioning disappears entirely?"],"explain":"Separate startup from runtime performance first; then pool, then serverless."},{"id":"ch08-e073","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the slogan.","text":"POOL = [[where]] machines come from. AUTOSCALING = how [[many]] machines the compute currently wants.","explain":"Two independent concepts that cooperate: the autoscaler asks, the pool serves (or asks the cloud)."},{"id":"ch08-e074","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Lost or kept when an all-purpose cluster terminates?","buckets":["Lost (ephemeral)","Kept (persistent)"],"items":[{"text":"Cached DataFrames","bucket":0},{"text":"Python variables / session state","bucket":0},{"text":"Shuffle & local temp files","bucket":0},{"text":"Delta table in S3/ADLS/GCS","bucket":1},{"text":"Unity Catalog metadata","bucket":1},{"text":"Notebook source code","bucket":1},{"text":"Compute configuration (runtime, worker type…)","bucket":1}],"explain":"Compute lifetime ≠ persistent data lifetime. Termination releases VMs and ephemeral state; config, tables, metadata, code and job definitions stay. (Final test Q14.)"},{"id":"ch08-e075","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Terminating a classic all-purpose compute deletes its configuration.","answer":false,"explain":"Terminate ≠ delete. VMs are released; name, runtime, worker type, autoscaling and settings remain, and you can press Start later."},{"id":"ch08-e076","type":"match","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match compute to its termination model.","pairs":[["All-purpose","Idle-time based auto termination"],["Classic Jobs compute","Job-lifecycle based"],["SQL warehouse","Auto Stop"],["Serverless notebook","Managed by Databricks"]],"explain":"Similar lifecycle ideas, different product settings."},{"id":"ch08-e077","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Typical default Auto Stop for a **Serverless** SQL warehouse (minutes)?","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"min","explain":"~10 min for serverless vs ~45 min for Pro/Classic, with different minimums."},{"id":"ch08-e078","type":"calc","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Typical default Auto Stop for a **Pro/Classic** SQL warehouse (minutes)?","answer":45,"tolerance":0,"unit":"min","explain":"45 min by default — 4.5× the serverless default, a cost difference worth knowing."},{"id":"ch08-e079","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Finance asks why the dev cluster `etl-development` cost money all weekend although nobody worked.","steps":[{"prompt":"First question?","options":[{"text":"Is it an all-purpose cluster and is auto termination configured?","ok":true,"fb":"All-purpose stays up while idle unless auto termination stops it."},{"text":"Did the Delta tables grow?","ok":false,"fb":"Storage isn't what keeps a cluster billing."},{"text":"Is Photon on?","ok":false,"fb":"Photon doesn't keep a cluster alive."}]},{"prompt":"Auto termination is OFF. What else could keep it busy even if ON?","options":[{"text":"A scheduled job or running notebook attached to it","ok":true,"fb":"Activity resets the idle timer — e.g. a job wrongly pointed at all-purpose."},{"text":"The cluster's DBR is LTS","ok":false,"fb":"Runtime choice doesn't affect idleness."},{"text":"Unity Catalog grants","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions don't keep compute alive."}]},{"prompt":"Best fix?","options":[{"text":"Enable auto termination (e.g. 30 min) and move scheduled work to Jobs compute","ok":true,"fb":"Idle-based termination for interactive; lifecycle-based compute for jobs."},{"text":"Delete the cluster configuration every Friday","ok":false,"fb":"Terminate ≠ delete; you'd lose the config for nothing."},{"text":"Switch to DBR ML","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to cost from idleness."}]}],"explain":"All-purpose = idle-time based termination; jobs compute = lifecycle based. Configure the former, use the latter for scheduled work."},{"id":"ch08-e080","type":"order","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order all-purpose auto termination.","items":["Active (commands running)","Idle (no commands)","Configured idle time passes (e.g. 30 min)","Terminate"],"explain":"Inactivity-based automatic termination is a classic compute configuration."},{"id":"ch08-e081","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"After the cluster auto-terminated over lunch, Nikos re-runs a cell and gets `NameError: name 'orders_df' is not defined`. He's worried the orders table is gone.","steps":[{"prompt":"What happened?","options":[{"text":"Termination wiped ephemeral session state (variables, cached DataFrames); the table is fine","ok":true,"fb":"Python state lives on compute; Delta tables live in cloud storage."},{"text":"The Delta table was deleted with the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Compute lifetime ≠ persistent data lifetime."},{"text":"Unity Catalog revoked his SELECT","ok":false,"fb":"That would be a permission error, not NameError."}]},{"prompt":"What should he do?","options":[{"text":"Restart/attach and re-run the cells that define orders_df (re-read the table)","ok":true,"fb":"Recreate ephemeral state from persistent data."},{"text":"Restore the table with time travel","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing happened to the table."},{"text":"Ask an admin to recover the cluster's RAM","ok":false,"fb":"Ephemeral memory is gone by design."}]}],"explain":"What dies with compute: memory, caches, shuffle/temp files, Python state. What survives: tables, UC metadata, notebooks, job definitions."},{"id":"ch08-e082","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"CAN USE on a SQL warehouse = SELECT on all tables.","answer":false,"explain":"CAN USE is a permission on the compute only. Unity Catalog data privileges are separate. (Final test Q17.)"},{"id":"ch08-e083","type":"match","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the warehouse ACL level to what it allows.","pairs":[["CAN VIEW","See the warehouse / details"],["CAN USE","Run queries on it"],["CAN MONITOR","Query history/profiles/monitoring + run queries"],["CAN MANAGE","Change configuration and permissions"],["IS OWNER","Ownership / admin-like control"]],"explain":"Gate 1 levels. None of them grants table data — that's Gate 2 (UC)."},{"id":"ch08-e084","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Nikos runs `SELECT * FROM prod.finance.payroll;` on `analytics-wh` and gets PERMISSION_DENIED. He insists: \"But I have CAN USE on the warehouse!\"","steps":[{"prompt":"Which gate is failing?","options":[{"text":"Gate 2 — Unity Catalog data privileges","ok":true,"fb":"He could submit the query, so Gate 1 (warehouse) is open."},{"text":"Gate 1 — the warehouse ACL","ok":false,"fb":"With CAN USE he can use the warehouse; the denial is about data."},{"text":"Neither — the warehouse is too small","ok":false,"fb":"Size never produces PERMISSION_DENIED."}]},{"prompt":"Which privileges must he have on the data path?","options":[{"text":"USE CATALOG on prod, USE SCHEMA on prod.finance, SELECT on payroll","ok":true,"fb":"All three are needed for the query."},{"text":"CAN MANAGE on the warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"More warehouse power doesn't grant data."},{"text":"Only SELECT on payroll","ok":false,"fb":"He also needs USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA on the parents."}]},{"prompt":"Who should fix it?","options":[{"text":"The data owner / UC admin grants the UC privileges (if appropriate)","ok":true,"fb":"Data access is governed in Unity Catalog."},{"text":"The warehouse owner gives him IS OWNER","ok":false,"fb":"Ownership of compute doesn't open data."},{"text":"Switch to a Dedicated warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"Warehouses have no Dedicated mode."}]}],"explain":"Warehouse ACL + Unity Catalog permissions = query possible. Permission to use compute ≠ permission to use data."},{"id":"ch08-e085","type":"order","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the checks for PERMISSION_DENIED on a SQL warehouse query.","items":["Gate 1: can I use the warehouse (CAN USE+)?","Is the error about the warehouse or a data object?","USE CATALOG on the catalog?","USE SCHEMA on the schema?","SELECT on the table?"],"explain":"Compute gate first, then the UC path top-down: catalog → schema → table."},{"id":"ch08-e086","type":"write","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the Unity Catalog grants so `nikos@company.com` can run `SELECT * FROM prod.finance.payroll` (he already has CAN USE on the warehouse).","solution":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `nikos@company.com`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.finance TO `nikos@company.com`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.finance.payroll TO `nikos@company.com`;","keywords":["use catalog","on catalog prod","use schema","on schema prod.finance","grant select","prod.finance.payroll"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Gate 2 needs all three: USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA on the parents plus SELECT on the table. No warehouse permission can replace these."},{"id":"ch08-e087","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`analysts` have CAN USE on `analytics-wh` and SELECT on `prod.sales.*` only. Which query succeeds?","options":["SELECT * FROM prod.hr.salary","SELECT * FROM prod.sales.orders","Both — same warehouse","Neither — they need CAN MANAGE"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same warehouse, different data permissions: sales ✅, hr ❌."},{"id":"ch08-e088","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A performance engineer needs query history, query profiles and warehouse monitoring, but must NOT change size, scaling or permissions. Which level?","options":["CAN VIEW","CAN MONITOR","CAN MANAGE","IS OWNER"],"answer":1,"explain":"CAN MONITOR allows running queries plus monitoring/query-profile capabilities, without configuration rights."},{"id":"ch08-e089","type":"spotbug","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which line of this warehouse request is invalid?","lines":["Warehouse type: Serverless SQL","Size: Medium","Scaling: min 1, max 3 clusters","Access mode: Dedicated","Permissions: analysts CAN USE"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"Remove 'Access mode' — use warehouse ACL + Unity Catalog grants instead.","explain":"SQL warehouses don't have the Standard/Dedicated selector; access is warehouse ACL + UC governance. (Final test Q21.)"},{"id":"ch08-e090","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless SQL Warehouse + Dedicated access mode is a valid combination.","answer":false,"explain":"Not a valid axis — warehouses have their own warehouse ACL + UC security model. (Final test Q21.)"},{"id":"ch08-e091","type":"order","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the three layers of pipeline access.","items":["Pipeline ACL — can you access/control the pipeline object?","Run-as identity — whose identity executes the pipeline?","Unity Catalog privileges — what data can that identity access?"],"explain":"Object → identity → data. Mnemonic: **ORD** (Object, Run-as, Data)."},{"id":"ch08-e092","type":"match","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the pipeline ACL level to its capability.","pairs":[["CAN VIEW","See pipeline / details"],["CAN RUN","Start/stop a pipeline update"],["CAN MANAGE","Edit settings + permissions + run"],["IS OWNER","Owner"]],"explain":"Layer 1 controls the pipeline object, not the data it reads or writes."},{"id":"ch08-e093","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Maria has CAN RUN on a pipeline but no SELECT on its source table. She can still trigger a successful run.","answer":true,"explain":"Yes — if the pipeline's run-as identity has the required source/target privileges. CAN RUN lets her start it; the run-as identity's privileges let it execute. (Final test Q18.)"},{"id":"ch08-e094","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"CAN VIEW on a pipeline = SELECT on its output tables.","answer":false,"explain":"Pipeline object ACL and Unity Catalog data ACL are different. Output tables are UC objects with their own permissions. (Final test Q19.)"},{"id":"ch08-e095","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which identity does a production pipeline use to read sources and write targets?","options":["Whoever clicks Start","The configured run-as principal — usually a service principal","The workspace admin","The SQL warehouse owner"],"answer":1,"explain":"Updates execute as the run-as identity. Default = creator; Databricks recommends a service principal for production. (Final test Q20.)"},{"id":"ch08-e096","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You have CAN RUN on `orders_pipeline`. You click Start; the update begins but fails reading `prod.raw.orders` with a permission error. You personally CAN read that table.","steps":[{"prompt":"Layer 1 — is the pipeline ACL the problem?","options":[{"text":"No — the update started, so CAN RUN works","ok":true,"fb":"Layer 1 is fine; move on."},{"text":"Yes — I need CAN MANAGE","ok":false,"fb":"CAN RUN is enough to start/stop updates."},{"text":"Yes — I need IS OWNER","ok":false,"fb":"Ownership isn't needed to run."}]},{"prompt":"Layer 2 — what do you check next?","options":[{"text":"The pipeline's run-as identity","ok":true,"fb":"The update runs as that identity, not as you — your own SELECT is irrelevant."},{"text":"My own SELECT privilege again","ok":false,"fb":"You already have it; the pipeline doesn't use it unless it runs as you."},{"text":"The pipeline's Photon setting","ok":false,"fb":"Photon doesn't cause permission errors."}]},{"prompt":"Run-as = `pipeline-prod-sp`. Layer 3 — what's likely missing?","options":[{"text":"USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT on the source for the service principal","ok":true,"fb":"Grant least privilege to the SP on the data path."},{"text":"CAN ATTACH TO on the pipeline's cluster","ok":false,"fb":"No such cluster permission for serverless pipelines; and it's data that's denied."},{"text":"CAN MONITOR on a SQL warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated compute."}]}],"explain":"Three layers: object ACL, run-as identity, UC privileges of that identity. Debug them in order (ORD)."},{"id":"ch08-e097","type":"order","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'pipeline runs fail although I can run it'.","items":["Did the update actually start (Layer 1 OK)?","Who is the run-as identity?","Does it have USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA on sources/target?","Does it have SELECT on every source table?","Does it have CREATE TABLE / CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW on the target schema?"],"explain":"Object → identity → data, then data top-down: containers, sources, target creation rights."},{"id":"ch08-e098","type":"write","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write least-privilege grants for `pipeline-prod-sp` to read `prod.raw.orders` and create tables/materialized views in `prod.silver`.","solution":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.raw TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.raw.orders TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA, CREATE TABLE, CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;","keywords":["use catalog","use schema","select on table prod.raw.orders","create table","create materialized view","prod.silver"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Layer 3 for the run-as SP: containers (USE CATALOG/SCHEMA), SELECT on sources, CREATE TABLE/MATERIALIZED VIEW on the target schema — and nothing more."},{"id":"ch08-e099","type":"spotbug","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The pipeline SP still can't read `prod.raw.orders`. Which grant is missing/wrong?","lines":["GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;","GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.raw.orders TO `nikos@company.com`;","GRANT USE SCHEMA, CREATE TABLE ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.raw TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.raw.orders TO `pipeline-prod-sp`;","explain":"SELECT was granted to Nikos, not to the run-as identity — and the SP also lacks USE SCHEMA on prod.raw. The pipeline runs as the SP, so grants must target it."},{"id":"ch08-e100","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Maria (CAN VIEW on the pipeline, not admin) wants to open the backing pipeline from the `prod.gold.revenue` materialized view and read it. What may she need?","options":["Nothing — CAN VIEW covers it","SELECT on prod.gold.revenue (plus USE CATALOG/SCHEMA) and possibly REFRESH on that object","CAN MANAGE on the pipeline","CAN USE on Default Automated Compute"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pipeline ACL ≠ data ACL. Reading needs UC privileges; opening the backing pipeline from a ST/MV may also need REFRESH for non-admins."},{"id":"ch08-e101","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"On a serverless pipeline, you grant users CAN ATTACH TO on its cluster.","answer":false,"explain":"Serverless pipelines have no user-managed cluster sizing/configuration/security — there's nothing to attach to."},{"id":"ch08-e102","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement about **classic pipeline** compute is correct?","options":["It's an ordinary all-purpose cluster you can attach notebooks to","You get more infra options, but the pipeline manages the compute lifecycle and runtime (no free Spark version pinning)","It has no infrastructure options at all","It requires Dedicated access"],"answer":1,"explain":"Classic pipelines expose some infrastructure choices, but lifecycle and runtime are pipeline-managed."},{"id":"ch08-e103","type":"match","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the serverless access object to what it controls.","pairs":[["Default Interactive Compute","Serverless notebooks / Databricks Connect"],["Default Automated Compute","Serverless Jobs / Lakeflow pipelines"],["CAN USE","Allowed to use that serverless compute"],["CAN MANAGE","Manage it, incl. who may use it"]],"explain":"Serverless has access management — just not Standard/Dedicated. Admins can restrict which users/groups use serverless."},{"id":"ch08-e104","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A new analyst can't run any notebook on serverless compute, while colleagues can.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check?","options":[{"text":"Whether she has CAN USE on Default Interactive Compute","ok":true,"fb":"That object controls serverless notebooks/Databricks Connect access."},{"text":"Her Standard/Dedicated access mode","ok":false,"fb":"Not a serverless concept."},{"text":"Her CAN USE on the SQL warehouse","ok":false,"fb":"Different compute."}]},{"prompt":"And for her scheduled serverless jobs?","options":[{"text":"Default Automated Compute (CAN USE)","ok":true,"fb":"It governs serverless Jobs and Lakeflow pipelines."},{"text":"Default Interactive Compute again","ok":false,"fb":"That's for interactive use."},{"text":"Pipeline CAN VIEW","ok":false,"fb":"Object ACL of a pipeline, not serverless compute access."}]}],"explain":"Standard/Dedicated is not a universal access axis; serverless uses Default Interactive / Default Automated Compute with CAN USE / CAN MANAGE."},{"id":"ch08-e105","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the run-as rules.","text":"By default a pipeline runs as its [[creator]], but run-as can be changed to another user or a [[service principal]]. Databricks recommends a service principal for [[production]].","bank":["admin","group","development"],"explain":"Production pipelines shouldn't depend on a person's account and permissions."},{"id":"ch08-e106","type":"order","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order what happens when you press **Start** on a classic all-purpose cluster.","items":["Need compute","Get driver VM (possibly from pool)","Get min worker VMs","Start/install DBR","Start Spark driver","Start Spark executors","Photon available/enabled","Spark session ready","Notebook attaches"],"explain":"Machines first, then software, then Spark processes, then the session your notebook uses."},{"id":"ch08-e107","type":"match","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Read the configuration: match each field to its layer.","pairs":[["All-purpose","Lifecycle / purpose"],["Classic","Infrastructure management"],["Standard","Access / security mode"],["DBR 18 LTS","Software environment"],["r6i.2xlarge driver","Driver hardware"],["min 2 / max 8","Horizontal scaling policy"],["Pool","VM provisioning source"]],"explain":"Each parameter has a different place in the architecture."},{"id":"ch08-e108","type":"order","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the path of `df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` after the cluster is up.","items":["Notebook","Driver","Catalyst","Physical plan","Photon","Tasks","Workers","Partitions"],"explain":"The whole chain: notebook → driver plans with Catalyst → Photon executes tasks on workers over partitions."},{"id":"ch08-e109","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"In the Spark compute matrix, which is TRUE for **Serverless Notebook**?","options":["Pool ✅","Min/max autoscale ✅","Photon enabled, scaling managed, no Standard/Dedicated selector","You choose driver type"],"answer":2,"explain":"Serverless notebook: DBR/driver/worker managed, Photon enabled, automatic scaling managed, no pool, access mode not a selector."},{"id":"ch08-e110","type":"bucket","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Classic pipeline or serverless pipeline?","buckets":["Classic pipeline","Serverless pipeline"],"items":[{"text":"Legacy Hive metastore possible","bucket":0},{"text":"Unity Catalog required","bucket":1},{"text":"Instance types configurable","bucket":0},{"text":"Vertical scaling automatic","bucket":1},{"text":"Some classic controls (pool/custom settings)","bucket":0},{"text":"Photon always enabled","bucket":1}],"explain":"Both share pipeline ACL and run-as; they differ on infrastructure control, UC requirement, Hive and scaling."},{"id":"ch08-e111","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which row is ❌ for ALL three SQL warehouse types?","options":["Photon","Warehouse ACL","DBR selector and Standard/Dedicated","Auto Stop"],"answer":2,"explain":"No warehouse has a DBR selector or Standard/Dedicated. All have Photon, size, concurrent cluster scaling, warehouse ACL, UC data ACL and Auto Stop; IWM is serverless-only, Predictive IO serverless+Pro."},{"id":"ch08-e112","type":"free","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"What's the essential difference between classic and serverless? Then place the runtime in the stack.","model":"Classic: you manage/configure the infrastructure envelope (VMs, driver/worker types, scaling, DBR, Photon, pools, termination). Serverless: you specify the workload and Databricks manages the envelope (runtime, Photon, machine types, scaling, provisioning, lifecycle). Stack from the app side: your code → Spark/Photon → Databricks Runtime → driver/workers → VM/CPU/RAM.","rubric":["Classic: you configure the infrastructure envelope","Serverless: you specify the workload; Databricks manages","Stack: code → Spark/Photon → DBR → driver/workers → VMs"],"explain":"Final test Q24–25: serverless collapses the bottom half of the stack."},{"id":"ch08-e113","type":"order","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the stack from the application side down to hardware.","items":["Your code","Spark / Photon","Databricks Runtime","Driver / workers","VM / CPU / RAM"],"explain":"Hardware ↑ runtime ↑ execution engine/Spark ↑ your application — the same stack read bottom-up. (Final test Q25.)"},{"id":"ch08-e114","type":"tf","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless collapses most of the bottom half of the stack: managed runtime, Photon, machine types, scaling, provisioning and lifecycle.","answer":true,"explain":"That collapse is exactly the value of serverless."},{"id":"ch08-e115","type":"write","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the axis-by-axis spec (`Field: value` per line) of an `etl-development` classic all-purpose cluster: Standard access, DBR 18 LTS, Photon enabled, driver r6i.2xlarge, worker r6i.xlarge, autoscaling min 2 max 8.","solution":"Name: etl-development\nPurpose: All-purpose\nInfrastructure: Classic\nAccess mode: Standard\nRuntime: DBR 18 LTS\nPhoton: Enabled\nDriver: r6i.2xlarge\nWorker: r6i.xlarge\nAutoscaling: min 2, max 8","keywords":["All-purpose","Classic","Standard","DBR 18 LTS","Photon","r6i.2xlarge","r6i.xlarge","min 2","max 8"],"lang":"text","explain":"This is the source's complete configuration — one compute, each field on a different layer (purpose, infra, access, software, engine, driver HW, worker HW, scaling)."},{"id":"ch08-e116","type":"write","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the PySpark that the start-up chain eventually executes: total `amount` per `country` from table `orders` (start from `spark.table`).","solution":"spark.table(\"orders\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")","keywords":["spark.table","groupby(\"country\")","sum(\"amount\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Driver + Catalyst plan it, Photon executes supported operators as tasks on workers over partitions."},{"id":"ch08-e117","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s14","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which pair is NOT correct (classic all-purpose vs serverless Jobs)?","options":["Auto termination: configurable vs lifecycle-based","GPU: possible classic config vs AI Runtime separately","Pool: ✅ vs ❌","Job use: intended vs N/A"],"answer":3,"explain":"Job use for all-purpose is 'supported but discouraged for prod', and for serverless Jobs it's the 'intended use'. 'N/A' belongs to serverless notebook."},{"id":"ch08-e118","type":"mcq","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"What is **worker type**?","options":["The number of workers","The cloud VM shape of each worker/executor node","The Spark version on the worker","Standard vs Dedicated"],"answer":1,"explain":"Worker type = which cloud VM type each worker uses. On multi-node classic compute each worker runs a Spark executor. (Final test Q6.)"},{"id":"ch08-e119","type":"odd","section":"ch08-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one does NOT describe the worker side of a classic multi-node cluster?","options":["Runs a Spark executor","Processes partitions in parallel","Holds the SparkContext and notebook state","Its VM shape is the worker type"],"answer":2,"explain":"SparkContext and notebook state live on the **driver**. Workers run executors that process partitions."},{"id":"ch08-e120","type":"odd","section":"ch08-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one does NOT belong with the others (things Photon is)?","options":["Native C++ engine","Vectorized columnar batches","Uses SIMD on CPUs","A new programming API you must call"],"answer":3,"explain":"Photon runs under the same Spark API — no new programming model. The other three describe how it executes."},{"id":"ch08-e121","type":"odd","section":"ch08-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a kind of pool the Databricks 'Pool' setting refers to?","options":["Pre-provisioned idle VMs","Instance Pool","Thread pool in the driver","Warm machines for classic clusters"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Pool setting means an **Instance Pool** of cloud VMs — not a thread, connection, data or executor pool."},{"id":"ch08-e122","type":"odd","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are Gate-2 (data) privileges. Which is the odd one out?","options":["USE CATALOG","USE SCHEMA","SELECT","CAN USE"],"answer":3,"explain":"CAN USE is a warehouse ACL (Gate 1, compute). USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT are Unity Catalog data privileges (Gate 2)."},{"id":"ch08-e123","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Maria has CAN VIEW on `revenue_pipeline`. She runs `SELECT * FROM prod.gold.revenue;` and is denied.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you conclude first?","options":[{"text":"Her pipeline permission doesn't cover the output table's data","ok":true,"fb":"Pipeline object ACL ≠ Unity Catalog data ACL."},{"text":"The pipeline failed and the table is empty","ok":false,"fb":"An empty table wouldn't produce a permission error."},{"text":"She needs CAN RUN","ok":false,"fb":"Running the pipeline still wouldn't grant SELECT on the output."}]},{"prompt":"What does she need?","options":[{"text":"SELECT on prod.gold.revenue (+ USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA), and REFRESH if she must open the backing pipeline from the MV","ok":true,"fb":"Output tables are UC objects with their own permissions."},{"text":"CAN MANAGE on the pipeline","ok":false,"fb":"More pipeline power ≠ data access."},{"text":"CAN USE on Default Automated Compute","ok":false,"fb":"That's serverless compute access."}]}],"explain":"Pipeline permission ≠ output data permission."},{"id":"ch08-e124","type":"order","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the 'driver OOM on collect()' playbook.","items":["Find the collect()-style action","Compare the result size with the DRIVER's RAM","Try to keep the work distributed (aggregate/write on workers)","Only if data must come back: choose a larger driver type"],"explain":"Locate the action, compare with driver RAM, avoid the transfer, size up only if truly needed."},{"id":"ch08-e125","type":"order","section":"ch08-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the 'why is my cluster still costing money?' checks.","items":["Is it all-purpose compute?","Is auto termination configured (and to what)?","Is a notebook or job keeping it active?","Should scheduled work move to Jobs compute?","Check warehouse Auto Stop / pool idle VMs elsewhere"],"explain":"Mnemonic IDLE: all-purpose? auto-termination set? lingering jobs/notebooks? elsewhere (warehouses, pools)?"},{"id":"ch08-e126","type":"scenario","section":"ch08-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A classic production job ran for months; after someone switched its cluster from DBR 17.3 LTS to the newest DBR, results subtly changed.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's the most likely cause?","options":[{"text":"The new DBR ships different Spark/library versions and optimizer behaviour","ok":true,"fb":"DBRs differ in Spark versions, APIs, libraries, optimizer behaviour."},{"text":"The Delta table got corrupted","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing indicates corruption; the runtime changed."},{"text":"Unity Catalog permissions changed","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions would cause errors, not subtle result changes."}]},{"prompt":"Best practice going forward?","options":[{"text":"Pin production jobs to an LTS DBR and test new runtimes before switching","ok":true,"fb":"LTS = longer support/stability horizon."},{"text":"Always use the newest DBR in production","ok":false,"fb":"That's the advice for development, not operational jobs."},{"text":"Switch to DBR ML","ok":false,"fb":"ML runtime doesn't add stability for ETL."}]}],"explain":"Development → current DBR; operational jobs → LTS. Runtime changes can subtly change behaviour."},{"id":"ch08-e127","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete.","text":"`df.collect()` moves rows from the [[workers]] to the [[driver]]. With 50 GB of data and 16 GB of driver RAM you will very likely get a driver [[OOM|out of memory]].","bank":["executor","pool","warehouse"],"explain":"The fix is to avoid collecting big data or, if needed, to choose a larger driver type."},{"id":"ch08-e128","type":"cloze","section":"ch08-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the core rule.","text":"Warehouse [[ACL]] + Unity Catalog [[permissions|privileges]] = query possible. Permission to use [[compute]] ≠ permission to use [[data]].","bank":["Photon","pool"],"explain":"This pattern repeats across Databricks: warehouses, jobs and pipelines."}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch09","num":9,"title":"Compute Mastery: From VM to Task","subtitle":"Cores, partitions, waves, memory, spill, OOM, autoscaling and the compute decisions behind them","emoji":"⚙️","sourcePages":"306–358","mantra":"Workers × cores = slots, partitions = tasks, and no amount of hardware fixes too few partitions, skew or a collect() on the driver.","objectives":["You can compute executors, task slots and waves from any compute configuration (driver excluded).","You can explain why partition count, not cluster size, caps a stage's parallelism — and why autoscaling can't fix one partition or skew.","You can reason about scale up vs scale out, memory per core, execution vs storage memory, and spill vs failure.","You can tell driver OOM from executor OOM and walk the memory-debugging chain before adding RAM.","You can name the bottleneck (CPU / memory / I/O / skew) from Spark UI task metrics and pick the matching fix.","You can choose serverless vs classic and Standard vs Dedicated with the selection algorithm, and explain pools, spot, termination and permissions.","You can write spark.conf.get, getNumPartitions, repartition, coalesce and explain('formatted') and know their serverless caveats.","You can dodge the certification traps: executors per worker, zero workers, autoscaling vs instance size, Dedicated ≠ performance, dynamic allocation."],"sections":[{"id":"ch09-s01","title":"What You Buy: The Compute Stack","hook":"Look at any compute config and instantly know how many machines, cores and parallel tasks you really have.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Phase 1 goal**: look at a compute configuration and say *how many machines* you have, *what runs on each*, roughly *how many Spark tasks run at once*, *where OOM can happen*, *how shuffle is affected*, *what autoscaling changes*, and *why serverless or classic*."},{"t":"flow","items":["VM","Driver / Worker","Executor","Core","Task","Partition","Stage","Job"],"caption":"Phase 1 builds from the lowest level up (part 1: the execution hierarchy)"},{"t":"flow","items":["Autoscaling / node sizing","Access mode","Pools / Spot / termination","Serverless abstraction","Debugging + Spark UI"],"caption":"Part 2: the configuration and operations layers on top"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Today's default","text":"Databricks currently recommends **serverless** as the default for most new workloads, and **classic** only when you need capabilities or configuration that serverless does not support."},{"t":"p","text":"Say you create **classic compute** with: Driver = 8 cores / 32 GB, Workers = 4 × (8 cores / 32 GB)."},{"t":"diagram","text":"                DRIVER VM\n               8 CPU / 32 GB\n                    |\n     +----------+---+------+----------+\n     v          v          v          v\n WORKER 1   WORKER 2   WORKER 3   WORKER 4\n  8 cores    8 cores    8 cores    8 cores\n  32 GB      32 GB      32 GB      32 GB","caption":"1 driver machine + 4 worker machines = 5 cloud VMs"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: how many cloud VMs is that, and how many executor cores?","text":"**5 VMs** (1 driver + 4 workers). Executor cores = 4 × 8 = **32**, not 40. Worker RAM ≈ 4 × 32 = **128 GB**. The driver is a separate coordinator, not distributed capacity."},{"t":"table","head":["Resource","Count","Why"],"rows":[["Cloud VMs","1 + 4 = 5","Every node, driver included, is a VM you pay for"],["Executor (worker) cores","4 × 8 = 32","Only worker cores run distributed tasks"],["Worker RAM","4 × 32 GB = 128 GB","Driver RAM is separate"],["Driver","8 cores / 32 GB","Coordinator: plans and schedules, not a task slot pool"]],"caption":"What you actually bought"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Don't add the driver to worker capacity","text":"Saying \"**40 worker cores**\" (32 + 8 driver cores) is the classic counting mistake. The driver's cores are **not** executor cores."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Head chef vs line cooks","text":"The **driver** is the head chef who reads orders and assigns dishes. The workers are the cooking stations. Hiring a bigger head chef adds no extra stove."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"VM","def":"A cloud virtual machine; every driver and worker node is one."},{"term":"Driver","def":"The coordinator node: holds SparkSession/SparkContext, plans queries, schedules tasks."},{"term":"Worker","def":"A node that does distributed processing; in Databricks classic it runs one executor."},{"term":"Executor","def":"The Spark process on a worker that runs tasks."},{"term":"Core","def":"A CPU core of an executor; ≈ one task slot."},{"term":"Task","def":"The smallest basic execution unit of a stage; processes one partition."},{"term":"Partition","def":"A chunk of data; one partition → one task within a stage."},{"term":"Stage","def":"A set of tasks that run without a shuffle in between."},{"term":"Job","def":"All stages triggered by one action (count, display, write…)."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-s02","title":"Driver vs Executors (Level-up)","hook":"The driver plans, the executors do. Mixing them up leads to wrong sizing and wrong OOM fixes.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 already taught *driver = coordinator, worker = processing*. Here you add the **full list of driver jobs**, the **plan pipeline**, and the **one-executor-per-worker** rule."},{"t":"list","items":["SparkSession","SparkContext","query planning","DAG construction","stage scheduling","task scheduling","tracking executors","collecting task metadata / results","notebook interpreter / session"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks describes the **driver** as the node that keeps the state of attached notebooks, maintains the SparkContext, interprets commands and coordinates the executors."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"result = (\n    spark.table(\"sales.orders\")\n         .groupBy(\"country\")\n         .sum(\"amount\")\n)\ndisplay(result)","caption":"An e-shop revenue-per-country query"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: does the driver fetch all rows and start adding amounts?","text":"No. The driver first builds a **plan**: Logical Plan (Aggregate ← Scan sales.orders) → Optimized Plan → Physical Plan. Then it splits the physical plan into stages/tasks that the **workers** run on the actual partitions."},{"t":"flow","items":["Logical plan","Optimized plan","Physical plan","Stages & tasks","Executors run tasks"],"caption":"Driver organizes, workers execute"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Scan sales.orders          (on workers, per partition)\n     |\nPartial Aggregate          (sum per country, per partition)\n     |\nExchange                   (shuffle by country)\n     |\nFinal Aggregate            (combine partial sums)","caption":"The physical plan of the groupBy"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"DRIVER","points":["coordination / control","plans, schedules, tracks","receives results/metadata"]},{"title":"WORKERS","points":["distributed processing","run tasks on actual partitions","hold shuffle/spill/cache locally"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Not 100% true for every implementation detail, but **driver ≠ worker** is the right mental model and it is fundamental."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"One executor per worker","text":"In vanilla Spark, worker machines and executors can relate in complex ways. In **Databricks classic**, Databricks runs **one Spark executor per worker node** — that's why the docs use *worker* and *executor* almost interchangeably. 8 workers → normally 8 executors."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Worker 1 ── Executor 1\nWorker 2 ── Executor 2\nWorker 3 ── Executor 3"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Certification trap: executors","text":"\"Databricks classic compute has 6 workers. How many Spark executors?\" → **6**. One executor per worker. Don't add one for the driver and don't multiply by cores."}]},{"id":"ch09-s03","title":"Cores, Tasks, Partitions & Waves","hook":"The most useful sizing formula in Spark: tasks ÷ task slots = waves.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Spark uses **cores** as the basis for task parallelism: roughly **one active task per available executor core**. Databricks states a 1:1 mapping between task slots and available cores."},{"t":"p","text":"So an 8-core worker can usually run about **8 tasks concurrently**."},{"t":"p","text":"A **task** is the smallest basic execution unit of a Spark stage. Within a stage, **1 partition → 1 task**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"# DataFrame with 100 partitions\ndf.filter(\"amount > 10\").count()\n# → this stage has ~100 tasks","caption":"100 partitions → 100 tasks"},{"t":"diagram","text":"4 workers × 8 cores = 32 task slots\n\nWave 1 → 32 tasks\nWave 2 → 32 tasks\nWave 3 → 32 tasks\nWave 4 →  4 tasks   (28 slots idle)\n         ───\n         100 tasks = 4 waves","caption":"Tasks beyond the slot count run in waves (conceptually)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The formulas","text":"**Task slots** = workers × cores per worker (driver excluded).\n**Waves** ≈ ceil(tasks ÷ slots), assuming similar task durations."},{"t":"table","head":["Partitions in stage","On 32 slots","Verdict"],"rows":[["4","4 tasks run, 28 cores idle","Huge cluster, not enough parallel work"],["32","Fills all 32 slots","Very good fit"],["1,000","32 at a time until done","Fine — more partitions than cores is not a problem by itself"],["1,000,000 tiny","Endless tiny tasks","Scheduling / task-startup overhead becomes significant"]],"caption":"The partition count decides how many parallel pieces of work exist"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Big cluster, tiny parallelism","text":"A 32-core cluster reading a dataset with **4 partitions** keeps 28 cores idle. Parallelism is capped by partitions, not by the size of the bill."},{"t":"p","text":"End-to-end: `spark.read.table(\"orders\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` with 64 input partitions. **Stage 1** = 64 tasks (task 1 → partition 1 … task 64 → partition 64). The groupBy causes a **shuffle**; if Spark uses 200 shuffle partitions, **Stage 2** = 200 tasks."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Stage 1: 64 input partitions  → 64 tasks\n            |  shuffle (groupBy)\n            v\nStage 2: 200 shuffle partitions → 200 tasks","caption":"Partition count is stage-specific"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"No single partition count per job","text":"**Partition count is stage-specific.** A job does not have one partition number — each stage has its own."},{"t":"p","text":"On 4 workers × 8 cores (32 slots): Stage 1 = 64 tasks → **2 waves**. Stage 2 = 200 tasks → 200/32 = 6.25 → **7 waves**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Waves are conceptual","text":"Tasks don't all take the same time. In practice **skew** can make one task run far longer than the others, so real execution isn't neat waves."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Exercise 2: 6 workers × 16 cores, stage with 768 partitions. How many task slots and waves?","text":"Slots = 6 × 16 = **96**. Waves ≈ 768 / 96 = **8**, if all tasks have similar duration."}]},{"id":"ch09-s04","title":"Scale Up vs Scale Out & Memory per Core","hook":"Same total cores and RAM can still behave very differently — the shape of the cluster matters.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 defined *scale up* (bigger machines) and *scale out* (more machines). Here you learn **why the shape changes behaviour** and the **memory-per-core** heuristic."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Cluster A — scale up","points":["2 workers × 16 cores × 128 GB","Totals: 32 cores, 256 GB","few large workers"]},{"title":"Cluster B — scale out","points":["8 workers × 4 cores × 32 GB","Totals: 32 cores, 256 GB","many small workers"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"The **totals are identical**. Databricks uses exactly this kind of example in its sizing best practices — and the point is that equal cores ≠ equal performance."},{"t":"list","items":["network traffic","shuffle fan-out","executor heap size","per-task memory","scheduling","fault impact","local disk"],"ordered":false},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: A and B both have 8 GB per core. So what actually differs?","text":"The **executor heap** (one 128 GB executor vs one 32 GB executor), **shuffle fan-out** (8 workers exchange blocks with more peers over the network), **fault impact** (losing 1 of 2 workers loses 50% of capacity; 1 of 8 loses 12.5%) and **local disk** per node."},{"t":"p","text":"**Per-task memory example**: each task needs ~10 GB of working memory. A worker with 4 cores / 16 GB runs 4 concurrent tasks."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Worker: 4 cores / 16 GB\n\n task1 10GB  task2 10GB  task3 10GB  task4 10GB\n └──────────────── 40 GB working set ────────┘\n                     vs 16 GB RAM\n                  → spill or OOM","caption":"4 × 10 GB = 40 GB potential working set in a 16 GB worker"},{"t":"p","text":"A worker with 4 cores / **64 GB** has a much larger memory-per-core ratio and handles the same tasks comfortably."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Memory per core > raw GB","text":"**Memory per core** matters more than total GB. Worker A: 4 cores / 16 GB = **4 GB/core**. Worker B: 4 cores / 32 GB = **8 GB/core**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"When you suspect a memory problem","text":"Databricks suggests trying a **higher memory-per-core ratio** to see whether behaviour changes, and recommends **memory-optimized workers** for heavy shuffle, spill and memory-intensive workloads."}]},{"id":"ch09-s05","title":"Where Worker RAM Goes: Execution, Storage & Spill","hook":"A 32 GB worker never gives 32 GB to your rows — and running out of RAM usually means spill, not failure.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Don't imagine **32 GB worker = 32 GB available for Spark rows**. Several consumers share worker RAM."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Worker RAM\n├── JVM heap\n│   ├── Spark execution memory\n│   ├── cached data\n│   ├── JVM objects\n│   └── metadata\n├── off-heap / native memory\n├── Python worker processes\n├── Photon / native processing\n└── OS / Databricks services","caption":"Who eats worker memory"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Not a simple formula","text":"The exact allocation is **not** simply RAM ÷ cores. But for sizing, **memory per core** is still a useful heuristic."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Execution memory","points":["sort","shuffle","hash aggregation","join hash tables"]},{"title":"Storage memory","points":["df.cache()","persisted blocks"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Spark has **unified memory management**: execution and storage can, to some degree, share the available managed memory."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers want","text":"You don't need every memory fraction by heart. You need this: **join / sort / groupBy can eat execution memory even if you never called `cache()`**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Spill**: a task must sort 20 GB but can't keep all intermediate data in RAM. Spark writes intermediate data to the **local worker disk** and keeps going."},{"t":"flow","items":["RAM insufficient","Write intermediate data to local worker disk","Continue processing"],"caption":"What spill is"},{"t":"p","text":"The Spark UI shows spill as **Memory Bytes Spilled** and **Disk Bytes Spilled**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Spill ≠ failure","text":"**Spill does not mean the job is broken.** Spark is designed so operations can spill. But *huge, repeated* spill can make a workload very slow."},{"t":"list","items":["insufficient memory","too-large partitions","skew","bad join","too few shuffle partitions"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Fix direction","text":"Databricks lists memory-intensive **shuffle/spill** workloads as a use case for **memory-optimized workers** — after you've ruled out skew and bad partitioning."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when the Spark UI shows spill","questions":["Is this a little spill or huge, repeated spill that slows the stage?","Is the spill concentrated in one task (skew) or spread across all tasks?","Are my partitions too large because there are too few shuffle partitions?","Is a join or aggregation building a big hash table in execution memory?","Would a higher memory-per-core worker change the behaviour?"]}]},{"id":"ch09-s06","title":"Shuffle & the Temporary Local Disk","hook":"Shuffle writes to local disks and crosses the network — and none of that survives the cluster.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")"},{"t":"p","text":"**Map-side tasks** produce shuffle output and write it as **shuffle files on each worker's local disk**. Downstream tasks then **pull** those blocks over the network."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Worker 1 local disk: shuffle files ─┐\nWorker 2 local disk: shuffle files ─┼─ network ─> downstream tasks\nWorker 3 local disk: shuffle files ─┘\n\nWorker A ── shuffle blocks ── network ──> Worker B","caption":"Shuffle = local disk + network"},{"t":"p","text":"So heavy **shuffle** puts pressure on four resources at once:"},{"t":"list","items":["CPU","memory","network","local disk"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"Executors' **local disks** are used, among other things, for shuffle files, spill and caching."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: what happens to those local files when the cluster stops?","text":"The **worker VM is destroyed**. Local shuffle files, cached partitions, temp files and spill files can all be lost."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Local worker disk","points":["temporary compute storage","shuffle, spill, cache, temp files","gone when the VM is destroyed"]},{"title":"Delta table storage","points":["persistent cloud storage","your tables and their transaction log","survives any cluster"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Local disk is not storage","text":"**Local worker disk = temporary compute storage.** Never rely on cached/local data surviving; persist results as Delta tables in cloud storage."}]},{"id":"ch09-s07","title":"Driver OOM vs Executor OOM","hook":"Two different crashes, two different fixes — and \"double the cluster\" is usually the wrong first move.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Driver OOM","points":["df.collect() / df.toPandas() on large data","huge result","huge query plan","too many task metadata","large local Python structures"]},{"title":"Executor OOM","points":["skewed partition","large hash join","huge window partition","too few shuffle partitions","large UDF memory","stateful streaming state"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Example**: Workers = 20 × 64 GB, Driver = 8 GB. You run `rows = df.collect()` on a 100 GB result. The workers are fine — the **driver** collapses."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"rows = df.collect()   # pulls every row into driver memory\npdf = df.toPandas()   # same: materializes the data on the driver","caption":"The two classic driver killers"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Databricks' list","text":"Databricks names **skew, large broadcasts, UDFs, unpartitioned windows and too few shuffle partitions** as common causes of memory failures."},{"t":"p","text":"**Bad reasoning**: OOM → double the cluster. **Better**: walk a diagnosis chain first."},{"t":"flow","items":["Which executor/task?","One partition dominates?","Skew?","Spill?","Join?","Window?","Too few partitions?","Memory per core?"],"caption":"Memory debugging chain — before adding RAM"},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Mnemonic","text":"**W**ise **D**octors **S**can **S**ymptoms, **J**udge **W**ounds, **P**rescribe **M**edicine → **W**hich task · **D**ominant partition · **S**kew · **S**pill · **J**oin · **W**indow · **P**artitions too few · **M**emory/core."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"100 workers can't split one partition","text":"If the problem is **one 100 GB partition**, having 100 workers barely helps: a single task must still process those 100 GB."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when something dies with OOM","questions":["Did the driver die or an executor? (Where is the error coming from?)","If the driver: did I call collect(), toPandas() or build a huge result/plan?","If an executor: which task failed, and does one partition dominate in size?","Is there skew, a big hash join or broadcast, or an unpartitioned window?","Are there too few shuffle partitions, making each partition huge?","Only then: would a higher memory-per-core worker help?"]}]},{"id":"ch09-s08","title":"Autoscaling From First Principles (Level-up)","hook":"Autoscaling adds task slots — it never adds partitions and never fixes skew.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 covered *classic min/max workers* vs *serverless managed scaling*. Here you do the **slot math**, see **what autoscaling cannot fix**, and meet two exam traps."},{"t":"p","text":"Worker = 8 cores / 32 GB, **Min workers = 2**, **Max workers = 8**. Minimum capacity = 2 × 8 = **16 task slots**; maximum = 8 × 8 = **64 task slots**."},{"t":"flow","items":["16 slots","24 slots","40 slots","64 slots"],"caption":"Parallel task capacity can move between limits depending on the workload"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"What does NOT change","text":"Worker size stays **8 cores / 32 GB each**. Autoscaling never turns \"2 huge VMs\" into \"8 tiny VMs\". It is **horizontal autoscaling**: it changes the *number* of workers."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a stage has 1 partition. You scale from 2 to 100 workers. How many tasks?","text":"**1.** So about 1 core is busy and hundreds are idle. **Autoscaling doesn't magically create partitions.**"},{"t":"p","text":"**Skew failure**: a stage with 100 partitions — 99 of 100 MB, one of **200 GB**. Scaling 4 → 40 workers makes the 99 small ones finish fast, but the job ends only when **the straggler** finishes."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Skew ≠ missing capacity","text":"When one giant task dominates, the problem is **skew**, not insufficient parallel capacity. More workers just means more idle cores waiting."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Exercise 3: 4 workers × 8 cores, stage of 30 partitions. Autoscale to 8 workers — big speed-up?","text":"No. 32 slots already run all **30 tasks** at once. At 8 workers you have 64 slots but still only 30 tasks → essentially **no gain in task parallelism**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Classic autoscaling","points":["worker type = X","min = 2, max = 8 (you set them)","changes worker count only"]},{"title":"Serverless autoscaling","points":["you just run the workload","Databricks manages instances, capacity, scaling, runtime","serverless Jobs: automatic scaling + platform-managed Photon"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Serverless does autoscale","text":"**Serverless autoscales**, but it's usually *not* the manual `min_workers` / `max_workers` axis. You can't set min_workers=2, max_workers=10 for ordinary serverless notebook compute the classic way."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Certification trap: autoscaling","text":"Classic autoscaling primarily changes the **number of workers**, **not the worker instance size**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Certification trap: Spark dynamic allocation","text":"Vanilla Spark has `spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled`. Databricks explicitly lists the **dynamic-allocation properties as unsupported** on classic compute and tells you to use **Databricks autoscaling** instead."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before turning up max workers","questions":["How many tasks does the slow stage actually have compared with my task slots?","Are tasks fewer than slots already (more workers = more idle cores)?","Is one straggler task dominating the stage (skew)?","Am I trying to fix this with spark.dynamicAllocation instead of Databricks autoscaling?"]}]},{"id":"ch09-s09","title":"Single Node, Zero Workers & Access Modes (Level-up)","hook":"Single node ≠ zero workers, and Dedicated ≠ faster. Two favourite exam traps.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 introduced single/multi-node and Standard/Dedicated. New here: the **zero-workers trap**, **Auto** access mode, the **2026 UI change**, and the rule *capability, not performance*."},{"t":"diagram","text":"┌────────────────────┐\n│ DRIVER             │\n│ + Spark local work │\n└────────────────────┘\n   (no worker nodes)","caption":"Single-node compute"},{"t":"p","text":"Classic doesn't have to mean a distributed cluster. In **Single node** mode there are no separate worker nodes — Databricks says the **driver also acts as the worker**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Single node is good for","points":["small development","small datasets","some local ML","non-distributed workloads"]},{"title":"Single node is NOT for","points":["multi-TB Spark ETL"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Certification trap: zero workers","text":"A **multi-node** compute with **0 workers**: can it run Spark distributed commands? **No.** Docs: without worker nodes, *non-Spark* commands run on the driver but **Spark commands fail**. If you want single-machine Spark, pick **Single Node** — a separate mode."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"2026 nuance: the UI changed","text":"The current UI has **no separate obvious \"multi-node\" selector**: compute is multi-node by default and you choose **Single node** when desired. **Driver type** moved under **Advanced Performance** and defaults to **Auto**. Older tutorials and screenshots look different."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Standard","points":["many users share one compute with workload isolation","Databricks' recommended mode for most workloads","Python, SQL, Scala","R not supported"]},{"title":"Dedicated","points":["assigned to a specific user or (in supported setups) a group","for RDD APIs, R, GPU, ML Runtime","privileged / lower-level machine access"]},{"title":"Auto","points":["not a third access model","Databricks picks Standard or Dedicated by runtime/hardware","prefers Standard; switches to Dedicated for ML Runtime, GPU or a fairly old DBR"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Practical rule for data engineering**: **Standard** by default. Dedicated only when you can say \"I need a specific capability that Standard doesn't support\"."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Dedicated is not a performance tier","text":"Don't pick **Dedicated** because it \"sounds faster\" or because \"production is important\". It's a **security/capability mode**, not a speed setting."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Certification trap: Standard vs Dedicated","text":"Choose Dedicated only when the workload **requires** capabilities Standard can't support. **Standard** is the recommended classic access mode for most workloads."}]},{"id":"ch09-s10","title":"Pools & Spot Instances (Level-up)","hook":"Pools answer \"where do the VMs come from?\" — spot answers \"how cheap can they be?\"","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 explained what a pool is and pool lifecycle. Here: the **full mental model** (pool vs cluster, pool vs autoscaling, serverless vs pools) plus **spot instances**."},{"t":"flow","items":["Start cluster","Ask cloud provider for VMs","Wait for VM provisioning","Prepare runtime","Spark starts"],"caption":"Without a pool: every start pays the provisioning wait"},{"t":"diagram","text":"POOL (pre-warmed VM capacity)\n├── idle VM\n├── idle VM\n├── idle VM\n└── used VM\n\ncluster → pool → warm instance available → attach quickly"},{"t":"p","text":"Pool parameters (as described for the AWS implementation) include:"},{"t":"list","items":["minimum idle instances","maximum capacity","idle instance auto termination"],"ordered":false},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Pool","points":["a reservoir of infrastructure instances","can serve more than one compute"]},{"title":"Compute (cluster)","points":["a Spark execution environment","draws its nodes from the pool"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":"            POOL\n     ┌───────┼───────┐\n     v       v       v\n Cluster A Cluster B Cluster C"},{"t":"p","text":"Cluster wants to grow **2 → 6 workers**. The **autoscaler** decides \"I need +4\". The **pool** answers \"here are 4 warm VMs\"."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Two different questions","text":"**Autoscaler: HOW MANY?** **Pool: WHERE DO THE VMs COME FROM?** A pool never decides how many workers Spark needs."},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless vs pools**: serverless makes the pool problem a **platform concern**. That's why Databricks now recommends serverless instead of pools where the workload is compatible. Serverless doesn't support user-configured instance pools."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"On-demand VM","points":["provider promises normal availability","more expensive"]},{"title":"Spot VM","points":["cheap spare capacity","can be reclaimed by the cloud provider"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"If a spot worker disappears, Spark can often **retry** its tasks: task lost → scheduled elsewhere. So spot is attractive for **fault-tolerant batch**, not for every workload."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Spot and latency-sensitive streaming","text":"Current Databricks **real-time streaming mode requires spot off**, because interruptions are bad for latency-sensitive persistent processing."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Driver on spot?","text":"You usually want the **driver** stable. Lose a worker → some tasks retry. Lose the driver → the **Spark application itself** is disrupted. Architectures separate driver reliability from worker cost optimization."}]},{"id":"ch09-s11","title":"Termination, Lifecycle & Permissions (Level-up)","hook":"Terminated is not deleted, and \"can attach\" is not \"can read the data\".","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 covered termination and auto-termination basics and SQL-warehouse ACLs. New here: exactly **what survives**, **jobs lifecycle**, **compute permission levels** and **serverless permission objects**."},{"t":"flow","items":["running","no commands","idle","X minutes","terminate"],"caption":"Auto-termination of an all-purpose cluster"},{"t":"p","text":"Why? Interactive clusters **cost money even when the developer went for coffee**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Gone after termination","points":["VMs","cache","shuffle files","process / executor memory","local temp files","Python variables","runtime processes"]},{"title":"Remains after termination","points":["DBR version","worker type & driver type","autoscaling settings","name","permissions"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Termination ≠ delete.** Press **Start** and a **fresh** compute is created from the saved configuration — but all ephemeral state is gone."},{"t":"table","head":["Ephemeral (lost)","Persistent (kept)"],"rows":[["executor memory","Delta tables"],["cache","workspace notebooks"],["shuffle","Unity Catalog metadata"],["local temporary files","compute configuration"],["runtime processes","job definitions"]],"caption":"Big-test answer: what disappears vs what remains"},{"t":"flow","items":["Job starts","Compute starts","Tasks run","Job finishes","Compute goes away"],"caption":"Jobs compute lifecycle"},{"t":"p","text":"Jobs compute is governed by the **workload lifecycle**, not mainly by an idle timeout. Databricks generally discourages all-purpose compute for automated production jobs."},{"t":"table","head":["Compute permission","What it allows"],"rows":[["CAN ATTACH TO","Attach a notebook and use the compute"],["CAN RESTART","Also start / restart / terminate it"],["CAN MANAGE","Also change compute configuration and permissions"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Compute ACL ≠ Unity Catalog ACL","text":"You can have **CAN ATTACH TO** on the cluster and still have **SELECT prod.payroll = NO** → the query fails. Compute permissions never grant data access."},{"t":"p","text":"**Serverless permissions**: serverless doesn't mean everyone has access. The workspace has serverless permission objects — **Default Interactive Compute** (notebooks / Databricks Connect) and **Default Automated Compute** (Jobs / Pipelines) — with **CAN USE** and management permissions."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a query fails on a cluster I can attach to","questions":["Is this a compute problem (can't attach/start) or a data problem (permission denied on a table)?","Which identity is actually running the query?","Does that identity have the Unity Catalog privileges (e.g. SELECT) on the table?","Am I confusing CAN ATTACH TO / CAN MANAGE with data access?"]}]},{"id":"ch09-s12","title":"Photon in the Execution Stack (Level-up)","hook":"Photon speeds up SQL/DataFrame operators on CPUs — not Python loops, not GPUs.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up from ch08","text":"Ch08 explained what Photon is and where it's configured. Here: where it sits in the **execution stack**, and why **UDFs** reduce its benefit."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Spark DataFrame / SQL\n        |\n     Catalyst\n        |\n  Physical Plan\n        |\n┌──────────────────┐\n│ Photon native    │\n│   or Spark JVM   │\n└──────────────────┘\n        |\n   workers / CPU","caption":"Photon is an execution engine under the physical plan"},{"t":"p","text":"**Photon** is a native, vectorized engine. It's enabled in **serverless** and **SQL warehouses**; on classic all-purpose / jobs / pipelines it's configurable, and in the current UI it's **default-on**."},{"t":"list","items":["scans","filters","joins","aggregations","Delta / Parquet writes","SQL / DataFrames"],"ordered":false},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"for x in range(10_000_000):\n    do_python_work(x)","caption":"Photon does NOT turn this into fast native execution"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Photon is not a Python accelerator — nor a GPU","text":"Photon is **CPU-native vectorized query execution**. It won't speed up arbitrary Python loops, and it has nothing to do with GPUs."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Built-in functions","points":["F.sum(), F.when(), F.col(), F.regexp_extract()","visible to the optimizer","native engine has a much bigger chance to accelerate"]},{"title":"Opaque Python UDF","points":["my_python_udf(...)","a black box to the optimizer","less optimizer-friendly"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Best practice","text":"Prefer **built-in Spark SQL / DataFrame functions** wherever possible."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Larger driver or Photon?","text":"For a **CPU-bound SQL aggregation**, **Photon / query optimization** is far more relevant than a bigger driver — the aggregation runs on the workers."}]},{"id":"ch09-s13","title":"Serverless Trade-offs & the Compute Selection Algorithm","hook":"Serverless removes the knobs — that's both the limitation and the benefit.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Classic UI knobs","points":["DBR","driver node type","worker node type","worker count","min/max workers","Photon","pool","spot","access mode","Spark configs","init scripts …"]},{"title":"Serverless","points":["much less infrastructure configuration","Databricks manages instances, scaling, runtime"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks does **not** give you control in serverless over:"},{"t":"list","items":["compute policies","instance pools","most Spark cluster configurations","compute-scoped init scripts","compute-scoped libraries"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Limitation AND benefit","text":"Losing these knobs is not only a limitation — it's the **abstraction benefit**: no node sizing, no pool tuning, no idle clusters."},{"t":"p","text":"**What you sacrifice: control.** Current serverless limitations include:"},{"t":"list","items":["no R","no RDD APIs","Spark Connect APIs only","no cluster instance pools","limited Spark configs"],"ordered":false},{"t":"diagram","text":"START\n  |\nCan serverless support the workload?\n  ├── YES → prefer serverless\n  └── NO  → classic required\n              |\n        Need shared general DE/SQL?\n              ├── yes → Standard\n              └── need R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime?\n                        → Dedicated","caption":"Compute selection algorithm"},{"t":"p","text":"This matches current Databricks recommendations."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a team needs R for analysis. Which compute?","text":"Serverless has **no R** → classic required. Standard doesn't support R → **classic Dedicated**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Order of the questions matters","text":"Ask **\"can serverless do it?\"** first. Only if not, go classic, then pick **Standard** unless you need a Dedicated-only capability."}]},{"id":"ch09-s14","title":"Diagnosing Workloads: Spark UI & Bottleneck Types","hook":"Before buying more hardware, read the task-metric distributions and name the bottleneck.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Real sizing case**: daily batch ETL, 2 TB input, operations filter → join → groupBy. Driver 8 cores/32 GB; workers 4 × (8 cores/32 GB) → 32 worker cores, 128 GB; **autoscale 4–12**."},{"t":"p","text":"The initial scan creates **2,000 partitions**. At 4 workers: **32** concurrent tasks. At 12 workers: **96** concurrent tasks. So the scan phase can gain a lot of parallelism."},{"t":"p","text":"But after the shuffle the **join stage** has 499 partitions of 2 GB and **one of 300 GB**. Scaling 4 → 12 makes the small ones finish faster, but the 300 GB task stays the bottleneck."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Spark UI, join stage:\n  many tasks .......... done\n  one task: huge duration\n            huge shuffle read\n            huge spill\n\nDiagnosis: SKEW  — not \"need 50 workers\""},{"t":"flow","items":["Stages","Open the slow stage","Task metrics","Compare distributions (median vs max)"],"caption":"Spark UI path for a slow stage"},{"t":"list","items":["Duration","Input Size","Shuffle Read","Shuffle Write","Records","Memory Spill","Disk Spill","GC Time"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The skew signal","text":"**Median task = 20 sec, max task = 15 min**, with a huge shuffle read on the max task → **strong skew signal**."},{"t":"table","head":["Bottleneck","Signals","Potential fixes"],"rows":[["CPU-bound","CPU high, little spill, memory OK, tasks balanced","more cores, compute-optimized workers, Photon, better algorithm/query — not just more RAM"],["Memory-bound","high spill, executor OOM, GC high, CPU not fully useful","higher memory/core, more partitions, remove skew, smaller broadcast, different join, memory-optimized worker"],["I/O-bound","CPU low, tasks spend time reading","fix storage/file layout, too many small files, network, poor pruning, reading too much data"]],"caption":"Name the bottleneck first"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Scaling the wrong resource","text":"For an **I/O-bound** job, scaling CPU may not help. For a **CPU-bound** job, more RAM won't help."},{"t":"table","head":["Workload symptom","First direction of thought"],"rows":[["CPU consistently saturated","more/faster cores, Photon, query optimization"],["Executor OOM","memory/core, skew, partitions, join/window"],["Huge disk spill","memory pressure, partition size, skew"],["Driver OOM","collect/toPandas, driver size, plan/result size"],["Long cluster startup","serverless or pool"],["Idle interactive cost","auto-termination"],["Variable workload","autoscaling"],["Need R/RDD/GPU","Dedicated classic"],["Typical DE workloads","Standard or serverless"],["Tiny number of partitions","adding workers won't help"],["Few extreme stragglers","skew investigation"]],"caption":"Classic config best-practice matrix"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a stage is slow","questions":["Which stage is slow, and how many tasks does it have vs my slots?","Is max task duration far above the median (skew)?","Does the slowest task also have the biggest shuffle read / spill?","Is CPU high (CPU-bound), spill/GC high (memory-bound) or CPU low while reading (I/O-bound)?","Am I about to scale the resource that isn't the bottleneck?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Every micro-batch is a Spark job, so skew/spill/shuffle diagnosis applies unchanged — plus a deadline: the batch must finish before the next trigger. Streaming adds **state** (growth, watermark, state store) and **source/sink** (throughput, API latency, storage) branches to the debugging tree; compare `inputRowsPerSecond` vs `processedRowsPerSecond` before CPU. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, sections 14–15.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch09-s15","title":"Hands-on: Config, Partitions, Plans & Experiments","hook":"Five lines of code connect compute sizing to what Spark actually executes.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"spark.version                                   # Spark version\nspark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")  # inspect a Spark config","caption":"Inspect runtime and Spark config"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks documents **`spark.conf.get(...)`** as the way to inspect Spark configuration."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.rdd.getNumPartitions()","caption":"See the partition count"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"RDD access needed","text":"`df.rdd.getNumPartitions()` needs **RDD access** → fine on compatible classic compute, but **not on serverless** (Spark Connect), where RDD APIs aren't supported. There, reason from the **query plan** and **Spark UI / query profile** instead."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"repartition(n)","points":["df2 = df.repartition(200)","redistributes data into n partitions","usually involves a shuffle","can increase or decrease"]},{"title":"coalesce(n)","points":["df2 = df.coalesce(20)","reduces partitions without full redistribution","useful when there are too many partitions","dangerous if collapsed too aggressively (lost parallelism)"]}]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df.explain(\"formatted\")","caption":"Look for: Exchange, HashAggregate, BroadcastHashJoin, SortMergeJoin"},{"t":"p","text":"A line like `Exchange hashpartitioning(customer_id, 200)` tells you a **shuffle** is happening: data is redistributed by `customer_id` into 200 partitions. This bridges compute sizing with execution."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ndf = spark.range(0, 10_000_000)\ndf = df.withColumn(\"group_id\", F.col(\"id\") % 100)\n\nresult = df.groupBy(\"group_id\").count()\nresult.explain(\"formatted\")   # look for Exchange\ndisplay(result)               # then open the Spark UI","caption":"Experiment 1: see a shuffle"},{"t":"flow","items":["Job","Stage","Tasks","Shuffle"],"caption":"What to inspect in the Spark UI after display(result)"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"tiny_parallelism = df.coalesce(1)\ntiny_parallelism.count()\n\ndf.count()   # compare","caption":"Experiment 2: create bad parallelism"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself during experiment 2","questions":["Why are most cores idle? (Answer: only one partition → one task.)"]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"many = df.repartition(500)\n(many.groupBy(\"group_id\").count()\n     .write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"noop\").save())","caption":"Experiment 3: force more partitions, then inspect the number of tasks"},{"t":"p","text":"The **`noop`** format runs the whole computation but writes nothing — handy for timing and inspecting tasks without creating files."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The point of experiment 3","text":"Not \"500 is good\". The point is to *see* the chain: **partitioning → number of tasks → usable core parallelism**."}]},{"id":"ch09-s16","title":"Phase-1 Boss Test & Can-You-Explain","hook":"25 exam-style questions plus the eight sentences you must be able to say without notes.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The **Big Phase-1 test** is in the exercises below. Try every question before reading the explanation."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Say it without notes","text":"If you can explain each statement below out loud, Phase 1 is done."},{"t":"list","items":["A classic Databricks cluster has one Spark driver and one executor per worker.","Each worker contributes CPU cores, and Spark generally maps one task slot to each available executor core.","Partitions determine how many independent tasks a stage can expose — so adding workers only helps if the stage has enough useful parallelism.","Worker memory is used for execution, caching, native/Python processing and platform overhead; when execution data doesn't fit, Spark may spill to local worker disk.","Driver memory is separate: collect() and toPandas() can OOM the driver even when executors have huge aggregate RAM.","Classic autoscaling changes worker count between configured limits; serverless also scales, but Databricks controls the infrastructure.","Standard and Dedicated are access/capability modes, not performance tiers.","Pools accelerate provisioning of classic compute; they do not decide the worker count — autoscaling does."],"ordered":true},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: which single number limits a stage's parallelism even on a 1,000-core cluster?","text":"The stage's **partition count**. One partition → one task; tasks can't exceed partitions."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Next practical step","text":"Open a realistic **Create Compute** form field by field and design three clusters: an **interactive development** cluster, a **production batch ETL** cluster and a **memory-heavy Spark job** — then predict their Spark UI behaviour and debug deliberately bad configurations."}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch09-d01","title":"Executor OOM (or executors keep dying)","section":"ch09-s07","symptom":"A stage fails with executor out-of-memory / executor lost errors; the reflex is to double the cluster.","askYourself":["Which executor and which task died — is it one task or all of them?","Does one partition dominate in size (input / shuffle read)?","Is that domination skew in the join or groupBy key?","Is there heavy spill before the crash?","Is a join building a large hash table or a large broadcast?","Is there an unpartitioned or huge window partition?","Are there too few shuffle partitions, so every partition is huge?","Only now: would a higher memory-per-core worker change the behaviour?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the failed stage in the Spark UI and sort tasks by duration / shuffle read.","why":"Tells you whether one task or all tasks are under memory pressure."},{"do":"Compare median vs max of Input Size, Shuffle Read, Memory/Disk Spill.","why":"A max far above the median = skew; uniform high values = too few partitions or too little memory/core."},{"do":"Read the plan for join strategy and windows.","why":"BroadcastHashJoin of a big table or a window without partitionBy can blow executor memory.","code":"df.explain(\"formatted\")"},{"do":"Check the shuffle partition setting.","why":"Too few shuffle partitions makes each one too big.","code":"spark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")"},{"do":"Fix the cause (skew handling, more partitions, smaller broadcast, different join); then, if still needed, test a higher memory/core or memory-optimized worker.","why":"Hardware is the last lever, not the first."}],"rootCauses":["skewed partition","large hash join / large broadcast","huge or unpartitioned window","too few shuffle partitions","large UDF memory","stateful streaming state","too little memory per core"],"fix":"Diagnose before scaling: remove skew, raise partition count, change join/broadcast, partition windows; then increase memory per core (memory-optimized workers) if the workload genuinely needs it.","mnemonic":"Wise Doctors Scan Symptoms, Judge Wounds, Prescribe Medicine (Which task · Dominant partition · Skew · Spill · Join · Window · Partitions · Memory/core)"},{"id":"ch09-d02","title":"Driver OOM after collect() / toPandas()","section":"ch09-s07","symptom":"The notebook crashes or the driver restarts right after df.collect() or df.toPandas(), while the workers have plenty of RAM.","askYourself":["Did the driver die, not an executor?","Which action pulled data to the driver — collect(), toPandas(), a huge display/result?","How big is that result compared with the driver's RAM?","Could I aggregate, filter or limit on the workers first?","Could I write the result to a Delta table instead of bringing it to the driver?","Is something else bloating the driver — huge plan, many task metadata, large local Python objects?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm the failing component is the driver.","why":"Driver OOM and executor OOM have different fixes."},{"do":"Find the action that materializes data on the driver.","why":"collect()/toPandas() move all distributed rows into one machine.","code":"rows = df.collect()\npdf = df.toPandas()"},{"do":"Estimate result size vs driver memory (e.g. 100 GB result vs 8 GB driver).","why":"Aggregate worker RAM is irrelevant — only the driver's RAM counts here."},{"do":"Reduce on the workers (aggregate/filter/limit) or write to a table.","why":"Keeps the heavy work distributed.","code":"df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").saveAsTable(\"main.sales.result\")"},{"do":"Only if you truly need a large driver-side result, pick a larger driver type.","why":"Driver size is the last lever."}],"rootCauses":["collect()/toPandas() on large data","huge result","huge query plan","too many task metadata","large local Python structures"],"fix":"Keep results distributed (aggregate, filter, write to Delta); bring only small results to the driver; size the driver up only when a large driver-side result is genuinely required.","mnemonic":"Driver = desk, not warehouse: never pour the warehouse onto the desk."},{"id":"ch09-d03","title":"More workers, no speed-up (autoscaling doesn't help)","section":"ch09-s08","symptom":"You raised max workers (or autoscaling added nodes) but the slow stage takes just as long; most cores are idle.","askYourself":["How many tasks does the slow stage have?","How many task slots did I have before scaling (workers × cores)?","Are tasks ≤ slots already, so new workers can only sit idle?","Does the stage have 1 or very few partitions (coalesce(1), single file, tiny shuffle)?","Is one straggler task dominating instead (skew)?","Should I repartition / raise shuffle partitions instead of adding workers?"],"steps":[{"do":"Count the tasks of the slow stage in the Spark UI.","why":"Parallelism is capped by partitions: 1 partition → 1 task."},{"do":"Compute slots = workers × cores and compare with the task count.","why":"30 tasks on 32 slots already run in one wave; 64 slots change nothing."},{"do":"Check for coalesce(1) or a single-partition source.","why":"A collapsed partition count kills parallelism.","code":"df.rdd.getNumPartitions()   # classic only"},{"do":"Increase useful parallelism (repartition, more shuffle partitions) or fix skew.","why":"Creates tasks the extra cores can actually run.","code":"df2 = df.repartition(200)"}],"rootCauses":["too few partitions / tasks for the slots","coalesce(1) or single partition","skewed straggler task"],"fix":"Match partitions to slots (repartition / shuffle partitions) or fix skew; autoscaling only adds slots, never partitions.","mnemonic":"Count TASKS before you count WORKERS."},{"id":"ch09-d04","title":"One straggler task (data skew)","section":"ch09-s14","symptom":"99% of tasks finish in seconds; one runs for minutes with huge shuffle read and spill. Scaling out barely changes job time.","askYourself":["In the stage's task metrics, how far is the max duration from the median?","Does the slowest task also have the largest shuffle read / input size / records?","Does that task show huge memory or disk spill?","Which key is it partitioned by (join key, groupBy key)?","Would more workers or more RAM actually redistribute that data? (No.)"],"steps":[{"do":"Spark UI → Stages → open the slow stage → task metrics.","why":"Distributions reveal skew, averages hide it."},{"do":"Compare Duration, Shuffle Read, Records, Spill: median vs max.","why":"median 20 s vs max 15 min with huge shuffle read = strong skew signal."},{"do":"Identify the skewed key in the join/groupBy.","why":"Skew is a data-distribution problem."},{"do":"Address the distribution (skew handling) rather than scaling.","why":"More memory may reduce spill/failure but doesn't redistribute data."}],"rootCauses":["one key holds a huge share of the rows (e.g. 300 GB partition among 2 GB ones)","bad join on a skewed key"],"fix":"Treat it as skew: fix the data distribution for the skewed key; don't buy 50 workers or double RAM as a cure.","mnemonic":"Median vs Max: a big gap means a skewed map."},{"id":"ch09-d05","title":"Huge disk spill / memory-bound slow stage","section":"ch09-s05","symptom":"The stage is very slow; Spark UI shows large Memory Bytes Spilled and Disk Bytes Spilled, high GC time, but no failure.","askYourself":["Is the spill huge and repeated, or a small normal amount?","Is spill concentrated in a few tasks (skew) or spread over all (memory pressure)?","Are partitions too large because shuffle partitions are too few?","Is a join/aggregation building big hash tables (execution memory)?","What is my memory per core, and would a memory-optimized worker change behaviour?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check spill per task in the stage's task metrics.","why":"Separates skew from general memory pressure."},{"do":"Check partition size and the shuffle partition count.","why":"Too-large partitions force spill.","code":"spark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")"},{"do":"Inspect the join strategy in the plan.","why":"A bad join can explode execution memory.","code":"df.explain(\"formatted\")"},{"do":"Then test higher memory per core / memory-optimized workers.","why":"Databricks recommends them for memory-intensive shuffle/spill workloads."}],"rootCauses":["insufficient memory","too-large partitions","skew","bad join","too few shuffle partitions"],"fix":"Spill isn't failure: fix partition size/skew/join first; if the workload really is memory-intensive, use higher memory/core or memory-optimized workers.","mnemonic":"Spill = symptom, not sentence: find out WHY it spills."},{"id":"ch09-d06","title":"Slow job: which bottleneck is it? (CPU / memory / I/O)","section":"ch09-s14","symptom":"A job is slow but not failing; someone proposes \"just add more workers/RAM\".","askYourself":["Is CPU high with little spill and balanced tasks? (CPU-bound)","Is spill high, GC high, maybe executor OOM, while CPU isn't doing useful work? (memory-bound)","Is CPU low while tasks spend their time reading? (I/O-bound)","Are there few extreme stragglers? (skew, not capacity)","Does the fix I'm proposing target that resource?"],"steps":[{"do":"Look at cluster metrics (CPU) and Spark UI task metrics (spill, GC, input).","why":"Each bottleneck has a different fingerprint."},{"do":"CPU-bound → more/faster cores, compute-optimized workers, Photon, better query.","why":"More RAM won't help a CPU-bound job."},{"do":"Memory-bound → higher memory/core, more partitions, remove skew, smaller broadcast, different join.","why":"Targets memory pressure."},{"do":"I/O-bound → fix file layout, small files, pruning, read less data.","why":"Scaling CPU may not help I/O."}],"rootCauses":["CPU saturation","memory pressure (spill/GC/OOM)","I/O: small files, poor pruning, reading too much, network"],"fix":"Name the bottleneck first, then apply the matching row of the best-practice matrix.","mnemonic":"C-M-I: CPU hot? Memory spilling? I/O waiting?"},{"id":"ch09-d07","title":"Spark commands fail on a cluster with 0 workers","section":"ch09-s09","symptom":"A multi-node compute was configured with 0 workers; plain Python works in the notebook, but every Spark command fails.","askYourself":["Is this a multi-node compute with zero workers, or a real Single Node compute?","Do non-Spark commands work while Spark commands fail?","Do I actually need distributed workers, or just single-machine Spark?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the compute configuration: worker count and node mode.","why":"Docs: without workers, non-Spark commands run on the driver but Spark commands fail."},{"do":"For single-machine Spark, recreate/edit as Single node.","why":"Single Node is a separate mode where the driver also acts as worker."},{"do":"For distributed work, set ≥ 1 worker (or autoscaling).","why":"Spark needs executors on workers in multi-node mode."}],"rootCauses":["multi-node compute with zero workers is not the same as Single node mode"],"fix":"Use Single node mode for single-machine Spark, or add workers to multi-node compute.","mnemonic":"Zero workers ≠ Single node."},{"id":"ch09-d08","title":"Query fails although I can attach to the cluster","section":"ch09-s11","symptom":"Notebook attaches fine (CAN ATTACH TO), but SELECT on prod.payroll fails with a permission error.","askYourself":["Is this a compute permission problem or a data permission problem?","Which identity is running the query?","Does that identity have Unity Catalog SELECT on the table?","Am I assuming CAN ATTACH TO / CAN MANAGE grants data access?"],"steps":[{"do":"Separate the two gates: compute ACL vs Unity Catalog ACL.","why":"Compute ACL ≠ UC ACL."},{"do":"Check the UC grants on the table for the running identity.","why":"Data access is granted only in Unity Catalog.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE prod.payroll;"},{"do":"Request the needed UC privilege (or use an allowed table).","why":"No compute permission level can fix a data permission."}],"rootCauses":["missing Unity Catalog privilege on the table","confusing compute ACL with data ACL"],"fix":"Get the correct Unity Catalog privilege; compute permissions (CAN ATTACH TO / RESTART / MANAGE) never grant data access.","mnemonic":"Attach ≠ Access."},{"id":"ch09-d09","title":"Cluster takes too long to start / idle cost too high","section":"ch09-s10","symptom":"Every classic cluster start waits several minutes for VMs; or interactive clusters run up costs while idle.","askYourself":["Is the wait in VM provisioning (cloud) rather than in Spark itself?","Can this workload run on serverless (startup becomes a platform concern)?","If classic is required, would a pool with warm idle instances help?","Is the cost from idle interactive clusters with no or long auto-termination?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check whether serverless supports the workload.","why":"Databricks recommends serverless instead of pools where compatible."},{"do":"If classic: attach the compute to an instance pool (min idle instances, max capacity, idle auto termination).","why":"Warm VMs skip the provisioning wait."},{"do":"For idle cost: set auto-termination on all-purpose clusters; use jobs compute for automation.","why":"Interactive clusters cost money even while idle."}],"rootCauses":["VM provisioning latency","no pool / not serverless","missing or long auto-termination"],"fix":"Long startup → serverless or pool. Idle interactive cost → auto-termination (and jobs compute for production).","mnemonic":"Slow start → Serverless or Spare (pool) VMs."},{"id":"ch09-d10","title":"getNumPartitions / RDD code fails on serverless","section":"ch09-s15","symptom":"df.rdd.getNumPartitions() (or other RDD code) raises an error on serverless notebook compute.","askYourself":["Am I on serverless (Spark Connect), where RDD APIs aren't supported?","Can I get the information from the query plan or the Spark UI / query profile instead?","Do I truly need RDD APIs — if so, does that require classic (Dedicated) compute?"],"steps":[{"do":"Confirm the compute type.","why":"Serverless supports Spark Connect APIs only; no RDD APIs."},{"do":"Use the plan instead.","why":"Exchange hashpartitioning(..., N) shows shuffle partitioning.","code":"df.explain(\"formatted\")"},{"do":"If RDD APIs are mandatory, move to compatible classic compute (Dedicated if Standard can't support it).","why":"Selection algorithm: serverless can't → classic."}],"rootCauses":["RDD APIs are unsupported on serverless (Spark Connect)"],"fix":"Reason from explain/Spark UI/query profile on serverless; use compatible classic compute when RDD access is required.","mnemonic":"Serverless speaks Spark Connect, not RDD."},{"id":"ch09-d11","title":"spark.dynamicAllocation settings do nothing on Databricks","section":"ch09-s08","symptom":"You set spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled=true on a classic cluster (vanilla Spark habit) and executors don't scale as expected.","askYourself":["Am I applying a vanilla-Spark property that Databricks lists as unsupported?","Is Databricks autoscaling (min/max workers) configured instead?","On serverless, am I trying to control scaling that the platform manages?"],"steps":[{"do":"Remove the dynamic-allocation properties.","why":"Databricks lists them as unsupported on classic compute."},{"do":"Enable Databricks autoscaling with min/max workers.","why":"That is Databricks' supported scaling mechanism."}],"rootCauses":["using unsupported spark.dynamicAllocation.* properties"],"fix":"Use Databricks autoscaling (classic min/max workers) or serverless managed scaling, never spark.dynamicAllocation.","mnemonic":"On Databricks, the autoscaler allocates — not Spark."}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Counting driver cores as worker cores","text":"Driver 4 cores + 10 × 8-core workers is 80 worker cores, not 84.","fix":"Task slots = workers × cores per worker; exclude the driver."},{"title":"Expecting more than one executor per worker","text":"In Databricks classic there is one executor per worker node; 6 workers → 6 executors.","fix":"Executors = workers (classic)."},{"title":"One partition count per job","text":"Partition count is stage-specific: e.g. 64 input partitions, then 200 shuffle partitions.","fix":"Reason stage by stage."},{"title":"Huge cluster, few partitions","text":"4 partitions on 32 cores leaves 28 cores idle.","fix":"Raise useful parallelism (repartition / shuffle partitions) before adding workers."},{"title":"Too many tiny partitions","text":"1,000,000 tiny tasks make scheduling/startup overhead dominate useful work.","fix":"Aim for reasonably sized partitions; coalesce when there are far too many."},{"title":"Equal totals = equal performance","text":"2×16-core/128 GB and 8×4-core/32 GB both total 32 cores/256 GB but differ in heap, shuffle fan-out, fault impact, local disk.","fix":"Look at shape and memory per core, not just totals."},{"title":"Looking only at total GB","text":"4 concurrent 10 GB tasks on a 16 GB worker spill or OOM.","fix":"Check memory per core (e.g. 4 vs 8 GB/core)."},{"title":"Assuming worker RAM = row memory","text":"Worker RAM is shared by JVM heap (execution, cache, objects, metadata), off-heap, Python workers, Photon, OS/services.","fix":"Use memory per core as a heuristic, not RAM/cores as an exact formula."},{"title":"No cache() = no memory pressure","text":"Joins, sorts and groupBys consume execution memory even without cache().","fix":"Remember execution memory vs storage memory."},{"title":"Treating spill as failure","text":"Spill is a normal mechanism; only huge repeated spill is a performance problem.","fix":"Investigate partition size, skew, joins, shuffle partitions."},{"title":"Treating local disk as persistent","text":"Shuffle, spill, cache and temp files on worker disks vanish with the VM.","fix":"Persist results to Delta tables in cloud storage."},{"title":"OOM → double the cluster","text":"One 100 GB partition stays one task even with 100 workers.","fix":"Walk the memory debugging chain first."},{"title":"collect()/toPandas() on big data","text":"Materializes everything on the driver; driver OOM even with huge worker RAM.","fix":"Aggregate/filter on workers or write to a table."},{"title":"Autoscaling to fix low parallelism or skew","text":"Autoscaling adds slots, not partitions; a 1-partition stage stays 1 task; a straggler stays a straggler.","fix":"Fix partitioning or skew."},{"title":"Autoscaling changes worker size","text":"Classic autoscaling changes worker count only; the instance type stays the same.","fix":"Horizontal scaling only; choose a bigger worker type explicitly for scale-up."},{"title":"Using spark.dynamicAllocation on Databricks","text":"Databricks lists dynamic-allocation properties as unsupported on classic.","fix":"Use Databricks autoscaling."},{"title":"Expecting classic min/max workers on serverless notebooks","text":"Serverless scales, but not via min_workers/max_workers.","fix":"Accept platform-managed scaling or use classic if you need that control."},{"title":"Multi-node with 0 workers","text":"Non-Spark commands work on the driver, Spark commands fail.","fix":"Use Single node mode for single-machine Spark."},{"title":"Dedicated for 'important' production","text":"Dedicated is a security/capability mode, not a performance tier.","fix":"Standard by default; Dedicated only for R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime/privileged needs."},{"title":"Reading 'Auto' access mode as a third model","text":"Auto just lets Databricks choose Standard or Dedicated.","fix":"Know it prefers Standard and switches to Dedicated for ML Runtime, GPU, old DBR."},{"title":"Thinking a pool decides worker count","text":"Autoscaler decides HOW MANY; pool decides WHERE VMs come from.","fix":"Keep the two roles separate."},{"title":"Driver on spot","text":"Losing the driver disrupts the whole Spark application; losing a worker only retries tasks.","fix":"Keep the driver on stable capacity; use spot for workers in fault-tolerant batch."},{"title":"Spot for latency-sensitive streaming","text":"Real-time streaming mode requires spot off.","fix":"Use on-demand for latency-sensitive persistent processing."},{"title":"Termination = deletion","text":"Termination destroys VMs and ephemeral state but keeps the configuration and permissions.","fix":"Press Start to recreate; persist data in Delta."},{"title":"Compute ACL = data access","text":"CAN ATTACH TO doesn't grant SELECT on prod.payroll.","fix":"Grant Unity Catalog privileges separately."},{"title":"Photon = GPU / Python accelerator","text":"Photon is CPU-native vectorized SQL/DataFrame execution; Python loops and opaque UDFs gain little.","fix":"Prefer built-in functions; Photon for scans/joins/aggregations."},{"title":"Bigger driver for CPU-bound aggregations","text":"Aggregations run on workers; driver size doesn't help.","fix":"Photon / query optimization / more cores."},{"title":"getNumPartitions() on serverless","text":"RDD APIs aren't supported on serverless (Spark Connect).","fix":"Use explain / Spark UI / query profile."},{"title":"coalesce(1) collapses parallelism","text":"One partition → one task; most cores idle.","fix":"Coalesce moderately; repartition when you need more parallelism."},{"title":"Old UI screenshots","text":"The 2026 UI has no explicit multi-node selector and moved driver type under Advanced Performance (default Auto).","fix":"Expect multi-node by default; choose Single node explicitly."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"Driver 8c/32GB + 4 workers × 8c/32GB: how many VMs, executor cores, worker RAM?","a":"5 VMs; 32 executor cores; 128 GB worker RAM (driver excluded).","section":"ch09-s01"},{"q":"Name 5 things that run on the driver.","a":"SparkSession, SparkContext, query planning, DAG construction, stage/task scheduling, tracking executors, collecting results, notebook session.","section":"ch09-s02"},{"q":"Physical plan of groupBy(country).sum(amount)?","a":"Scan → Partial Aggregate → Exchange → Final Aggregate.","section":"ch09-s02"},{"q":"Workers vs executors in Databricks classic?","a":"One executor per worker node — so 6 workers = 6 executors.","section":"ch09-s02"},{"q":"Task slot rule?","a":"≈ one task slot per available executor core (1:1).","section":"ch09-s03"},{"q":"Partition → task rule?","a":"Within a stage, 1 partition → 1 task.","section":"ch09-s03"},{"q":"Waves formula?","a":"waves ≈ ceil(tasks ÷ (workers × cores)), assuming similar task durations.","section":"ch09-s03"},{"q":"100 tasks on 32 slots — waves?","a":"4 waves: 32, 32, 32, 4.","section":"ch09-s03"},{"q":"Is partition count per job or per stage?","a":"Per stage — e.g. 64 input partitions, then 200 shuffle partitions.","section":"ch09-s03"},{"q":"Why can 1,000,000 tiny partitions be bad?","a":"Scheduling / task-startup overhead dominates useful work.","section":"ch09-s03"},{"q":"Scale up vs scale out?","a":"Up = fewer, bigger workers; out = more, smaller workers.","section":"ch09-s04"},{"q":"What does cluster shape affect?","a":"Network traffic, shuffle fan-out, executor heap, per-task memory, scheduling, fault impact, local disk.","section":"ch09-s04"},{"q":"Memory per core of 4 cores / 32 GB?","a":"8 GB/core.","section":"ch09-s04"},{"q":"What does Databricks suggest when you suspect a memory problem?","a":"Try a higher memory-per-core ratio / memory-optimized workers.","section":"ch09-s04"},{"q":"Execution memory is used by…?","a":"Sort, shuffle, hash aggregation, join hash tables.","section":"ch09-s05"},{"q":"Storage memory is used by…?","a":"cache() / persisted blocks.","section":"ch09-s05"},{"q":"Spill = ?","a":"Writing intermediate data to local worker disk when RAM is insufficient, then continuing.","section":"ch09-s05"},{"q":"Spark UI spill metrics?","a":"Memory Bytes Spilled and Disk Bytes Spilled.","section":"ch09-s05"},{"q":"Huge repeated spill may indicate…?","a":"Insufficient memory, too-large partitions, skew, bad join, too few shuffle partitions.","section":"ch09-s05"},{"q":"Where are shuffle files written?","a":"On the workers' local disks; downstream tasks pull them over the network.","section":"ch09-s06"},{"q":"Heavy shuffle pressures which 4 resources?","a":"CPU, memory, network, local disk.","section":"ch09-s06"},{"q":"Is worker local disk persistent?","a":"No — temporary compute storage, lost when the VM is destroyed.","section":"ch09-s06"},{"q":"Typical driver OOM causes?","a":"collect()/toPandas() on big data, huge result/plan, many task metadata, big local Python objects.","section":"ch09-s07"},{"q":"Typical executor OOM causes?","a":"Skewed partition, large hash join, huge window partition, too few shuffle partitions, UDF memory, streaming state.","section":"ch09-s07"},{"q":"Memory debugging chain (mnemonic)?","a":"Wise Doctors Scan Symptoms, Judge Wounds, Prescribe Medicine: which task, dominant partition, skew, spill, join, window, partitions, memory/core.","section":"ch09-s07"},{"q":"Min 2 / max 8 workers of 8 cores: slot range?","a":"16 → 64 task slots.","section":"ch09-s08"},{"q":"What does classic autoscaling never change?","a":"Worker instance size/type — it only changes worker count.","section":"ch09-s08"},{"q":"Stage with 1 partition, scale 2 → 100 workers?","a":"Still 1 task; hundreds of cores idle.","section":"ch09-s08"},{"q":"spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled on Databricks?","a":"Listed as unsupported on classic — use Databricks autoscaling.","section":"ch09-s08"},{"q":"Does serverless autoscale?","a":"Yes, but platform-managed — no classic min/max workers.","section":"ch09-s08"},{"q":"Multi-node compute with 0 workers?","a":"Non-Spark commands run on the driver; Spark commands fail. Use Single node.","section":"ch09-s09"},{"q":"Access mode Auto?","a":"Not a third model: Databricks chooses Standard (preferred) or Dedicated (ML Runtime, GPU, old DBR).","section":"ch09-s09"},{"q":"Why choose Dedicated?","a":"Needed capability: R, RDD APIs, GPU, ML Runtime, privileged machine access.","section":"ch09-s09"},{"q":"Standard supports which languages?","a":"Python, SQL, Scala — not R.","section":"ch09-s09"},{"q":"Autoscaler vs pool?","a":"Autoscaler: HOW MANY. Pool: WHERE DO THE VMs COME FROM.","section":"ch09-s10"},{"q":"Pool parameters?","a":"Minimum idle instances, maximum capacity, idle instance auto termination.","section":"ch09-s10"},{"q":"Why spot is cheaper but riskier?","a":"It's spare capacity the cloud provider can reclaim.","section":"ch09-s10"},{"q":"Why keep the driver off spot?","a":"Losing the driver disrupts the whole Spark application; losing a worker only retries tasks.","section":"ch09-s10"},{"q":"What remains after termination?","a":"Config (DBR, node types, autoscaling, name, permissions), Delta tables, notebooks, UC metadata, job definitions.","section":"ch09-s11"},{"q":"Compute permission levels?","a":"CAN ATTACH TO, CAN RESTART, CAN MANAGE.","section":"ch09-s11"},{"q":"Serverless permission objects?","a":"Default Interactive Compute (notebooks/Databricks Connect) and Default Automated Compute (Jobs/Pipelines).","section":"ch09-s11"},{"q":"Photon = GPU?","a":"No — CPU-native vectorized query execution.","section":"ch09-s12"},{"q":"Serverless limitations?","a":"No R, no RDD APIs, Spark Connect APIs only, no instance pools, limited Spark configs.","section":"ch09-s13"},{"q":"Compute selection algorithm?","a":"Serverless if it supports the workload → else classic: Standard for shared DE/SQL, Dedicated for R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime.","section":"ch09-s13"},{"q":"Strong skew signal in Spark UI?","a":"Max task duration ≫ median (e.g. 15 min vs 20 s) with huge shuffle read on the max.","section":"ch09-s14"},{"q":"CPU-bound vs memory-bound vs I/O-bound?","a":"CPU high & balanced / spill+GC+OOM / CPU low while reading.","section":"ch09-s14"},{"q":"Inspect shuffle partitions setting?","a":"spark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")","section":"ch09-s15"},{"q":"repartition vs coalesce?","a":"repartition redistributes (shuffle, up or down); coalesce reduces without full redistribution.","section":"ch09-s15"},{"q":"What does Exchange hashpartitioning(customer_id, 200) mean?","a":"A shuffle redistributing data by customer_id into 200 partitions.","section":"ch09-s15"},{"q":"Streaming-specific branches of the debugging tree?","a":"State (growth, watermark, state store) and Source/Sink (throughput, API latency, storage) — on top of Spark shuffle/skew/spill/join.","section":"ch09-s14","src":"part2"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch09-e001","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Exercise 1 from the source. Driver: 4 cores / 16 GB. Workers: 10 × (8 cores / 32 GB). How many **worker cores** are there?","explain":"10 × 8 = **80** worker cores. Not 84: the driver's 4 cores are coordination capacity, not worker execution capacity. Only worker cores become task slots.","answer":80,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cores"},{"id":"ch09-e002","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Same compute (driver 4c/16GB + 10 workers of 8c/32GB). How many **cloud VMs** are you paying for?","explain":"1 driver VM + 10 worker VMs = **11** VMs. The driver doesn't add task slots, but it is still a machine you pay for — every node is a VM.","answer":11,"tolerance":0,"unit":"VMs"},{"id":"ch09-e003","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"q":"Driver: 8 cores / 32 GB. Workers: 4 × (8 cores / 32 GB). Which statement is correct?","explain":"Executors only live on workers: 4 × 8 = 32 cores and 4 × 32 = 128 GB. The tempting 40 cores / 160 GB adds the driver, which is a separate coordinator. There are 5 VMs (driver included), not 4.","options":["40 executor cores and 160 GB worker RAM","32 executor cores and 128 GB worker RAM","32 executor cores, 4 VMs in total","40 executor cores, 5 VMs in total"],"answer":1,"why":["Adds the driver's 8 cores and 32 GB — classic mistake.","Correct: only workers count as distributed capacity.","Core count is right, but there are 5 VMs (the driver is a VM too).","VM count right, core count wrong (driver cores added)."]},{"id":"ch09-e004","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"q":"Driver: 16 cores / 128 GB. Workers: 6 × (16 cores / 64 GB). How many **GB of worker RAM** in total?","explain":"6 × 64 = **384 GB**. The driver's 128 GB is not part of the distributed worker memory — it serves planning, results and notebook state.","answer":384,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB"},{"id":"ch09-e005","type":"order","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Phase 1 builds from the lowest level up. Put the execution hierarchy in the order the source teaches it (lowest → most abstract).","explain":"VM → Driver/Worker → Executor → Core → Task → Partition → Stage → Job. Machines host the driver/workers, workers host executors, executor cores run tasks, each task processes a partition, tasks form stages, stages form a job.","items":["VM","Driver / Worker","Executor","Core","Task","Partition","Stage","Job"]},{"id":"ch09-e006","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"q":"Databricks currently recommends classic compute as the default for most new workloads and serverless only for special cases.","explain":"False — it's the other way round. Databricks recommends **serverless** as the default for most new workloads, and classic when you need capabilities or configuration that serverless doesn't support.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e007","type":"match","section":"ch09-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Match each term to its definition.","explain":"The driver coordinates, the executor is the process on a worker, a task is the smallest unit of a stage, a partition is the chunk of data a task processes, and a stage is a group of tasks between shuffles.","pairs":[["Driver","Plans queries and schedules tasks"],["Executor","Spark process on a worker that runs tasks"],["Task","Smallest basic execution unit of a stage"],["Partition","Chunk of data processed by one task"],["Stage","Set of tasks that run without a shuffle in between"]]},{"id":"ch09-e008","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Certification trap: a Databricks classic compute has **6 workers**. How many Spark executors does it have?","explain":"Databricks classic runs **one executor per worker node**, so 6 workers → 6 executors. The driver is not an executor, and executors aren't multiplied by cores — cores are task slots inside an executor.","options":["6","7","6 × cores per worker","It depends on spark.executor.instances"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: one executor per worker.","The driver doesn't run an executor in multi-node compute.","Cores are task slots inside one executor, not separate executors.","That's vanilla-Spark thinking; Databricks classic fixes one executor per worker."]},{"id":"ch09-e009","type":"order","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"You run `spark.table(\"sales.orders\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` and `display(result)`. Order what the driver/cluster does.","explain":"The driver never starts adding rows itself: it builds the logical plan, optimizes it, produces a physical plan, cuts it into stages and tasks, and only then do executors process the actual partitions.","items":["Build logical plan (Aggregate ← Scan)","Optimize the plan","Produce the physical plan","Split into stages and tasks","Executors run tasks on partitions","Result returned to the notebook"]},{"id":"ch09-e010","type":"order","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Order the operators of the physical plan for `groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")`.","explain":"Scan reads partitions, Partial Aggregate sums per country inside each partition, Exchange shuffles partial results by country, Final Aggregate combines them. The Exchange is the stage boundary.","items":["Scan","Partial Aggregate","Exchange","Final Aggregate"]},{"id":"ch09-e011","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","compare"],"q":"Driver or workers? Sort each responsibility.","explain":"The driver is coordination/control: sessions, planning, DAG and scheduling, notebook state. Workers do distributed processing: they scan, aggregate and shuffle the actual partitions.","buckets":["Driver","Workers (executors)"],"items":[{"text":"SparkContext / SparkSession","bucket":0},{"text":"Query planning and DAG construction","bucket":0},{"text":"Task scheduling and tracking executors","bucket":0},{"text":"Notebook interpreter / session state","bucket":0},{"text":"Scanning partition 37 of sales.orders","bucket":1},{"text":"Partial aggregation of rows in a partition","bucket":1},{"text":"Writing shuffle files to local disk","bucket":1},{"text":"Collecting task metadata/results","bucket":0}]},{"id":"ch09-e012","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"In Databricks classic documentation, *worker* and *executor* are often used almost interchangeably because Databricks runs one executor per worker node.","explain":"True. In vanilla Spark the mapping can be more complex, but Databricks classic runs exactly one Spark executor per worker node, so the terms are near-synonyms there.","answer":true},{"id":"ch09-e013","type":"odd","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Which one is NOT a driver responsibility?","explain":"Tracking executors, stage scheduling and holding the SparkContext are all coordination jobs of the driver. Scanning a partition is actual data processing — done by a task on an executor.","options":["Tracking executors","Stage scheduling","Scanning partition 37 of sales.orders","Holding the SparkContext"],"answer":2},{"id":"ch09-e014","type":"free","section":"ch09-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","concept"],"q":"Interview: \"When I run a groupBy-sum in a notebook, does the driver add up all the amounts?\" Answer in your own words.","explain":"A strong answer separates planning from execution and mentions the physical plan.","model":"No. The driver builds a logical plan, optimizes it and creates a physical plan (Scan → Partial Aggregate → Exchange → Final Aggregate). It splits that into stages and tasks and schedules them on the executors. The workers scan their partitions, compute partial sums, shuffle by key and compute the final sums; the driver only coordinates and receives the (small) result.","rubric":["Driver builds/optimizes plans","Physical plan: partial aggregate, exchange, final aggregate","Workers process the partitions","Driver coordinates/schedules and receives results"]},{"id":"ch09-e015","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"4 workers × 8 cores. A stage has **100 partitions**. Roughly how many **waves** of tasks?","explain":"Slots = 4 × 8 = 32. 100 / 32 = 3.125 → **4 waves** (32, 32, 32, 4). Conceptual only — real tasks vary in duration.","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch09-e016","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"q":"Same 32 slots. In the **last** wave of that 100-task stage, how many tasks run?","explain":"Three full waves run 96 tasks; the last wave runs 100 − 96 = **4** tasks while 28 slots sit idle. That idle tail is why partition counts that are multiples of the slot count are slightly more efficient.","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"tasks"},{"id":"ch09-e017","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"4 workers × 8 cores. After a groupBy, Stage 2 has **200 shuffle partitions**. How many waves?","explain":"200 / 32 = 6.25 → you need **7 waves** (round up). Stage 1 with 64 input partitions needs only 2 waves.","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch09-e018","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"q":"Exercise 2: 6 workers × 16 cores per worker. How many **task slots**?","explain":"6 × 16 = **96** task slots: one slot per executor core, driver excluded.","answer":96,"tolerance":0,"unit":"slots"},{"id":"ch09-e019","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"q":"Exercise 2 continued: 96 task slots, stage with **768 partitions**. Approximately how many waves?","explain":"768 / 96 = **8 waves**, if all tasks have similar duration. Skew would break that neat picture.","answer":8,"tolerance":0,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch09-e020","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"q":"Cluster with 32 available cores reads a dataset with only **4 partitions**. How many cores sit idle during that stage?","explain":"4 partitions → 4 tasks → 4 busy cores, **28 idle**. A huge cluster without enough parallel work is wasted money.","answer":28,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cores"},{"id":"ch09-e021","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"`orders` has 64 input partitions; you run `groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")` and Spark uses 200 shuffle partitions. How many tasks does the post-shuffle stage have?","explain":"The downstream stage processes shuffle partitions, so it has **200** tasks. The 64 belongs to Stage 1 (scan). Partition count is stage-specific.","options":["64","200","264","32 (one per core)"],"answer":1,"why":["That's Stage 1 (the scan stage).","Correct: one task per shuffle partition.","Tasks of different stages aren't added into one stage.","Cores are slots; they don't determine the number of tasks."]},{"id":"ch09-e022","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"q":"A Spark job has one partition count that applies to all of its stages.","explain":"False. **Partition count is stage-specific**: e.g. the scan stage has 64 input partitions and the post-groupBy stage has 200 shuffle partitions.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e023","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"q":"Having 1,000 partitions on a 32-core cluster is a problem simply because there are more partitions than cores.","explain":"False. They just run 32 at a time until done. The problem only appears when partitions become *tiny* in huge numbers (e.g. 1,000,000), so scheduling/task-startup overhead dominates.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e024","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"A stage creates **1,000,000 tiny tasks**. What becomes the significant cost?","explain":"Each task has scheduling and startup cost; with a million tiny tasks that overhead rivals or exceeds the useful work. It's not about cores being too few — 32 at a time is fine for 1,000 normal-sized tasks.","options":["Scheduling / task-startup overhead","Driver RAM for storing Delta tables","Spot instance reclamation","Photon compilation of each row"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e025","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","concept"],"q":"Fill in the sizing formulas.","explain":"Slots come from workers × cores per worker (driver excluded); each slot runs ~1 task; tasks beyond the slot count run in waves.","text":"Task slots = [[workers|number of workers]] × [[cores per worker|cores]]. Within a stage, 1 partition → 1 [[task]]. Waves ≈ tasks ÷ [[slots|task slots]], rounded up.","bank":["driver cores","executors per core","stages"]},{"id":"ch09-e026","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Worker A: 4 cores / 16 GB. What is its **memory per core** (GB)?","explain":"16 / 4 = **4 GB/core**. Compare with 4 cores / 32 GB = 8 GB/core — the same number of tasks, each with twice the memory headroom.","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB/core"},{"id":"ch09-e027","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Each task needs about 10 GB of working memory. A worker with 4 cores / 16 GB runs 4 concurrent tasks. What is the **potential working set** in GB?","explain":"4 × 10 = **40 GB** of potential working set in a 16 GB worker → expect spill or OOM. A 4-core / 64 GB worker has a much better memory-per-core ratio for this workload.","answer":40,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB"},{"id":"ch09-e028","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","concept"],"q":"Cluster A: 2 workers × 16 cores × 128 GB. Cluster B: 8 workers × 4 cores × 32 GB. Which statement is true?","explain":"Both total 32 cores / 256 GB, but behaviour can differ: A has big executor heaps and fewer network peers; B has more shuffle fan-out and smaller fault impact per worker. Equal totals ≠ equal performance.","options":["They will perform identically because totals are equal","Totals are equal, but network, shuffle fan-out, heap size and fault impact differ","B has more total cores","A is scale out, B is scale up"],"answer":1,"why":["Totals alone don't determine behaviour.","Correct.","Both have 32 cores.","Reversed: A (few large) = scale up, B (many small) = scale out."]},{"id":"ch09-e029","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"q":"Two clusters with identical total cores and RAM will behave identically.","explain":"False. Shape matters: network traffic, shuffle fan-out, executor heap size, per-task memory, scheduling, fault impact and local disk all depend on how capacity is split across workers.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e030","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"q":"Scale up (few large workers) or scale out (many small workers)? Sort the characteristics.","explain":"Few large workers = bigger executor heaps and fewer network peers, but each lost worker removes a big share of capacity. Many small workers = more shuffle fan-out over the network but smaller fault impact per node.","buckets":["Scale up (few large)","Scale out (many small)"],"items":[{"text":"Cluster A: 2 × 16 cores × 128 GB","bucket":0},{"text":"Cluster B: 8 × 4 cores × 32 GB","bucket":1},{"text":"Larger executor heap per worker","bucket":0},{"text":"More shuffle fan-out between nodes","bucket":1},{"text":"Losing one worker removes a large share of capacity","bucket":0},{"text":"Losing one worker removes a small share of capacity","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e031","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"You suspect a memory problem in a shuffle-heavy job. Which experiment does Databricks suggest first among these?","explain":"Databricks suggests trying a **higher memory-per-core ratio** (e.g. memory-optimized workers) to see whether behaviour changes. More workers of the same type don't change per-task memory; a bigger driver doesn't help executors.","options":["Try workers with a higher memory-per-core ratio","Add more workers of the same type","Increase the driver size","Turn off Photon"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e032","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","compare"],"q":"Cluster A (2 workers) loses one worker to a failure. What **percent** of worker capacity is lost?","explain":"1 of 2 workers = **50%**. In Cluster B (8 workers) one failure costs only 12.5%. That's the *fault impact* difference between scale up and scale out.","answer":50,"tolerance":0,"unit":"%"},{"id":"ch09-e033","type":"odd","section":"ch09-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Which is NOT one of the things that differ between scale up and scale out?","explain":"Network traffic, shuffle fan-out and per-task memory all depend on cluster shape. The Delta table storage format is a property of the data in cloud storage, independent of compute shape.","options":["Network traffic","Shuffle fan-out","Per-task memory","Delta table storage format"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch09-e034","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Execution memory or storage memory?","explain":"Execution memory serves operations in flight (sort, shuffle, hash aggregation, join hash tables). Storage memory holds cached/persisted data. With unified memory management they can share managed memory to some degree.","buckets":["Execution memory","Storage memory"],"items":[{"text":"sort","bucket":0},{"text":"shuffle","bucket":0},{"text":"hash aggregation","bucket":0},{"text":"join hash tables","bucket":0},{"text":"df.cache()","bucket":1},{"text":"persisted blocks","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e035","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"If you never call `cache()`, Spark memory pressure can't happen.","explain":"False. **join / sort / groupBy** use execution memory even without any cache(). This is exactly the point interviewers want you to know.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e036","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"q":"If the Spark UI shows Disk Bytes Spilled, the job has failed.","explain":"False. **Spill ≠ failure**: Spark is designed so operations can spill to local disk and continue. Only huge, repeated spill is a problem — mostly for speed.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e037","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"A stage shows huge, repeated spill. Which can be causes? (select all)","explain":"Huge repeated spill points to insufficient memory, too-large partitions, skew, a bad join, or too few shuffle partitions. More workers don't cause spill, and Photon isn't a spill cause listed here.","multi":true,"options":["Insufficient memory","Too-large partitions","Skew","Too few shuffle partitions","Too many workers"],"answer":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"ch09-e038","type":"match","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Match each workload element to the worker-memory consumer it uses.","explain":"Cached DataFrames live in the JVM heap as cached data; hash joins use execution memory; Python processes run outside the JVM; Photon uses native memory; the OS and Databricks services also take their share.","pairs":[["df.cache() blocks","JVM heap: cached data"],["Hash join build side","JVM heap: Spark execution memory"],["Python UDF processes","Python worker processes"],["Photon vectorized operators","Photon / native processing"],["Node agents and the operating system","OS / Databricks services"]]},{"id":"ch09-e039","type":"order","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Order what happens when a task spills.","explain":"The task needs more memory than available, Spark writes intermediate data to the worker's local disk, processing continues, and the Spark UI records Memory/Disk Bytes Spilled.","items":["Task's intermediate data outgrows available RAM","Spark writes intermediate data to local worker disk","Processing continues","Spark UI shows Memory/Disk Bytes Spilled"]},{"id":"ch09-e040","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"q":"Name the two Spark UI spill metrics.","explain":"Memory Bytes Spilled is the in-memory size of the spilled data; Disk Bytes Spilled is its size on disk.","text":"Spark UI spill metrics: [[Memory]] Bytes Spilled and [[Disk]] Bytes Spilled.","bank":["Shuffle","Network","Cache"]},{"id":"ch09-e041","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"q":"A worker has 32 GB RAM. How much is available for your rows?","explain":"Worker RAM is shared by the JVM heap (execution, cache, JVM objects, metadata), off-heap/native memory, Python workers, Photon and OS/Databricks services. It's not a simple RAM ÷ cores formula — memory per core is a heuristic.","options":["All 32 GB","Exactly 32 GB ÷ cores per task","Less: RAM is shared by JVM heap, off-heap, Python workers, Photon and OS/services","Only what was cached with cache()"],"answer":2},{"id":"ch09-e042","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"A daily aggregation got 3× slower. No failure, but the Spark UI shows tens of GB of Disk Bytes Spilled and high GC time spread across **all** tasks of the shuffle stage.","explain":"Spill is a symptom: find out why it spills.","steps":[{"prompt":"First interpretation?","options":[{"text":"Memory pressure across all tasks — not a single skewed task","ok":true,"fb":"Uniform spill points to partitions too large / memory per core too low rather than skew."},{"text":"The job has failed and must be restarted","ok":false,"fb":"Spill ≠ failure; the job is just slow."},{"text":"Driver OOM","ok":false,"fb":"The spill is in executor tasks."}]},{"prompt":"What do you check next?","options":[{"text":"Partition size and spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","ok":true,"fb":"Too few shuffle partitions → each partition too big → spill."},{"text":"The compute permissions","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions don't affect memory."},{"text":"The auto-termination timeout","ok":false,"fb":"Cost setting, not performance."}]},{"prompt":"Partitions are reasonable now, but spill remains high. Next lever?","options":[{"text":"Higher memory per core / memory-optimized workers","ok":true,"fb":"Databricks recommends them for memory-intensive shuffle/spill workloads."},{"text":"More workers of the same small type","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't change memory per task."},{"text":"coalesce(1)","ok":false,"fb":"Makes partitions even bigger."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e043","type":"order","section":"ch09-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Huge spill is slowing a stage. Order your investigation.","explain":"Size the spill, see whether it's concentrated (skew) or spread, check partition size/shuffle partitions, look at the join, and only then change memory per core.","items":["Is the spill huge and repeated?","Concentrated in one task or spread across all?","Partitions too large / too few shuffle partitions?","Is a join building large hash tables?","Try higher memory per core / memory-optimized workers"]},{"id":"ch09-e044","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"During `df.groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")`, where do map-side tasks put their shuffle output?","explain":"Map-side tasks write shuffle files to their worker's **local disk**; downstream tasks pull the blocks over the **network**. Shuffle data is not written to the Delta table or the driver.","options":["On each worker's local disk, fetched over the network","In the Delta table's cloud storage","In the driver's memory","In the Unity Catalog metastore"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e045","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Spill and shuffle files on worker local disks survive cluster termination, so the next run can reuse them.","explain":"False. Local worker disk is **temporary compute storage**: when the VM is destroyed, shuffle, cache, temp and spill files can be lost. Persistent data belongs in Delta tables in cloud storage.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e046","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Heavy shuffle puts pressure on which resources? (select all)","explain":"Shuffle serializes data (CPU), buffers it (memory), writes it locally (local disk) and transfers it between workers (network). The UC metastore isn't involved in moving shuffle blocks.","multi":true,"options":["CPU","Memory","Network","Local disk","Unity Catalog metastore"],"answer":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"ch09-e047","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Lost when the cluster stops, or persists?","explain":"Anything on worker local disk or in memory is ephemeral. Delta data lives in cloud storage and table metadata in Unity Catalog.","buckets":["Lost (temporary compute storage)","Persists"],"items":[{"text":"Shuffle files","bucket":0},{"text":"Cached partitions","bucket":0},{"text":"Spill files","bucket":0},{"text":"Temp files","bucket":0},{"text":"Delta table data","bucket":1},{"text":"Unity Catalog metadata","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e048","type":"order","section":"ch09-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Order the life of a groupBy shuffle.","explain":"Map-side tasks process their partitions and write shuffle output locally; downstream tasks fetch blocks over the network and compute the final aggregate.","items":["Map-side tasks compute partial aggregates","Shuffle files written to each worker's local disk","Downstream tasks fetch shuffle blocks over the network","Final aggregate computed per key"]},{"id":"ch09-e049","type":"free","section":"ch09-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"A colleague caches a cleaned DataFrame on an all-purpose cluster and says \"tomorrow we'll just reuse the cache\". Explain the problem.","explain":"Cache and local files are ephemeral; persistence means writing to Delta.","model":"Cached partitions live in executor memory/local disk, which is temporary compute storage. When the cluster terminates (e.g. auto-termination overnight), the worker VMs are destroyed and the cache is lost. To reuse the data tomorrow, write it to a Delta table in persistent cloud storage.","rubric":["Cache lives on executors / local disk","Termination destroys worker VMs","Ephemeral state is lost","Persist to a Delta table"]},{"id":"ch09-e050","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Workers: 20 × 64 GB. Driver: 8 GB. You run `rows = df.collect()` on a 100 GB result. What happens?","explain":"collect() brings every row to the driver. 100 GB into an 8 GB driver → **driver OOM**, while workers are fine. The 1,280 GB of worker RAM is irrelevant for a driver-side result.","options":["Works: 1,280 GB of worker RAM is plenty","Driver OOM — the workers are fine","Executor OOM on one worker","Spark spills the result to the driver's disk and continues"],"answer":1,"why":["Aggregate worker RAM doesn't help the driver.","Correct.","The workers just send their partitions; the driver collapses.","collect() results must fit in driver memory."]},{"id":"ch09-e051","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Driver OOM or executor OOM? Sort the causes.","explain":"Driver OOM comes from things concentrated on the coordinator (collected results, huge plans, task metadata, local Python objects). Executor OOM comes from heavy per-task work (skew, hash joins, windows, too few partitions, UDF memory, streaming state).","buckets":["Driver OOM","Executor OOM"],"items":[{"text":"df.toPandas() on a huge table","bucket":0},{"text":"huge query plan","bucket":0},{"text":"too many task metadata","bucket":0},{"text":"large local Python structures","bucket":0},{"text":"skewed partition","bucket":1},{"text":"large hash join","bucket":1},{"text":"huge window partition","bucket":1},{"text":"too few shuffle partitions","bucket":1},{"text":"stateful streaming state","bucket":1},{"text":"large UDF memory","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e052","type":"order","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"An executor dies with OOM. Put the diagnosis chain in order (before touching cluster size).","explain":"Locate the failing executor/task, check whether one partition dominates, which points to skew; then look for spill, join and window issues, the shuffle partition count, and only finally memory per core. Mnemonic: Wise Doctors Scan Symptoms, Judge Wounds, Prescribe Medicine.","items":["Which executor/task failed?","Does one partition dominate?","Is it skew?","Is there heavy spill?","Is a join the culprit?","Is a window the culprit?","Too few partitions?","Memory per core?"]},{"id":"ch09-e053","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"q":"If one partition is 100 GB and causes OOM, scaling to 100 workers solves it.","explain":"False. A single task must still process the whole 100 GB partition — 99 workers just sit idle. Fix the partitioning/skew instead.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e054","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Nightly job: `orders.join(customers, \"customer_id\").groupBy(\"country\").sum(\"amount\")`. Stage 7 fails: *ExecutorLostFailure … OutOfMemoryError*. Your manager says \"double the cluster\".","explain":"Doubling first is bad reasoning. The chain is: which task → dominant partition → skew → spill/join/window → partitions → memory per core.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Double the number of workers and rerun","ok":false,"fb":"Hardware first is the bad-reasoning path: if one partition is huge, extra workers stay idle."},{"text":"Open stage 7 in the Spark UI and find which tasks failed / are biggest","ok":true,"fb":"Right — locate the failing executor/task and compare it with the others."},{"text":"Switch the cluster to Dedicated access mode","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode is a capability/security setting, not a memory fix."}]},{"prompt":"Task metrics: median shuffle read 1.5 GB, max 180 GB (the failing task), heavy spill on that task only. Diagnosis?","options":[{"text":"Skew on the join key — one partition dominates","ok":true,"fb":"Correct: max ≫ median with huge shuffle read on one task is the skew signature."},{"text":"Too few cores overall","ok":false,"fb":"Most tasks finished quickly; capacity isn't the limit."},{"text":"Driver OOM","ok":false,"fb":"The error is ExecutorLost on a task — it's an executor problem."}]},{"prompt":"Best next move?","options":[{"text":"Address the skewed key's data distribution, then rerun","ok":true,"fb":"Skew is a data-distribution problem; fix the distribution."},{"text":"Double RAM per worker and hope","ok":false,"fb":"More RAM may reduce failure/spill but doesn't redistribute skewed data."},{"text":"coalesce(1) to simplify the stage","ok":false,"fb":"That collapses parallelism and makes it far worse."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e055","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"A data scientist runs `pdf = df.toPandas()` on a 60 GB table. The notebook detaches with a driver error. Workers: 10 × 64 GB, driver: 16 GB.","explain":"toPandas() materializes distributed data on the driver; keep work distributed or reduce data first.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which component failed and why?","options":[{"text":"Driver: toPandas() pulled 60 GB into a 16 GB driver","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — aggregate worker RAM doesn't matter for driver-side materialization."},{"text":"Executors: 60 GB is too much for 10 workers","ok":false,"fb":"640 GB of worker RAM handles 60 GB easily; the workers weren't the problem."},{"text":"Spot reclamation of a worker","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing points to a lost worker; the failure follows toPandas()."}]},{"prompt":"What's the best fix?","options":[{"text":"Aggregate/filter/sample in Spark first, toPandas() only the small result (or write to a table)","ok":true,"fb":"Keeps the heavy lifting distributed."},{"text":"Add 10 more workers","ok":false,"fb":"Workers don't help a driver-side collect."},{"text":"Enable Photon","ok":false,"fb":"Photon accelerates SQL operators; it doesn't shrink what you collect to the driver."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e056","type":"spotbug","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"q":"This notebook keeps crashing the driver on a 2 TB orders table. Click the buggy line.","explain":"`collect()` pulls all 2 TB onto the driver → driver OOM. Aggregate on the workers and write the result to a table (or collect only the small aggregate).","lines":["orders = spark.table(\"main.shop.orders\")","paid = orders.filter(\"status = 'PAID'\")","rows = paid.collect()","total = sum(r['amount'] for r in rows)","print(total)"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\ntotal = paid.agg(F.sum(\"amount\")).first()[0]   # aggregate on workers, bring back 1 row"},{"id":"ch09-e057","type":"odd","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"q":"Three of these are typical *executor* memory-failure causes listed by Databricks. Which one is the odd one out?","explain":"Skew, large broadcasts and unpartitioned windows all blow up memory inside executors. `toPandas()` materializes data on the **driver** — a driver OOM cause.","options":["Skewed partitions","Large broadcasts","Unpartitioned windows","df.toPandas() on a big table"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch09-e058","type":"free","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","debug"],"q":"Interview: \"What's the difference between a driver OOM and an executor OOM, and how would you debug each?\"","explain":"Name causes on both sides and a diagnosis-before-hardware approach.","model":"Driver OOM happens on the coordinator, typically from collect()/toPandas() of large data, huge results or plans, many task metadata or big local Python objects — workers may be perfectly fine. I'd find the action that pulls data to the driver and keep the work distributed. Executor OOM happens inside a worker during task processing: skewed partitions, big hash joins/broadcasts, huge windows, too few shuffle partitions, UDF memory or streaming state. I'd open the stage in the Spark UI, find the failing task, compare max vs median partition size, check spill/join/window and shuffle partitions, and only then consider higher memory per core.","rubric":["Driver OOM causes (collect/toPandas, plan/result size)","Executor OOM causes (skew, joins, windows, partitions)","Debug with Spark UI task metrics","Hardware/memory-per-core as last step"]},{"id":"ch09-e059","type":"order","section":"ch09-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"The driver crashed right after a notebook cell ran. Order your driver-OOM checks.","explain":"Confirm it's the driver, find the action that pulled data to it, compare the result size with driver RAM, keep the work distributed — and only then consider a bigger driver. Mnemonic: the driver is a desk, not a warehouse.","items":["Confirm the driver (not an executor) failed","Find the action that pulled data to the driver (collect/toPandas)","Compare result size with driver RAM","Aggregate/filter on workers or write to a table instead","Only if truly needed: larger driver type"]},{"id":"ch09-e060","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Classic autoscaling: worker = 8 cores / 32 GB, min 2, max 8. **Minimum** task slots?","explain":"2 × 8 = **16** task slots at the minimum. The worker type never changes, only the count.","answer":16,"tolerance":0,"unit":"slots"},{"id":"ch09-e061","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Same autoscaling config (8-core workers, min 2, max 8). **Maximum** task slots?","explain":"8 × 8 = **64** task slots. Capacity can move e.g. 16 → 24 → 40 → 64 depending on the workload.","answer":64,"tolerance":0,"unit":"slots"},{"id":"ch09-e062","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"q":"When classic autoscaling scales from 2 to 8 workers, it may also switch to a larger worker instance type.","explain":"False. Autoscaling is **horizontal**: it changes the number of workers, and each stays 8 cores / 32 GB. It never turns 2 huge VMs into 8 tiny ones or vice versa.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e063","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"q":"Exercise 3: 4 workers × 8 cores; a stage has 30 partitions. Autoscaling grows to 8 workers. How many cores are **idle** in that stage after scaling?","explain":"8 × 8 = 64 slots but only 30 tasks → **34 idle** cores. Before scaling, 32 slots already ran all 30 tasks at once, so the stage gains essentially nothing.","answer":34,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cores"},{"id":"ch09-e064","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"A stage takes 40 minutes. You raise max workers from 2 to 100 and autoscaling adds them — still 40 minutes. The Spark UI shows the stage has **1 task**.","explain":"Autoscaling adds slots, not partitions. One partition → one task → one busy core.","steps":[{"prompt":"Why didn't 98 more workers help?","options":[{"text":"The stage has 1 partition → 1 task; extra cores are idle","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly: autoscaling doesn't magically create partitions."},{"text":"Autoscaling was too slow to add workers","ok":false,"fb":"The workers were added; there was simply no work for them."},{"text":"Photon was off","ok":false,"fb":"Photon wouldn't split one task into many."}]},{"prompt":"Upstream code contains `df = df.coalesce(1)`. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Remove it / repartition to a sensible number before the heavy work","ok":true,"fb":"Restores parallelism the cluster can use."},{"text":"Raise max workers to 200","ok":false,"fb":"Still 1 task."},{"text":"Switch to a bigger driver","ok":false,"fb":"The driver isn't running the task."}]},{"prompt":"After fixing, how do you verify?","options":[{"text":"Check the stage's task count in the Spark UI (or getNumPartitions on classic)","ok":true,"fb":"Tasks per stage should now be close to the partition count you set."},{"text":"Check the cluster's autoscaling min/max","ok":false,"fb":"Configuration doesn't prove the stage now has parallel tasks."},{"text":"Check the DBR version","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to partitioning."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e065","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"q":"Stage of 100 partitions: 99 × 100 MB and 1 × **200 GB**. You autoscale 4 → 40 workers. What happens?","explain":"The 99 small tasks finish quickly either way; the job ends when the **straggler** (200 GB task) finishes. The problem is skew, not insufficient parallel capacity.","options":["The job becomes ~10× faster","Small tasks finish fast; the 200 GB straggler still decides job time","Spark automatically splits the 200 GB partition across the new workers","The job fails because autoscaling can't exceed 32 workers"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e066","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Coming from vanilla Spark, you set `spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled=true` on Databricks classic compute. What's correct?","explain":"Databricks explicitly lists the dynamic-allocation properties as **unsupported** on classic and tells you to use **Databricks autoscaling** (min/max workers). It's a favourite exam/interview distinction.","options":["It's the recommended way to autoscale on Databricks","It's unsupported; use Databricks autoscaling instead","It only works with Photon","It works only on serverless"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e067","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Serverless compute has no autoscaling at all — that's why there's no min/max workers field.","explain":"False. Serverless **does** autoscale; Databricks manages instances, capacity, scaling and runtime. There's no min/max field because scaling is platform-managed, not because it doesn't exist.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e068","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"q":"Complete the exam rule.","explain":"Classic autoscaling is horizontal: it changes worker count between your min and max, never the instance type.","text":"Classic autoscaling is [[horizontal]] scaling: it changes the [[number of workers|worker count]], not the [[worker instance size|instance size|worker size|worker type]].","bank":["vertical","driver size","partition count"]},{"id":"ch09-e069","type":"order","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Autoscaling isn't speeding up a slow stage. Order your checks.","explain":"Count tasks first, compare with slots, look for a collapsed partition count, check for a skewed straggler, and only then change partitioning — not max workers.","items":["Count the tasks of the slow stage","Compute task slots (workers × cores)","Check for coalesce(1) / single-partition source","Check for a skewed straggler task","Repartition / fix skew instead of adding workers"]},{"id":"ch09-e070","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"q":"A Spark veteran sets `spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled true` in a classic cluster's Spark config, expecting executors to scale. Nothing scales as hoped.","explain":"On Databricks, the autoscaler allocates — not Spark.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's wrong?","options":[{"text":"Databricks lists dynamic-allocation properties as unsupported on classic","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — use Databricks autoscaling."},{"text":"The property name needs a databricks. prefix","ok":false,"fb":"There's no such supported variant."},{"text":"Dynamic allocation only works on serverless","ok":false,"fb":"Serverless scaling is platform-managed; no Spark property controls it."}]},{"prompt":"Fix?","options":[{"text":"Remove the property and enable autoscaling with min/max workers","ok":true,"fb":"That's the supported mechanism."},{"text":"Add more spark.dynamicAllocation.* properties","ok":false,"fb":"Still unsupported."},{"text":"Switch to Dedicated","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode doesn't control scaling."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e071","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"A multi-node compute configured with 0 workers can still run Spark commands, because the driver will do the work.","explain":"False. Docs: without worker nodes, **non-Spark** commands run on the driver but **Spark commands fail**. For single-machine Spark choose **Single node**, a separate mode where the driver also acts as worker.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e072","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Your team needs **R** notebooks on classic compute. Which access mode?","explain":"Standard supports Python, SQL and Scala but **not R**. R is one of the capabilities that requires **Dedicated**. Auto would pick for you, but the requirement itself means Dedicated.","options":["Standard","Dedicated","Any — access mode doesn't affect languages","Serverless"],"answer":1,"why":["Standard doesn't support R.","Correct: R requires Dedicated.","Access mode does constrain capabilities like R and RDD APIs.","Serverless has no R either."]},{"id":"ch09-e073","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","concept"],"q":"The current compute UI shows **Access mode: Auto**. What does that mean?","explain":"Auto isn't a third semantic model: Databricks picks Standard or Dedicated depending on runtime/hardware. It prefers Standard but switches to Dedicated for e.g. ML Runtime, GPU or a fairly old DBR.","options":["A third access model between Standard and Dedicated","Databricks chooses Standard or Dedicated based on runtime/hardware","Access is granted automatically to all workspace users","The compute switches modes at runtime per query"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e074","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"q":"Standard is enough, or Dedicated needed?","explain":"Standard covers shared DE/SQL work in Python, SQL and Scala. R, RDD APIs, GPU, ML Runtime and privileged machine access are Dedicated territory.","buckets":["Standard is fine","Dedicated needed"],"items":[{"text":"Shared PySpark ETL development by a team","bucket":0},{"text":"SQL analysis","bucket":0},{"text":"Scala Spark job","bucket":0},{"text":"R notebooks","bucket":1},{"text":"Legacy code using RDD APIs","bucket":1},{"text":"GPU deep-learning training","bucket":1},{"text":"ML Runtime","bucket":1},{"text":"Privileged / low-level machine access","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e075","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"q":"Dedicated access mode should be chosen for important production jobs because it is the higher-performance tier.","explain":"False. Dedicated is a **security/capability mode**, not a performance tier. Choose it only when the workload needs something Standard can't do (R, RDD, GPU, ML Runtime, privileged access).","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e076","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Which workload is NOT a good fit for Single node compute?","explain":"Single node (driver also acts as worker) suits small development, small datasets, some local ML and non-distributed work — not multi-TB Spark ETL, which needs distributed workers.","options":["Small development notebook","Some local ML on a small dataset","Multi-TB Spark ETL","Non-distributed Python workload"],"answer":2},{"id":"ch09-e077","type":"match","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"2026 UI nuance: match each element to how the current UI handles it.","explain":"Compute is multi-node by default, Single node is chosen explicitly, driver type moved under Advanced Performance (default Auto), and access mode can be Auto. Old tutorials show a different UI.","pairs":[["Multi-node selector","Gone: compute is multi-node by default"],["Single node","Chosen explicitly when desired"],["Driver type","Under Advanced Performance, defaults to Auto"],["Access mode Auto","Databricks picks Standard or Dedicated"]]},{"id":"ch09-e078","type":"odd","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Auto access mode normally prefers Standard. Which of these does NOT make it switch to Dedicated?","explain":"Auto moves to Dedicated when you pick ML Runtime, GPU or a fairly old DBR. Photon is an engine toggle and doesn't require Dedicated.","options":["Selecting ML Runtime","Selecting a GPU instance","Selecting a fairly old DBR","Enabling Photon"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch09-e079","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"q":"A colleague created a multi-node compute with workers = 0 to save money. `print(1+1)` works, but `spark.range(10).count()` fails.","explain":"Zero workers ≠ Single node.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's going on?","options":[{"text":"Multi-node with 0 workers: non-Spark commands run on the driver, Spark commands fail","ok":true,"fb":"That's exactly the documented behaviour."},{"text":"The DBR version is too old for spark.range","ok":false,"fb":"spark.range is basic Spark; the issue is the missing workers."},{"text":"Photon doesn't support range()","ok":false,"fb":"Photon isn't the problem here."}]},{"prompt":"They only need small single-machine Spark. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Use Single node compute","ok":true,"fb":"Single node is the separate mode where the driver acts as worker."},{"text":"Switch access mode to Dedicated","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode doesn't add workers."},{"text":"Set spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled=true","ok":false,"fb":"Unsupported on Databricks and wouldn't create a worker anyway."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e080","type":"order","section":"ch09-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Spark commands fail on a compute with 0 workers. Order the fix path.","explain":"Check node mode and worker count, confirm the symptom (non-Spark ok, Spark fails), then pick Single node for single-machine Spark or add workers for distributed work.","items":["Check node mode and worker count","Confirm non-Spark commands work but Spark commands fail","Decide: single-machine or distributed?","Single node mode — or add ≥1 worker"]},{"id":"ch09-e081","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"A cluster attached to a pool autoscales from 2 to 6 workers. Who decides that 4 more workers are needed?","explain":"The **autoscaler** decides HOW MANY (+4). The **pool** only answers WHERE THE VMs COME FROM (4 warm instances). A pool never decides Spark's worker count.","options":["The pool","The autoscaler","The cloud provider","The driver's SparkContext via dynamic allocation"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e082","type":"match","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each component to the question it answers.","explain":"Autoscaler: how many workers. Pool: where the VMs come from. Compute: the Spark execution environment. Serverless: the platform manages capacity itself.","pairs":[["Autoscaler","HOW MANY workers?"],["Pool","WHERE DO the VMs come from?"],["Compute (cluster)","WHERE does Spark execute?"],["Serverless","Capacity is a platform concern"]]},{"id":"ch09-e083","type":"order","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Order the start-up path of a classic cluster **without** a pool.","explain":"Without a pool every start asks the cloud for VMs and waits for provisioning before the runtime is prepared and Spark starts — that wait is what pools remove.","items":["Start cluster","Ask cloud provider for VMs","Wait for VM provisioning","Prepare runtime","Spark starts"]},{"id":"ch09-e084","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"One instance pool can serve only one cluster.","explain":"False. A pool is a reservoir of infrastructure instances and can serve several computes (Cluster A, B, C).","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e085","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"A spot worker is reclaimed mid-job. What typically happens, and which workload suits spot best?","explain":"Spark can often retry the lost tasks elsewhere (task lost → schedule elsewhere), which makes spot attractive for **fault-tolerant batch**. Latency-sensitive streaming is a poor fit.","options":["The whole job always fails; spot suits nothing","Lost tasks are rescheduled; good for fault-tolerant batch","Data in Delta is lost; use only for dev","Spark switches the driver to spot"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e086","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Current Databricks real-time streaming mode requires spot instances to be turned off.","explain":"True (per the source). Interruptions from reclaimed spot capacity are bad for latency-sensitive persistent processing.","answer":true},{"id":"ch09-e087","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"q":"Why do architectures usually keep the **driver** on stable (on-demand) capacity even when workers use spot?","explain":"Losing a worker means some tasks retry. Losing the driver disrupts the **Spark application itself**. So driver reliability is separated from worker cost optimization.","options":["The driver is cheaper on-demand","Losing the driver disrupts the whole Spark application; losing a worker only retries tasks","Spot can't run the SparkContext binary","Unity Catalog requires on-demand drivers"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e088","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Serverless compute lets you attach your own instance pool to reduce startup time.","explain":"False. Serverless doesn't support user-configured instance pools — it makes the pool problem a platform concern, which is why Databricks recommends serverless instead of pools where compatible.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e089","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","concept"],"q":"Name the three pool parameters from the source (AWS implementation).","explain":"Minimum idle instances keep warm VMs ready, maximum capacity caps the pool size, idle instance auto termination releases unused warm VMs.","text":"Pool parameters: minimum [[idle]] instances, maximum [[capacity]], and idle instance auto [[termination]].","bank":["workers","partitions","spot"]},{"id":"ch09-e090","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Every morning the team waits ~7 minutes for their classic all-purpose cluster to start, and at night the cluster sits idle for hours.","explain":"Slow start → serverless or pool; idle cost → auto-termination.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's the first question for the slow start?","options":[{"text":"Can serverless support this workload?","ok":true,"fb":"Databricks recommends serverless instead of pools where compatible."},{"text":"Should we add more workers?","ok":false,"fb":"More workers mean more VMs to provision, not faster start."},{"text":"Should we switch to Dedicated?","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode doesn't affect provisioning time."}]},{"prompt":"They need a compute-scoped init script, so classic is required. How do you cut startup time?","options":[{"text":"Attach the cluster to an instance pool with warm idle instances","ok":true,"fb":"Warm VMs skip the provisioning wait."},{"text":"Set spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled","ok":false,"fb":"Unsupported on Databricks and unrelated to start-up."},{"text":"Use spot for the driver","ok":false,"fb":"Cheaper, not faster — and riskier for the driver."}]},{"prompt":"And the idle overnight cost?","options":[{"text":"Set auto-termination after X idle minutes","ok":true,"fb":"Interactive clusters cost money while idle."},{"text":"Delete the cluster every night","ok":false,"fb":"Termination already keeps the config; deleting loses it."},{"text":"Raise min workers","ok":false,"fb":"That increases idle cost."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e091","type":"order","section":"ch09-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Cluster start-up is too slow. Order your decisions.","explain":"Prefer serverless where compatible; if classic is required, use a pool with warm instances and tune its parameters; separately fix idle cost with auto-termination.","items":["Check whether serverless supports the workload","If classic required: attach to an instance pool","Tune min idle instances / max capacity","Set auto-termination for idle interactive cost"]},{"id":"ch09-e092","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"After an all-purpose cluster terminates: gone or kept?","explain":"Termination destroys the VMs and everything ephemeral on them, but keeps the configuration (DBR, node types, autoscaling, name, permissions). Press Start and a fresh compute is built from it.","buckets":["Gone","Kept"],"items":[{"text":"Cached DataFrames","bucket":0},{"text":"Python variables","bucket":0},{"text":"Shuffle files","bucket":0},{"text":"Local temp files","bucket":0},{"text":"DBR version setting","bucket":1},{"text":"Worker/driver type","bucket":1},{"text":"Autoscaling settings","bucket":1},{"text":"Compute permissions","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e093","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"You have **CAN ATTACH TO** on a cluster. What can you do?","explain":"CAN ATTACH TO lets you attach a notebook and use the compute. Start/restart/terminate needs CAN RESTART; changing config/permissions needs CAN MANAGE. None of them grants data access — that's Unity Catalog.","options":["Attach a notebook and use the compute","Restart and terminate the cluster","Change the worker type","Read any table in the workspace"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e094","type":"match","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Match each compute permission to what it adds.","explain":"The levels are cumulative: ATTACH TO = use; RESTART = + start/restart/terminate; MANAGE = + change configuration and permissions.","pairs":[["CAN ATTACH TO","Attach a notebook and run code"],["CAN RESTART","Start / restart / terminate"],["CAN MANAGE","Change configuration and permissions"]]},{"id":"ch09-e095","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Nikos attaches his notebook to the shared cluster without problems, but `SELECT * FROM prod.payroll` fails with a permission error.","explain":"Compute ACL ≠ Unity Catalog ACL.","steps":[{"prompt":"What kind of problem is this?","options":[{"text":"Data permission (Unity Catalog), not compute permission","ok":true,"fb":"He can use the compute; the table grant is the missing gate."},{"text":"Compute permission — he needs CAN MANAGE","ok":false,"fb":"No compute permission level grants data access."},{"text":"The cluster is in Standard mode and can't read payroll","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode isn't a table-permission mechanism."}]},{"prompt":"What do you check?","options":[{"text":"Whether the running identity has SELECT on prod.payroll in Unity Catalog","ok":true,"fb":"Correct gate to inspect."},{"text":"The cluster's autoscaling settings","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to authorization."},{"text":"Whether Photon is on","ok":false,"fb":"Photon has nothing to do with permissions."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e096","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Jobs compute is governed mainly by an idle auto-termination timeout, like all-purpose clusters.","explain":"False. Jobs compute follows the **workload lifecycle**: job starts → compute starts → tasks run → job finishes → compute goes away. Idle timeouts are the all-purpose mechanism.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e097","type":"order","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Order the jobs compute lifecycle.","explain":"Jobs compute exists only for the job's duration — that's why Databricks prefers it over all-purpose compute for automated production jobs.","items":["Job starts","Compute starts","Tasks run","Job finishes","Compute goes away"]},{"id":"ch09-e098","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"q":"Which serverless permission object governs **Jobs and Pipelines**?","explain":"The workspace has **Default Interactive Compute** (notebooks / Databricks Connect) and **Default Automated Compute** (Jobs / Pipelines), each with CAN USE and management permissions. Serverless doesn't mean everyone has access.","options":["Default Interactive Compute","Default Automated Compute","CAN ATTACH TO on the serverless cluster","There are no serverless permissions — everyone can use it"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e099","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"q":"After you press Start on a terminated cluster, your previous Python variables and cache are restored.","explain":"False. Start creates a **fresh** compute from the saved config. Cache, shuffle, process memory, local temp files and Python variables are gone.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e100","type":"order","section":"ch09-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"A query fails on a cluster you can attach to. Order your checks.","explain":"First classify compute vs data problem, then identify the running identity, then check its UC privileges, then request the right grant — never \"fix\" it with compute permissions.","items":["Classify: compute problem or data-permission problem?","Identify which identity runs the query","Check Unity Catalog privileges on the table","Request the missing UC privilege"]},{"id":"ch09-e101","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Enabling Photon will make a Python `for` loop over 10 million items run as fast native code.","explain":"False. Photon is a native vectorized engine for SQL/DataFrame operators (scans, filters, joins, aggregations, writes). It doesn't magically convert arbitrary Python loops.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e102","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"A SQL aggregation is clearly CPU-bound on the workers. Which change is more likely to help?","explain":"The aggregation runs on the workers, so a larger **driver** barely matters. **Photon / query optimization** directly speeds up CPU-heavy SQL operators.","options":["A larger driver","Photon / query optimization","Dedicated access mode","A longer auto-termination timeout"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e103","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Does Photon accelerate it well?","explain":"Photon shines on SQL/DataFrame operators: scans, filters, joins, aggregations, Delta/Parquet writes. Opaque Python code and UDF logic are black boxes it can't vectorize.","buckets":["Photon accelerates well","Little or no Photon benefit"],"items":[{"text":"Scans","bucket":0},{"text":"Filters","bucket":0},{"text":"Joins","bucket":0},{"text":"Aggregations","bucket":0},{"text":"Delta/Parquet writes","bucket":0},{"text":"Python for-loop over items","bucket":1},{"text":"Logic inside an opaque Python UDF","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e104","type":"spotbug","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"q":"This transformation runs slowly even with Photon on. Click the line that makes it optimizer-unfriendly.","explain":"The Python UDF is an opaque black box: Catalyst can't see inside it and the native engine can't vectorize it. The same logic with built-in functions (`F.when`, `F.col`) is visible to the optimizer.","lines":["from pyspark.sql import functions as F","@F.udf('string')","def tier(amount): return 'big' if amount > 100 else 'small'","orders = spark.table('main.shop.orders')","out = orders.withColumn('tier', tier('amount'))"],"bugs":[4],"fix":"out = orders.withColumn('tier', F.when(F.col('amount') > 100, 'big').otherwise('small'))"},{"id":"ch09-e105","type":"write","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"q":"Rewrite with built-ins (no UDF): add a column `tier` = `'big'` when `amount > 100`, else `'small'`, on DataFrame `orders`.","explain":"`F.when(...).otherwise(...)` with `F.col` keeps the logic inside Spark's optimizer and gives Photon a chance to accelerate it.","lang":"python","solution":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nout = orders.withColumn(\"tier\", F.when(F.col(\"amount\") > 100, \"big\").otherwise(\"small\"))","keywords":["withColumn","F.when","F.col","otherwise"]},{"id":"ch09-e106","type":"order","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Order the execution stack from your code down to the hardware.","explain":"Your DataFrame/SQL code is optimized by Catalyst into a physical plan, which is executed either by Photon native code or the Spark JVM, on the workers' CPUs.","items":["Spark DataFrame / SQL","Catalyst","Physical plan","Photon native or Spark JVM","Workers / CPU"]},{"id":"ch09-e107","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Where is Photon configured vs always on?","explain":"Serverless and SQL warehouses have Photon enabled; on classic all-purpose/jobs/pipelines it's configurable and default-on in the current UI.","options":["Always on everywhere, never configurable","Enabled in serverless and SQL warehouses; configurable (default-on) on classic","Only available on GPU clusters","Only available on Dedicated access mode"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e108","type":"order","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the compute selection algorithm.","explain":"Ask first whether serverless can support the workload; only if not is classic required, and then Standard is the default unless you need R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime (Dedicated).","items":["Can serverless support the workload?","Yes → prefer serverless","No → classic required","Shared general DE/SQL → Standard","Need R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime → Dedicated"]},{"id":"ch09-e109","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which of these can you NOT control on serverless compute?","explain":"Serverless doesn't give user control over compute policies, instance pools, most Spark cluster configs, compute-scoped init scripts or compute-scoped libraries. Choosing what your code does — tables, SQL, DataFrame logic — is still yours.","options":["Which tables your query reads","Compute-scoped init scripts","The SQL you write","Which notebook you run"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e110","type":"odd","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Three of these are current serverless limitations. Which is the odd one out?","explain":"No R, no RDD APIs and Spark Connect APIs only are real serverless limitations. Photon is platform-managed and on in serverless — it's not a limitation.","options":["No R","No RDD APIs","Spark Connect APIs only","No Photon"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch09-e111","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","compare"],"q":"Run the selection algorithm: serverless, classic Standard or classic Dedicated?","explain":"Default to serverless when supported. If a custom cluster Spark config or init script is required for shared DE work → classic Standard. R, RDD, GPU → classic Dedicated.","buckets":["Serverless","Classic Standard","Classic Dedicated"],"items":[{"text":"Scheduled PySpark ETL that serverless supports","bucket":0},{"text":"Ad-hoc SQL/Python notebook exploration","bucket":0},{"text":"Shared team DE work needing a compute-scoped init script","bucket":1},{"text":"Shared ETL needing a Spark config serverless doesn't allow","bucket":1},{"text":"R analysis","bucket":2},{"text":"Legacy RDD-based job","bucket":2},{"text":"GPU model training","bucket":2}]},{"id":"ch09-e112","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"q":"Serverless's reduced configurability is purely a limitation with no benefit.","explain":"False. It's also the **abstraction benefit**: no node sizing, pool tuning, spot strategy or idle cluster management for you to get wrong.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e113","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Complete the current serverless limitations list.","explain":"Serverless trades control for abstraction: no R, no RDD APIs, Spark Connect APIs only, no cluster instance pools, limited Spark configs.","text":"Serverless: no [[R]], no [[RDD]] APIs, [[Spark Connect]] APIs only, no cluster instance [[pools]], limited Spark [[configs|configurations]].","bank":["Python","SQL","Photon","Delta"]},{"id":"ch09-e114","type":"free","section":"ch09-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"q":"Interview: \"How do you decide between serverless and classic compute, and between Standard and Dedicated?\"","explain":"Order of questions + capability-based reasoning.","model":"I first ask whether serverless supports the workload; if yes I prefer it, since Databricks manages instances, scaling and runtime. If not — e.g. I need R, RDD APIs, compute-scoped init scripts/libraries or Spark configs serverless doesn't allow — classic is required. On classic, Standard is the default for shared DE/SQL work; I choose Dedicated only for capabilities Standard can't support such as R, RDD APIs, GPU, ML Runtime or privileged machine access — never as a performance tier.","rubric":["Serverless first if supported","Classic when capability/config missing","Standard default on classic","Dedicated for R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime, not performance"]},{"id":"ch09-e115","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Spark UI for a slow stage: median task 20 s, max task 15 min, and the max task has a huge shuffle read. Diagnosis?","explain":"Max ≫ median with an outsized shuffle read on one task is a **strong skew signal**. More workers won't shorten the straggler.","options":["Strong skew signal","CPU-bound workload","Driver OOM","Too many workers"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e116","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Classify the signals: CPU-bound, memory-bound or I/O-bound?","explain":"CPU-bound: CPU high, little spill, balanced tasks. Memory-bound: high spill, executor OOM, high GC, CPU not doing useful work. I/O-bound: CPU low while tasks spend time reading.","buckets":["CPU-bound","Memory-bound","I/O-bound"],"items":[{"text":"CPU high, little spill, tasks balanced","bucket":0},{"text":"Memory OK, CPU saturated","bucket":0},{"text":"High spill and high GC time","bucket":1},{"text":"Executor OOM","bucket":1},{"text":"CPU low, tasks spend time reading","bucket":2},{"text":"Thousands of small files, poor pruning","bucket":2}]},{"id":"ch09-e117","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"q":"Sizing case: scan creates **2,000 partitions**; autoscale reaches **12 workers × 8 cores**. Roughly how many waves for the scan stage?","explain":"12 × 8 = 96 concurrent tasks; 2,000 / 96 ≈ 20.8 → **21 waves**. At 4 workers (32 slots) it would be 63 waves — so the scan phase gains a lot from autoscaling.","answer":21,"tolerance":0,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch09-e118","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"q":"Same scan of 2,000 partitions at the **minimum of 4 workers × 8 cores**. How many waves?","explain":"4 × 8 = 32 slots; 2,000 / 32 = 62.5 → **63 waves**. Plenty of balanced tasks means more workers really do help this stage.","answer":63,"tolerance":0,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch09-e119","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Daily 2 TB ETL (filter → join → groupBy) on 4–12 autoscaling workers. The scan is fast, but the join stage takes 3 hours. Someone wants 50 workers.","explain":"The source's sizing case: skew in the join stage, not capacity.","steps":[{"prompt":"Where do you look first?","options":[{"text":"Spark UI → Stages → the join stage → task metrics distributions","ok":true,"fb":"Distributions (median vs max) reveal what averages hide."},{"text":"Just set max workers to 50","ok":false,"fb":"Hardware before diagnosis is the anti-pattern."},{"text":"The driver's memory settings","ok":false,"fb":"The slow part is a worker stage."}]},{"prompt":"You see 499 tasks done (~2 GB each) and one task with huge duration, huge shuffle read (300 GB) and huge spill. Diagnosis?","options":[{"text":"Skew","ok":true,"fb":"One partition dwarfs the rest — classic skew."},{"text":"Need 50 workers","ok":false,"fb":"The 300 GB task is still one task on one core."},{"text":"I/O-bound storage","ok":false,"fb":"Most tasks finished quickly; the straggler is data-distribution driven."}]},{"prompt":"Would doubling RAM cure it?","options":[{"text":"No: it may reduce spill/failure but doesn't redistribute skewed data","ok":true,"fb":"Skew is a data-distribution problem."},{"text":"Yes: spill disappears so skew disappears","ok":false,"fb":"The straggler still processes 300 GB alone."},{"text":"Yes, if you also enable Dedicated","ok":false,"fb":"Access mode is unrelated to performance."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e120","type":"match","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Best-practice matrix: match the symptom to the first direction of thought.","explain":"Each symptom points to a different lever: idle cost → auto-termination, long startup → serverless/pool, driver OOM → collect/toPandas/driver size, stragglers → skew, few partitions → workers won't help, variable load → autoscaling.","pairs":[["Idle interactive cost","Auto-termination"],["Long cluster startup","Serverless or pool"],["Driver OOM","collect/toPandas, driver size, plan/result size"],["Few extreme stragglers","Skew investigation"],["Tiny number of partitions","Adding workers won't help"],["Variable workload","Autoscaling"]]},{"id":"ch09-e121","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"CPU is low and tasks spend most time reading. What's the likely direction?","explain":"That's I/O-bound: look at storage/file layout, too many small files, network, poor pruning, or simply reading too much data. Scaling CPU may not help.","options":["Add compute-optimized workers","File layout / small files / pruning / read less data","Bigger driver","Disable Photon"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e122","type":"order","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Order the Spark UI path for diagnosing a slow stage.","explain":"Go to Stages, open the slow one, look at task metrics, and compare distributions (median vs max) of duration, input, shuffle read/write, spill and GC.","items":["Open the Stages tab","Open the slow stage","Look at the task metrics","Compare distributions: median vs max"]},{"id":"ch09-e123","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"q":"For a CPU-bound workload (CPU saturated, little spill, balanced tasks), adding RAM is the primary fix.","explain":"False. CPU-bound calls for more/faster cores, compute-optimized workers, Photon or a better algorithm/query — not just more RAM.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e124","type":"odd","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"Which is NOT one of the task-metric distributions you inspect for a slow stage?","explain":"Duration, Shuffle Read, GC Time (plus Input Size, Shuffle Write, Records, Memory/Disk Spill) are task metrics. Unity Catalog grants aren't Spark UI performance metrics.","options":["Duration","Shuffle Read","GC Time","Unity Catalog grants"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch09-e125","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"An hourly job got slower. Cluster metrics: CPU ~95% on all workers, almost no spill, task durations balanced. Your teammate proposes memory-optimized workers.","explain":"Identify the bottleneck before choosing hardware.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which bottleneck type is this?","options":[{"text":"CPU-bound","ok":true,"fb":"High CPU, little spill, balanced tasks."},{"text":"Memory-bound","ok":false,"fb":"No spill/GC/OOM signals."},{"text":"I/O-bound","ok":false,"fb":"CPU would be low if tasks were waiting on reads."}]},{"prompt":"Best first direction?","options":[{"text":"Photon / query optimization / more or faster cores (compute-optimized)","ok":true,"fb":"Matches the CPU-bound row of the matrix."},{"text":"Memory-optimized workers","ok":false,"fb":"More RAM doesn't help a CPU-saturated job."},{"text":"Longer auto-termination","ok":false,"fb":"Cost setting, not performance."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e126","type":"order","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"A job is slow and someone says \"add hardware\". Order your bottleneck triage.","explain":"C-M-I: check CPU, then memory signals, then I/O, then stragglers — and only then match the fix to the bottleneck.","items":["CPU high and tasks balanced? (CPU-bound)","Spill/GC/OOM high? (memory-bound)","CPU low while reading? (I/O-bound)","Few extreme stragglers? (skew)","Apply the matching fix from the matrix"]},{"id":"ch09-e127","type":"write","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Write the Python to inspect the current value of the shuffle-partitions Spark config.","explain":"`spark.conf.get(...)` is the documented way to inspect Spark configuration; `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` controls how many partitions a shuffle produces.","lang":"python","solution":"spark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")","keywords":["spark.conf.get","spark.sql.shuffle.partitions"]},{"id":"ch09-e128","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the code to print the Spark version and the partition count of `df` (classic compute).","explain":"`spark.version` returns the Spark version; `df.rdd.getNumPartitions()` needs RDD access — fine on compatible classic compute, not on serverless.","asCode":true,"text":"print(spark.[[version]])\nprint(df.[[rdd]].[[getNumPartitions]]())","bank":["partitions","conf","numPartitions"]},{"id":"ch09-e129","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"q":"`df.rdd.getNumPartitions()` works the same on serverless notebook compute as on classic.","explain":"False. It needs RDD access, and serverless (Spark Connect) doesn't support RDD APIs. On serverless, reason from the query plan and Spark UI / query profile.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e130","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","syntax"],"q":"You wrote 5,000 tiny partitions and want ~20 for the final write, without a full shuffle. Which is the better fit?","explain":"`coalesce(20)` reduces partitions without full redistribution — ideal for too many partitions. `repartition(20)` would do a shuffle. But don't collapse too aggressively (e.g. coalesce(1)) or you lose parallelism.","options":["df.repartition(20)","df.coalesce(20)","df.coalesce(1)","spark.conf.get(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\")"],"answer":1,"why":["Works, but usually triggers a shuffle.","Correct: reduces partitions without full redistribution.","Collapses parallelism to one task.","That only reads a config value."]},{"id":"ch09-e131","type":"write","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"q":"Experiment 1: create a 10-million-row DataFrame with a `group_id` = `id % 100`, count rows per group, and print the formatted plan.","explain":"`spark.range` creates the `id` column; `F.col(\"id\") % 100` creates 100 groups; `explain(\"formatted\")` shows the Exchange (shuffle) caused by groupBy.","lang":"python","solution":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\ndf = spark.range(0, 10_000_000)\ndf = df.withColumn(\"group_id\", F.col(\"id\") % 100)\nresult = df.groupBy(\"group_id\").count()\nresult.explain(\"formatted\")","keywords":["spark.range","withColumn","% 100","groupBy","explain(\"formatted\")"]},{"id":"ch09-e132","type":"spotbug","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"q":"On a 64-core cluster this job uses only one core for the heavy aggregation. Click the culprit line.","explain":"`coalesce(1)` collapses the data into one partition → one task → one busy core. Coalesce is a narrow operation, so the whole upstream stage runs as a single task too.","lines":["df = spark.table(\"main.shop.orders\")","df = df.coalesce(1)","agg = df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").sum(\"amount\")","agg.write.mode(\"overwrite\").saveAsTable(\"main.shop.cust_totals\")"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"df = spark.table(\"main.shop.orders\")   # keep source parallelism\n# coalesce (moderately) only right before writing, if files are too many"},{"id":"ch09-e133","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"q":"`df.explain(\"formatted\")` shows `Exchange hashpartitioning(customer_id, 200)`. What does the **200** most likely reflect?","explain":"Exchange = shuffle; hashpartitioning by customer_id into **200** partitions — typically the value of `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`. So the next stage will have about 200 tasks.","options":["The number of workers","The number of shuffle partitions (→ ~200 tasks in the next stage)","The number of input files","The broadcast threshold in MB"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e134","type":"write","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"q":"Experiment 3: repartition `df` into 500 partitions, count rows per `group_id`, and run it with a write that produces no output files (overwrite mode).","explain":"The `noop` format executes the whole plan but writes nothing, so you can inspect the number of tasks in the Spark UI. The lesson: partitioning → number of tasks → usable core parallelism.","lang":"python","solution":"many = df.repartition(500)\nmany.groupBy(\"group_id\").count().write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"noop\").save()","keywords":["repartition(500)","groupBy","format(\"noop\")","mode(\"overwrite\")","save()"]},{"id":"ch09-e135","type":"cloze","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"q":"Fill in the operator names to look for in `explain(\"formatted\")` output.","explain":"Exchange = shuffle boundary; HashAggregate = aggregation; BroadcastHashJoin vs SortMergeJoin = the join strategy chosen.","text":"A shuffle appears as [[Exchange]]; an aggregation as [[HashAggregate]]; joins as [[BroadcastHashJoin]] or [[SortMergeJoin]].","bank":["FileScan","Project","CartesianProduct"]},{"id":"ch09-e136","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"You run Experiment 2: `df.coalesce(1).count()` vs `df.count()` on a large DataFrame. In the Spark UI most cores are idle during the first one.","explain":"Ask yourself: why are most cores idle?","steps":[{"prompt":"Why are most cores idle?","options":[{"text":"Only one partition → one task","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly the lesson of the experiment."},{"text":"The cluster is still autoscaling","ok":false,"fb":"Even fully scaled, one task can use one core."},{"text":"count() is executed on the driver","ok":false,"fb":"count() runs as tasks on executors and sums partial counts."}]},{"prompt":"How do you confirm in the Spark UI?","options":[{"text":"Look at the stage's number of tasks (1 vs many)","ok":true,"fb":"Task count per stage = partitions."},{"text":"Check the cluster's DBR version","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant."},{"text":"Check the Delta table history","ok":false,"fb":"Not related to execution parallelism."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e137","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"q":"In Experiment 1, `group_id = id % 100` over `spark.range(0, 10_000_000)`. How many rows does `result` (groupBy count) have?","explain":"`id % 100` yields values 0–99 → **100** groups, each with 100,000 rows. The shuffle still moves partial counts across workers (Exchange).","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows"},{"id":"ch09-e138","type":"scenario","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"On serverless notebook compute, `df.rdd.getNumPartitions()` raises an error saying the RDD API isn't supported.","explain":"Serverless speaks Spark Connect, not RDD.","steps":[{"prompt":"Why does it fail?","options":[{"text":"Serverless supports Spark Connect APIs only — no RDD APIs","ok":true,"fb":"Correct."},{"text":"The DataFrame has zero partitions","ok":false,"fb":"It would return 0, not an API error."},{"text":"You lack CAN MANAGE on the compute","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions aren't the issue."}]},{"prompt":"How do you see partitioning on serverless?","options":[{"text":"Use df.explain(\"formatted\") and the Spark UI / query profile","ok":true,"fb":"Exchange hashpartitioning(..., N) shows the shuffle partitioning."},{"text":"Call df.rdd.partitions instead","ok":false,"fb":"Still the RDD API."},{"text":"Switch Photon off","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to API support."}]}]},{"id":"ch09-e139","type":"order","section":"ch09-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"q":"RDD code fails on serverless. Order the response.","explain":"Confirm the compute type, use plan/Spark UI instead, and move to compatible classic compute only if RDD APIs are truly required.","items":["Confirm you're on serverless (Spark Connect)","Get the information from explain / Spark UI / query profile","If RDD APIs are mandatory: move to compatible classic compute"]},{"id":"ch09-e140","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Q1. Driver: 4 cores. Workers: 5 × 8 cores. How many **executor cores**?","explain":"5 × 8 = **40**. The driver's cores aren't executor cores.","answer":40,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cores"},{"id":"ch09-e141","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Q2. A stage has 160 partitions and the cluster has 40 worker cores. Approximately how many waves under ideal balance?","explain":"160 / 40 = **4 waves**, assuming balanced task durations.","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch09-e142","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q3. You go from 5 workers → 20 workers, but the stage only has 8 partitions. Will it scale well?","explain":"No. Only 8 tasks exist, so the extra workers mostly leave cores idle. Partitions cap parallelism.","options":["Yes, roughly 4× faster","No — only 8 tasks exist; most new cores stay idle","Yes, Spark splits partitions to match workers","Only if Photon is on"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e143","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q4. What is the relationship between worker and executor in Databricks classic?","explain":"Databricks classic runs **one executor per worker node** — which is why the terms are used almost interchangeably.","options":["One executor per worker node","One executor per core","One executor per cluster","Executors run only on the driver"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e144","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","debug"],"q":"Q5. A single task spills 80 GB while all other tasks spill almost nothing. What do you suspect first?","explain":"Data **skew** / an unusually large partition. If memory were globally too small, all tasks would spill.","options":["Data skew / unusually large partition","Not enough workers","Driver too small","Photon disabled"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e145","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":3,"tags":["exam","debug"],"q":"Q6. All executors die with OOM during a hash aggregation. Which do you investigate? (select all that apply)","explain":"Investigate partition sizes, shuffle partition count, skew, hash-table size, join strategy, memory/core, UDFs and windows. Driver type and auto-termination have nothing to do with executor-side hash aggregation memory.","multi":true,"options":["Partition sizes and shuffle partition count","Skew","Hash-table size / join strategy","Memory per core","UDFs and windows","Auto-termination timeout"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"id":"ch09-e146","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"q":"Q7. The driver crashes after `df.toPandas()`. Workers have plenty of RAM. Why?","explain":"`toPandas()` **materializes the distributed data on the driver**. Worker RAM doesn't help; the driver's memory is the limit.","options":["toPandas() materializes all distributed data on the driver","Workers ran out of memory and took the driver down","Photon doesn't support pandas","Standard access mode blocks pandas"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e147","type":"match","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","compare"],"q":"Q8 + Q9. Match each phrase to its meaning.","explain":"More workers = horizontal scale-out; larger worker type = vertical scale-up; autoscaling 2–10 = worker count varies between 2 and 10 with the same worker type.","pairs":[["More workers","Horizontal scale-out"],["Larger worker type","Vertical scale-up"],["Classic autoscaling 2–10","Worker count varies 2–10, same worker type"]]},{"id":"ch09-e148","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q10. Serverless compute autoscales.","explain":"True — but the infrastructure scaling is **platform-managed**, not a classic min/max worker setting.","answer":true},{"id":"ch09-e149","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q11. You can set traditional `min_workers=2, max_workers=10` for ordinary serverless notebook compute.","explain":"False — not using the classic configuration model. Serverless scaling is managed by Databricks.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e150","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q12 + Q13. Which pairing of reasons is correct?","explain":"Dedicated: when you need R, RDD APIs, GPU/ML Runtime, privileged machine access or other things Standard can't do. Standard: general DE/ETL and shared collaborative workloads — the recommended classic default.","options":["Dedicated for production importance; Standard for dev only","Dedicated for R/RDD/GPU/ML Runtime/privileged access; Standard for general shared DE/ETL (recommended default)","Dedicated for speed; Standard for cost","Dedicated for SQL; Standard for Python"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e151","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q14. What problem does a pool solve?","explain":"It **reduces instance provisioning / startup latency** by keeping warm instances ready.","options":["Reduces provisioning/startup latency with warm instances","Decides how many workers Spark needs","Grants data access","Fixes data skew"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e152","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q15. A pool decides how many workers Spark needs.","explain":"False. Autoscaling determines worker count; the pool only provides instances.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e153","type":"bucket","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q16 + Q17. What disappears when compute terminates, and what remains?","explain":"Ephemeral: executor memory, cache, shuffle, local temporary files, runtime processes. Persistent: Delta tables, workspace notebooks, Unity Catalog metadata, compute configuration, job definitions.","buckets":["Disappears (ephemeral)","Remains (persistent)"],"items":[{"text":"Executor memory","bucket":0},{"text":"Cache","bucket":0},{"text":"Shuffle data","bucket":0},{"text":"Runtime processes","bucket":0},{"text":"Delta tables","bucket":1},{"text":"Workspace notebooks","bucket":1},{"text":"Unity Catalog metadata","bucket":1},{"text":"Job definitions","bucket":1},{"text":"Compute configuration","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch09-e154","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q18. Why is Spot cheaper but riskier?","explain":"Spot is spare capacity that the **cloud provider can reclaim**. Cheap, but workers can disappear (tasks then retry).","options":["The cloud provider can reclaim spot capacity","Spot VMs have fewer cores","Spot disables Photon","Spot data isn't encrypted"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e155","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"q":"Q19. Photon means GPU acceleration.","explain":"False. Photon is **CPU-native vectorized** query execution, not GPU.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e156","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q20. Which is more likely to help a CPU-bound SQL aggregation?","explain":"Photon / query optimization is far more directly relevant; the driver doesn't execute the aggregation.","options":["Larger driver","Photon / query optimization","More RAM per worker"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch09-e157","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q21. You have 64 cores but 1 partition. What's the limiting resource?","explain":"**Parallelism**: one partition → one task → one core busy, 63 idle.","options":["Parallelism (one partition → one task)","RAM","Network","Driver CPU"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e158","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q22. You have 500 balanced partitions and only 8 cores. What's one obvious avenue?","explain":"With plenty of balanced, independent tasks, **more cores/workers can help** (500 / 8 ≈ 63 waves today).","options":["More cores/workers","coalesce(1)","Bigger driver","Switch to Dedicated"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e159","type":"tf","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"q":"Q23. More RAM automatically cures skew.","explain":"False. Skew is a **data-distribution** problem. More memory may reduce failure/spill but doesn't redistribute the skewed data.","answer":false},{"id":"ch09-e160","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q24. What does an `Exchange` in a physical plan usually tell you?","explain":"A **data redistribution / shuffle boundary** is likely occurring — and a new stage starts after it.","options":["A shuffle / data redistribution boundary","A Delta transaction commit","A broadcast variable was created by the driver","Photon was disabled"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch09-e161","type":"mcq","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"q":"Q25. Why can too many tiny partitions also be bad? (select all)","explain":"Task scheduling/setup overhead, tiny files, excessive metadata and inefficient execution can dominate useful work. Tiny partitions don't cause driver collect() issues by themselves.","multi":true,"options":["Task scheduling/setup overhead","Tiny files","Excessive metadata","Inefficient execution dominates useful work","They force collect() onto the driver"],"answer":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"ch09-e162","type":"free","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"q":"Say it without notes: explain Phase 1 in ~6 sentences (driver/executors, slots, partitions, memory/spill, driver memory, autoscaling, access modes, pools).","explain":"The closing \"what you should be able to explain\" list.","model":"A classic Databricks cluster has one driver and one executor per worker. Each worker contributes cores and Spark maps one task slot per executor core. Partitions determine how many tasks a stage exposes, so adding workers only helps with enough useful parallelism. Worker memory serves execution, caching, native/Python processing and platform overhead; when execution data doesn't fit, Spark spills to local disk. Driver memory is separate, so collect()/toPandas() can OOM the driver even with huge executor RAM. Classic autoscaling changes worker count within limits; serverless also scales but Databricks controls the infrastructure. Standard and Dedicated are access/capability modes, not performance tiers, and pools speed up provisioning but don't decide worker count.","rubric":["One driver + one executor per worker","1 task slot per core; partitions → tasks","Memory consumers + spill to local disk","Driver OOM via collect/toPandas","Autoscaling changes worker count; serverless managed","Access modes ≠ performance; pools ≠ worker count"]},{"id":"ch09-e163","type":"free","section":"ch09-s16","difficulty":3,"tags":["interview","compare"],"q":"Next step from the source: design three classic clusters — (1) interactive development, (2) production batch ETL, (3) memory-heavy Spark job. For each, name the key settings and one Spark UI behaviour you'd predict.","explain":"Apply sizing, lifecycle and memory-per-core reasoning.","model":"(1) Interactive dev: all-purpose, Standard access mode, small autoscaling range (or single node for tiny data), short auto-termination, optionally a pool for fast start; expect short stages and idle periods. (2) Production batch ETL: jobs compute (or serverless jobs if supported), Standard, autoscaling sized to the scan's partitions, Photon on, spot workers but a stable driver; expect a wide scan stage with many waves and shuffle stages sized by shuffle partitions — watch for stragglers. (3) Memory-heavy job: memory-optimized workers with high GB/core, enough shuffle partitions, skew checks; expect spill metrics and GC time to be the things to monitor, and OOM if memory per core is too low.","rubric":["Dev: all-purpose + auto-termination (+pool/single node)","Batch: jobs compute/serverless, autoscaling, spot workers + stable driver","Memory-heavy: memory-optimized / high GB per core, partitions","Predicts Spark UI signals (waves, stragglers, spill/GC)"]},{"id":"ch09-e901","type":"calc","section":"ch09-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A streaming micro-batch has 100 partitions: 99 finish in 2 s, one skewed partition takes 30 s. The trigger is 5 s. What is the minimum duration of each micro-batch in seconds?","answer":30,"tolerance":0,"unit":"seconds","explain":"The stage ends with its slowest task → ≥ 30 s, six times the trigger, so backlog grows every batch. Adding workers doesn't split one partition; fix the skew/key design.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch10","num":10,"title":"The Mastery Roadmap","subtitle":"PREVIEW — a map of everything ahead: 10 phases, the 17-question lesson blueprint, 4 interfaces","emoji":"🗺️","sourcePages":"271–306 (+ teasers p178–179, p400–402)","mantra":"Learn Databricks in dependency order — compute up to CI/CD — and attack every feature with the same 17 questions.","objectives":["You can list the 10 phases in dependency order and explain why compute comes first.","You can recite the 17-question lesson blueprint and use it to learn any new feature.","You can sort any command or setting into SQL, PySpark, platform config or CLI.","You can give a crisp 1–2 line definition of every term the roadmap mentions (Auto Loader, AQE, ABAC, Bundles…).","You can walk the PERMISSION_DENIED, task-failed and slow-job debugging chains in order.","You can answer both certification-style and engineering-style (skew) questions."],"sections":[{"id":"ch10-s01","title":"The New Course Contract & Ground Already Covered","hook":"From here on every lesson = theory AND real syntax. First, take stock of what you already own.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The learner asked: *continue teaching Databricks in depth — what's the plan?* and insisted on learning **both theory/best practices AND syntax/practical use** of the platform."},{"t":"p","text":"The tutor's answer: stop teaching **one concept at a time**, cut off from the real Databricks. Instead, build the whole platform as if preparing for a job, real projects and certification **at the same time**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The contract for every phase","text":"Each unit must do **both**: theory / architecture / best practices **plus** real UI, SQL, PySpark, CLI/YAML syntax, debugging and exercises."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Why this matches the exam","text":"The current **Data Engineer Associate** exam no longer tests only transformations. It also covers architecture, compute, Delta/Unity Catalog, ingestion, orchestration, Spark troubleshooting and deployment concepts."},{"t":"diagram","text":"FOUNDATIONS (done)\n├── Spark execution foundations\n├── partitions / shuffle\n├── joins / physical plans\n├── batch / streaming / incremental\n├── CDC\n├── schema evolution\n├── medallion architecture\n└── Databricks fundamentals\n    ├── account / workspace\n    ├── control vs compute plane\n    ├── serverless vs classic\n    ├── all-purpose / jobs / pipelines\n    ├── SQL warehouses\n    ├── DBR / runtime\n    ├── Photon\n    ├── driver / workers\n    ├── pools\n    ├── autoscaling\n    └── compute access","caption":"What is already covered before the roadmap starts"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Control plane","def":"The Databricks-managed backend: web app, notebooks, job scheduler, cluster manager, Unity Catalog services."},{"term":"Compute plane","def":"Where your data is actually processed — VMs in your cloud account (classic) or in Databricks' account (serverless)."},{"term":"Serverless vs classic","def":"Serverless: you specify the workload, Databricks manages the infrastructure. Classic: you configure the VMs (sizes, workers, runtime) yourself."},{"term":"All-purpose / jobs / pipelines compute","def":"Interactive cluster for notebooks / ephemeral cluster for one job run / compute that runs a declarative pipeline."},{"term":"SQL warehouse","def":"Compute dedicated to SQL and BI queries (Databricks SQL)."},{"term":"DBR (Databricks Runtime)","def":"The versioned software image on the cluster: Spark + Delta + libraries + OS."},{"term":"Photon","def":"Databricks' native vectorized (C++) query engine that speeds up SQL/DataFrame operations."},{"term":"Pool","def":"A set of idle, ready-to-use VM instances that cuts cluster start and autoscaling time."},{"term":"Compute access (access mode)","def":"Who may use the compute and how it isolates users — e.g. **Standard** (shared) or **Dedicated** (single user/group)."}]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why does the tutor insist on showing real configs and source files from now on?","text":"Because two lessons about a tool without ever seeing one **real configuration or source file** are useless for a job. Knowing names is not knowing the platform."},{"t":"p","text":"The roadmap below has **10 phases**, a project that grows with you, a fixed **lesson blueprint**, and **four interfaces** learned in parallel."}]},{"id":"ch10-s02","title":"Dependency Order: The 10-Phase Map","hook":"Learn layers in the order they depend on each other — not in the order of the sidebar menu.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"What we will NOT do","text":"We will **not** blindly follow the workspace menu order (Workspace → Catalog → Compute → Jobs → SQL …). That order is good for a **product tour**, not for learning."},{"t":"p","text":"Instead the plan follows **dependency order**: each next layer needs the previous one."},{"t":"diagram","text":" 1 Compute\n   ↓\n 2 Delta Lake\n   ↓\n 3 Unity Catalog\n   ↓\n 4 Ingestion\n   ↓\n 5 Structured Streaming\n   ↓\n 6 Declarative Pipelines\n   ↓\n 7 Jobs\n   ↓\n 8 Databricks SQL\n   ↓\n 9 Performance & Debugging\n   ↓\n10 Production CI/CD","caption":"Learning order = dependency order"},{"t":"table","head":["#","Phase","Goal (one line)"],"rows":[["1","Compute Mastery","Configure, reason about, troubleshoot and optimize real Spark workloads."],["2","Delta Lake in Practice","Move from Delta internals to using Delta correctly in production."],["3","Unity Catalog Mastery","Storage, namespace, governance, identities and permissions as ONE system."],["4","Data Ingestion","Ingest files/external sources correctly, incrementally, reproducibly."],["5","Structured Streaming","Streaming from execution model to production reliability."],["6","Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines","Production declarative pipelines with current syntax."],["7","Lakeflow Jobs & Orchestration","Orchestrate production workflows reliably."],["8","Databricks SQL","SQL analytics & BI compute — not a black box."],["9","Performance & Debugging","Diagnose slowdowns with evidence, not by blindly scaling."],["10","Production Eng., CLI, CI/CD","Operate Databricks as a production software platform."]],"caption":"The 10 phases and their stated goals"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Menu order (product tour)","points":["Workspace → Catalog → Compute → Jobs → SQL …","Shows where buttons are","Concepts appear before what they depend on"]},{"title":"Dependency order (learning)","points":["Compute → Delta → UC → … → CI/CD","Each layer builds on the previous","You can explain *why*, not just *where*"]}]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why does compute come BEFORE Delta?","text":"Every Delta command (MERGE, OPTIMIZE, a streaming write) finally runs as **Spark tasks on compute**. To reason about why a MERGE is slow, why OPTIMIZE costs what it costs or why a write creates small files, you need cores, task slots, partitions, shuffle and memory first."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"The plan as a file","text":"The tutor exported the whole roadmap as `databricks_mastery_phased_plan.md` — Markdown so it works as a syllabus in Obsidian or Git. The roadmap's audience: technical interviews, certifications, real-world DE projects, production debugging, platform operations, CI/CD and deployment."}]},{"id":"ch10-s03","title":"The 17-Question Lesson Blueprint","hook":"One fixed route through every topic — so information never feels scattered again.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The learner had struggled with **slides and scattered information**. Fix: every lesson follows roughly the **same route**, a standard structure of questions."},{"t":"list","items":["What problem are we solving?","How would we solve it without Databricks?","Which Databricks component solves it?","How does it work internally?","Where does it sit in the architecture?","How do I use it in the UI?","What is the real syntax?","What does a production example look like?","What alternatives exist?","When should I NOT use it?","Best practices","Common mistakes","Debugging workflow","Certification traps","Exercises","Full solutions","Mini-project extension"],"ordered":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Questions 6–8 get special weight","text":"**6. UI, 7. real syntax, 8. production example.** The learner had rightly complained that two lessons on a tool without a single real configuration or source file are useless."},{"t":"table","head":["Block","Questions","Purpose"],"rows":[["WHY","1–3","Problem → life without Databricks → which component"],["HOW","4–5","Internals and place in the architecture"],["USE","6–8","UI, real syntax, production example"],["JUDGE","9–12","Alternatives, when NOT to use, best practices, common mistakes"],["BREAK","13–14","Debugging workflow, certification traps"],["PROVE","15–17","Exercises, full solutions, mini-project extension"]],"caption":"Group the 17 into 6 blocks to memorize them"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"13 vs 17","text":"In the chat the tutor first listed **13** steps (ending at *Debugging*). The exported plan adds **14 Certification traps, 15 Exercises, 16 Full solutions, 17 Mini-project extension**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"A pilot's checklist","text":"Like a pre-flight checklist, the 17 questions stop you from skipping the boring-but-critical parts — especially *When should I NOT use it?* and *Debugging*."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why ask “How would we solve it without Databricks?”","text":"It exposes the **real problem** and shows exactly what the Databricks component abstracts away (e.g. without Auto Loader you'd track processed file names yourself). Once you see the manual version, the feature's design and its failure modes make sense."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when you meet ANY new Databricks feature","questions":["What problem does this solve, in one sentence?","How would I do it by hand without Databricks?","Where does it sit: control plane, compute, storage, catalog?","Have I seen it in the UI AND written its real syntax?","When would I deliberately NOT use it?","How does it fail, and where would I look first?"]}]},{"id":"ch10-s04","title":"Four Interfaces in Parallel & the Growing Project","hook":"SQL, PySpark, platform config and CLI — plus one e-commerce lakehouse that grows with every phase.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Syntax will **not** be limited to PySpark. Four interfaces are learned **in parallel**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"SQL\n├── CREATE / ALTER / MERGE\n├── COPY INTO / GRANT / REVOKE\n└── Delta DML, Unity Catalog SQL\nPySpark\n├── DataFrame API\n├── read/write APIs\n├── streaming APIs\n└── Delta APIs\nPlatform / config\n├── compute configuration\n├── Jobs\n├── Pipelines\n└── Bundles YAML\nCLI / external development\n├── databricks ...\n├── Git\n├── Databricks Connect\n└── deployment workflows","caption":"The four interfaces"},{"t":"table","head":["Interface","Typical examples","Where you write it"],"rows":[["SQL","`CREATE`, `ALTER`, `MERGE`, `COPY INTO`, `GRANT`, `REVOKE`","SQL editor, notebook SQL cells, pipeline .sql files"],["PySpark","`spark.read`, `df.write`, `spark.readStream`, `DeltaTable`","Notebooks, Python files, pipelines"],["Platform / config","Compute settings, Jobs, Pipelines, Bundles YAML","UI forms or `databricks.yml`"],["CLI / external dev","`databricks bundle deploy`, Git, Databricks Connect","Terminal, IDE, CI system"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"UI ↔ object mapping","text":"Wherever there is a UI, the tutor will also explain: **where do I click in the workspace, and which Databricks object does what I see correspond to?**"},{"t":"p","text":"Instead of a separate **toy example** per lesson (which loses the big picture), one **production-style project** grows with you."},{"t":"diagram","text":"E-COMMERCE LAKEHOUSE\nsources/\n├── customers CDC\n├── orders JSON\n├── products CSV\n└── events stream\n      ↓\nBRONZE: customers_raw, orders_raw,\n        products_raw, events_raw\n      ↓\nSILVER: customers, orders,\n        products, events\n      ↓\nGOLD:   daily_revenue, customer_360,\n        product_performance,\n        conversion_metrics","caption":"The ongoing project"},{"t":"list","items":["Auto Loader","CDC","`MERGE`","Lakeflow pipelines","Data-quality checks","Unity Catalog security","Jobs","SQL dashboards","Performance tuning","CI/CD","Service principals","Dev/prod environments"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The end goal","text":"At the end you won't just have *seen* Databricks concepts — you'll know **how they connect into one production system**."}]},{"id":"ch10-s05","title":"Phase 1 — Compute Mastery (Preview)","hook":"Read a cluster config and predict what it means for a real Spark job.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** understand compute deeply enough to configure, reason about, troubleshoot and optimize real Spark workloads — understand **every field**, not just the names."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Official compute split","text":"Databricks officially splits compute into **serverless**, **classic** and **SQL warehouses**. Classic compute includes **all-purpose**, **jobs** and **Lakeflow pipeline** resources."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Driver","def":"The node running your program's main process: builds the plan, schedules tasks, collects results."},{"term":"Worker","def":"A VM that hosts an executor and does the actual data processing."},{"term":"Executor","def":"The JVM process on a worker that runs tasks and holds cached/shuffle data."},{"term":"Cores","def":"CPU cores; each executor core runs one task at a time."},{"term":"Executor memory","def":"The JVM heap of the executor, used for execution (joins, aggregations, sorts) and storage (cache)."},{"term":"Task slot","def":"One executor core = one slot = one task running concurrently."},{"term":"Partition","def":"A chunk of data; one task processes one partition."},{"term":"Worker / driver type","def":"The VM instance type (cores, RAM, disk) chosen for workers / for the driver."},{"term":"Autoscaling","def":"Databricks adds/removes workers between a min and max based on load."},{"term":"Pools","def":"Pre-warmed idle instances that make clusters start and scale faster."},{"term":"Spot vs on-demand","def":"Spot: cheap spare capacity that can be reclaimed. On-demand: full price, not reclaimed."},{"term":"Compute policies","def":"Admin rules that restrict/preset which compute settings users may choose (and cost limits)."},{"term":"Access modes","def":"Isolation/sharing model — **Standard** (multi-user, shared) or **Dedicated** (single user or group)."},{"term":"Termination / auto-termination","def":"Stopping the cluster; auto-termination stops it after N idle minutes to save cost."}]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"What serverless abstracts away","def":"VM types, worker counts, spot/on-demand, runtime image and cluster startup — Databricks manages them."},{"term":"What remains configurable","def":"Mainly your code, the **environment** (environment version + Python dependencies) and a few job/notebook settings."},{"term":"Serverless notebooks / jobs / pipelines","def":"The same workloads run on Databricks-managed, fast-starting compute."},{"term":"Limitations","def":"Some features/configs available on classic (custom Spark configs, init scripts, specific instance types…) are not available."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"Driver:  16 cores / 64 GB\nWorkers: 8 cores / 32 GB\nWorkers: min 2, max 10\nDBR:     18 LTS\nPhoton:  enabled\nAccess mode: Standard","caption":"Practice config from the plan"},{"t":"list","items":["Total available cores","Approximate task parallelism","Where executor memory lives","Effect of 2 vs 10 workers","Shuffle behavior","Where spill happens","When the driver OOMs","When an executor OOMs","Spark UI interpretation"],"ordered":false},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: with workers min 2 / max 10, 8 cores each, how many tasks can run at once?","text":"Only **worker** cores run tasks: 2 × 8 = **16** slots at minimum, 10 × 8 = **80** at maximum. The driver's 16 cores schedule work; they do not add task slots."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Don't count the driver","text":"A classic mistake in parallelism math is adding driver cores to the task slots. Tasks run on **executors on workers**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Immediate next lesson — Compute Part 3: From VM to Spark Task.** Config: driver + 4 workers, 8 cores and 32 GB RAM per worker, DBR 18 LTS, Photon enabled, autoscale 2–8."},{"t":"list","items":["What process runs on the driver? On a worker?","What exactly is an executor? One or many per worker?","What is an executor core, and how does a core relate to a Spark task?","With 4 × 8 cores, how many tasks run concurrently?","What happens with 1,000 partitions? With only 4?","Where is the executor heap? What is memory overhead?","What happens during shuffle? What is written to local disk? Where does spill occur?","What changes when autoscaling 2 → 8 workers? What changes with Photon?","Which details disappear from user management in serverless?","How do these show up in the Spark UI (Executor/Stage/Task views)?"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Too few partitions","text":"With fewer partitions than task slots, cores sit idle no matter how big the cluster is."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: 4 workers × 8 cores, a stage with 1,000 partitions vs 4 partitions?","text":"32 slots. 1,000 partitions → tasks run in about ⌈1000/32⌉ = **32 waves**. 4 partitions → only **4 tasks**, so 28 cores sit **idle** — too few partitions waste the cluster."}]},{"id":"ch10-s06","title":"Phase 2 — Delta Lake in Practice (Preview)","hook":"You know Parquet + _delta_log + snapshots. Now you'll actually USE Delta in production.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Already understood:** Parquet + `_delta_log` + snapshots + MVCC + ADD/REMOVE actions. **Goal now:** use Delta **correctly in production**."},{"t":"table","head":["SQL","What it does (1 line)"],"rows":[["`CREATE TABLE`","Create a (Delta) table — schema, location/managed, properties."],["`INSERT`","Append (or `INSERT OVERWRITE`) rows."],["`UPDATE` / `DELETE`","Change / remove rows matching a predicate (new table version)."],["`MERGE`","Upsert: match source to target and update/insert/delete in one atomic commit."],["`TRUNCATE`","Remove all rows, keep the table definition."],["`ALTER TABLE`","Change schema/properties: add columns, set properties, clustering…"],["`DESCRIBE DETAIL`","Current table-level metadata: format, location, numFiles, size, features."],["`DESCRIBE HISTORY`","Per-version provenance: version, timestamp, user, operation, metrics."],["`RESTORE`","Create a NEW current version whose state equals an old version."],["`VACUUM`","Physically delete unreferenced files older than the retention period."],["`OPTIMIZE`","Compact small files / re-layout active files."]],"caption":"Phase 2 SQL syntax"},{"t":"table","head":["PySpark / Delta API","Meaning"],"rows":[["`spark.read.table(...)`","Read a catalog table as a DataFrame."],["`df.write`","DataFrameWriter (v1): `.mode(...)`, `.format(...)`, `.save/.saveAsTable`."],["`df.writeTo(...)`","DataFrameWriterV2: `.append()`, `.overwritePartitions()`, `.createOrReplace()`."],["`saveAsTable(...)`","Write the DataFrame as a named table (columns matched by **name** on append)."],["`insertInto(...)`","Insert into an existing table — columns matched by **position**."],["`DeltaTable.forName(...)`","Get a DeltaTable object for programmatic DML."],["`.merge(...)` / `.update(...)` / `.delete(...)`","MERGE / UPDATE / DELETE via the Python API."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"insertInto is positional","text":"`insertInto` maps columns **by position**, not by name. If the DataFrame's column order differs from the table's, values silently land in the wrong columns (or fail on type)."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Append vs overwrite","def":"Append adds rows; overwrite replaces the table's (or a partition's) contents."},{"term":"Dynamic overwrite","def":"Overwrite only the partitions present in the incoming data (`partitionOverwriteMode=dynamic`), leaving others untouched."},{"term":"Idempotent MERGE","def":"A MERGE you can re-run on the same input without creating duplicates or double-applying changes."},{"term":"CDC","def":"Change Data Capture: a feed of inserts/updates/deletes to apply to a target."},{"term":"Deduplication","def":"Keep one row per key (e.g. `ROW_NUMBER()` over key ordered by timestamp)."},{"term":"Late-arriving data","def":"Records that arrive after newer records for the same key/time — must not overwrite newer state."},{"term":"Schema enforcement","def":"Delta rejects writes whose schema doesn't match the table."},{"term":"Schema evolution","def":"Explicitly allow new columns to be added on write (e.g. `mergeSchema`)."},{"term":"Type widening","def":"Change a column to a wider type (e.g. INT → BIGINT) without rewriting data files."},{"term":"Time travel","def":"Query an older snapshot: `VERSION AS OF` / `TIMESTAMP AS OF`."},{"term":"Deletion vectors","def":"Mark rows as deleted/changed in a side file instead of rewriting the whole Parquet file immediately."}]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Small files","def":"Too many tiny files → metadata, file-open and task-scheduling overhead."},{"term":"OPTIMIZE","def":"Compacts small files into larger ones (and applies clustering)."},{"term":"Data skipping","def":"Uses per-file statistics to skip files that cannot contain matching rows."},{"term":"File statistics","def":"Min/max/null counts per column stored in the Delta log for each file."},{"term":"Liquid Clustering","def":"Flexible, incremental data layout by clustering keys (`CLUSTER BY`) — keys can change without a full rewrite; replaces static partitioning/Z-order."},{"term":"Predictive optimization","def":"For Unity Catalog managed tables, Databricks automatically runs OPTIMIZE / VACUUM / ANALYZE when it judges they're needed."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"OPTIMIZE ≠ VACUUM","text":"**OPTIMIZE** reorganizes active files/layout. **VACUUM** physically deletes expired, unreferenced files. History is not a backup — VACUUM and retention remove old files."}]},{"id":"ch10-s07","title":"Phase 3 — Unity Catalog Mastery (Preview)","hook":"Much more than catalog.schema.table: storage → namespace → identities → permissions as ONE system.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** understand storage, namespace, governance, identities and permissions as **one coherent system**, built from storage upward."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Cloud storage (s3://, abfss://, gs://)\n      ↑\nExternal location  (path + credential)\n      ↑\nStorage credential (cloud IAM identity)\n\nUnity Catalog\n└── Metastore\n    └── Catalog\n        └── Schema\n            ├── Managed table\n            ├── External table\n            ├── Volume\n            ├── View\n            ├── Materialized view\n            ├── Function\n            └── Model","caption":"Storage and namespace model"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Metastore","def":"Top-level UC container (one per region) holding all catalogs and their permissions."},{"term":"Catalog","def":"First level of the 3-level namespace `catalog.schema.object` (e.g. `prod`)."},{"term":"Schema","def":"Second level (database) inside a catalog, e.g. `prod.silver`."},{"term":"Managed table","def":"UC manages both metadata AND the data files' lifecycle (DROP deletes data)."},{"term":"External table","def":"UC manages metadata; data lives at a path you control (DROP keeps the files)."},{"term":"Volume","def":"UC object governing **non-tabular files** (CSV, images…), path `/Volumes/catalog/schema/volume/`."},{"term":"View / Materialized view","def":"Saved query / saved query whose results are precomputed and refreshed."},{"term":"Function","def":"A UDF registered in a schema (also used for row filters and column masks)."},{"term":"Model","def":"A registered ML model governed by UC."},{"term":"Storage credential","def":"UC object wrapping a cloud identity (IAM role / managed identity) allowed to access storage."},{"term":"External location","def":"A cloud storage path + the storage credential used to access it, governed with grants."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE CATALOG ...;  CREATE SCHEMA ...;\nCREATE TABLE ...;    CREATE VOLUME ...;\n\nGRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `data-engineers`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `data-engineers`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.silver.orders TO `analysts`;\n\nREVOKE ... ;  SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE prod.silver.orders;","caption":"Phase 3 SQL syntax"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Three keys to read one table","text":"To `SELECT` from `prod.silver.orders` a principal needs **USE CATALOG** on `prod`, **USE SCHEMA** on `prod.silver` and **SELECT** on the table (or inherit them from a higher level)."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Users / groups","def":"Human identities; groups bundle them so you grant once to a team."},{"term":"Service principal","def":"A non-human identity for automation — jobs, pipelines, CI/CD deployments."},{"term":"Ownership","def":"Each securable has one owner who has all privileges on it and can grant them."},{"term":"Privilege inheritance","def":"A grant on a catalog or schema flows down to all objects inside it."},{"term":"Least privilege","def":"Grant only the minimum privileges needed for the task."},{"term":"Row filter","def":"A UDF attached to a table that decides which rows each user may see."},{"term":"Column mask","def":"A UDF attached to a column that returns a masked value (e.g. `***`) for unauthorized users."},{"term":"ABAC","def":"Attribute-based access control: policies driven by governed **tags** (e.g. `pii`) apply filters/masks at scale."},{"term":"Lineage","def":"UC automatically records which tables/columns were read to produce which others."},{"term":"Audit / governance","def":"Logs of who accessed/changed what (e.g. audit logs, system tables)."},{"term":"Delta Sharing","def":"Open protocol to share live data securely with other organizations/platforms."},{"term":"Lakehouse Federation","def":"Query external databases (e.g. PostgreSQL, Snowflake) through UC foreign catalogs without ingesting the data."}]},{"t":"flow","items":["PERMISSION_DENIED","Who am I?","Which principal runs the job?","USE CATALOG?","USE SCHEMA?","SELECT / MODIFY?","Ownership?","Storage credential?"],"caption":"Permission-debugging chain"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"It works for me, it fails in the job","text":"A job may run as a **different principal** (job owner or a service principal) than you. Your notebook succeeding proves nothing about the job's identity."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself on PERMISSION_DENIED","questions":["Who am I right now (`current_user()`)?","Which principal is the job/pipeline actually running as?","Does that principal have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Does it have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Does it have SELECT (read) or MODIFY (write) on the object?","Is ownership involved (e.g. ALTER/DROP needs owner)?","For paths/external data: is the storage credential / external location granted?"]},{"t":"diagram","text":"metastore → catalog → schema\n→ managed / external tables → volumes\n→ storage credentials / external locations\n→ grants / ownership → service principals\n→ row filters / masks / ABAC → lineage\n→ systematic PERMISSION_DENIED debugging","caption":"Phase 3 teaching order (teaser at the end of Phase 2)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Earlier teaser (end of Delta Part 2)","text":"The Part 3 teaser listed: `catalog.schema.object`, managed vs external tables, **managed storage**, volumes, external locations, storage credentials, ownership, `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, `SELECT`, `MODIFY`, privilege inheritance, service principals, row filters, column masks, lineage and, above all, real PERMISSION_DENIED debugging scenarios."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → the full Phase 3","text":"This preview is now a full chapter: 16 sections, the 8-layer algorithm (identity first, plus binding, fine-grained policies, storage and compute), six debugging cases and the Phase-3 big test — see Chapter 11: Unity Catalog Mastery.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch10-s08","title":"Phase 4 — Data Ingestion (Preview)","hook":"From spark.read to COPY INTO to Auto Loader — and breaking pipelines on purpose.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** ingest files and external sources **correctly, incrementally and reproducibly**. Start simple, end with Auto Loader in depth."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"header\", True)\n        .schema(schema)\n        .load(path)\n)\n\nspark.read.json(...)\nspark.read.parquet(...)","caption":"Core batch reads"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Explicit schema","points":["You pass `.schema(schema)`","No extra pass over the data","Wrong-typed data becomes null/fails predictably","Best for production"]},{"title":"Schema inference","points":["Spark samples/scans the files to guess types","Extra read cost on CSV/JSON","Types may change between runs","Fine for exploration"]}]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"`COPY INTO`","def":"SQL command that loads files from a location into a Delta table **idempotently** — already-loaded files are skipped on re-run."},{"term":"Auto Loader","def":"Incremental file ingestion as a stream: processes only new files that land in a directory."},{"term":"`cloudFiles`","def":"The source format name for Auto Loader: `spark.readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")`."},{"term":"File discovery","def":"How Auto Loader finds new files: directory listing or file notification."},{"term":"Directory listing","def":"Default mode: lists the input directory to find new files."},{"term":"File notification","def":"Uses cloud storage events/queues to learn about new files — scales to huge directories."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"Stores which files/offsets were already processed so a restart continues exactly where it stopped."},{"term":"`schemaLocation`","def":"`cloudFiles.schemaLocation`: where Auto Loader stores the inferred schema and its evolution."},{"term":"Schema inference","def":"Auto Loader infers the schema from sampled files (JSON/CSV columns default to STRING unless type inference is on)."},{"term":"Schema hints","def":"`cloudFiles.schemaHints`: force specific types for some columns, e.g. `amount DECIMAL(10,2)`."},{"term":"Schema evolution","def":"When new columns appear, Auto Loader updates the schema (default: stop the stream, restart with new columns)."},{"term":"Rescued data column","def":"`_rescued_data`: captures values that don't fit the schema instead of losing them."},{"term":"Duplicate prevention / incremental semantics","def":"Checkpointed file tracking means each file is ingested once — \"exactly-once-ish\" as long as the checkpoint is intact."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"(\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)\n        .load(source_path)\n)","caption":"Auto Loader skeleton"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"COPY INTO","points":["SQL command, batch-style","Idempotent: skips loaded files","Good for thousands of files / scheduled loads"]},{"title":"Auto Loader","points":["Structured Streaming source (`cloudFiles`)","Checkpoint + schemaLocation","Scales to millions of files, schema evolution, rescued data"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"\"Exactly-once-ish\"","text":"Exactly-once ingestion depends on the **checkpoint**. Delete or change it and Auto Loader treats every file as new → **duplicates**."},{"t":"list","items":["Build ingestion pipelines","Intentionally break them","Diagnose missing files","Diagnose duplicate files","Diagnose schema drift","Diagnose malformed rows"],"ordered":false},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when ingestion looks wrong","questions":["Is it missing, duplicated, drifted or malformed data?","Did the files actually land in the path the stream reads?","Was the checkpoint deleted, moved or shared between two streams?","Did the source schema change (new/renamed columns)?","Is anything sitting in `_rescued_data`?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → the full Phase 4","text":"Chapter 12 is the complete Phase 4: CSV options and modes, StructType/DDL schemas, `_metadata.file_path`, every COPY INTO clause (PATTERN, FILES, VALIDATE, transforms, two mergeSchemas), Auto Loader internals, 13 debugging playbooks and the Big Test. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch10-s09","title":"Phase 5 — Structured Streaming (Preview)","hook":"From micro-batches to production reliability — and why exactly-once is subtle.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** understand streaming from the **execution model** to **production reliability**, back in Spark but inside Databricks."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"query = (\n    events\n        .writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .trigger(processingTime=\"30 seconds\")\n        .toTable(\"bronze.events\")\n)","caption":"Core syntax: spark.readStream … .writeStream"},{"t":"table","head":["Line","Meaning"],"rows":[["`.writeStream`","Turns the streaming DataFrame into a continuously running query."],["`.format(\"delta\")`","Sink is a Delta table (transactional → supports exactly-once)."],["`checkpointLocation`","Where offsets, commits and state are stored for recovery."],["`.trigger(processingTime=\"30 seconds\")`","Start a micro-batch every 30 seconds."],["`.toTable(\"bronze.events\")`","Start the query writing into that table; returns a StreamingQuery."]]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Micro-batch","def":"The stream is processed as a series of small batch jobs."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"Durable record of progress (offsets, commits, state) used on restart."},{"term":"Offset","def":"Position in the source up to which data has been read."},{"term":"State","def":"Data kept across micro-batches for aggregations, dedup, joins (in the state store)."},{"term":"Watermark","def":"`withWatermark(col, delay)`: how late data may arrive before state for old windows is dropped."},{"term":"Event time vs processing time","def":"When the event happened (column in data) vs when Spark processes it."},{"term":"Late data","def":"Events arriving after newer events; beyond the watermark they may be ignored."},{"term":"Stateless vs stateful","def":"Row-by-row ops (filter, select) vs ops that remember (aggregations, dedup, stream joins)."}]},{"t":"table","head":["Output mode","What is written each trigger"],"rows":[["append","Only new rows that are final (won't change again)."],["update","Only rows that changed since the last trigger."],["complete","The whole result table every trigger (aggregations)."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Why exactly-once is subtle","text":"End-to-end exactly-once needs a **replayable source + checkpoint + idempotent/transactional sink**. You can still create duplicates: deleting/changing the checkpoint, two queries writing the same table, non-idempotent `foreachBatch` logic, or duplicates already in the source."},{"t":"list","items":["Duplicate scenarios","Restart behavior","State-store debugging"],"ordered":false},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you restart a stream with a NEW checkpoint path on the same source. What happens?","text":"The new query has no memory of processed offsets/files, so it starts from the beginning (or from the configured starting point) and **reprocesses data → duplicates** in an append sink."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Phase 5 is now a full chapter: triggers incl. AvailableNow and serverless rules, stateless vs stateful, event time and windows, watermarks, output modes (Delta sink: append + complete only), checkpoint internals, exactly-once boundaries, joins, foreachBatch + MERGE, 11 debugging playbooks and the Phase 5 mini-project — see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming.","src":"part2"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Nuance → Delta sink & serverless triggers","text":"Two corrections to remember for the preview above: the **update** output mode is not supported by the Delta sink (use foreachBatch + MERGE), and a `processingTime` trigger is not supported on serverless notebooks/jobs (use AvailableNow, or a Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode for always-on).","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch10-s10","title":"Phase 6 — Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines (Preview)","hook":"Declare tables; let the engine build the DAG, run incrementally and enforce quality.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** build production declarative pipelines with **current** Databricks syntax and concepts — while still recognizing older terminology."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Delta Live Tables (DLT)\n        ↓\nLakeflow Declarative Pipelines\n        ↓\n(Lakeflow) Spark Declarative Pipelines","caption":"Same product line, renamed twice"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Recognize legacy tutorials","text":"Older material says **DLT**, `import dlt`, `APPLY CHANGES INTO` or the `LIVE.` prefix. The current names are Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines and `AUTO CDC`. Learn the current form; recognize the old."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze_orders\nAS\nSELECT *\nFROM STREAM read_files(...);","caption":"SQL-style pipeline source"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"`read_files`","def":"SQL table-valued function that reads files from a path; with `STREAM` it ingests incrementally (Auto Loader)."},{"term":"Streaming table","def":"Delta table fed by a streaming query; each new input row is processed once (append-style, incremental)."},{"term":"Materialized view","def":"Table whose content is the precomputed result of a query, refreshed (incrementally when possible)."},{"term":"Temporary view","def":"Intermediate dataset inside the pipeline, not published to the catalog."},{"term":"Dependencies / DAG generation","def":"The engine reads which datasets each query references and builds the execution graph automatically."},{"term":"Incremental computation","def":"Process only new/changed data instead of recomputing everything."},{"term":"Expectations","def":"Data-quality rules on a dataset: `CONSTRAINT c EXPECT (cond)` with action warn (default), `DROP ROW` or `FAIL UPDATE`."},{"term":"`AUTO CDC`","def":"Declarative CDC apply (formerly `APPLY CHANGES`): orders changes by a sequence column and applies upserts/deletes."},{"term":"SCD Type 1","def":"Overwrite the old value — keep only current state."},{"term":"SCD Type 2","def":"Keep history: close the old row (`__END_AT`) and insert a new version."},{"term":"Serverless vs classic pipeline compute","def":"Databricks-managed compute vs a pipeline cluster you configure."},{"term":"Pipeline parameters","def":"Key-value settings of the pipeline referenced from source code (e.g. catalog name per env)."},{"term":"Event log","def":"The pipeline's log of events — progress, data-quality metrics, lineage, errors — queryable as a table."},{"term":"Pipeline monitoring / debugging","def":"Pipeline UI graph + event log to find failing flows, dropped rows and slow updates."}]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Streaming table","points":["Input: append-only/streaming source","Each row processed once","Ideal for bronze ingestion"]},{"title":"Materialized view","points":["Input: any query (joins, aggregations)","Result kept correct on refresh","Ideal for silver/gold aggregates"]}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver_orders (\n  CONSTRAINT valid_id EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL)\n    ON VIOLATION DROP ROW\n)\nAS SELECT * FROM STREAM(bronze_orders);","caption":"An expectation (illustration)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Forgetting STREAM","text":"A streaming table must read a **streaming** source: `FROM STREAM read_files(...)` or `FROM STREAM(table)`. Without `STREAM` it's a batch query and the definition fails."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → read_files before Lakeflow","text":"`read_files(path, format => ..., schema => ...)` also works as a plain SQL reader for ad-hoc exploration; with `STREAM` inside a streaming table it runs Auto Loader, so checkpoints, string inference, schema evolution and `_rescued_data` all apply. (see Chapter 12: Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader.)","src":"part2"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Level-up → Structured Streaming","text":"Why Phase 6 follows Phase 5: always-running serverless streams are best built as **Lakeflow pipelines in continuous mode**, and recomputation when dimensions change fits declarative/materialized patterns better than a long-running stream-static join. see Chapter 13: Structured Streaming, sections 4 & 11.","src":"part2"}]},{"id":"ch10-s11","title":"Phase 7 — Lakeflow Jobs & Orchestration (Preview)","hook":"How everything before becomes a reliable production workflow.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** orchestrate production workflows reliably — via the **UI and declarative configuration**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"        ingest\n          │\n          ▼\n        bronze\n          │\n     ┌────┴────┐\n     ▼         ▼\n customers   orders\n     │         │\n     └────┬────┘\n          ▼\n         gold\n          │\n          ▼\n    quality check","caption":"A real job DAG (customers and orders run in parallel)"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Job","def":"The orchestrated workflow definition (tasks + schedule + settings)."},{"term":"Task","def":"One unit of work in a job: notebook, Python, SQL, pipeline, run-job…"},{"term":"Run","def":"One execution of a job (each task gets a task run)."},{"term":"Task dependency","def":"`depends_on`: a task starts only after its upstream tasks finish."},{"term":"Parameters","def":"Job/task inputs (key-value) passed to the code."},{"term":"Dynamic value references","def":"Placeholders like `{{job.run_id}}` or `{{job.start_time.iso_date}}` resolved at run time."},{"term":"Retries / timeouts","def":"Re-run a failed task N times / fail a task that runs too long."},{"term":"Notifications","def":"Email/webhook alerts on start, success, failure, duration."},{"term":"If/else","def":"Condition task that branches the DAG based on a value."},{"term":"For-each","def":"Runs a nested task once per item of a list."},{"term":"Run-job task","def":"A task that triggers another job."}]},{"t":"table","head":["Trigger","Starts a run when…"],"rows":[["Schedule","a cron-style time arrives (e.g. daily 02:00)."],["Continuous","always — a new run starts as soon as the previous ends."],["File arrival","new files land in a monitored storage location."],["Table update","a monitored source table is updated."]]},{"t":"table","head":["Compute choice","What it is"],"rows":[["Serverless job compute","Databricks-managed; no infra to configure."],["Classic job compute / new job cluster","A cluster created for the run and terminated after."],["Shared job cluster","One job cluster reused by several tasks of the same job."],["Existing all-purpose compute","Reuse an interactive cluster — convenient for dev, pricier and less isolated for prod."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Prod jobs on all-purpose compute","text":"Interactive clusters are pricier, shared and less isolated — and they hide missing job dependencies (\"works on my cluster\"). Use serverless job compute or job clusters and declare libraries in the job."},{"t":"flow","items":["Task failed","Upstream?","Compute?","Runtime?","Library?","Permissions?","Parameter?","Data?","Spark execution?"],"caption":"Debugging model"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a task fails","questions":["Did an upstream task fail or produce bad output?","Did the compute start (quota, policy, spot loss)?","Is it a runtime/DBR version issue?","Is a library missing or conflicting?","Does the run-as identity have permissions?","Was a parameter missing or wrong?","Is the input data unexpected (empty, schema drift)?","Is it a Spark execution error (OOM, skew, timeout)?"]}]},{"id":"ch10-s12","title":"Phase 8 — Databricks SQL (Preview)","hook":"SQL warehouses are not a black box ‘for BI’ — you'll size, profile and tune them.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** master SQL analytics and BI compute rather than treating SQL warehouses as a black box."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"SQL Editor","def":"Workspace UI to write and run SQL against a warehouse."},{"term":"Queries","def":"Saved SQL statements you can reuse, schedule and share."},{"term":"Query history","def":"List of past queries with status, duration, user and warehouse."},{"term":"Query profile","def":"Visual per-operator breakdown of a query's execution — the DBSQL debugging tool."},{"term":"SQL warehouse","def":"Compute for SQL: **Serverless**, **Pro** or **Classic**."},{"term":"Warehouse size","def":"T-shirt size (2X-Small … 4X-Large) of each cluster → speed of individual queries."},{"term":"Min/max clusters","def":"How many clusters the warehouse scales across → concurrency (many users)."},{"term":"Autoscaling / auto-stop","def":"Adds clusters under load / stops the warehouse after idle minutes."},{"term":"AI/BI dashboards","def":"Databricks' native dashboards built on SQL datasets."},{"term":"Alerts","def":"Scheduled query + condition that sends a notification when met."},{"term":"Materialized views / streaming tables","def":"Also creatable in DBSQL, refreshed on schedule."},{"term":"Statement Execution API","def":"REST API to run SQL on a warehouse from any application."},{"term":"JDBC / ODBC","def":"Standard drivers for external tools — e.g. **Power BI**, **Tableau** connectivity."}]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Serverless warehouse","points":["Databricks-managed compute","Starts in seconds","Recommended default"]},{"title":"Pro / Classic warehouse","points":["Compute runs in your cloud account","Slower start","Classic has fewer performance features than Pro"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Wrong warehouse knob","text":"Raising **size** doesn't fix queueing, and raising **max clusters** doesn't fix one heavy query."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Size vs clusters","text":"**Size** makes each query faster (bigger cluster). **Max clusters** lets more queries run at once (concurrency). Queries *queueing* → raise max clusters; one *heavy* query slow → bigger size or tune the query."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"SQL performance tuning","text":"Phase 8 ends with SQL performance tuning — start from the **query profile**, not by guessing."}]},{"id":"ch10-s13","title":"Phase 9 — Performance & Debugging (Preview)","hook":"\"Job went from 8 to 42 minutes\" — evidence, not a bigger cluster.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One of the most important units for **interviews and real work**. Driven by real **symptom** cases."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"Job used to take 8 min.\nNow takes 42 min.","caption":"Example case"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Not: \"use a bigger cluster\"","text":"**Goal:** diagnose slowdowns using **evidence** instead of blindly scaling compute."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Query slow\n   ↓\nSpark UI\n   ↓\nFind slow stage\n   ↓\nFind slow tasks\n   ↓\nCompare task distribution\n   ↓\nSkew? Shuffle? Spill? I/O?\nJoin? GC? Small files?","caption":"Investigation process"},{"t":"table","head":["Spark UI metric","What it tells you"],"rows":[["Jobs / Stages / Tasks","Hierarchy: action → stages split at shuffles → one task per partition."],["Input size","Bytes read from the source by the stage/task."],["Output size","Bytes written to the sink."],["Shuffle write","Bytes written by map-side tasks for the next stage."],["Shuffle read","Bytes fetched by reduce-side tasks — uneven = skew."],["Spill (memory)","Size (in memory, deserialized) of data that had to be spilled."],["Spill (disk)","Size of that spilled data on disk (serialized) — any spill = memory pressure."],["GC time","Time the JVM paused for garbage collection — high → memory pressure."],["Task duration","Compare min/median/max — a long tail = skew or stragglers."],["Records","Rows read/written per task — reveals uneven partitions."]]},{"t":"table","head":["Plan operator","Meaning"],"rows":[["`Exchange`","A shuffle (or broadcast) — data redistributed; stage boundary."],["`BroadcastHashJoin`","Small side copied to every executor; big side not shuffled."],["`SortMergeJoin`","Both sides shuffled by key, sorted, then merged — the large-large join."],["`HashAggregate`","Aggregation with a hash map (often partial → Exchange → final)."],["`Scan`","Reading the source; shows pushed filters / files read."],["`Filter`","Row predicate applied to the data."],["Whole-stage codegen","`WholeStageCodegen` / `*(n)`: fuses several operators into one generated function."],["Photon operators","`Photon...` nodes run in the Photon engine; non-Photon nodes show fallbacks."]]},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Data skew","def":"A few keys/partitions hold far more data than others → a few very slow tasks."},{"term":"Salting","def":"Append a random suffix to a hot key to spread it across many partitions."},{"term":"Broadcasting","def":"Send a small table to all executors to avoid shuffling the big one."},{"term":"AQE","def":"Adaptive Query Execution: re-optimizes at runtime — coalesces shuffle partitions, switches join strategy, splits skewed partitions."},{"term":"Partition tuning","def":"Choose a sensible number/size of partitions (e.g. `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`)."},{"term":"`repartition` vs `coalesce`","def":"repartition = full shuffle to N partitions (up or down); coalesce = merge partitions without full shuffle (down only)."},{"term":"Small file problem / file compaction","def":"Many tiny files slow reads; compaction (`OPTIMIZE`) merges them."},{"term":"Data skipping / Liquid Clustering","def":"Skip irrelevant files via stats; cluster data so skipping works well."},{"term":"Driver OOM","def":"Driver runs out of memory — e.g. `collect()`/`toPandas()` of big data, huge broadcast, too much metadata."},{"term":"Executor OOM","def":"An executor runs out of memory — e.g. a giant skewed partition, oversized partitions."},{"term":"GC pressure / spill","def":"Memory too tight: lots of GC time, data spilled to disk."}]},{"t":"table","head":["Bound by","Typical evidence"],"rows":[["CPU-bound","Cores busy, little spill/IO wait; heavy UDFs, decompression, complex expressions."],["Memory-bound","Spill, high GC time, OOMs."],["I/O-bound","Time dominated by reading/writing storage or shuffle fetch; many small files."]]},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a job got slower","questions":["What changed: data volume, code, cluster, runtime?","Which stage got slow?","Inside that stage, are ALL tasks slow or only a few?","Is shuffle read/write much bigger than before?","Is there spill or high GC time?","Is the join strategy what I expected (broadcast vs sort-merge)?","Did the number/size of input files explode (small files)?"]}]},{"id":"ch10-s14","title":"Phase 10 — Production Engineering, CLI & CI/CD (Preview)","hook":"Not a developer who only knows how to press ‘Run All’.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Goal:** move beyond notebooks and operate Databricks as a **production software platform**."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Databricks CLI","def":"Command-line tool (`databricks ...`) to manage workspace objects, jobs, bundles, etc."},{"term":"Git integration / Git folders","def":"Clone a Git repo into the workspace and commit/pull/branch from there (formerly *Repos*)."},{"term":"Workspace files","def":"Non-notebook files (`.py`, `.yml`, `.json`) stored in the workspace."},{"term":"Python modules","def":"Reusable `.py` code imported by notebooks/jobs instead of copy-paste."},{"term":"Python wheels","def":"Packaged library (`.whl`) installed on compute."},{"term":"Dependency management","def":"Pinning libraries per cluster/job/serverless environment for reproducible runs."},{"term":"Databricks Connect","def":"Run Spark code from your local IDE against remote Databricks compute."},{"term":"IDE workflows","def":"Develop locally (e.g. VS Code), test, then deploy."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Declarative Automation Bundles","text":"Per the source, the **current name of the former Databricks Asset Bundles (DABs)**. You describe jobs, pipelines, source files, deployment targets and other resources as **version-controlled configuration**, then validate and deploy via CI/CD."},{"t":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"bundle:\n  name: orders_project\n\nresources:\n  jobs:\n    orders_job:          # resource KEY\n      name: orders-job   # display name\n      tasks:\n        ...\n\ntargets:\n  dev:\n    mode: development\n  prod:\n    mode: production","caption":"databricks.yml — the primary bundle file (YAML)"},{"t":"code","lang":"bash","code":"databricks bundle validate\ndatabricks bundle deploy -t dev\ndatabricks bundle run orders_job -t dev","caption":"Bundle commands"},{"t":"table","head":["Command","Does"],"rows":[["`bundle validate`","Checks the bundle configuration for errors before deploying."],["`bundle deploy -t dev`","Uploads files and creates/updates resources in the `dev` target."],["`bundle run orders_job -t dev`","Runs the deployed resource identified by its **key** in the `dev` target."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Key, not display name","text":"`bundle run` takes the resource **key** (`orders_job`), not the job's display `name` (`orders-job`)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Target modes","text":"`mode: development` deploys per-user copies (names prefixed with `[dev user]`, schedules paused). `mode: production` is for the real deployment, ideally run as a **service principal**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Personal identity in CI/CD","text":"Deploying or running production with a person's token is risky and breaks when people leave or change roles. Use a **service principal** as the deployment identity."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Git branching","def":"Feature branches → PR → main; deployments come from tracked commits."},{"term":"Validation / testing","def":"Run `bundle validate` + unit/integration tests in CI before deploy."},{"term":"Environment-specific configuration","def":"Per-target settings (catalog, cluster size, schedule) in the bundle."},{"term":"Dev / test / prod","def":"Separate environments; promote the same code through them."},{"term":"Service principals / deployment identities","def":"Prod deploys and runs under a non-human identity, not a person's account."},{"term":"Reproducible deployments / rollbacks","def":"Same commit → same deployment; roll back by redeploying a previous version."},{"term":"Secrets / configuration","def":"Credentials kept in secret scopes, never hard-coded."},{"term":"Production release workflow","def":"Branch → CI validate/test → deploy to test → approve → deploy to prod."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-s15","title":"Assessment Strategy: Certification vs Engineering Questions","hook":"Two kinds of questions — the second kind is what makes you genuinely good.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Certification-style","points":["Pick the right product/feature","Short scenario → one correct choice","e.g. which compute for a scheduled job?"]},{"title":"Engineering-style","points":["Read symptoms and numbers","Form a hypothesis, prove it with evidence","Propose fixes"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Certification example:** *You need to run a scheduled PySpark workload without managing infrastructure. Which compute should you use?* → **Serverless Jobs** (serverless job compute)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Engineering example:** *A 2 TB ETL job went from 12 to 45 minutes. One stage has 1,000 tasks; 997 finish in 20 seconds, 3 run for 15 minutes while spilling 80 GB each.*"},{"t":"diagram","text":"task duration\n 15 min │                     ███ ← 3 tasks\n        │                     ███   80 GB spill\n        │                     ███   each\n 20 s   │████████████████████ ███\n        └───────────────────────────\n          997 tasks            3","caption":"Long-tail task distribution = skew signature"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Expected reasoning","text":"**Likely data skew.** Then know *why*, *how to prove it in the Spark UI* and *which fixes exist*."},{"t":"list","items":["Spark UI","Task-duration distribution","Shuffle read/write","Spill","Partition sizes","Join/grouping keys","Physical plan"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"How to answer","text":"Name the hypothesis (skew), show the evidence (median ≈ 20 s vs max ≈ 15 min, shuffle read and spill concentrated in 3 tasks), find the hot key (e.g. one huge customer or NULLs), then fixes: AQE skew-join handling, salting, broadcasting the small side, handling hot keys/NULLs separately."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself on a straggler stage","questions":["Are only a few tasks slow while most are fast?","Do those tasks read far more shuffle data/records?","Are they the ones spilling?","Which join or groupBy key feeds that stage?","Is one key value (or NULL) dominating?","Does the plan show a SortMergeJoin that could be broadcast?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"More hardware ≠ fix for skew","text":"Adding workers doesn't help: the 3 huge partitions are still processed by 3 single tasks."}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch10-d01","title":"How to learn ANY new Databricks feature (the 17 questions)","section":"ch10-s03","symptom":"You meet a new feature (Auto Loader, AUTO CDC, Bundles…) and the information feels scattered: you know names and slides but couldn't build or debug it.","askYourself":["What problem are we solving?","How would I solve it without Databricks?","Which Databricks component solves it?","How does it work internally?","Where does it sit in the architecture?","How do I use it in the UI?","What is the real syntax?","What does a production example look like?","What alternatives exist?","When should I NOT use it?","What are the best practices?","What are the common mistakes?","What is the debugging workflow?","What are the certification traps?","Have I done the exercises?","Have I checked the full solutions?","How do I extend the mini-project with it?"],"steps":[{"do":"WHY (1–3): write one sentence for the problem and sketch the manual, non-Databricks solution.","why":"Anchors the feature to a real need and shows what it automates."},{"do":"HOW (4–5): draw where it runs (control plane, compute, storage, catalog) and its internal state.","why":"Internals explain behavior and failure modes."},{"do":"USE (6–8): find it in the UI, write the real syntax, read a production example.","why":"The tutor insists most on 6–8: no lesson without a real config or source file."},{"do":"JUDGE (9–12): list alternatives, when NOT to use, best practices, common mistakes.","why":"Knowing the boundary of a tool is what interviews test."},{"do":"BREAK (13–14): break it on purpose, write the ordered debug checklist, note exam traps.","why":"Debugging is top priority for real work."},{"do":"PROVE (15–17): do exercises, compare with full solutions, add it to the e-commerce lakehouse.","why":"The growing project ties every feature into one system."}],"rootCauses":["Learning from scattered slides","Learning names without real syntax/config","Skipping ‘when NOT to use’ and debugging"],"fix":"Walk the 17 questions in order, with special weight on 6–8 (UI, syntax, production example) and 13 (debugging).","mnemonic":"WHY → HOW → USE → JUDGE → BREAK → PROVE"},{"id":"ch10-d02","title":"PERMISSION_DENIED on a Unity Catalog object","section":"ch10-s07","symptom":"A query or job fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED` (e.g. reading `prod.silver.orders`), often while the same code works for you interactively.","askYourself":["Who am I right now?","Which principal is the job/pipeline actually running as?","Does that principal have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Does it have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Does it have SELECT (read) or MODIFY (write) on the object?","Does the operation need ownership (ALTER, DROP, grant)?","For path-based/external data: is the storage credential / external location granted?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check your own identity.","why":"Confirms who you are testing as.","code":"SELECT current_user();"},{"do":"Check the job's run-as identity in the job settings.","why":"Jobs may run as the owner or a service principal, not you."},{"do":"Show grants top-down: catalog → schema → table.","why":"SELECT alone is useless without USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG prod;\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod.silver;\nSHOW GRANTS ON TABLE prod.silver.orders;"},{"do":"Check ownership if the operation is ALTER/DROP.","why":"Some operations require being the owner."},{"do":"For external paths check the external location and storage credential grants.","why":"Data access to cloud paths goes through them."},{"do":"Grant the minimal missing privilege.","why":"Least privilege.","code":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `sp-etl`;"}],"rootCauses":["Job runs as a different principal","Missing USE CATALOG or USE SCHEMA","Missing SELECT/MODIFY","Operation requires ownership","Missing grant on external location/storage credential"],"fix":"Identify the real principal, walk USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → SELECT/MODIFY → ownership → storage credential, and grant only the missing privilege (preferably to a group or service principal).","mnemonic":"WHO → WHICH → CATALOG → SCHEMA → OBJECT → OWNER → STORAGE"},{"id":"ch10-d03","title":"A Lakeflow Job task failed","section":"ch10-s11","symptom":"A job run is red: one task failed (e.g. `gold`), downstream tasks were skipped.","askYourself":["Did an upstream task fail or produce bad output?","Did the compute start correctly?","Is it a runtime (DBR) issue?","Is a library missing or conflicting?","Does the run-as identity have the permissions?","Was a parameter missing or wrong?","Is the input data unexpected?","Is it a Spark execution problem (OOM, skew, timeout)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the failed run and the task's error output.","why":"The error class usually points to one bucket."},{"do":"Check upstream task runs and their outputs.","why":"Many failures are just propagated bad inputs."},{"do":"Check cluster events / compute start, runtime version and libraries.","why":"Infra and dependency issues are cheap to rule out."},{"do":"Check the run-as principal and grants.","why":"Jobs often run as a different identity."},{"do":"Check resolved parameters and input data.","why":"Wrong date parameter or schema drift breaks code."},{"do":"Only then open the Spark UI for execution issues.","why":"Deepest, most expensive check last."}],"rootCauses":["Upstream failure","Compute start failure","Runtime incompatibility","Missing library","Permissions","Bad parameter","Unexpected data","Spark execution (OOM/skew)"],"fix":"Walk the model in order; fix the specific bucket (e.g. declare the library in the job environment, grant the principal, correct the parameter) and repair-run the failed tasks.","mnemonic":"Up-Comp-Run-Lib · Perm-Param-Data-Spark"},{"id":"ch10-d04","title":"Job got slow: 8 min → 42 min","section":"ch10-s13","symptom":"A job that used to take 8 minutes now takes 42; nothing obviously failed.","askYourself":["What changed: data volume, code, cluster, runtime?","Which stage became slow?","Are all tasks in that stage slow, or only a few?","Did shuffle read/write grow?","Is there spill (memory/disk) or high GC time?","Did the join strategy change (BroadcastHashJoin → SortMergeJoin)?","Is it I/O: many small files or a huge scan?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the Spark UI and sort stages by duration.","why":"Find where the time went."},{"do":"Inside the slow stage, read summary metrics (min/median/max).","why":"Uniform vs long-tail distribution separates volume/plan issues from skew."},{"do":"Check shuffle read/write, spill and GC time.","why":"Reveals shuffle growth and memory pressure."},{"do":"Read the physical plan (Exchange, join type, Scan).","why":"Plan changes explain sudden slowdowns."},{"do":"Check input file counts/sizes.","why":"Small files make jobs I/O-bound."},{"do":"Apply the targeted fix (AQE, broadcast, salting, compaction, partition tuning).","why":"Evidence-driven instead of blind scaling."}],"rootCauses":["Data skew","Shuffle growth","Spill / GC pressure","Join strategy changed","Small files / I/O","Too few or too many partitions"],"fix":"Locate stage → tasks → distribution, confirm the hypothesis with metrics and the plan, and fix the cause rather than adding hardware.","mnemonic":"Stage → Tasks → Spread → (Skew Shuffle Spill IO Join GC Files)"},{"id":"ch10-d05","title":"Spark UI checklist: a few straggler tasks (skew)","section":"ch10-s15","symptom":"1,000 tasks: 997 finish in ~20 s, 3 run for 15 minutes and spill 80 GB each; the job went from 12 to 45 minutes.","askYourself":["Are only a few tasks slow while most are fast?","Do the slow tasks read far more shuffle data and records?","Are the slow tasks the ones spilling?","Is GC time high only on those tasks?","Which join or grouping key feeds this stage?","Does one key value (or NULL) dominate?","Does the physical plan show a SortMergeJoin that could be a broadcast?"],"steps":[{"do":"Stage page → Summary metrics: compare median vs max duration.","why":"Max ≫ median = long tail."},{"do":"Compare shuffle read size / records per task.","why":"Uneven shuffle read proves uneven partitions."},{"do":"Check spill (memory/disk) and GC time per task.","why":"Huge partitions don't fit in execution memory."},{"do":"Count rows per key on the join/group key.","why":"Finds the hot key.","code":"df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count().orderBy(F.desc(\"count\")).show(10)"},{"do":"Read the physical plan for the join strategy.","why":"Broadcasting the small side removes the shuffle of the big side."},{"do":"Fix: AQE skew join, salting, broadcast, or separate hot-key/NULL handling.","why":"Redistributes the work."}],"rootCauses":["Hot key (one customer/product dominating)","Many NULL join keys","Shuffle join where broadcast is possible"],"fix":"Prove skew from task-duration distribution, shuffle read and spill, find the hot key, then use AQE skew handling, salting or broadcasting — not more workers.","mnemonic":"Tail · Shuffle · Spill · Key · Plan"},{"id":"ch10-d06","title":"Ingestion broke: missing, duplicate, drifted or malformed data","section":"ch10-s08","symptom":"Bronze has missing rows, every row twice, new columns not appearing, or garbage values after an Auto Loader / COPY INTO load.","askYourself":["Which symptom is it: missing, duplicate, schema drift or malformed rows?","Did the files really land in the path the stream reads?","Was the checkpoint deleted, moved, or shared between two streams?","Did the source schema change (new/renamed/retyped columns)?","Is anything sitting in `_rescued_data`?","Am I using an explicit schema or inference/hints?"],"steps":[{"do":"Classify the symptom and find affected files/rows.","why":"Each symptom has a different root cause."},{"do":"List the source path and compare with what was ingested.","why":"Missing data is often files in the wrong place."},{"do":"Verify checkpointLocation is unchanged and unique per stream.","why":"A new/shared checkpoint causes re-ingestion → duplicates."},{"do":"Inspect the schema in schemaLocation and the stream's schema-evolution behavior.","why":"New columns stop the stream until restart (default mode)."},{"do":"Query rows where `_rescued_data IS NOT NULL`.","why":"Shows values that didn't fit the schema."}],"rootCauses":["Files in the wrong path","Checkpoint deleted/changed/shared","Schema drift","Malformed rows / type mismatches"],"fix":"Keep one stable checkpoint per stream, use schemaLocation + hints (or explicit schema), monitor `_rescued_data`, and repair data with dedup or RESTORE.","mnemonic":"Path · Checkpoint · Schema · Rescued"},{"id":"ch10-d07","title":"Duplicates in a streaming target after restart","section":"ch10-s09","symptom":"After a restart or failure, a streaming Delta table contains duplicate events.","askYourself":["Did the checkpoint location change or get deleted?","Is another query writing to the same target?","Is my foreachBatch logic idempotent?","Did the source itself deliver duplicates?","Do I need dedup with a watermark?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the checkpointLocation before and after the restart.","why":"A new checkpoint restarts from the beginning."},{"do":"Check DESCRIBE HISTORY on the target for unexpected writers.","why":"Two queries with different checkpoints double-write.","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY bronze.events;"},{"do":"Review foreachBatch code for plain INSERTs.","why":"Retried batches re-run non-idempotent writes; use MERGE on a key."},{"do":"Check the source for duplicates and add dedup (with watermark) if needed.","why":"Exactly-once can't remove duplicates already in the source."}],"rootCauses":["Checkpoint changed/deleted","Multiple writers","Non-idempotent foreachBatch","Duplicates in source"],"fix":"Keep one stable checkpoint per query, make custom writes idempotent (MERGE), dedup with a watermark when the source can duplicate, and clean up with dedup/RESTORE.","mnemonic":"Checkpoint · Writers · Idempotent · Source"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Following the menu instead of dependencies","text":"Learning in workspace-menu order presents concepts before their prerequisites.","fix":"Follow dependency order: Compute → Delta → UC → Ingestion → Streaming → Pipelines → Jobs → SQL → Performance → CI/CD."},{"title":"Names without syntax","text":"Two lessons on a tool without seeing a real config or source file leave you unable to use it.","fix":"Always cover lesson questions 6–8: UI, real syntax, production example."},{"title":"Counting driver cores as task slots","text":"Only executor cores on workers run tasks; adding the driver's cores overestimates parallelism.","fix":"Task slots ≈ workers × cores per worker."},{"title":"Too few partitions","text":"4 partitions on 32 cores leave 28 cores idle.","fix":"Have at least as many (similar-size) partitions as task slots; usually several times more."},{"title":"insertInto is positional","text":"`insertInto` maps columns by position; a different column order silently corrupts data.","fix":"Select columns in table order or use `saveAsTable`/`writeTo`, which resolve by name."},{"title":"OPTIMIZE vs VACUUM confusion","text":"OPTIMIZE re-lays out active files; VACUUM deletes unreferenced files physically.","fix":"Compaction → OPTIMIZE; storage cleanup → VACUUM (mind the retention)."},{"title":"SELECT alone is not enough","text":"Without USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA the table is unreachable even with SELECT.","fix":"Grant USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT (or inherit from above)."},{"title":"‘It works for me’ ≠ the job has access","text":"Jobs can run as the owner or a service principal with different grants.","fix":"Always check the job's run-as principal first."},{"title":"Changing/deleting a checkpoint","text":"Auto Loader and streams lose their memory of processed data → full re-ingestion and duplicates.","fix":"Treat checkpoint paths as fixed, one per stream."},{"title":"Batch Auto Loader read","text":"`spark.read.format(\"cloudFiles\")` is wrong: Auto Loader is a streaming source.","fix":"Use `spark.readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")`."},{"title":"Believing exactly-once means no duplicates","text":"Duplicates still arise from new checkpoints, multiple writers, non-idempotent foreachBatch or a duplicating source.","fix":"Stable checkpoints, idempotent writes (MERGE), dedup with watermark."},{"title":"Streaming table without STREAM","text":"`FROM read_files(...)` without STREAM is a batch query and can't define a streaming table.","fix":"Write `FROM STREAM read_files(...)` / `FROM STREAM(table)`."},{"title":"Legacy names confusion","text":"DLT, Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines and Spark Declarative Pipelines are the same product line; APPLY CHANGES is now AUTO CDC.","fix":"Learn the current names, recognize the old ones in tutorials."},{"title":"Production jobs on all-purpose compute","text":"Interactive clusters are pricier and less isolated, and hide missing job dependencies.","fix":"Use serverless job compute or job clusters; declare libraries in the job."},{"title":"Wrong warehouse knob","text":"Bigger size doesn't fix queueing; more clusters doesn't fix one heavy query.","fix":"Queueing → max clusters; one slow query → query profile, size, layout."},{"title":"Scaling blindly","text":"Adding workers doesn't fix skew: a huge partition is still one task.","fix":"Spark UI → slow stage → task distribution → targeted fix (AQE, salting, broadcast)."},{"title":"bundle run with the display name","text":"`databricks bundle run orders-job` uses the job's name instead of its resource key.","fix":"`databricks bundle run orders_job -t dev`."},{"title":"Deploying prod with a personal identity","text":"Personal credentials in CI/CD are risky and break when people leave.","fix":"Deploy and run production as a service principal."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"What does every phase of the new course combine?","a":"Theory/architecture/best practices + real UI, SQL, PySpark, CLI/YAML syntax, debugging and exercises.","section":"ch10-s01"},{"q":"What does the current DE Associate exam cover besides transformations?","a":"Architecture, compute, Delta/UC, ingestion, orchestration, Spark troubleshooting, deployment concepts.","section":"ch10-s01"},{"q":"Why not learn in menu order?","a":"It's a product tour; learning needs dependency order — each layer needs the previous one.","section":"ch10-s02"},{"q":"The 10 phases in order?","a":"Compute → Delta → Unity Catalog → Ingestion → Structured Streaming → Declarative Pipelines → Jobs → Databricks SQL → Performance & Debugging → Production CI/CD.","section":"ch10-s02"},{"q":"Why compute before Delta?","a":"Every Delta operation runs as Spark tasks on compute; cores, partitions, shuffle and memory explain Delta's cost and failures.","section":"ch10-s02"},{"q":"Lesson blueprint in 6 blocks?","a":"WHY (1–3) → HOW (4–5) → USE (6–8) → JUDGE (9–12) → BREAK (13–14) → PROVE (15–17).","section":"ch10-s03"},{"q":"Which lesson questions get special weight?","a":"6–8: UI, real syntax, production example.","section":"ch10-s03"},{"q":"Questions 14–17 of the blueprint?","a":"Certification traps, Exercises, Full solutions, Mini-project extension.","section":"ch10-s03"},{"q":"The four interfaces learned in parallel?","a":"SQL, PySpark, Platform/config (compute, Jobs, Pipelines, Bundles YAML), CLI/external dev (databricks …, Git, Databricks Connect).","section":"ch10-s04"},{"q":"The project's four sources?","a":"customers CDC, orders JSON, products CSV, events stream.","section":"ch10-s04"},{"q":"The project's gold tables?","a":"daily_revenue, customer_360, product_performance, conversion_metrics.","section":"ch10-s04"},{"q":"Official compute categories?","a":"Serverless, classic (all-purpose, jobs, Lakeflow pipelines), SQL warehouses.","section":"ch10-s05"},{"q":"Task slots with 4 workers × 8 cores?","a":"32 (driver cores don't count).","section":"ch10-s05"},{"q":"Workers 8 cores, min 2 / max 10 → slots?","a":"16 at minimum, 80 at maximum.","section":"ch10-s05"},{"q":"4 partitions on 32 slots?","a":"Only 4 tasks; 28 cores idle.","section":"ch10-s05"},{"q":"What does serverless abstract away?","a":"VM types, worker counts, spot/on-demand, pools, runtime image/startup; you keep code + environment dependencies.","section":"ch10-s05"},{"q":"DESCRIBE DETAIL vs DESCRIBE HISTORY?","a":"DETAIL = current table metadata (format, location, numFiles, size); HISTORY = per-version operations/metrics.","section":"ch10-s06"},{"q":"insertInto vs saveAsTable column matching?","a":"insertInto = by position; saveAsTable (append) / writeTo = by name.","section":"ch10-s06"},{"q":"Dynamic overwrite?","a":"Overwrite only the partitions present in the incoming data.","section":"ch10-s06"},{"q":"Type widening?","a":"Change a column to a wider type (INT → BIGINT) without rewriting data files.","section":"ch10-s06"},{"q":"Predictive optimization?","a":"Automatic OPTIMIZE/VACUUM/ANALYZE for UC managed tables.","section":"ch10-s06"},{"q":"UC hierarchy?","a":"Metastore → catalog → schema → tables/views/volumes/functions/models.","section":"ch10-s07"},{"q":"Storage credential vs external location?","a":"Credential = cloud IAM identity; external location = cloud path + credential.","section":"ch10-s07"},{"q":"Privileges to read prod.silver.orders?","a":"USE CATALOG prod + USE SCHEMA prod.silver + SELECT on the table.","section":"ch10-s07"},{"q":"PERMISSION_DENIED chain?","a":"Who am I → job principal → USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → SELECT/MODIFY → ownership → storage credential.","section":"ch10-s07"},{"q":"Row filter vs column mask vs ABAC?","a":"Filter hides rows; mask hides column values; ABAC applies them via tag-driven policies at scale.","section":"ch10-s07"},{"q":"Delta Sharing vs Lakehouse Federation?","a":"Sharing = give your data to others; Federation = query external databases in place.","section":"ch10-s07"},{"q":"COPY INTO in one line?","a":"Idempotent SQL load of files into a Delta table; skips already-loaded files.","section":"ch10-s08"},{"q":"Auto Loader in one line?","a":"`spark.readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")` — checkpointed incremental ingestion of new files.","section":"ch10-s08"},{"q":"Directory listing vs file notification?","a":"Listing scans the path (default); notification uses cloud events/queues for huge directories.","section":"ch10-s08"},{"q":"_rescued_data?","a":"Column capturing values that don't fit the schema.","section":"ch10-s08"},{"q":"Three output modes?","a":"append (new final rows), update (changed rows), complete (whole result).","section":"ch10-s09"},{"q":"Exactly-once needs?","a":"Replayable source + checkpoint + idempotent/transactional sink.","section":"ch10-s09"},{"q":"Pipeline naming history?","a":"Delta Live Tables → Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines → Spark Declarative Pipelines.","section":"ch10-s10"},{"q":"Streaming table vs materialized view?","a":"ST: each input row processed once (append sources, bronze). MV: precomputed query result kept correct (aggregates).","section":"ch10-s10"},{"q":"Expectation actions?","a":"Warn/keep (default), ON VIOLATION DROP ROW, ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE.","section":"ch10-s10"},{"q":"AUTO CDC?","a":"Declarative CDC apply (formerly APPLY CHANGES) supporting SCD1 and SCD2.","section":"ch10-s10"},{"q":"Four job trigger types?","a":"Schedule, continuous, file arrival, table update.","section":"ch10-s11"},{"q":"Task-failed debugging model?","a":"Upstream → compute → runtime → library → permissions → parameter → data → Spark execution.","section":"ch10-s11"},{"q":"Warehouse size vs max clusters?","a":"Size = speed of each query; max clusters = concurrency.","section":"ch10-s12"},{"q":"Slow-query investigation?","a":"Spark UI → slow stage → slow tasks → compare distribution → skew/shuffle/spill/IO/join/GC/small files.","section":"ch10-s13"},{"q":"Exchange in a plan?","a":"A shuffle/redistribution of data — a stage boundary.","section":"ch10-s13"},{"q":"repartition vs coalesce?","a":"repartition = full shuffle, up or down; coalesce = merge without full shuffle, down only.","section":"ch10-s13"},{"q":"Primary bundle file and the 3 commands?","a":"databricks.yml; bundle validate, bundle deploy -t dev, bundle run orders_job -t dev.","section":"ch10-s14"},{"q":"Old name of Declarative Automation Bundles?","a":"Databricks Asset Bundles (DABs).","section":"ch10-s14"},{"q":"Scheduled PySpark, no infra management?","a":"Serverless Jobs (serverless job compute).","section":"ch10-s15"},{"q":"997 tasks 20 s, 3 tasks 15 min + 80 GB spill?","a":"Likely data skew — verify via task distribution, shuffle read/write, spill, partition sizes, keys, plan.","section":"ch10-s15"},{"q":"Auto Loader's default sample for initial schema inference?","a":"Up to 50 GB or 1000 files, whichever is reached first.","section":"ch10-s08","src":"part2"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch10-e001","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What is the new contract for every phase of the course?","explain":"Every unit must combine theory/architecture/best practices with real UI, SQL, PySpark, CLI/YAML syntax, debugging and exercises. The tutor explicitly dropped the isolated ‘one concept at a time’ style because it stayed cut off from the real platform.","options":["Theory first; syntax only in a final project","Both theory/best practices AND real UI/SQL/PySpark/CLI syntax, debugging and exercises","Only certification-style multiple choice drills","Only hands-on notebooks without architecture"],"answer":1,"why":["Syntax is not postponed — it's in every lesson.","Correct: both, in every phase.","Exams are one goal among job and projects.","Architecture and best practices are mandatory too."]},{"id":"ch10-e002","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"The current Databricks Data Engineer Associate exam mainly tests DataFrame transformations; deployment and troubleshooting are out of scope.","explain":"False. The source stresses the exam now covers architecture, compute, Delta/Unity Catalog, ingestion, orchestration, Spark troubleshooting and deployment concepts — not only transformations. That's exactly why the plan covers all of them.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e003","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Sort these already-covered topics into the two groups of the ‘Already covered’ map.","explain":"Foundations are engine/data-modeling ideas (Spark execution, partitions, shuffle, CDC, schema evolution, medallion). Databricks fundamentals are platform concepts (control vs compute plane, Photon, pools, SQL warehouses, access). Keeping the split clear tells you whether a question is about Spark itself or about the Databricks platform.","buckets":["Foundations","Databricks fundamentals"],"items":[{"text":"partitions / shuffle","bucket":0},{"text":"CDC","bucket":0},{"text":"medallion architecture","bucket":0},{"text":"schema evolution","bucket":0},{"text":"joins / physical plans","bucket":0},{"text":"control vs compute plane","bucket":1},{"text":"Photon","bucket":1},{"text":"pools","bucket":1},{"text":"SQL warehouses","bucket":1},{"text":"compute access","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e004","type":"match","section":"ch10-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each already-covered fundamental to its one-line definition.","explain":"The control plane is Databricks' managed backend; the compute plane is where data is processed. DBR is the software image, Photon the vectorized engine, and pools are pre-warmed VMs that shorten start-up. These terms reappear in Phase 1 in much more depth.","pairs":[["Control plane","Databricks-managed backend: UI, scheduler, cluster manager"],["Compute plane","Where your data is actually processed"],["DBR","Versioned image: Spark + Delta + libraries + OS"],["Photon","Native vectorized C++ query engine"],["Pool","Pre-warmed idle VMs for faster cluster start"]]},{"id":"ch10-e005","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which item was NOT already covered before the roadmap started?","explain":"Row filters/column masks belong to Phase 3 (Unity Catalog governance) and are still ahead. Photon, pools and autoscaling were all part of the Databricks fundamentals already taught.","options":["Photon","Pools","Row filters and column masks","Autoscaling"],"answer":2},{"id":"ch10-e006","type":"order","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Put the FIRST five phases of the roadmap in dependency order.","explain":"Compute comes first because everything runs on it; Delta is the storage format; Unity Catalog governs those tables; ingestion loads data into governed tables; streaming generalizes ingestion into continuous processing. Each layer needs the previous one.","items":["Compute","Delta Lake","Unity Catalog","Ingestion","Structured Streaming"]},{"id":"ch10-e007","type":"order","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Put the LAST five phases of the roadmap in order.","explain":"Declarative pipelines build on streaming; Jobs orchestrate pipelines and notebooks; Databricks SQL serves the results; performance & debugging needs all prior layers; production CI/CD packages everything for deployment.","items":["Declarative Pipelines","Jobs","Databricks SQL","Performance and Debugging","Production CI/CD"]},{"id":"ch10-e008","type":"free","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain in your own words why the plan puts **compute before Delta**.","explain":"A good answer links Delta operations to their execution on compute and to dependency order.","model":"Every Delta operation — MERGE, OPTIMIZE, a streaming write — is finally executed as Spark tasks on a driver and executors. To understand why a MERGE shuffles, why OPTIMIZE takes time, why a write creates many small files or why something OOMs, I need cores, task slots, partitions, memory and shuffle first. The plan follows dependency order: each layer needs the previous one, and compute is the bottom layer.","rubric":["Delta commands run as Spark tasks on compute","Mentions cores/tasks/partitions/shuffle/memory as prerequisites","Explains performance/cost of Delta ops depends on compute","Names dependency order (each layer needs the previous)"]},{"id":"ch10-e009","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Why does the plan refuse to follow the workspace menu order (Workspace → Catalog → Compute → Jobs → SQL)?","explain":"Menu order is good for a product tour, not for learning — it presents concepts before what they depend on. Dependency order lets you explain why things work, not just where buttons are.","options":["The menu changes too often","Menu order is a product tour; learning needs dependency order","The menu hides Unity Catalog","The exam follows reverse menu order"],"answer":1,"why":["Not the stated reason.","Correct — the exact reasoning in the source.","UC is visible in the menu (Catalog).","Nothing like this is stated."]},{"id":"ch10-e010","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In the roadmap, Jobs (orchestration) comes before Declarative Pipelines.","explain":"False. Pipelines are Phase 6 and Jobs are Phase 7: you first learn to build pipelines, then how to orchestrate them (and notebooks, SQL…) in production workflows.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e011","type":"match","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the phase number to its topic.","explain":"1 Compute, 2 Delta, 3 Unity Catalog, 4 Ingestion, 5 Streaming, 6 Declarative Pipelines, 7 Jobs, 8 Databricks SQL, 9 Performance & Debugging, 10 Production/CI-CD. Knowing the numbers helps you navigate the plan file.","pairs":[["Phase 3","Unity Catalog Mastery"],["Phase 4","Data Ingestion"],["Phase 6","Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines"],["Phase 8","Databricks SQL"],["Phase 9","Performance and Debugging"],["Phase 10","Production Engineering, CLI, and CI/CD"]]},{"id":"ch10-e012","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the principle behind the learning order.","explain":"The curriculum follows dependency order rather than the Databricks menu layout, because each next layer needs the previous one. The menu order would be a product tour.","text":"The curriculum follows [[dependency]] order rather than the Databricks [[menu]] layout: each next layer needs the [[previous]] one. The first phase is [[Compute]].","bank":["alphabetical","exam","next","Delta"]},{"id":"ch10-e013","type":"order","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the first 8 questions of the standard lesson structure.","explain":"The route goes from WHY (problem, without Databricks, which component) to HOW (internals, architecture) to USE (UI, syntax, production example). Starting from the problem keeps features anchored to what they solve.","items":["What problem are we solving?","How would we solve it without Databricks?","Which Databricks component solves it?","How does it work internally?","Where does it sit in the architecture?","How do I use it in the UI?","What is the real syntax?","What does a production example look like?"]},{"id":"ch10-e014","type":"order","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order questions 9–17 of the standard lesson structure.","explain":"After USE comes JUDGE (alternatives, when not, best practices, common mistakes), then BREAK (debugging, certification traps), then PROVE (exercises, full solutions, mini-project). Debugging comes after you know the common mistakes.","items":["What alternatives exist?","When should I not use it?","Best practices","Common mistakes","Debugging workflow","Certification traps","Exercises","Full solutions","Mini-project extension"]},{"id":"ch10-e015","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which questions of the lesson structure does the tutor say he will insist on most?","explain":"6–8: UI, real syntax and a production example. The learner had complained that two lessons on a tool without seeing a single real configuration or source file were useless.","options":["1–3 (problem framing)","6–8 (UI, real syntax, production example)","9–10 (alternatives, when not to use)","15–17 (exercises, solutions, mini-project)"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e016","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Fill in the lesson-structure blocks.","explain":"Questions 1–3 frame the problem, 6–8 are the practical core, 13 is the debugging workflow and 17 extends the ongoing project. Remember the six blocks WHY-HOW-USE-JUDGE-BREAK-PROVE.","text":"Question 1 asks what [[problem]] we are solving; question 2 asks how we would solve it [[without Databricks]]; question 10 asks when we should [[not]] use it; question 13 is the [[debugging]] workflow; question 17 is the [[mini-project]] extension.","bank":["syntax","always","pricing","UI"]},{"id":"ch10-e017","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"The standard lesson structure starts with the real syntax so you can try things immediately.","explain":"False. It starts with *What problem are we solving?* and *How would we solve it without Databricks?*. Syntax is question 7 — after you know what the component is and where it sits.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e018","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You apply the 17-question structure to a feature you have never studied: **Auto Loader**.","steps":[{"prompt":"Question 1 — what problem are we solving?","options":[{"text":"Ingest only the NEW files that keep landing in cloud storage, without reprocessing old ones","ok":true,"fb":"Right — incremental file ingestion is the core problem."},{"text":"Making queries on gold tables faster","ok":false,"fb":"That's a performance/layout concern, not what Auto Loader solves."},{"text":"Granting analysts access to tables","ok":false,"fb":"That's Unity Catalog's job."}]},{"prompt":"Question 2 — how would you solve it without Databricks?","options":[{"text":"Keep my own table of processed file names and run a cron script that lists the folder and loads unseen files","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly — and this shows what Auto Loader automates: discovery + state (checkpoint) + schema handling."},{"text":"Reload the entire folder every run and overwrite the table","ok":false,"fb":"That works but re-reads everything; it's the inefficiency Auto Loader removes."},{"text":"It can't be done without Databricks","ok":false,"fb":"It can — the manual version exposes the real mechanics."}]},{"prompt":"Question 10 — when should you NOT use it?","options":[{"text":"For a one-off load of a few known files, where a plain `spark.read` or `COPY INTO` is simpler","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — ‘when NOT to use’ forces you to know the alternatives (question 9)."},{"text":"Never — Auto Loader is always the best choice","ok":false,"fb":"Every tool has a ‘when not’; skipping question 10 is a classic gap."},{"text":"Whenever the data is JSON","ok":false,"fb":"JSON is a supported `cloudFiles.format`."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e019","type":"free","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is question 2 — *How would we solve it without Databricks?* — valuable?","explain":"Look for: reveals the real problem, shows what the component abstracts, helps with failure modes/alternatives.","model":"Doing it by hand exposes the actual problem and the moving parts (state, scheduling, schema handling). Then I can see exactly what the Databricks component automates and why it's designed that way, which also tells me how it can fail and what the alternatives are.","rubric":["Exposes the real underlying problem","Shows what the component abstracts/automates","Helps understand design, failure modes or alternatives"]},{"id":"ch10-e020","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s03","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Lesson question **13. Debugging workflow** in action: you just learned Jobs and your first job task fails with a vague error.","steps":[{"prompt":"What mindset does question 13 ask for first?","options":[{"text":"A systematic, ordered checklist of where the failure can come from","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — every lesson ends with an ordered debugging model (e.g. upstream → compute → runtime → …)."},{"text":"Re-run until it passes","ok":false,"fb":"Retries hide the cause; they're a setting, not a diagnosis."},{"text":"Increase the cluster size","ok":false,"fb":"That's the ‘blind scaling’ the plan explicitly rejects."}]},{"prompt":"The upstream task succeeded and compute started fine. Next?","options":[{"text":"Continue down the list: runtime → library → permissions → parameter → data → Spark execution","ok":true,"fb":"Right — keep walking the model instead of jumping to a favorite guess."},{"text":"Assume it's Spark skew","ok":false,"fb":"Skew is the last bucket (Spark execution); check cheaper causes first."},{"text":"Delete the job and recreate it","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys evidence (run history) without explaining anything."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e021","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which of the four interfaces does each item belong to?","explain":"SQL covers DDL/DML and governance statements; PySpark covers DataFrame, read/write, streaming and Delta APIs; platform config covers compute, Jobs, Pipelines and Bundles YAML; CLI/external covers `databricks …` commands, Git and Databricks Connect.","buckets":["SQL","PySpark","Platform / config","CLI / external dev"],"items":[{"text":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE …","bucket":0},{"text":"COPY INTO …","bucket":0},{"text":"DeltaTable.forName(…)","bucket":1},{"text":"spark.readStream","bucket":1},{"text":"databricks.yml resources","bucket":2},{"text":"worker type & autoscaling settings","bucket":2},{"text":"databricks bundle deploy -t dev","bucket":3},{"text":"Databricks Connect from VS Code","bucket":3},{"text":"MERGE INTO …","bucket":0},{"text":"Job task dependencies","bucket":2}]},{"id":"ch10-e022","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Second round — interface for each item.","explain":"REVOKE and Delta DML are SQL; read/write and streaming APIs are PySpark; pipeline settings and Bundles YAML are platform configuration; Git and deployment workflows are CLI/external development.","buckets":["SQL","PySpark","Platform / config","CLI / external dev"],"items":[{"text":"REVOKE","bucket":0},{"text":"UPDATE / DELETE (Delta DML)","bucket":0},{"text":"df.writeTo(…)","bucket":1},{"text":".writeStream","bucket":1},{"text":"Pipeline settings","bucket":2},{"text":"Bundles YAML targets","bucket":2},{"text":"Git","bucket":3},{"text":"Deployment workflows","bucket":3}]},{"id":"ch10-e023","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Place each table/source of the e-commerce lakehouse in its layer.","explain":"Sources are raw feeds (customers CDC, orders JSON, products CSV, events stream). Bronze keeps raw copies (`*_raw`), silver holds cleaned entities, gold holds business aggregates like `daily_revenue` and `customer_360`.","buckets":["sources","BRONZE","SILVER","GOLD"],"items":[{"text":"orders JSON","bucket":0},{"text":"events stream","bucket":0},{"text":"customers_raw","bucket":1},{"text":"products_raw","bucket":1},{"text":"customers","bucket":2},{"text":"events","bucket":2},{"text":"daily_revenue","bucket":3},{"text":"customer_360","bucket":3},{"text":"conversion_metrics","bucket":3},{"text":"product_performance","bucket":3}]},{"id":"ch10-e024","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"The plan uses a separate toy example in each lesson so that topics don't interfere with each other.","explain":"False. Separate toy examples make you lose the big picture. The plan builds ONE production-style project (an e-commerce lakehouse) that grows with every phase.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e025","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In the project, which source arrives as a CDC feed?","explain":"Customers arrive as CDC (inserts/updates/deletes), orders as JSON files, products as CSV and events as a stream. CDC is where MERGE and AUTO CDC will be practiced.","options":["customers","orders","products","events"],"answer":0},{"id":"ch10-e026","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are listed under ‘Platform / configuration’. Which one is not?","explain":"Jobs, Pipelines and Bundles YAML are platform configuration. The DataFrame API is a PySpark interface.","options":["Jobs","Pipelines","Bundles YAML","DataFrame API"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch10-e027","type":"match","section":"ch10-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the source feed to its format in the project.","explain":"Each source deliberately uses a different shape so the course can practice CDC, JSON/CSV file ingestion and streaming on one project.","pairs":[["customers","CDC"],["orders","JSON"],["products","CSV"],["events","stream"]]},{"id":"ch10-e028","type":"calc","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Compute Part 3 config: 4 workers × 8 cores each (plus a driver). Roughly how many Spark tasks can run concurrently?","explain":"Task slots = worker cores = 4 × 8 = 32 (one task per executor core). The driver schedules tasks but does not run them, so its cores are not counted.","answer":32,"tolerance":0,"unit":"tasks"},{"id":"ch10-e029","type":"calc","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Phase 1 practice config: driver 16 cores; workers 8 cores each, min 2, max 10. What is the MAXIMUM number of concurrent task slots?","explain":"Only worker cores count: 10 × 8 = 80. Adding the driver's 16 (= 96) is the classic trap. At minimum scale you'd have 2 × 8 = 16 slots.","answer":80,"tolerance":0,"unit":"task slots"},{"id":"ch10-e030","type":"calc","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"With 32 task slots, a stage has 1,000 partitions of similar size. About how many ‘waves’ of tasks are needed?","explain":"Each wave runs up to 32 tasks: 1000 / 32 = 31.25, so 32 waves (the last one partial). Many partitions keep all cores busy; too few would leave cores idle.","answer":32,"tolerance":1,"unit":"waves"},{"id":"ch10-e031","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"With a 16-core driver and 2 workers of 8 cores, the cluster can run 32 tasks in parallel.","explain":"False. Tasks run on executors on the workers: 2 × 8 = 16 slots. The driver's cores coordinate (planning, scheduling, collecting results) and don't add task slots.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e032","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","calc"],"quick":false,"q":"4 workers × 8 cores, but the stage has only 4 partitions. What happens?","explain":"One task per partition → only 4 tasks, so 28 of 32 cores sit idle. Too few partitions waste the cluster no matter how big it is — that's why partition count is part of compute mastery.","options":["32 tasks run, each with 1/8 of a partition","4 tasks run; 28 cores sit idle","Spark automatically splits into 32 partitions always","The job fails"],"answer":1,"why":["A partition is not split across tasks automatically.","Correct.","Spark does not always repartition to match cores.","Nothing fails; it's just inefficient."]},{"id":"ch10-e033","type":"match","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the classic compute term to its definition.","explain":"A task slot is one executor core. Pools keep warm VMs; policies restrict settings; spot instances are cheap but reclaimable; auto-termination stops idle clusters to save money.","pairs":[["Task slot","One executor core = one concurrently running task"],["Pool","Pre-warmed idle instances to start/scale faster"],["Compute policy","Admin rules restricting allowed compute settings"],["Spot instance","Cheap spare capacity that can be reclaimed"],["Auto-termination","Stops the cluster after N idle minutes"]]},{"id":"ch10-e034","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Serverless compute: is each item managed for you, or still something you configure?","explain":"Serverless abstracts the infrastructure envelope (instance types, worker counts, spot/on-demand, pools, driver size). You still own your code, the environment version and Python dependencies, and job/notebook parameters.","buckets":["Abstracted away by Databricks","Still yours to configure"],"items":[{"text":"Worker instance type","bucket":0},{"text":"Number of workers / autoscale bounds","bucket":0},{"text":"Spot vs on-demand","bucket":0},{"text":"Pools","bucket":0},{"text":"Python dependencies (environment)","bucket":1},{"text":"Your notebook/job code","bucket":1},{"text":"Task parameters","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e035","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Officially, classic compute includes three resource kinds. Which item is NOT one of them?","explain":"Databricks splits compute into serverless, classic and SQL warehouses; classic covers all-purpose, jobs and Lakeflow pipeline resources. SQL warehouses are their own category.","options":["All-purpose compute","Jobs compute","Lakeflow pipeline compute","SQL warehouse"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch10-e036","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the official compute taxonomy.","explain":"Three top-level categories: serverless, classic, SQL warehouses. Classic itself contains all-purpose, jobs and Lakeflow pipeline resources.","text":"Databricks officially splits compute into [[serverless]], [[classic]] and [[SQL warehouses]]; classic includes [[all-purpose]], jobs and Lakeflow pipeline resources.","bank":["pools","spot","Photon"]},{"id":"ch10-e037","type":"match","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each Delta SQL command to what it does.","explain":"DESCRIBE DETAIL = current table metadata; DESCRIBE HISTORY = per-version provenance; RESTORE = new version with old state; VACUUM = physical delete of unreferenced files; OPTIMIZE = compaction/layout; TRUNCATE = remove all rows.","pairs":[["DESCRIBE DETAIL","Current metadata: format, location, numFiles, size"],["DESCRIBE HISTORY","Version, timestamp, user, operation, metrics"],["RESTORE","New current version equal to an old state"],["VACUUM","Physically delete expired unreferenced files"],["OPTIMIZE","Compact small files / re-layout"],["TRUNCATE","Remove all rows, keep the table"]]},{"id":"ch10-e038","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"SQL syntax or PySpark/Delta API?","explain":"Phase 2 teaches both sides: SQL statements (MERGE, ALTER TABLE, DESCRIBE HISTORY, VACUUM) and the Python APIs (writeTo, DeltaTable.forName, insertInto, saveAsTable).","buckets":["SQL","PySpark / Delta API"],"items":[{"text":"MERGE","bucket":0},{"text":"ALTER TABLE","bucket":0},{"text":"DESCRIBE HISTORY","bucket":0},{"text":"VACUUM","bucket":0},{"text":"df.writeTo(...)","bucket":1},{"text":"DeltaTable.forName(...)","bucket":1},{"text":"insertInto(...)","bucket":1},{"text":"saveAsTable(...)","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e039","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"`df.write.insertInto(\"prod.silver.orders\")` matches DataFrame columns to table columns by name.","explain":"False — `insertInto` is positional. If the DataFrame's column order differs, values silently go into the wrong columns (or fail on type mismatch). Select columns in table order or use `saveAsTable`/`writeTo` which resolve by name.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e040","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A daily job rewrites yesterday's partition of a partitioned table. Which technique replaces only the partitions present in the new data?","explain":"Dynamic (partition) overwrite only replaces partitions that appear in the incoming DataFrame. A plain overwrite would wipe every other day's data; append would duplicate yesterday.","options":["Plain `mode(\"overwrite\")`","Dynamic overwrite (`partitionOverwriteMode=dynamic`)","`mode(\"append\")`","`VACUUM` then append"],"answer":1,"why":["Static overwrite replaces the whole table.","Correct.","Re-running append creates duplicates.","VACUUM deletes unreferenced files; it doesn't replace data."]},{"id":"ch10-e041","type":"match","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the Delta engineering topic to its definition.","explain":"Type widening changes a type to a wider one without rewrite; deletion vectors mark rows deleted without immediate rewrite; idempotent MERGE can be re-run safely; late-arriving data must not overwrite newer state; schema enforcement rejects mismatched writes.","pairs":[["Type widening","INT → BIGINT without rewriting data files"],["Deletion vectors","Mark rows deleted instead of rewriting the Parquet file"],["Idempotent MERGE","Re-running the same input causes no duplicates"],["Late-arriving data","Old events arriving after newer ones for the same key"],["Schema enforcement","Writes with a mismatched schema are rejected"]]},{"id":"ch10-e042","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a ‘physical optimization’ topic of Phase 2?","explain":"Small files, OPTIMIZE, data skipping, file statistics, liquid clustering and predictive optimization are physical layout topics. Time travel is an engineering/usage topic (querying old snapshots).","options":["Liquid Clustering","Data skipping","Time travel","Predictive optimization"],"answer":2},{"id":"ch10-e043","type":"write","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Using the Delta Python API, delete all rows with `status = 'test'` from `prod.silver.orders`.","explain":"`DeltaTable.forName(spark, name)` returns a DeltaTable object; `.delete(condition)` commits a DELETE as a new table version — the same result as SQL `DELETE FROM … WHERE …`.","solution":"from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\norders = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"prod.silver.orders\")\norders.delete(\"status = 'test'\")","keywords":["DeltaTable.forName",".delete(","prod.silver.orders"],"lang":"python"},{"id":"ch10-e044","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"OPTIMIZE vs VACUUM vs predictive optimization.","explain":"OPTIMIZE reorganizes active files; VACUUM physically deletes expired unreferenced files; predictive optimization runs OPTIMIZE/VACUUM/ANALYZE automatically for UC managed tables. Confusing OPTIMIZE and VACUUM is a common exam trap.","text":"[[OPTIMIZE]] compacts and re-lays out active files; [[VACUUM]] physically deletes expired, unreferenced files; predictive optimization runs them automatically for Unity Catalog [[managed]] tables.","bank":["RESTORE","external","TRUNCATE"]},{"id":"ch10-e045","type":"order","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the Unity Catalog namespace from the top down to a table.","explain":"Metastore (top container per region) → catalog → schema → table/view/volume/function/model. The 3-level name `catalog.schema.table` lives inside one metastore.","items":["Metastore","Catalog","Schema","Table"]},{"id":"ch10-e046","type":"order","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the topics as Phase 3 will teach them (teaser at the end of Phase 2).","explain":"Namespace first (catalog → schema → tables → volumes), then storage access (credentials/locations), then permissions (grants/ownership → service principals → fine-grained row filters/masks/ABAC), then lineage. Systematic PERMISSION_DENIED debugging ties it together.","items":["Catalog","Schema","Managed / external tables","Volumes","Storage credentials / external locations","Grants / ownership","Service principals","Row filters / masks / ABAC","Lineage"]},{"id":"ch10-e047","type":"order","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the PERMISSION_DENIED debugging chain.","explain":"Start with identity (who am I, which principal runs the job), then walk the privileges top-down (USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → SELECT/MODIFY), then ownership, and finally storage credentials for path-based access.","items":["Who am I?","What principal is the job using?","USE CATALOG?","USE SCHEMA?","SELECT / MODIFY?","Ownership?","Storage credential?"]},{"id":"ch10-e048","type":"write","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the three GRANTs that let group `analysts` read `prod.silver.orders` (nothing more).","explain":"Reading requires USE CATALOG on the catalog, USE SCHEMA on the schema and SELECT on the table. Granting only SELECT is the classic mistake — without the USE privileges the table is unreachable.","solution":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.silver.orders TO `analysts`;","keywords":["GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod","GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.silver","GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.silver.orders","`analysts`"],"lang":"sql"},{"id":"ch10-e049","type":"spotbug","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Click the buggy line in this grant script for `data-engineers`.","explain":"`USE CATALOG` is a catalog-level privilege; on a schema you grant `USE SCHEMA`. The securable type in `ON …` must match the privilege.","lines":["GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod TO `data-engineers`;","GRANT USE CATALOG ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `data-engineers`;","GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.silver.orders TO `data-engineers`;"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `data-engineers`;"},{"id":"ch10-e050","type":"match","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the storage/namespace object to its definition.","explain":"Storage credential = cloud identity; external location = path + credential; volume = governed non-tabular files; managed table = UC owns data lifecycle; external table = data at your path; metastore = top-level container.","pairs":[["Storage credential","Wraps a cloud IAM role / managed identity"],["External location","Cloud path + the credential used to access it"],["Volume","Governed non-tabular files under /Volumes/…"],["Managed table","UC manages metadata AND data files; DROP deletes data"],["External table","Data stays at your path; DROP keeps files"],["Metastore","Top-level UC container per region"]]},{"id":"ch10-e051","type":"match","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the governance feature to its definition.","explain":"Row filters hide rows; column masks hide values; ABAC applies policies via tags at scale; lineage tracks data flow; Delta Sharing shares data externally; Lakehouse Federation queries external databases in place.","pairs":[["Row filter","UDF deciding which rows a user sees"],["Column mask","UDF returning a masked value for unauthorized users"],["ABAC","Tag-driven policies applying filters/masks at scale"],["Lineage","Automatic record of which tables/columns feed which"],["Delta Sharing","Open protocol to share live data with other orgs"],["Lakehouse Federation","Query external databases without ingesting them"]]},{"id":"ch10-e052","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A scheduled job fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED` reading `prod.silver.orders`. When you run the same notebook yourself, it works.","steps":[{"prompt":"First question to ask?","options":[{"text":"Which principal is the job actually running as?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — your success proves only that YOU have access. The job may run as its owner or a service principal."},{"text":"Is the cluster big enough?","ok":false,"fb":"Permissions have nothing to do with cluster size."},{"text":"Should I VACUUM the table?","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated to access control."}]},{"prompt":"The job runs as service principal `sp-etl`. `SHOW GRANTS` shows SELECT on the table for `sp-etl`. Next?","options":[{"text":"Check USE SCHEMA on `prod.silver` and USE CATALOG on `prod` for `sp-etl`","ok":true,"fb":"Right — SELECT on the table is useless without the USE privileges on its parents."},{"text":"Grant ALL PRIVILEGES on the metastore to be safe","ok":false,"fb":"Violates least privilege and hides the real gap."},{"text":"Conclude the error is a Databricks bug","ok":false,"fb":"Walk the chain before blaming the platform."}]},{"prompt":"USE SCHEMA is missing. What's the least-privilege fix?","options":[{"text":"`GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod.silver TO `sp-etl`` (and USE CATALOG if also missing)","ok":true,"fb":"Minimal grant that closes exactly the gap."},{"text":"Make `sp-etl` the owner of the catalog","ok":false,"fb":"Ownership grants far more than needed."},{"text":"Run the job as your personal user","ok":false,"fb":"Production jobs should not depend on a person's account."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e053","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Granting `SELECT` on a table is sufficient for a user to query it.","explain":"False. The user also needs `USE CATALOG` on the parent catalog and `USE SCHEMA` on the parent schema (directly or inherited). This is the most common Unity Catalog permission trap.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e054","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which identity should a production job or CI/CD deployment run as?","explain":"Service principals are non-human identities meant for automation. Personal accounts break when people leave or change roles and violate least-privilege separation.","options":["The most senior engineer's user","A service principal","The workspace admin","Any user in the `analysts` group"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e055","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a Unity Catalog securable object inside a schema?","explain":"Tables, volumes and functions live inside schemas and are governed by UC. A cluster pool is a compute resource, not a data object in the catalog hierarchy.","options":["Table","Volume","Function","Cluster pool"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch10-e056","type":"match","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the Auto Loader term to its meaning.","explain":"`cloudFiles` is the source format; schemaLocation stores the inferred schema; schema hints force types; `_rescued_data` keeps non-conforming values; file notification uses cloud events instead of listing directories.","pairs":[["cloudFiles","Auto Loader's source format name"],["schemaLocation","Where the inferred schema and its evolution are stored"],["Schema hints","Force types for specific columns"],["Rescued data column","Captures values that don't fit the schema"],["File notification","Discovers new files via cloud storage events/queues"],["Directory listing","Default discovery: lists the input path"]]},{"id":"ch10-e057","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement best contrasts COPY INTO and Auto Loader?","explain":"Both are idempotent at the file level. COPY INTO is a SQL batch command, good for thousands of files; Auto Loader is a streaming source with checkpoint, schema evolution and rescued data, built to scale to millions of files.","options":["COPY INTO reprocesses all files every run; Auto Loader doesn't","COPY INTO is an idempotent SQL load; Auto Loader is a checkpointed streaming source that scales to huge file volumes","Auto Loader only works with CSV","They are two names for the same feature"],"answer":1,"why":["COPY INTO skips already-loaded files.","Correct.","Auto Loader supports JSON, CSV, Parquet, Avro…","Different features with different interfaces."]},{"id":"ch10-e058","type":"spotbug","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Find the bug in this Auto Loader read.","explain":"Auto Loader is a Structured Streaming source: it must be started with `spark.readStream`, not batch `spark.read`. The rest (cloudFiles.format, schemaLocation, load) is fine.","lines":["df = (","    spark.read","        .format(\"cloudFiles\")","        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")","        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)","        .load(source_path)",")"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"    spark.readStream"},{"id":"ch10-e059","type":"write","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write an Auto Loader read of JSON files from `source_path`, storing the schema at `schema_path`.","explain":"`readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")` selects Auto Loader; `cloudFiles.format` names the file format; `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` is where the inferred schema is tracked between runs.","solution":"df = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)\n        .load(source_path)\n)","keywords":["readStream","cloudFiles","cloudFiles.format","json","cloudFiles.schemaLocation",".load("],"lang":"python"},{"id":"ch10-e060","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The bronze `orders_raw` table suddenly contains every order twice. Nothing changed in the source folder.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your first hypothesis to check?","options":[{"text":"Was the Auto Loader checkpoint deleted, moved, or changed?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — the checkpoint is the memory of which files were processed. Without it every file looks new."},{"text":"The cluster ran out of memory","ok":false,"fb":"OOM would fail the run, not duplicate data."},{"text":"Schema hints are wrong","ok":false,"fb":"Hints affect types, not how many times files are read."}]},{"prompt":"Git history shows someone changed `checkpointLocation` to a new path last night. What happened?","options":[{"text":"The new checkpoint had no file state, so all existing files were ingested again","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly — that's why ingestion is only ‘exactly-once-ish’."},{"text":"Auto Loader merged both checkpoints","ok":false,"fb":"Checkpoints are never merged."},{"text":"Nothing — the checkpoint path doesn't matter","ok":false,"fb":"It is the single most important piece of state."}]},{"prompt":"Best clean-up and prevention?","options":[{"text":"Deduplicate (or restore to the version before the bad run) and treat checkpoint paths as fixed, per-stream configuration","ok":true,"fb":"Fix the data with DESCRIBE HISTORY/RESTORE or dedup, and never casually change or share checkpoints."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint again to reset","ok":false,"fb":"That would re-ingest everything a third time."},{"text":"Switch to schema inference","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated to duplicates."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e061","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Re-running the same `COPY INTO` over the same files loads those files a second time.","explain":"False. COPY INTO is idempotent: it tracks already-loaded files and skips them, so a re-run typically loads 0 new rows. (Only a force option would reload them.)","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e062","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Schema inference on CSV/JSON is free — Spark already knows the types without reading the data.","explain":"False. Inference requires an extra pass/sample over the data and the guessed types can change between runs. An explicit schema avoids that cost and makes production behavior predictable.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e063","type":"order","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order a sensible debug path for ‘rows are missing from bronze’.","explain":"First confirm the symptom, then whether the files are actually where the stream reads, then the stream's state (checkpoint), then schema/malformed rows that may have been rescued or dropped. Cheap facts before deep hypotheses.","items":["Confirm which rows/files are missing","Check the files actually landed in the path the stream reads","Check the checkpoint (already processed? wrong path?)","Check schema drift / `_rescued_data` for malformed rows"]},{"id":"ch10-e064","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the Auto Loader skeleton.","explain":"Auto Loader = `readStream` + format `cloudFiles`; options are prefixed `cloudFiles.` (format, schemaLocation).","asCode":true,"text":"spark.[[readStream]]\n  .format(\"[[cloudFiles]]\")\n  .option(\"cloudFiles.[[format]]\", \"json\")\n  .option(\"cloudFiles.[[schemaLocation]]\", schema_path)\n  .load(source_path)","bank":["read","delta","checkpointLocation","header"]},{"id":"ch10-e065","type":"match","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the streaming term to its meaning.","explain":"Micro-batches are small batch jobs; the checkpoint stores progress; offsets mark source position; state is kept across batches; the watermark bounds lateness and lets old state be dropped.","pairs":[["Micro-batch","Stream processed as a series of small batch jobs"],["Checkpoint","Durable progress: offsets, commits, state"],["Offset","Source position read up to"],["State","Data remembered across batches (aggregations, dedup)"],["Watermark","Bound on lateness so old state can be dropped"]]},{"id":"ch10-e066","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Stateless or stateful transformation?","explain":"Stateless ops look at one row at a time (filter, select, withColumn). Stateful ops must remember data across micro-batches: aggregations, windowed counts, streaming dedup, stream-stream joins — they need state and usually a watermark.","buckets":["Stateless","Stateful"],"items":[{"text":"filter(amount > 0)","bucket":0},{"text":"select / withColumn","bucket":0},{"text":"explode a JSON array","bucket":0},{"text":"groupBy(customer).count()","bucket":1},{"text":"window aggregation over 10 minutes","bucket":1},{"text":"dropDuplicates with watermark","bucket":1},{"text":"stream-stream join","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e067","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which output mode writes the ENTIRE result table on every trigger?","explain":"Complete mode rewrites the whole aggregated result each trigger. Append writes only new finalized rows; update writes only rows that changed.","options":["append","update","complete","overwrite"],"answer":2},{"id":"ch10-e068","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the streaming write from the plan.","explain":"The checkpoint is set via `checkpointLocation`, the trigger via `processingTime`, and `toTable` starts the query into the target table.","asCode":true,"text":"query = (events\n  .[[writeStream]]\n  .format(\"delta\")\n  .option(\"[[checkpointLocation]]\", checkpoint)\n  .trigger([[processingTime]]=\"30 seconds\")\n  .[[toTable]](\"bronze.events\"))","bank":["write","schemaLocation","save","interval"]},{"id":"ch10-e069","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Because Structured Streaming writing to Delta is exactly-once, duplicates in the target are impossible.","explain":"False — that's why the plan says exactly-once is subtle. Exactly-once holds for a given query + checkpoint + transactional sink. Changing/deleting the checkpoint, two queries writing the same table, non-idempotent `foreachBatch` logic or duplicates already in the source all produce duplicates.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e070","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"After a restart, the streaming table `bronze.events` contains a block of duplicated events.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you inspect first?","options":[{"text":"The checkpointLocation used before and after the restart","ok":true,"fb":"Same checkpoint → resume from stored offsets; new checkpoint → reprocess from the start."},{"text":"The trigger interval","ok":false,"fb":"The interval changes batch frequency, not correctness."},{"text":"The cluster's worker count","ok":false,"fb":"Scale doesn't create duplicates."}]},{"prompt":"The checkpoint was unchanged. The query uses `foreachBatch` doing a plain INSERT into another table. Hypothesis?","options":[{"text":"A batch was retried after partial failure and the non-idempotent INSERT ran twice","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — inside foreachBatch you must make writes idempotent (e.g. MERGE on a key)."},{"text":"Watermarks always create duplicates","ok":false,"fb":"Watermarks bound lateness; they don't duplicate data."},{"text":"Delta is not transactional","ok":false,"fb":"Delta is transactional; the problem is the custom write logic."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e071","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"An order event is created at 10:00 on a phone but reaches Spark at 10:07. What is 10:00?","explain":"Event time is when the event happened (a column in the data); processing time is when Spark processes it. Windowing and watermarks should normally use event time so late arrivals land in the right window.","options":["Processing time","Event time","Watermark","Offset"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e072","type":"order","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the questions for ‘duplicates appeared in a streaming target’.","explain":"Check the cheapest, most common cause first (checkpoint identity), then whether another writer exists, then custom write logic, then the source itself.","items":["Did the checkpoint location change or get deleted?","Is another query writing to the same target?","Is foreachBatch logic idempotent?","Did the source itself deliver duplicates?"]},{"id":"ch10-e073","type":"order","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the naming history of Databricks' declarative pipeline product.","explain":"Delta Live Tables (DLT) → Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines → (Lakeflow) Spark Declarative Pipelines. Same product line; the plan teaches the current form while making sure you recognize legacy tutorials.","items":["Delta Live Tables","Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines","Spark Declarative Pipelines"]},{"id":"ch10-e074","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Streaming table or materialized view?","explain":"Streaming tables process append-only/streaming input once per row — ideal for bronze ingestion with `STREAM read_files`. Materialized views keep the result of any query (joins, aggregations) correct on refresh — ideal for gold aggregates.","buckets":["Streaming table","Materialized view"],"items":[{"text":"Bronze ingestion with STREAM read_files","bucket":0},{"text":"Each input row processed once","bucket":0},{"text":"Append-only event source","bucket":0},{"text":"daily_revenue aggregate","bucket":1},{"text":"Join of customers and orders kept correct","bucket":1},{"text":"Precomputed query result refreshed","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e075","type":"spotbug","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"This pipeline definition fails. Which line is wrong?","explain":"A streaming table must be defined by a streaming query. `read_files(...)` without `STREAM` is a batch read; it should be `FROM STREAM read_files(...)`.","lines":["CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze_orders","AS","SELECT *","FROM read_files('/Volumes/shop/raw/orders', format => 'json');"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"FROM STREAM read_files('/Volumes/shop/raw/orders', format => 'json');"},{"id":"ch10-e076","type":"write","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the SQL pipeline definition of a streaming table `bronze_orders` that incrementally reads files with `read_files(...)`.","explain":"`CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE … AS SELECT * FROM STREAM read_files(...)` is the canonical bronze ingestion pattern; STREAM makes it incremental (Auto Loader under the hood).","solution":"CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze_orders\nAS\nSELECT *\nFROM STREAM read_files(...);","keywords":["create or refresh streaming table","bronze_orders","from stream read_files"],"lang":"sql"},{"id":"ch10-e077","type":"match","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the pipeline concept to its definition.","explain":"Expectations are data-quality rules; AUTO CDC applies change feeds; SCD1 overwrites; SCD2 keeps history; the event log records progress/quality/lineage; temporary views are intermediate and not published.","pairs":[["Expectations","Data-quality rules: warn, DROP ROW or FAIL UPDATE"],["AUTO CDC","Declarative apply of a change feed (formerly APPLY CHANGES)"],["SCD Type 1","Overwrite old values, keep current state only"],["SCD Type 2","Keep history with versioned rows"],["Event log","Queryable record of progress, quality metrics, errors"],["Temporary view","Intermediate dataset not published to the catalog"]]},{"id":"ch10-e078","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Delta Live Tables and Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines are two unrelated products.","explain":"False. They are the same product line renamed: Delta Live Tables → Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines → Spark Declarative Pipelines. Old tutorials (DLT, `import dlt`, APPLY CHANGES) still describe the same concepts.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e079","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A customer moves from Athens to Thessaloniki. You must keep BOTH addresses with validity periods for historical reports. Which pattern?","explain":"SCD Type 2 keeps history by closing the old row and inserting a new version. SCD Type 1 would overwrite Athens with Thessaloniki and lose history. AUTO CDC supports both via its SCD type setting.","options":["SCD Type 1","SCD Type 2","Temporary view","Complete output mode"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e080","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT a dataset type you define in a declarative pipeline?","explain":"Streaming tables, materialized views and temporary views are pipeline datasets. A SQL warehouse is compute for Databricks SQL, not a dataset.","options":["Streaming table","Materialized view","Temporary view","SQL warehouse"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch10-e081","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the expectation that drops rows with a NULL order_id.","explain":"Expectations are declared as `CONSTRAINT name EXPECT (condition)`; `ON VIOLATION DROP ROW` drops bad rows (default is to keep and only record them; `FAIL UPDATE` stops the update). Violations are counted in the event log.","asCode":true,"text":"CONSTRAINT valid_id [[EXPECT]] (order_id IS NOT NULL)\n  ON [[VIOLATION]] [[DROP ROW]]","bank":["CHECK","ERROR","DELETE"]},{"id":"ch10-e082","type":"match","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the job trigger to when it starts a run.","explain":"Schedules are time-based; continuous keeps one run always going; file-arrival reacts to new files in storage; table-update reacts to changes in monitored tables.","pairs":[["Schedule","At a cron-style time"],["Continuous","Immediately after the previous run ends"],["File arrival","When new files land in a monitored location"],["Table update","When a monitored source table changes"]]},{"id":"ch10-e083","type":"order","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the tasks of the example job DAG (customers and orders run in parallel — treat them as one step).","explain":"ingest → bronze → (customers ‖ orders) → gold → quality check. gold depends on both silver tasks; the quality check runs last on the final outputs.","items":["ingest","bronze","customers + orders (parallel)","gold","quality check"]},{"id":"ch10-e084","type":"order","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the ‘task failed’ debugging model.","explain":"Upstream first (cheapest), then infrastructure (compute, runtime, library), then identity (permissions), then inputs (parameters, data), and finally Spark execution itself.","items":["Upstream?","Compute?","Runtime?","Library?","Permissions?","Parameter?","Data?","Spark execution?"]},{"id":"ch10-e085","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Nightly job `orders_daily`: task `gold` fails with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'holidays'`.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which bucket of the debugging model is this?","options":[{"text":"Library","ok":true,"fb":"A missing Python module is a dependency/library problem."},{"text":"Permissions","ok":false,"fb":"A permission error would say PERMISSION_DENIED / access denied."},{"text":"Data","ok":false,"fb":"Data issues show up as schema/value errors, not missing modules."}]},{"prompt":"The task runs fine on your all-purpose cluster. Why does the job fail?","options":[{"text":"The job's compute (job cluster / serverless environment) doesn't have the library installed","ok":true,"fb":"Each compute has its own libraries; your interactive cluster's packages don't follow the job."},{"text":"The job runs an older Spark that can't import Python","ok":false,"fb":"All runtimes run Python; it's the missing package."},{"text":"Retries are disabled","ok":false,"fb":"Retrying won't install the library."}]},{"prompt":"Best production fix?","options":[{"text":"Declare the dependency in the job's task libraries / serverless environment (ideally pinned, in the bundle)","ok":true,"fb":"Reproducible: the dependency is part of the job definition."},{"text":"Point the job at your all-purpose cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Works by accident, costs more and isn't isolated or reproducible."},{"text":"`pip install` inside the notebook manually once","ok":false,"fb":"Manual one-off steps aren't reproducible."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e086","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Where does each job compute option sit?","explain":"Serverless job compute is Databricks-managed. New job clusters, shared job clusters and existing all-purpose clusters are classic compute you configure; only the job clusters are created for the run and terminated after.","buckets":["Serverless (managed)","Classic (you configure)"],"items":[{"text":"Serverless job compute","bucket":0},{"text":"New job cluster","bucket":1},{"text":"Shared job cluster","bucket":1},{"text":"Existing all-purpose compute","bucket":1},{"text":"Classic job compute","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e087","type":"match","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the Jobs concept to its definition.","explain":"A job is the definition, a task a unit of work, a run one execution; dynamic value references are resolved at run time; run-job triggers another job; for-each loops a nested task.","pairs":[["Job","The orchestrated workflow definition"],["Task","One unit of work inside a job"],["Run","One execution of a job"],["Dynamic value reference","Placeholder like {{job.run_id}} resolved at run time"],["Run-job task","A task that triggers another job"],["For-each","Runs a nested task once per list item"]]},{"id":"ch10-e088","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Running production jobs on an existing all-purpose cluster is the recommended default.","explain":"False. All-purpose compute is meant for interactive work: it's pricier and shared/less isolated. Production jobs normally use serverless job compute or job clusters created per run.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e089","type":"match","section":"ch10-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the Databricks SQL feature to its purpose.","explain":"Query history lists past runs; the query profile breaks one query down by operator; alerts notify on conditions; AI/BI dashboards visualize; the Statement Execution API runs SQL over REST; JDBC/ODBC connect BI tools.","pairs":[["Query history","List of past queries with duration and user"],["Query profile","Per-operator execution breakdown of one query"],["Alerts","Notify when a scheduled query meets a condition"],["AI/BI dashboards","Native Databricks dashboards"],["Statement Execution API","Run SQL on a warehouse via REST"],["JDBC / ODBC","Drivers for Power BI / Tableau"]]},{"id":"ch10-e090","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"At 9:00, 60 analysts open dashboards; each query is simple but many are QUEUED. What do you change on the SQL warehouse?","explain":"Queueing is a concurrency problem: raise max clusters so the warehouse scales out. A bigger size speeds up individual heavy queries but doesn't add parallel capacity as effectively.","options":["Increase warehouse size","Increase max clusters","Disable auto-stop","Switch to an all-purpose cluster"],"answer":1,"why":["Size helps heavy single queries, not queueing.","Correct — scale out for concurrency.","Auto-stop only affects idle time.","All-purpose clusters aren't for BI serving."]},{"id":"ch10-e091","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Author, monitor/debug, or connect?","explain":"Authoring happens in the SQL editor, queries and dashboards; monitoring/debugging uses query history, query profile and alerts; external access goes through JDBC/ODBC, Power BI/Tableau and the Statement Execution API.","buckets":["Author","Monitor / debug","Connect externally"],"items":[{"text":"SQL Editor","bucket":0},{"text":"AI/BI dashboards","bucket":0},{"text":"Query history","bucket":1},{"text":"Query profile","bucket":1},{"text":"Alerts","bucket":1},{"text":"JDBC / ODBC","bucket":2},{"text":"Statement Execution API","bucket":2},{"text":"Tableau connectivity","bucket":2}]},{"id":"ch10-e092","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Auto-stop shuts the SQL warehouse down after a period of inactivity to save cost.","explain":"True. Auto-stop stops an idle warehouse after the configured minutes; the next query starts it again (fast for serverless).","answer":true},{"id":"ch10-e093","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT a SQL warehouse type?","explain":"SQL warehouses come as Serverless, Pro and Classic. All-purpose is a classic cluster type for notebooks, not a warehouse type.","options":["Serverless","Pro","Classic","All-purpose"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch10-e094","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"One gold dashboard query takes 3 minutes; all others are fast and nothing is queued.","steps":[{"prompt":"Where do you look first?","options":[{"text":"The query profile of that query","ok":true,"fb":"It shows which operator (scan, join, aggregate) eats the time and how much data is read."},{"text":"Raise max clusters","ok":false,"fb":"Nothing is queued — concurrency isn't the problem."},{"text":"Recreate the dashboard","ok":false,"fb":"The dashboard isn't the slow part; the query is."}]},{"prompt":"The profile shows a full scan of a huge table with most time in the Scan. Reasonable direction?","options":[{"text":"Improve data layout/skipping (clustering on filter columns) or precompute with a materialized view","ok":true,"fb":"Less data read = faster; MVs precompute heavy aggregations."},{"text":"Disable Photon","ok":false,"fb":"Photon speeds up scans; disabling it slows things down."},{"text":"Shorten auto-stop","ok":false,"fb":"Auto-stop affects idle cost, not query speed."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e095","type":"order","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the investigation process for ‘query slow’.","explain":"Go from coarse to fine: Spark UI → the slow stage → the slow tasks inside it → compare their distribution → then test hypotheses (skew, shuffle, spill, I/O, join, GC, small files). Never start with ‘bigger cluster’.","items":["Query slow","Open Spark UI","Find slow stage","Find slow tasks","Compare task distribution","Test hypotheses: skew / shuffle / spill / I/O / join / GC / small files"]},{"id":"ch10-e096","type":"match","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the physical-plan operator to its meaning.","explain":"Exchange = shuffle; BroadcastHashJoin = small side copied everywhere; SortMergeJoin = shuffle + sort + merge; HashAggregate = hash-based aggregation; WholeStageCodegen = fused generated code; Scan = reading the source.","pairs":[["Exchange","Shuffle / redistribution of data (stage boundary)"],["BroadcastHashJoin","Small side copied to every executor"],["SortMergeJoin","Both sides shuffled by key, sorted, merged"],["HashAggregate","Aggregation using a hash map"],["WholeStageCodegen","Several operators fused into one generated function"],["Scan","Reading the data source"]]},{"id":"ch10-e097","type":"match","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the Spark UI metric to what it tells you.","explain":"Shuffle write/read measure data moved between stages; spill (memory/disk) shows data that didn't fit; GC time shows JVM memory pressure. Uneven shuffle read across tasks is the classic skew signal.","pairs":[["Shuffle write","Bytes written by map tasks for the next stage"],["Shuffle read","Bytes fetched by reduce tasks"],["Spill (memory)","In-memory size of data that had to be spilled"],["Spill (disk)","On-disk size of spilled data"],["GC time","Time paused for JVM garbage collection"]]},{"id":"ch10-e098","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"You need to reduce 2,000 tiny output partitions to 50 before writing, as cheaply as possible. Which call?","explain":"`coalesce(50)` merges existing partitions without a full shuffle — cheap, down-only. `repartition(50)` does a full shuffle (useful to rebalance or increase), which costs more here.","options":["repartition(50)","coalesce(50)","repartition(5000)","AQE must be disabled first"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e099","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Which bottleneck does each piece of evidence point to?","explain":"Spill, high GC and OOMs mean memory pressure. Long time reading storage, thousands of tiny files or slow shuffle fetch mean I/O-bound. Busy cores with heavy UDFs/expressions and no spill mean CPU-bound.","buckets":["CPU-bound","Memory-bound","I/O-bound"],"items":[{"text":"Cores at 100%, heavy Python UDF, no spill","bucket":0},{"text":"Complex expressions/decompression dominate","bucket":0},{"text":"Large spill (disk)","bucket":1},{"text":"High GC time","bucket":1},{"text":"Executor OOM","bucket":1},{"text":"Thousands of tiny input files","bucket":2},{"text":"Time dominated by reading storage","bucket":2}]},{"id":"ch10-e100","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"When a job slows from 8 to 42 minutes, the first step is to use a bigger cluster.","explain":"False. The plan explicitly rejects blind scaling: open the Spark UI, find the slow stage and tasks, compare distributions and test hypotheses. If it's skew, more nodes won't help at all.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e101","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The DRIVER crashes with OutOfMemory right after `big_df.collect()`. Most likely cause?","explain":"`collect()` (and `toPandas()`) pulls all rows into the driver's memory — a classic driver OOM. Executor OOMs come from huge partitions/skew on workers, not from collecting results.","options":["A skewed partition on one executor","Collecting a large result to the driver","Too many shuffle partitions","Photon is enabled"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e102","type":"match","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the performance technique to its definition.","explain":"Salting spreads hot keys; broadcasting avoids shuffling the big side; AQE re-optimizes at runtime; compaction fixes small files; data skipping avoids reading irrelevant files.","pairs":[["Salting","Add a random suffix to a hot key to spread it"],["Broadcasting","Send a small table to all executors"],["AQE","Runtime re-optimization: coalesce, switch joins, split skew"],["File compaction","Merge many small files into larger ones"],["Data skipping","Use file stats to avoid reading files"]]},{"id":"ch10-e103","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case from the plan: **Job used to take 8 min. Now takes 42 min.**","steps":[{"prompt":"First move?","options":[{"text":"Open the Spark UI and find which stage got slow","ok":true,"fb":"Evidence first: locate the slow stage."},{"text":"Double the workers","ok":false,"fb":"Blind scaling; if it's skew it won't help."},{"text":"Rewrite the job in SQL","ok":false,"fb":"No evidence that the language is the issue."}]},{"prompt":"Stage 7 (a join) went from 1 to 35 minutes. Inside it?","options":[{"text":"Compare task metrics: min/median/max duration, shuffle read, spill per task","ok":true,"fb":"The distribution tells skew vs uniform slowness."},{"text":"Check the driver logs only","ok":false,"fb":"The driver log rarely shows per-task imbalance."},{"text":"Restart the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys the UI evidence."}]},{"prompt":"All tasks are uniformly slower and shuffle read grew 5×; the plan shows SortMergeJoin where it used to be BroadcastHashJoin. Interpretation?","options":[{"text":"The dimension table grew past the broadcast threshold, so the join switched to a shuffle join","ok":true,"fb":"Uniform slowdown + more shuffle + changed join strategy = data growth changed the plan; consider broadcast hints, filtering, or layout."},{"text":"Classic data skew","ok":false,"fb":"Skew shows a few slow tasks, not a uniform slowdown."},{"text":"GC is broken","ok":false,"fb":"No GC evidence was mentioned."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e104","type":"odd","section":"ch10-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Three are physical-plan operators. Which one is a Spark UI task metric instead?","explain":"Exchange, SortMergeJoin and HashAggregate appear in physical plans. GC time is a per-task metric shown in the Spark UI.","options":["Exchange","SortMergeJoin","HashAggregate","GC time"],"answer":3},{"id":"ch10-e105","type":"order","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the bundle commands for a first deployment to dev.","explain":"Validate the configuration, deploy the resources to the dev target, then run the deployed job by its resource key.","items":["databricks bundle validate","databricks bundle deploy -t dev","databricks bundle run orders_job -t dev"]},{"id":"ch10-e106","type":"spotbug","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The bundle defines `resources.jobs.orders_job` with `name: orders-job`. Which command is wrong?","explain":"`bundle run` takes the resource KEY (`orders_job`), not the display name (`orders-job`). Validate and deploy are correct.","lines":["databricks bundle validate","databricks bundle deploy -t dev","databricks bundle run orders-job -t dev"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"databricks bundle run orders_job -t dev"},{"id":"ch10-e107","type":"write","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the `targets` section of `databricks.yml` with a `dev` target in development mode and a `prod` target in production mode.","explain":"Targets define deployment environments; `mode: development` gives per-user, prefixed, paused deployments, while `mode: production` marks the real deployment (ideally run as a service principal).","solution":"targets:\n  dev:\n    mode: development\n  prod:\n    mode: production","keywords":["targets:","dev:","mode: development","prod:","mode: production"],"lang":"yaml"},{"id":"ch10-e108","type":"match","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the developer-workflow tool to its definition.","explain":"The CLI drives Databricks from a terminal; Git folders bring repos into the workspace; workspace files hold non-notebook code/config; wheels package libraries; Databricks Connect runs local IDE code on remote compute.","pairs":[["Databricks CLI","Terminal tool: `databricks …` commands"],["Git folders","Git repo cloned into the workspace (formerly Repos)"],["Workspace files","Non-notebook files (.py, .yml) in the workspace"],["Python wheel","Packaged library (.whl) installed on compute"],["Databricks Connect","Run local IDE Spark code on remote Databricks compute"]]},{"id":"ch10-e109","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Per the source, Declarative Automation Bundles is the current name for what used to be called Databricks Asset Bundles.","explain":"True (per the source). Bundles describe jobs, pipelines, source files and deployment targets as version-controlled YAML (`databricks.yml`) and are deployed with `databricks bundle …`. Expect both names in docs and tutorials.","answer":true},{"id":"ch10-e110","type":"cloze","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the bundle basics.","explain":"The primary bundle file is `databricks.yml` (YAML). `-t` selects the target; `validate` checks configuration before deploying.","text":"The primary bundle file is [[databricks.yml]]. You check it with `databricks bundle [[validate]]`, deploy with `databricks bundle deploy [[-t]] dev`, and run a job with `databricks bundle [[run]] orders_job -t dev`.","bank":["bundle.json","start","--env"]},{"id":"ch10-e111","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Your CI pipeline deploys the bundle to prod. Which identity should it use?","explain":"A service principal: a non-human deployment identity with exactly the needed permissions. Personal credentials in CI are a security and continuity risk.","options":["A developer's personal access token","A service principal","The workspace admin account","No identity — bundles deploy anonymously"],"answer":1},{"id":"ch10-e112","type":"free","section":"ch10-s14","difficulty":3,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview: why define jobs and pipelines in a bundle instead of clicking them together in the UI?","explain":"Look for version control, review, reproducible multi-environment deploys, CI/CD, rollbacks, service-principal identity.","model":"With a bundle, jobs/pipelines/source files and targets are version-controlled configuration. Changes are reviewed in Git, validated and tested in CI, and deployed the same way to dev/test/prod with per-environment settings. Deployments are reproducible and can be rolled back by redeploying a previous commit, and production runs under a service principal instead of someone's account. Clicking in the UI is manual, unreviewed and hard to reproduce.","rubric":["Version control / code review","Reproducible deployments across dev/test/prod (targets)","CI/CD validation & testing","Rollback by redeploying a previous version","Service principal / deployment identity"]},{"id":"ch10-e113","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"You need to run a **scheduled PySpark workload without managing infrastructure**. Which compute?","explain":"Serverless jobs compute: scheduled (a job), PySpark, and Databricks manages the infrastructure. All-purpose is interactive; classic job clusters require you to configure VMs; a SQL warehouse runs SQL, not PySpark jobs.","options":["All-purpose cluster","Classic job cluster","Serverless Jobs","SQL warehouse"],"answer":2,"why":["Interactive and you manage it.","Classic = you manage the VM configuration.","Correct.","Warehouses run SQL, not PySpark workloads."]},{"id":"ch10-e114","type":"scenario","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"A 2 TB ETL job went from 12 to 45 minutes. One stage has 1,000 tasks: 997 finish in 20 s, 3 run 15 min spilling 80 GB each.","steps":[{"prompt":"Most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"text":"Data skew — a few partitions hold far more data","ok":true,"fb":"A long tail of a few huge tasks is the skew signature."},{"text":"The cluster is too small","ok":false,"fb":"997 tasks finish quickly; capacity is fine."},{"text":"Too many small files","ok":false,"fb":"Small files make many tasks slow-ish, not 3 extreme stragglers."}]},{"prompt":"How do you prove it in the Spark UI?","options":[{"text":"Stage summary metrics: median vs max duration, shuffle read/records and spill concentrated in the 3 tasks","ok":true,"fb":"Evidence: max ≫ median and the same 3 tasks read far more shuffle data."},{"text":"Check the job's total duration only","ok":false,"fb":"Total duration can't distinguish skew from other causes."},{"text":"Look at the notebook code formatting","ok":false,"fb":"Not evidence."}]},{"prompt":"Then what?","options":[{"text":"Find the hot join/grouping key (e.g. NULL or one giant customer) and apply AQE skew handling, salting, or broadcasting the small side","ok":true,"fb":"Fix the distribution, not the hardware."},{"text":"Add 20 workers","ok":false,"fb":"The 3 huge partitions still run as 3 single tasks."},{"text":"Increase GC time","ok":false,"fb":"GC time is a symptom metric, not a setting to raise."}]}]},{"id":"ch10-e115","type":"calc","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"In the 2 TB case, 3 straggler tasks each spill 80 GB. How many GB spill in total from those tasks?","explain":"3 × 80 GB = 240 GB of spill concentrated in 3 tasks out of 1,000 — further evidence that the data is unevenly distributed (skew), not that the whole cluster lacks memory.","answer":240,"tolerance":0,"unit":"GB"},{"id":"ch10-e116","type":"bucket","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Certification-style or engineering-style question?","explain":"Certification-style questions ask you to pick the correct product/feature for a short scenario. Engineering-style questions give symptoms and numbers and expect hypothesis → evidence → fix.","buckets":["Certification-style","Engineering-style"],"items":[{"text":"Which compute for a scheduled PySpark workload without infra management?","bucket":0},{"text":"Which command shows a table's version history?","bucket":0},{"text":"Which privilege is needed to access a schema?","bucket":0},{"text":"3 of 1,000 tasks take 15 min and spill 80 GB — investigate","bucket":1},{"text":"Job went from 8 to 42 minutes — what do you do?","bucket":1},{"text":"Bronze suddenly has every row twice — find the cause","bucket":1}]},{"id":"ch10-e117","type":"order","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the verification list the plan gives after hypothesizing skew.","explain":"Start in the Spark UI, look at the task-duration distribution, then shuffle read/write and spill per task, then partition sizes, then identify the join/grouping key, and confirm with the physical plan.","items":["Spark UI","Task-duration distribution","Shuffle read/write","Spill","Partition sizes","Join/grouping keys","Physical plan"]},{"id":"ch10-e118","type":"free","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":3,"tags":["interview","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Answer the engineering-style question as in an interview: *3 of 1,000 tasks take 15 min and spill 80 GB each; the rest take 20 s. What is happening and how do you investigate?*","explain":"Look for: skew hypothesis, UI evidence, hot key, fixes, why scaling doesn't help.","model":"It's very likely data skew: a few partitions got far more data than the rest, so three tasks process huge partitions, run out of execution memory and spill. I'd confirm in the Spark UI stage view — median duration ~20 s vs max ~15 min, and those three tasks have much larger shuffle read/records and all the spill. Then I'd find the join or groupBy key feeding that stage and check its value distribution (often NULLs or one giant customer) and confirm the join type in the physical plan. Fixes: enable/tune AQE skew-join handling, salt the hot key, broadcast the small side, or handle the hot key/NULLs separately. Adding nodes wouldn't help, because the big partitions still run as single tasks.","rubric":["Hypothesis: data skew","Evidence: median vs max duration, shuffle read and spill per task in Spark UI","Find the hot key (join/grouping key, NULLs)","Fixes: AQE skew handling, salting, broadcast, separate hot-key handling","Explains why more hardware doesn't fix it"]},{"id":"ch10-e119","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"If 997 tasks finish in 20 s and 3 take 15 minutes, the cluster simply needs more workers.","explain":"False. The cluster handles 997 tasks quickly; the problem is 3 oversized partitions. More workers can't split a single task, so the stragglers stay slow — this is skew.","answer":false},{"id":"ch10-e801","type":"spotbug","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":3,"tags":["pitfall","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"The folder contains `orders_001.csv`, `orders_002.csv`, `customers_001.csv`. This COPY INTO loads nothing. Which line?","lines":["COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw","FROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/incoming/'","FILEFORMAT = CSV","PATTERN = 'orders_.*[.]csv'","FORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true');"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"PATTERN = 'orders_*.csv'","explain":"COPY INTO's PATTERN is a **glob**, not a regex. `'orders_.*[.]csv'` would need a literal dot after `orders_`; `'orders_*.csv'` matches the order files.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch10-e802","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Because Auto Loader ingests each file exactly once (with an intact checkpoint), the Bronze table cannot contain two rows with the same order_id.","answer":false,"explain":"Exactly-once is about files. Two distinct files (an upstream retry or resend) can carry the same order_id, and both are correctly ingested once. Business-key dedup/MERGE belongs in Silver.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch10-e901","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The Delta streaming sink supports all three output modes: append, update and complete.","answer":false,"explain":"No — append and complete only. Update-like semantics into Delta are built with foreachBatch + MERGE.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch10-e902","type":"mcq","section":"ch10-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"The preview's code uses `.trigger(processingTime=\"30 seconds\")`. You run it as a serverless job. What happens / what should you use?","options":["Works the same as on classic","Not supported on serverless — use availableNow on a schedule, or Lakeflow continuous mode for always-on","Serverless silently converts it to real-time mode","It runs once and fails"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serverless notebooks/jobs support AvailableNow (and deprecated Once), not time-based ProcessingTime or real-time triggers. Schedule an AvailableNow job, or use a Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode for an always-on stream.","src":"part2"},{"id":"ch10-e903","type":"tf","section":"ch10-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"In a long-running stream-static join, updating the dimension table automatically recomputes all previously written enriched rows.","answer":false,"explain":"No. New micro-batches may join against the newer dimension, but already-written outputs are not recomputed. If correctness requires recomputation, materialized/declarative pipeline patterns (Phase 6) are often a better fit.","src":"part2"}],"src":"part1"},{"id":"ch11","num":11,"title":"Unity Catalog Mastery","subtitle":"Phase 3 — metastore → catalog → schema → object, grants, storage, volumes, policies and the PERMISSION_DENIED algorithm","emoji":"🛡️","sourcePages":"403–456","mantra":"Every Unity Catalog question is four questions — WHO? WHAT OBJECT? WHAT ACTION? WHERE ARE THE BYTES? — and every PERMISSION_DENIED is a walk down the whole authorization chain, not just \"do I have SELECT?\".","objectives":["You can explain why Unity Catalog exists, what it stores (metadata, permissions, lineage, storage references) and what it does NOT store (the bytes).","You can place metastore, workspace, catalog, schema and object correctly and parse any `catalog.schema.object` name.","You can write real GRANT / REVOKE / SHOW GRANTS statements and predict the effect of USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, inheritance, ownership, MANAGE, ALL PRIVILEGES and BROWSE.","You can contrast managed vs external tables and volumes, including what DROP does to the files.","You can explain the chain cloud IAM → storage credential → external location → external table/volume → files, and managed storage locations.","You can protect data with row filters, column masks and ABAC, and use lineage for impact analysis and debugging.","You can design the grants of a production lakehouse (groups + service principals, least privilege).","You can debug any PERMISSION_DENIED with the 8-layer algorithm (I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions) and the six classic cases."],"sections":[{"id":"ch11-s01","title":"The Four Questions & Why Unity Catalog Exists","hook":"Four questions explain almost all of Unity Catalog. Learn them first and the rest of the phase slots in around them.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The most important mental model of Phase 3: every governance question breaks into **four questions**, asked top to bottom."},{"t":"diagram","text":"WHO?            user / group / service principal\n   │\n   ▼\nWHAT OBJECT?    metastore\n                └── catalog\n                    └── schema\n                        ├── table     ├── view\n                        ├── volume    ├── function\n                        └── model\n   │\n   ▼\nWHAT ACTION?    USE / SELECT / MODIFY / CREATE / READ VOLUME / ...\n   │\n   ▼\nWHERE ARE       managed storage\nTHE BYTES?      or  external location → cloud object storage","caption":"The Phase-3 mental model: WHO → WHAT OBJECT → WHAT ACTION → WHERE ARE THE BYTES"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Make this one diagram clear","text":"Once **WHO / WHAT OBJECT / WHAT ACTION / WHERE ARE THE BYTES** is clear, almost all of Unity Catalog starts to make sense. Every later section answers one of these four."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why does Unity Catalog exist?** Start without Databricks. A company keeps its data in **S3 / ADLS** folders and has **100 developers**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"S3 / ADLS\n├── finance/\n│   ├── payroll/\n│   └── invoices/\n├── sales/\n│   ├── orders/\n│   └── customers/\n└── marketing/","caption":"Just folders. Nothing here says who may read payroll."},{"t":"p","text":"Without **central governance** you must answer by hand:"},{"t":"list","items":["Who can **read** payroll? Who can **write** orders?","Who is the **owner**?","Which **pipeline** created this table? Which **dashboard** uses this column?","Which **service principal** has access?","What happens if someone reads the **storage path directly**, bypassing permissions?"],"ordered":false},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: what if permissions live only in the S3 bucket, Azure Storage, notebooks, clusters and BI tools?","text":"You get **fragmented governance**: five places, five permission models, no single answer to \"who has what?\". Unity Catalog makes governance **central**."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks describes Unity Catalog as a **hierarchical governance layer**: every governed asset is a **securable object**, privileges go to **users, groups or service principals**, the **metastore** is the top-level container, and assets live in the three-level namespace `catalog.schema.object`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Unity Catalog ≠ storage","text":"UC is **not** a giant database filesystem and does **not** store the Parquet bytes. It is **metadata + namespace + permissions + ownership + lineage + governance policies + storage references**. The bytes live in **S3 / ADLS / GCS**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Unity Catalog\n     │  governs\n     ▼\ntable metadata\n     │  points to / manages\n     ▼\ncloud storage  (S3 / ADLS / GCS)","caption":"UC governs metadata that points at (or manages) cloud storage"},{"t":"callout","kind":"analogy","title":"Library catalogue + security desk","text":"UC is the library's **catalogue and security desk**: it knows every book, who may borrow it and where it sits. The **books themselves** stand on shelves elsewhere — your cloud storage."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Recap → Chapter 1","text":"Metadata ≠ data and UC ≠ Delta Lake were introduced in Chapter 1 (\"Table Metadata & the Unity Catalog Namespace\"). This chapter goes deep."}]},{"id":"ch11-s02","title":"Metastore, Workspace & the Hierarchy","hook":"The metastore sits above everything, and the workspace is not part of the namespace at all. Misplace either and your debugging goes wrong from the first step.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The hierarchy from the top: one **metastore** holds **catalogs**, catalogs hold **schemas**, schemas hold **objects**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"METASTORE\n├── CATALOG: dev\n│   ├── SCHEMA: bronze\n│   ├── SCHEMA: silver\n│   └── SCHEMA: gold\n├── CATALOG: prod\n│   ├── SCHEMA: bronze\n│   ├── SCHEMA: silver\n│   └── SCHEMA: gold\n└── CATALOG: sandbox","caption":"Metastore → catalogs → schemas"},{"t":"diagram","text":"prod\n└── silver\n    ├── orders            (table)\n    ├── customers         (table)\n    ├── products          (table)\n    ├── files_volume      (volume)\n    └── normalize_email() (function)","caption":"Inside a schema: tables, volumes, functions …"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks treats the **catalog** as the **primary unit of data isolation**, and the **schema** as a finer organizational / access-control layer."},{"t":"p","text":"The **metastore** is the **top-level container of Unity Catalog for metadata and permissions**. It exposes the three-level namespace `catalog.schema.object`."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"A metastore IS","points":["the top-level UC container","metadata about catalogs, tables, volumes, external locations, credentials, shares, permissions …","regional (usually one per region)"]},{"title":"A metastore is NOT","points":["a table","a schema","a storage bucket","a workspace"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Usually there is **one metastore per region**, and many workspaces in that region attach to it — so they share governance."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Databricks Account\n├── Workspace A ──┐\n├── Workspace B ──┼──►  Metastore Europe\n└── Workspace C ──┘","caption":"Many workspaces, one regional metastore → common governance"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Workspace","points":["operational development environment","notebooks, jobs, compute live here","where you WORK"]},{"title":"Metastore","points":["regional Unity Catalog governance container","`prod.sales.orders` is REGISTERED here","where objects are GOVERNED"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"So two workspaces can see the **same governed object** when all three hold:"},{"t":"flow","items":["Both attached to the same metastore","Principal has the permission","No workspace binding forbids it","Access ✓"],"caption":"Three conditions for cross-workspace access"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Metastore vs catalog","text":"**Metastore** = top **regional** governance container. **Catalog** = primary **logical / data isolation** container **inside** the metastore. You do **not** create a metastore per project by default — isolation usually starts at the **catalog** level."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"The workspace is not in the namespace","text":"`catalog.schema.object` has no workspace level. A notebook **lives in** a workspace; `prod.sales.orders` is **registered in** the metastore. \"Workspace above catalog\" is wrong."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Recap → Chapter 4","text":"Account vs workspace (one account, many workspaces, metastores configured at account level) is in Chapter 4, \"Account vs Workspace\"."}]},{"id":"ch11-s03","title":"Catalogs, Schemas, Objects & Creating Them","hook":"Catalog = big boundary, schema = organization, object = the thing. Creating each already needs hierarchical privileges.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"prod . sales . orders\n^^^^   ^^^^^   ^^^^^^\ncatalog schema object","caption":"Three names, three levels"},{"t":"p","text":"The **catalog** is the first level. Think of it as a large **logical / security boundary**."},{"t":"table","head":["Design style","Example catalogs"],"rows":[["By environment","`dev`, `test`, `prod`"],["By business unit","`finance`, `sales`, `marketing`"],["Mixed","`prod_finance`, `prod_sales`, `dev_shared`"]],"caption":"There is no single right structure"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks recommends that catalogs represent **meaningful isolation boundaries** — environment, business unit, team or project — so that **hierarchical grants stay simple and logical**."},{"t":"p","text":"The **schema** is the second level: a child of a catalog that can contain **tables, views, volumes, models and functions**. Organize schemas by **layer, team, domain or use case**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"By layer","points":["prod.bronze","prod.silver","prod.gold"]},{"title":"By domain","points":["prod.finance","prod.logistics","prod.crm"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"The third name is the **object**: table, view, volume, function, model, materialized view, streaming table … So the general form is **`catalog.schema.object`**, not always `catalog.schema.table`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Recap → Chapter 4: fully qualified names","text":"`SELECT * FROM prod.sales.orders` is **fully qualified**; `SELECT * FROM orders` depends on `current_catalog()` / `current_schema()` (set with `USE CATALOG` / `USE SCHEMA`). In production, fully qualified names are safer because they **remove ambiguity**. Details: Chapter 4, \"Namespaces and Fully Qualified Names\"."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE CATALOG ecommerce;\nUSE CATALOG ecommerce;\n\nCREATE SCHEMA ecommerce.silver;\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce.silver;   -- idempotent","caption":"Creating a catalog and a schema"},{"t":"table","head":["You want to…","Privilege you typically need"],"rows":[["`CREATE CATALOG`","`CREATE CATALOG` on the **metastore**"],["`CREATE SCHEMA` in `ecommerce`","`USE CATALOG` **+** `CREATE SCHEMA` on the parent catalog `ecommerce`"]],"caption":"The first example that permissions are hierarchical"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"CREATE SCHEMA needs two privileges","text":"`CREATE SCHEMA` alone is not enough: you also need `USE CATALOG` on the parent catalog. It is the first time you see that UC permissions are **hierarchical**."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when CREATE CATALOG / CREATE SCHEMA fails","questions":["Am I creating a catalog — then do I have CREATE CATALOG on the metastore?","Am I creating a schema — do I have BOTH USE CATALOG and CREATE SCHEMA on the parent catalog?","Am I using the fully qualified name, so the schema lands in the catalog I think?","Is the identity running this the one that holds the grants (me vs a job's service principal)?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s04","title":"Securables, Principals, Groups & Service Principals","hook":"Grants connect a principal to a securable through a privilege. Grant to groups and run production as service principals, and an access list stays manageable.","blocks":[{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Recap → Chapter 3","text":"Principals (user / group / service principal), securables and the GRANT grammar were introduced in Chapter 3. New here: the full securable list, account-level provisioning, and why production **ownership and writes** belong to groups / service principals."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Securable object","def":"An object registered in Unity Catalog on which grants can be given to a principal: catalog, schema, table, volume, **external location, storage credential**, function …"},{"term":"Principal","def":"An identity that receives permissions: **user** (`nikos@company.com`), **group** (`data_engineers`), **service principal** (`orders-etl-prod`)."},{"term":"Privilege","def":"An allowed **action** on a securable, e.g. `SELECT`, `MODIFY`, `USE SCHEMA`, `READ VOLUME`."}]},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks recommends provisioning principals at the **account level** and using **groups** instead of individual users wherever possible."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- painful: one grant per person (×200 tables!)\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.gold.daily_sales TO `nikos@company.com`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.gold.daily_sales TO `maria@company.com`;\n-- ... John, Eva ...\n\n-- better: one grant to the group\nGRANT SELECT\nON TABLE prod.gold.daily_sales\nTO `analysts`;","caption":"Grant to groups, manage membership"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Maria joins the team\n        │\n        ▼\nadd Maria to group `analysts`\n        │\n        ▼\n0 table grants changed  (not 200)","caption":"Membership management replaces grant sprawl"},{"t":"p","text":"A **service principal** is a machine identity. The **02:00 daily ETL** should not depend on `nikos@company.com`: if Nikos leaves, his account is **disabled** and production stops. Use `orders-etl-prod` instead."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Production ownership & writes → groups / service principals","text":"Databricks recommends assigning **production ownership and write access** to **groups or service principals**, not personal users."},{"t":"table","head":["Part","Meaning"],"rows":[["`GRANT`","give"],["`SELECT`","which permission (privilege)"],["`ON TABLE`","which object type"],["`ecommerce.silver.orders`","which object (securable)"],["`TO analysts`","to which principal"]],"caption":"Parsing `GRANT SELECT ON TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders TO analysts;`"}]},{"id":"ch11-s05","title":"USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA & the Read Path","hook":"`USE` lets you through the doors but doesn't let you read anything. Reading a table takes three keys.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG\nON CATALOG ecommerce\nTO `analysts`;"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"USE CATALOG does NOT mean","points":["they can read all tables","SELECT on everything inside"]},{"title":"USE CATALOG means","points":["they can **traverse / use** the catalog as a namespace","still subject to permissions on each object"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks states explicitly: `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` are **prerequisite access privileges** — on their own they do **not** grant `SELECT`."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE SCHEMA\nON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold\nTO `analysts`;      -- still NOT \"SELECT everything\""},{"t":"diagram","text":"USE CATALOG ecommerce      (door of the building)\n        +\nUSE SCHEMA  gold           (door of the floor)\n        +\nSELECT      daily_sales    (key to the room)\n        =\nSELECT * FROM ecommerce.gold.daily_sales  ✓","caption":"The read path: three keys"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG ecommerce             TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA  ecommerce.gold        TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT      ON TABLE   ecommerce.gold.daily_sales TO `analysts`;\n\n-- now this works:\nSELECT * FROM ecommerce.gold.daily_sales;","caption":"The minimum to read one table"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first (source exercise §21): `analysts` have USE CATALOG ecommerce and USE SCHEMA ecommerce.gold, but no SELECT. What does `SELECT * FROM ecommerce.gold.daily_sales` do?","text":"It **fails**. `USE` gives no permission on the **data** — it only lets you traverse the namespace."},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging Case 2** — the mirror image: SELECT on `daily_revenue` ✅, USE CATALOG ✅, **USE SCHEMA ❌**. The query can still fail, because you **cannot traverse the schema**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"USE privileges are prerequisites, not data access","text":"Reading needs **USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT** (on the table, or inherited). Missing any one → denied. Having only the USE pair → also denied."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when I have SELECT but still get PERMISSION_DENIED","questions":["Do I (or my group) have USE CATALOG on the parent catalog?","Do I have USE SCHEMA on the exact parent schema — not on a sibling schema?","Is the SELECT on this object (or inherited from schema/catalog)?","Am I really the identity running the query?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s06","title":"Inheritance, SELECT vs MODIFY, SHOW GRANTS & REVOKE","hook":"Grants flow down the hierarchy. That saves thousands of grants, and it explains why a REVOKE can leave access in place.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Privilege inheritance**: a grant on a parent can flow to its children, following the Unity Catalog inheritance model."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT SELECT\nON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold\nTO `analysts`;\n-- flows to the tables/views inside ecommerce.gold"},{"t":"diagram","text":"CATALOG ecommerce        grant here → flows to all schemas below\n└── SCHEMA gold          grant here → flows to all objects below\n    ├── daily_sales       ← inherits\n    ├── customer_360      ← inherits\n    └── (new table)       ← inherits too","caption":"Grants on parents flow to children"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks recommends using the hierarchy and inheritance to avoid **thousands of per-table grants**."},{"t":"table","head":["Privilege","Meaning","Typical principal"],"rows":[["`SELECT`","read the table","analyst"],["`MODIFY`","write / change table data (broad DML)","data engineer / pipeline service principal"],["finer-grained DML (e.g. `INSERT`, beta)","narrower write capability instead of broad MODIFY","a pipeline that only appends"]],"caption":"SELECT vs MODIFY"},{"t":"p","text":"**MODIFY** has historically covered **broad DML**. Current Unity Catalog also has **finer-grained DML privileges in beta** (e.g. `INSERT`), so you can grant narrower write rights than MODIFY. The basic model is still analyst → SELECT and engineer or pipeline SP → MODIFY, depending on the architecture."},{"t":"p","text":"**SHOW GRANTS** answers the most basic debugging question: **who has what?**"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE  ecommerce.gold.daily_sales;\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold;\nSHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG ecommerce;\n\n-- optional: filter by principal\nSHOW GRANTS `analysts` ON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold;","caption":"Inspect every level of the hierarchy"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"REVOKE SELECT\nON TABLE ecommerce.gold.daily_sales\nFROM `analysts`;","caption":"REVOKE uses FROM, not TO"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"REVOKE on the table ≠ access gone","text":"You can remove the **direct** table grant and the user still reads, because `SELECT` on the **schema** or **catalog** is inherited. Permission debugging must always inspect **inheritance**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging Case 3**: a user had SELECT on `daily_revenue`. The admin runs REVOKE on the table, but the user **still reads**. Likely cause: `SELECT ON SCHEMA gold`, or a catalog-level inherited grant."},{"t":"diagram","text":"SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce        SELECT? ── yes → still readable\n        │ no\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.gold   SELECT? ── yes → still readable\n        │ no\nSHOW GRANTS ON TABLE   ...daily_revenue      (revoked) → access really gone","caption":"Case 3: inspect the whole hierarchy, not just the table"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a REVOKE didn't remove access","questions":["Did I revoke from the right principal — the group the user actually belongs to?","Is there SELECT on the parent schema that the table inherits?","Is there SELECT (or ALL PRIVILEGES) on the parent catalog?","Is the user in another group that still holds a grant?","Is the user the owner (or does the user hold MANAGE) and so able to re-grant?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s07","title":"Ownership, MANAGE, ALL PRIVILEGES & BROWSE","hook":"Some privileges let you change the rules rather than read data. Use them sparingly and give them to groups.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Every securable has an owner.** The owner has far more power than plain SELECT. Typically the owner can:"},{"t":"list","items":["**manage privileges** (grant / revoke to others)","**modify / manage** the object","**transfer ownership**"],"ordered":false},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Bad","points":["`prod.gold.revenue`","owner = Nikos","Nikos leaves → orphaned governance"]},{"title":"Better","points":["`prod.gold.revenue`","owner = `data_platform_admins`","group membership changes, ownership stays"]}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE prod.gold.revenue SET OWNER TO `data_platform_admins`;","caption":"Transfer ownership to a group"},{"t":"p","text":"**MANAGE** is a privilege for **delegated governance**: a principal with MANAGE can manage grants and ownership-related operations **without being the owner**."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT MANAGE ON SCHEMA prod.gold TO `data_platform_admins`;"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"ALL PRIVILEGES is not literally everything","text":"Current UC docs: **`MANAGE`, `EXTERNAL USE LOCATION` and `EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA` are NOT included in `ALL PRIVILEGES`.** Classic certification trap."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA / EXTERNAL USE LOCATION","def":"Privileges that let **external (non-Databricks) engines** access UC data in a schema / location via UC's APIs. Must be granted explicitly — never via ALL PRIVILEGES."},{"term":"ALL PRIVILEGES","def":"Every applicable privilege on the securable **except** MANAGE / EXTERNAL USE … — and it is evaluated **dynamically**, so privileges added to the platform later are included automatically."}]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- common bad practice\nGRANT ALL PRIVILEGES\nON CATALOG prod_ecommerce\nTO `all_company_users`;","caption":"Don't do this"},{"t":"p","text":"Why is that bad? It may allow **much more** than each principal needs, and `ALL PRIVILEGES` is **evaluated dynamically** — as new applicable privileges are added, they're included. Databricks recommends **restraint with both ALL PRIVILEGES and MANAGE**."},{"t":"p","text":"**BROWSE** allows **discoverability / metadata visibility** without data access. Users can discover that a dataset **exists**, then **request access** before running `SELECT`. Lineage visibility can also use BROWSE semantics for object discovery."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT BROWSE ON CATALOG prod TO `account users`;","caption":"Everyone can discover; nobody reads without SELECT"},{"t":"table","head":["Privilege / role","Read data?","Change grants?"],"rows":[["owner","can manage the object fully","yes"],["`MANAGE`","not by itself","yes (delegated)"],["`ALL PRIVILEGES`","yes (SELECT, MODIFY …)","no MANAGE — excluded"],["`BROWSE`","no — metadata only","no"],["`SELECT`","yes","no"]],"caption":"Power ladder"}]},{"id":"ch11-s08","title":"Managed vs External Tables","hook":"The difference is who controls where the files live and when they die. It decides what DROP TABLE does to your data.","blocks":[{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: does \"managed\" mean the data sits inside Databricks' servers and \"external\" outside?","text":"**No.** Both can live in **your own cloud object storage**. The difference is **who controls the location and lifecycle of the underlying files**."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks: **managed assets** get UC governance **and** storage-lifecycle management; **external assets** get UC governance, but the storage lifecycle stays **your** (or an external system's) responsibility."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders (\n  id     BIGINT,\n  amount DECIMAL(10,2)\n);                      -- no LOCATION → managed","caption":"Managed table: Unity Catalog decides the path"},{"t":"diagram","text":"catalog / schema managed storage root\n            │\n            ▼\nUnity-generated internal location\n            │\n            ▼\n        Delta files","caption":"Where a managed table's files go"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders_ext\nUSING DELTA\nLOCATION 's3://company-data/orders/';   -- or the Azure/GCS equivalent","caption":"External table: YOU specify the location"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"MANAGED TABLE — UC controls","points":["metadata","permissions","lineage","storage location","storage lifecycle"]},{"title":"EXTERNAL TABLE — UC controls","points":["metadata","permissions","lineage","— YOU control: storage location + storage lifecycle"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["","Managed","External"],"rows":[["Who picks the path?","Unity Catalog","You (`LOCATION`)"],["`DROP TABLE` metadata","removed","removed"],["`DROP TABLE` files","deleted by UC as part of the managed lifecycle","**remain** in storage"]],"caption":"The certification classic"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"DROP TABLE: managed vs external","text":"`DROP TABLE managed_table` → UC handles metadata **and** underlying file lifecycle. `DROP TABLE external_table` → UC removes the metadata, but the **files remain**. Know this cold."},{"t":"p","text":"**Which is recommended?** For new Databricks-first workloads, Databricks generally recommends **managed tables** — more automation, optimization and governance integration."},{"t":"p","text":"**External tables make sense** when: existing data already lives at known paths; multiple non-Databricks systems control the storage lifecycle; migration constraints; interoperability requirements."},{"t":"p","text":"**External table lifecycle example.** `s3://company/legacy/orders/` already holds Delta data."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.legacy_orders\nUSING DELTA\nLOCATION 's3://company/legacy/orders/';"},{"t":"list","items":["Prerequisite: the location falls under a **governed external location** + the principal has the required privileges.","UC now **registers metadata**. It does **not** necessarily rewrite the data."],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Check the type before you DROP","text":"`DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED <name>` shows **Type** (MANAGED / EXTERNAL) and **Location**. Ten seconds that prevent \"where did my data go?\" — or \"why is my bucket still full?\"."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before / after a DROP TABLE","questions":["Is this table MANAGED or EXTERNAL (DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED)?","If managed: am I OK with UC deleting the underlying files as part of the lifecycle?","If external: do I expect the files to stay — and who will clean them up?","Do other systems still read these files directly?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s09","title":"Managed Storage, Storage Credentials & External Locations","hook":"This is where the bytes physically are, and how Databricks is allowed to reach them, one layer at a time.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Where does a managed table physically go? UC can have a **managed storage location** at three levels: **metastore, catalog, schema**. The **most specific (lowest) available** location wins."},{"t":"diagram","text":"schema managed location     ← highest priority (if set)\n        │ else\ncatalog managed location\n        │ else\nmetastore managed location","caption":"Most specific managed location wins"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks now often recommends **catalog-level managed storage** as the isolation boundary."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE CATALOG ecommerce\nMANAGED LOCATION 's3://company-uc/ecommerce/';\n\nCREATE SCHEMA ecommerce.finance\nMANAGED LOCATION 's3://company-uc/ecommerce/finance/';","caption":"Managed locations on catalog and schema"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Requirements for MANAGED LOCATION","text":"The path must lie **under a Unity Catalog external location**, and you need the **`CREATE MANAGED STORAGE`** privilege (on that external location)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Storage credential.** Databricks must authenticate to the cloud for `s3://company-raw-data/`. You don't want every user holding an **AWS access key + secret key**. So UC has an object that **abstracts a long-term cloud credential**:"},{"t":"table","head":["Cloud","What the storage credential wraps"],"rows":[["AWS","IAM role"],["Azure","managed identity / service principal"],["GCP","service account"]]},{"t":"p","text":"A storage credential alone answers \"**with which identity** can I talk to the cloud?\" — not \"**which path** do we govern?\". The **external location** combines **cloud path + storage credential** and is itself a securable."},{"t":"diagram","text":"AWS IAM role / Azure identity\n        │\n        ▼\nStorage Credential      \"IAM role X\"\n        │\n        ▼\nExternal Location       path = s3://company/raw/\n        │               credential = IAM role X\n        ▼\nExternal Table / External Volume\n        │\n        ▼\n      Files","caption":"The full chain — one of the most important UC diagrams"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- storage credentials are usually created by an admin\n-- (Catalog Explorer / CLI / API) after the cloud IAM setup\nSHOW STORAGE CREDENTIALS;\n\nCREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION finance_loc\n  URL 's3://company/finance/'\n  WITH (STORAGE CREDENTIAL cred_a);\n\nGRANT CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE\n  ON EXTERNAL LOCATION finance_loc\n  TO `data_engineers`;","caption":"External location = path + credential; grant on the location"},{"t":"p","text":"**Why not grant on the storage credential directly?** A credential may reach a **huge storage scope**. An external location like `s3://company/finance/` scopes access to **that path only**. Databricks recommends granting e.g. `CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE` on the **external location** rather than on the credential."},{"t":"diagram","text":"credential A\n├── s3://company/raw/       (external location 1)\n├── s3://company/archive/   (external location 2)\n└── s3://company/shared/    (external location 3)\n     each with its own UC access boundary","caption":"One credential, many external locations"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Storage credential","points":["HOW Databricks authenticates to cloud storage","wraps IAM role / managed identity / service account","can be broad"]},{"title":"External location","points":["WHICH cloud path + WHICH credential","a securable path boundary","grant privileges here (path-scoped least privilege)"]},{"title":"External table","points":["table METADATA pointing at tabular data","lives INSIDE a governed path","not a synonym of external location"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Two confusable pairs","text":"**Storage credential vs external location**: identity vs path + identity. **External location vs external table**: governed storage path vs table metadata pointing at data inside that path. Not synonyms."},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging Case 4**: the **managed** table works, the **external** table fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED` / a storage access error. External storage adds **extra layers**: external location, storage credential, workspace binding, cloud IAM."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a managed table works but the external one fails","questions":["Is the table's LOCATION covered by an external location at all?","Do I (or the job's principal) hold the needed privilege on that external location?","Is the external location / storage credential bound to this workspace?","Does the cloud IAM role behind the storage credential actually allow this path?","Was the IAM role / trust policy changed or the bucket policy tightened recently?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s10","title":"Volumes: Governed Files","hook":"Not everything is a table. PDFs, images, configs and raw files get UC governance through volumes.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A **table** is for tabular data: rows / columns with SQL / DataFrame semantics. What about **PDF, JPEG, JSON config, ML files, checkpoints, text documents, binary artifacts, raw files**? You don't want to make them all tables."},{"t":"p","text":"**Volume** = a Unity Catalog **governed storage object for non-tabular / file-based data**."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- managed volume: UC picks the storage location\nCREATE VOLUME ecommerce.raw.documents;\n\n-- external volume: you point at an existing path\nCREATE EXTERNAL VOLUME ecommerce.raw.documents_ext\nLOCATION 's3://company/documents/';","caption":"Managed vs external volume"},{"t":"diagram","text":"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/sample.json\n         ^^^^^^^^^ ^^^ ^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^\n         catalog   schema volume  file path","caption":"Volume path anatomy"},{"t":"table","head":["","Managed volume","External volume"],"rows":[["Location chosen by","Unity Catalog","you (`LOCATION`)"],["`DROP VOLUME`","can remove underlying data (managed lifecycle)","metadata removed, **files remain**"]],"caption":"Same lifecycle logic as tables"},{"t":"table","head":["Asset","Read","Write"],"rows":[["Table","`SELECT`","`MODIFY`"],["Volume","`READ VOLUME`","`WRITE VOLUME`"]],"caption":"Different privilege names"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT READ VOLUME\nON VOLUME ecommerce.raw.documents\nTO `data_scientists`;"},{"t":"table","head":["To…","You need"],"rows":[["read files","`USE CATALOG` + `USE SCHEMA` + `READ VOLUME`"],["write files","`USE CATALOG` + `USE SCHEMA` + `READ VOLUME` + `WRITE VOLUME`"]],"caption":"Current volume privilege model (per the source)"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"path = \"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/sample.json\"\nwith open(path, \"r\") as f:\n    text = f.read()\n\ndf = spark.read.json(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/events/\")","caption":"File access: plain Python or Spark"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Volume = governed file-path semantics","text":"Databricks recommends **table names** for tables and **`/Volumes/...`** paths for direct non-tabular file access, instead of writing raw cloud URIs everywhere."},{"t":"table","head":["Need","Table","Volume"],"rows":[["SQL rows/columns","✅","❌"],["DataFrame table semantics","✅","❌"],["PDF / images / files","❌","✅"],["`SELECT *`","✅","❌"],["`READ VOLUME`","❌","✅"],["Delta table history","✅","❌ (not as table semantics)"]],"caption":"Tables vs volumes — an easy certification question"},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging Case 5**: the user has USE CATALOG ✅ and USE SCHEMA ✅, but `open(\"/Volumes/.../file.json\")` fails. Check **READ VOLUME** — and for writes, **WRITE VOLUME** too."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a /Volumes/ file can't be read or written","questions":["Is the path really /Volumes/<catalog>/<schema>/<volume>/… (right names, right order)?","Do I have USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA on the parents?","Do I have READ VOLUME on this volume (not SELECT — that's for tables)?","For writes: do I also have WRITE VOLUME?","If it's an external volume: are the external location / credential / cloud IAM fine?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"SELECT does nothing for volumes","text":"Granting `SELECT` on a schema doesn't open its volumes. Volumes use **READ VOLUME / WRITE VOLUME**."}]},{"id":"ch11-s11","title":"Workspace Bindings & the UC Compute Requirement","hook":"Correct grants are not the whole story. The workspace you are in and the compute you use can still block access.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You have two workspaces, `dev-workspace` and `prod-workspace`, and a catalog `prod`. You can say: **the `prod` catalog may be used only from specific workspaces.** That is a **workspace binding**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"                     catalog prod  (bound to prod-workspace only)\n                       ▲        ✗\n                       │        │\nprod-workspace ────────┘        └──────── dev-workspace\n   access possible                  access FAILS\n                                    (even with all grants)","caption":"Workspace binding: a separate access-control mechanism"},{"t":"p","text":"UC uses workspace bindings as a **separate access-control mechanism** for **catalogs, external locations and storage credentials**. So the user can hold the permission and access still fails, because the **current workspace has no binding** to the catalog."},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging implication.** The user has USE CATALOG `prod`, USE SCHEMA `prod.finance`, SELECT `prod.finance.payroll` — and still fails. Possible cause: **`prod` catalog not bound to this workspace.**"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"User privilege ≠ the only authorization boundary","text":"Grants answer \"may this principal do X?\". Workspace bindings answer \"may X be used **from this workspace** at all?\". Both must say yes."},{"t":"p","text":"**UC compute requirement.** Having permissions isn't enough: the **compute must be Unity Catalog-compatible**. Classic compute with **Standard** or **Dedicated** access mode can access UC; **serverless** and **Databricks SQL** compute support it."},{"t":"diagram","text":"permissions correct\n        +\nwrong / legacy compute (not UC-compatible)\n        =\nstill failure","caption":"Correct grants on the wrong compute still fail"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Recap → Chapters 7 & 8","text":"Access modes Standard vs Dedicated (Chapter 7) and SQL-warehouse Gate 2 (Chapter 8) meet here: the access mode is what makes classic compute **UC-capable**."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when every grant looks right but access still fails","questions":["Which workspace am I in — and is the catalog (or external location / credential) bound to it?","Would the same query work from the prod workspace?","Is my compute UC-compatible: Standard/Dedicated access mode, serverless, or a SQL warehouse?","Am I on an old/legacy cluster configuration someone left running?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s12","title":"Row Filters, Column Masks & ABAC","hook":"SELECT decides whether you can query a table. Fine-grained policies decide which rows and which values you see.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Row-level security.** Table `sales(country, revenue)` has GR, DE, FR rows. You want: Greek team → only GR rows; German team → only DE rows."},{"t":"p","text":"A **row filter** is a **SQL UDF applied at query time**: rows where it returns **false** are removed."},{"t":"diagram","text":"SELECT * FROM sales\n        │\n        ▼\n   row filter UDF\n        ├── TRUE  → row visible\n        └── FALSE → row removed","caption":"Row filter at query time"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE FUNCTION security.country_filter(country STRING)\nRETURN\n  CASE\n    WHEN is_account_group_member('global_sales') THEN TRUE\n    WHEN is_account_group_member('greece_sales') AND country = 'GR' THEN TRUE\n    ELSE FALSE\n  END;\n\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.gold.sales\nSET ROW FILTER security.country_filter\nON (country);","caption":"Row filter: UDF + ALTER TABLE … SET ROW FILTER … ON (col)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Design production UDFs carefully","text":"The pattern above is the basic mechanism; production filter functions need careful design (every group covered, default FALSE, tested)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Column mask.** Table `customer_id, name, email, salary`: analysts need the rows but not the real salary. A mask is a **query-time policy on a column value**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"stored value:            5000\nquery as finance_admin:  5000\nquery as analyst:        ****   (or NULL / bucketed)","caption":"Same stored value, different visible value"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE FUNCTION security.mask_salary(salary DECIMAL(10,2))\nRETURN CASE\n  WHEN is_account_group_member('finance_admins') THEN salary\n  ELSE NULL\nEND;\n\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.gold.employees\nALTER COLUMN salary SET MASK security.mask_salary;","caption":"Column mask: UDF + ALTER COLUMN … SET MASK"},{"t":"p","text":"**ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control).** For 5 tables you can attach filters/masks one by one. For **10,000 tables** that doesn't scale. ABAC uses **governed tags** (e.g. `sensitivity = pii`) plus **policies** (e.g. if a column is tagged pii → mask it for users without privilege)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.silver.customers\nALTER COLUMN email SET TAGS ('sensitivity' = 'pii');\n-- an ABAC policy then masks every column tagged pii","caption":"Tag once; the policy follows the tag"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks now recommends ABAC for **centralized, tag-driven policies** across many objects. ABAC can apply **row filters, column masks**, and now **policy-based GRANT/DENY** capabilities in supported scopes."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Table-level mask / filter","points":["manually attach to table A","manually attach to table B …","good for per-table logic"]},{"title":"ABAC","points":["tag: PII","policy: mask PII","new tagged table tomorrow → automatically covered","consistent across many tables"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging Case 6**: \"I have SELECT, why do I see `****`?\" Because **SELECT ≠ unfiltered raw visibility**. Fine-grained policies change row/column visibility. Check **column mask, row filter, ABAC**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"SELECT ≠ seeing everything","text":"Missing rows or masked values with a successful query is not a permission bug in the grants. It is a **row filter / column mask / ABAC** policy doing its job."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a query succeeds but rows are missing or values are masked","questions":["Does this table have a row filter (which function, on which column)?","Does this column have a mask?","Is an ABAC policy matching a tag on this table/column?","Which groups am I in — does the policy function check a group I'm not in (is_account_group_member)?","Is the masking intended for my role, or is my group membership wrong?"]}]},{"id":"ch11-s13","title":"Lineage, Catalog Explorer, Federation & Delta Sharing","hook":"Lineage shows who depends on what before you change anything. Federation and Delta Sharing reach data beyond your own lakehouse.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"bronze.orders\n     ↓\nsilver.orders\n     ↓\ngold.daily_revenue\n     ↓\n  dashboard","caption":"UC captures upstream / downstream relationships"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks collects **lineage automatically** for queries run on Databricks, **down to column level**, aggregated **across workspaces that share the same metastore**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why it matters — impact analysis.** You want to change `silver.orders.customer_id`. A blind `ALTER …` could break `gold.customer_360`, a dashboard, an ML feature, a report. Lineage shows `customer_id → gold.customer_360 → executive_dashboard` **before** you change it."},{"t":"p","text":"**Debugging with lineage.** The dashboard shows wrong revenue. Walk **upstream**: dashboard ↑ gold.daily_revenue ↑ silver.orders ↑ bronze.orders, and ask **\"in which layer did the wrong value first appear?\"** — the layer-by-layer debugging you already know."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a dashboard number is wrong","questions":["What is the dashboard's direct upstream table (lineage)?","Is the value already wrong in gold.daily_revenue?","Is it wrong in silver.orders?","Is it wrong in bronze.orders (i.e. at ingestion)?","So in which layer did the wrong value FIRST appear — and what transformation sits just before it?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Catalog Explorer** is the UC UI in the workspace. It is a **governance UI**, not just a file browser."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Catalog Explorer\n└── ecommerce\n    ├── bronze\n    │   └── orders_raw\n    ├── silver\n    │   └── orders\n    └── gold\n        └── daily_sales\n\nper object (if permitted): schema · details · permissions\n  · history · lineage · tags · dependencies","caption":"Catalog Explorer"},{"t":"p","text":"**Lakehouse Federation** — query data where it lives, **without full ingestion**. External databases (PostgreSQL, Snowflake, SQL Server …) are exposed under UC governance: **connection → foreign catalog → external database metadata**."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- sketch; connector options vary (deep dive later)\nCREATE CONNECTION pg_sales TYPE postgresql\nOPTIONS (host 'pg.company.com', port '5432',\n         user secret('pg', 'user'), password secret('pg', 'pwd'));\n\nCREATE FOREIGN CATALOG pg_sales_cat\nUSING CONNECTION pg_sales\nOPTIONS (database 'sales');","caption":"Connection → foreign catalog"},{"t":"p","text":"`CREATE CONNECTION` / `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` privileges are part of the UC privilege model."},{"t":"p","text":"**Delta Sharing** — the opposite-ish use case: share governed data **outward** with another organization / recipient. It is an **open sharing protocol** integrated with UC: your UC tables → **share** → **recipient**, without necessarily copying files by hand."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- sketch; deep dive in cross-organization sharing\nCREATE SHARE ecommerce_share;\nALTER SHARE ecommerce_share ADD TABLE prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue;\nCREATE RECIPIENT partner_co;\nGRANT SELECT ON SHARE ecommerce_share TO RECIPIENT partner_co;","caption":"Tables → share → recipient"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Lakehouse Federation","points":["query data where it lives ELSEWHERE","connection → foreign catalog","pull-style: you read external DBs"]},{"title":"Delta Sharing","points":["safely expose / share data OUTWARD","share → recipient","push-style: others read your data"]}]}]},{"id":"ch11-s14","title":"Production Architecture, Grants & Hands-on Labs","hook":"Everything comes together in one e-commerce lakehouse: the catalog layout, the groups, the service principals and least-privilege grants, then built for real in SQL.","blocks":[{"t":"diagram","text":"CATALOG prod_ecommerce\n├── bronze\n│   ├── orders_raw\n│   ├── customers_raw\n│   └── ingest_files      [volume]\n├── silver\n│   ├── orders\n│   ├── customers\n│   └── products\n└── gold\n    ├── daily_revenue\n    ├── customer_360\n    └── product_performance","caption":"Production e-commerce catalog"},{"t":"table","head":["Groups","Service principals"],"rows":[["`data_engineers`","`orders_ingest_sp`"],["`analysts`","`silver_transform_sp`"],["`finance_analysts`","`gold_pipeline_sp`"],["`platform_admins`","—"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- engineers\nGRANT USE CATALOG    ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce        TO `data_engineers`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA     ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.silver TO `data_engineers`;\nGRANT SELECT, MODIFY ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.silver TO `data_engineers`;","caption":"Engineer grants"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Even tighter: writes for service principals only","text":"A production architecture may reserve **direct MODIFY for service principals** — Databricks explicitly recommends this as a best practice. Humans read; pipelines write."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- analysts: Gold only\nGRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce      TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT      ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;","caption":"Analysts can query Gold — but not Silver (no grants there)"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"-- gold pipeline: read Silver, write Gold\nGRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce        TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.silver TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT SELECT      ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.silver TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.gold   TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT MODIFY      ON SCHEMA  prod_ecommerce.gold   TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;","caption":"read Silver + write Gold = least privilege"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"What a real gold pipeline may also need","text":"MODIFY covers writing data. A pipeline that **creates new tables** in gold also needs `CREATE TABLE` on the schema, and a MERGE that reads its target needs `SELECT` on gold too. Grant these only when the job really does them."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Common bad practice","text":"`GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce TO all_company_users;` — far more than anyone needs, and it grows over time because ALL PRIVILEGES is evaluated dynamically."},{"t":"table","head":["Item","Value"],"rows":[["Metastore","Europe"],["Catalog","`prod_ecommerce`"],["Managed storage","`cloud://company-uc/prod-ecommerce/`"],["Schemas","`bronze`, `silver`, `gold`, `raw_files`"],["Tables","`prod_ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`, `prod_ecommerce.silver.orders`, `prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue`"],["Volume","`prod_ecommerce.raw_files.incoming`"],["Service principals","`orders_ingest_sp`, `transform_sp`"]],"caption":"Full mini-project configuration"},{"t":"p","text":"**Practical SQL lab** — build a dev copy and inspect it."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE CATALOG IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev;\n\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev.bronze;\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev.silver;\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev.gold;\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev.files;\n\nCREATE TABLE ecommerce_dev.silver.orders (\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      DECIMAL(10,2),\n  country     STRING\n);\n\nCREATE VOLUME ecommerce_dev.files.incoming;","caption":"Lab 1 — create objects"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SHOW CATALOGS;\nSHOW SCHEMAS IN ecommerce_dev;\nSHOW TABLES  IN ecommerce_dev.silver;\nDESCRIBE TABLE ecommerce_dev.silver.orders;","caption":"Lab 2 — inspect objects (basic UC navigation)"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG ecommerce_dev      TO `training_analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA  ecommerce_dev.gold TO `training_analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT      ON SCHEMA  ecommerce_dev.gold TO `training_analysts`;\n\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA ecommerce_dev.gold;","caption":"Lab 3 — grant practice"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA ecommerce_dev.files TO `data_engineers`;\nGRANT READ VOLUME, WRITE VOLUME\nON VOLUME ecommerce_dev.files.incoming\nTO `data_engineers`;\n-- (data_engineers also need USE CATALOG on ecommerce_dev)","caption":"Lab 4 — volume practice (grants)"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"path = \"/Volumes/ecommerce_dev/files/incoming/test.txt\"\nwith open(path, \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"hello unity catalog\")\n\nwith open(path, \"r\") as f:\n    print(f.read())","caption":"Lab 4 — write then read a file in the volume"},{"t":"p","text":"**Phase 3 production exercise.** Build `ecommerce_dev` with `bronze`, `silver`, `gold`, `files` (in your head or a sandbox) and run the whole flow:"},{"t":"flow","items":["CREATE CATALOG","CREATE SCHEMA","CREATE TABLE","CREATE VOLUME","GRANT USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA","GRANT SELECT / MODIFY","GRANT READ / WRITE VOLUME","SHOW GRANTS","REVOKE"],"caption":"The full UC flow"},{"t":"p","text":"Then imagine two roles — **analyst** and **pipeline service principal** — and decide who may: read Gold, write Gold, read Silver, create table, write volume, manage grants."}]},{"id":"ch11-s15","title":"The PERMISSION_DENIED Algorithm & the Six Cases","hook":"The main outcome of Phase 3: stop thinking \"I don't have SELECT\" and walk the whole authorization chain.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT * FROM prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue;\n-- PERMISSION_DENIED"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Don't ask for admin","text":"\"Give me admin\" fixes the symptom, breaks least privilege, and teaches you nothing. **Debug by layers.**"},{"t":"diagram","text":"1. Which principal is ACTUALLY executing?        (Identity)\n        ↓\n2. Right workspace / catalog binding?            (Binding)\n        ↓\n3. USE CATALOG?                                  (Catalog)\n        ↓\n4. USE SCHEMA?                                   (Schema)\n        ↓\n5. SELECT (object privilege)?                    (Object)\n        ↓\n6. Row filter / column mask / ABAC?              (Fine-grained)\n        ↓\n7. External-storage permissions, if relevant?    (Storage)\n        ↓\n8. Compute compatible with Unity Catalog?        (Compute)","caption":"The production-grade permission-debugging model"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Mnemonic: I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions","text":"**I**dentity · **B**inding · **C**atalog · **S**chema · **O**bject · **F**ine-grained · **S**torage · **C**ompute."},{"t":"table","head":["Case","Symptom","Layer","Check"],"rows":[["1 Wrong identity","notebook works, job fails","Identity","job run-as (service principal) grants"],["2 SELECT, no USE SCHEMA","denied although SELECT exists","Schema","USE SCHEMA on parent schema"],["3 Revoked but still readable","REVOKE had no effect","Object (inheritance)","SELECT on schema / catalog"],["4 Managed OK, external fails","storage access error","Storage (+ Binding)","external location, credential, binding, cloud IAM"],["5 Volume file fails","open('/Volumes/…') fails","Object (volume)","READ VOLUME (+ WRITE VOLUME)"],["6 Sees ****","query OK, values masked","Fine-grained","column mask / row filter / ABAC"]],"caption":"The six debugging cases on the algorithm"},{"t":"p","text":"**Case 1 — Wrong identity.** You (Nikos) have SELECT ✅ in the notebook. The job runs as `orders-etl-sp`, which has **no** SELECT ❌. The job fails."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Run button ≠ workload identity","text":"The identity that presses **Run** is not necessarily the identity the workload **uses**. A job may execute as a service principal with different UC privileges."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself on every PERMISSION_DENIED (I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions)","questions":["Which principal is actually executing — me, a job run-as, a pipeline service principal?","Am I in a workspace bound to this catalog / external location?","Does that principal have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Does it have USE SCHEMA on the schema?","Does it have the object privilege (SELECT / MODIFY / READ VOLUME …), directly or inherited?","Is a row filter, column mask or ABAC policy changing what it sees?","For external data: are external location, storage credential and cloud IAM OK?","Is the compute Unity Catalog-compatible?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The Phase-3 outcome","text":"Never again think of a permission error as just \"I don't have SELECT\". Think of the **whole authorization chain**."}]},{"id":"ch11-s16","title":"Confusable Pairs & the Phase-3 Big Test","hook":"Rapid-fire recall over the whole chapter: these are the questions interviews and certifications ask.","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Pair","First","Second"],"rows":[["Managed vs external DROP","UC handles metadata + file lifecycle","metadata removed, files remain"],["Storage credential vs external location","HOW Databricks authenticates (cloud identity)","WHICH path + which credential (securable)"],["External location vs external table","governed storage path","table metadata pointing at data inside that path"],["Metastore vs catalog","top regional governance container","primary logical/data isolation container inside it"],["USE SCHEMA vs SELECT","traverse the namespace","read the data"],["Table vs volume","rows/columns, SELECT / MODIFY","files, READ VOLUME / WRITE VOLUME"],["Federation vs Delta Sharing","query data where it lives elsewhere","share data outward"]],"caption":"Confusable pairs — know both sides"},{"t":"p","text":"**Big test — Phase 3.** Every question of the source test is an exercise below (Q23–Q27 are missing in the source export)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Classic traps in one list","text":"USE ≠ SELECT · ALL PRIVILEGES excludes MANAGE / EXTERNAL USE … · REVOKE on table ≠ access gone · DROP external keeps files · workspace ≠ namespace level · SELECT ≠ unfiltered · permissions + wrong compute = failure."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: one sentence that sums up Phase 3?","text":"Don't think of a permission error as \"I don't have SELECT\" — think of the whole authorization chain: **I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions**."}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch11-d01","title":"PERMISSION_DENIED — the 8-layer algorithm","section":"ch11-s15","symptom":"`PERMISSION_DENIED` (or INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES) on a Unity Catalog object, e.g. `SELECT * FROM prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue`.","askYourself":["Which principal is ACTUALLY executing — me, a job run-as, a pipeline service principal?","Am I in a workspace that is bound to this catalog / external location / credential?","Does that principal have USE CATALOG on the catalog?","Does it have USE SCHEMA on the exact parent schema?","Does it have the object privilege (SELECT / MODIFY / READ VOLUME …), directly or inherited from schema/catalog?","Is a row filter, column mask or ABAC policy changing what it sees?","For external data: are the external location, storage credential and cloud IAM OK?","Is the compute Unity Catalog-compatible (Standard/Dedicated, serverless, SQL warehouse)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Identify the executor.","why":"Every grant is evaluated for the executing principal.","code":"SELECT current_user();  -- interactive\n-- jobs/pipelines: check the run-as identity in settings"},{"do":"Check the workspace binding of the catalog / external location.","why":"A binding can block access despite perfect grants."},{"do":"Show grants top-down for that principal.","why":"USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → object privilege; inheritance included.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce;\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold;\nSHOW GRANTS ON TABLE prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue;"},{"do":"If the query succeeds but rows/values look wrong, inspect filters, masks and ABAC.","why":"SELECT ≠ unfiltered raw visibility."},{"do":"For external tables/volumes, follow the storage chain.","why":"External location → storage credential → cloud IAM.","code":"DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED prod_ecommerce.silver.legacy_orders;"},{"do":"Confirm the compute is UC-compatible.","why":"Correct permissions + wrong legacy compute = still failure."}],"rootCauses":["Job runs as a different service principal","Workspace not bound to the catalog","Missing USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA","Missing object privilege (or wrong level)","Fine-grained policy hides rows/values","External location / credential / IAM problem","Non-UC-compatible compute"],"fix":"Fix the exact missing layer for the exact principal (usually a group or service principal) — never \"give me admin\".","mnemonic":"I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions — Identity · Binding · Catalog · Schema · Object · Fine-grained · Storage · Compute"},{"id":"ch11-d02","title":"Case 1 — notebook works, job fails (wrong identity)","section":"ch11-s15","symptom":"Your notebook reads the table; the scheduled job running the same code fails with PERMISSION_DENIED.","askYourself":["Is the job running as a different identity than me?","Which service principal is the run-as identity?","Does that service principal have USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and the object privilege?","Was the run-as recently changed?","Am I tempted to make it run as my personal user — and why is that wrong for production?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open the job settings and read the run-as identity.","why":"The identity that presses Run is not necessarily the workload identity."},{"do":"Show grants for that service principal down the hierarchy.","why":"Find the exact missing privilege.","code":"SHOW GRANTS `orders-etl-sp` ON SCHEMA prod.silver;"},{"do":"Grant only what the job needs to the service principal.","why":"Least privilege for machine identities.","code":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod.silver.orders TO `orders-etl-sp`;"}],"rootCauses":["Job runs as `orders-etl-sp`, which lacks SELECT","Run-as changed to an under-privileged identity"],"fix":"Grant the missing privilege(s) to the job's service principal; keep production on service principals, not personal users.","mnemonic":"Run button ≠ run-as"},{"id":"ch11-d03","title":"Case 2 — SELECT exists but USE SCHEMA doesn't","section":"ch11-s05","symptom":"User has SELECT on `prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue` (and USE CATALOG) but the query is denied.","askYourself":["Do I have USE CATALOG on the parent catalog?","Do I have USE SCHEMA on this exact schema — not on a sibling like silver?","Is the SELECT on this object or inherited?","Am I the identity that holds these grants?"],"steps":[{"do":"Show grants on the schema.","why":"USE SCHEMA is a prerequisite for data access.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold;"},{"do":"Grant USE SCHEMA to the group.","why":"Adds only the missing prerequisite.","code":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;"}],"rootCauses":["USE SCHEMA missing","USE SCHEMA granted on the wrong schema"],"fix":"Grant USE SCHEMA (and USE CATALOG if missing) — SELECT alone can't traverse the namespace.","mnemonic":"Three keys: building, floor, room"},{"id":"ch11-d04","title":"Case 3 — REVOKE done, access remains","section":"ch11-s06","symptom":"Admin ran `REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue FROM analysts`, yet analysts still read the table.","askYourself":["Did I revoke from the group the user actually reads through?","Is there SELECT on the parent schema that the table inherits?","Is there SELECT or ALL PRIVILEGES on the parent catalog?","Is the user in another group with a grant?","Is the user owner or MANAGE holder (able to re-grant)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Show grants at schema and catalog level.","why":"Inherited grants survive a table-level revoke.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold;\nSHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce;"},{"do":"Restructure: move SELECT from schema to the specific tables (or move the sensitive table to another schema).","why":"You can't block an inherited grant by revoking lower down."}],"rootCauses":["SELECT ON SCHEMA gold","Catalog-level inherited grant","Another group membership"],"fix":"Find the inherited grant and restructure where SELECT is granted; always inspect the hierarchy.","mnemonic":"Grants flow down — look up"},{"id":"ch11-d05","title":"Case 4 — managed table works, external table fails","section":"ch11-s09","symptom":"Managed tables work; an external table returns PERMISSION_DENIED or a storage access error.","askYourself":["Do the UC grants work (does a managed table in the same schema work)?","What is the table's LOCATION?","Is that path under an external location — and do I have privileges on it?","Is the external location / storage credential bound to this workspace?","Does the cloud IAM identity behind the storage credential allow this path?"],"steps":[{"do":"Get the location.","why":"Know which path you're dealing with.","code":"DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED prod.silver.legacy_orders;"},{"do":"Check external locations covering it.","why":"No governed path → no access.","code":"SHOW EXTERNAL LOCATIONS;"},{"do":"Check bindings and the storage credential.","why":"Bindings also apply to external locations and credentials.","code":"DESCRIBE EXTERNAL LOCATION legacy_loc;"},{"do":"Escalate to the cloud team for IAM.","why":"UC can't grant more than the cloud identity is allowed."}],"rootCauses":["No external location for the path","Missing privilege on the external location","Workspace binding","Cloud IAM policy changed"],"fix":"Repair the failing storage layer (external location, credential, binding or cloud IAM) — never hand out raw cloud keys.","mnemonic":"External = extra layers: Location · Credential · Binding · IAM"},{"id":"ch11-d06","title":"Case 5 — /Volumes file inaccessible","section":"ch11-s10","symptom":"`open(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/file.json\")` fails although the user has USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA.","askYourself":["Is the path /Volumes/<catalog>/<schema>/<volume>/… in the right order?","Do I have READ VOLUME on this volume?","For writes, do I also have WRITE VOLUME?","Am I using SELECT/MODIFY by mistake (table privileges)?","If it's an external volume, is the storage chain OK?"],"steps":[{"do":"Verify the path.","why":"Swapped names point to a non-existent volume."},{"do":"Grant READ VOLUME (and WRITE VOLUME for writes).","why":"Volume privileges are separate from table privileges.","code":"GRANT READ VOLUME, WRITE VOLUME ON VOLUME ecommerce.raw.documents TO `data_engineers`;"}],"rootCauses":["READ VOLUME missing","WRITE VOLUME missing for writes","Wrong path","External storage chain broken"],"fix":"Grant READ VOLUME (plus WRITE VOLUME for writes) on the volume.","mnemonic":"Files ≠ rows: READ/WRITE VOLUME"},{"id":"ch11-d07","title":"Case 6 — \"I have SELECT, why do I see ****?\"","section":"ch11-s12","symptom":"Query succeeds, but salary shows `****` / NULL, or rows are missing.","askYourself":["Did the query succeed (so grants are fine)?","Is there a column mask on this column?","Is there a row filter on this table?","Does an ABAC policy match a tag on this table/column?","Which groups am I in — does the policy check a group I'm not in?"],"steps":[{"do":"Inspect the table definition for filters/masks.","why":"Masks and filters are attached to the table/column.","code":"DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED ecommerce.gold.employees;"},{"do":"Check ABAC policies and tags in Catalog Explorer.","why":"Tag-driven policies apply without per-table attachment."},{"do":"Decide: intended? If access is approved, change group membership — don't drop the policy.","why":"The policy is doing its job."}],"rootCauses":["Column mask","Row filter","ABAC policy on tagged data","User not in the privileged group"],"fix":"Explain that SELECT ≠ unfiltered visibility; adjust group membership only if the business approves.","mnemonic":"SELECT opens the door, policies dim the lights"},{"id":"ch11-d08","title":"Grants correct, still denied — workspace binding","section":"ch11-s11","symptom":"User has USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT, but the query fails in one workspace and works in another.","askYourself":["Which workspace am I in?","Is the catalog bound only to specific workspaces?","Are the external location / storage credential bound to this workspace?","Should this workspace have access at all (isolation by design)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the same query from another workspace.","why":"Same principal + same grants + different result → binding."},{"do":"Check the catalog's workspace bindings in Catalog Explorer.","why":"Bindings are a separate access-control mechanism."},{"do":"Use the bound workspace, or request a binding change if intended.","why":"Grants can't override a missing binding."}],"rootCauses":["Catalog not bound to this workspace","External location / credential not bound"],"fix":"Run from a bound workspace or have an admin add the binding — if the isolation design allows it.","mnemonic":"User privilege ≠ only boundary"},{"id":"ch11-d09","title":"Grants correct, still denied — compute not UC-compatible","section":"ch11-s11","symptom":"Same user and grants work on a SQL warehouse but fail on an old cluster.","askYourself":["Does the same query work on a SQL warehouse or serverless?","What is this cluster's access mode?","Is it Standard or Dedicated — or a legacy configuration?","Did someone reuse an old cluster definition?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare on serverless / SQL warehouse.","why":"Isolates the compute layer."},{"do":"Check the access mode in the cluster config.","why":"Standard/Dedicated are UC-capable."},{"do":"Switch to UC-compatible compute.","why":"Permissions correct + wrong legacy compute = still failure."}],"rootCauses":["Legacy / non-UC access mode","Old cluster definition reused"],"fix":"Use Standard/Dedicated classic compute, serverless, or a SQL warehouse.","mnemonic":"Right grants, wrong engine"},{"id":"ch11-d10","title":"DROP TABLE surprise: data gone / storage not freed","section":"ch11-s08","symptom":"After DROP TABLE either the data disappeared from storage (unexpected) or the storage bill didn't drop (unexpected).","askYourself":["Was the table MANAGED or EXTERNAL?","If managed: did I know UC deletes the files as part of the lifecycle?","If external: did I expect the files to stay?","Do other systems still read those files?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the type and location before dropping.","why":"DROP semantics depend on the type.","code":"DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED ecommerce.silver.orders_ext;"},{"do":"For external tables, delete unwanted files with storage tooling (or re-register with CREATE TABLE … LOCATION).","why":"UC removed only the metadata."}],"rootCauses":["Managed table: UC handles file deletion","External table: files remain"],"fix":"Know managed vs external DROP semantics; check the type before every DROP.","mnemonic":"Managed dies whole; external leaves its body"},{"id":"ch11-d11","title":"CREATE CATALOG / CREATE SCHEMA (… MANAGED LOCATION) fails","section":"ch11-s03","symptom":"`CREATE SCHEMA ecommerce.gold` or `CREATE CATALOG ecommerce MANAGED LOCATION 's3://…'` fails with a permission error.","askYourself":["Who is actually running the statement?","Catalog: do I have CREATE CATALOG on the metastore?","Schema: do I have BOTH USE CATALOG and CREATE SCHEMA on the parent catalog?","Managed location: is the path under a UC external location?","Managed location: do I have CREATE MANAGED STORAGE on that external location?"],"steps":[{"do":"Show grants on the metastore / parent catalog.","why":"Creation privileges are granted on the parent.","code":"SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG ecommerce;"},{"do":"For MANAGED LOCATION, list external locations and check the path is under one.","why":"Managed locations must sit under an external location.","code":"SHOW EXTERNAL LOCATIONS;"},{"do":"Grant the missing privilege to the right group.","why":"Least privilege.","code":"GRANT USE CATALOG, CREATE SCHEMA ON CATALOG ecommerce TO `data_engineers`;"}],"rootCauses":["Missing CREATE CATALOG","Missing USE CATALOG or CREATE SCHEMA","Path not under an external location","Missing CREATE MANAGED STORAGE"],"fix":"Grant the creation privilege on the parent (plus USE prerequisites); for managed locations, use a path under an external location and CREATE MANAGED STORAGE.","mnemonic":"Create on the parent"},{"id":"ch11-d12","title":"Dashboard shows wrong revenue — debug with lineage","section":"ch11-s13","symptom":"The executive dashboard shows wrong revenue numbers.","askYourself":["What is the dashboard's upstream table (lineage)?","Is the value already wrong in gold.daily_revenue?","Is it wrong in silver.orders?","Is it wrong in bronze.orders?","In which layer did the wrong value FIRST appear — and which transformation feeds it?"],"steps":[{"do":"Open lineage for the dashboard in Catalog Explorer.","why":"Lineage gives the path upstream."},{"do":"Query each layer for the same day/key.","why":"Find the first wrong layer.","code":"SELECT SUM(amount) FROM prod_ecommerce.silver.orders WHERE order_date = '2026-01-09';"},{"do":"Fix the transformation into the first wrong layer, then rerun downstream.","why":"The bug lives just before the first wrong layer."}],"rootCauses":["Bad join/duplicates in silver→gold","Bad cleaning in bronze→silver","Ingestion issue in bronze"],"fix":"Walk lineage upstream to the first wrong layer and fix that transformation.","mnemonic":"Walk upstream until it's right"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"Thinking Unity Catalog stores your data","text":"UC holds metadata, permissions, lineage and storage references; the bytes are in S3/ADLS/GCS.","fix":"Separate governance (UC) from storage (cloud) in every explanation and debug."},{"title":"Putting the workspace in the namespace","text":"A notebook lives in a workspace; tables are registered in the metastore. `catalog.schema.object` has no workspace level.","fix":"Remember: workspace = operational environment; metastore = regional governance container."},{"title":"One metastore per project","text":"Metastores are regional (usually one per region). Isolation starts at the catalog level.","fix":"Create a new catalog, not a new metastore."},{"title":"CREATE SCHEMA with only CREATE SCHEMA","text":"Schema creation also needs USE CATALOG on the parent catalog.","fix":"Grant USE CATALOG + CREATE SCHEMA on the parent catalog."},{"title":"USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA mistaken for data access","text":"USE privileges are traversal prerequisites; they never grant SELECT.","fix":"Read path = USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT."},{"title":"SELECT without USE SCHEMA","text":"SELECT on a table is useless if the parent schema can't be traversed.","fix":"Grant USE SCHEMA on the exact parent schema."},{"title":"REVOKE on a table assumed to remove access","text":"Inherited SELECT on the schema or catalog keeps access alive.","fix":"SHOW GRANTS on schema and catalog; restructure grants."},{"title":"REVOKE … TO","text":"REVOKE uses FROM, GRANT uses TO.","fix":"REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE t FROM `group`;"},{"title":"ALL PRIVILEGES assumed to be everything","text":"MANAGE, EXTERNAL USE LOCATION and EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA are excluded.","fix":"Grant MANAGE explicitly where delegated governance is intended."},{"title":"GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES to all users","text":"Violates least privilege, and ALL PRIVILEGES grows dynamically as new privileges appear.","fix":"Grant minimal, specific privileges to groups; be restrained with ALL PRIVILEGES and MANAGE."},{"title":"Personal owners and personal run-as in production","text":"When the person leaves, ownership/jobs break.","fix":"Groups own production objects; service principals run pipelines."},{"title":"BROWSE confused with SELECT","text":"BROWSE only reveals that objects exist (metadata).","fix":"Use BROWSE for discovery; grant SELECT only after an access request."},{"title":"Managed = inside Databricks servers","text":"Both managed and external data can live in your cloud storage; the difference is who controls location and lifecycle.","fix":"Ask \"who controls the files' location and lifecycle?\""},{"title":"Dropping an external table to free storage","text":"DROP on an external table removes metadata only; files remain.","fix":"Delete files with storage tooling if truly unwanted; check type before DROP."},{"title":"Dropping a managed table you still need","text":"For managed tables UC handles deletion of the underlying files.","fix":"DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED before DROP; know the type."},{"title":"MANAGED LOCATION on an ungoverned path","text":"Managed locations must be under a UC external location and need CREATE MANAGED STORAGE.","fix":"Create/choose an external location first; grant CREATE MANAGED STORAGE."},{"title":"Granting on the storage credential instead of the external location","text":"Credentials can reach a huge storage scope.","fix":"Grant CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE etc. on the external location (path-scoped)."},{"title":"External location vs external table confusion","text":"Location = governed path; table = metadata pointing at data in that path.","fix":"Keep the chain: credential → location → table/volume → files."},{"title":"Using SELECT for volumes","text":"Volumes use READ VOLUME / WRITE VOLUME.","fix":"Grant USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + READ VOLUME (+ WRITE VOLUME)."},{"title":"Ignoring workspace bindings","text":"A catalog/location/credential may be bound to specific workspaces; grants can't override that.","fix":"Check bindings when grants look right but access fails in one workspace."},{"title":"Correct grants on non-UC compute","text":"Legacy compute fails even with perfect permissions.","fix":"Use Standard/Dedicated, serverless or SQL warehouses."},{"title":"SELECT expected to show raw values","text":"Row filters, column masks and ABAC change visibility at query time.","fix":"Check filters/masks/ABAC and group membership."},{"title":"Row filter defaulting to TRUE","text":"ELSE TRUE shows every row to unmatched users.","fix":"Deny by default: ELSE FALSE."},{"title":"Per-table masks for thousands of tables","text":"Manual attachment doesn't scale and misses new tables.","fix":"Use ABAC: governed tags + central policy."},{"title":"Assuming the Run-button identity is the workload identity","text":"Jobs/pipelines may run as a service principal.","fix":"Check run-as first (layer 1 of the algorithm)."},{"title":"Asking for admin on PERMISSION_DENIED","text":"Fixes the symptom, breaks least privilege, teaches nothing.","fix":"Walk I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"The four questions of the Phase-3 mental model?","a":"WHO? → WHAT OBJECT? → WHAT ACTION? → WHERE ARE THE BYTES?","section":"ch11-s01"},{"q":"What is fragmented governance?","a":"Permissions scattered across storage, notebooks, clusters and BI tools — no single answer to \"who has what?\".","section":"ch11-s01"},{"q":"What does Unity Catalog hold?","a":"Metadata + namespace + permissions + ownership + lineage + governance policies + storage references — not the bytes.","section":"ch11-s01"},{"q":"Top-level Unity Catalog object?","a":"The metastore.","section":"ch11-s02"},{"q":"How many metastores per region, typically?","a":"One; many workspaces in the region attach to it.","section":"ch11-s02"},{"q":"Workspace vs metastore?","a":"Workspace = operational development environment. Metastore = regional UC governance container.","section":"ch11-s02"},{"q":"Three conditions for two workspaces to see the same object?","a":"Same metastore + principal has permission + no workspace binding forbids it.","section":"ch11-s02"},{"q":"Metastore vs catalog?","a":"Metastore = top regional governance container. Catalog = primary logical/data isolation container inside it.","section":"ch11-s02"},{"q":"Primary unit of data isolation?","a":"The catalog.","section":"ch11-s03"},{"q":"What can a schema contain?","a":"Tables, views, volumes, models, functions (and materialized views, streaming tables …).","section":"ch11-s03"},{"q":"Privileges to create a schema?","a":"USE CATALOG + CREATE SCHEMA on the parent catalog.","section":"ch11-s03"},{"q":"Privilege to create a catalog?","a":"CREATE CATALOG on the metastore.","section":"ch11-s03"},{"q":"Securable object?","a":"A UC object on which privileges can be granted to principals (catalog, schema, table, volume, external location, storage credential, function …).","section":"ch11-s04"},{"q":"Three kinds of principal?","a":"User, group, service principal.","section":"ch11-s04"},{"q":"Where should principals be provisioned?","a":"At the account level; prefer groups over individual users.","section":"ch11-s04"},{"q":"Why service principals for production?","a":"Stable machine identity decoupled from employee lifecycle.","section":"ch11-s04"},{"q":"Does USE CATALOG give SELECT?","a":"No — USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA are prerequisites only.","section":"ch11-s05"},{"q":"Minimum grants to read one table?","a":"USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT (on table or inherited).","section":"ch11-s05"},{"q":"Case 2 cause?","a":"SELECT exists but USE SCHEMA is missing → can't traverse the schema.","section":"ch11-s05"},{"q":"What does GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA do to new tables?","a":"They inherit it — inheritance covers current and future child objects.","section":"ch11-s06"},{"q":"SELECT vs MODIFY?","a":"SELECT reads; MODIFY writes/changes data (broad DML). Finer DML like INSERT exists in beta.","section":"ch11-s06"},{"q":"Command to answer \"who has what?\"","a":"SHOW GRANTS ON <TABLE|SCHEMA|CATALOG> <name>;","section":"ch11-s06"},{"q":"REVOKE grammar?","a":"REVOKE <privilege> ON <type> <name> FROM <principal>;","section":"ch11-s06"},{"q":"Case 3: REVOKE on table but user still reads — why?","a":"Inherited SELECT on the schema or catalog.","section":"ch11-s06"},{"q":"What can an owner do?","a":"Manage privileges, modify/manage the object, transfer ownership.","section":"ch11-s07"},{"q":"Who should own production objects?","a":"Groups (e.g. data_platform_admins), not individuals.","section":"ch11-s07"},{"q":"What is MANAGE?","a":"Delegated governance: manage grants/ownership-related operations without being owner.","section":"ch11-s07"},{"q":"What is excluded from ALL PRIVILEGES?","a":"MANAGE, EXTERNAL USE LOCATION, EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA.","section":"ch11-s07"},{"q":"Why is ALL PRIVILEGES risky over time?","a":"It's evaluated dynamically — new applicable privileges are added automatically.","section":"ch11-s07"},{"q":"What does BROWSE allow?","a":"Discovering that objects exist (metadata) without data access.","section":"ch11-s07"},{"q":"Managed vs external — the real difference?","a":"Who controls the files' location and lifecycle (UC vs you/external system).","section":"ch11-s08"},{"q":"DROP managed table?","a":"UC handles metadata and deletion of underlying files.","section":"ch11-s08"},{"q":"DROP external table?","a":"Metadata removed; files remain.","section":"ch11-s08"},{"q":"When does an external table make sense?","a":"Existing data at known paths, non-Databricks lifecycle control, migration constraints, interoperability.","section":"ch11-s08"},{"q":"Managed storage location priority?","a":"Schema > catalog > metastore (most specific wins).","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"Requirements for MANAGED LOCATION?","a":"Path under a UC external location + CREATE MANAGED STORAGE.","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"Storage credential?","a":"UC object abstracting a long-term cloud identity (IAM role / managed identity / service account).","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"External location?","a":"Cloud path + storage credential, as a securable.","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"The full storage chain?","a":"Cloud IAM → storage credential → external location → external table/volume → files.","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"Why grant on external location, not credential?","a":"Path-scoped least privilege.","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"Case 4 extra layers?","a":"External location, storage credential, workspace binding, cloud IAM.","section":"ch11-s09"},{"q":"What is a volume?","a":"A UC-governed object for non-tabular/file data, accessed via /Volumes/catalog/schema/volume/…","section":"ch11-s10"},{"q":"Volume privileges?","a":"READ VOLUME, WRITE VOLUME (+ USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA).","section":"ch11-s10"},{"q":"Drop an external volume?","a":"Metadata removed; files remain.","section":"ch11-s10"},{"q":"What is a workspace binding?","a":"A restriction on which workspaces can use a catalog, external location or storage credential.","section":"ch11-s11"},{"q":"Which compute supports UC?","a":"Classic Standard/Dedicated, serverless, Databricks SQL.","section":"ch11-s11"},{"q":"Row filter?","a":"SQL UDF applied at query time; rows where it returns false are removed.","section":"ch11-s12"},{"q":"Syntax to attach a row filter?","a":"ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER f ON (col);","section":"ch11-s12"},{"q":"Syntax to attach a column mask?","a":"ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN c SET MASK f;","section":"ch11-s12"},{"q":"ABAC?","a":"Attribute-based access control: governed tags + central policies (filters, masks, GRANT/DENY) across many objects.","section":"ch11-s12"},{"q":"Case 6: SELECT but ****?","a":"SELECT ≠ unfiltered raw visibility — column mask / row filter / ABAC.","section":"ch11-s12"},{"q":"Lineage scope?","a":"Automatic, down to column level, across workspaces sharing the metastore.","section":"ch11-s13"},{"q":"Debugging with lineage — the key question?","a":"In which layer did the wrong value first appear?","section":"ch11-s13"},{"q":"Federation vs Delta Sharing?","a":"Federation: query data where it lives elsewhere (connection → foreign catalog). Delta Sharing: share data outward (share → recipient).","section":"ch11-s13"},{"q":"Gold pipeline SP grants?","a":"Read Silver (USE SCHEMA + SELECT) + write Gold (USE SCHEMA + MODIFY) + USE CATALOG.","section":"ch11-s14"},{"q":"Permission-debugging mnemonic?","a":"I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions — Identity, Binding, Catalog, Schema, Object, Fine-grained, Storage, Compute.","section":"ch11-s15"},{"q":"Why can a job fail while your notebook works?","a":"It may run as a service principal with different privileges.","section":"ch11-s15"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch11-e001","type":"order","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Put the four Unity Catalog questions in the order the mental model asks them.","items":["WHO? (user / group / service principal)","WHAT OBJECT? (metastore → catalog → schema → object)","WHAT ACTION? (USE / SELECT / MODIFY / CREATE / READ VOLUME)","WHERE ARE THE BYTES? (managed storage or external location)"],"explain":"The model goes from the **identity**, to the **object** in the hierarchy, to the **action** (privilege), and finally to the **physical bytes** in cloud storage. Most permission bugs sit in one of these four layers."},{"id":"ch11-e002","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Sort each item into the question of the mental model it answers.","buckets":["WHO?","WHAT OBJECT?","WHAT ACTION?","WHERE ARE THE BYTES?"],"items":[{"text":"`nikos@company.com`","bucket":0},{"text":"group `analysts`","bucket":0},{"text":"`orders-etl-prod` service principal","bucket":0},{"text":"`prod.sales.orders`","bucket":1},{"text":"volume `prod.raw.documents`","bucket":1},{"text":"`SELECT`","bucket":2},{"text":"`READ VOLUME`","bucket":2},{"text":"`s3://company/raw/` via an external location","bucket":3},{"text":"managed storage of the catalog","bucket":3}],"explain":"Principals answer **WHO**; securables in the hierarchy answer **WHAT OBJECT**; privileges answer **WHAT ACTION**; managed storage / external locations answer **WHERE ARE THE BYTES**. Mixing these layers up is the root of most UC confusion."},{"id":"ch11-e003","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Permissions for the same data live in S3 bucket policies, Azure Storage ACLs, notebook ACLs, cluster settings and the BI tool. What is this called?","options":["Fragmented governance","Least privilege","Data isolation","Workspace binding"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct — many systems, many permission models.","Least privilege is about giving minimal rights, not where rights live.","Data isolation is a design goal (often a catalog boundary).","Workspace binding is a UC feature restricting which workspaces see an object."],"explain":"When permissions are scattered across storage, notebooks, clusters and BI tools you have **fragmented governance** — no single place answers \"who has what?\". UC exists to make governance **central**. Least privilege is a principle, not this problem."},{"id":"ch11-e004","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which governance question can bucket-level storage permissions alone **never** answer?","options":["Which dashboard uses the column `orders.customer_id`?","Can this IAM role read `s3://company/finance/`?","Is this bucket encrypted?","Who can list the `sales/` prefix?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Storage permissions know about **paths and identities**, not about **tables, columns, pipelines or dashboards**. Questions like \"which dashboard uses this column?\" need **lineage**, which UC captures centrally."},{"id":"ch11-e005","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Unity Catalog stores the Parquet bytes of your Delta tables inside its own database.","answer":false,"explain":"False. UC holds **metadata, namespace, permissions, ownership, lineage, policies and storage references**. The actual data files live in **cloud object storage** (S3 / ADLS / GCS) — even for managed tables."},{"id":"ch11-e006","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which items does Unity Catalog hold? (choose all)","options":["Table metadata and the namespace","Permissions and ownership","Lineage","References to storage locations","The Parquet data files themselves"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"UC = metadata + namespace + permissions + ownership + lineage + governance policies + storage references. The **data files** stay in cloud storage; UC only points to (or manages) them."},{"id":"ch11-e007","type":"odd","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one doesn't belong?","options":["S3","ADLS","GCS","Unity Catalog"],"answer":3,"explain":"S3, ADLS and GCS are **cloud object stores** where the bytes live. Unity Catalog is the **governance layer** that points at them — it is not storage."},{"id":"ch11-e008","type":"free","section":"ch11-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain to a new colleague, in 3–4 sentences, why a company with 100 developers and data in S3 folders needs Unity Catalog.","model":"Without central governance, permissions live in many places (bucket policies, notebooks, clusters, BI tools), so nobody can answer who may read payroll, who owns a table, which pipeline created it or which dashboard uses a column. Unity Catalog adds one hierarchical governance layer over the storage: every asset is a securable object in a catalog.schema.object namespace, privileges go to users, groups and service principals, and lineage is captured automatically. The bytes stay in cloud storage — UC governs metadata, permissions and storage references.","rubric":["Fragmented governance across many tools","UC = one central, hierarchical governance layer","Securables + privileges + principals","Lineage / ownership questions answered","UC does not store the bytes"],"explain":"A strong answer names the problem (fragmented governance), the solution (central hierarchical layer with securables, privileges, principals) and the boundary (UC governs; storage keeps the bytes)."},{"id":"ch11-e009","type":"order","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Order these levels from top (broadest) to bottom.","items":["Metastore","Catalog (e.g. `prod`)","Schema (e.g. `silver`)","Object (e.g. table `orders`)"],"explain":"Metastore is the top-level container, then catalog (first name), schema (second name), object (third name). The workspace is **not** part of this chain."},{"id":"ch11-e010","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Databricks calls one level \"the primary unit of data isolation\". Which?","options":["Catalog","Schema","Metastore","Workspace"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Schema is finer-grained organization inside a catalog.","Usually one per region, shared by many workspaces — too broad.","Not part of the namespace."],"explain":"The **catalog** is the primary data-isolation unit (e.g. `dev` vs `prod`). The schema is a finer organizational/access layer; the metastore is regional and usually shared; the workspace is an operational environment, not a namespace level."},{"id":"ch11-e011","type":"odd","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A metastore is NOT three of these. Which option is the odd one out because it IS what a metastore is?","options":["a table","a storage bucket","a workspace","the top-level container for UC metadata and permissions"],"answer":3,"explain":"A metastore is the **top-level container for Unity Catalog metadata and permissions**. It is not a table, not a schema, not a storage bucket and not a workspace."},{"id":"ch11-e012","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which of these does the metastore hold metadata about? (choose all)","options":["Catalogs and tables","Volumes","External locations and storage credentials","Shares and permissions","Running clusters"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"The metastore tracks catalogs, tables, volumes, external locations, credentials, shares and permissions. **Clusters** are compute resources in a workspace, not metastore objects."},{"id":"ch11-e013","type":"calc","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A company has 3 workspaces in Europe and 2 workspaces in the US, using the typical pattern. How many Unity Catalog metastores do they usually have?","answer":2,"tolerance":0,"unit":"metastores","explain":"Typically **one metastore per region**, shared by all workspaces in that region: one for Europe, one for the US = **2**. You don't create one metastore per workspace or per project."},{"id":"ch11-e014","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Where does each thing belong?","buckets":["Lives in a WORKSPACE (operational)","Registered in the METASTORE (governed)"],"items":[{"text":"a notebook","bucket":0},{"text":"a job definition","bucket":0},{"text":"an all-purpose cluster","bucket":0},{"text":"table `prod.sales.orders`","bucket":1},{"text":"catalog `prod`","bucket":1},{"text":"an external location","bucket":1},{"text":"a storage credential","bucket":1}],"explain":"Notebooks, jobs and compute are **workspace** objects (where you work). Catalogs, tables, external locations and credentials are **metastore** objects (where data is governed) — which is why several workspaces can see the same table."},{"id":"ch11-e015","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Workspace B wants to query `prod.sales.orders`, created from workspace A. Which conditions must hold? (choose all)","options":["Both workspaces are attached to the same metastore","The principal has the needed privileges","No workspace binding blocks the catalog in workspace B","The table was re-created inside workspace B"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"The table is registered in the **metastore**, not in workspace A. B sees it if it shares the metastore, the principal is granted access, and no **workspace binding** excludes B. Nothing needs to be re-created."},{"id":"ch11-e016","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A team lead wants to isolate a new project's data. What is the default Databricks recommendation?","options":["Create a new catalog (and grants) inside the existing regional metastore","Create a new metastore for the project","Create a new workspace — that isolates the data","Create a new S3 bucket and skip UC"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Metastores are regional containers; not one per project.","A workspace is an operational environment; data stays in the shared metastore.","Skipping UC brings back fragmented governance."],"explain":"Isolation usually starts at the **catalog** level. A new metastore per project is **not** the default pattern (usually one per region), and a new workspace does not isolate governed data on its own — workspaces sharing a metastore see the same objects."},{"id":"ch11-e017","type":"match","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each part of `prod.finance.payroll` to its level.","pairs":[["prod","catalog"],["finance","schema"],["payroll","object (here a table)"]],"explain":"The three-level namespace is **catalog.schema.object**. The first name is the isolation boundary, the second organizes, the third is the actual asset."},{"id":"ch11-e018","type":"odd","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one cannot be the third name (the object) in `catalog.schema.object`?","options":["a volume","a function","a materialized view","a workspace"],"answer":3,"explain":"Objects inside a schema include tables, views, volumes, functions, models, materialized views and streaming tables. A **workspace** is an operational environment outside the namespace."},{"id":"ch11-e019","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"A company is designing catalogs. Classify each design.","buckets":["By environment","By business unit","Mixed"],"items":[{"text":"`dev`, `test`, `prod`","bucket":0},{"text":"`finance`, `sales`, `marketing`","bucket":1},{"text":"`prod_finance`, `prod_sales`, `dev_shared`","bucket":2},{"text":"`staging`, `prod`","bucket":0},{"text":"`hr`, `logistics`","bucket":1}],"explain":"All three are valid; there is no single right structure. Databricks' rule: catalogs should be **meaningful isolation boundaries** (environment, business unit, team, project) so grants stay simple."},{"id":"ch11-e020","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Databricks prescribes exactly one correct catalog layout: one catalog per environment (dev/test/prod).","answer":false,"explain":"False. Environment, business unit, team, project or mixed layouts are all valid. The guidance is that catalogs represent **meaningful isolation boundaries** so hierarchical grants stay simple."},{"id":"ch11-e021","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"You run `USE CATALOG prod; USE SCHEMA sales;` and then `SELECT * FROM orders;`. Which table do you read, and why is this risky in production code?","options":["`prod.sales.orders`; the short name depends on session context, so the same code may read another table elsewhere","`orders` in the default `hive_metastore`; USE is ignored by SELECT","An error — short names are not allowed in Unity Catalog","`prod.sales.orders`; there is no risk because USE is permanent for the workspace"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unqualified names resolve against the **current catalog and schema**. That works, but the same code run with a different context resolves to a different object — so production code prefers **fully qualified names**. USE only affects the current session."},{"id":"ch11-e022","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the statements to create the catalog and an idempotent schema.","text":"[[CREATE CATALOG]] ecommerce;\n[[USE CATALOG]] ecommerce;\nCREATE SCHEMA [[IF NOT EXISTS]] ecommerce.silver;","bank":["CREATE DATABASE","USE SCHEMA","OR REPLACE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`CREATE CATALOG` makes the catalog, `USE CATALOG` sets the session context, and `IF NOT EXISTS` makes the schema creation safe to re-run."},{"id":"ch11-e023","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Maria runs `CREATE SCHEMA ecommerce.gold;` and gets a permission error. Which pair of privileges does she typically need?","options":["`USE CATALOG` + `CREATE SCHEMA` on catalog `ecommerce`","`CREATE SCHEMA` on the metastore only","`SELECT` on catalog `ecommerce`","`CREATE CATALOG` on the metastore"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct — hierarchical.","CREATE SCHEMA is granted on the parent catalog.","SELECT reads data; it doesn't create schemas.","That creates catalogs, not schemas."],"explain":"Schema creation needs `USE CATALOG` **and** `CREATE SCHEMA` on the **parent catalog**. `CREATE CATALOG` is for making catalogs; `SELECT` is a data-reading privilege."},{"id":"ch11-e024","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"To create a new catalog you typically need the `CREATE CATALOG` privilege on the metastore.","answer":true,"explain":"True. Catalogs are children of the metastore, so `CREATE CATALOG` is granted on the metastore — the same hierarchical logic as `CREATE SCHEMA` on the parent catalog."},{"id":"ch11-e025","type":"write","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write SQL that creates catalog `ecommerce` (only if missing), creates schema `silver` in it (only if missing), and then sets both as the current context.","solution":"CREATE CATALOG IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce;\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce.silver;\nUSE CATALOG ecommerce;\nUSE SCHEMA silver;","keywords":["create catalog if not exists","create schema if not exists ecommerce.silver","use catalog","use schema"],"lang":"sql","explain":"`IF NOT EXISTS` makes setup scripts re-runnable; `USE CATALOG` / `USE SCHEMA` only set the session context — they don't grant anything."},{"id":"ch11-e026","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`CREATE SCHEMA ecommerce.gold;` fails with a permission error for Maria. Walk it like an engineer.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Who is actually running the statement (Maria interactively, or a job's service principal)?","ok":true,"fb":"Right — always confirm the identity before reading grants."},{"text":"Ask an admin for ALL PRIVILEGES on the metastore.","ok":false,"fb":"That breaks least privilege and teaches you nothing about the cause."},{"text":"Re-create the catalog.","ok":false,"fb":"Destructive and unrelated — the catalog exists."}]},{"prompt":"Maria runs it herself. Which grants do you inspect?","options":[{"text":"`SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG ecommerce;` — look for USE CATALOG and CREATE SCHEMA for Maria or her groups.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct: schema creation is authorized on the parent catalog."},{"text":"`SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE ecommerce.gold.x;`","ok":false,"fb":"The schema doesn't exist yet; table grants are irrelevant."},{"text":"Check the cluster's autoscaling settings.","ok":false,"fb":"Compute sizing has nothing to do with a privilege error."}]},{"prompt":"She has CREATE SCHEMA but no USE CATALOG. Fix?","options":[{"text":"`GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG ecommerce TO` her group.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — CREATE SCHEMA needs USE CATALOG as a prerequisite."},{"text":"Grant SELECT on the catalog.","ok":false,"fb":"SELECT reads data; it doesn't let you traverse/use the catalog for creation."},{"text":"Grant CREATE CATALOG on the metastore.","ok":false,"fb":"Over-privileged and still not the missing piece."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e027","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Classify each item.","buckets":["Principal (WHO)","Securable (WHAT OBJECT)","Privilege (WHAT ACTION)"],"items":[{"text":"`nikos@company.com`","bucket":0},{"text":"`data_engineers`","bucket":0},{"text":"`orders-etl-prod`","bucket":0},{"text":"external location `finance_loc`","bucket":1},{"text":"storage credential `cred_a`","bucket":1},{"text":"function `normalize_email()`","bucket":1},{"text":"`SELECT`","bucket":2},{"text":"`MODIFY`","bucket":2},{"text":"`READ VOLUME`","bucket":2}],"explain":"Principals receive grants, securables are what grants are **on**, privileges are the **action** granted. External locations and storage credentials are securables too — you grant privileges on them."},{"id":"ch11-e028","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A storage credential is a securable object in Unity Catalog.","answer":true,"explain":"True. Any object registered in UC on which grants can be given is securable — including **storage credentials** and **external locations**, not only tables."},{"id":"ch11-e029","type":"calc","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"4 analysts each need SELECT on 50 gold tables. How many GRANT statements do you need if you grant per individual user (one table per statement)?","answer":200,"tolerance":0,"unit":"grants","explain":"4 users × 50 tables = **200** grants. With a group `analysts` you grant 50 (or 1 at schema level with inheritance), and a new hire needs **0** new grants — just group membership."},{"id":"ch11-e030","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"When Maria joins the analytics team and all grants were made to group `analysts`, you must add her to each of the 200 table grants.","answer":false,"explain":"False. You add Maria to the `analysts` group; **zero** grants change. That's exactly why groups are preferred: membership management instead of permission sprawl."},{"id":"ch11-e031","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Databricks recommends provisioning users, groups and service principals at the workspace level, separately in each workspace.","answer":false,"explain":"False. The recommendation is to provision principals at the **account level** (and use groups), so the same identities work across workspaces attached to the metastore."},{"id":"ch11-e032","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A production job definition. Which line is the governance problem?","lines":["name: orders_nightly_etl","schedule: \"0 0 2 * * ?\"   # 02:00 daily","run_as: nikos@company.com","task: notebooks/silver_orders"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"run_as: orders-etl-prod   # service principal","explain":"A production job should not run as a **personal user**: if Nikos leaves and his account is disabled, the 02:00 ETL stops. Run it as a **service principal** — a stable machine identity decoupled from employee lifecycle."},{"id":"ch11-e033","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the grant that lets group `analysts` read the table.","text":"GRANT [[SELECT]]\nON [[TABLE]] ecommerce.silver.orders\n[[TO]] `analysts`;","bank":["MODIFY","SCHEMA","FROM"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Grammar: **GRANT** privilege **ON** object-type object **TO** principal. `FROM` belongs to REVOKE."},{"id":"ch11-e034","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"(Source exercise §21) `analysts` have `USE CATALOG ecommerce` and `USE SCHEMA ecommerce.gold`, but no SELECT. What happens to `SELECT * FROM ecommerce.gold.daily_sales`?","options":["It fails — USE gives no permission on the data","It succeeds — USE SCHEMA implies SELECT on its tables","It returns column names but no rows","It succeeds only on a SQL warehouse"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Classic misconception: USE ≠ SELECT.","No partial result — the query is denied.","Warehouses enforce the same UC privileges."],"explain":"USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA only let you **traverse** the namespace. Data access needs `SELECT` on the table (or inherited from the schema/catalog). No compute type changes that."},{"id":"ch11-e035","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which set of grants is the minimum for group `analysts` to read `ecommerce.gold.daily_sales`?","options":["USE CATALOG on `ecommerce` + USE SCHEMA on `ecommerce.gold` + SELECT on the table","SELECT on the table only","USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA only","ALL PRIVILEGES on catalog `ecommerce`"],"answer":0,"explain":"Three keys: building door (USE CATALOG), floor door (USE SCHEMA), room key (SELECT). ALL PRIVILEGES would work but violates least privilege."},{"id":"ch11-e036","type":"write","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the three GRANT statements so group `analysts` can run `SELECT * FROM ecommerce.gold.daily_sales;` — nothing more.","solution":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG ecommerce TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE ecommerce.gold.daily_sales TO `analysts`;","keywords":["grant use catalog on catalog ecommerce","grant use schema on schema ecommerce.gold","grant select on table ecommerce.gold.daily_sales","to `analysts`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA are prerequisites; SELECT on the table grants the data. Granting SELECT at table level (not schema) is the least-privilege version."},{"id":"ch11-e037","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"These grants should let `analysts` read `ecommerce.gold.daily_sales`, but the query is denied. Find the buggy line.","lines":["GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG ecommerce TO `analysts`;","GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA ecommerce.silver TO `analysts`;","GRANT SELECT ON TABLE ecommerce.gold.daily_sales TO `analysts`;"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;","explain":"USE SCHEMA was granted on the **sibling** schema `silver`, not on `gold`. Without USE SCHEMA on the parent schema, the table can't be reached even with SELECT — Debugging Case 2."},{"id":"ch11-e038","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A user has SELECT on `prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue` and USE CATALOG on `prod_ecommerce`, yet `SELECT * FROM prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue` returns PERMISSION_DENIED.","steps":[{"prompt":"The user swears \"I have SELECT!\". What do you check?","options":[{"text":"`SHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold;` — is there USE SCHEMA for the user or their groups?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes: SELECT is useless if you can't traverse the parent schema."},{"text":"Re-grant SELECT on the table.","ok":false,"fb":"SELECT is already there; repeating it changes nothing."},{"text":"Restart the cluster.","ok":false,"fb":"Compute restarts don't change UC privileges."}]},{"prompt":"No USE SCHEMA anywhere for them. Least-privilege fix?","options":[{"text":"`GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO` the user's group.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — that adds the missing prerequisite only."},{"text":"`GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold`.","ok":false,"fb":"Works but grants far more than needed (MODIFY, CREATE …)."},{"text":"`GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold`.","ok":false,"fb":"SELECT on the schema still isn't USE SCHEMA; the traversal privilege is missing."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e039","type":"order","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case 2 — order your checks when a user has SELECT on a table but is denied.","items":["Confirm the identity actually running the query","Check USE CATALOG on the parent catalog","Check USE SCHEMA on the exact parent schema","Confirm SELECT on the table (direct or inherited)","Grant only the missing prerequisite to the group"],"explain":"Walk **top-down**: identity first, then the namespace prerequisites (catalog, schema), then the object privilege. Fix by adding only what's missing — usually `USE SCHEMA` — to a group."},{"id":"ch11-e040","type":"free","section":"ch11-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"(Interview) Explain the difference between USE SCHEMA and SELECT.","model":"USE SCHEMA allows a principal to traverse/use the schema namespace, but it does not grant access to table data. To query a table, the user typically also needs USE CATALOG on the parent catalog and SELECT on the table or an inherited grant from the schema or catalog.","rubric":["USE SCHEMA = traverse / use the namespace","No data access by itself","Also needs USE CATALOG","SELECT on table or inherited parent grant"],"explain":"The interview answer distinguishes **namespace traversal** (USE) from **data access** (SELECT) and names the full read path."},{"id":"ch11-e041","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold TO analysts` was run last month (plus USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA). Today a new table `ecommerce.gold.churn` is created. Can analysts read it?","options":["Yes — the schema-level SELECT is inherited by objects in the schema, including new ones","No — inheritance only covers tables that existed at grant time","Only after re-running the GRANT","Only if the new table is managed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Inheritance flows from the schema to its children, so new tables are covered automatically. That's the power — and the risk — of hierarchical grants."},{"id":"ch11-e042","type":"calc","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Schema `gold` has 2,000 tables. Three groups need read access. How many `GRANT SELECT` statements do you need if you grant at **schema** level and rely on inheritance?","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"grants","explain":"One schema-level grant per group = **3** (plus the USE prerequisites). Per table it would be 2,000 × 3 = 6,000 — exactly the grant sprawl Databricks tells you to avoid with inheritance."},{"id":"ch11-e043","type":"match","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each privilege to its meaning.","pairs":[["SELECT","read the table"],["MODIFY","broad write/change of table data"],["INSERT (beta)","narrower write capability than MODIFY"],["USE SCHEMA","traverse the schema namespace"]],"explain":"SELECT reads, MODIFY is broad DML, finer-grained DML privileges such as INSERT (beta) narrow writes, USE SCHEMA only traverses."},{"id":"ch11-e044","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Basic production model: who typically gets what on `prod.silver`?","buckets":["Analyst","Data engineer / pipeline SP"],"items":[{"text":"SELECT on gold tables","bucket":0},{"text":"MODIFY on silver tables","bucket":1},{"text":"write the nightly MERGE output","bucket":1},{"text":"read dashboards' source tables","bucket":0},{"text":"append new rows from ingestion","bucket":1}],"explain":"Analysts read (SELECT); engineers and especially pipeline service principals write (MODIFY or finer DML). Databricks recommends reserving direct writes in production for service principals."},{"id":"ch11-e045","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Fill in the statements to inspect grants at every level.","text":"SHOW GRANTS ON [[TABLE]] ecommerce.gold.daily_sales;\nSHOW GRANTS ON [[SCHEMA]] ecommerce.gold;\nSHOW GRANTS ON [[CATALOG]] ecommerce;","bank":["DATABASE","WORKSPACE","METASTORE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`SHOW GRANTS ON <type> <name>` answers \"who has what?\". Checking all three levels catches inherited grants."},{"id":"ch11-e046","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Find the syntax bug in this revoke.","lines":["-- remove direct read access","REVOKE SELECT","ON TABLE ecommerce.gold.daily_sales","TO `analysts`;"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"FROM `analysts`;","explain":"GRANT … **TO** principal, but REVOKE … **FROM** principal. Using TO in a REVOKE is a syntax error."},{"id":"ch11-e047","type":"write","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Revoke group `analysts`' direct SELECT on `ecommerce.gold.daily_sales`, then show the grants on the schema to check for inherited access.","solution":"REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE ecommerce.gold.daily_sales FROM `analysts`;\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA ecommerce.gold;","keywords":["revoke select","on table ecommerce.gold.daily_sales","from `analysts`","show grants on schema ecommerce.gold"],"lang":"sql","explain":"After a REVOKE, always look **up** the hierarchy: a schema- or catalog-level SELECT keeps the access alive."},{"id":"ch11-e048","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"After `REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue FROM analysts`, members of analysts can no longer read that table.","answer":false,"explain":"Not necessarily. If `SELECT` exists on schema `gold` or catalog `prod_ecommerce`, the table **inherits** it and access remains. Always inspect the hierarchy."},{"id":"ch11-e049","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The admin revoked `SELECT ON TABLE prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue FROM analysts`. An analyst still reads the table.","steps":[{"prompt":"First move?","options":[{"text":"`SHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold;` and `SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce;`","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — look for inherited SELECT above the table."},{"text":"Run the REVOKE again.","ok":false,"fb":"The direct grant is already gone; repeating it changes nothing."},{"text":"Assume caching and wait an hour.","ok":false,"fb":"Guessing. The usual cause is inheritance — verify it."}]},{"prompt":"You find `SELECT ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO analysts`. Goal: analysts read all gold tables EXCEPT daily_revenue. Best approach?","options":[{"text":"Remove the schema-level SELECT and grant SELECT on the specific gold tables they should read (or move daily_revenue to a schema they can't read).","ok":true,"fb":"Right — inheritance can't be overridden by revoking at a lower level; restructure the grants."},{"text":"Revoke at table level again, harder.","ok":false,"fb":"A table-level REVOKE doesn't block a schema-level inherited grant."},{"text":"Make the analysts owners of the table.","ok":false,"fb":"That gives them more power, not less."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e050","type":"order","section":"ch11-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case 3 — order your checks when access survives a REVOKE.","items":["Confirm which principal/group the user reads through","SHOW GRANTS on the table (direct grant really gone?)","SHOW GRANTS on the parent schema (inherited SELECT?)","SHOW GRANTS on the parent catalog (inherited SELECT / ALL PRIVILEGES?)","Restructure grants at the right level"],"explain":"Go bottom-up from the object you revoked: table → schema → catalog. Inherited grants higher up keep access alive; fix by restructuring where SELECT is granted."},{"id":"ch11-e051","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Who should own the production table `prod.gold.revenue`?","options":["The group `data_platform_admins`","Nikos, who created it","Every analyst","The workspace"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Personal ownership breaks when Nikos leaves.","Ownership is far more than reading — too much power.","Workspaces are not principals."],"explain":"Databricks recommends **groups** (or service principals) as owners of production objects. A personal owner disappears with the employee; analysts need SELECT, not ownership; a workspace can't own UC objects."},{"id":"ch11-e052","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which abilities does an object's owner typically have that a principal with only SELECT does not? (choose all)","options":["Manage privileges on the object (grant/revoke)","Modify / manage the object","Transfer ownership","Bypass row filters on every table in the metastore"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Owners can manage privileges, manage the object and transfer ownership. Ownership of one object doesn't grant powers across the whole metastore."},{"id":"ch11-e053","type":"write","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Transfer ownership of `prod.gold.revenue` from Nikos to the group `data_platform_admins`.","solution":"ALTER TABLE prod.gold.revenue SET OWNER TO `data_platform_admins`;","keywords":["alter table prod.gold.revenue","owner to","`data_platform_admins`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"`ALTER <type> <name> [SET] OWNER TO principal` transfers ownership. Group ownership keeps production governance stable when people leave."},{"id":"ch11-e054","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"`ALL PRIVILEGES` includes `MANAGE`.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Current UC docs exclude **MANAGE, EXTERNAL USE LOCATION and EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA** from ALL PRIVILEGES. A certification favourite."},{"id":"ch11-e055","type":"odd","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are covered by `ALL PRIVILEGES` on a schema. Which one is not?","options":["SELECT","MODIFY","CREATE TABLE","MANAGE"],"answer":3,"explain":"MANAGE (delegated governance) is excluded from ALL PRIVILEGES, as are EXTERNAL USE LOCATION / EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA. Data and create privileges are included."},{"id":"ch11-e056","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A platform team wants Maria to manage grants on schema `prod.gold` without making her (or anyone) the owner. Which privilege?","options":["MANAGE","ALL PRIVILEGES","BROWSE","USE SCHEMA"],"answer":0,"explain":"**MANAGE** is the delegated-governance privilege: manage grants and ownership-related operations without being owner. ALL PRIVILEGES excludes MANAGE; BROWSE is discovery only; USE SCHEMA only traverses."},{"id":"ch11-e057","type":"free","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is `GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce TO all_company_users` a bad idea? Mention the dynamic evaluation.","model":"It violates least privilege: every employee gets far more than they need (writes, creates, etc.) on all of production, which increases the blast radius of mistakes or compromised accounts. And ALL PRIVILEGES is evaluated dynamically: as new applicable privileges are added to the platform they are automatically included, so the grant silently grows over time. Databricks recommends restraint with ALL PRIVILEGES and MANAGE.","rubric":["Violates least privilege / huge blast radius","Everyone gets write/create on prod","Dynamic evaluation: future privileges included automatically","Restraint with ALL PRIVILEGES and MANAGE"],"explain":"Two reasons: too broad today, and it grows tomorrow because ALL PRIVILEGES is evaluated dynamically."},{"id":"ch11-e058","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"You want every employee to discover which datasets exist in catalog `prod` (and request access), without reading any data. Which privilege?","options":["BROWSE on the catalog","SELECT on the catalog","USE CATALOG only","MANAGE on the catalog"],"answer":0,"explain":"**BROWSE** gives metadata visibility/discoverability without data access. SELECT would expose data; MANAGE would let them change grants; USE CATALOG alone is a traversal prerequisite, not a discovery feature."},{"id":"ch11-e059","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A principal with BROWSE on a catalog can run `SELECT *` on its tables.","answer":false,"explain":"False. BROWSE shows that objects **exist** (metadata), not their data. To query, the user still requests and receives USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT."},{"id":"ch11-e060","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"\"Managed\" means the data is stored on Databricks' servers; \"external\" means it is stored in your cloud account.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Both can be in **your** cloud object storage. The difference is **who controls the location and lifecycle** of the files: UC (managed) or you / an external system (external)."},{"id":"ch11-e061","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"For an EXTERNAL table, who is responsible for what?","buckets":["Unity Catalog","You / the external system"],"items":[{"text":"metadata","bucket":0},{"text":"permissions","bucket":0},{"text":"lineage","bucket":0},{"text":"choosing the storage location","bucket":1},{"text":"deleting files when no longer needed","bucket":1}],"explain":"External tables still get full UC **governance** (metadata, permissions, lineage). Only **location + lifecycle** move to you. For managed tables UC does all five."},{"id":"ch11-e062","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the external table definition.","text":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders_ext\n[[USING]] DELTA\n[[LOCATION]] 's3://company-data/orders/';","bank":["PATH","STORED AS","MANAGED LOCATION"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`LOCATION` is what makes the table external: you specify the path. Omit it and UC picks a managed path."},{"id":"ch11-e063","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"`CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders (id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(10,2));` — which kind of table is this, and who decides its storage path?","options":["Managed — Unity Catalog decides the path (under the managed storage root)","External — the default bucket of the workspace","External — the path is the catalog name","Neither: a table without LOCATION is only a view"],"answer":0,"explain":"No `LOCATION` → **managed** table; UC generates an internal location under the schema/catalog/metastore managed storage root."},{"id":"ch11-e064","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"You run `DROP TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders_ext;` on an EXTERNAL table. What happens?","options":["UC metadata is removed; the files at the external path remain","Metadata and files are both deleted","Nothing — external tables can't be dropped","Files are deleted but metadata stays"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","That's the managed behavior.","They can be dropped — only metadata goes.","Backwards."],"explain":"Dropping an **external** table removes the catalog entry only; the underlying files stay where you put them. For a **managed** table, UC also handles deleting the files."},{"id":"ch11-e065","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A cleanup script. Which comment reveals a wrong assumption?","lines":["-- orders_ext is an EXTERNAL table at s3://company-data/orders/","DROP TABLE ecommerce.silver.orders_ext;","-- storage cost for s3://company-data/orders/ is now freed","-- recreate later with CREATE TABLE ... LOCATION if needed"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"-- metadata removed; files at s3://company-data/orders/ remain → delete them with your storage tooling if truly unwanted","explain":"Dropping an external table does **not** delete the files, so storage costs remain. (The last line is right: you can re-register the same path later.)"},{"id":"ch11-e066","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Managed or external? Classify each situation.","buckets":["Prefer MANAGED","EXTERNAL makes sense"],"items":[{"text":"a brand-new Databricks-first silver table","bucket":0},{"text":"you want maximum automation/optimization integration","bucket":0},{"text":"10 TB of legacy Delta data already at known S3 paths","bucket":1},{"text":"a non-Databricks system controls the files' lifecycle","bucket":1},{"text":"migration constraints force data to stay at its path","bucket":1},{"text":"a new gold aggregate for dashboards","bucket":0}],"explain":"Default to managed for new Databricks workloads (automation, optimization, governance integration). External fits existing paths, external lifecycle control, migration constraints and interoperability."},{"id":"ch11-e067","type":"order","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Registering existing legacy Delta data as an external table — order the steps.","items":["Delta data already exists at s3://company/legacy/orders/","An external location governs a path covering it","The principal holds the required privileges (e.g. CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE on the external location)","Run CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.legacy_orders USING DELTA LOCATION '…'","UC registers the metadata (data not rewritten)"],"explain":"Data first, then governance of the path (external location), then privileges, then the CREATE TABLE … LOCATION, which only registers metadata."},{"id":"ch11-e068","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"`CREATE TABLE … USING DELTA LOCATION 's3://company/legacy/orders/'` on existing Delta data necessarily rewrites all the data files.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Unity Catalog **registers metadata** pointing at the existing files; it does not necessarily rewrite them."},{"id":"ch11-e069","type":"write","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Register the existing Delta data at `s3://company/legacy/orders/` as table `ecommerce.silver.legacy_orders`.","solution":"CREATE TABLE ecommerce.silver.legacy_orders\nUSING DELTA\nLOCATION 's3://company/legacy/orders/';","keywords":["create table ecommerce.silver.legacy_orders","using delta","location 's3://company/legacy/orders/'"],"lang":"sql","explain":"External table = CREATE TABLE … LOCATION. It works only if the path is under a governed external location and you have the privileges."},{"id":"ch11-e070","type":"free","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"(Interview) What is the difference between a managed table and an external table in Unity Catalog?","model":"Both are governed by Unity Catalog for metadata, access control, auditing and lineage. For a managed table, Unity Catalog additionally controls the underlying storage location and file lifecycle. For an external table, I provide the storage path and the underlying files remain externally managed; dropping the external table removes the catalog metadata but does not delete the underlying files.","rubric":["Both: UC governance (metadata, access, auditing, lineage)","Managed: UC controls location + file lifecycle","External: I provide the path, lifecycle external","DROP external → metadata gone, files stay"],"explain":"The strong answer starts with what is **the same** (governance) before the difference (location + lifecycle) and the DROP consequence."},{"id":"ch11-e071","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Monday: a colleague says \"I dropped `silver.orders_old` to save storage, but the S3 bill didn't change\". Tuesday: another dropped `silver.orders_tmp` and \"the data is gone from storage\".","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check for both tables?","options":[{"text":"Whether each was MANAGED or EXTERNAL (e.g. from the CREATE statement / DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED history or audit).","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — DROP behavior depends entirely on the table type."},{"text":"Whether the cluster was Photon-enabled.","ok":false,"fb":"Compute engine doesn't decide DROP semantics."},{"text":"Whether the user had SELECT.","ok":false,"fb":"Both DROPs succeeded; this isn't a permission problem."}]},{"prompt":"`orders_old` was EXTERNAL. Explain the unchanged bill.","options":[{"text":"DROP removed only the UC metadata; the files at its LOCATION remain until someone deletes them with storage tooling.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — external lifecycle is your responsibility."},{"text":"UC deletes external files after 30 days.","ok":false,"fb":"UC doesn't manage external file lifecycle."},{"text":"The DROP failed silently.","ok":false,"fb":"The table entry is gone; the files were never UC's to delete."}]},{"prompt":"`orders_tmp` was MANAGED. Lesson for next time?","options":[{"text":"Check the type before DROP: a managed DROP lets UC delete the underlying files as part of its lifecycle.","ok":true,"fb":"Right — managed = UC owns location and lifecycle."},{"text":"Never use managed tables.","ok":false,"fb":"Managed is the recommended default; just know the semantics."},{"text":"Always add LOCATION so nothing is ever deleted.","ok":false,"fb":"Making everything external pushes lifecycle work and governance gaps onto you."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e072","type":"order","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which managed storage location wins? Order from HIGHEST to LOWEST priority.","items":["Schema managed location","Catalog managed location","Metastore managed location"],"explain":"The most specific (lowest-level) available location wins: schema, else catalog, else metastore. Databricks often recommends catalog-level storage as the isolation boundary."},{"id":"ch11-e073","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Catalog `ecommerce` has MANAGED LOCATION `s3://uc/ecommerce/`; schema `ecommerce.finance` has MANAGED LOCATION `s3://uc/finance/`. A managed table is created in `ecommerce.finance`. Where do its files go?","options":["Under `s3://uc/finance/`","Under `s3://uc/ecommerce/`","Under the metastore root","Wherever the cluster's DBFS root is"],"answer":0,"explain":"The **most specific** available managed location wins: the schema's. The catalog location would be used only if the schema had none."},{"id":"ch11-e074","type":"write","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Create catalog `ecommerce` whose managed tables are stored under `s3://company-uc/ecommerce/`.","solution":"CREATE CATALOG ecommerce\nMANAGED LOCATION 's3://company-uc/ecommerce/';","keywords":["create catalog ecommerce","managed location","'s3://company-uc/ecommerce/'"],"lang":"sql","explain":"`MANAGED LOCATION` (not `LOCATION`) sets the catalog's managed storage root. It must be under an external location and you need CREATE MANAGED STORAGE."},{"id":"ch11-e075","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`CREATE CATALOG ecommerce MANAGED LOCATION 's3://company-uc/ecommerce/'` fails. Which are plausible causes? (choose all)","options":["The path is not under any Unity Catalog external location","You lack CREATE MANAGED STORAGE on the external location","You lack CREATE CATALOG on the metastore","The cluster has too few workers"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Managed locations must sit under an external location, need CREATE MANAGED STORAGE, and catalog creation itself needs CREATE CATALOG. Cluster size is irrelevant to authorization."},{"id":"ch11-e076","type":"match","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each cloud to what its storage credential typically wraps.","pairs":[["AWS","IAM role"],["Azure","managed identity / service principal"],["GCP","service account"]],"explain":"A storage credential abstracts a **long-term cloud identity** so users never handle raw access keys."},{"id":"ch11-e077","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"What does an external location add that a storage credential alone doesn't have?","options":["A specific cloud path (URI) bound to the credential, as a securable boundary","A cloud identity to authenticate with","The table schema","A compute cluster"],"answer":0,"explain":"The credential says **who** (which cloud identity). The external location says **where** (which path) + which credential, and it's a securable you grant on."},{"id":"ch11-e078","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the external location definition.","text":"CREATE [[EXTERNAL LOCATION]] finance_loc\n  [[URL]] 's3://company/finance/'\n  WITH ([[STORAGE CREDENTIAL]] cred_a);","bank":["LOCATION","PATH","IAM ROLE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION name URL '<path>' WITH (STORAGE CREDENTIAL <cred>)` binds a path to a credential."},{"id":"ch11-e079","type":"order","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the chain from cloud identity down to the files.","items":["AWS IAM role / Azure identity","Storage credential","External location (s3://company/raw/)","External table / external volume","Files"],"explain":"Cloud identity → credential (UC abstraction) → external location (path + credential) → table/volume metadata → the actual files."},{"id":"ch11-e080","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Why does Databricks recommend granting `CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE` on an external location instead of on the storage credential?","options":["It scopes the permission to a specific path — path-scoped least privilege","Storage credentials can't receive grants","External locations are faster","Credentials expire every hour"],"answer":0,"explain":"A credential may reach a huge storage scope; an external location like `s3://company/finance/` limits access to that path. Credentials **are** securables — the point is granularity."},{"id":"ch11-e081","type":"write","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Allow group `data_engineers` to create external tables only under the external location `finance_loc`.","solution":"GRANT CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE\nON EXTERNAL LOCATION finance_loc\nTO `data_engineers`;","keywords":["grant create external table","on external location finance_loc","to `data_engineers`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Granting on the external location (not the credential) scopes the privilege to its path."},{"id":"ch11-e082","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"One storage credential can be used by several external locations, each with its own UC access boundary.","answer":true,"explain":"True. E.g. credential A backs `s3://company/raw/`, `.../archive/` and `.../shared/`, and each location gets separate grants."},{"id":"ch11-e083","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"\"External location\" and \"external table\" are synonyms.","answer":false,"explain":"False. An **external location** is a governed storage **path** (+ credential). An **external table** is **table metadata** pointing at tabular data **inside** such a governed path."},{"id":"ch11-e084","type":"free","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"(Interview) Storage credential vs external location?","model":"A storage credential represents the cloud identity Databricks can use to authenticate to object storage (an IAM role, managed identity or service account). An external location binds that credential to a specific governed cloud-storage URI, giving Unity Catalog a securable path boundary on which privileges can be granted. One credential can back many external locations.","rubric":["Credential = cloud identity for authentication","Location = URI + credential","Location is a securable path boundary","Grant privileges on the location"],"explain":"Identity (credential) vs path-scoped securable (location)."},{"id":"ch11-e085","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`SELECT * FROM prod.silver.orders` (managed) works. `SELECT * FROM prod.silver.legacy_orders` (external at s3://company/legacy/orders/) fails with a storage access error.","steps":[{"prompt":"You already confirmed USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT on both tables. Where next?","options":[{"text":"The extra external-storage layers: external location, storage credential, workspace binding, cloud IAM.","ok":true,"fb":"Right — the managed table proves the UC grants work; the difference is the storage path."},{"text":"Re-grant SELECT on the external table.","ok":false,"fb":"SELECT is already fine; the managed one works with the same grants."},{"text":"Switch to a bigger cluster.","ok":false,"fb":"Sizing doesn't cause storage access errors."}]},{"prompt":"Which external-location question first?","options":[{"text":"Is `s3://company/legacy/orders/` covered by an external location, and is it bound to this workspace?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — no governed path, or a location not usable in this workspace, means no access."},{"text":"Is the table partitioned?","ok":false,"fb":"Partitioning doesn't affect authorization."},{"text":"Does the table have a column mask?","ok":false,"fb":"Masks change values, they don't cause storage access errors."}]},{"prompt":"Location exists and is bound. The cloud team rotated the IAM role's policy last night. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Have the cloud team restore read access for the IAM role behind the storage credential to that path.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — UC can't grant what the cloud identity itself is not allowed to do."},{"text":"Give the users AWS access keys.","ok":false,"fb":"Bypasses governance — exactly what storage credentials exist to avoid."},{"text":"Convert the table to managed by DROP + CREATE.","ok":false,"fb":"Drastic and doesn't fix the broken credential."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e086","type":"order","section":"ch11-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case 4 — order the extra checks for an external table that fails while managed tables work.","items":["Confirm UC grants (USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT) — managed works, so likely fine","Find the table's LOCATION (DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED)","Check the external location covering that path + privileges on it","Check workspace binding of the external location / credential","Check the storage credential and the cloud IAM permissions behind it"],"explain":"Start from what's proven (UC grants), then follow the storage chain outward: location → external location → binding → credential → cloud IAM."},{"id":"ch11-e087","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Table or volume?","buckets":["Table","Volume"],"items":[{"text":"daily order rows queried with SQL","bucket":0},{"text":"invoice PDFs","bucket":1},{"text":"product JPEG images","bucket":1},{"text":"JSON config files for a job","bucket":1},{"text":"customer dimension with Delta history","bucket":0},{"text":"raw landing files before ingestion","bucket":1}],"explain":"Tables are for rows/columns with SQL/DataFrame semantics and Delta history. Volumes govern non-tabular or raw files — PDFs, images, configs, landing files."},{"id":"ch11-e088","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the statements: a managed volume, then an external one.","text":"CREATE [[VOLUME]] ecommerce.raw.documents;\nCREATE [[EXTERNAL VOLUME]] ecommerce.raw.documents_ext\n[[LOCATION]] 's3://company/documents/';","bank":["TABLE","MANAGED VOLUME","URL"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`CREATE VOLUME` = managed (UC picks the location). `CREATE EXTERNAL VOLUME … LOCATION` = you specify the path."},{"id":"ch11-e089","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Dropping an external volume deletes the files at its LOCATION.","answer":false,"explain":"False. As with external tables: metadata is removed, **underlying files remain**. A managed volume drop can remove the data as part of the managed lifecycle."},{"id":"ch11-e090","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which set lets `data_scientists` READ files in volume `ecommerce.raw.documents`?","options":["USE CATALOG ecommerce + USE SCHEMA ecommerce.raw + READ VOLUME on the volume","SELECT on the volume","USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA only","READ FILES on the schema"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volumes use **READ VOLUME / WRITE VOLUME**, plus the usual USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA prerequisites. SELECT is a table privilege."},{"id":"ch11-e091","type":"match","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the action to the privilege it needs (beyond USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA).","pairs":[["read a table","SELECT"],["write a table","MODIFY"],["read volume files","READ VOLUME"],["write volume files","WRITE VOLUME (+ READ VOLUME)"]],"explain":"Tables: SELECT / MODIFY. Volumes: READ VOLUME / WRITE VOLUME. Table and volume permissions are **not** identical."},{"id":"ch11-e092","type":"write","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Grant group `data_scientists` read access to files in volume `ecommerce.raw.documents` (assume they already have the USE privileges).","solution":"GRANT READ VOLUME\nON VOLUME ecommerce.raw.documents\nTO `data_scientists`;","keywords":["grant read volume","on volume ecommerce.raw.documents","to `data_scientists`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Object type keyword is `VOLUME`; the privilege is `READ VOLUME`."},{"id":"ch11-e093","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"This code should read a JSON file from volume `documents` in schema `raw` of catalog `ecommerce`. Which line is wrong?","lines":["# read config from the governed volume","path = \"/Volumes/raw/ecommerce/documents/sample.json\"","with open(path, \"r\") as f:","    text = f.read()"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"path = \"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/sample.json\"","explain":"Volume paths follow the namespace order: `/Volumes/<catalog>/<schema>/<volume>/<file>`. Swapping catalog and schema points at a non-existent volume."},{"id":"ch11-e094","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is the recommended way to reach a non-tabular file governed by UC?","options":["`/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/sample.json`","`s3://company/documents/sample.json` everywhere in code","`SELECT * FROM ecommerce.raw.documents`","Copy it into a Delta table first"],"answer":0,"explain":"Databricks recommends **/Volumes/...** paths for direct non-tabular file access (governed file-path semantics) and table names for tables — not raw cloud URIs scattered through code."},{"id":"ch11-e095","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A volume gives you Delta table history (DESCRIBE HISTORY) for its files.","answer":false,"explain":"False. Delta table history is a **table** feature. Volumes govern files; they don't give table semantics or history."},{"id":"ch11-e096","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A user with USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA runs `open(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/documents/file.json\")` and it fails.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's the first privilege to check?","options":[{"text":"READ VOLUME on `ecommerce.raw.documents`.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — USE privileges are prerequisites; READ VOLUME opens the files."},{"text":"SELECT on the schema.","ok":false,"fb":"SELECT is a table privilege; it doesn't open volumes."},{"text":"MODIFY on the volume.","ok":false,"fb":"MODIFY is for tables; volumes use READ/WRITE VOLUME."}]},{"prompt":"Granted READ VOLUME; reading works. Now the user's job writes `open(path, \"w\")` and fails. Next?","options":[{"text":"Grant WRITE VOLUME as well (READ + WRITE VOLUME for writes).","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — writes need WRITE VOLUME on top of the read set."},{"text":"Grant ALL PRIVILEGES on the catalog.","ok":false,"fb":"Massive over-grant for one write."},{"text":"Make it an external volume.","ok":false,"fb":"Volume type doesn't remove the need for WRITE VOLUME."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e097","type":"order","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case 5 — order the checks for a failing /Volumes/ file access.","items":["Verify the path order /Volumes/<catalog>/<schema>/<volume>/…","USE CATALOG on the catalog","USE SCHEMA on the schema","READ VOLUME on the volume","WRITE VOLUME too, if it's a write","External volume? then external location / credential / IAM"],"explain":"Path first (cheapest), then the namespace prerequisites, then the volume privileges, and the storage layers only for external volumes."},{"id":"ch11-e098","type":"free","section":"ch11-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"(Interview) Volume vs table?","model":"A table provides structured relational semantics over rows and columns, while a Unity Catalog volume provides governed access to non-tabular files in cloud storage. Tables use privileges such as SELECT and MODIFY, whereas volumes use READ VOLUME and WRITE VOLUME. Files in volumes are accessed through /Volumes/catalog/schema/volume/ paths.","rubric":["Table = rows/columns relational semantics","Volume = governed non-tabular files","SELECT/MODIFY vs READ VOLUME/WRITE VOLUME","/Volumes/... paths"],"explain":"Name the semantics difference and the privilege difference."},{"id":"ch11-e099","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which UC objects can be restricted with workspace bindings (per the source)? (choose all)","options":["Catalogs","External locations","Storage credentials","Individual table columns"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Workspace bindings apply to **catalogs, external locations and storage credentials**. Column-level control is done with masks/ABAC, not bindings."},{"id":"ch11-e100","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"If a user holds USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT on `prod.finance.payroll`, the query must succeed from any workspace attached to the metastore.","answer":false,"explain":"False. A **workspace binding** can restrict `prod` to specific workspaces. User privileges are not the only authorization boundary."},{"id":"ch11-e101","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Nikos has USE CATALOG prod, USE SCHEMA prod.finance and SELECT on prod.finance.payroll. From `dev-workspace` the query fails; his teammate in `prod-workspace` runs the same query fine.","steps":[{"prompt":"Grants are identical for both (same group). What's the strongest hypothesis?","options":[{"text":"Catalog `prod` is bound only to `prod-workspace`.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — same principal grants, different workspace → binding."},{"text":"Nikos lacks SELECT.","ok":false,"fb":"Same group, same grants; the teammate succeeds."},{"text":"The table is external.","ok":false,"fb":"Then it would fail in both workspaces."}]},{"prompt":"Binding confirmed. What's the right fix?","options":[{"text":"Run it from prod-workspace, or — if dev access is truly intended — have an admin add a binding for dev-workspace.","ok":true,"fb":"Bindings are deliberate isolation; change them only if the design allows it."},{"text":"Grant ALL PRIVILEGES on prod to Nikos.","ok":false,"fb":"Grants can't override a missing workspace binding."},{"text":"Copy payroll into the dev catalog.","ok":false,"fb":"That copies sensitive data around and defeats the isolation."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e102","type":"order","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the checks when grants look correct but a catalog is unreachable from your workspace.","items":["Confirm the running identity","Confirm USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA / SELECT","Check which workspace you are in","Check the catalog's workspace bindings","Retry from a bound workspace or request a binding change"],"explain":"Identity and grants first (the common causes), then the separate workspace-binding boundary."},{"id":"ch11-e103","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which compute can access Unity Catalog? (choose all)","options":["Classic compute with Standard access mode","Classic compute with Dedicated access mode","Serverless compute","Databricks SQL warehouses","A legacy cluster configuration that isn't UC-compatible"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"multi":true,"explain":"Standard and Dedicated classic compute, serverless and Databricks SQL support UC. A legacy/non-UC configuration fails even with perfect grants."},{"id":"ch11-e104","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"If all Unity Catalog permissions are correct, the query succeeds on any cluster.","answer":false,"explain":"False. **permissions correct + wrong legacy compute = still failure.** Compute must be UC-compatible."},{"id":"ch11-e105","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A notebook on an old team cluster fails to read `prod.silver.orders`; the same user reads it fine from a SQL warehouse.","steps":[{"prompt":"Grants are the same in both cases. Which layer differs?","options":[{"text":"Compute: the old cluster may not be UC-compatible.","ok":true,"fb":"Same principal + same grants + different compute → compute layer."},{"text":"The table's row filter.","ok":false,"fb":"Row filters apply regardless of compute; they wouldn't cause a fail-vs-success split here."},{"text":"USE SCHEMA.","ok":false,"fb":"Then the warehouse query would fail too."}]},{"prompt":"Fix?","options":[{"text":"Use a cluster with Standard (or Dedicated) access mode, or serverless.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — UC-compatible compute."},{"text":"Grant SELECT again.","ok":false,"fb":"Grants are already fine."},{"text":"Add more workers.","ok":false,"fb":"Size doesn't make a cluster UC-compatible."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e106","type":"order","section":"ch11-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the checks when the same user and grants work on a SQL warehouse but fail on a cluster.","items":["Confirm identity and grants are identical in both runs","Re-run the query on serverless / a SQL warehouse to isolate compute","Inspect the cluster's access mode","Switch to Standard/Dedicated access mode or serverless"],"explain":"Same principal + same grants + different compute isolates the compute layer; UC needs Standard/Dedicated, serverless or Databricks SQL."},{"id":"ch11-e107","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What does a row filter do?","options":["A SQL UDF evaluated at query time; rows where it returns false are removed from the result","A job that deletes forbidden rows from the table files","A view the user must query instead of the table","A cluster setting that hides partitions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Row filters are **query-time** policies — stored data is untouched; different users see different rows of the same table."},{"id":"ch11-e108","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Attach the row filter to the table.","text":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.gold.sales\nSET [[ROW FILTER]] security.country_filter\n[[ON]] ([[country]]);","bank":["MASK","USING","WHERE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`ALTER TABLE … SET ROW FILTER <function> ON (<column>)` binds the UDF; the listed column is passed as the function's argument."},{"id":"ch11-e109","type":"calc","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"With the `country_filter` from the lesson, table `sales` has 100 GR, 80 DE and 20 FR rows. How many rows does a member of `greece_sales` (and NOT of `global_sales`) see with `SELECT *`?","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"The function returns TRUE for global_sales (no), then for greece_sales AND country='GR' → only the **100** GR rows; DE and FR rows hit ELSE FALSE and are removed."},{"id":"ch11-e110","type":"write","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a row filter function `security.country_filter(country STRING)` that returns TRUE for members of `global_sales`, TRUE for `greece_sales` members on GR rows, otherwise FALSE — and attach it to `ecommerce.gold.sales` on column `country`.","solution":"CREATE FUNCTION security.country_filter(country STRING)\nRETURN\n  CASE\n    WHEN is_account_group_member('global_sales') THEN TRUE\n    WHEN is_account_group_member('greece_sales') AND country = 'GR' THEN TRUE\n    ELSE FALSE\n  END;\n\nALTER TABLE ecommerce.gold.sales\nSET ROW FILTER security.country_filter\nON (country);","keywords":["create function security.country_filter","is_account_group_member('global_sales')","is_account_group_member('greece_sales')","else false","set row filter security.country_filter","on (country)"],"lang":"sql","explain":"A filter UDF returns a boolean per row; `is_account_group_member` makes it user-aware. Default FALSE is the safe choice."},{"id":"ch11-e111","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This filter should show GR rows to greece_sales and everything to global_sales. Which line makes it leak data?","lines":["CREATE FUNCTION security.country_filter(country STRING)","RETURN CASE","  WHEN is_account_group_member('global_sales') THEN TRUE","  WHEN is_account_group_member('greece_sales') AND country = 'GR' THEN TRUE","  ELSE TRUE","END;"],"bugs":[4],"fix":"  ELSE FALSE","explain":"`ELSE TRUE` shows every row to everyone not matched above — the filter filters nothing. Default must be **FALSE** (deny by default)."},{"id":"ch11-e112","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A column mask changes the value stored in the Delta files for users without privilege.","answer":false,"explain":"False. The stored value (e.g. 5000) is unchanged; the mask is a **query-time** policy, so finance_admin sees 5000 and an analyst sees **** / NULL / a bucket."},{"id":"ch11-e113","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Attach the mask function to the salary column.","text":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.gold.employees\n[[ALTER COLUMN]] salary SET [[MASK]] security.mask_salary;","bank":["ROW FILTER","MODIFY COLUMN","POLICY"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Masks are set per column: `ALTER TABLE … ALTER COLUMN <col> SET MASK <function>`."},{"id":"ch11-e114","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"You have 10,000 tables and want every column tagged `sensitivity = pii` masked for users without privilege — including tables created next month. Best tool?","options":["ABAC: governed tags + a central policy","A column mask attached manually to each table","A row filter on each table","Revoke SELECT from everyone"],"answer":0,"explain":"ABAC scales: tag the data, write one policy; a new tagged table tomorrow is **automatically covered**. Manual per-table masks don't scale and are easy to forget."},{"id":"ch11-e115","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Per-table policy or ABAC?","buckets":["Direct row filter / column mask","ABAC (tags + policy)"],"items":[{"text":"one table with unique country logic","bucket":0},{"text":"mask every PII-tagged column across the lakehouse","bucket":1},{"text":"new tagged tables must be covered automatically","bucket":1},{"text":"a special salary rule for one HR table","bucket":0},{"text":"consistent policy enforcement across thousands of tables","bucket":1}],"explain":"Databricks recommends ABAC for consistent enforcement across many tables; direct filters/masks fit per-table logic."},{"id":"ch11-e116","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"ABAC can apply row filters and column masks, and now also policy-based GRANT/DENY capabilities in supported scopes.","answer":true,"explain":"True per the source: ABAC policies cover row filters, column masks and policy-based GRANT/DENY in supported scopes."},{"id":"ch11-e117","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"An analyst says: \"I have SELECT on `ecommerce.gold.employees`, but salary shows `****`. Fix my permissions!\"","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you tell yourself first?","options":[{"text":"SELECT ≠ unfiltered raw visibility — look for fine-grained policies.","ok":true,"fb":"Right: the query succeeded, so grants are fine; a policy changed the values."},{"text":"Grant MODIFY so they can see real values.","ok":false,"fb":"MODIFY is write access and doesn't bypass masks."},{"text":"The data is corrupted.","ok":false,"fb":"Stored values are intact; masking is query-time."}]},{"prompt":"Where do you look?","options":[{"text":"Column mask on `salary`, row filter on the table, and any ABAC policy matching tags on the column.","ok":true,"fb":"Those three change visibility."},{"text":"SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG only.","ok":false,"fb":"Grants don't explain masked values."},{"text":"The cluster's Spark config.","ok":false,"fb":"Masks are UC policies, not Spark settings."}]},{"prompt":"A mask returns real salary only to `finance_admins`. The analyst isn't in it. Correct outcome?","options":[{"text":"Working as designed; if the business approves, add them to the right group — don't remove the mask.","ok":true,"fb":"The policy is doing its job; access changes go through group membership."},{"text":"Drop the mask for everyone.","ok":false,"fb":"That exposes salary to all readers."},{"text":"Give the analyst ownership.","ok":false,"fb":"Ownership is not a way around governance."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e118","type":"order","section":"ch11-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case 6 — order the checks for \"query works but values are masked / rows missing\".","items":["Confirm the query succeeded (so base grants are fine)","Check for a column mask on the column","Check for a row filter on the table","Check ABAC policies matching tags","Check the user's group memberships used by the policy functions"],"explain":"A successful query rules out missing grants. Then inspect the fine-grained layers and the group memberships those policies test."},{"id":"ch11-e119","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which statements about UC lineage are true per the source? (choose all)","options":["Collected automatically for queries run on Databricks","Can go down to column level","Aggregated across workspaces sharing the same metastore","Must be declared manually in a YAML file"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"Lineage is automatic, column-level and metastore-wide. No manual declaration is needed."},{"id":"ch11-e120","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Before renaming `silver.orders.customer_id`, what should you do?","options":["Check its column-level lineage to find downstream tables, dashboards, features and reports","Just run ALTER — Delta keeps history","Run VACUUM first","Grant MODIFY to everyone so they can fix their code"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lineage enables **impact analysis**: see `customer_id → gold.customer_360 → executive_dashboard` before breaking it. Time travel doesn't fix broken consumers."},{"id":"ch11-e121","type":"order","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Dashboard revenue is wrong. Order the upstream walk.","items":["dashboard","gold.daily_revenue","silver.orders","bronze.orders"],"explain":"Walk upstream along lineage from the symptom; the first layer where the value is already wrong is where the bug was introduced (it's in the transformation into that layer)."},{"id":"ch11-e122","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The executive dashboard shows revenue 3× too high for yesterday.","steps":[{"prompt":"First step?","options":[{"text":"Open lineage for the dashboard to find its upstream table(s).","ok":true,"fb":"Lineage gives you the path to walk."},{"text":"Re-run every pipeline.","ok":false,"fb":"Re-running hides the evidence and may repeat the bug."},{"text":"Grant analysts MODIFY to fix the numbers.","ok":false,"fb":"Never patch outputs by hand."}]},{"prompt":"gold.daily_revenue is 3× too high; silver.orders looks correct. Conclusion?","options":[{"text":"The bug was introduced in the silver → gold transformation (e.g. a bad join duplicating rows).","ok":true,"fb":"First layer where the value is wrong = the transformation just before it."},{"text":"Bronze ingestion is broken.","ok":false,"fb":"Silver is correct, so the input to gold is fine."},{"text":"The dashboard tool is miscalculating.","ok":false,"fb":"Gold is already wrong upstream of the dashboard."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e123","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Catalog Explorer is basically a file browser for cloud storage.","answer":false,"explain":"False. It's a **governance UI**: per object you can see schema, details, permissions, history, lineage, tags and dependencies (as permitted)."},{"id":"ch11-e124","type":"match","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each need to the feature.","pairs":[["Query a PostgreSQL DB under UC governance without ingesting it","Lakehouse Federation"],["Give a partner company governed read access to gold tables","Delta Sharing"],["Restrict catalog `prod` to the prod workspace","Workspace binding"],["See which dashboards use a column","Lineage"]],"explain":"Federation brings external data **in** (query in place); Delta Sharing sends governed data **out**; bindings restrict workspaces; lineage tracks dependencies."},{"id":"ch11-e125","type":"order","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Lakehouse Federation — order the objects from your UC down to the external data.","items":["Unity Catalog metastore","Connection (to PostgreSQL / Snowflake / SQL Server …)","Foreign catalog","External database metadata / tables"],"explain":"A connection holds how to reach the external system; a foreign catalog mirrors its databases into the UC namespace for governed queries."},{"id":"ch11-e126","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the Delta Sharing flow.","text":"CREATE [[SHARE]] ecommerce_share;\nALTER SHARE ecommerce_share [[ADD TABLE]] prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue;\nCREATE [[RECIPIENT]] partner_co;\nGRANT SELECT ON SHARE ecommerce_share TO RECIPIENT partner_co;","bank":["CONNECTION","FOREIGN CATALOG","INSERT"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Tables → **share** → **recipient**: create the share, add tables, create the recipient, grant the share to the recipient."},{"id":"ch11-e127","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Phase 3 production exercise: who should be able to do each action?","buckets":["Analyst","Pipeline service principal","Platform admins group (owner / MANAGE)"],"items":[{"text":"read Gold","bucket":0},{"text":"write Gold","bucket":1},{"text":"read Silver","bucket":1},{"text":"create table (in the pipeline's target schema)","bucket":1},{"text":"write the ingestion volume","bucket":1},{"text":"manage grants","bucket":2}],"explain":"Analysts read Gold only. The pipeline SP reads Silver, writes Gold, creates its tables and writes landing files (in practice the ingest SP writes the volume). Managing grants is governance: owner group / MANAGE holders — neither analysts nor pipelines."},{"id":"ch11-e128","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"With the analyst grants from the lesson (USE CATALOG prod_ecommerce, USE SCHEMA + SELECT on gold), what happens to `SELECT * FROM prod_ecommerce.silver.orders` for an analyst?","options":["Denied — no USE SCHEMA / SELECT on silver","Allowed — USE CATALOG covers all schemas","Allowed — SELECT on gold is inherited by silver","Allowed but masked"],"answer":0,"explain":"Grants are scoped: Gold yes, Silver no. USE CATALOG is only a prerequisite, and grants flow **down** a hierarchy, never sideways between sibling schemas."},{"id":"ch11-e129","type":"write","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the grants so `gold_pipeline_sp` can read every table in `prod_ecommerce.silver` and write to `prod_ecommerce.gold` — nothing else.","solution":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.silver TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.silver TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;\nGRANT MODIFY ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO `gold_pipeline_sp`;","keywords":["grant use catalog on catalog prod_ecommerce","grant use schema on schema prod_ecommerce.silver","grant select on schema prod_ecommerce.silver","grant use schema on schema prod_ecommerce.gold","grant modify on schema prod_ecommerce.gold","`gold_pipeline_sp`"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Read Silver (USE SCHEMA + SELECT) and write Gold (USE SCHEMA + MODIFY), with one USE CATALOG. Schema-level grants inherit to all tables."},{"id":"ch11-e130","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Grants for analysts in production. Which line violates least privilege?","lines":["GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce TO `analysts`;","GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;","GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;","GRANT MODIFY ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO `analysts`;"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"-- remove: analysts only read; writes go to service principals","explain":"Analysts need to **read** Gold. MODIFY lets them change production data; Databricks recommends reserving direct writes for service principals."},{"id":"ch11-e131","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"`GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON CATALOG prod_ecommerce TO all_company_users` is acceptable as long as you review it once a year, because ALL PRIVILEGES is a fixed list.","answer":false,"explain":"False twice: it violates least privilege, and ALL PRIVILEGES is **evaluated dynamically** — new applicable privileges are included automatically as they're added."},{"id":"ch11-e132","type":"match","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Mini-project configuration: match the object kind to its name.","pairs":[["Catalog","prod_ecommerce"],["Volume","prod_ecommerce.raw_files.incoming"],["Gold table","prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue"],["Service principal","orders_ingest_sp"],["Metastore","Europe"]],"explain":"One regional metastore (Europe), one production catalog with bronze/silver/gold/raw_files schemas, tables per layer, a landing volume, and service principals for ingest and transform."},{"id":"ch11-e133","type":"match","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 2: match the inspection command to what it lists.","pairs":[["SHOW CATALOGS;","all catalogs you can see"],["SHOW SCHEMAS IN ecommerce_dev;","schemas of one catalog"],["SHOW TABLES IN ecommerce_dev.silver;","tables of one schema"],["DESCRIBE TABLE ecommerce_dev.silver.orders;","columns and types of one table"]],"explain":"Top-down navigation: catalogs → schemas → tables → columns. Basic UC navigation from SQL."},{"id":"ch11-e134","type":"write","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 1: create catalog `ecommerce_dev` (if missing), schema `files` in it (if missing) and the managed volume `incoming`.","solution":"CREATE CATALOG IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev;\nCREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce_dev.files;\nCREATE VOLUME ecommerce_dev.files.incoming;","keywords":["create catalog if not exists ecommerce_dev","create schema if not exists ecommerce_dev.files","create volume ecommerce_dev.files.incoming"],"lang":"sql","explain":"No LOCATION → managed volume under the catalog/schema managed storage."},{"id":"ch11-e135","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 3: complete the grant practice for `training_analysts`, then inspect.","text":"GRANT [[USE CATALOG]] ON CATALOG ecommerce_dev TO `training_analysts`;\nGRANT [[USE SCHEMA]] ON SCHEMA ecommerce_dev.gold TO `training_analysts`;\nGRANT [[SELECT]] ON SCHEMA ecommerce_dev.gold TO `training_analysts`;\n[[SHOW GRANTS]] ON SCHEMA ecommerce_dev.gold;","bank":["MODIFY","BROWSE","DESCRIBE"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Namespace first (USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA), then data (SELECT on the schema, inherited by its tables), then verify with SHOW GRANTS."},{"id":"ch11-e136","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 4 grants `data_engineers` USE SCHEMA on `ecommerce_dev.files` and READ VOLUME, WRITE VOLUME on `incoming` — and nothing else. What could still block `open(\"/Volumes/ecommerce_dev/files/incoming/test.txt\", \"w\")`?","options":["Missing USE CATALOG on `ecommerce_dev`","Missing MODIFY on the volume","Missing SELECT on the schema","Volumes can't be written from Python"],"answer":0,"explain":"The source lab omits it, but the read/write path needs **USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + READ VOLUME (+ WRITE VOLUME)**. MODIFY/SELECT are table privileges; Python `open()` on /Volumes paths is supported."},{"id":"ch11-e137","type":"order","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the 8 layers of the permission-debugging algorithm.","items":["Which principal actually executes?","Workspace / catalog binding","USE CATALOG","USE SCHEMA","SELECT (object privilege)","Row filter / column mask / ABAC","External-storage permissions (if relevant)","UC-compatible compute"],"explain":"I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions: Identity → Binding → Catalog → Schema → Object → Fine-grained → Storage → Compute. Identity is first because every later check depends on WHO is being authorized."},{"id":"ch11-e138","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the mnemonic layers (I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions).","text":"[[Identity]] → [[Binding]] → Catalog → Schema → [[Object]] → [[Fine-grained|Fine grained]] → Storage → [[Compute]]","bank":["Ownership","Cluster size","Photon"],"explain":"Identity (actual principal), Binding (workspace), Catalog (USE CATALOG), Schema (USE SCHEMA), Object (SELECT/MODIFY…), Fine-grained (filters/masks/ABAC), Storage (external location/IAM), Compute (UC-compatible)."},{"id":"ch11-e139","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You hit PERMISSION_DENIED on `prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue`. What is the FIRST question of the algorithm?","options":["Which principal is actually executing this?","Do I have SELECT?","Is the table external?","Can I get admin?"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Step 5 — and only meaningful for the right identity.","Step 7 — only relevant for external data.","Not a debugging step; breaks least privilege."],"explain":"Identity first: all grants are evaluated for the **executing** principal. Checking SELECT for yourself is pointless if a job runs as a service principal. Asking for admin isn't debugging."},{"id":"ch11-e140","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A scheduled job writing `prod_ecommerce.gold.daily_revenue` fails with PERMISSION_DENIED. It worked yesterday. Walk the algorithm.","steps":[{"prompt":"Step 1?","options":[{"text":"Check the job's run-as principal (did someone change it to a different SP?).","ok":true,"fb":"Identity first."},{"text":"Check that YOU have MODIFY on gold.","ok":false,"fb":"You're not the one executing."},{"text":"Enlarge the cluster.","ok":false,"fb":"Not an authorization layer."}]},{"prompt":"Run-as is still `gold_pipeline_sp`. You're in the prod workspace on a serverless job. Next?","options":[{"text":"SHOW GRANTS on catalog, schema gold and the table for `gold_pipeline_sp` (USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, MODIFY).","ok":true,"fb":"Catalog → schema → object for the right principal."},{"text":"Look at the dashboard's lineage.","ok":false,"fb":"Lineage helps with wrong values, not with denied writes."},{"text":"Drop and recreate the table.","ok":false,"fb":"Destructive and doesn't address authorization."}]},{"prompt":"You find a REVOKE MODIFY ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold FROM gold_pipeline_sp in yesterday's audit. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Restore `GRANT MODIFY ON SCHEMA prod_ecommerce.gold TO gold_pipeline_sp` (after confirming it was a mistake).","ok":true,"fb":"Restore exactly the missing privilege — least privilege preserved."},{"text":"Make the job run as your personal user.","ok":false,"fb":"Production writes should not depend on a personal identity."},{"text":"Grant ALL PRIVILEGES on the catalog to the SP.","ok":false,"fb":"Way beyond what's needed."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e141","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Map each symptom to the algorithm layer you should suspect first.","buckets":["Identity","Binding","Fine-grained policy","Storage / Compute"],"items":[{"text":"notebook works, scheduled job fails","bucket":0},{"text":"same grants, works in prod-workspace, fails in dev-workspace","bucket":1},{"text":"query succeeds but salary shows ****","bucket":2},{"text":"GR analyst sees only GR rows","bucket":2},{"text":"managed table OK, external table storage error","bucket":3},{"text":"same user/grants, fails only on an old legacy cluster","bucket":3}],"explain":"Symptoms point to layers: a different executor → identity; different workspace → binding; succeeded-but-altered results → fine-grained; external-only or compute-only failures → storage/compute."},{"id":"ch11-e142","type":"spotbug","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A junior's runbook for PERMISSION_DENIED. Which step is in the wrong place?","lines":["1. Check SELECT on the table","2. Which principal actually executes?","3. Check USE CATALOG","4. Check USE SCHEMA","5. Check filters / masks / ABAC"],"bugs":[0],"fix":"Move 'Which principal actually executes?' to step 1 (then binding, USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT …)","explain":"Checking SELECT first — for whom? Identity must come first, and the object privilege comes **after** the namespace prerequisites (USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA)."},{"id":"ch11-e143","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"The identity that presses \"Run\" on a job is always the identity the job uses to access data.","answer":false,"explain":"False. The job may **run as** a service principal (e.g. `orders-etl-sp`) with different UC privileges. Check the run-as identity."},{"id":"ch11-e144","type":"scenario","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your notebook reads `prod.silver.orders` fine. The same code as a scheduled job fails with PERMISSION_DENIED.","steps":[{"prompt":"First question?","options":[{"text":"Which identity does the job run as?","ok":true,"fb":"The classic Case 1."},{"text":"Is my SELECT still there?","ok":false,"fb":"Your notebook works — your SELECT is fine."},{"text":"Is the table corrupted?","ok":false,"fb":"Then your notebook would fail too."}]},{"prompt":"Job runs as `orders-etl-sp`. Next?","options":[{"text":"SHOW GRANTS for `orders-etl-sp` on catalog, schema and table.","ok":true,"fb":"Check the right principal's chain."},{"text":"Change run-as to your own user so it works.","ok":false,"fb":"Production shouldn't depend on a personal account (it breaks when you leave)."},{"text":"Grant yourself more privileges.","ok":false,"fb":"You're not the one being denied."}]},{"prompt":"`orders-etl-sp` lacks SELECT on the table. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Grant the SP exactly what the job needs (USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT …).","ok":true,"fb":"Least privilege for the machine identity."},{"text":"Grant ALL PRIVILEGES on prod to the SP.","ok":false,"fb":"Over-privileged."},{"text":"Add the SP to the `admins` group.","ok":false,"fb":"Over-privileged and opaque."}]}]},{"id":"ch11-e145","type":"order","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Case 1 — order the steps when a job fails but your notebook works.","items":["Notice: same code, different executor","Open the job settings and find the run-as identity","SHOW GRANTS for that service principal at catalog → schema → object","Grant the missing privilege(s) to the SP","Re-run the job"],"explain":"The job's identity is different from yours. Find it, inspect its grants, and grant only what's missing."},{"id":"ch11-e146","type":"free","section":"ch11-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"In your own words: what does \"think the whole authorization chain, not just SELECT\" mean?","model":"A permission error can come from any layer: the wrong executing identity (job run-as), a workspace binding, missing USE CATALOG or USE SCHEMA, the object privilege itself (or an inherited grant), fine-grained policies (row filters, column masks, ABAC), the external storage chain (external location, credential, cloud IAM), or non-UC-compatible compute. So I walk the layers in order instead of assuming SELECT is missing or asking for admin.","rubric":["Identity / run-as","Binding","USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA","Object privilege incl. inheritance","Fine-grained policies","Storage chain","Compute compatibility","Not 'ask for admin'"],"explain":"The Phase-3 outcome: a permission error is a chain, and you walk it in order."},{"id":"ch11-e147","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Q1. What is the top-level Unity Catalog object?","options":["Metastore","Catalog","Workspace","Account"],"answer":0,"explain":"The **metastore** is the top-level UC container for metadata and permissions. Catalogs live inside it; workspaces and accounts aren't UC namespace objects."},{"id":"ch11-e148","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Q2. What does `prod.finance.payroll` mean?","text":"catalog = [[prod]], schema = [[finance]], object = [[payroll]]","explain":"Three-level namespace: catalog.schema.object."},{"id":"ch11-e149","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q3. The workspace is above the catalog in the Unity Catalog namespace.","answer":false,"explain":"No. The workspace is an operational environment; the catalog lives inside a UC **metastore**."},{"id":"ch11-e150","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q4. Multiple workspaces can use one metastore.","answer":true,"explain":"Yes — typically within the same regional metastore architecture, subject to workspace bindings and permissions."},{"id":"ch11-e151","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q5. What is a securable object?","options":["A Unity Catalog object on which privileges can be granted to principals","Any encrypted file in S3","A user, group or service principal","A cluster with Dedicated access mode"],"answer":0,"explain":"Securable = UC object you can grant on. The principal is the WHO; encryption and clusters are unrelated."},{"id":"ch11-e152","type":"odd","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q6. A principal receives permissions. Which one is NOT a principal?","options":["user","group","service principal","schema"],"answer":3,"explain":"Principals are users, groups and service principals. A schema is a **securable**."},{"id":"ch11-e153","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q7. USE CATALOG lets me SELECT every table in the catalog.","answer":false,"explain":"No. It's only a traversal prerequisite."},{"id":"ch11-e154","type":"order","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q8. The typical read path — order the grants from outermost to innermost.","items":["USE CATALOG","USE SCHEMA","SELECT"],"explain":"Catalog → schema → object."},{"id":"ch11-e155","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q9. Why are groups preferred to individual grants?","options":["They centralize membership management and reduce permission sprawl","Groups get higher query priority","Individual users can't receive grants","Groups bypass row filters"],"answer":0,"explain":"One grant per group, membership changes instead of grant changes. Users **can** receive grants — it just doesn't scale."},{"id":"ch11-e156","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q10. Why use service principals for production pipelines?","options":["Stable machine identity, decoupled from individual employee lifecycle","They're cheaper per DBU","They skip Unity Catalog checks","They can't be granted privileges"],"answer":0,"explain":"If an employee leaves, a personal-identity pipeline breaks; an SP doesn't. SPs are fully subject to UC grants."},{"id":"ch11-e157","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q11. What is ownership?","options":["An administrative/control relationship with an object, including broad management and grant-delegation capabilities","Another name for SELECT","The workspace where the object was created","Write access to the files"],"answer":0,"explain":"Owners can manage the object, manage privileges and transfer ownership — far more than SELECT."},{"id":"ch11-e158","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q12. Is ALL PRIVILEGES literally every possible privilege? Which are excluded? (choose all)","options":["MANAGE","EXTERNAL USE LOCATION","EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA","SELECT","MODIFY"],"answer":[0,1,2],"multi":true,"explain":"No — MANAGE, EXTERNAL USE LOCATION and EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA are excluded."},{"id":"ch11-e159","type":"bucket","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q13. Managed vs external: who controls the storage lifecycle?","buckets":["UC controls governance + storage lifecycle","UC governance only; you/external system control lifecycle"],"items":[{"text":"managed table","bucket":0},{"text":"external table","bucket":1},{"text":"managed volume","bucket":0},{"text":"external volume","bucket":1}],"explain":"Managed → UC owns location and lifecycle. External → UC governs, you control location and lifecycle."},{"id":"ch11-e160","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q14. Dropping an external table deletes its files.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Underlying files remain; only UC metadata is removed."},{"id":"ch11-e161","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q15. What happens to the files when you drop a MANAGED table?","options":["Unity Catalog manages their deletion as part of the managed lifecycle","They always remain forever","They move to the external location","They become a volume"],"answer":0,"explain":"For managed tables UC owns the file lifecycle, including deletion after DROP."},{"id":"ch11-e162","type":"match","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q16–Q18. Match each question to its answer.","pairs":[["Storage credential?","governed abstraction of a cloud authentication identity"],["External location?","cloud path + storage credential, as a securable"],["Why grant on an external location, not the raw credential?","better path-scoped least privilege"]],"explain":"Credential = identity, location = path + identity, grants on location = path-scoped least privilege."},{"id":"ch11-e163","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q19. What is a volume?","options":["A Unity Catalog governed container for non-tabular files","A Delta table without schema","A disk attached to the driver","A backup of a catalog"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volumes govern files (PDFs, images, raw files) with /Volumes/... paths."},{"id":"ch11-e164","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q20. Volume read privileges?","text":"USE [[CATALOG]] + USE [[SCHEMA]] + [[READ VOLUME]]","bank":["SELECT","WRITE VOLUME","MODIFY"],"explain":"Same prerequisites as tables, but the object privilege is READ VOLUME."},{"id":"ch11-e165","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q21. Table permissions and volume permissions are identical.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Tables use e.g. SELECT, MODIFY; volumes use READ VOLUME, WRITE VOLUME."},{"id":"ch11-e166","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q22. What is a workspace binding?","options":["A restriction specifying which workspaces can access certain UC objects (catalogs, external locations, storage credentials)","Attaching a notebook to a cluster","The link between an account and its billing","A grant from a workspace admin to a user"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bindings restrict where (from which workspaces) catalogs, external locations and credentials can be used — independent of user grants."},{"id":"ch11-e167","type":"tf","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Q28. Unity Catalog captures column-level lineage.","answer":true,"explain":"Yes, for supported Databricks queries/workloads."},{"id":"ch11-e168","type":"cloze","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Q29. Fill in the likely debugging sequence for PERMISSION_DENIED.","text":"actual [[principal]] → workspace [[binding]] → USE CATALOG → USE SCHEMA → object [[privilege]] → [[fine-grained]] policies → storage/external location → [[compute]] compatibility","bank":["owner","cluster size","region"],"explain":"Identity, binding, catalog, schema, object, fine-grained, storage, compute — I Bet Cats Sit On Fluffy Silk Cushions."},{"id":"ch11-e169","type":"mcq","section":"ch11-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Q30. Why can a job fail although your notebook succeeds?","options":["The job may execute as a different service principal with different UC privileges","Jobs ignore Unity Catalog","Notebooks cache permissions forever","Jobs can only read managed tables"],"answer":0,"explain":"Different executor, different grants — Case 1 of the algorithm."}],"src":"part2"},{"id":"ch12","num":12,"title":"Data Ingestion: Files, COPY INTO & Auto Loader","subtitle":"spark.read, schemas, COPY INTO, Auto Loader, checkpoints, schema evolution, rescued data — and debugging every ingestion incident","emoji":"📥","sourcePages":"456–510","mantra":"Land files faithfully and exactly once: the reader needs a grammar (schema), the loader needs a memory (COPY INTO tracking or a checkpoint), and business duplicates are Silver's job.","objectives":["You can read CSV, JSON and Parquet with spark.read, set the right parsing options and choose explicit schema (StructType or DDL) over inference for production.","You can explain why re-running spark.read duplicates data and how COPY INTO's file tracking makes loads retryable and idempotent — including the 'changed file is still skipped' subtlety.","You can write COPY INTO with FILEFORMAT, FORMAT_OPTIONS, COPY_OPTIONS, PATTERN, FILES, VALIDATE and a SELECT transformation, and tell the two mergeSchema options apart.","You can write a production Auto Loader Bronze stream (cloudFiles, schemaLocation, checkpointLocation, toTable, availableNow) and explain schemaLocation vs checkpointLocation.","You can predict Auto Loader's schema inference (strings by default, 50 GB / 1000-file sample, hints, inferColumnTypes), schema evolution modes and rescued data.","You can explain why file-level exactly-once does not prevent duplicate business keys, and where dedup/MERGE belongs.","You can choose between spark.read, COPY INTO and Auto Loader and answer the classic exam traps.","You can debug missing rows, overwritten files, schema shifts, missing columns, duplicate IDs, malformed CSV and checkpoint incidents with an ordered checklist."],"sections":[{"id":"ch12-s01","title":"Ingestion: The Problem & the Ladder","hook":"Almost every pipeline starts with files you didn't write — this chapter is how you land them safely, once.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Ingestion** is the process by which data that lives *outside* your target platform is brought *into* it — e.g. into a Delta table governed by Unity Catalog."},{"t":"diagram","text":" External system\n   ├── CSV files\n   ├── JSON\n   ├── Parquet\n   ├── API export          ──►  ingestion  ──►  Delta table\n   ├── Kafka                                    (Databricks)\n   ├── Database\n   └── object storage","caption":"Many sources, one goal: a reliable Delta table"},{"t":"p","text":"This chapter (Phase 4) focuses on **file-based ingestion**: data that arrives as files in cloud storage or a Unity Catalog Volume."},{"t":"p","text":"**Running example.** An e-shop's operational system writes a new JSON file every **5 minutes** into `s3://company/orders/` — `orders_2026_09_28_0001.json`, `…_0002.json`, `…_0003.json` …"},{"t":"flow","items":["new files","Bronze","Silver","Gold"],"caption":"Where the files must end up"},{"t":"p","text":"The first problem is deceptively simple: **how do I read these files — and only the ones I haven't read yet?**"},{"t":"diagram","text":" data lives somewhere\n        ↓\n I must DISCOVER it\n        ↓\n I must READ it\n        ↓\n I must INTERPRET it with a schema\n        ↓\n I must REMEMBER what I already processed\n        ↓\n I must WRITE it reliably to Delta","caption":"The six questions every ingestion pipeline answers"},{"t":"diagram","text":" FILES → spark.read → schema → batch ingestion\n       → COPY INTO → Auto Loader → incremental discovery\n       → checkpoint + schemaLocation → schema evolution\n       → rescued data → production debugging","caption":"The ladder of this chapter: lowest abstraction first"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Recap: you've met the outlines before","text":"Chapter 2 introduced batch vs incremental, checkpoints, schema enforcement/evolution and the medallion layers; Chapter 10 previewed Phase 4. Here every one of those ideas gets its real syntax, its failure modes and its debugging checklist."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: the orders folder gains 288 files a day. Name three things your loader must decide or remember.","text":"(1) **Which files are new** (discovery), (2) **what shape they have** (schema), (3) **what it already loaded** (state), so a retry or tomorrow's run doesn't load yesterday's files again."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Ingestion","def":"Bringing data from outside the platform into it (here: files → Delta)."},{"term":"File-based ingestion","def":"The source delivers data as files (CSV/JSON/Parquet…) in object storage or a Volume."},{"term":"Landing zone","def":"The storage path/Volume where source files arrive before ingestion."},{"term":"Incremental ingestion","def":"Each run processes only what is new since the last run."},{"term":"Reproducible","def":"Re-running the pipeline on the same inputs gives the same table — no surprises, no duplicates."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-s02","title":"spark.read & CSV Parsing Options","hook":"A CSV file is a grammar — if you don't tell the parser the rules, it reads nonsense without complaining.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"`spark.read` is the most basic PySpark ingestion API. It is a **batch read**: it reads what is in the path *right now*."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.read.format(\"json\").load(path)"},{"t":"flow","items":["path currently contains files","Spark enumerates input","reads them","returns a DataFrame"],"caption":"What a batch read does"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"orders = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"json\")\n        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/incoming/orders/\")\n)\n\n# shorter, identical:\norders = spark.read.json(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/incoming/orders/\")","caption":"Long form vs shorthand"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Two spellings, one reader","text":"`spark.read.format(\"csv\").load(p)` ≡ `spark.read.csv(p)`; same for `json` and `parquet`. The long form is handy when the format is a variable."},{"t":"p","text":"**CSV** needs parsing options. The `header` option says: *the first line holds the column names*."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n        .load(path)\n)","caption":"CSV with header"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"order_id,customer_id,amount\n1001,17,25.90\n1002,18,10.50","caption":"orders.csv"},{"t":"table","head":["header option","Column names","What happens to line 1"],"rows":[["\"true\"","order_id, customer_id, amount","Used as names"],["not set / \"false\"","_c0, _c1, _c2","Read as a **data row** (\"order_id\",\"customer_id\",\"amount\")"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Delimiter**: CSV doesn't always mean comma. A pipe-separated file needs `.option(\"delimiter\", \"|\")` (alias `sep`)."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"# file:  1001|17|25.90\n#        1002|18|10.50\ndf = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"delimiter\", \"|\")\n        .load(path)\n)","caption":"Pipe-delimited CSV"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":".option(\"header\", \"true\")\n.option(\"delimiter\", \",\")\n.option(\"quote\", '\"')\n.option(\"escape\", '\"')\n.option(\"multiLine\", \"true\")\n.option(\"nullValue\", \"NULL\")","caption":"Options you'll meet constantly"},{"t":"table","head":["Option","Tells the parser…","Example problem it solves"],"rows":[["header","line 1 = column names","columns named _c0, _c1…"],["delimiter / sep","the field separator","`|` or `;` files read as 1 column"],["quote","which char wraps a field","`\"Athens, GR\"` split into 2 fields"],["escape","how a quote inside a quoted field is escaped","`\"He said \"\"hi\"\"\"` breaks parsing"],["multiLine","a quoted field may contain newlines","an address with a line break becomes 2 rows"],["nullValue","which text means NULL","the string 'NULL' stored as text"],["mode","what to do with malformed rows","PERMISSIVE / DROPMALFORMED / FAILFAST"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**`mode`** (added for clarity — you need it to debug bad CSV): **PERMISSIVE** (default) keeps the row and sets unparsable fields to null; **DROPMALFORMED** silently drops bad rows; **FAILFAST** throws on the first bad row. In PERMISSIVE you can keep the raw bad line with `columnNameOfCorruptRecord` (e.g. `_corrupt_record`)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The file format is a grammar","text":"Don't memorize every option. Learn to think: *what is the structure of this file, and have I told the parser?* Separator, quoting, escaping, line breaks, null spelling, header."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"No header option → header becomes data","text":"Reading a CSV with a header line but without `.option(\"header\",\"true\")` gives columns `_c0, _c1…` AND an extra data row containing the column names — which later fails every cast."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"CSV without schema = all STRING","text":"Plain CSV has no native types. With neither `.schema(...)` nor `inferSchema=true`, every CSV column is read as **STRING**."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you read `1001|17|25.90` with default CSV options. How many columns do you get?","text":"**One** column `_c0` containing the whole line `1001|17|25.90` — the parser looked for commas, found none. No error is raised: wrong grammar silently gives wrong data."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a CSV reads wrong","questions":["Did I set `header` — and does the file actually have a header line?","Is the separator really a comma, or `|`, `;`, tab?","Do fields contain the separator or newlines inside quotes (quote / escape / multiLine)?","How does the source spell NULL (`NULL`, empty, `NA`)?","Which `mode` decides what happens to malformed rows — and am I silently dropping or nulling data?"]}]},{"id":"ch12-s03","title":"Schema Inference vs Explicit Schema","hook":"Guessing types costs an extra pass over the data — and tomorrow's guess may differ from today's.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"If you don't give a schema, Spark can **infer** one. For CSV you must ask for it with `inferSchema`."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = (\n    spark.read\n        .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n        .option(\"inferSchema\", \"true\")\n        .csv(path)\n)"},{"t":"table","head":["Input column","Sample values","Inferred type"],"rows":[["id","1, 2","INT"],["amount","15.2, 20.1","DOUBLE"]]},{"t":"p","text":"Why does inference **cost** something? To know whether `\"123\"` is INT, LONG or STRING, Spark must **look at the data** first."},{"t":"flow","items":["inspect input","decide types","then read the actual data"],"caption":"Inference = an extra pass"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Explicit schema skips the inference pass","text":"Databricks documentation says it explicitly: supplying a schema lets the reader **skip schema inference**, which speeds up the read."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql.types import (\n    StructType, StructField,\n    LongType, DoubleType, StringType, TimestampType,\n)\n\nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"order_id\",    LongType(),      True),\n    StructField(\"customer_id\", LongType(),      True),\n    StructField(\"amount\",      DoubleType(),    True),\n    StructField(\"status\",      StringType(),    True),\n    StructField(\"order_ts\",    TimestampType(), True),\n])\n\ndf = spark.read.schema(schema).json(path)","caption":"Explicit schema with StructType (the 3rd arg = nullable)"},{"t":"p","text":"With `.schema(...)` you say: **\"Don't guess. This is the contract.\"**"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"schema = \"\"\"\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      DOUBLE,\n  status      STRING,\n  order_ts    TIMESTAMP\n\"\"\"\n\ndf = spark.read.schema(schema).json(path)","caption":"Same contract as a DDL-formatted string"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"schema = \"\"\"\n  order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount DOUBLE,   status STRING\n\"\"\"\n\ndf = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n        .schema(schema)\n        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/\")\n)\ndisplay(df)","caption":"Lab: CSV ingestion with an explicit schema"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks accepts a schema either as a **StructType** or as a **DDL string**. The DDL string is often more readable and easy to review in Git."},{"t":"table","head":["DDL type","StructType class"],"rows":[["BIGINT","LongType()"],["INT","IntegerType()"],["DOUBLE","DoubleType()"],["STRING","StringType()"],["TIMESTAMP","TimestampType()"]]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Schema inference","points":["Convenient for **exploratory** work","Extra pass over the data","Types can change between runs","Nobody reviewed the contract"]},{"title":"Explicit schema","points":["Default choice for **production** ingestion","**Faster** (no inference pass)","**Predictable** and **version-controlled**","No accidental type surprises; easier validation"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":" File 1 (Mon)        File 2 (Tue)\n amount              amount\n 100                 100.50\n 200\n   ↓ infer             ↓ infer\n amount: INT         amount: DOUBLE   ← type changed!","caption":"The schema-inference bug: same column, different guess"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Inference is a moving target","text":"Monday's files make Spark infer `amount INT`; Tuesday's file has `100.50` → the inferred type changes (or parsing breaks). Downstream writes then hit schema mismatches."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Engineering question: why explicit schema in production?","text":"It **avoids an inference pass**, gives a **stable data contract**, makes **failures predictable**, prevents **accidental type drift**, and lets schema changes be **reviewed** instead of silently inferred."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: with an explicit schema `amount DOUBLE`, a row arrives with `amount = \"abc\"`. What happens in the default PERMISSIVE mode?","text":"The row is kept and `amount` becomes **null** (optionally the raw record goes to a corrupt-record or rescued-data column). No type drift — the contract held; the bad *value* is what you now investigate."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before relying on schema inference","questions":["Is this exploration or a production pipeline?","Would a different guess tomorrow (INT → DOUBLE) break a downstream write?","Who owns the contract with the upstream team — and is it written down?","Could a reviewer see a schema change in Git, or would it happen silently?"]}]},{"id":"ch12-s04","title":"JSON, Parquet & read_files()","hook":"Text formats need parsing, typed formats carry their schema — and SQL has its own file reader.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**JSON** (as read by Spark by default) is one object per line — *JSON Lines*:"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"{\"order_id\": 1, \"customer\": \"Maria\", \"amount\": 20.5}\n{\"order_id\": 2, \"customer\": \"John\",  \"amount\": 50.0}","caption":"orders.json"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = spark.read.json(path)                 # types inferred from values\ndf = spark.read.schema(schema).json(path)  # explicit contract"},{"t":"p","text":"JSON can be **nested**. A nested object becomes a **struct** column:"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"{\"order_id\": 1, \"customer\": {\"id\": 100, \"name\": \"Maria\"}}"},{"t":"diagram","text":" root\n  ├── order_id: long\n  └── customer: struct\n        ├── id: long\n        └── name: string","caption":"Inferred schema (printSchema) — access with customer.name"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"One record spread over many lines?","text":"Pretty-printed JSON (one object across several lines, or a big array) needs `.option(\"multiLine\", \"true\")`; the default reader expects one JSON object per line."},{"t":"p","text":"**Parquet** is typed: each file stores its **schema metadata**. `spark.read.parquet(path)` needs no CSV-style parsing options or type guessing."},{"t":"table","head":["Format","Nature","Where types come from"],"rows":[["CSV","textual","weak / none — you or inference decide"],["JSON","semi-structured (nested)","inferred from the content"],["Parquet","columnar, binary","schema encoded in the file"]]},{"t":"p","text":"That's why lakehouses typically **ingest raw CSV/JSON** and then write **Delta (Parquet-backed) tables**: from then on the types live with the data."},{"t":"p","text":"**`read_files()`** is Databricks' SQL table-valued function for reading files. It reads CSV, JSON, XML, text, binary, Parquet, Avro and ORC, and can infer format and schema."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *\nFROM read_files(\n  '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/',\n  format => 'json'\n);\n\nSELECT *\nFROM read_files(\n  '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/',\n  format => 'csv',\n  schema => 'order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE'\n);","caption":"read_files: inferred vs explicit schema (named args use =>)"},{"t":"p","text":"Where you'll use it: **Databricks SQL**, **Lakeflow pipelines**, and **ad-hoc file exploration**."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze_orders\nAS\nSELECT *\nFROM STREAM read_files(\n  '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/',\n  format => 'json'\n);","caption":"Preview of Phase 6: read_files inside a streaming table"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"STREAM read_files = Auto Loader underneath","text":"When `read_files` is used with `STREAM` in a streaming table, Databricks runs **Auto Loader** for incremental file ingestion. Everything you learn about Auto Loader (checkpoints, schema inference, rescued data) applies."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why does Parquet not need a `header` or `inferSchema` option?","text":"Because the **schema is stored in the file** itself (column names + types in the footer). There's nothing to guess and no header line to interpret."}]},{"id":"ch12-s05","title":"Metadata Columns: Which File Did This Row Come From?","hook":"When a bad row shows up in Silver, the first question is 'which file?' — make Bronze able to answer.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When you ingest files you often want to know: **from which file did this row come? when was the file created? which source sent it?**"},{"t":"diagram","text":" business data          +   ingestion metadata\n order_id, amount           _source_file, _ingested_at","caption":"A Bronze row = what the source said + how it got here"},{"t":"p","text":"Spark file sources expose a hidden **`_metadata`** column. You must **select it explicitly** — `SELECT *` doesn't include it."},{"t":"table","head":["Field","Meaning"],"rows":[["_metadata.file_path","Full path of the source file"],["_metadata.file_name","File name only"],["_metadata.file_size","Size in bytes"],["_metadata.file_modification_time","Last modification timestamp of the file"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\nbronze_df = (\n    spark.read.schema(schema).json(path)\n        .select(\n            \"*\",\n            F.col(\"_metadata.file_path\").alias(\"_source_file\"),\n            F.current_timestamp().alias(\"_ingested_at\"),\n        )\n)","caption":"Adding audit columns (works the same on an Auto Loader stream)"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT *, _metadata.file_path AS _source_file\nFROM read_files('/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/', format => 'json');","caption":"Same idea in SQL"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Legacy spelling","text":"Older code uses `input_file_name()`. Prefer `_metadata.file_path`: it's the current, documented way and also gives size and modification time."},{"t":"diagram","text":" Silver bad row\n      ↓  (keys / lineage)\n Bronze source row  ── _source_file = .../orders_0912.json\n      ↓\n original file in the landing zone","caption":"Audit trail that metadata columns make possible"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Source-file metadata = audit evidence","text":"Good Bronze systems add `_source_file` and `_ingestion_ts`/`_ingested_at`. When someone asks *\"where did this bad record come from?\"*, you answer with a query, not a guess. That's operational observability."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"_metadata isn't in SELECT *","text":"The `_metadata` column is hidden: `select(\"*\")` or `SELECT *` won't return it, so audit columns silently go missing."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a Silver row looks wrong","questions":["Which Bronze row produced it (same business key, same batch)?","What is that Bronze row's `_source_file`?","When was it ingested (`_ingested_at`) — before or after the incident?","Is the original file still in the landing zone, and unchanged?"]}]},{"id":"ch12-s06","title":"The Re-read Problem → COPY INTO Idempotency","hook":"spark.read has no memory: run it twice and you load everything twice. COPY INTO remembers files.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Day 1: folder `orders/` has `file1.json`, `file2.json`. You run `spark.read.json(path)` and append to Bronze. Day 2 a `file3.json` lands. You run the same code. **What does it read?**"},{"t":"diagram","text":" Day 1 run           Day 2 run\n orders/             orders/\n ├── file1  ✔        ├── file1  ✔  (again!)\n └── file2  ✔        ├── file2  ✔  (again!)\n                     └── file3  ✔","caption":"spark.read reads the whole current folder every time"},{"t":"p","text":"**All three files** — not just `file3`. Appended to Bronze, file1 and file2 are now **duplicated**. That's the core problem of incremental ingestion."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"(\n    df.write\n      .mode(\"append\")\n      .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)\n# run twice over the same files  →  duplicate rows","caption":"Lab: plain batch load — run it twice on purpose"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"No file-history memory","text":"Plain `spark.read` keeps **no record** of which files it has seen. Re-reading the same source file and appending = duplicates. The duplication is the intended lesson of the lab."},{"t":"p","text":"**Naive fix**: restrict the path by date, e.g. `path = \"/orders/2026-09-28/*.json\"`. It works only if the **upstream storage layout is perfectly reliable**."},{"t":"list","items":["**Late files** — yesterday's file lands after today's run started","**Backfills** — upstream re-delivers last month","**Re-uploads** — same day folder gets a corrected file","**Timezone bugs** — '2026-09-28' in UTC vs local time","**Renamed files** — naming convention changes","**Missing expected folders** — the job fails or silently loads nothing"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Date-folder filtering is fragile incremental logic","text":"Filtering by filename/date assumes a perfect upstream layout. Late files, backfills, re-uploads, timezone bugs, renames and missing folders all cause skipped or duplicated data."},{"t":"p","text":"**`COPY INTO`** is the first Databricks ingestion primitive with memory. It loads files into a **Delta table** and is **retryable and idempotent**: files already loaded are **skipped** on later runs."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/'\nFILEFORMAT = JSON;"},{"t":"diagram","text":" Run 1: file1, file2 present  → load file1, load file2\n Run 2: nothing new           → skip file1, skip file2\n file3.json lands\n Run 3:                       → skip file1, skip file2, load file3","caption":"Idempotent = safe to re-run"},{"t":"p","text":"Compare that with naive *read the entire folder every run*: re-running COPY INTO after a failure or by accident doesn't duplicate anything."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"A changed file is still 'already loaded'","text":"Databricks: if a file was already loaded, COPY INTO **skips it on later runs even if the file's contents have changed**. Same tracked file identity ≠ 'reload because content changed'."},{"t":"p","text":"**`force`** (added for clarity): `COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')` disables the idempotency check and loads the files again *regardless* of history — useful for a deliberate reload, dangerous by accident (duplicates)."},{"t":"p","text":"The **target must be a Delta table**. You can create it with a schema first:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw (\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      DOUBLE,\n  status      STRING\n);\n\nCOPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/'\nFILEFORMAT = JSON;"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS (\n  'header'    = 'true',\n  'delimiter' = ','\n);","caption":"CSV COPY INTO: parser options go in FORMAT_OPTIONS"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true', 'inferSchema' = 'true');\n-- run it twice: the 2nd run skips the already-loaded files","caption":"Lab: clear/rebuild the table, then run COPY INTO twice"},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you re-run the exact same COPY INTO 5 minutes later and no new files arrived. How many rows are added?","text":"**Zero.** Every file is already in COPY INTO's load history, so all are skipped. The statement still succeeds — that's what idempotent means."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before re-running a load","questions":["Does this tool remember which files it loaded (COPY INTO / Auto Loader) or not (spark.read)?","If a file was corrected upstream, did it arrive as a NEW file or overwrite the old one?","Am I about to use `force` — and what will dedupe the duplicates it creates?","Is my target a Delta table?"]}]},{"id":"ch12-s07","title":"COPY INTO Options: PATTERN, FILES, VALIDATE, Transforms & Schema","hook":"COPY INTO isn't a byte copy: it filters, validates, casts and can evolve the target schema.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**PATTERN** filters which source files are loaded. Folder `incoming/` holds `orders_001.csv`, `orders_002.csv`, `customers_001.csv` — you want only orders:"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/incoming/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nPATTERN = 'orders_*.csv'\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true');"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"PATTERN is a glob, not a regex","text":"COPY INTO's PATTERN is a **glob pattern** (`*`, `?`, `[a-z]`, `{a,b}`). The tutoring notes wrote a regex-style `'orders_.*[.]csv'` — as a glob that means 'orders_.' + anything + '.csv' and would NOT match `orders_001.csv`."},{"t":"p","text":"**FILES** loads an explicit list of file names (relative to the FROM path) — up to **1000 files per statement**. FILES and PATTERN can't be combined in one statement."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/'\nFILEFORMAT = JSON\nFILES = ('file1.json', 'file2.json');"},{"t":"p","text":"**VALIDATE** checks the files **without writing data**: do they parse, is the schema compatible (or evolvable), do nullability and CHECK constraints hold?"},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true')\nVALIDATE ALL;      -- or: VALIDATE 15 ROWS","caption":"Lab: validate first, inspect the result, then load"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"A pre-production / debugging tool","text":"Run `VALIDATE ALL` against a new feed or a suspicious batch before the real load. Nothing is committed, so it's safe on production targets."},{"t":"p","text":"COPY INTO can **transform** while loading — the FROM can be a SELECT over the files: projection, casts, functions, even metadata."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM (\n  SELECT\n    order_id::BIGINT,\n    customer_id::BIGINT,\n    amount::DOUBLE,\n    upper(status)        AS status,\n    _metadata.file_path  AS _source_file\n  FROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/'\n)\nFILEFORMAT = JSON;"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Not a byte copy","text":"COPY INTO parses files and can project, cast and compute columns. The target still gets typed Delta rows."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/path/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('inferSchema' = 'true', 'mergeSchema' = 'true')\nCOPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true');","caption":"Inference + schema evolution in COPY INTO"},{"t":"table","head":["Where","mergeSchema means…"],"rows":[["FORMAT_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')","Merge the schemas of the **source files** (file A has col x, file B has col y → read both)"],["COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')","**Evolve the target Delta table's** schema to accept new columns"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Two different mergeSchema options","text":"`FORMAT_OPTIONS mergeSchema` reconciles schemas across source files; `COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema` evolves the target Delta schema. Setting only one may not give you new columns in the table."},{"t":"p","text":"Nice trick (added for clarity): you can create a **schemaless placeholder** Delta table with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw;` and let COPY INTO with `mergeSchema` set the schema on first load."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you use FILES with 2,500 file names. What happens?","text":"One statement accepts at most **1000** file names, so you split into **3 statements** (1000 + 1000 + 500) — or use a PATTERN / let idempotency pick new files instead."}]},{"id":"ch12-s08","title":"Auto Loader: cloudFiles, readStream & the Bronze Skeleton","hook":"When the folder holds millions of files and keeps growing, you need discovery with memory — that's Auto Loader.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Databricks' guidance on scale: **thousands of files over time → COPY INTO can be fine; millions+ → prefer Auto Loader**. Auto Loader discovers files more efficiently, processes them in incremental batches and has richer schema inference/evolution."},{"t":"p","text":"The problem: `orders/` holds **5,000,000** files and **+100** arrive every 10 seconds. A naive loader lists all 5,000,000 names each time to find the 100 new ones."},{"t":"diagram","text":" every trigger:\n   list 5,000,000 names  ──►  compare with what I loaded  ──►  100 new\n   (cost grows with the folder, not with the new data)","caption":"Naive discovery doesn't scale"},{"t":"p","text":"**Auto Loader** keeps **incremental state** about what it has already discovered and processed, so work scales with new arrivals."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"orders = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/\")\n)","caption":"Auto Loader read"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"`cloudFiles` = Auto Loader","text":"The magic identifier `format(\"cloudFiles\")` means *use Auto Loader*. The actual file format goes in `cloudFiles.format`."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why `readStream`?** Auto Loader is built on **Spark Structured Streaming**. That doesn't mean the source is Kafka or a live event feed — the source is **files**. The *processing model* is streaming: incremental arrivals → micro-batches of new files."},{"t":"flow","items":["incremental arrivals","micro-batches","new files only"],"caption":"Streaming as a processing model, not a source type"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\nsource_path     = \"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/\"\nschema_path     = \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/orders_schema/\"\ncheckpoint_path = \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/orders_checkpoint/\"\n\norders = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)\n        .load(source_path)\n        .withColumn(\"_ingested_at\", F.current_timestamp())\n)\n\nquery = (\n    orders.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint_path)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)","caption":"Production-style Bronze ingestion skeleton — memorize this shape"},{"t":"table","head":["Piece","Role"],"rows":[["readStream.format(\"cloudFiles\")","Auto Loader source"],["cloudFiles.format","Format of the files (json, csv, parquet…)"],["cloudFiles.schemaLocation","Where inferred schema + its history are stored"],[".load(source_path)","Landing-zone path to watch"],["writeStream.format(\"delta\")","Delta sink"],["checkpointLocation","Where stream progress/state is stored"],[".toTable(...)","Target Unity Catalog table"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Lab:** start the stream, drop a new file into `orders_json/`, and observe that **only the new arrival** is processed."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Misconception","points":["Auto Loader = a faster spark.read","Auto Loader turns files into Kafka"]},{"title":"Reality","points":["Its edge is **incremental discovery + state tracking + schema handling + streaming integration**, not reading each file faster","The **source stays files**; only the processing model is streaming/incremental"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Auto Loader is not 'faster spark.read'","text":"Auto Loader doesn't read an individual file faster. Its value is incremental file discovery, remembered state, schema handling and streaming integration."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: where does the word `json` go in an Auto Loader read — `.format(\"json\")`?","text":"No. `.format(\"cloudFiles\")` selects Auto Loader; the file format goes in **`.option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")`**. Writing `.format(\"json\")` with readStream gives a plain file stream without Auto Loader's features."}]},{"id":"ch12-s09","title":"schemaLocation vs checkpointLocation & What Happens Inside","hook":"Two folders, two memories: one remembers the SHAPE of the data, the other remembers HOW FAR you got.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An Auto Loader pipeline has **two state locations**. Beginners mix them up constantly."},{"t":"diagram","text":" cloudFiles.schemaLocation          checkpointLocation\n ─────────────────────────          ──────────────────────────\n \"What SHAPE does the data have?\"   \"How FAR has this stream got?\"\n  • schema I inferred                • files already processed\n  • how it evolved                   • offsets / batch ids\n  • schema versions  (_schemas/)     • stream metadata & state\n set on the READ (cloudFiles.*)     set on the WRITE (writeStream)","caption":"Different questions, different owners"},{"t":"p","text":"**schemaLocation**: Auto Loader must remember *which schema it discovered, how it evolved, which versions existed*. Databricks stores the inferred schema in a **`_schemas`** folder under `cloudFiles.schemaLocation`."},{"t":"p","text":"**checkpointLocation**: the streaming query's **progress and state** — conceptually *files processed: A, B, C* plus stream metadata — so a restart continues correctly."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Same directory is allowed, same role is not","text":"Databricks examples sometimes point both options at the same directory (the schema goes into its `_schemas` subfolder). That works — but the two *roles* stay different: schema history vs stream progress."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"schemaLocation ≠ progress","text":"`cloudFiles.schemaLocation` stores **schema history/state**, not stream progress. Progress lives in the **checkpoint**. Exam questions swap them on purpose."},{"t":"diagram","text":" files:   A   B   C\n stream:  A ✔ B ✔ ──✖ cluster dies\n checkpoint: \"A, B done\"\n restart  ──►  reads checkpoint  ──►  processes C only","caption":"Restart: continue, don't start from zero"},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What exactly does the checkpoint protect?","text":"It stores the streaming query's **progress and state**, so after a restart the query continues from the correct point instead of rediscovering the input as if it were new."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Checkpoint on ephemeral disk","text":"`checkpointLocation=\"/tmp/checkpoint\"` lives on a worker's local disk. When the cluster terminates, the checkpoint vanishes → the stream loses all progress."},{"t":"p","text":"**Do I still need schemaLocation with an explicit schema?** If you fully provide `.schema(...)` and don't use Auto Loader-managed inference/evolution, schemaLocation isn't required in the same way. Whenever Auto Loader **infers or evolves** the schema, `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` is critical. The **checkpoint is always required** for the write."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Initial run","points":["1. discover files","2. infer schema","3. persist schema state","4. decide which files are new","5. process batch","6. write Delta transaction","7. update checkpoint"]},{"title":"Next run","points":["1. read checkpoint","2. discover files","3. skip already-processed files","4. process only new","5. commit","6. advance checkpoint"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The heart of Auto Loader","text":"Commit the Delta transaction, *then* advance the checkpoint. That ordering is why a crash in between doesn't lose or double-count files when the sink is Delta."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a stream processed A and B, then the cluster died. The checkpoint is intact. File C arrived meanwhile. What happens on restart?","text":"Auto Loader reads the checkpoint (A, B done), discovers C as new and processes **only C**. It does not start from zero."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when setting up a new Auto Loader stream","questions":["Is my checkpoint on persistent storage (Volume / cloud path), not /tmp?","Is this checkpoint path used by this one query only?","Am I inferring the schema — then where is schemaLocation?","Would I be able to explain which of the two folders holds what?"]}]},{"id":"ch12-s10","title":"Checkpoint Incidents: Deleted, Shared, Re-pointed","hook":"A checkpoint is operational state, not a cache — delete it, share it or re-point it and the stream forgets or confuses its past.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Classic incident:** A, B, C are processed. Someone deletes the checkpoint (\"cleanup\"). The next start has **no ingestion history**."},{"t":"diagram","text":" before:  checkpoint = {A, B, C}     Bronze = A B C\n rm -r checkpoint\n restart: checkpoint = {}            sees A B C (+D) as NEW\n          Bronze = A B C  A B C D   ← duplicates","caption":"Deleted checkpoint → files may be seen as new"},{"t":"p","text":"Depending on the pipeline and sink you may **duplicate ingestion** or need **recovery/reconciliation**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Checkpoint = operational state","text":"Deleting, resetting or moving a checkpoint wipes the stream's memory of what it processed. Re-ingestion and duplicates follow."},{"t":"p","text":"**Recovery options** (decide deliberately): restore the checkpoint if you have a copy; or restart fresh and **reconcile** — e.g. delete Bronze rows of re-ingested files by `_source_file`, or dedupe/MERGE downstream; or start a new checkpoint with `cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles = false` so only files arriving *after* the start are ingested (risk: files that landed during the gap are skipped)."},{"t":"diagram","text":" Pipeline A ──┐\n              ├──► checkpoint=/shared/checkpoint   ✖ corrupt state\n Pipeline B ──┘","caption":"Two queries, one checkpoint"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Shared checkpoint path","text":"Two unrelated streaming queries writing to the same checkpoint location can corrupt each other's progress and state: one may skip files, the other reprocess, or the stream fails."},{"t":"p","text":"**Re-pointing the source**: the checkpoint belonged to a query reading `/orders/sourceA`. You change the code to read `/orders/sourceB` but keep the same checkpoint. **Dangerous** — the checkpoint describes the logical query and its source topology, not a generic cache."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Changed source, same checkpoint","text":"Keeping a checkpoint after changing the source path (or other topology) mixes the old query's progress with a new input: files can be skipped, re-read or the stream can fail."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself whenever a stream's history looks wrong","questions":["Is the checkpoint location exactly the same as in the last good run?","Was the checkpoint deleted, moved or overwritten (deploy, cleanup script, path typo)?","Does any other query write to the same checkpoint path?","Did the source path or query shape change while the checkpoint stayed the same?","Do Bronze rows show old `_source_file`s with new `_ingested_at` values?"]}]},{"id":"ch12-s11","title":"File Discovery & Auto Loader Schema Inference","hook":"How Auto Loader finds new files, and why its default schema is all STRING — on purpose.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Auto Loader must learn **which new files appeared**. Two conceptual mechanisms:"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Directory listing","points":["List the path","Compare against tracked state","Discover new files","Simple, no extra cloud setup (default)","Can get expensive with huge file counts"]},{"title":"File notification / managed file events","points":["New file → cloud event","Auto Loader learns about it from events","Avoids repeated massive listings","Classic: `cloudFiles.useNotifications`; current: managed file events on UC external locations"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":" LISTING:       list path → diff vs state → new files\n NOTIFICATION:  new file → cloud event → Auto Loader knows"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The takeaway that won't change","text":"Exact cloud-specific implementations evolve; the concept stays: Auto Loader can **discover new files incrementally without a full directory scan each time**."},{"t":"p","text":"Auto Loader supports schema inference/evolution for **JSON, CSV, XML, Avro and Parquet** — with different semantics for text vs typed formats."},{"t":"table","head":["Reader","JSON `{\"id\": 1, \"amount\": 20.5}` becomes"],"rows":[["spark.read (inference)","id LONG, amount DOUBLE"],["Auto Loader (default)","id STRING, amount STRING"],["Auto Loader + inferColumnTypes=true","id LONG, amount DOUBLE (like DataFrameReader)"]]},{"t":"p","text":"For **JSON, CSV and XML** Auto Loader infers every field as **STRING** by default — to reduce schema-evolution failures from type mismatches. Typed formats (Parquet, Avro) keep their encoded types."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":".option(\"cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes\", \"true\")","caption":"Ask for real types instead"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Auto Loader defaults text formats to STRING","text":"Unlike `spark.read` with inferSchema, Auto Loader infers JSON/CSV/XML columns as STRING unless `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true`."},{"t":"diagram","text":" today:     amount = 19.50        tomorrow:  amount = \"unknown\"\n Bronze amount DOUBLE  → parse fails / value rescued\n Bronze amount STRING  → keeps \"19.50\" and \"unknown\"  ✔\n                         → Silver validates & casts","caption":"Why Bronze often prefers strings"},{"t":"flow","items":["Bronze: preserve the source","Silver: enforce clean types"],"caption":"Medallion philosophy"},{"t":"p","text":"**Initial inference samples**, it doesn't scan your whole history: by default up to **50 GB or 1000 files, whichever limit is reached first** (tunable via `cloudFiles.schemaInference.sampleSize.numBytes` / `.numFiles`)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Schema hints** let you steer inference for specific columns without declaring every column:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":".option(\"cloudFiles.schemaHints\", \"order_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE\")"},{"t":"p","text":"For **typed formats like Parquet**, a hint tells the reader *which type to read the column as* — it is **not** just a cast applied after reading. `schema hint ≠ withColumn(cast(...))`."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Inferred once, remembered after","text":"Inference runs when the stream first builds its schema and the result is persisted in schemaLocation. Flipping `inferColumnTypes` later on an existing stream doesn't magically re-infer the stored schema."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when Auto Loader columns come out as STRING","questions":["Is the source JSON/CSV/XML, where STRING is the default?","Did I set `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` before the schema was first persisted?","Do I actually want raw strings in Bronze and typed casts in Silver?","Which few columns need types now — would schema hints be enough?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a vendor sends 3,000 JSON files of 10 MB each. Which sampling limit stops initial inference first?","text":"**1000 files** (10 GB) is reached long before 50 GB. Inference sees only the first 1000 files — a column that appears only in later files isn't in the initial schema (schema evolution handles it later)."}]},{"id":"ch12-s12","title":"Schema Evolution, Rescued Data & Quarantine","hook":"When the source changes shape, you want control — not a silent mutation and not lost data.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yesterday: `order_id, amount`. Today the source adds **`discount_code`**. Auto Loader can detect and **evolve** the schema — depending on the configured mode."},{"t":"diagram","text":" new unexpected column discovered\n          ↓\n Auto Loader records the schema change (schemaLocation)\n          ↓\n stream FAILS / stops (default mode)\n          ↓\n restart → new schema used, column included","caption":"Why schema evolution can stop a stream"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Stopping is a feature","text":"In many production contexts this is desirable: **don't silently mutate pipeline semantics without control.** Run the stream under a Job with retries so the restart picks up the new schema."},{"t":"table","head":["cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode","On a new column…"],"rows":[["addNewColumns (default when no schema given)","Stream fails; new column added to the schema; restart continues with it"],["rescue","Schema never evolves; stream keeps running; new columns go to the rescued data column"],["failOnNewColumns","Stream fails and stays failed until you update the schema or remove the file"],["none (default when you provide a schema)","Schema doesn't evolve, new columns ignored (not rescued unless rescuedDataColumn is set)"]],"caption":"Modes added for clarity — the source says 'depending on configured mode'"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":".option(\"cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode\", \"addNewColumns\")   # or rescue / failOnNewColumns / none"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"A failing stream after a new column isn't (necessarily) a bug","text":"With the default addNewColumns mode, the first batch that contains a new column stops the stream after recording the new schema. Restarting picks it up."},{"t":"p","text":"**Rescued data** is one of Databricks' best ingestion ideas. Expected schema `id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE`; incoming:"},{"t":"code","lang":"text","code":"{\"id\": 10, \"amount\": \"UNKNOWN\", \"new_field\": \"abc\"}"},{"t":"diagram","text":" id            = 10\n amount        = null\n _rescued_data = {\"amount\": \"UNKNOWN\", \"new_field\": \"abc\"}","caption":"Conceptual rescued row: nothing is lost"},{"t":"p","text":"Instead of silently throwing away mismatched or unexpected fields, Auto Loader / `read_files` put them in a **rescued data column** (`_rescued_data`) — values that don't match the expected type, and fields not in the schema."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Without rescue","points":["bad row → drop?","fail?","corrupt?"]},{"title":"With rescue","points":["valid columns load normally","unexpected content preserved","Silver can inspect, alert, quarantine, fix"]}]},{"t":"diagram","text":" Robust Bronze row\n ├── business/source fields : order_id, customer_id, amount, status\n ├── _ingested_at           : ingestion timestamp\n ├── _source_file           : source filename\n └── _rescued_data          : whatever didn't fit","caption":"Bronze architecture pattern = excellent auditability"},{"t":"diagram","text":" Bronze\n   ↓ validation\n   ├── _rescued_data IS NULL     → Silver\n   └── _rescued_data IS NOT NULL → quarantine.orders_bad","caption":"Quarantine pattern"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"bronze = spark.read.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n\n(bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NOT NULL\")\n       .write.mode(\"append\")\n       .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.quarantine.orders_bad\"))\n\nclean = bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NULL\")   # continues to Silver"},{"t":"p","text":"**Explicit schema with Auto Loader** — very common when the upstream contract is known. Note the deliberate STRING for `amount` and `order_ts`:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"schema = \"\"\"\n  order_id    BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount      STRING,\n  status      STRING,\n  order_ts    STRING\n\"\"\"\n\norders = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .schema(schema)\n        .option(\"rescuedDataColumn\", \"_rescued_data\")   # keep what doesn't fit\n        .load(source)\n)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Explicit schema hides new columns","text":"With a strict explicit schema the evolution mode defaults to `none`: a new upstream column does NOT appear as a regular column and isn't rescued unless you set `rescuedDataColumn`."},{"t":"list","items":["**Lab — schema evolution:** start with `{\"id\":1,\"amount\":\"20.0\"}`, then add `{\"id\":2,\"amount\":\"25.0\",\"discount\":\"SAVE10\"}`. Watch schemaLocation get a new schema version and the stream stop/restart.","**Lab — bad data:** add `{\"id\":3,\"amount\":{\"unexpected\":true}}`. If `amount` is typed (hint/inferColumnTypes), the object can't fit → `amount` null + value in `_rescued_data`. If `amount` is STRING it may simply be kept as raw JSON text. Either way: **don't just make it pass — know where unexpected data goes.**"],"ordered":false},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when an Auto Loader stream stops on a schema change","questions":["Is this the documented addNewColumns stop-and-restart, not a real failure?","Which file introduced the new column, and is the change intentional?","Does schemaLocation now hold a new schema version?","Should this feed evolve, rescue, or fail loudly?","Will a job retry restart the stream automatically?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why is quarantining by `_rescued_data IS NOT NULL` better than dropping those rows in Bronze?","text":"Because Bronze keeps the evidence. Quarantine isolates suspicious rows from Silver while preserving them for alerting, investigation and reprocessing once the rule or the source is fixed."}]},{"id":"ch12-s13","title":"Exactly-once, Bronze → MERGE & Production Architecture","hook":"Each file loaded once is not the same as each order stored once — know which layer guarantees what.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Auto Loader is designed for **exactly-once file ingestion** into Delta — when combined correctly with checkpointing and a supported (transactional) sink."},{"t":"p","text":"That does **not** mean duplicates can never appear in your business table. Duplicates can come from the **source data itself**:"},{"t":"diagram","text":" file A: order_id=100        file B: order_id=100\n   processed once ✔            processed once ✔\n              ↓                       ↓\n     Bronze: two rows with order_id = 100   (correctly!)\n\n file-level exactly-once  ≠  business-key deduplication"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Auto Loader tracks FILES, not business entities","text":"`fileA processed once` + `fileB processed once` does not imply `order_id 100 appears once`. Business-key dedup/upsert is Silver's job."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Exactly-once ≠ no duplicate keys","text":"Exactly-once file ingestion guarantees each *file* is ingested once. Two distinct files (an upstream retry, a resend, an update) can legitimately contain the same order_id."},{"t":"flow","items":["Files","Auto Loader","Bronze append","dedup / CDC","MERGE","Silver"],"caption":"Common architecture"},{"t":"p","text":"This cleanly separates **ingestion correctness** (every file exactly once) from **business-state correctness** (every order once, latest version)."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"MERGE INTO ecommerce.silver.orders AS t\nUSING (\n  SELECT *\n  FROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\n  QUALIFY row_number() OVER (\n    PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY _ingested_at DESC) = 1\n) AS s\nON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","caption":"Sketch: dedupe Bronze by business key, then MERGE into Silver (column lists simplified)"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Bad Bronze","points":["ingest","+ business rules","+ join 8 tables","+ calculate revenue","+ drop malformed source data"]},{"title":"Better","points":["**Bronze**: land faithfully","**Silver**: validate, dedup, cast, join, apply CDC","Recovery is easy: fix Silver code, rebuild from Bronze"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Transforming (and dropping) during ingestion","text":"Bronze that applies business rules, joins and drops malformed rows loses the raw evidence; when a rule is wrong you can't rebuild."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Why not write Auto Loader directly into Silver?","text":"Bronze gives **source fidelity, replay, audit, schema-drift handling, rescued data and debugging**. Silver then enforces **business keys, types, deduplication, CDC and quality**. The separation minimizes coupling between ingestion and business transformation."},{"t":"diagram","text":" Cloud / Volume landing zone\n   ↓  immutable files\n Auto Loader\n   ↓\n Bronze  : raw + metadata + rescued data\n   ↓  validation\n Silver  : typed + dedup + CDC\n   ↓\n Gold","caption":"The production ingestion architecture to internalize"},{"t":"p","text":"**Production assignment** — build `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/` → Auto Loader → `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw` with `order_id, customer_id, amount, status, _ingested_at, _rescued_data`, then deliberately test:"},{"t":"list","items":["Ingest the first JSON file","Restart the stream","Add a second file","Verify the first wasn't reprocessed","Add a new column","Observe schema evolution","Add a malformed value","Inspect rescued data","Delete the checkpoint — in a sandbox only","Observe the changed behavior","Restore/recreate safely","Add a duplicate business key in a different file","Verify Auto Loader processes each file once","Prove the business duplicate still exists","Deduplicate downstream"],"ordered":true},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: in step 14 the duplicate order_id is still in Bronze although every file was processed exactly once. Is Auto Loader broken?","text":"No. Two **different files** each contained order_id 100, and each file was correctly ingested once. File identity ≠ business identity — step 15 (dedupe downstream) is where that's fixed."}]},{"id":"ch12-s14","title":"Choosing the Tool: spark.read vs COPY INTO vs Auto Loader","hook":"Same goal, three tools — pick by file count, arrival pattern, schema churn and who writes the code.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sometimes you don't want a permanently running stream. You want: **process all new files now, then stop.** That's the **AvailableNow** trigger."},{"t":"flow","items":["start","process backlog","reach latest known input","stop"],"caption":"AvailableNow"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"query = (\n    orders.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint_path)\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)\nquery.awaitTermination()   # optional: block until done","caption":"Scheduled, batch-like incremental ingestion with Auto Loader"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Streaming engine, batch-like job","text":"AvailableNow shows that the streaming engine can implement **batch-like incremental jobs**: schedule it hourly in a Job, it catches up and stops — with checkpoint guarantees and no always-on cluster."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"COPY INTO","points":["run command","discover unprocessed source files","load","finish"]},{"title":"Auto Loader","points":["stateful incremental stream","discover files continuously / in batches","checkpoint progress","load incrementally"]}]},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Use COPY INTO when","points":["moderate number of files (thousands over time)","SQL-centric workflow","simple ingestion","easy retriable/idempotent loading","reloading selected files matters (FILES, force)"]},{"title":"Use Auto Loader when","points":["large / continuously growing source","millions of files","frequent arrivals","schema changes (richer evolution)","streaming/incremental, high-scale production"]}]},{"t":"table","head":["Property","spark.read","COPY INTO","Auto Loader"],"rows":[["Basic batch read","✅","✅","via streaming model"],["Remembers processed files","❌","✅","✅"],["Idempotent file load","❌ by itself","✅","✅ with checkpoint/sink semantics"],["SQL-friendly","limited","✅","SQL via Lakeflow / read_files"],["Huge file counts","weak/manual","okay, moderate scale","strongest"],["Continuous ingestion","❌","repeated runs","✅"],["Schema evolution","manual","supported","strongest / flexible"],["Checkpoint","❌","internal tracking","explicit streaming checkpoint"],["Streaming integration","❌","❌ (not as a stream)","✅"]],"caption":"The comparison table"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Millions of files arriving continuously","text":"Exam: 'millions of files arriving continuously in cloud object storage — which mechanism?' Answer: **Auto Loader** — not repeated spark.read, and typically not COPY INTO at that scale."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a team with SQL-only analysts gets ~20 CSV files a day from a partner and occasionally must reload one specific file. Which tool?","text":"**COPY INTO**: moderate volume, SQL-centric, idempotent re-runs, and `FILES = (...)` (+ `force` when deliberate) handles the selective reload."}]},{"id":"ch12-s15","title":"Debugging I: Missing Rows & Overwritten Files","hook":"'New files exist but no rows appear' has eight possible breakpoints — check them in order.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Symptom: the landing zone has new files, Bronze has **no new rows**. Don't guess — walk the chain from the source to the sink:"},{"t":"flow","items":["New files exist?","Path matches?","Extension/format right?","Discovered?","Checkpoint: already processed?","Parsing failure?","Schema issue?","Sink write failure?"],"caption":"Systematic order: source → sink"},{"t":"table","head":["Breakpoint","Typical evidence"],"rows":[["Files don't exist","Upstream says 'sent', listing of the path shows nothing"],["Path mismatch","Stream reads `/raw/order/`, files land in `/raw/orders/`"],["Wrong extension/format","cloudFiles.format json, files are `.csv` / `.json.gz` / filtered out"],["Not discovered","Stream progress shows 0 input files"],["Already processed","Same file names as before (overwrite) → checkpoint says done"],["Parsing failure","Rows null / in `_rescued_data` / corrupt records"],["Schema issue","Stream stopped on schema change; rows rejected"],["Sink failure","Write error, permissions, table dropped"]]},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when new files exist but no rows appear","questions":["Do the new files actually exist where I think (list the path)?","Does the stream's `.load()` path exactly match where they land?","Are the extension and format what the reader expects?","Did Auto Loader discover them (query progress: numFilesOutstanding / input rows)?","Does the checkpoint consider them already processed (same names, overwritten)?","Did parsing fail (nulls, `_rescued_data`, corrupt records)?","Is the stream stopped on a schema change?","Did the write to the sink fail (permissions, table changed)?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**File accidentally overwritten**: COPY INTO may still treat a previously loaded file as processed **even if its contents changed** — the correction never arrives. Auto Loader behaves similarly by default (it doesn't reprocess modified files unless `cloudFiles.allowOverwrites` is enabled, which risks duplicates)."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Mutable delivery (avoid)","points":["upload `file.csv`","later mutate `file.csv`","later mutate again","`orders_latest.json` overwritten repeatedly"]},{"title":"Immutable delivery (prefer)","points":["`orders_20260928_001.json`","`orders_20260928_002.json`","a correction = a NEW file","every file identity appears once"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Overwriting source files in place","text":"Incremental loaders reason about **file identity**. An overwritten file may be skipped as 'already loaded' (lost correction) or, with overwrites allowed, reprocessed (duplicates)."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a corrected source file doesn't show up","questions":["Was the correction delivered as a new file or as an overwrite of a loaded one?","Does the loader consider that file identity already processed?","Is the file's modification time later than its rows' `_ingested_at`?","If I force a reload, what removes the old rows first?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Immutable landing zones are easier to reason about","text":"Incremental systems reason much more reliably about **new file identities** than about mutable files."}]},{"id":"ch12-s16","title":"Debugging II: Schema Shifts, Missing Columns, Duplicate IDs & Bad CSV","hook":"Four incidents you will meet in your first month — each with its own ordered questions.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Schema suddenly changes.** Yesterday `amount STRING`; today the source sends `amount` as a **STRUCT**."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when the source schema suddenly changes","questions":["Was the source contract intentionally changed?","Did only one corrupt file do this?","Should the field be rescued?","Should the schema evolve?","Should the stream fail?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Don't blindly allow everything","text":"Turning on every evolution option lets an accidental upstream change (e.g. amount becoming a struct) flow silently into Silver and Gold."},{"t":"p","text":"**New column missing.** Upstream adds `discount`, but Bronze doesn't show it. Check: **schema evolution mode**, **schemaLocation state**, a **provided explicit schema**, **rescued data**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Explicit schema swallows new columns","text":"If you supplied a strict explicit schema, a new field won't appear as a regular column automatically (mode `none`). Look in `_rescued_data` — if you enabled it."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a new upstream column is missing","questions":["Which schemaEvolutionMode is in effect?","What does the latest schema in schemaLocation contain?","Am I providing a strict explicit schema without the new field?","Is the value sitting in `_rescued_data` — is rescue even enabled?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Duplicated business IDs.** Rows show `order_id=100` twice. **Do not immediately blame Auto Loader.**"},{"t":"table","head":["Question","If yes →"],"rows":[["Same source file loaded twice?","Ingestion problem (checkpoint reset, force, plain re-read)"],["Two different files both contain the ID?","Source behavior — dedupe in Silver"],["Upstream retry re-sent the order?","Source behavior — dedupe in Silver"],["Silver MERGE/dedup missing or wrong key?","Transformation problem"],["Checkpoint reset?","Ingestion problem — reconcile"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"File idempotency vs business-key idempotency","text":"This is exactly why we separate **file idempotency** (Auto Loader / COPY INTO) from **business-key idempotency** (dedup / MERGE in Silver)."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when you see duplicated business IDs","questions":["Was the same source file loaded twice?","Do two different files both contain the ID?","Was it an upstream retry?","Is the Silver MERGE/dedup missing or keyed wrongly?","Was a checkpoint reset?"]},{"t":"p","text":"**Malformed CSV.** Expected `id,amount` / `1,10.5`. Bad line: `2,\"10.5` — an **unclosed quote**."},{"t":"p","text":"Possible results: **parse error**, **corrupt/rescued data**, or **unexpected columns** (the quote swallows the separator and the next line). Investigate the parser options: `quote`, `escape`, `multiLine`, `delimiter`, `header`."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n        .option(\"quote\", '\"')\n        .option(\"escape\", '\"')\n        .option(\"multiLine\", \"true\")\n        .option(\"mode\", \"PERMISSIVE\")\n        .option(\"columnNameOfCorruptRecord\", \"_corrupt_record\")\n        .schema(\"id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE, _corrupt_record STRING\")\n        .load(path)\n)","caption":"Make bad lines visible instead of guessing"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a CSV has malformed rows","questions":["What does the raw line look like?","Is there an unclosed or unescaped quote (quote / escape)?","Do quoted fields contain newlines (multiLine)?","Are delimiter and header right?","Is my mode hiding bad rows (DROPMALFORMED) or surfacing them?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"multiLine + broken quote = swallowed rows","text":"With `multiLine=true`, an unclosed quote can make the parser treat the following lines as part of one field — several good rows can vanish into one bad record."}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch12-d01","title":"Inferred column types drift between runs","section":"ch12-s03","symptom":"Yesterday's load worked; today the write fails with a schema mismatch, or `amount` arrives as DOUBLE/STRING instead of INT. Nobody changed the code.","askYourself":["Is this read using `inferSchema` (or JSON's default inference) instead of an explicit schema?","What type did yesterday's run infer, and what does today's run infer for the same column?","Which new file introduced the different values (e.g. `100.50`, `unknown`)?","Is the new value legitimate (contract change) or a bad record?","Should Bronze keep this column as STRING and cast in Silver instead?"],"steps":[{"do":"Print both schemas and diff them.","why":"Confirms it's an inference change, not a code change.","code":"df.printSchema()"},{"do":"Find the file with the new value pattern using the source-file path.","why":"Pins down which delivery changed.","code":"df.select(\"amount\", \"_metadata.file_path\").where(\"amount NOT RLIKE '^[0-9]+$'\")"},{"do":"Ask the upstream owner whether the type change is intended.","why":"Decides evolve vs reject."},{"do":"Replace inference with an explicit schema (DDL string) in Git.","why":"Stops future silent drift.","code":"schema = \"order_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE, status STRING\"\ndf = spark.read.schema(schema).json(path)"}],"rootCauses":["Schema inferred from different samples each run","Upstream started sending decimals/strings","No deliberate schema policy"],"fix":"Pin the contract: explicit schema (or STRING in Bronze + typed casts in Silver), and handle genuinely new types through a reviewed change.","mnemonic":"GUESS → DIFF → FILE → OWNER → PIN"},{"id":"ch12-d02","title":"Bronze doubled after re-running a plain batch load","section":"ch12-s06","symptom":"Row count in `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw` jumped to exactly 2× (or 3×) after someone re-ran the ingestion notebook. Same order_ids appear several times, with different `_ingested_at`.","askYourself":["Was this load a plain `spark.read` + `mode(\"append\")` with no file tracking?","Did the run read files that a previous run already loaded (same `_source_file` twice)?","Was the job retried or re-run manually?","Can I delete the duplicate load cleanly (by `_ingested_at` / Delta version)?","Should this load be COPY INTO or Auto Loader instead?"],"steps":[{"do":"Count rows per source file and ingestion time.","why":"Same file with 2+ ingestion times = re-read duplicates.","code":"SELECT _source_file, _ingested_at, count(*)\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nGROUP BY ALL ORDER BY _source_file;"},{"do":"Check DESCRIBE HISTORY for the extra WRITE/append versions.","why":"Shows which runs appended.","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw;"},{"do":"Remove the duplicate load (RESTORE to the version before, or DELETE by `_ingested_at`).","why":"Return to a correct state."},{"do":"Replace the loader with COPY INTO or Auto Loader.","why":"Tracks loaded files so re-runs are safe."}],"rootCauses":["spark.read has no file-history memory","Manual or automatic re-run of an append job","No idempotent loader"],"fix":"Clean up the duplicate append, then switch to an idempotent file loader (COPY INTO / Auto Loader).","mnemonic":"READ-AGAIN = APPEND-AGAIN"},{"id":"ch12-d03","title":"Checkpoint deleted: old files re-ingested","section":"ch12-s10","symptom":"After a 'cleanup' or redeploy, Bronze row count jumps. Old files' rows appear again with today's `_ingested_at`; downstream sees duplicates.","askYourself":["Is the checkpoint location the same as before — and does it still contain data?","Who or what deleted/reset it (cleanup job, redeploy, path change)?","Which `_source_file`s were ingested twice, and in which Delta versions?","Do I have a copy of the old checkpoint to restore?","If not: reconcile by source file, dedupe downstream, or restart with includeExistingFiles=false?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the configured checkpointLocation with the last good run and list its contents.","why":"Proves a reset vs a different bug."},{"do":"Find files ingested more than once.","why":"Measures the blast radius.","code":"SELECT _source_file, count(DISTINCT _ingested_at) AS loads\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nGROUP BY _source_file HAVING loads > 1;"},{"do":"Restore the checkpoint copy if one exists; otherwise delete the duplicate load (by `_ingested_at` / RESTORE).","why":"Get Bronze back to one copy per file."},{"do":"Protect the checkpoint path (permissions, no cleanup jobs on /system).","why":"Prevents a repeat."}],"rootCauses":["Checkpoint deleted or reset","Checkpoint path changed in a deploy","Cleanup script treating checkpoints as temp data"],"fix":"Restore or reconcile, then protect checkpoint paths as operational state. If a fresh start is unavoidable, decide explicitly between re-ingest+dedupe and includeExistingFiles=false.","mnemonic":"WHERE → WHO → WHICH FILES → RESTORE or RECONCILE → PROTECT"},{"id":"ch12-d04","title":"Two streams share one checkpoint","section":"ch12-s10","symptom":"Two pipelines behave strangely at the same time: one skips files, the other reprocesses or fails with checkpoint/offset errors. Their configs both say `checkpointLocation=/shared/checkpoint`.","askYourself":["Which queries write to this checkpoint path?","Did they start sharing it after a copy-paste or a refactor of a common config?","Which files did each stream skip or reprocess?","Can each query get its own fresh checkpoint without losing or duplicating data?"],"steps":[{"do":"Grep job/pipeline configs for the checkpoint path.","why":"Find every query using it."},{"do":"Stop both queries.","why":"Prevent further state corruption."},{"do":"Give each query a unique checkpoint path derived from its target.","why":"One query = one checkpoint.","code":".option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/orders_bronze_checkpoint/\")"},{"do":"Reconcile missing/duplicate files per target using `_source_file`.","why":"Fix the data the confusion caused."}],"rootCauses":["Copy-pasted config","Shared 'common' checkpoint variable","Misunderstanding checkpoint as a generic folder"],"fix":"One checkpoint per streaming query, named after its target; reconcile both targets after the split.","mnemonic":"ONE QUERY, ONE CHECKPOINT"},{"id":"ch12-d05","title":"Source path changed, checkpoint kept","section":"ch12-s10","symptom":"After changing `.load(\"/orders/sourceA\")` to `.load(\"/orders/sourceB\")` the stream fails, ingests nothing, or ingests an unexpected mix of files.","askYourself":["Did the source path (or query shape) change while the checkpoint stayed the same?","What does the checkpoint believe the source is?","Should sourceB start from scratch (backfill) or only new files?","Will sourceA's data still be needed in the same target?"],"steps":[{"do":"Diff the stream definition against the last good deploy.","why":"Confirms a topology change."},{"do":"Create a new checkpoint (and schemaLocation if needed) for the new source.","why":"The old checkpoint describes another query."},{"do":"Decide backfill: includeExistingFiles=true (default) to load sourceB history, or false for only new files.","why":"Explicit choice instead of accidental."},{"do":"Verify counts per `_source_file` prefix in the target.","why":"Proves no gap/overlap."}],"rootCauses":["Checkpoint treated as a generic cache","Path change in config without state plan"],"fix":"New source/topology → new checkpoint + explicit backfill decision.","mnemonic":"NEW SOURCE → NEW CHECKPOINT"},{"id":"ch12-d06","title":"Auto Loader table has every column as STRING","section":"ch12-s11","symptom":"The new Bronze table from Auto Loader shows `order_id STRING, amount STRING, order_ts STRING`; a downstream sum fails or sorts '100' before '20'.","askYourself":["Is the source a text format (JSON/CSV/XML) where Auto Loader defaults to STRING?","Did I set `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` — and before the schema was first persisted?","Do I actually want strings in Bronze (preserve source) and types in Silver?","Which few columns really need types early — would schema hints do?","Is a full explicit schema justified because the upstream contract is known?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check the schema stored under schemaLocation (`_schemas`) and the table schema.","why":"Confirms default string inference."},{"do":"Decide the layer for typing: Bronze strings + Silver casts is often the safest.","why":"Keeps raw values like 'unknown' visible."},{"do":"If types are needed at ingestion: add schemaHints for key columns or inferColumnTypes for a new stream.","why":"Targeted, reviewed typing.","code":".option(\"cloudFiles.schemaHints\", \"order_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE\")"},{"do":"Cast in Silver with validation.","why":"Bad values become quarantinable instead of breaking ingestion.","code":"SELECT CAST(order_id AS BIGINT) AS order_id, TRY_CAST(amount AS DOUBLE) AS amount FROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw"}],"rootCauses":["Default string inference for text formats (by design)","inferColumnTypes not set (or set after schema persisted)"],"fix":"Not a bug: keep strings in Bronze and cast in Silver, or use hints / inferColumnTypes / explicit schema deliberately.","mnemonic":"TEXT → STRING; decide WHERE to type"},{"id":"ch12-d07","title":"Stream stopped right after a new column appeared","section":"ch12-s12","symptom":"The Auto Loader job failed with an error saying a new field/column was detected (schema change). It worked for weeks. Upstream says they 'added a small field'.","askYourself":["Is this the documented addNewColumns behavior (stop, record, restart) rather than a real bug?","Which file introduced the new column — and is the change intentional?","Did the schema in schemaLocation get a new version with the column?","Should this feed evolve, rescue, or fail loudly (schemaEvolutionMode)?","Does the job have retries so a restart picks up the evolved schema?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read the failure message and the latest schema version under schemaLocation/_schemas.","why":"Confirms a schema-evolution stop."},{"do":"Find the file with the new column (`_metadata.file_path` / landing-zone timestamps).","why":"Ties the change to a delivery."},{"do":"Confirm with upstream that the column is intended.","why":"Evolution is a contract decision."},{"do":"Restart (or rely on job retries); verify the column appears.","why":"addNewColumns continues with the new schema after restart."},{"do":"Set schemaEvolutionMode deliberately for this feed.","why":"rescue / failOnNewColumns if evolution should not be automatic.","code":".option(\"cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode\", \"rescue\")"}],"rootCauses":["New column + default addNewColumns mode","Job without retries","Unannounced upstream change"],"fix":"Treat the stop as a control point: confirm intent, restart (job retries), and choose an evolution mode per feed.","mnemonic":"STOP → SCHEMA → FILE → OWNER → RESTART → MODE"},{"id":"ch12-d08","title":"New files exist but no rows appear","section":"ch12-s15","symptom":"Upstream says files were delivered; the landing zone listing shows them; `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw` has no new rows. The stream/job shows no errors.","askYourself":["Do the new files actually exist where I think?","Does the stream's load path exactly match the landing path?","Are the extension and format what the reader expects?","Did Auto Loader discover them at all (query progress)?","Does the checkpoint say they were already processed (same names)?","Did parsing fail (nulls / `_rescued_data` / corrupt records)?","Is the stream stopped on a schema change?","Did the write to the sink fail?"],"steps":[{"do":"List the landing path and compare with the stream's `.load()` path.","why":"Covers existence + path mismatch.","code":"display(dbutils.fs.ls(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/\"))"},{"do":"Check format/extension vs `cloudFiles.format` and any filters.","why":"A `.csv.gz` or `.JSON` may not be what you expect."},{"do":"Inspect the query's recent progress (input rows, files).","why":"Proves whether discovery happened.","code":"query.recentProgress"},{"do":"Compare the new file names with already-ingested `_source_file`s.","why":"Same names = considered processed."},{"do":"Query `_rescued_data` / nulls for the latest ingestion.","why":"Parsing problems hide rows' content."},{"do":"Check the stream status/errors and the sink table.","why":"Schema stops and sink failures end the chain."}],"rootCauses":["Files never arrived","Path mismatch","Wrong extension/format","Not discovered","Overwritten names already processed","Parsing failure","Schema change stop","Sink write failure"],"fix":"Find the first breakpoint along source → sink and fix exactly there (path, format, naming, parser options, schema policy or sink).","mnemonic":"EXIST · PATH · FORMAT · FOUND · DONE? · PARSE · SCHEMA · SINK"},{"id":"ch12-d09","title":"Source file overwritten: the correction never arrived","section":"ch12-s15","symptom":"Upstream fixed wrong amounts by re-uploading `orders_0928.csv` under the same name. Bronze/Silver still show the old wrong amounts. COPY INTO runs succeed.","askYourself":["Was the correction delivered as a NEW file or by overwriting an already-loaded one?","Does COPY INTO / Auto Loader consider that file identity already processed?","Do Bronze rows from that file show the old `_ingested_at` only?","Do I need a deliberate reload (FILES + force) and cleanup of the old rows?","Can upstream switch to immutable, uniquely named deliveries?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the file's modification time with the Bronze `_ingested_at` for that `_source_file`.","why":"Modified after ingestion = overwritten in place."},{"do":"Confirm the loader skipped it (no new rows for that file).","why":"COPY INTO skips loaded files even if changed."},{"do":"Delete the old rows of that file, then reload deliberately.","why":"Avoid duplicates while getting the correction in.","code":"DELETE FROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw WHERE _source_file LIKE '%orders_0928.csv';\n\nCOPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFILES = ('orders_0928.csv')\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true')\nCOPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true');"},{"do":"Agree on immutable delivery (corrections as new files).","why":"Prevents the next lost correction."}],"rootCauses":["Source overwrote an already-loaded file","Loader tracks file identity, not content","No immutable-delivery agreement"],"fix":"Reload deliberately (cleanup + FILES + force) and move upstream to immutable, uniquely named files.","mnemonic":"SAME NAME = SAME FILE (to the loader)"},{"id":"ch12-d10","title":"Source schema suddenly changed (STRING → STRUCT)","section":"ch12-s16","symptom":"Bronze worked yesterday with `amount STRING`; today Auto Loader stops, rescues lots of values, or `amount` contains JSON blobs like `{\"value\":19.5,\"currency\":\"EUR\"}`.","askYourself":["Was the source contract intentionally changed?","Did only one corrupt file do this, or every new file?","Should the field be rescued (keep running, inspect later)?","Should the schema evolve (intentional, agreed change)?","Should the stream fail (contract violation that needs review)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Find which files carry the new shape (`_source_file`, `_rescued_data`).","why":"One file = bad delivery; all files = contract change."},{"do":"Ask upstream whether the change is intentional and documented.","why":"Policy depends on intent."},{"do":"Choose: rescue (schemaEvolutionMode rescue / rescued column), evolve deliberately, or fail.","why":"Don't blindly allow everything."},{"do":"Adapt Silver parsing (e.g. extract `amount.value`) only after the decision.","why":"Keeps Silver correct across old and new shapes."}],"rootCauses":["Intentional upstream contract change","A single corrupt/test file","No agreed evolution policy"],"fix":"Establish intent and scope, then deliberately rescue, evolve or fail; never 'enable everything'.","mnemonic":"INTENT → ONE FILE? → RESCUE / EVOLVE / FAIL"},{"id":"ch12-d11","title":"New upstream column missing from Bronze","section":"ch12-s16","symptom":"Upstream added `discount` last week. Bronze `orders_raw` has no `discount` column; nothing failed.","askYourself":["Which schemaEvolutionMode is in effect (explicit default none? rescue?)","What does the latest schema under schemaLocation contain?","Am I providing a strict explicit schema that doesn't list `discount`?","Is the value sitting in `_rescued_data` — and is rescue even enabled?","Did the stream actually restart after an addNewColumns stop?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read the stream definition: `.schema(...)`, schemaEvolutionMode, rescuedDataColumn.","why":"Explicit schema → mode none → column ignored."},{"do":"Inspect the latest schema version in schemaLocation.","why":"With inference + addNewColumns the new column should appear there."},{"do":"Query `_rescued_data` for `discount`.","why":"In rescue mode (or with rescuedDataColumn) the values are preserved.","code":"SELECT _rescued_data FROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw WHERE _rescued_data LIKE '%discount%' LIMIT 10;"},{"do":"If the column is wanted: add it to the explicit schema / switch mode, and backfill from rescued data or source files.","why":"Deliberate evolution plus recovery of the missed values."}],"rootCauses":["Strict explicit schema (mode none)","rescue mode keeps it in `_rescued_data`","Stream not restarted after evolution stop"],"fix":"Check mode → schemaLocation → explicit schema → rescued data; then evolve deliberately and backfill.","mnemonic":"MODE · SCHEMALOC · EXPLICIT · RESCUED"},{"id":"ch12-d12","title":"Duplicated business IDs in Bronze/Silver","section":"ch12-s16","symptom":"`order_id = 100` appears twice (or more) in Bronze and/or Silver.","askYourself":["Was the same source file loaded twice?","Do two different files both contain the ID?","Was it an upstream retry/resend?","Is the Silver MERGE/dedup missing or keyed wrongly?","Was a checkpoint reset (or COPY INTO forced)?"],"steps":[{"do":"Group the duplicates by `_source_file` and `_ingested_at`.","why":"Same file twice = ingestion; different files = source.","code":"SELECT order_id, _source_file, _ingested_at\nFROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nWHERE order_id = 100;"},{"do":"If same file twice: look for checkpoint resets, force reloads, plain re-reads.","why":"Fix state and reconcile Bronze."},{"do":"If different files: confirm with upstream (retry/resend/update).","why":"Expected source behavior."},{"do":"Check the Silver MERGE: dedupe source by key, match on business key only.","why":"Business-key idempotency belongs here."}],"rootCauses":["Same file ingested twice (checkpoint reset / force / re-read)","Distinct files with same key (retry/resend)","Missing or wrong Silver dedup/MERGE"],"fix":"Separate file idempotency (fix loads/state) from business-key idempotency (dedupe/MERGE in Silver). Don't immediately blame Auto Loader.","mnemonic":"SAME FILE? · OTHER FILE? · RETRY? · MERGE? · RESET?"},{"id":"ch12-d13","title":"Malformed CSV rows","section":"ch12-s16","symptom":"A CSV feed produces parse errors, rows with null amounts, values in `_rescued_data`/`_corrupt_record`, or rows with unexpected extra columns — e.g. a line `2,\"10.5`.","askYourself":["What does the raw line look like around the failure?","Is the quote character right — and is there an unclosed quote?","How are quotes escaped inside fields (escape)?","Do quoted fields contain newlines (multiLine)?","Is the delimiter right, and is the header option correct?","Which mode is hiding or surfacing the bad rows?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read the raw text of the file to see the exact bad line.","why":"You need the real grammar, not a guess.","code":"spark.read.text(path).show(20, truncate=False)"},{"do":"Compare quote/escape/multiLine/delimiter/header with the file.","why":"Each is one grammar rule."},{"do":"Make bad rows visible: PERMISSIVE + columnNameOfCorruptRecord (or rescued data).","why":"Evidence instead of silent nulls/drops.","code":".option(\"mode\", \"PERMISSIVE\")\n.option(\"columnNameOfCorruptRecord\", \"_corrupt_record\")"},{"do":"Fix options or get upstream to fix the export; quarantine the bad rows.","why":"Clean data continues; bad data stays inspectable."}],"rootCauses":["Unclosed or unescaped quotes","Embedded newlines without multiLine","Wrong delimiter/header","Mode silently dropping/nulling rows"],"fix":"Look at the raw line, align quote/escape/multiLine/delimiter/header with the file, keep bad rows visible and quarantined.","mnemonic":"RAW → QUOTE → ESCAPE → MULTILINE → DELIM/HEADER → MODE"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"No header option → header becomes data","text":"Reading a CSV with a header line but without `.option(\"header\",\"true\")` gives columns `_c0, _c1…` AND an extra data row containing the column names — which later fails every cast.","fix":"Always state `header` explicitly for CSV (and check the first rows with display())."},{"title":"CSV without schema = all STRING","text":"Plain CSV has no native types. With neither `.schema(...)` nor `inferSchema=true`, every CSV column is read as **STRING**.","fix":"Pass an explicit schema (production) or set inferSchema for exploration."},{"title":"Inference is a moving target","text":"Monday's files make Spark infer `amount INT`; Tuesday's file has `100.50` → the inferred type changes (or parsing breaks). Downstream writes then hit schema mismatches.","fix":"Have a deliberate schema policy: explicit schema (or Auto Loader with schemaLocation + hints) for production ingestion."},{"title":"STREAM read_files = Auto Loader underneath","text":"When `read_files` is used with `STREAM` in a streaming table, Databricks runs **Auto Loader** for incremental file ingestion. Everything you learn about Auto Loader (checkpoints, schema inference, rescued data) applies.","fix":"Treat `STREAM read_files(...)` as the SQL face of Auto Loader."},{"title":"_metadata isn't in SELECT *","text":"The `_metadata` column is hidden: `select(\"*\")` or `SELECT *` won't return it, so audit columns silently go missing.","fix":"Select `_metadata` (or `_metadata.file_path`) explicitly and alias it, e.g. `_source_file`."},{"title":"Date-folder filtering is fragile incremental logic","text":"Filtering by filename/date assumes a perfect upstream layout. Late files, backfills, re-uploads, timezone bugs, renames and missing folders all cause skipped or duplicated data.","fix":"Use a tool that tracks processed files: COPY INTO or Auto Loader."},{"title":"A changed file is still 'already loaded'","text":"Databricks: if a file was already loaded, COPY INTO **skips it on later runs even if the file's contents have changed**. Same tracked file identity ≠ 'reload because content changed'.","fix":"Deliver corrections as NEW files (immutable delivery). To deliberately reload, use `COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')` and handle duplicates."},{"title":"PATTERN is a glob, not a regex","text":"COPY INTO's PATTERN is a **glob pattern** (`*`, `?`, `[a-z]`, `{a,b}`). The tutoring notes wrote a regex-style `'orders_.*[.]csv'` — as a glob that means 'orders_.' + anything + '.csv' and would NOT match `orders_001.csv`.","fix":"Write globs: `PATTERN = 'orders_*.csv'`. Test with VALIDATE or a small run."},{"title":"Two different mergeSchema options","text":"`FORMAT_OPTIONS mergeSchema` reconciles schemas across source files; `COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema` evolves the target Delta schema. Setting only one may not give you new columns in the table.","fix":"Remember: FORMAT = files being read, COPY = target being written."},{"title":"Auto Loader is not 'faster spark.read'","text":"Auto Loader doesn't read an individual file faster. Its value is incremental file discovery, remembered state, schema handling and streaming integration.","fix":"Answer 'why Auto Loader?' with discovery + state + schema + streaming, not raw speed."},{"title":"schemaLocation ≠ progress","text":"`cloudFiles.schemaLocation` stores **schema history/state**, not stream progress. Progress lives in the **checkpoint**. Exam questions swap them on purpose.","fix":"Shape → schemaLocation. Progress → checkpointLocation."},{"title":"Checkpoint on ephemeral disk","text":"`checkpointLocation=\"/tmp/checkpoint\"` lives on a worker's local disk. When the cluster terminates, the checkpoint vanishes → the stream loses all progress.","fix":"Put checkpoints on persistent storage: a Unity Catalog Volume or cloud path, e.g. `/Volumes/ecommerce/system/orders_checkpoint/`."},{"title":"Checkpoint = operational state","text":"Deleting, resetting or moving a checkpoint wipes the stream's memory of what it processed. Re-ingestion and duplicates follow.","fix":"Never treat checkpoints as disposable temp data. Back them up/protect them; reset only deliberately, with a reconciliation plan."},{"title":"Shared checkpoint path","text":"Two unrelated streaming queries writing to the same checkpoint location can corrupt each other's progress and state: one may skip files, the other reprocess, or the stream fails.","fix":"One checkpoint location per streaming query — derive it from the target name, e.g. `/Volumes/<cat>/system/<table>_checkpoint/`."},{"title":"Changed source, same checkpoint","text":"Keeping a checkpoint after changing the source path (or other topology) mixes the old query's progress with a new input: files can be skipped, re-read or the stream can fail.","fix":"New source/topology → new checkpoint, plus an explicit backfill/reconciliation plan."},{"title":"Auto Loader defaults text formats to STRING","text":"Unlike `spark.read` with inferSchema, Auto Loader infers JSON/CSV/XML columns as STRING unless `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true`.","fix":"Expect strings in Bronze; use inferColumnTypes, schema hints or Silver casts when you need types."},{"title":"Inferred once, remembered after","text":"Inference runs when the stream first builds its schema and the result is persisted in schemaLocation. Flipping `inferColumnTypes` later on an existing stream doesn't magically re-infer the stored schema.","fix":"Decide inference options before the first run; for an existing stream use schema hints or Silver casts (or a deliberately new stream with a new schemaLocation/checkpoint)."},{"title":"A failing stream after a new column isn't (necessarily) a bug","text":"With the default addNewColumns mode, the first batch that contains a new column stops the stream after recording the new schema. Restarting picks it up.","fix":"Run Auto Loader as a Job with retries; decide per feed whether new columns should evolve, be rescued, or fail loudly."},{"title":"Explicit schema hides new columns","text":"With a strict explicit schema the evolution mode defaults to `none`: a new upstream column does NOT appear as a regular column and isn't rescued unless you set `rescuedDataColumn`.","fix":"Add `.option(\"rescuedDataColumn\", \"_rescued_data\")` and monitor it, or change the schema deliberately."},{"title":"Exactly-once ≠ no duplicate keys","text":"Exactly-once file ingestion guarantees each *file* is ingested once. Two distinct files (an upstream retry, a resend, an update) can legitimately contain the same order_id.","fix":"Deduplicate / MERGE on the business key in Silver (or the CDC layer)."},{"title":"Transforming (and dropping) during ingestion","text":"Bronze that applies business rules, joins and drops malformed rows loses the raw evidence; when a rule is wrong you can't rebuild.","fix":"Ingestion ≠ transformation: keep Bronze conservative, do business logic in Silver/Gold."},{"title":"Millions of files arriving continuously","text":"Exam: 'millions of files arriving continuously in cloud object storage — which mechanism?' Answer: **Auto Loader** — not repeated spark.read, and typically not COPY INTO at that scale.","fix":"Scale + continuous arrivals → Auto Loader."},{"title":"Overwriting source files in place","text":"Incremental loaders reason about **file identity**. An overwritten file may be skipped as 'already loaded' (lost correction) or, with overwrites allowed, reprocessed (duplicates).","fix":"Agree on immutable landing zones: unique file names, corrections as new files."},{"title":"Don't blindly allow everything","text":"Turning on every evolution option lets an accidental upstream change (e.g. amount becoming a struct) flow silently into Silver and Gold.","fix":"Decide per change: evolve (intentional), rescue (unexpected but keep), or fail (contract violation)."},{"title":"multiLine + broken quote = swallowed rows","text":"With `multiLine=true`, an unclosed quote can make the parser treat the following lines as part of one field — several good rows can vanish into one bad record.","fix":"Inspect raw lines around the failure, fix quote/escape settings, and keep corrupt/rescued records visible instead of dropping them."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"What is ingestion?","a":"Bringing data that lives outside the platform into it — here, files into Delta tables.","section":"ch12-s01"},{"q":"Six questions every ingestion pipeline answers?","a":"Where is the data → discover → read → interpret with schema → remember what's processed → write reliably to Delta.","section":"ch12-s01"},{"q":"Is `spark.read` batch or incremental?","a":"Batch: reads the path's current contents, no memory of earlier runs.","section":"ch12-s02"},{"q":"CSV without the header option gives which column names?","a":"`_c0, _c1, _c2…` — and the header line becomes a data row.","section":"ch12-s02"},{"q":"Option for a pipe-separated file?","a":"`.option(\"delimiter\", \"|\")` (alias `sep`).","section":"ch12-s02"},{"q":"Three CSV `mode` values?","a":"PERMISSIVE (default, nulls + optional corrupt column), DROPMALFORMED (drop), FAILFAST (throw).","section":"ch12-s02"},{"q":"CSV with no schema and no inferSchema → column types?","a":"All STRING.","section":"ch12-s02"},{"q":"Why does schema inference cost time?","a":"Spark must inspect the data to decide types before the real read (extra pass).","section":"ch12-s03"},{"q":"Two ways to pass an explicit schema?","a":"A StructType of StructFields, or a DDL string like `\"order_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE\"`.","section":"ch12-s03"},{"q":"Five benefits of explicit schema in production?","a":"Faster (no inference pass), predictable, version-controlled, no type surprises, easier validation.","section":"ch12-s03"},{"q":"Schema inference bug example?","a":"Monday amounts 100, 200 → INT; Tuesday 100.50 → DOUBLE: the inferred type changes between runs.","section":"ch12-s03"},{"q":"What does a nested JSON object become?","a":"A struct column (e.g. `customer.name`).","section":"ch12-s04"},{"q":"Why doesn't Parquet need inferSchema?","a":"Its schema (names + types) is stored in the file.","section":"ch12-s04"},{"q":"CSV vs JSON vs Parquet in one line each?","a":"CSV: textual, weak types. JSON: semi-structured, types from content. Parquet: columnar, schema in file.","section":"ch12-s04"},{"q":"What is `read_files`?","a":"Databricks SQL table-valued function reading CSV/JSON/XML/text/binary/Parquet/Avro/ORC, with format/schema inference.","section":"ch12-s04"},{"q":"`STREAM read_files(...)` in a streaming table uses…?","a":"Auto Loader under the hood.","section":"ch12-s04"},{"q":"How do you get the source file path of a row?","a":"`_metadata.file_path` (hidden column; select it explicitly).","section":"ch12-s05"},{"q":"Minimum audit columns in Bronze?","a":"`_source_file` and `_ingested_at` (plus `_rescued_data`).","section":"ch12-s05"},{"q":"Run plain spark.read + append twice on the same files →?","a":"Duplicate rows: no file-history memory.","section":"ch12-s06"},{"q":"Problems of filtering input by date folder?","a":"Late files, backfills, re-uploads, timezone bugs, renamed files, missing folders.","section":"ch12-s06"},{"q":"What does COPY INTO remember?","a":"Which source files it has already loaded → retryable, idempotent loads.","section":"ch12-s06"},{"q":"Does COPY INTO reload an already-loaded file whose content changed?","a":"No — skipped even if modified (unless `COPY_OPTIONS ('force'='true')`).","section":"ch12-s06"},{"q":"COPY INTO target must be…?","a":"A Delta table.","section":"ch12-s06"},{"q":"What is PATTERN in COPY INTO?","a":"A glob filter on source file names, e.g. `'orders_*.csv'`.","section":"ch12-s07"},{"q":"FILES limit per COPY INTO statement?","a":"Up to 1000 file names.","section":"ch12-s07"},{"q":"What does VALIDATE do?","a":"Checks parsing, schema compatibility and constraints without writing (`VALIDATE ALL` / `VALIDATE n ROWS`).","section":"ch12-s07"},{"q":"FORMAT_OPTIONS vs COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema?","a":"FORMAT: merge source files' schemas. COPY: evolve the target Delta schema.","section":"ch12-s07"},{"q":"COPY INTO vs Auto Loader by scale?","a":"Thousands of files → COPY INTO fine; millions+ → Auto Loader.","section":"ch12-s08"},{"q":"What does `format(\"cloudFiles\")` mean?","a":"Use Auto Loader; the file format goes in `cloudFiles.format`.","section":"ch12-s08"},{"q":"Why `readStream` for Auto Loader?","a":"It's built on Structured Streaming: new files processed incrementally in micro-batches.","section":"ch12-s08"},{"q":"Two Auto Loader misconceptions?","a":"'It's a faster spark.read' and 'it turns files into Kafka' — both false.","section":"ch12-s08"},{"q":"schemaLocation vs checkpointLocation?","a":"schemaLocation = shape (inferred schema + history, `_schemas`). checkpoint = progress/state.","section":"ch12-s09"},{"q":"Why must checkpoints be persistent?","a":"They must survive compute restart/termination — never `/tmp`.","section":"ch12-s09"},{"q":"Is schemaLocation needed with a full explicit schema?","a":"Not in the same way (no inference/evolution); the checkpoint still is.","section":"ch12-s09"},{"q":"Checkpoint deleted → ?","a":"Stream loses progress; old files may be seen as new → duplicates or reconciliation.","section":"ch12-s10"},{"q":"Can two streams share a checkpoint?","a":"No — one checkpoint per streaming query.","section":"ch12-s10"},{"q":"Changed the source path — keep the checkpoint?","a":"No: checkpoint belongs to the query's source topology; new source → new checkpoint.","section":"ch12-s10"},{"q":"Two file-discovery modes?","a":"Directory listing vs file notification / managed file events.","section":"ch12-s11"},{"q":"Auto Loader default types for JSON/CSV/XML?","a":"STRING — unless `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true`.","section":"ch12-s11"},{"q":"Default sample for initial inference?","a":"Up to 50 GB or 1000 files, whichever first.","section":"ch12-s11"},{"q":"What do schema hints do?","a":"`cloudFiles.schemaHints`: set types for specific columns while inferring the rest; for Parquet they're not a post-read cast.","section":"ch12-s11"},{"q":"Default schemaEvolutionMode and what it does?","a":"addNewColumns (no schema given): stream stops on a new column, records it, continues after restart.","section":"ch12-s12"},{"q":"What is `_rescued_data`?","a":"A column preserving values/fields that don't match the schema instead of losing them.","section":"ch12-s12"},{"q":"Quarantine pattern?","a":"Rows with `_rescued_data IS NOT NULL` → quarantine table; clean rows → Silver.","section":"ch12-s12"},{"q":"Explicit schema + new upstream column →?","a":"Ignored (mode none) unless rescuedDataColumn is set.","section":"ch12-s12"},{"q":"Does exactly-once file ingestion prevent duplicate order_ids?","a":"No — distinct files can contain the same key; dedupe/MERGE in Silver.","section":"ch12-s13"},{"q":"Why keep Bronze between Auto Loader and Silver?","a":"Source fidelity, replay, audit, schema drift, rescued data, debugging.","section":"ch12-s13"},{"q":"What does AvailableNow do?","a":"`.trigger(availableNow=True)`: process all available files, then stop — batch-like incremental jobs.","section":"ch12-s14"},{"q":"When COPY INTO?","a":"Moderate file counts, SQL-centric, simple, idempotent re-runs, selective reloads.","section":"ch12-s14"},{"q":"New files but no rows — the 8-step chain?","a":"Exist → path → format → discovered → already processed → parse → schema → sink.","section":"ch12-s15"},{"q":"Why immutable source files?","a":"Loaders track file identity; overwrites get skipped (lost corrections) or duplicated.","section":"ch12-s15"},{"q":"Duplicate IDs — first question?","a":"Same file loaded twice, or two different files containing the ID?","section":"ch12-s16"},{"q":"Parser options to check for malformed CSV?","a":"quote, escape, multiLine, delimiter, header (and mode).","section":"ch12-s16"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch12-e001","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Which statement best defines **ingestion** in this chapter?","options":["Bringing data that lives outside the target platform into it, e.g. files → a Delta table","Aggregating Silver data into Gold metrics","Optimizing small files inside a Delta table","Granting SELECT on a Bronze table"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: outside → inside.","That's Gold-layer transformation.","That's table maintenance.","That's governance."],"explain":"Ingestion is the *entry* step: external data (files, exports, streams) gets into the platform. Aggregating to Gold is transformation, OPTIMIZE is table maintenance and GRANT is governance — all happen *after* data has been ingested."},{"id":"ch12-e002","type":"order","section":"ch12-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Put the six questions of an ingestion pipeline in order.","items":["Where does the data live?","Discover which files exist / are new","Read the files","Interpret them with a schema","Remember what was already processed","Write reliably to Delta"],"explain":"You can't read what you haven't discovered, can't type what you haven't read, and must record progress *before* the next run — otherwise you'll re-read. The final write must be transactional (Delta) so a crash doesn't leave half a load."},{"id":"ch12-e003","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Phase 4 ingestion is mainly about consuming Kafka topics.","answer":false,"explain":"Phase 4 focuses on **file-based ingestion**: CSV/JSON/Parquet files landing in storage. Kafka appears in the list of external sources, but message-bus streaming belongs to the Structured Streaming phase."},{"id":"ch12-e004","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"The operational system writes one orders file every 5 minutes, around the clock. How many files land per day?","answer":288,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"24 h × 60 min / 5 min = 288 files/day — about 105,000 per year. That's why 're-read the whole folder every run' becomes painful quickly and why file-tracking tools exist."},{"id":"ch12-e005","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are external **sources** that ingestion reads from. Which one is not?","options":["A folder of CSV exports","An API export dumped as JSON","A Kafka topic","The Gold `daily_sales` table"],"answer":3,"explain":"CSV exports, API dumps and Kafka topics all live outside the lakehouse and must be ingested. A Gold table is the *end* of the pipeline inside the platform — it's an output, not an ingestion source."},{"id":"ch12-e006","type":"match","section":"ch12-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each rung of the ladder to what it adds.","pairs":[["spark.read","Reads whatever is in the path right now"],["COPY INTO","Remembers which files it already loaded (SQL, idempotent)"],["Auto Loader","Incremental file discovery as a stream"],["cloudFiles.schemaLocation","Remembers the inferred schema and its evolution"],["Rescued data column","Keeps values that don't fit the schema"]],"explain":"Each rung solves a problem the previous one leaves open: spark.read has no memory; COPY INTO adds per-file memory; Auto Loader adds scalable discovery + checkpointed progress; schemaLocation adds schema memory; rescued data stops information loss."},{"id":"ch12-e007","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"`spark.read.json(path)` is best described as…","options":["A batch read of whatever files are in the path at this moment","An incremental read that skips files already read yesterday","A streaming read that waits for new files","A read that only loads files modified today"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","No memory: that's COPY INTO / Auto Loader.","That's readStream/Auto Loader.","No such default filter exists."],"explain":"`spark.read` is plain **batch**: it enumerates the path's *current* contents and reads them all. It has no memory of previous runs — that's exactly the gap COPY INTO and Auto Loader fill."},{"id":"ch12-e008","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete a CSV read that uses the first line as column names.","text":"df = (\n    spark.[[read]]\n        .format(\"[[csv]]\")\n        .option(\"[[header]]\", \"true\")\n        .[[load]](path)\n)","bank":["readStream","inferSchema","save","json"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`spark.read` → `.format(\"csv\")` → `.option(\"header\",\"true\")` → `.load(path)`. `readStream` would make it a streaming read, and `inferSchema` is a different option (types, not names)."},{"id":"ch12-e009","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"If you read a CSV without the header option, Spark names the columns `_c0`, `_c1`, `_c2`, …","answer":true,"explain":"Without `header=true` Spark doesn't know names exist, so it generates positional names — and also treats the real header line as a data row. That second effect is the nasty part."},{"id":"ch12-e010","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A pipe-separated file (`1001|17|25.90`) is read with `spark.read.format(\"csv\").load(path)`. What do you get?","options":["One column `_c0` holding each whole line","Three correctly split columns","An immediate parse error","An empty DataFrame"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Only with delimiter '|'.","No error: PERMISSIVE parsing just yields one field.","Rows are read, just not split."],"explain":"The default separator is a comma. With no comma in the line, the whole line is one field. Spark does not error — that's why a wrong grammar is dangerous: it gives quietly wrong data."},{"id":"ch12-e011","type":"write","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write PySpark that reads a **pipe-delimited** CSV **with a header line** from `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_pipe/` into `df`.","solution":"df = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n        .option(\"delimiter\", \"|\")\n        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_pipe/\")\n)","keywords":["spark.read","format(\"csv\")","option(\"header\", \"true\")","option(\"delimiter\", \"|\")",".load("],"lang":"python","explain":"Two grammar facts must be told to the parser: the first line is names (`header`) and the separator is `|` (`delimiter`, alias `sep`). Leave either out and you get `_c0` columns or one-column rows."},{"id":"ch12-e012","type":"match","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each CSV option to the problem it solves.","pairs":[["quote","A field like \"Athens, GR\" is split into two"],["escape","A quote character inside a quoted field breaks parsing"],["multiLine","A quoted address containing a line break becomes two rows"],["nullValue","The text NULL is stored as a string instead of null"],["delimiter","A ;-separated file is read as one column"]],"explain":"Each option describes one grammar rule. Debug a broken CSV by asking which rule the parser got wrong: separators, quoting, escaping, newlines inside fields or null spelling."},{"id":"ch12-e013","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"The file `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_pipe/` looks like `order_id|customer_id|amount` then `1001|17|25.90`. Which line is wrong?","lines":["df = (","    spark.read","        .format(\"csv\")","        .option(\"header\", \"true\")","        .option(\"delimiter\", \",\")","        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_pipe/\")",")"],"bugs":[4],"fix":".option(\"delimiter\", \"|\")","explain":"The header option is right, but the delimiter says comma while the file uses pipes. Result: a single column named `order_id|customer_id|amount` holding whole lines — no error, just wrong data."},{"id":"ch12-e014","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Sort each behaviour into the CSV `mode` that produces it.","buckets":["PERMISSIVE","DROPMALFORMED","FAILFAST"],"items":[{"text":"Default mode","bucket":0},{"text":"Keeps the bad row, sets unparsable fields to null","bucket":0},{"text":"Can keep the raw bad line in `_corrupt_record`","bucket":0},{"text":"Bad rows silently disappear","bucket":1},{"text":"Row counts shrink without any error","bucket":1},{"text":"Job throws on the first malformed row","bucket":2},{"text":"Best when any bad row must stop the load","bucket":2}],"explain":"PERMISSIVE keeps everything (nulls + optional corrupt-record column), DROPMALFORMED hides problems by dropping rows, FAILFAST makes problems loud. In Bronze, silently dropping (DROPMALFORMED) is usually the worst choice because you lose evidence."},{"id":"ch12-e015","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A CSV read with neither `.schema(...)` nor `inferSchema=true` returns typed columns (INT, DOUBLE…) automatically.","answer":false,"explain":"CSV is plain text with no native types. Without a schema or inference, every column is **STRING**. JSON is different: the JSON reader infers types from the values by default."},{"id":"ch12-e016","type":"free","section":"ch12-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"In your own words: what does it mean that \"the file format is a grammar\", and how does that help you debug a broken CSV read?","model":"A parser can only split and type a file correctly if it knows the file's rules: separator, header line, quote and escape characters, whether fields contain newlines, how nulls are spelled. When a CSV reads wrong I don't randomly toggle options — I look at the raw file, list its grammar rules and compare them with the options I passed (header, delimiter, quote, escape, multiLine, nullValue, mode).","rubric":["Parser needs the file's structural rules","Names concrete rules: separator, header, quote/escape, multiLine, null spelling","Debug by comparing raw file vs options passed","Wrong grammar often gives silently wrong data, not errors"],"explain":"Thinking of options as grammar turns option-guessing into a checklist. It also explains why the worst CSV bugs don't throw: the parser happily applies the wrong grammar."},{"id":"ch12-e017","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Why does schema inference on CSV/JSON cost time?","options":["Spark must inspect the data to decide types before reading it for real","Spark must contact Unity Catalog for every column","Inference rewrites the files as Parquet first","Inference forces a shuffle of all rows"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","UC isn't involved in file type guessing.","No rewrite happens.","No shuffle needed for inference."],"explain":"Types aren't stored in CSV/JSON, so Spark samples/scans input, decides types, then reads again. Explicit schema removes that pass. Unity Catalog, rewriting to Parquet and shuffles have nothing to do with inference."},{"id":"ch12-e018","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Supplying an explicit schema lets the reader skip the schema-inference pass.","answer":true,"explain":"That's the documented performance benefit: with `.schema(...)` the reader trusts your contract and goes straight to reading. Predictability is the other, often bigger, benefit."},{"id":"ch12-e019","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the explicit schema.","text":"schema = [[StructType]]([\n    [[StructField]](\"order_id\", [[LongType]](), True),\n    StructField(\"amount\", [[DoubleType]](), True),\n    StructField(\"status\", StringType(), True),\n])\ndf = spark.read.[[schema]](schema).json(path)","bank":["IntegerType","inferSchema","StructColumn","FloatType"],"asCode":true,"explain":"A StructType is a list of StructFields (name, type instance, nullable). BIGINT ↔ LongType, DOUBLE ↔ DoubleType. The reader method is `.schema(...)` — `inferSchema` is the opposite idea."},{"id":"ch12-e020","type":"write","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a **DDL-string** schema for `order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE, status STRING` and use it to read JSON from `path` into `df`.","solution":"schema = \"order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE, status STRING\"\n\ndf = (\n    spark.read\n        .schema(schema)\n        .json(path)\n)","keywords":["order_id bigint","amount double","status string",".schema(schema)",".json("],"lang":"python","explain":"Databricks accepts a DDL string anywhere a StructType is accepted — no imports needed, and it reads like a CREATE TABLE column list. The reader then skips inference."},{"id":"ch12-e021","type":"match","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the DDL type to its StructType class.","pairs":[["BIGINT","LongType()"],["INT","IntegerType()"],["DOUBLE","DoubleType()"],["STRING","StringType()"],["TIMESTAMP","TimestampType()"]],"explain":"BIGINT is a 64-bit long, INT a 32-bit integer. Mixing them up (INT for order IDs) is a classic overflow risk on large ID ranges."},{"id":"ch12-e022","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This schema crashes when the read runs. Which line is buggy?","lines":["schema = StructType([","    StructField(\"order_id\", LongType(), True),","    StructField(\"amount\", DoubleType, True),","    StructField(\"status\", StringType(), True),","])","df = spark.read.schema(schema).json(path)"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"StructField(\"amount\", DoubleType(), True),","explain":"`DoubleType` without parentheses passes the *class*, not a DataType *instance*. StructField needs an instance like `DoubleType()`. A DDL string avoids this whole class of typos."},{"id":"ch12-e023","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one is NOT a reason to prefer explicit schema for production ingestion?","options":["Faster: no inference pass","Predictable and version-controlled contract","No accidental type surprises","New upstream columns are adopted automatically without review"],"answer":3,"explain":"An explicit schema does the opposite of auto-adopting: changes must be made deliberately and reviewed. That's a feature — but it means you need a plan for new columns (rescued data / evolution policy)."},{"id":"ch12-e024","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Exploration or production? Choose the better default.","buckets":["Schema inference is fine","Use an explicit schema"],"items":[{"text":"Peeking at a vendor's sample file in a notebook","bucket":0},{"text":"One-off ad-hoc analysis of a CSV","bucket":0},{"text":"Nightly Bronze load feeding finance dashboards","bucket":1},{"text":"A pipeline whose downstream MERGE expects BIGINT keys","bucket":1},{"text":"A job reviewed and deployed through CI/CD","bucket":1},{"text":"Figuring out what columns a new feed even has","bucket":0}],"explain":"Inference trades predictability for convenience — fine when a human is looking at the result. Anything scheduled, shared, or feeding typed downstream tables needs a contract that doesn't change silently."},{"id":"ch12-e025","type":"write","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab (CSV ingestion): the file has `order_id,customer_id,amount,status` + rows like `1001,10,19.50,NEW`. Read `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/` as CSV with the header line and an explicit DDL schema (`order_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE, status STRING`), then `display` it.","solution":"schema = \"\"\"\n  order_id BIGINT,\n  customer_id BIGINT,\n  amount DOUBLE,\n  status STRING\n\"\"\"\n\ndf = (\n    spark.read\n        .format(\"csv\")\n        .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n        .schema(schema)\n        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/\")\n)\ndisplay(df)","keywords":["format(\"csv\")","option(\"header\", \"true\")",".schema(schema)","order_id bigint","amount double","display(df)"],"lang":"python","explain":"Header gives the names, the explicit schema gives the types — no inference pass, and the contract is reviewable. `header` is still needed with an explicit schema, otherwise the header line is parsed as a (malformed) data row."},{"id":"ch12-e026","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"When you pass an explicit schema to a CSV read, the `header` option no longer matters.","answer":false,"explain":"The schema sets names and types, but the parser still needs to know that line 1 is a header. Without `header=true` the header line is parsed as data (and fails the BIGINT/DOUBLE casts → nulls or corrupt rows)."},{"id":"ch12-e027","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Monday's CSVs have `amount` = 100, 200 (inferred INT). Tuesday a file has `100.50`. What is the risk with inferSchema?","options":["The inferred type changes (INT → DOUBLE) or parsing breaks, so downstream writes can hit schema mismatches","Spark rejects Tuesday's file because INT is locked forever","Nothing — inference always picks the widest type ever seen","Spark converts 100.50 to 100 silently and keeps INT"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Inference has no memory between runs.","It only knows the data it sampled this run.","Not what happens; the type guess changes instead."],"explain":"Inference only sees the data it's given *this run*. A different sample → a different type. That's why ingestion needs a deliberate schema policy instead of letting each run guess."},{"id":"ch12-e028","type":"free","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Engineering question: **why would you prefer an explicit schema in production?** Give at least four reasons.","model":"An explicit schema avoids the inference pass (faster), gives a stable, version-controlled data contract, makes failures predictable (a bad value fails or is rescued the same way every time), prevents accidental type drift between runs, and forces schema changes to be reviewed instead of silently inferred. Databricks documents that supplying a schema lets the reader skip inference.","rubric":["Performance: skips inference pass","Stable/version-controlled contract","Predictable failures, easier debugging","No silent type drift","Schema changes are reviewed / controlled evolution"],"explain":"Big-Test Q2 answer in one line: performance, predictable contract, easier debugging, controlled evolution."},{"id":"ch12-e029","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your nightly CSV load (with `inferSchema=true`) suddenly fails writing to `bronze.orders_raw`: *schema mismatch on column amount*. The code hasn't changed in weeks.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Compare the inferred schema of today's input with the table schema","ok":true,"fb":"Right: an unchanged job + a new mismatch screams 'the inferred type changed'."},{"text":"Enable mergeSchema so the write passes","ok":false,"fb":"That hides the symptom and may widen your table's types without review."},{"text":"Restart the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Compute restarts don't change how types are inferred from data."}]},{"prompt":"Today's inference says `amount: double`; the table has `amount: int`. Next?","options":[{"text":"Find which file contains decimal amounts and ask upstream if that's intended","ok":true,"fb":"Yes: decide whether it's a contract change or a bad delivery."},{"text":"Cast amount to int in the read and move on","ok":false,"fb":"Truncating 100.50 to 100 silently corrupts money values."},{"text":"Delete today's files","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys evidence and real orders."}]},{"prompt":"Upstream confirms: prices now have cents. Long-term fix?","options":[{"text":"Use an explicit schema (e.g. DECIMAL/DOUBLE) committed to Git and evolve the table deliberately","ok":true,"fb":"Predictable, reviewed, no more guessing per run."},{"text":"Keep inferSchema but run the job twice","ok":false,"fb":"Inference is still a per-run guess."},{"text":"Switch to DROPMALFORMED mode","ok":false,"fb":"That would silently drop the decimal rows."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e030","type":"order","section":"ch12-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'inferred types drift between runs'.","items":["Confirm the read relies on inference","Diff yesterday's vs today's inferred schema","Locate the file that introduced the new values","Ask upstream whether the change is intended","Pin an explicit schema (or STRING in Bronze) in Git"],"explain":"Prove the mechanism (inference) before acting, find the evidence (file), decide with the owner, then remove the cause (pinned contract). Jumping straight to 'enable evolution' skips the decision."},{"id":"ch12-e031","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"By default `spark.read.json` expects one JSON object per line.","answer":true,"explain":"The default is JSON Lines. A pretty-printed object spanning several lines needs `multiLine=true`, otherwise you'll see corrupt records."},{"id":"ch12-e032","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"You read `{\"order_id\": 1, \"customer\": {\"id\": 100, \"name\": \"Maria\"}}` with `spark.read.json`. What type is `customer`?","options":["struct<id: long, name: string>","string","map<string,string>","array<string>"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Only with a schema declaring STRING.","Map only if you declare it.","Arrays come from JSON lists [...]."],"explain":"A nested JSON object becomes a **struct** with its own typed fields; you access `customer.name`. It is not flattened to a string, and Spark doesn't choose map unless you declare one."},{"id":"ch12-e033","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Sort each property to its format.","buckets":["CSV","JSON","Parquet"],"items":[{"text":"Textual, weak/no native types","bucket":0},{"text":"Needs header/delimiter options","bucket":0},{"text":"Semi-structured with nested objects","bucket":1},{"text":"Types inferred from content","bucket":1},{"text":"Columnar","bucket":2},{"text":"Schema encoded in the file","bucket":2},{"text":"No parsing options needed to get types","bucket":2}],"explain":"CSV is flat text; JSON is text with structure; Parquet is binary, columnar and self-describing. That's why raw CSV/JSON is landed and then written into Delta/Parquet-backed tables."},{"id":"ch12-e034","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Parquet files need `inferSchema=true` to get typed columns.","answer":false,"explain":"Parquet stores its schema in the file's metadata, so types come for free. `inferSchema` is a CSV-parser option."},{"id":"ch12-e035","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the SQL that reads CSV files with an explicit schema.","text":"SELECT *\nFROM [[read_files]](\n  '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/',\n  format [[=>]] 'csv',\n  [[schema]] => 'order_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE'\n);","bank":["read_csv","=","columns","spark.read"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`read_files` is a table-valued function with named arguments written `name => value`. `format` and `schema` are the two you'll use most."},{"id":"ch12-e036","type":"write","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a Databricks SQL query that reads JSON files from `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/` with `read_files`.","solution":"SELECT *\nFROM read_files(\n  '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/',\n  format => 'json'\n);","keywords":["select","from read_files(","/volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/","format => 'json'"],"lang":"sql","explain":"In SQL you don't need spark.read: `read_files(path, format => ...)` returns a table you can SELECT from. Omit `format` and it can infer it; add `schema =>` for a contract."},{"id":"ch12-e037","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which one can `read_files` NOT read directly?","options":["Avro files","ORC files","XML files","A Kafka topic"],"answer":3,"explain":"read_files reads **files** (CSV, JSON, XML, text, binary, Parquet, Avro, ORC). A Kafka topic is a message stream, consumed with a streaming Kafka source, not a file reader."},{"id":"ch12-e038","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"In a Lakeflow streaming table, `SELECT * FROM STREAM read_files('/Volumes/.../orders/', format => 'json')` ingests files using…","options":["Auto Loader under the hood","Repeated full spark.read of the folder","COPY INTO","A Kafka connector"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","That wouldn't be incremental.","COPY INTO is a separate SQL command.","Files aren't Kafka."],"explain":"In streaming-table context Databricks uses **Auto Loader** for incremental file ingestion. So checkpoints, schema inference/evolution and rescued data behave as in this chapter."},{"id":"ch12-e039","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Which expression gives the full path of the file a row came from?","options":["`_metadata.file_path`","`_source_file()`","`spark.read.path`","`current_file()`"],"answer":0,"explain":"`_metadata` is the hidden file-metadata column; `file_path` is its full-path field. You then usually alias it to `_source_file`. The other options don't exist."},{"id":"ch12-e040","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"`df.select(\"*\")` on a file-based DataFrame automatically includes the `_metadata` column.","answer":false,"explain":"`_metadata` is hidden by design so it doesn't pollute schemas; you must select it (or its fields) explicitly."},{"id":"ch12-e041","type":"write","section":"ch12-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Add two audit columns to `df` (read from files): `_source_file` from the file path metadata, and `_ingested_at` = current timestamp. Use `F` = `pyspark.sql.functions`.","solution":"bronze_df = df.select(\n    \"*\",\n    F.col(\"_metadata.file_path\").alias(\"_source_file\"),\n    F.current_timestamp().alias(\"_ingested_at\"),\n)","keywords":["_metadata.file_path","alias(\"_source_file\")","current_timestamp()","alias(\"_ingested_at\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"File path + ingestion time are the minimum audit pair. With them, a Silver incident can be traced to a concrete file and moment instead of guessed."},{"id":"ch12-e042","type":"order","section":"ch12-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A finance analyst finds a wrong amount in Silver. Order the trace.","items":["Identify the Silver row and its business key","Find the matching Bronze row(s)","Read the Bronze row's `_source_file` and `_ingested_at`","Open the original file in the landing zone","Decide: source error or transformation error"],"explain":"Walk backwards one layer at a time. Without `_source_file` the chain breaks at step 3 and you're left grepping thousands of files."},{"id":"ch12-e043","type":"free","section":"ch12-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Why are `_source_file` and `_ingested_at` worth adding to every Bronze table? Answer as you would to a teammate who says 'they waste space'.","model":"They are cheap audit evidence. When a Silver or Gold value is wrong, they let me trace the row back to the exact file and load time, prove whether the source sent bad data or our transformation broke it, find all rows from a bad delivery to quarantine or reprocess, and detect duplicate loads (same file twice). A few bytes per row buy fast incident resolution.","rubric":["Trace Silver → Bronze → original file","Distinguish source error vs transformation error","Find/reprocess all rows of one bad file","Detect duplicate or late loads","Cheap compared to debugging time"],"explain":"Observability is a design decision made at ingestion time; you can't add the file path later once the files are gone."},{"id":"ch12-e044","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the SQL that keeps the source file path.","text":"SELECT *, [[_metadata]].[[file_path]] AS _source_file\nFROM read_files('/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/', format => 'json');","bank":["input","path","_file"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`_metadata.file_path` works in SQL file reads too (read_files, COPY INTO's SELECT). Alias it so the column has a stable Bronze name."},{"id":"ch12-e045","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Two files (1,000 rows each) sit in `orders/`. You run `spark.read.json(path)` + `.mode(\"append\")` into Bronze **three times** with no new files. How many rows does Bronze hold?","answer":6000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"Each run re-reads both files (2,000 rows) and appends: 3 × 2,000 = 6,000 rows, 4,000 of them duplicates. spark.read has no memory of earlier runs."},{"id":"ch12-e046","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Same folder, but with COPY INTO: run 1 (file1, file2 present), run 2 (nothing new), then file3 (1,000 rows) lands and run 3 executes. Total rows in Bronze?","answer":3000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"Run 1 loads 2,000; run 2 skips both files; run 3 skips file1/file2 and loads file3 → 3,000 rows, no duplicates. COPY INTO tracks loaded files."},{"id":"ch12-e047","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Re-running the same `COPY INTO` against the same source directory loads the already-loaded files a second time.","answer":false,"explain":"Already-loaded files are **skipped by default** — that's COPY INTO's idempotency and a classic exam trap. Only `COPY_OPTIONS ('force'='true')` disables it."},{"id":"ch12-e048","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"What does COPY INTO remember between runs?","options":["Which source files it has already loaded into the target","The last value of every business key","The full content hash of every row","Only the timestamp of the last run"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct (Big-Test Q4).","No business-key tracking.","It tracks files, not row hashes.","A timestamp alone couldn't skip late/old files reliably."],"explain":"COPY INTO keeps per-file load history for the target table, which makes it retryable and idempotent at the **file** level. It does not know about business keys or row hashes."},{"id":"ch12-e049","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"If `file1.json` was loaded yesterday and upstream overwrote it today with corrected content, a normal COPY INTO run will load the new content.","answer":false,"explain":"Already-loaded files are skipped **even if modified**. The correction is silently ignored — which is exactly why sources should deliver new, immutable files."},{"id":"ch12-e050","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"You *deliberately* need COPY INTO to reload files it already loaded. Which clause?","options":["COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')","FORMAT_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')","VALIDATE ALL","PATTERN = '*'"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct — and expect duplicates unless you clean up.","Schema merging, not reloading.","Validation writes nothing.","Filtering doesn't bypass load history."],"explain":"`force` disables the idempotency check. mergeSchema is about schemas, VALIDATE doesn't write at all, and PATTERN only filters names — already-loaded matches are still skipped."},{"id":"ch12-e051","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Problems of 'filter the path by today's date' incremental logic?","buckets":["Real risk of the naive approach","Not a problem it has"],"items":[{"text":"A late file for yesterday lands after today's run","bucket":0},{"text":"Upstream backfills last month","bucket":0},{"text":"A corrected file is re-uploaded into the same day folder","bucket":0},{"text":"UTC vs local-time folder names","bucket":0},{"text":"The expected folder doesn't exist today","bucket":0},{"text":"Spark can't read JSON from a dated folder","bucket":1},{"text":"Delta can't append to a table twice a day","bucket":1}],"explain":"All the real risks come from assuming a perfect upstream layout. Spark and Delta are fine with dated folders — the logic of 'which files are new' is what's fragile."},{"id":"ch12-e052","type":"write","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write COPY INTO that loads CSV files with a header line from `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/` into `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`.","solution":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS (\n  'header' = 'true'\n);","keywords":["copy into ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw","from '/volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'","fileformat = csv","format_options","'header' = 'true'"],"lang":"sql","explain":"COPY INTO = target, FROM source path, FILEFORMAT, then parser options in FORMAT_OPTIONS. The target must be Delta."},{"id":"ch12-e053","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the COPY INTO statement.","text":"[[COPY INTO]] ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\n[[FROM]] '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/'\n[[FILEFORMAT]] = JSON;","bank":["INSERT INTO","FORMAT","LOCATION"],"asCode":true,"explain":"The three mandatory pieces: `COPY INTO <delta table>`, `FROM <path or SELECT>`, `FILEFORMAT = <format>`."},{"id":"ch12-e054","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This COPY INTO fails. Which lines are wrong?","lines":["COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw","FROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'","FORMAT = CSV","OPTIONS (","  'header' = 'true'",");"],"bugs":[2,3],"fix":"FILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS (\n  'header' = 'true'\n);","explain":"The keywords are `FILEFORMAT` (not FORMAT) and `FORMAT_OPTIONS` for parser options (not OPTIONS). `COPY_OPTIONS` is a separate clause for load behavior like mergeSchema or force."},{"id":"ch12-e055","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"COPY INTO can write into any table format, e.g. a CSV-backed external table.","answer":false,"explain":"The COPY INTO target must be a **Delta table**. The idempotent load history lives with that Delta target."},{"id":"ch12-e056","type":"free","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q3: what happens if you run `spark.read` over the same whole directory every day — and how would you fix it?","model":"It re-reads the directory's current contents every day — all old files plus the new ones — because spark.read keeps no file history. Appending that to Bronze duplicates everything already loaded. Fixes: manually restrict the input (fragile: late files, backfills, re-uploads), or use a tool that tracks processed files: COPY INTO (idempotent, skips loaded files) or Auto Loader (checkpointed incremental discovery).","rubric":["Reads all current files again","No file-history memory","Append → duplicates","Manual path filtering is fragile","COPY INTO / Auto Loader track processed files"],"explain":"The question tests whether you see that 'batch read' and 'incremental' are different properties."},{"id":"ch12-e057","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A teammate re-ran the Bronze notebook (`spark.read.json(path)` → `.mode(\"append\")`) after a timeout. Now `orders_raw` has every order twice.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is your first hypothesis to verify?","options":[{"text":"The re-run re-read files that were already loaded","ok":true,"fb":"Yes: plain spark.read reads the whole folder every time."},{"text":"Auto Loader's checkpoint was deleted","ok":false,"fb":"There's no Auto Loader here — it's a plain batch read."},{"text":"Delta wrote each row twice internally","ok":false,"fb":"Delta writes exactly what the DataFrame contains."}]},{"prompt":"How do you prove it?","options":[{"text":"Group by `_source_file` and `_ingested_at`: each file appears with two ingestion times","ok":true,"fb":"That's the fingerprint of a re-read."},{"text":"Run OPTIMIZE and recount","ok":false,"fb":"OPTIMIZE compacts files; it doesn't change row counts."},{"text":"Check cluster logs for OOM","ok":false,"fb":"Memory has nothing to do with duplication here."}]},{"prompt":"Durable fix?","options":[{"text":"Switch to COPY INTO (or Auto Loader) and clean up the duplicate append","ok":true,"fb":"Idempotent loading makes the next retry harmless."},{"text":"Tell everyone never to re-run notebooks","ok":false,"fb":"Retries are normal; the loader must be safe to re-run."},{"text":"Add `.distinct()` before the write","ok":false,"fb":"Distinct within one run doesn't stop duplicates across runs."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e058","type":"order","section":"ch12-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'Bronze doubled after a re-run'.","items":["Confirm the loader is plain spark.read + append","Group rows by `_source_file` and `_ingested_at`","Find the extra append in DESCRIBE HISTORY","Remove the duplicate load (RESTORE or DELETE)","Replace the loader with COPY INTO / Auto Loader"],"explain":"Mechanism → evidence → history → repair → prevention. Fixing data without changing the loader guarantees the next retry repeats the incident."},{"id":"ch12-e059","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"What does `PATTERN` do in COPY INTO?","options":["Filters which source files are loaded, by a glob on their names","Validates each row against a regex","Renames the loaded files","Sets the CSV delimiter"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Rows aren't regex-validated by PATTERN.","COPY INTO never renames files.","Delimiter lives in FORMAT_OPTIONS."],"explain":"PATTERN is a source-file **filter** (Big-Test Q6). It's a glob on file names/paths — not row validation, not renaming, not a parser option."},{"id":"ch12-e060","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The folder holds `orders_001.csv`, `orders_002.csv`, `customers_001.csv`. This loads **nothing**. Which line is the bug?","lines":["COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw","FROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/incoming/'","FILEFORMAT = CSV","PATTERN = 'orders_.*[.]csv'","FORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true');"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"PATTERN = 'orders_*.csv'","explain":"PATTERN is a **glob**. `'orders_.*[.]csv'` as a glob needs a literal dot right after `orders_`, so `orders_001.csv` doesn't match. The glob `'orders_*.csv'` does. (This is a correction of the tutoring notes' regex-style example.)"},{"id":"ch12-e061","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"You must load an explicit list of 2,500 file names with the FILES clause. What is the minimum number of COPY INTO statements?","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"statements","explain":"FILES accepts at most 1000 names per statement: ceil(2500 / 1000) = 3 statements (1000 + 1000 + 500)."},{"id":"ch12-e062","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"You can use FILES and PATTERN together in the same COPY INTO statement.","answer":false,"explain":"They're two alternative ways of choosing files: an explicit list *or* a glob. Pick one per statement."},{"id":"ch12-e063","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What does `VALIDATE ALL` do in COPY INTO?","options":["Checks parsing, schema compatibility and constraints for all data without writing anything","Loads the data and then runs CHECK constraints","Deletes rows that fail validation from the target","Re-validates files that were already loaded"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","VALIDATE never writes.","It never deletes.","It's about the files in this statement, not history."],"explain":"VALIDATE is a dry run (Big-Test Q7): parse/schema/nullability/CHECK constraints are tested, **nothing is written**. The other options all imply a write or a delete."},{"id":"ch12-e064","type":"write","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a COPY INTO that only **validates** (no load) CSV files with a header in `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/` against `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`.","solution":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_csv/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true')\nVALIDATE ALL;","keywords":["copy into ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw","fileformat = csv","'header' = 'true'","validate all"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Add `VALIDATE ALL` (or `VALIDATE n ROWS`) at the end. Use it before the first real load of a new feed or when debugging a suspect batch."},{"id":"ch12-e065","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are checked by COPY INTO's VALIDATE. Which one is not?","options":["Files parse with the given format options","Schema is compatible with (or can evolve into) the target","NOT NULL / CHECK constraints hold","No business key appears twice across files"],"answer":3,"explain":"VALIDATE checks parsing, schema and table constraints. Business-key uniqueness is not a file-level concern — duplicates across files are handled downstream (dedup / MERGE in Silver)."},{"id":"ch12-e066","type":"write","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a COPY INTO from JSON in `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/` into `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw` that casts `order_id` to BIGINT, `amount` to DOUBLE and stores `upper(status) AS status`.","solution":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM (\n  SELECT\n    order_id::BIGINT,\n    amount::DOUBLE,\n    upper(status) AS status\n  FROM '/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/'\n)\nFILEFORMAT = JSON;","keywords":["copy into ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw","from (","select","::bigint","::double","upper(status) as status","fileformat = json"],"lang":"sql","explain":"The FROM becomes a SELECT over the path. Casts with `::TYPE`, functions like upper(), and `_metadata.file_path` are all allowed — COPY INTO is a parse-project-load, not a byte copy."},{"id":"ch12-e067","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"COPY INTO is just a byte-level file copy into the table's storage.","answer":false,"explain":"COPY INTO parses the files with the given format and can project, cast and compute columns via a SELECT before writing Delta rows."},{"id":"ch12-e068","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which mergeSchema does what?","buckets":["FORMAT_OPTIONS mergeSchema","COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema"],"items":[{"text":"Combines columns across source files that differ","bucket":0},{"text":"Concerns the files being read","bucket":0},{"text":"Adds new columns to the target Delta table","bucket":1},{"text":"Concerns the table being written","bucket":1},{"text":"File A has `coupon`, file B doesn't — read both into one schema","bucket":0},{"text":"Target table gets a new `discount_code` column","bucket":1}],"explain":"FORMAT_OPTIONS = how to read the source; COPY_OPTIONS = how to load into the target. Big-Test Q25 is exactly this distinction."},{"id":"ch12-e069","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete COPY INTO with inference and target-schema evolution.","text":"COPY INTO ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\nFROM '/path/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\n[[FORMAT_OPTIONS]] ('inferSchema' = 'true', 'mergeSchema' = 'true')\n[[COPY_OPTIONS]] ('[[mergeSchema]]' = 'true');","bank":["OPTIONS","TBLPROPERTIES","overwriteSchema","force"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Parser/source options go in FORMAT_OPTIONS; load behavior (target evolution, force) goes in COPY_OPTIONS."},{"id":"ch12-e070","type":"free","section":"ch12-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q25: explain the difference between `FORMAT_OPTIONS mergeSchema` and `COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema`.","model":"FORMAT_OPTIONS mergeSchema tells the reader to reconcile/merge the schemas of the source files being read (different files may have different columns). COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema allows the load to evolve the target Delta table's schema, adding new columns. To get a brand-new source column into the table you typically need both.","rubric":["FORMAT_OPTIONS = source files' schemas merged","COPY_OPTIONS = target Delta schema evolves","Often both needed for a new column"],"explain":"Mnemonic: FORMAT reads, COPY writes."},{"id":"ch12-e071","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"The folder holds 5,000,000 files and 100 new ones arrive per trigger. With naive full listing, how many file names are scanned per *new* file discovered?","answer":50000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"names","explain":"5,000,000 / 100 = 50,000 names listed for every new file found. The cost tracks the size of the folder, not the new data — that's what Auto Loader's incremental state avoids."},{"id":"ch12-e072","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"What does `.format(\"cloudFiles\")` mean?","options":["Use the Auto Loader source","Read files from any cloud provider in batch","Write the stream to cloud storage","Use COPY INTO under the hood"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","It's a streaming source, not batch.","It's a read-side format.","COPY INTO is a separate SQL command."],"explain":"`cloudFiles` is the Auto Loader source provider (Big-Test Q9). The actual file format is set with `cloudFiles.format`."},{"id":"ch12-e073","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Why does Auto Loader use `spark.readStream` even though the source is just files?","options":["Because it uses the Structured Streaming incremental-processing model (micro-batches of new files)","Because files are converted into Kafka messages first","Because readStream reads each file faster","Because batch reads can't read JSON"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Misconception: the source remains files.","Misconception: the gain is discovery/state, not per-file speed.","spark.read.json reads JSON fine."],"explain":"Big-Test Q10: Auto Loader is built on Structured Streaming. 'Streaming' here describes *how* new files are processed (incrementally, with a checkpoint), not the type of source."},{"id":"ch12-e074","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Auto Loader converts incoming files into a Kafka-like message log before processing them.","answer":false,"explain":"Misconception. The source remains files in storage; Auto Loader just discovers and processes them incrementally using the streaming engine."},{"id":"ch12-e075","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The main advantage of Auto Loader is that it reads each individual file faster than spark.read.","answer":false,"explain":"Its advantage is incremental discovery, state tracking (what's processed), schema handling and streaming integration — not per-file read speed."},{"id":"ch12-e076","type":"write","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a full Auto Loader Bronze stream: JSON files from `source_path`, schema stored at `schema_path`, checkpoint at `checkpoint_path`, written to `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`.","solution":"orders = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)\n        .load(source_path)\n)\n\nquery = (\n    orders.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint_path)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)","keywords":["readstream","format(\"cloudfiles\")","\"cloudfiles.format\", \"json\"","\"cloudfiles.schemalocation\", schema_path",".load(source_path)","writestream","\"checkpointlocation\", checkpoint_path","totable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Two halves: the read (cloudFiles + format + schemaLocation + load) and the write (writeStream + checkpointLocation + toTable). Forgetting either state location is the #1 skeleton bug."},{"id":"ch12-e077","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the Auto Loader skeleton.","text":"orders = (\n    spark.[[readStream]]\n        .format(\"[[cloudFiles]]\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.[[format]]\", \"json\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.[[schemaLocation]]\", schema_path)\n        .load(source_path)\n)\nquery = (\n    orders.[[writeStream]]\n        .option(\"[[checkpointLocation]]\", checkpoint_path)\n        .[[toTable]](\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)","bank":["read","autoloader","saveAsTable","checkpoint"],"asCode":true,"explain":"readStream + cloudFiles on the read side; writeStream + checkpointLocation + toTable on the write side. schemaLocation is a `cloudFiles.` option; checkpointLocation is not."},{"id":"ch12-e078","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"This 'Auto Loader' stream runs, but has no Auto Loader schema inference or file tracking features. Which line is wrong?","lines":["orders = (","    spark.readStream","        .format(\"json\")","        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)","        .load(source_path)",")"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")","explain":"`.format(\"json\")` creates a plain file stream; the `cloudFiles.*` options are then ignored. Auto Loader requires `.format(\"cloudFiles\")` plus `cloudFiles.format`."},{"id":"ch12-e079","type":"match","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each skeleton piece to its role.","pairs":[["format(\"cloudFiles\")","Selects Auto Loader"],["cloudFiles.format","Format of the incoming files"],["cloudFiles.schemaLocation","Stores inferred schema + history"],["checkpointLocation","Stores stream progress/state"],["toTable","Names the target table"]],"explain":"Read side: what to read and how to interpret it. Write side: where to put it and how to remember progress."},{"id":"ch12-e080","type":"free","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q1: what's the main difference between `spark.read` and Auto Loader?","model":"spark.read is ordinary batch file reading: it reads whatever is in the path right now and remembers nothing. Auto Loader runs on Structured Streaming and adds incremental file discovery and stateful processing: it tracks (in a checkpoint) which files were processed, picks up only new files, and manages schema inference/evolution in schemaLocation.","rubric":["spark.read = batch, current contents, no memory","Auto Loader = Structured Streaming source (cloudFiles)","Incremental discovery of new files","Checkpointed state / schema handling"],"explain":"Speed of reading a single file is not the difference — memory and discovery are."},{"id":"ch12-e081","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Per Databricks guidance, which ingestion tool fits a source that grows to **millions of files** over time?","options":["Auto Loader","COPY INTO","A nightly spark.read of the whole folder","INSERT INTO ... SELECT"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Suitable for thousands, not the recommended choice at millions+.","Re-reads everything every night.","No file discovery at all."],"explain":"COPY INTO is fine for thousands of files; at millions+ Auto Loader's incremental discovery and state are the recommended approach."},{"id":"ch12-e082","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"`cloudFiles.schemaLocation` and `checkpointLocation` are two names for the same thing.","answer":false,"explain":"Big-Test Q13: No. schemaLocation = schema inference/evolution history; checkpointLocation = streaming progress/state. They may even share a directory, but they answer different questions."},{"id":"ch12-e083","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","compare"],"quick":true,"q":"Which location holds it?","buckets":["cloudFiles.schemaLocation","checkpointLocation"],"items":[{"text":"The schema Auto Loader inferred","bucket":0},{"text":"Previous schema versions after evolution","bucket":0},{"text":"The `_schemas` folder","bucket":0},{"text":"Which files were already processed","bucket":1},{"text":"Batch ids / offsets of the stream","bucket":1},{"text":"What lets a restart continue from file C","bucket":1}],"explain":"Shape vs progress. If the question is about columns and types → schemaLocation. If it's about what's done → checkpoint."},{"id":"ch12-e084","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Exam trap: what does `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` store?","options":["Schema history/state for inference and evolution","Stream progress (processed files)","A copy of the raw files","The Delta transaction log"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","That's checkpointLocation.","Auto Loader never copies raw files there.","That's in the table's _delta_log."],"explain":"schemaLocation is schema memory. Progress is the checkpoint's job; the Delta log belongs to the target table; raw files stay in the landing zone."},{"id":"ch12-e085","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Files A, B, C have 100 rows each. The stream processed A and B, then the cluster died. It restarts with the same intact checkpoint. How many rows does the target hold after the restart has caught up?","answer":300,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"Checkpoint says A and B are done → only C (100 rows) is processed on restart: 200 + 100 = 300. No file is reprocessed."},{"id":"ch12-e086","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"This stream lost all its progress after the cluster auto-terminated overnight. Which line is the cause?","lines":["query = (","    orders.writeStream","        .format(\"delta\")","        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/tmp/orders_checkpoint\")","        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")",")"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/orders_checkpoint/\")","explain":"`/tmp` is ephemeral local disk on the cluster. When compute terminates, the checkpoint disappears and the stream restarts without memory. Checkpoints must survive compute (Big-Test Q15)."},{"id":"ch12-e087","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A checkpoint must survive compute restarts and terminations, so it belongs on persistent storage.","answer":true,"explain":"Compute is disposable; streaming state is not. A Volume or cloud path outlives any cluster."},{"id":"ch12-e088","type":"order","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order what Auto Loader does on its **initial** run.","items":["Discover files","Infer schema","Persist schema state","Decide which files are new","Process the batch","Write the Delta transaction","Update the checkpoint"],"explain":"Schema must exist (and be saved) before files can be parsed; the checkpoint advances only after the Delta commit, so a crash between them re-processes rather than loses a batch."},{"id":"ch12-e089","type":"order","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order what Auto Loader does on a **subsequent** run.","items":["Read the checkpoint","Discover files","Skip already-processed files","Process only new files","Commit to Delta","Advance the checkpoint"],"explain":"The run starts from remembered state, not from scratch — that's the whole difference from spark.read."},{"id":"ch12-e090","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"You give Auto Loader a full explicit `.schema(...)` and don't use inference or evolution. Which statement is right?","options":["schemaLocation isn't required in the same way, but checkpointLocation still is","Neither location is needed","schemaLocation is still mandatory, checkpoint is optional","You must set both to the same path"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Without a checkpoint the stream can't track progress.","Reversed.","Allowed, but not required."],"explain":"schemaLocation matters for Auto Loader-managed inference/evolution. The checkpoint is always needed for the streaming write to remember progress."},{"id":"ch12-e091","type":"free","section":"ch12-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Engineering question: what exactly does the checkpoint protect?","model":"The checkpoint stores the streaming query's progress and state — which input (files/offsets) has been processed and committed, plus the query's metadata and any operator state. After a restart, the query reads it and continues from the correct point instead of rediscovering the whole input as new, which would reprocess or duplicate data.","rubric":["Stores progress (processed files/offsets)","Stores state/metadata of the query","Restart continues from correct point","Without it: rediscovery as new → reprocessing/duplicates"],"explain":"Note what it does NOT protect: business-key uniqueness, or a sink that isn't transactional."},{"id":"ch12-e092","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Files A, B, C (100 rows each) were ingested. The checkpoint is deleted, file D (100 rows) lands, and the stream restarts with a new empty checkpoint and default options. How many rows does Bronze hold now?","answer":700,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"With no history, A, B, C are seen as new again (300 duplicate rows) plus D (100): 300 + 300 + 100 = 700. That's why a checkpoint reset needs a reconciliation plan."},{"id":"ch12-e093","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Big-Test Q14: what happens if the checkpoint disappears?","options":["The stream loses its progress/state; recovery or reconciliation may be needed and duplicate ingestion becomes possible","Nothing — Auto Loader rebuilds progress from the Delta log automatically","The stream refuses to ever start again","Only the schema is lost; progress is kept in schemaLocation"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","No automatic reconstruction of processed-file history.","It starts — with amnesia.","schemaLocation stores schema, not progress."],"explain":"Progress lives only in the checkpoint. Without it, previously processed files can look new. schemaLocation doesn't hold progress, and Auto Loader doesn't reconstruct file history from the target table."},{"id":"ch12-e094","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Two different streaming queries may safely share one checkpoint path if they write to different tables.","answer":false,"explain":"Big-Test Q16: checkpoint state belongs to **one** logical streaming query. Sharing corrupts semantics regardless of targets."},{"id":"ch12-e095","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"After changing an Auto Loader stream's source path, it's fine to keep the old checkpoint — it's just a cache.","answer":false,"explain":"The checkpoint belongs to the logical query and its source topology. Re-pointing the source with the same checkpoint is dangerous; use a new checkpoint and plan the backfill."},{"id":"ch12-e096","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Monday morning Bronze `orders_raw` has 2× the expected rows for all of last month. Friday's deploy 'cleaned up' the `/Volumes/ecommerce/system/` folder.","steps":[{"prompt":"First check?","options":[{"text":"Whether the stream's checkpoint folder was deleted or emptied","ok":true,"fb":"A cleanup of /system is the prime suspect: no checkpoint → old files look new."},{"text":"Whether upstream re-sent last month's files","ok":false,"fb":"Possible, but the deploy + doubling of every file points at the checkpoint first."},{"text":"Whether OPTIMIZE ran","ok":false,"fb":"OPTIMIZE never changes row counts."}]},{"prompt":"The checkpoint folder was recreated empty on Friday. How do you measure the damage?","options":[{"text":"Count distinct `_ingested_at` per `_source_file`: files loaded twice are the duplicates","ok":true,"fb":"Precise blast radius from your audit columns."},{"text":"Count rows per day","ok":false,"fb":"Shows that something doubled, not which files."},{"text":"Look at the Spark UI","ok":false,"fb":"The UI won't tell you which files were reprocessed last week."}]},{"prompt":"No checkpoint backup exists. Best repair?","options":[{"text":"Delete the second load of each duplicated file (by `_ingested_at`) and keep the new checkpoint going","ok":true,"fb":"Bronze returns to one copy per file and the stream continues with its fresh history."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint again and restart","ok":false,"fb":"That re-ingests everything a third time."},{"text":"Do nothing; Silver will handle it","ok":false,"fb":"Only if Silver dedupes on the right key — and Bronze stays wrong for audits."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e097","type":"order","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'checkpoint deleted'.","items":["Confirm the checkpoint path and its (missing) contents","Find who/what deleted it","Identify files ingested twice via `_source_file`","Restore the checkpoint or reconcile the duplicate load","Protect checkpoint paths from cleanup"],"explain":"Evidence first (was it really reset?), then cause, blast radius, repair, prevention. Restarting blindly before measuring makes reconciliation harder."},{"id":"ch12-e098","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"`orders_bronze` and `customers_bronze` streams both started acting up after a refactor that introduced `CHECKPOINT = \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/checkpoint\"` in a shared config module.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you suspect?","options":[{"text":"Both queries now write to the same checkpoint location","ok":true,"fb":"A shared constant for checkpoints is a classic refactor bug."},{"text":"The landing zone ran out of space","ok":false,"fb":"Wouldn't explain skipping vs reprocessing in two specific streams."},{"text":"Photon is disabled","ok":false,"fb":"Engine choice doesn't affect checkpoint semantics."}]},{"prompt":"Confirmed. What now?","options":[{"text":"Stop both, give each its own checkpoint path, then reconcile each target","ok":true,"fb":"Separate state first, then repair data."},{"text":"Keep one stream running and fix the other later","ok":false,"fb":"The running one keeps corrupting the shared state."},{"text":"Delete the shared checkpoint and restart both on it","ok":false,"fb":"Still shared — and now both re-ingest everything."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e099","type":"order","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'two streams share one checkpoint'.","items":["Find all queries using the checkpoint path","Stop the affected queries","Assign a unique checkpoint per query","Reconcile skipped/duplicated files per target","Restart and monitor"],"explain":"Stop the bleeding before fixing data; otherwise the shared state keeps changing under you."},{"id":"ch12-e100","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The upstream team moved deliveries from `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders_v1/` to `/orders_v2/`. You edited `.load(...)` and redeployed, keeping the checkpoint. Now Bronze gets nothing from v2.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"text":"The old checkpoint belongs to the v1 query; re-pointing the source with it is unsafe","ok":true,"fb":"Checkpoint state is tied to the query's source topology."},{"text":"v2 files are in a format Auto Loader can't read","ok":false,"fb":"Same format; the change was the path."},{"text":"The target table is full","ok":false,"fb":"Delta tables don't 'fill up'."}]},{"prompt":"Best fix?","options":[{"text":"New checkpoint for the v2 stream + explicit choice whether to backfill existing v2 files","ok":true,"fb":"Clean state and a deliberate backfill decision."},{"text":"Delete the v1 files so the checkpoint 'matches'","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys source data and doesn't fix the topology mismatch."},{"text":"Use the same checkpoint for a v1 and a v2 stream","ok":false,"fb":"That's the shared-checkpoint bug."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e101","type":"order","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'source changed, checkpoint kept'.","items":["Diff stream definition vs last good deploy","Create a new checkpoint for the new source","Decide backfill (includeExistingFiles)","Start the stream","Verify counts per source prefix"],"explain":"The decision about history (backfill or not) must be explicit; a new checkpoint by default ingests existing files."},{"id":"ch12-e102","type":"free","section":"ch12-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q14 + Q16: what happens if a checkpoint disappears, and why must two streams never share one?","model":"If the checkpoint disappears, the stream loses its record of what it processed; on restart previously processed files can be treated as new, so duplicate ingestion or a recovery/reconciliation effort follows. Two streams must not share one because checkpoint state belongs to one logical streaming query — its progress, offsets and metadata describe that query's source and plan; sharing makes queries overwrite/misread each other's state.","rubric":["Lost progress → files may look new","Duplicates or reconciliation needed","Checkpoint belongs to one logical query","Sharing corrupts each other's state"],"explain":"Both answers come from one idea: the checkpoint IS the stream's memory."},{"id":"ch12-e103","type":"match","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the discovery concept to its description.","pairs":[["Directory listing","List the path and diff it against tracked state"],["File notification","Cloud events tell Auto Loader a file was created"],["Managed file events","Databricks-managed events on UC external locations"],["Naive full listing every trigger","Cost grows with the size of the whole folder"]],"explain":"Both real mechanisms are incremental; notification-style discovery avoids repeatedly listing huge directories."},{"id":"ch12-e104","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"File-notification mode avoids repeatedly listing a huge input directory.","answer":true,"explain":"With notifications/file events, new files are announced by the cloud, so Auto Loader doesn't have to scan millions of names each trigger."},{"id":"ch12-e105","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Auto Loader (default options) reads JSON `{\"id\": 1, \"amount\": 20.5}`. What types are inferred?","options":["id STRING, amount STRING","id LONG, amount DOUBLE","id INT, amount FLOAT","id LONG, amount STRING"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","That's spark.read or inferColumnTypes=true.","Spark doesn't pick INT/FLOAT for JSON numbers.","No mixed rule like that."],"explain":"For JSON/CSV/XML, Auto Loader defaults to STRING to reduce type-mismatch evolution failures. `spark.read` would infer LONG/DOUBLE; Auto Loader does so only with `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true`."},{"id":"ch12-e106","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Make Auto Loader infer real column types for JSON.","text":"spark.readStream\n    .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n    .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n    .option(\"cloudFiles.[[inferColumnTypes]]\", \"[[true]]\")\n    .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)\n    .load(source_path)","bank":["inferSchema","false","schemaHints"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true` makes Auto Loader infer types like the DataFrameReader. `inferSchema` is the plain CSV reader option and isn't how Auto Loader is configured."},{"id":"ch12-e107","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"By default, Auto Loader infers CSV columns with real types, exactly like `spark.read.option(\"inferSchema\",\"true\")`.","answer":false,"explain":"Exam trap: Auto Loader's default for CSV/JSON/XML is STRING. Only `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes=true` gives DataFrameReader-like types."},{"id":"ch12-e108","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why do many Bronze tables intentionally keep source fields as STRING?","options":["So unexpected values like \"unknown\" are preserved instead of failing or being nulled; Silver validates and casts","Because Delta can't store DOUBLE","Because strings are faster to aggregate","Because Unity Catalog requires it"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Delta stores all common types.","Strings are usually slower for math.","UC doesn't impose that."],"explain":"Bronze's job is to preserve the source. A typed Bronze column turns tomorrow's `\"unknown\"` into a failure or a rescued value; a STRING keeps it, and Silver decides how to clean it."},{"id":"ch12-e109","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A feed has 200 Parquet files of 1 GB each. With default sampling limits, how many files does Auto Loader's initial schema inference read before stopping?","answer":50,"tolerance":0,"unit":"files","explain":"Limits: 50 GB or 1000 files, whichever comes first. At 1 GB per file the 50 GB limit is hit after 50 files."},{"id":"ch12-e110","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q18: how much input does Auto Loader sample by default for initial schema inference?","options":["Up to 50 GB or 1000 files, whichever is reached first","All files in the directory","Exactly the first file","Up to 1 GB or 100 files"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","It intentionally doesn't scan everything.","One file would be far too little.","Wrong numbers."],"explain":"Inference samples — it doesn't read the entire historical corpus. Columns that only appear later are handled by schema evolution."},{"id":"ch12-e111","type":"write","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Add schema hints so Auto Loader types `order_id` as BIGINT and `amount` as DOUBLE while inferring everything else (JSON source, schema at `schema_path`).","solution":"orders = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaHints\", \"order_id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE\")\n        .load(source_path)\n)","keywords":["\"cloudfiles.schemahints\"","order_id bigint","amount double","\"cloudfiles.schemalocation\""],"lang":"python","explain":"Hints override inference only for the named columns; the rest are still inferred (as STRING by default). It's the middle ground between full inference and a full explicit schema."},{"id":"ch12-e112","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"For Parquet sources, a schema hint is exactly the same as reading the column and then applying `cast(...)`.","answer":false,"explain":"For typed formats the hint tells the reader which type to read the column as; it's not a post-read cast. Semantics (overflow, precision, failures) can differ."},{"id":"ch12-e113","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"On an existing stream whose schema is already stored in schemaLocation, flipping `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` to true automatically re-types the stored columns on restart.","answer":false,"explain":"Inference runs when the schema is first built; afterwards Auto Loader works from the schema persisted in schemaLocation. Decide inference options before the first run; for an existing stream use schema hints, Silver casts, or a deliberately new stream."},{"id":"ch12-e114","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are ways to get typed columns out of Auto Loader on JSON. Which one isn't?","options":["`cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes = true`","`cloudFiles.schemaHints` for specific columns","An explicit `.schema(...)`","`cloudFiles.format = \"parquet\"` on JSON files"],"answer":3,"explain":"Setting the format to parquet doesn't convert JSON files — it would just fail to read them. The real levers are inferColumnTypes, hints, or an explicit schema (or casting in Silver)."},{"id":"ch12-e115","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Default Auto Loader inference: which formats come out as STRING columns and which keep encoded types?","buckets":["STRING by default","Types from the file"],"items":[{"text":"JSON","bucket":0},{"text":"CSV","bucket":0},{"text":"XML","bucket":0},{"text":"Parquet","bucket":1},{"text":"Avro","bucket":1}],"explain":"Text formats carry no reliable types, so Auto Loader plays safe with STRING; Parquet and Avro embed their schema."},{"id":"ch12-e116","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"An analyst complains: 'Bronze orders from Auto Loader — all columns are strings! Is ingestion broken?' Source: JSON files.","steps":[{"prompt":"What's your first reaction?","options":[{"text":"Check whether this is Auto Loader's default string inference for JSON","ok":true,"fb":"It's expected behavior for JSON/CSV/XML unless inferColumnTypes is set."},{"text":"Restart the stream with a new checkpoint","ok":false,"fb":"Resetting state doesn't change inference and risks duplicates."},{"text":"Convert the JSON files to CSV","ok":false,"fb":"CSV would also default to strings."}]},{"prompt":"It is the default. The analyst needs numeric amounts for a report. Best layer to fix it?","options":[{"text":"Silver: cast/validate types (Bronze keeps the raw strings)","ok":true,"fb":"Bronze preserves source; Silver enforces types — medallion philosophy."},{"text":"Bronze: drop rows whose amount isn't numeric","ok":false,"fb":"Bronze shouldn't throw away source data."},{"text":"Gold: cast in every dashboard query","ok":false,"fb":"Repeats logic in every consumer and hides bad values."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e117","type":"order","section":"ch12-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'every column is STRING'.","items":["Confirm source is JSON/CSV/XML with default inference","Inspect the schema stored in schemaLocation","Decide which layer should own typing","Add hints / inferColumnTypes only where justified","Cast and validate in Silver"],"explain":"Recognize the default before 'fixing' it; most of the time the fix is a Silver cast, not an ingestion change."},{"id":"ch12-e118","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Default Auto Loader (inferred schema, default mode). A file with a new column `discount` arrives. What happens?","options":["The stream fails after recording the new schema; on restart the column is included","The column is silently dropped forever","The whole table is overwritten with the new schema","The file is skipped and never processed"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","That's closer to mode none with explicit schema.","Evolution adds columns; it doesn't overwrite data.","The file is processed after restart."],"explain":"addNewColumns (default without a provided schema): stop, record evolved schema in schemaLocation, continue on restart. Nothing is dropped or skipped."},{"id":"ch12-e119","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which schemaEvolutionMode behaves like this?","buckets":["addNewColumns","rescue","failOnNewColumns","none"],"items":[{"text":"Stream stops, schema gains the column, restart continues","bucket":0},{"text":"Default when Auto Loader infers the schema","bucket":0},{"text":"Stream keeps running; new fields land in `_rescued_data`","bucket":1},{"text":"Stream stops and stays down until you change the schema or remove the file","bucket":2},{"text":"Default when you provide a schema: new columns ignored","bucket":3}],"explain":"Pick per feed: evolve (addNewColumns), keep running but capture (rescue), demand human review (failOnNewColumns), or ignore (none)."},{"id":"ch12-e120","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Configure Auto Loader to never evolve the schema but capture new fields in the rescued column.","text":".option(\"cloudFiles.[[schemaEvolutionMode]]\", \"[[rescue]]\")","bank":["mergeSchema","addNewColumns","none","schemaHints"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`rescue` mode keeps the schema fixed and the stream running, while unexpected columns are preserved in `_rescued_data`."},{"id":"ch12-e121","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Expected schema `id BIGINT, amount DOUBLE`. Incoming `{\"id\": 10, \"amount\": \"UNKNOWN\", \"new_field\": \"abc\"}` with rescued data enabled. Result?","options":["id=10, amount=null, _rescued_data has amount:\"UNKNOWN\" and new_field:\"abc\"","Row dropped","Stream fails on the type mismatch","amount=0, new_field ignored"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Rescue exists exactly to avoid dropping.","Type mismatches are rescued, not fatal.","Inventing 0 would corrupt the data."],"explain":"Valid fields load, the mismatching value and unexpected field are preserved in `_rescued_data`. No information is lost and nothing is fabricated (amount isn't set to 0)."},{"id":"ch12-e122","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"The rescued data column exists so mismatched or unexpected values are preserved instead of silently lost.","answer":true,"explain":"Big-Test Q19. It keeps the row loadable while preserving the problematic content for inspection, alerting, quarantine and fixes (Q20)."},{"id":"ch12-e123","type":"free","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q19 + Q20: what is rescued data and why is it useful?","model":"Rescued data is a special column (`_rescued_data`) where Auto Loader/read_files put values that don't match the expected schema — wrong types, unexpected fields — instead of losing them. It's useful for auditability (nothing silently dropped), handling schema drift, debugging what the source really sent, and quarantine patterns (route rows with rescued data away from Silver).","rubric":["Column for values/fields not matching the schema","Preserved instead of lost","Auditability + schema-drift handling","Debugging + quarantine"],"explain":"Short answer: unexpected data is kept where you can see it."},{"id":"ch12-e124","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these belong in a robust Bronze row. Which one does not?","options":["`_ingested_at`","`_source_file`","`_rescued_data`","`revenue_per_customer`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Bronze = source fields + ingestion metadata + rescued data. A business metric like revenue per customer is Gold work; computing it in Bronze couples ingestion to business rules."},{"id":"ch12-e125","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"This quarantine step sends the wrong rows to quarantine. Which line?","lines":["bronze = spark.read.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")","(bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NULL\")","       .write.mode(\"append\")","       .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.quarantine.orders_bad\"))","clean = bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NULL\")"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"(bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NOT NULL\")","explain":"Quarantine must receive rows WITH rescued content (`IS NOT NULL`). As written, clean rows are quarantined and suspicious rows are lost from both outputs."},{"id":"ch12-e126","type":"write","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write PySpark that routes Bronze rows with rescued data into `ecommerce.quarantine.orders_bad` (append) and keeps clean rows in a DataFrame `clean`.","solution":"bronze = spark.read.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n\n(bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NOT NULL\")\n       .write.mode(\"append\")\n       .saveAsTable(\"ecommerce.quarantine.orders_bad\"))\n\nclean = bronze.where(\"_rescued_data IS NULL\")","keywords":["_rescued_data is not null","saveastable(\"ecommerce.quarantine.orders_bad\")","_rescued_data is null"],"lang":"python","explain":"Two complementary filters on `_rescued_data`. Quarantine keeps evidence; clean rows continue to Silver."},{"id":"ch12-e127","type":"write","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write an Auto Loader read of JSON from `source` with an **explicit DDL schema** (`order_id BIGINT, amount STRING, status STRING`) that still keeps non-matching data in `_rescued_data`.","solution":"schema = \"order_id BIGINT, amount STRING, status STRING\"\n\norders = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"cloudFiles\")\n        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")\n        .schema(schema)\n        .option(\"rescuedDataColumn\", \"_rescued_data\")\n        .load(source)\n)","keywords":["format(\"cloudfiles\")",".schema(schema)","\"rescueddatacolumn\", \"_rescued_data\"",".load(source)"],"lang":"python","explain":"With an explicit schema Auto Loader doesn't infer or evolve, and new fields would be ignored. `rescuedDataColumn` keeps them visible. schemaLocation isn't needed for inference here; the write still needs a checkpoint."},{"id":"ch12-e128","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab: you add `{\"id\":3,\"amount\":{\"unexpected\":true}}` while `amount` is hinted as DOUBLE and rescued data is on. What do you expect?","options":["amount is null for that row and the object appears in `_rescued_data`","The stream deletes the file","amount becomes 1.0","All previous rows get amount null"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Auto Loader never deletes source files by default.","No such coercion.","Other rows are unaffected."],"explain":"An object can't fit a DOUBLE, so the value is rescued and the typed column is null. The point of the lab: know where unexpected data goes, not just make the pipeline pass."},{"id":"ch12-e129","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Your Auto Loader Bronze job failed overnight: the error mentions a newly detected field `discount`. It ran fine for months.","steps":[{"prompt":"First interpretation?","options":[{"text":"Likely expected schema-evolution behavior: the stream stopped after recording a new column","ok":true,"fb":"Default addNewColumns stops on new columns by design."},{"text":"The checkpoint is corrupt — delete it","ok":false,"fb":"Deleting the checkpoint causes re-ingestion and duplicates; it's not the issue."},{"text":"Disk is full on the driver","ok":false,"fb":"The error names a new field, not disk space."}]},{"prompt":"Upstream confirms `discount` is intentional. Next?","options":[{"text":"Restart the stream (or let job retries do it) and verify the column appears","ok":true,"fb":"On restart the evolved schema from schemaLocation is used."},{"text":"Switch to spark.read to avoid failures","ok":false,"fb":"You'd lose incremental state and re-read everything."},{"text":"Manually edit files to remove `discount`","ok":false,"fb":"Never mutate source evidence."}]},{"prompt":"For a sensitive finance feed you'd rather review every change. Which mode?","options":[{"text":"failOnNewColumns","ok":true,"fb":"It stays failed until a human updates the schema — explicit review."},{"text":"addNewColumns","ok":false,"fb":"That evolves automatically after restart."},{"text":"none","ok":false,"fb":"That silently ignores new columns."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e130","type":"order","section":"ch12-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'stream stopped after a new column'.","items":["Read the error and the latest schema version in schemaLocation","Find the file that introduced the column","Confirm intent with upstream","Restart / rely on job retries and verify","Choose the evolution mode for this feed"],"explain":"The stop is a checkpoint for humans. Confirm what changed and whether it's wanted before simply restarting."},{"id":"ch12-e131","type":"calc","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"File A and file B (two distinct files) each contain one row with order_id=100. Auto Loader with an intact checkpoint ingests both. How many order_id=100 rows does Bronze contain?","answer":2,"tolerance":0,"unit":"rows","explain":"Each file is processed exactly once, and each contains the row → 2 rows. Exactly-once is about files, not business keys."},{"id":"ch12-e132","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Big-Test Q21: exactly-once file ingestion guarantees there are no duplicate `customer_id` values in Bronze.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Distinct files can contain the same business key (upstream retries, resends, updates). Exactly-once covers file processing only."},{"id":"ch12-e133","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q22: where should business-key deduplication generally happen?","options":["Silver / the CDC processing layer","Inside Auto Loader's checkpoint","In the landing zone by deleting files","In Gold dashboards"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Checkpoints track files, not keys.","Never mutate the landing zone to dedupe.","Too late, and every consumer would repeat it."],"explain":"Bronze lands faithfully; Silver enforces business keys via dedup/MERGE/CDC. The checkpoint knows files, not keys; deleting source files destroys evidence; Gold is too late and repeats logic."},{"id":"ch12-e134","type":"free","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Engineering question: why doesn't file-level exactly-once guarantee no duplicate business rows?","model":"Because file identity is not business-entity identity. Auto Loader guarantees each file is ingested once, but two distinct files can legitimately contain the same order_id — an upstream retry, a re-sent export, or an updated version of the order. Both are processed once each, giving two business rows. Therefore dedup/MERGE on the business key belongs downstream in Silver/CDC.","rubric":["File identity ≠ business entity identity","Distinct files can share a key (retry, resend, update)","Each processed once → still 2 rows","Dedup/MERGE downstream (Silver)"],"explain":"Interviewers love this: it separates people who memorized 'exactly-once' from people who understand what is counted once."},{"id":"ch12-e135","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"This Silver MERGE still produces duplicate order_ids when an order arrives in two different files. Which line is the bug?","lines":["MERGE INTO ecommerce.silver.orders AS t","USING ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw_deduped AS s","ON t.order_id = s.order_id AND t._source_file = s._source_file","WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *","WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"ON t.order_id = s.order_id","explain":"Matching on `_source_file` too means the same order from a different file never matches → it's inserted again. MERGE must match on the business key only; file metadata is audit data, not identity."},{"id":"ch12-e136","type":"write","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a SQL MERGE that upserts the latest Bronze version of each `order_id` (by `_ingested_at`) into `ecommerce.silver.orders`.","solution":"MERGE INTO ecommerce.silver.orders AS t\nUSING (\n  SELECT *\n  FROM ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\n  QUALIFY row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY _ingested_at DESC) = 1\n) AS s\nON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;","keywords":["merge into ecommerce.silver.orders","row_number() over (partition by order_id","on t.order_id = s.order_id","when matched then update","when not matched then insert"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Dedupe the source first (one row per key, latest wins) — otherwise MERGE fails with multiple source rows matching one target row — then match on the business key only."},{"id":"ch12-e137","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Bronze or Silver responsibility?","buckets":["Bronze (land faithfully)","Silver (business correctness)"],"items":[{"text":"Keep raw values, even 'unknown'","bucket":0},{"text":"Add `_source_file`, `_ingested_at`, `_rescued_data`","bucket":0},{"text":"Replayable source of truth","bucket":0},{"text":"Cast to strict types","bucket":1},{"text":"Deduplicate by order_id","bucket":1},{"text":"Apply CDC / MERGE","bucket":1},{"text":"Join with customers","bucket":1}],"explain":"Bronze is conservative so it can be replayed; Silver applies the rules that might be wrong and might need to be re-run from Bronze."},{"id":"ch12-e138","type":"free","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Engineering question: why not write Auto Loader directly into Silver?","model":"Because Bronze buys source fidelity, replay, audit, schema-drift handling, rescued data and debugging. If Auto Loader wrote straight into a typed, deduplicated Silver table, any wrong cast, dedup rule or CDC logic would destroy raw evidence and I couldn't rebuild. With Bronze in between, Silver can enforce business keys, types, dedup, CDC and quality — and be rebuilt from Bronze when a rule changes. It decouples ingestion from business transformation.","rubric":["Source fidelity / replay","Audit + debugging","Schema drift + rescued data handled in Bronze","Silver enforces keys, types, dedup, CDC, quality","Decoupling ingestion from business logic"],"explain":"Short version: Bronze is your undo button."},{"id":"ch12-e139","type":"order","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q23: order the recommended file-ingestion architecture.","items":["Landing zone (immutable files)","Auto Loader","Bronze (raw + metadata + rescued data)","Validate / dedup / CDC","Silver","Gold"],"explain":"Immutable files make discovery reliable, Auto Loader ingests exactly once, Bronze preserves, the validation step enforces business correctness, Silver/Gold serve consumers."},{"id":"ch12-e140","type":"order","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Production assignment, part 1 — order the experiment steps.","items":["Ingest the first JSON file","Restart the stream","Add a second file","Verify the first wasn't reprocessed","Add a new column","Observe schema evolution","Add a malformed value","Inspect rescued data"],"explain":"Each step proves one property: checkpoint survives restarts, only new files are processed, schema evolution behaves as configured, and unexpected values are rescued rather than lost."},{"id":"ch12-e141","type":"order","section":"ch12-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Production assignment, part 2 — order the remaining steps.","items":["Delete the checkpoint (sandbox only)","Observe the changed behavior","Restore/recreate safely","Add a duplicate business key in a different file","Verify Auto Loader processes each file once","Prove the business duplicate still exists","Deduplicate downstream"],"explain":"Part 2 breaks state on purpose (only in a sandbox!), then proves the key lesson: file-level exactly-once leaves business duplicates for Silver."},{"id":"ch12-e142","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Exam trap: millions of files arrive continuously in cloud object storage. Which ingestion mechanism should you choose?","options":["Auto Loader","COPY INTO on a schedule","spark.read of the whole folder every 5 minutes","INSERT INTO from a view over the folder"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Typically not at that scale.","Re-reads and duplicates.","No file tracking."],"explain":"Auto Loader is the recommended choice for millions+ files and continuous arrivals: incremental discovery (listing or file events) and a streaming checkpoint. COPY INTO is fine for thousands; repeated spark.read re-reads everything."},{"id":"ch12-e143","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"COPY INTO or Auto Loader?","buckets":["COPY INTO","Auto Loader"],"items":[{"text":"Thousands of files over time, SQL team","bucket":0},{"text":"Need to reload two specific files on purpose","bucket":0},{"text":"Simple scheduled idempotent load","bucket":0},{"text":"Millions of files","bucket":1},{"text":"New files every few seconds","bucket":1},{"text":"Frequent upstream schema changes","bucket":1},{"text":"Streaming pipeline feeding Silver continuously","bucket":1}],"explain":"Big-Test Q8: Auto Loader wins for continuous/high-scale ingestion, millions of files and richer schema evolution; COPY INTO for moderate, SQL-centric, retriable loads."},{"id":"ch12-e144","type":"cloze","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Make the Auto Loader write process everything available, then stop.","text":"query = (\n    orders.writeStream\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint_path)\n        .[[trigger]]([[availableNow]]=True)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)","bank":["once","processingTime","option","continuous"],"asCode":true,"explain":"`.trigger(availableNow=True)` processes the backlog in (possibly several) batches and stops — ideal for scheduled incremental jobs."},{"id":"ch12-e145","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A stream started with `trigger(availableNow=True)` keeps running forever, waiting for new files.","answer":false,"explain":"AvailableNow processes all input available at start and then **stops**. Run it from a scheduled Job to get batch-like incremental ingestion."},{"id":"ch12-e146","type":"write","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the write side of an Auto Loader stream `orders` that runs as a scheduled incremental job: Delta, checkpoint at `checkpoint_path`, process all available files then stop, into `ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw`.","solution":"query = (\n    orders.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint_path)\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")\n)","keywords":["writestream","\"checkpointlocation\", checkpoint_path","trigger(availablenow=true)","totable(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders_raw\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Same skeleton as a continuous stream plus `.trigger(availableNow=True)`. The checkpoint still guarantees that the next scheduled run starts where this one stopped."},{"id":"ch12-e147","type":"match","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the property to the tool that is strongest at it (per the comparison table).","pairs":[["Explicit streaming checkpoint","Auto Loader"],["Simple SQL, idempotent re-runs, no stream","COPY INTO"],["Reads whatever is there now, no memory","spark.read"]],"explain":"spark.read has no memory; COPY INTO tracks files internally per run; Auto Loader keeps an explicit streaming checkpoint and integrates with streaming."},{"id":"ch12-e148","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Per the comparison table, how does COPY INTO handle 'continuous ingestion'?","options":["Through repeated runs (e.g. a scheduled job)","Natively as a stream","Not at all — it can only run once per table","Through file notifications"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","It's not a streaming source.","It can run any number of times — idempotently.","That's an Auto Loader discovery mode."],"explain":"COPY INTO is a command: run → load new files → finish. Continuity means scheduling it repeatedly; it isn't a stream. Auto Loader is the native streaming option."},{"id":"ch12-e149","type":"odd","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement does NOT describe Auto Loader?","options":["Stateful incremental stream","Discovers files continuously or in batches","Checkpoints its progress","Runs once, loads, and keeps no state between runs"],"answer":3,"explain":"'Run, load, finish' with only internal tracking is COPY INTO's mental model; spark.read keeps no state at all. Auto Loader is a stateful stream with a checkpoint."},{"id":"ch12-e150","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A partner drops ~30 CSV files per day into a Volume. Your team is SQL-first. Last week a bad file had to be reloaded after the partner fixed it. Which design?","steps":[{"prompt":"Pick the ingestion tool.","options":[{"text":"COPY INTO, scheduled","ok":true,"fb":"Moderate volume, SQL-centric, idempotent re-runs — COPY INTO's sweet spot."},{"text":"Always-on Auto Loader cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Works, but heavier than needed for 30 files/day and a SQL team."},{"text":"Nightly spark.read + append","ok":false,"fb":"Re-reads everything → duplicates."}]},{"prompt":"The partner overwrites `orders_0928.csv` with a fixed version. What happens on the next COPY INTO run?","options":[{"text":"It's skipped — already loaded, even though content changed","ok":true,"fb":"So corrections need a new file name or a deliberate reload."},{"text":"It's reloaded automatically","ok":false,"fb":"COPY INTO skips loaded files even if modified."},{"text":"COPY INTO fails","ok":false,"fb":"It succeeds — silently skipping the file."}]},{"prompt":"Best way to get the fix in?","options":[{"text":"Ask for corrections as new files; if unavoidable, reload that file with FILES + force and remove the old rows","ok":true,"fb":"Immutable delivery first; force only deliberately with cleanup."},{"text":"Drop the table and reload everything every day","ok":false,"fb":"Expensive and loses idempotency."},{"text":"Delete COPY INTO's history by recreating the Volume","ok":false,"fb":"Destructive and unnecessary."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e151","type":"order","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the checks for 'new files exist but no rows appear' (source → sink).","items":["Do the new files actually exist?","Does the path match?","Are extension/format correct?","Did Auto Loader discover them?","Does the checkpoint say already processed?","Parsing failure?","Schema issue?","Sink write failure?"],"explain":"Start at the source and move toward the sink; the first check that fails is where reality diverges from expectation. Jumping to 'schema issue' before confirming the files exist wastes hours."},{"id":"ch12-e152","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Files land in `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/`. The stream runs without errors but Bronze never gets new rows. Which line?","lines":["orders = (","    spark.readStream","        .format(\"cloudFiles\")","        .option(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"json\")","        .option(\"cloudFiles.schemaLocation\", schema_path)","        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/order/\")",")"],"bugs":[5],"fix":"        .load(\"/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/\")","explain":"The stream watches `/raw/order/` (singular) — a path mismatch, check #2 in the chain. No error is raised because an empty or different directory is perfectly valid."},{"id":"ch12-e153","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Bronze has no new rows. You've confirmed the files exist. What do you check next?","options":["Whether the stream's load path exactly matches where the files land","Whether the sink table has enough partitions","Whether Photon is enabled","Whether the schema hints are alphabetical"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Irrelevant to discovery.","Engine choice doesn't hide files.","Order doesn't matter."],"explain":"Next in the chain after 'files exist' is 'path matches'. Partitions, Photon and hint order don't stop files from being picked up."},{"id":"ch12-e154","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Big-Test Q24: incremental ingestion systems reason more reliably about new immutable file identities than about repeatedly mutated file names.","answer":true,"explain":"A loader that tracks file identity can't tell 'new content in an old name' reliably — it either skips it (lost update) or reprocesses it (duplicates). Unique, immutable files avoid both."},{"id":"ch12-e155","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which delivery convention is best for incremental ingestion?","options":["`orders_20260928_001.json`, `orders_20260928_002.json`, … never modified","`orders_latest.json` overwritten every 5 minutes","`orders.csv` appended in place","One folder per day, files replaced on corrections"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","Overwritten identity → skipped or duplicated.","Appending in place mutates a tracked file.","Replacing files mutates identities."],"explain":"Unique, immutable file names give each delivery a new identity that COPY INTO / Auto Loader can track exactly once. The others mutate an existing identity."},{"id":"ch12-e156","type":"free","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Big-Test Q24: why use immutable files in the landing zone? Mention what goes wrong otherwise with COPY INTO.","model":"Incremental loaders track file identity. With immutable, uniquely named files each delivery is a new identity and is loaded exactly once. If a source overwrites a file, COPY INTO (and Auto Loader by default) treat it as already loaded and skip it, so the correction is silently lost; forcing reloads or allowing overwrites risks duplicates. Immutable files make loads predictable, auditable and replayable.","rubric":["Loaders track file identity","New immutable file = new identity = loaded once","Overwritten file is skipped (lost correction)","Forcing/allowing overwrites → duplicates"],"explain":"Name corrections as new files, e.g. `orders_20260928_001_v2.json`."},{"id":"ch12-e157","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Upstream: 'We delivered 12 order files at 09:00.' Bronze: no new rows since 08:55. The Auto Loader stream shows status 'running'.","steps":[{"prompt":"First check?","options":[{"text":"List the landing path to see whether the 12 files are really there","ok":true,"fb":"Start at the source: existence first."},{"text":"Restart the stream with a new checkpoint","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys progress and risks duplicates before you know anything."},{"text":"Enable inferColumnTypes","ok":false,"fb":"Types don't make files appear."}]},{"prompt":"Files are in `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/2026/09/28/`. The stream loads `/Volumes/ecommerce/raw/orders/`. Query progress shows 12 new files read but 0 rows written. Next?","options":[{"text":"Check parsing: nulls / `_rescued_data` / a format or extension mismatch","ok":true,"fb":"Files were discovered, so the break is later in the chain — parsing."},{"text":"Check the path again","ok":false,"fb":"Progress shows the files were discovered, so the path is fine."},{"text":"Check the checkpoint for deletion","ok":false,"fb":"The files were read; the checkpoint isn't skipping them."}]},{"prompt":"The files are gzip CSV (`.csv.gz`) while `cloudFiles.format` is `json`. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Set the correct format (csv) and parser options for that feed — or a separate stream if both formats coexist","ok":true,"fb":"The grammar must match the files."},{"text":"Rename the files to .json","ok":false,"fb":"Renaming doesn't change content; it would still fail to parse."},{"text":"Drop the Bronze table","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated and destructive."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e158","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Finance says the 28 Sept amounts are still wrong although the partner 'fixed and re-uploaded the file this morning'. Your scheduled COPY INTO ran fine after the re-upload.","steps":[{"prompt":"Most likely explanation?","options":[{"text":"COPY INTO skipped the re-uploaded file because that file was already loaded","ok":true,"fb":"Already-loaded files are skipped even if modified."},{"text":"COPY INTO failed silently","ok":false,"fb":"It succeeded — skipping is normal behavior."},{"text":"Silver's MERGE is broken","ok":false,"fb":"Check ingestion first: Bronze never received the new content."}]},{"prompt":"How do you prove it?","options":[{"text":"Compare the file's modification time with `_ingested_at` of its Bronze rows","ok":true,"fb":"Modified after it was ingested → overwrite that was skipped."},{"text":"Count all Bronze rows","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't show which file content is loaded."},{"text":"Run VALIDATE ALL","ok":false,"fb":"Validation checks parse/schema, not load history."}]},{"prompt":"Fix now, and prevent next time?","options":[{"text":"Delete that file's old rows, reload it with FILES + force; ask the partner for new file names on corrections","ok":true,"fb":"Deliberate reload without duplicates + immutable delivery."},{"text":"Run COPY INTO with force on the whole folder","ok":false,"fb":"Reloads every file → mass duplicates."},{"text":"Wait for tomorrow's run","ok":false,"fb":"It will skip the file again."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e159","type":"order","section":"ch12-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'overwritten source file, correction missing'.","items":["Check whether the file was overwritten (mtime vs `_ingested_at`)","Confirm the loader skipped it","Delete that file's old rows","Reload just that file deliberately (FILES + force)","Agree on immutable delivery"],"explain":"Prove the overwrite, then reload surgically. Forcing without deleting the old rows first would leave both versions in Bronze."},{"id":"ch12-e160","type":"order","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Order the questions to ask when `amount` suddenly arrives as a STRUCT.","items":["Was the source contract intentionally changed?","Did only one corrupt file do this?","Should the field be rescued?","Should the schema evolve?","Should the stream fail?"],"explain":"First establish intent and blast radius (one file or all?), then choose the policy: rescue, evolve or fail. Policies chosen before knowing intent are guesses."},{"id":"ch12-e161","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"The safest response to an unexpected upstream schema change is to enable every schema-evolution option so the stream never fails again.","answer":false,"explain":"Blind evolution lets accidental upstream changes (like `amount` turning into a struct) flow silently into Silver and Gold. Decide per change: evolve if intentional, rescue if unexpected but worth keeping, fail if it violates the contract."},{"id":"ch12-e162","type":"bucket","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Duplicate `order_id=100` rows. Classify each cause.","buckets":["Ingestion problem","Source behavior (dedupe in Silver)","Transformation problem"],"items":[{"text":"Same source file loaded twice","bucket":0},{"text":"Checkpoint reset re-ingested old files","bucket":0},{"text":"COPY INTO run with force on the whole folder","bucket":0},{"text":"Two different files both contain order 100","bucket":1},{"text":"Upstream retry re-sent the order","bucket":1},{"text":"Silver MERGE missing","bucket":2},{"text":"MERGE matches on the wrong key","bucket":2}],"explain":"Classifying the duplicate tells you where to fix it. Ingestion problems → fix state/loads and reconcile Bronze; source behavior → expected, dedupe in Silver; transformation problems → fix the MERGE."},{"id":"ch12-e163","type":"mcq","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"A CSV line reads `2,\"10.5` (opening quote, no closing quote). Which parser options do you investigate first?","options":["quote, escape, multiLine, delimiter, header","cloudFiles.schemaLocation and checkpointLocation","PATTERN and FILES","mergeSchema in COPY_OPTIONS"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct.","State locations don't parse lines.","They choose files, not how lines parse.","Schema merging isn't a quoting fix."],"explain":"A quoting problem is a grammar problem: quote/escape decide how quotes are parsed, multiLine whether a quoted field may continue on the next line, delimiter/header the basic structure. State locations and file filters are unrelated."},{"id":"ch12-e164","type":"tf","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"In PERMISSIVE mode with `columnNameOfCorruptRecord`, malformed CSV lines can be kept in a corrupt-record column instead of being dropped.","answer":true,"explain":"PERMISSIVE keeps the row (nulls for unparsable fields) and can store the raw line in the named column — evidence you can inspect or quarantine."},{"id":"ch12-e165","type":"spotbug","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A CSV of addresses with quoted line breaks (`\"Main St\\nApt 4\"`) produces twice as many rows as expected, half of them garbage. Which line needs changing?","lines":["df = (","    spark.read","        .format(\"csv\")","        .option(\"header\", \"true\")","        .option(\"multiLine\", \"false\")","        .schema(schema)","        .load(path)",")"],"bugs":[4],"fix":"        .option(\"multiLine\", \"true\")","explain":"Quoted fields containing newlines need `multiLine=true`; otherwise each physical line becomes a row and the quoted address is split in two. (Only enable it when needed: it also changes how broken quotes behave.)"},{"id":"ch12-e166","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Today's Bronze rows show `amount` = `{\"value\":19.5,\"currency\":\"EUR\"}` instead of `19.5`. Silver casts now produce nulls.","steps":[{"prompt":"First question?","options":[{"text":"Was this contract change intentional, and does it affect all new files or one?","ok":true,"fb":"Intent + scope decide everything that follows."},{"text":"How do I enable automatic type widening everywhere?","ok":false,"fb":"Blindly allowing changes is how bugs reach Gold."},{"text":"Should I delete today's files?","ok":false,"fb":"Destroys evidence and real orders."}]},{"prompt":"Every new file has the new shape, and upstream confirms it's intended. Next?","options":[{"text":"Agree the new contract and update Silver to extract `value`/`currency` (evolve deliberately)","ok":true,"fb":"An intentional change gets a deliberate, reviewed adaptation."},{"text":"Set the stream to rescue mode forever and ignore it","ok":false,"fb":"Then every amount lives in `_rescued_data` and Silver stays broken."},{"text":"Cast the struct to DOUBLE","ok":false,"fb":"Not a valid cast; values stay null."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e167","type":"order","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'schema suddenly changed'.","items":["Find which files carry the new shape","Ask upstream if the change is intentional","Decide: rescue, evolve or fail","Adapt Silver parsing after the decision","Monitor rescued data for the next deliveries"],"explain":"Scope and intent come before policy; Silver changes come after the policy is decided."},{"id":"ch12-e168","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Upstream added `discount` 5 days ago. Bronze has no such column; the stream never failed. Your read uses `.schema(schema)` with 5 columns.","steps":[{"prompt":"What explains 'never failed, column missing'?","options":[{"text":"With a provided schema the evolution mode defaults to none: new columns are ignored","ok":true,"fb":"Explicit schema + mode none = silent ignore (unless rescued)."},{"text":"Auto Loader can't read new columns","ok":false,"fb":"It can — evolution is a configuration choice."},{"text":"The checkpoint is corrupt","ok":false,"fb":"Checkpoint corruption wouldn't selectively drop one field."}]},{"prompt":"Is the discount data lost?","options":[{"text":"Only if rescuedDataColumn wasn't set — otherwise it's in `_rescued_data`; and the source files still have it","ok":true,"fb":"Check rescued data first, the immutable source files second."},{"text":"Yes, permanently","ok":false,"fb":"Source files are still in the landing zone."},{"text":"No, it's in a hidden `discount` column","ok":false,"fb":"There's no hidden column."}]},{"prompt":"Fix?","options":[{"text":"Add `discount` to the schema deliberately and backfill the missed values","ok":true,"fb":"Reviewed evolution + recovery."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint to reprocess everything","ok":false,"fb":"That duplicates every file."},{"text":"Switch to inferSchema in batch","ok":false,"fb":"Loses incremental state."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e169","type":"order","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'new column missing'.","items":["Check schemaEvolutionMode","Inspect schemaLocation's latest schema","Check for a strict explicit schema","Look in `_rescued_data`","Evolve deliberately and backfill"],"explain":"The four checks from the source in order: mode, schemaLocation state, explicit schema, rescued data — then act."},{"id":"ch12-e170","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Silver `orders` has two rows for order_id 100. A colleague says 'Auto Loader is broken, it duplicates data'.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you do first?","options":[{"text":"Look at the Bronze rows for order 100 with `_source_file` and `_ingested_at`","ok":true,"fb":"Evidence before blame."},{"text":"Delete Auto Loader's checkpoint to 'reset' it","ok":false,"fb":"That would create real duplicates."},{"text":"Add DISTINCT to the Gold query","ok":false,"fb":"Hides the symptom."}]},{"prompt":"The two Bronze rows come from two different files (`..._001.json`, `..._007.json`). Conclusion?","options":[{"text":"Auto Loader worked: two files legitimately contain the same ID; Silver must dedupe/MERGE by order_id","ok":true,"fb":"File-level exactly-once ≠ business dedup."},{"text":"Auto Loader processed a file twice","ok":false,"fb":"Different file names = different files."},{"text":"The checkpoint was reset","ok":false,"fb":"A reset would show the SAME file twice."}]},{"prompt":"Silver uses MERGE ON order_id AND _source_file. Fix?","options":[{"text":"Dedupe the source by order_id and MERGE on order_id only","ok":true,"fb":"Business key only; file path is audit data."},{"text":"Add `_ingested_at` to the ON clause too","ok":false,"fb":"Makes matching even rarer → more duplicates."},{"text":"Switch Silver to append","ok":false,"fb":"Append guarantees duplicates."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e171","type":"order","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the duplicate-ID checks from the source's list.","items":["Same source file loaded twice?","Two different files both contain the ID?","Upstream retry?","Silver merge/dedup missing?","Checkpoint reset?"],"explain":"First distinguish ingestion vs source (same file vs different files), then the downstream dedup, then state resets. Each answer points to a different layer."},{"id":"ch12-e172","type":"scenario","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A partner CSV suddenly loads with fewer rows than the partner reports, and some `amount`s are null. No job failed.","steps":[{"prompt":"First step?","options":[{"text":"Look at the raw text lines of the file","ok":true,"fb":"Parsing problems are only understood from the raw grammar."},{"text":"Switch to FAILFAST in production immediately","ok":false,"fb":"Useful to surface errors, but first look at what's wrong."},{"text":"Re-run the load","ok":false,"fb":"Same input, same parse result."}]},{"prompt":"Raw line: `2,\"10.5` followed by normal lines. multiLine=true is set. Why are rows missing?","options":[{"text":"The unclosed quote makes the parser swallow following lines into one field","ok":true,"fb":"multiLine lets a quoted field continue across lines."},{"text":"The delimiter is wrong","ok":false,"fb":"Other lines parse fine with comma."},{"text":"The header option is missing","ok":false,"fb":"That would affect column names, not swallow rows."}]},{"prompt":"Best handling?","options":[{"text":"Keep bad records visible (corrupt/rescued column), quarantine them, and ask the partner to fix quoting","ok":true,"fb":"Evidence + clean flow + fix at the source."},{"text":"Use DROPMALFORMED and move on","ok":false,"fb":"Silently loses rows — the original complaint."},{"text":"Strip all quotes with a regex before reading","ok":false,"fb":"Breaks legitimate quoted fields containing commas."}]}]},{"id":"ch12-e173","type":"order","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the playbook for 'malformed CSV'.","items":["Read the raw lines around the failure","Check quote and escape settings","Check multiLine","Check delimiter and header","Make bad rows visible (PERMISSIVE + corrupt/rescued column)","Quarantine and fix at the source"],"explain":"Grammar rules from most to least likely for a broken quote, then visibility and remediation."},{"id":"ch12-e174","type":"match","section":"ch12-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Match each symptom to the FIRST thing you check.","pairs":[["New column missing from Bronze","Schema evolution mode / explicit schema"],["order_id duplicated","Same file twice or different files?"],["amount suddenly a STRUCT","Was the contract change intentional?"],["CSV rows swallowed after `2,\"10.5`","quote / escape / multiLine"],["New files, no rows","Do the files exist at the stream's path?"]],"explain":"Each incident has its own entry point. Starting at the right one is most of the speed of a good debugger."}],"src":"part2"},{"id":"ch13","num":13,"title":"Structured Streaming","subtitle":"Micro-batches, triggers, state, event time, watermarks, output modes, checkpoints, exactly-once and production debugging","emoji":"🌊","sourcePages":"510–569","mantra":"A stream is a table that never stops growing: the checkpoint remembers how far you got, state remembers what you need, and the watermark decides when you may forget.","objectives":["You can explain Structured Streaming from first principles: unbounded table, lazy readStream, micro-batches as Spark jobs, source → transformation → sink.","You can choose and write the right trigger (default, processingTime, availableNow, once, real-time) — including the serverless rules.","You can tell stateless from stateful operators and predict state growth.","You can reason in event time: assign events to windows, compute watermarks and decide whether a late event is accepted.","You can pick append / update / complete per query and sink, and implement update-like results with foreachBatch + MERGE.","You can explain what a checkpoint stores, when it is incompatible, and exactly what exactly-once does and does not guarantee.","You can write stream-static and stream-stream joins with watermarks and explain the min watermark policy.","You can debug slow, stalled, lossy, duplicating and state-heavy streams with ordered playbooks."],"sections":[{"id":"ch13-s01","title":"Streaming From First Principles: the Unbounded Table","hook":"Structured Streaming is not a different Spark — it is the same engine running over data that never ends.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Batch**: the input is known now (A, B, C, D). You process it, you get a result, you **stop**. The dataset is **bounded**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Streaming**: at 10:00 A and B arrive, at 10:01 C, at 10:02 D and E, at 10:03 F … You don't know where it ends. The dataset is logically **unbounded**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"BATCH (bounded)            STREAMING (unbounded)\n A B C D                    10:00  A B\n   │ process                10:01  C\n   ▼                        10:02  D E\n result → STOP              10:03  F\n                            ...    (no END)","caption":"Bounded vs unbounded input"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The unbounded table model","text":"Think of a stream as a **table that keeps growing**: every new event is a new row appended at the bottom. Your query is defined once over that table; the engine runs it **incrementally** over the new rows."},{"t":"p","text":"Because of that model you can write streaming transformations **almost like batch DataFrames**:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"result = (\n    stream_df\n        .filter(\"amount > 0\")\n        .groupBy(\"country\")\n        .count()\n)","caption":"Looks like batch — runs incrementally"},{"t":"p","text":"The difference is hidden in the engine: Spark keeps **execution progress** and, where needed, **intermediate state** between different arrivals of data."},{"t":"p","text":"The mental model of this whole chapter, top to bottom:"},{"t":"diagram","text":"STREAMING SOURCE\n      ↓\nreadStream\n      ↓\nincremental DataFrame plan\n      ↓\ntrigger\n      ↓\nmicro-batch / real-time execution\n      ↓\nstateless or stateful operators\n      ↓\nwriteStream\n      ↓\nsink\n      │\n      └── checkpoint: remembers progress + state","caption":"The Phase 5 mental model"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Why can streaming reuse batch DataFrame syntax?","text":"Because Structured Streaming uses Spark's **DataFrame / query-planning execution model**, applied **incrementally** over unbounded input. Same planner, same jobs/stages/tasks — just re-run on new data."},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Bounded dataset","def":"Input with a known end (yesterday's orders.csv). Process once, then stop."},{"term":"Unbounded dataset","def":"Input that logically keeps growing (Kafka topic, files landing forever)."},{"term":"Stateless vs stateful","def":"Whether the query must remember information from previous micro-batches (see section 5)."},{"term":"Checkpoint","def":"Durable storage of progress (offsets, commits) and state — the stream's memory."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"What this chapter builds on","text":"Batch vs streaming basics: Chapter 2. Partitions, skew and Spark UI: Chapter 9. Auto Loader specifics (cloudFiles, schemaLocation, file discovery): Chapter 12. Here we go **underneath** Auto Loader into the Structured Streaming engine itself."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: is a nightly job that only processes new rows 'streaming'?","text":"Not necessarily. Streaming describes an **unbounded input + incremental engine**. You'll see in section 4 that the streaming engine can even run as a scheduled job that stops (`availableNow`)."}]},{"id":"ch13-s02","title":"readStream, writeStream & the Lazy Start","hook":"One word — readStream instead of read — changes the whole execution model.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Batch read","points":["`spark.read.format(\"json\").load(path)`","\"Read whatever exists **now**.\"","Bounded batch DataFrame","Ends when the files are read"]},{"title":"Streaming read","points":["`spark.readStream.format(\"json\").schema(schema).load(path)`","\"Treat this source as **continuously evolving** and track my progress.\"","Streaming DataFrame","Never 'ends' by itself"]}]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"df = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"json\")\n        .schema(schema)      # streaming file sources need a schema\n        .load(path)\n)","caption":"First streaming DataFrame"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: read vs readStream","text":"`spark.read` → **bounded batch read**. `spark.readStream` → **streaming source**. Simple, but frequently tested — and calling `.writeStream` on a batch DataFrame fails."},{"t":"p","text":"**readStream does not start anything.** It is Spark lazy evaluation again: it only builds a streaming DataFrame / logical plan."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"events = spark.readStream.format(...).load(...)   # plan only\n\nquery = (\n    events.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")   # <- starts the query\n)","caption":"Execution starts at the sink"},{"t":"flow","items":["readStream → define source","transformations → define computation","writeStream → define sink","toTable()/start() → START the query"],"caption":"Who does what"},{"t":"p","text":"`toTable(name)` starts a query that writes into a table; `start()` starts a query whose sink is given by `format(...)`/path or by `foreachBatch`. Both return a **StreamingQuery** handle."},{"t":"p","text":"A full stream always has three parts: **SOURCE → TRANSFORMATION → SINK**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"events = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")          # SOURCE\n\nclean = events.filter(\"event_type IS NOT NULL\")                      # TRANSFORMATION\n\nquery = (\n    clean.writeStream\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")                         # SINK\n)","caption":"Source → transformation → sink, Delta to Delta"},{"t":"table","head":["Handle call","What it gives you"],"rows":[["`query.status`","What the query is doing right now (e.g. waiting for data, trigger active?)"],["`query.lastProgress`","Metrics of the last completed micro-batch (rows, rates, durations, state, watermark)"],["`query.recentProgress`","A list of the most recent progress reports"],["`query.awaitTermination()`","Block until the query stops (useful in jobs/scripts)"],["`query.stop()`","Stop the streaming query"]],"caption":"The StreamingQuery handle (monitoring in depth: section 15)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"No checkpoint = no memory","text":"Every production `writeStream` needs a durable `checkpointLocation` (e.g. a path in a Volume). It is how the query remembers progress across restarts (section 9)."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you run a cell that only contains `events = spark.readStream...load(path)`. Does Spark read any file?","text":"No. Like any transformation, it just defines a plan. Nothing executes until a streaming sink is started with `writeStream ... toTable()/start()` (or a notebook `display()` starts a query for you)."}]},{"id":"ch13-s03","title":"Micro-batches & the processingTime Trigger","hook":"Spark doesn't launch a job per event — it groups arrivals into small batches. Every batch lesson you learned still applies.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Events arrive at 12:00:00 (e1), 12:00:01 (e2), 12:00:02 (e3), 12:00:03 (e4), 12:00:04 (e5). Spark doesn't need a new distributed job for each event — it **groups** them."},{"t":"diagram","text":"arrivals:  e1  e2  e3  e4  e5\n           └─┬─┘   └─┬─┘   │\nMicro-batch 1: e1 e2\nMicro-batch 2: e3 e4\nMicro-batch 3: e5","caption":"Micro-batching"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Micro-batch = a small Spark job","text":"A **micro-batch** is a bounded chunk of newly available stream data, executed as a Spark job. new input → micro-batch N → job → stages → tasks → partitions."},{"t":"p","text":"So everything from Chapter 9 still applies inside every batch: **job, stage, task, partition, shuffle, join, spill, skew**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"query = (\n    events.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .outputMode(\"append\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .trigger(processingTime=\"5 seconds\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n)","caption":"5-second micro-batches (classic compute)"},{"t":"diagram","text":"00–05 s  → batch 0\n05–10 s  → batch 1\n10–15 s  → batch 2","caption":"Conceptually, with a 5-second trigger"},{"t":"p","text":"The **processingTime** trigger says **how often** Spark tries to start a new micro-batch. It is a schedule for attempts, not a promise of completion."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: the trigger is every 5 seconds, but one micro-batch needs 12 seconds. Does Spark run three batches in parallel to keep up?","text":"No. In the normal sequential micro-batch model, batch 2 starts only **after** batch 1 finishes (0 → 12 s, then the next). A **processing backlog** builds up and latency grows."},{"t":"diagram","text":"trigger = every 5 s, batch needs 12 s\nBatch 1: |0 ──────────── 12|\nBatch 2:                   |12 ──────────── 24|\nBatch 3:                                      |24 → ...\n→ backlog grows, end-to-end latency grows","caption":"Batches never overlap"},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Production signal","text":"Batches consistently longer than the trigger interval mean **ingestion rate > processing capacity**. The trigger can't fix that — only more capacity or a cheaper query can (section 14)."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when batches overrun the trigger","questions":["Is the batch duration consistently above the trigger interval, or was it one spike?","Is the input rate growing, or is the same input taking longer each batch?","Which stage inside the slow micro-batch dominates (shuffle, skew, state, sink)?"]}]},{"id":"ch13-s04","title":"Trigger Types: Default, AvailableNow, Once, Real-time & Serverless","hook":"The trigger decides WHEN batches run — including 'catch up, then stop', which is the Data Engineer's favourite.","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Trigger","Syntax","Meaning"],"rows":[["Default (processingTime = 0)","no `.trigger(...)`","Start the next micro-batch as soon as the previous one finishes"],["Fixed interval","`.trigger(processingTime=\"10 seconds\")`","Fixed-interval micro-batches"],["AvailableNow","`.trigger(availableNow=True)`","Process everything available now (incrementally), then **stop**"],["Once (deprecated)","`.trigger(once=True)`","Old 'one batch then stop' — replaced by AvailableNow"],["Real-time mode","dedicated real-time trigger (supported classic only)","Ultra-low-latency operational streaming, sub-second targets"]],"caption":"Today's important trigger models"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Once is deprecated","text":"Databricks deprecated `Trigger.Once` in favour of `Trigger.AvailableNow`. Once processes the backlog in a single batch; AvailableNow can split it into several batches (respecting rate limits) and still stops at the end."},{"t":"p","text":"**AvailableNow** in practice: every hour you want *'process all new records since last time, then stop'*."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"query = (\n    events.writeStream\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n)","caption":"Scheduled incremental processing"},{"t":"diagram","text":"Run 1:  available: A B C   → process A B C → stop\n         (checkpoint now knows A B C)\n1 hour later\nRun 2:  available: D E     → process D E   → stop","caption":"AvailableNow + checkpoint = incremental batch"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Streaming ≠ always-on","text":"The streaming engine can power **scheduled incremental batch** jobs. Databricks especially recommends AvailableNow for **serverless jobs**."},{"t":"p","text":"Why use the streaming engine for scheduled batch? You get **incremental source tracking, checkpointing, state management, fault recovery and incremental semantics** for free — instead of hand-writing:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"last_processed_timestamp = ...          # stored where? updated when?\ndf = spark.sql(f\"SELECT * FROM src WHERE event_ts > '{last_processed_timestamp}'\")","caption":"The manual progress tracking AvailableNow replaces"},{"t":"p","text":"This is exactly the model Auto Loader uses (Chapter 12): a streaming source + checkpoint + AvailableNow on a schedule."},{"t":"p","text":"**Real-time mode** (classic Databricks) targets **sub-second end-to-end latency** for ultra-low-latency operational workloads in supported scenarios. It is **not** the default choice for ordinary lakehouse ETL — micro-batch or AvailableNow is simpler, cheaper and easier to operate."},{"t":"table","head":["Trigger","Serverless notebooks/jobs"],"rows":[["AvailableNow","✅ supported — the recommended incremental trigger"],["Once","✅ supported but deprecated"],["ProcessingTime","❌ not supported"],["Real-time","❌ not supported"]],"caption":"Serverless streaming nuance (2026)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: serverless + always-on","text":"On serverless notebooks/jobs, time-based `processingTime` triggers aren't supported. For **always-running** serverless streams, the preferred architecture is a **Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode** (Phase 6)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: AvailableNow","text":"AvailableNow = **process the currently available backlog incrementally, then stop**. Not 'run forever', and not 'reprocess everything'."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you run an AvailableNow query twice in a row with no new data. What does run 2 process?","text":"Nothing new — the checkpoint already records that all available input was processed, so the query finds no new offsets and stops."}]},{"id":"ch13-s05","title":"Stateless vs Stateful & the State Store","hook":"Can each record be handled alone, or must Spark remember the past? That one question decides cost, risk and recovery.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"clean = (\n    events\n        .filter(\"amount > 0\")\n        .select(\"event_id\", \"customer_id\", \"amount\")\n)","caption":"Stateless"},{"t":"p","text":"For every record you can decide what to do **without remembering previous records**: event → filter → projection → output. Databricks defines **stateless** queries as queries that keep no intermediate state beyond **progress tracking** from source to sink."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"events.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count()","caption":"Stateful"},{"t":"p","text":"To answer *'customer 42 count = ?'* Spark must remember: batch 1 → customer 42 = 5; batch 2 brings 3 more → new total = 8. That memory is **state**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Stateless","points":["filter, select/projection, withColumn","Each row handled independently","Only progress (offsets/commits) in the checkpoint","Easy to change and restart"]},{"title":"Stateful","points":["aggregations (groupBy…agg/count)","distinct / dropDuplicates","stream-stream joins","custom stateful operations","State saved in the checkpoint → harder to change"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"What state really is","text":"**State** = information from previous micro-batches needed to correctly process the next ones. It is not the final output — it is the **working memory** of the streaming computation."},{"t":"table","head":["key","count"],"rows":[["customer_1","50"],["customer_2","22"],["customer_3","910"],["…","…"]],"caption":"State of groupBy(customer_id).count()"},{"t":"p","text":"**Why state can explode**: `groupBy(\"customer_id\").count()` with 1 million new customers per day and no reason to ever forget old ones."},{"t":"table","head":["Day","Keys in state"],"rows":[["Day 1","1M"],["Day 30","30M"],["Day 365","365M"]],"caption":"State grows forever"},{"t":"flow","items":["more memory","larger checkpoints","slower state lookups","slower batches","potential OOM"],"caption":"Consequences of unbounded state"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Unbounded state","text":"A stateful query without a time bound keeps every key forever. That is why **watermarks** (section 7) are so important."},{"t":"p","text":"**The state store.** Streaming state is not a Python dictionary on the driver. It is **distributed state** attached to the stateful operators, partitioned across executors and made durable via the checkpoint."},{"t":"flow","items":["stateful operator","distributed state store","checkpoint durability"]},{"t":"p","text":"For demanding stateful workloads Databricks currently recommends the **RocksDB state store with changelog checkpointing**: it scales state better and reduces state-checkpoint overhead and latency."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"spark.conf.set(\n  \"spark.sql.streaming.stateStore.providerClass\",\n  \"com.databricks.sql.streaming.state.RocksDBStateStoreProvider\")\nspark.conf.set(\n  \"spark.sql.streaming.stateStore.rocksdb.changelogCheckpointing.enabled\", \"true\")","caption":"Reference only — don't configure blindly; recognise when state is the bottleneck"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"State bottleneck ≠ compute bottleneck","text":"When **state** becomes the bottleneck, bigger compute alone isn't necessarily the fix — the state design (watermark, keys) and the **state-store architecture** may matter more. You don't need RocksDB internals yet."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when the state store is huge","questions":["How many keys does state hold — is key cardinality unbounded?","Is there a watermark, and is it much longer than real lateness?","How long are my windows?","Do my stream-stream joins have time bounds, and what is my dedup horizon?","Is one stateful key (e.g. 'anonymous') skewed?","Is this really a compute problem — or is the state semantically unbounded?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: is `groupBy(...).count()` stateful in batch Spark too?","text":"In a batch job it simply computes once and forgets. In **streaming**, yes — the aggregate must accumulate across micro-batches, so Spark keeps state between them."}]},{"id":"ch13-s06","title":"Event Time vs Processing Time & Event-time Windows","hook":"An order placed at 10:03 that arrives at 10:11 still belongs to 10:00–10:10. Which clock you use decides correctness.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"event_id\": 123,\n  \"event_time\": \"2026-09-28 10:03:00\"\n}","caption":"Arrives in Databricks at 10:11:00"},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Event time — 10:03","points":["When the **business event happened**","A column in the data (event_time)","What windows and watermarks use"]},{"title":"Processing time — 10:11","points":["When the **system saw/processed** it","The engine's wall clock","What triggers use"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"Why can they differ? A **mobile** client offline, **network delay**, a **Kafka backlog**, a **server outage**, **retries**, a **batch upload**, **clock skew**. The purchase happened at 10:03 but arrived at 10:11 → the record is **late-arriving**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: processing time vs event time","text":"Processing time = when Databricks processes it. Event time = the timestamp of when the event occurred. **Watermarks use event time**, not the arrival wall clock."},{"t":"p","text":"**Event-time windows**: sales per 10-minute window."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\nsales = (\n    events\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\"))\n        .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n)","caption":"Tumbling 10-minute windows"},{"t":"diagram","text":"windows:  [10:00–10:10)  [10:10–10:20)  [10:20–10:30)\nevent_time 10:03  ──► lands in [10:00–10:10)\n             ...even if it ARRIVED at 10:11","caption":"The window is chosen by event time"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Tumbling vs sliding windows","text":"`F.window(col, \"10 minutes\")` = **tumbling**: fixed, non-overlapping, each event in exactly one window. `F.window(col, \"10 minutes\", \"5 minutes\")` = **sliding** (window 10 min, slide 5 min): windows overlap, so each event lands in window/slide = **2** windows. (Sliding windows are standard Spark API, added here for completeness.)"},{"t":"p","text":"The window column is a struct with `window.start` and `window.end` — you'll use `window.start` later as a MERGE key (section 13)."},{"t":"p","text":"**The late-data problem.** At 10:10 you might say *'window 10:00–10:10 is finished'*. But at 10:15 an event with `event_time = 10:08` arrives — it belongs to the old window."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Wait forever","points":["correct for arbitrarily late data","**infinite state**"]},{"title":"Close immediately","points":["low state","**lose late data**"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"We need a compromise → the **watermark**."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: with 10-minute tumbling windows, which window does an event with event_time 10:19:59 land in — and one with 10:20:00?","text":"10:19:59 → **[10:10–10:20)**. 10:20:00 → **[10:20–10:30)**. Window start is inclusive, end is exclusive."}]},{"id":"ch13-s07","title":"Watermarks: Bounding State & Late Data","hook":"The watermark is the deal you make: 'I'll keep state for events up to X late — older ones I may drop.'","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"watermarked = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")\n)"},{"t":"p","text":"Meaning, approximately: *I'm willing to keep state so I can accept events that arrive up to about 10 minutes late **relative to the event-time progress I have observed**.*"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks defines a **watermark** as an event-time threshold that lets Structured Streaming **stop keeping old state** and **control late-arriving data**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Watermark ≠ wall-clock timer","text":"It does NOT mean 'each event gets 10 real minutes from its birth'. Spark tracks the **maximum event time seen** and computes: watermark ≈ max event time − delay. Max seen 12:00, delay 10 min → watermark ≈ **11:50**. State older than that may be considered final/evictable."},{"t":"diagram","text":"event-time axis ───────────────────────────────►\n            11:50                   12:00\n              │◄──── delay 10 min ────►│\n          watermark               max event time seen\n older than watermark → too late (may be dropped)\n newer than watermark → still accepted","caption":"Watermark = max event time − delay"},{"t":"p","text":"Why 'approximately'? The API guarantees the actual watermark stays **at least** the configured delay behind event-time progress; coordination between partitions (and, in micro-batch mode, the watermark being updated at the end of a batch and applied to the next) means you must not treat its instantaneous value as a perfect wall-clock cutoff."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"One-liner","text":"*Watermark is an event-time progress threshold, not a simple timer per row.*"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"The guarantee in one line","text":"Data **less late** than the delay is guaranteed to be aggregated. Data **later** than the threshold **may** be dropped — 'may', not 'will': don't build logic that relies on late rows being discarded."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"sales = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"15 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(\n            F.window(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\"),\n            F.col(\"country\"))\n        .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n)","caption":"Stateful aggregation with a watermark"},{"t":"table","head":["window","country","revenue"],"rows":[["10:00–10:10","GR","1200"],["10:00–10:10","DE","800"],["10:10–10:20","GR","400"]],"caption":"What the state might contain"},{"t":"p","text":"When the watermark passes sufficiently beyond an old window, that window's **state can be removed** → state stays **bounded**."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Short watermark (5 min)","points":["less state, lower memory","faster state operations","lower latency for append finalization","❌ records later than the tolerance may be dropped"]},{"title":"Long watermark (2 hours)","points":["accepts more late events","❌ more state & memory","❌ bigger checkpoints","❌ higher latency"]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Too small drops valid data","text":"Databricks describes exactly this **latency / state / correctness** trade-off and warns that a very small watermark can discard **valid** late records. Pick it from how late real data actually arrives."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: watermark purpose","text":"Correct: **bound state + define tolerated lateness / finalization**. Wrong: 'delete old table rows'. A watermark is **not a retention policy** — not VACUUM, not table retention, not a TTL. It never deletes historical Delta rows."},{"t":"p","text":"**'Late' is relative to query progress.** An event arrives 20 minutes after it happened. Late? If the stream's max seen event time is only 5 minutes ahead of it → **could still be accepted**. If the stream has already seen events 2 hours ahead → **could be too late**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"late ≠ now − event_time","text":"Lateness depends on **event-time watermark progress**, not on how long the event spent in transit."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when late events seem to be missing","questions":["What event_time did the missing events carry?","What was the maximum event time already observed when they arrived?","What watermark delay was configured?","Had the watermark already passed their window?","Am I reasoning from arrival time instead of event time?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: max event time seen = 10:20, watermark delay 5 min. An event with event_time 10:03 arrives. Accepted? What if the delay were 30 min?","text":"5 min → watermark ≈ 10:15 > 10:03 → **too late**, may be dropped. 30 min → watermark ≈ 09:50 < 10:03 → **still accepted** and updates its window's state."}]},{"id":"ch13-s08","title":"Output Modes: Append, Update, Complete","hook":"When a result can still change, which rows do you emit — the final ones, the changed ones, or everything?","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Mode","What it emits each trigger"],"rows":[["Append","Only rows considered **final** (will never change again)"],["Update","Rows whose result **changed** in this trigger"],["Complete","The **entire** current result/state every trigger"]]},{"t":"p","text":"**Append**: the 10:00–10:10 revenue may still change at 10:11 because of late data, so append must not emit it yet. With a watermark: wait until the window is considered final → **emit once**. For window aggregations, append writes after the watermark passes the window."},{"t":"p","text":"**Update**: batch 1 → GR revenue = 100 → emit `GR 100`. Batch 2 receives +50 → GR = 150 → emit `GR 150`. Downstream sees **updates**."},{"t":"p","text":"**Complete**: every trigger emits the whole aggregate. Batch 1: GR 100, DE 50. Batch 2: GR 150, DE 50, FR 20 — all of it rewritten."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Complete mode keeps all state","text":"In complete mode aggregation state is **not evicted by the watermark** (the whole result must be re-emitted). With large/unbounded key cardinality it gets expensive."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Delta sink: append + complete, NOT update","text":"Delta Lake streaming sinks support **append** and **complete**, not **update** directly. For update-like semantics into Delta use **foreachBatch + MERGE** (section 13). A classic certification/interview detail."},{"t":"p","text":"**Stateless streams**: `events.filter(...).select(...)` has no stateful row that changes later, so the append/update/complete distinction doesn't change operator behaviour (and complete mode isn't allowed without an aggregation)."},{"t":"table","head":["Query shape","Append","Update","Complete"],"rows":[["Stateless (filter/select)","✅ default","same effect as append","❌ needs an aggregation"],["Aggregation + watermark","✅ emits when window final","✅ changed rows","✅ (state not evicted)"],["Aggregation, no watermark","❌ never final → not allowed","✅","✅"],["Delta table sink","✅","❌ use foreachBatch+MERGE","✅"]],"caption":"Which modes make sense"},{"t":"p","text":"**Production example — event analytics.** Schema: `event_id STRING, user_id BIGINT, event_type STRING, event_time TIMESTAMP`."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\nevents = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n\ncounts = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"), \"event_type\")\n        .count()\n)\n\nquery = (\n    counts.writeStream\n        .outputMode(\"append\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\",\n                \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/event_counts_ckpt\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.event_counts\")\n)","caption":"Window + watermark + append → Delta"},{"t":"diagram","text":"events 10:01 click, 10:02 click, 10:03 purchase\n   → window [10:00–10:05) state: click=2, purchase=1\nlate event, event_time 10:04 → state updates\nwatermark advances past 10:05 (max event time ≥ ~10:25)\n   → append: window FINAL → emit row once\n   → window state removed","caption":"The complete lifecycle of one window"},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"\"Why does append output look delayed?\"","text":"Events arrive instantly but rows don't appear. **Stateful aggregate + append mode + watermark** means Spark must wait until it considers the aggregate final (window end + watermark delay). This is **intended behaviour**, not necessarily lag."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: output mode","text":"Append = finalized rows. Update = changed rows. Complete = entire result. And **not every sink supports every mode** — Delta doesn't support update directly."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: a dashboard must show click counts growing live (10:01 → 1, 10:02 → 2) in a Delta table. Which output mode?","text":"Not append (waits for finality) and not update (Delta sink doesn't support it). Use **foreachBatch + MERGE** to upsert the changing aggregates (section 13)."}]},{"id":"ch13-s09","title":"Checkpoint Internals: Offsets, Commits, State & Query Identity","hook":"In Chapter 2 the checkpoint was 'where I stopped'. Now we open the box — and see why it is part of the query's identity.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Databricks docs say a checkpoint directory contains at least **offsets**, **commits** and, for stateful workloads, **state** metadata/content."},{"t":"diagram","text":"checkpoint/\n│\n├── offsets/    what range each micro-batch READS\n├── commits/    which micro-batches were WRITTEN to the sink\n├── state/      state-store data of stateful operators\n└── metadata    the query's identity","caption":"Inside a checkpoint"},{"t":"terms","items":[{"term":"Offset","def":"The source's 'how far have I read?'. Kafka: partition 0 → offset 1050, partition 1 → offset 2040. File/Auto Loader sources have an equivalent progress concept."},{"term":"Commit log","def":"Record of the micro-batches that have successfully committed to the sink — a key part of exactly-once semantics."},{"term":"State","def":"State-store metadata/content for stateful operators (counts per customer, open windows, join buffers, dedup keys)."},{"term":"Metadata","def":"Identifies the query; changing it effectively means a different query."}]},{"t":"p","text":"**Offsets**: the checkpoint keeps the source offsets / progress for **each micro-batch**. **Commits**: suppose micro-batch 42 processed source range X — the checkpoint must also know *'was batch 42 written successfully to the sink?'*."},{"t":"p","text":"**State in the checkpoint**: for `events.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count()`, after a restart Spark must remember customer_1 = 120, customer_2 = 50 … Without it, a new event for customer_1 wouldn't know the previous total was 120."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: what a checkpoint stores","text":"Progress **offsets**, **commits** and **state** information (plus query metadata) — **not** just a 'last timestamp'."},{"t":"p","text":"**Why stateful queries are harder to change.** The checkpoint's state has a schema: key = customer_id, value = count. Change the code to key = country, value = sum(amount) and you can't necessarily reuse the same checkpoint. Databricks has explicit restrictions on what may change between restarts from the same checkpoint."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"A checkpoint is not a generic reusable folder","text":"It encodes the **query identity and state**. One checkpoint ↔ one query. Never share it between two streams."},{"t":"p","text":"**Deleting the checkpoint** (or changing `checkpointLocation`) makes the next run start as a **new query**: query identity, progress and state are gone."},{"t":"flow","items":["reprocessing","duplicate external side effects","loss of accumulated state","full rebuild required"],"caption":"What a lost checkpoint can mean (per pipeline)"},{"t":"table","head":["Change between deployments","Checkpoint reuse"],"rows":[["Some stateless filters / projections","Often OK — depends on the exact query"],["State keys (groupBy columns)","❌ dangerous / incompatible"],["State schema (agg functions, value columns)","❌ dangerous / incompatible"],["Source type (Kafka → Delta…)","❌ incompatible"],["Number/type of stateful operators","❌ incompatible"]],"caption":"Checkpoint & code deployment"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Rule for deployments","text":"Never assume 'same checkpoint' means 'new code will pick up seamlessly'. Consult the compatibility rules and **test stateful deployments** carefully."},{"t":"p","text":"**Stateful restart from a new checkpoint.** If you change the state architecture, the old checkpoint is incompatible and you may need **new checkpoint + full recomputation/backfill**. That is expensive — which is why **state schema is effectively production data**."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before deploying a change to a streaming query","questions":["Is this query stateless or stateful?","Does my change touch state keys, state schema, the source type or the stateful operators?","If the checkpoint becomes incompatible, can I afford a new checkpoint + backfill?","Have I tested the restart from the existing checkpoint in a non-prod copy?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Sandbox lab: checkpoint reset","text":"Run a stream, then restart it with a **new checkpoint location**: it behaves as a brand-new stream. That proves the checkpoint is part of query identity. **Only in a sandbox** — never casually in production."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: you delete the checkpoint of an append Bronze→Silver stream reading a Delta table and restart. What do you expect in Silver?","text":"The query starts as new, so it re-reads the source from its starting point and **re-appends rows already written → duplicates** (and any stateful results restart from zero)."}]},{"id":"ch13-s10","title":"Exactly-once: What It Really Means (and Streaming Dedup)","hook":"Exactly-once is a precise engine guarantee with sharp edges — not a promise that duplicates can't exist.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For a managed **Delta streaming sink**, three things cooperate so that a retried micro-batch never becomes a partial or duplicate commit:"},{"t":"flow","items":["source offsets","checkpoint commits","Delta transaction log"],"caption":"The exactly-once trio"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks states the Delta streaming sink provides **exactly-once processing guarantees**, even with concurrent streams or batch queries on the table."},{"t":"p","text":"**Failure scenario**: micro-batch 20 reads events 1000–1100 and writes to Delta. The system crashes exactly at the boundary. On restart the engine must decide: *was batch 20 committed?*"},{"t":"diagram","text":"1. write offsets/20  (plan: read 1000–1100)\n2. process batch 20\n3. Delta commit (tagged with query id + batch 20)\n4. write commits/20\n        ✗ crash somewhere\nrestart: offsets/20 exists, commits/20 missing\n  → batch 20 already in the Delta log?  skip it\n  → not in the log?                     retry it\nNEVER: \"half applied twice\"","caption":"Restart decision"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Exactly-once stops at the boundaries","text":"`foreachBatch(send_email)` sends 100 emails, then the process crashes before the checkpoint commit. On retry the same batch runs again → **emails sent again**. The email service has no Delta transaction semantics. Structured Streaming exactly-once ≠ every arbitrary side effect exactly once."},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks stresses that exactly-once guarantees in managed Delta flows **stop at the edges** — custom or non-transactional sinks. There you must make the write **idempotent**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Exactly-once ≠ no duplicate source events","text":"Kafka can deliver `event_id=123` twice as **two distinct records**. The engine processes each input record exactly once — and both duplicates land in the table. **Processing guarantee ≠ business deduplication** (same lesson as Auto Loader files, Chapter 12)."},{"t":"p","text":"**Streaming deduplication.** The simple version:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"unique = events.dropDuplicates([\"event_id\"])","caption":"Correct result — unbounded state"},{"t":"p","text":"Problem: to know whether `event_id=123` has **ever** appeared, Spark must remember every event ID **forever** → unbounded state. You usually need a **watermark / time bound**:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"unique = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"1 hour\")\n        .dropDuplicates([\"event_id\", \"event_time\"])\n)","caption":"Dedup state cleaned as event time progresses"},{"t":"p","text":"Including the watermarked `event_time` column in the dedup keys lets Spark drop old keys once the watermark passes them. **Deduplication is a stateful operation.**"},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark","text":"Newer Spark/Databricks runtimes also offer `events.withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"1 hour\").dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark([\"event_id\"])` — dedups on the key alone for duplicates arriving within the watermark delay, even if their timestamps differ slightly. (Standard API, beyond the source; the source's pattern above is the classic one.)"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when someone says 'it's exactly-once, so no duplicates'","questions":["Is the sink a managed Delta sink, or a custom / foreachBatch / external sink?","Could the source itself contain the same business event twice?","Was the checkpoint ever reset or changed?","Is there any non-idempotent side effect (emails, API calls, balance += x)?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Q18 — 'Exactly-once Delta sink means the source cannot contain duplicates.' True?","text":"**No.** Processing guarantees don't deduplicate semantically duplicated input records. Two records with the same event_id are two records to the engine."}]},{"id":"ch13-s11","title":"Streaming Joins: Stream-Static & Stream-Stream","hook":"Joining a live stream to a table is easy; joining two live streams forces Spark to wait — and waiting costs state.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"**Stream-static join**: live `orders` stream + a relatively static `products` table. Very common for enrichment."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"products = spark.table(\"ecommerce.silver.products\")   # static (batch) side\n\nenriched = (\n    orders_stream\n        .join(products, \"product_id\")\n)","caption":"stream events + static dimension → enriched stream"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Static side semantics","text":"If the static table changes, behaviour depends on the query/execution semantics and refresh architecture. With a Delta static side, **new** micro-batches may join against a newer version — but a long-running stream does **not** magically recompute already-written outputs because a dimension changed. If correctness requires recomputation when dimensions change, materialized/declarative pipeline patterns (Phase 6) may fit better."},{"t":"p","text":"**Stream-stream join**: `orders` stream + `payments` stream, matched on `order_id`. The order event arrives at 10:00, the payment at 10:02 → Spark must keep the **order in state** until its payment arrives. For how long? Forever? Without a watermark/time condition, state can grow **without bound**."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\norders_w   = orders.withWatermark(\"order_time\", \"30 minutes\")\npayments_w = payments.withWatermark(\"payment_time\", \"30 minutes\")\n\njoined = orders_w.alias(\"o\").join(\n    payments_w.alias(\"p\"),\n    F.expr(\"\"\"\n        o.order_id = p.order_id AND\n        p.payment_time BETWEEN o.order_time\n                           AND o.order_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR\n    \"\"\"),\n    \"inner\")","caption":"Key + time relationship + watermarks on both sides"},{"t":"p","text":"The join condition contains **key + time relationship**: same `order_id` **and** the payment occurs within the expected time range. Databricks recommends **watermarks on both inputs** so obsolete state can be discarded."},{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"Trap: watermarks and outer joins","text":"For stream-stream **outer** joins a watermark (plus a time constraint) is **required**: the engine must know when an unmatched record can be considered final and emitted with NULLs."},{"t":"p","text":"**Multiple watermarks.** orders: 10 min delay, payments: 1 hour delay. Spark must compute **one global watermark**. The default policy is **min** — the slowest stream decides progress. You can choose **max**, but then records of the slower stream may be dropped; Databricks warns to use it with care."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.streaming.multipleWatermarkPolicy\", \"min\")   # default\n# \"max\" = faster progress, but slow-stream records may be dropped","caption":"The policy setting"},{"t":"diagram","text":"orders watermark   → 14:00\npayments watermark → 13:00\npolicy max: global 14:00 → state for 13:xx may be dropped\n            but payments for 13:xx can still arrive  ✗\npolicy min: global 13:00 → follows the SLOWEST input  ✓","caption":"Why 'min' protects correctness"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before writing a stream-stream join","questions":["How long can a matching record from the other stream arrive after this one?","Do both inputs have a watermark on their event-time column?","Does my join condition include a time range, not just the key?","Is it an outer join (then watermark + time constraint are mandatory)?","Will the global watermark (min) be held back by a slow input?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: why do stream-stream joins need state at all, when stream-static joins don't buffer the stream?","text":"Because a record from one stream may need to **wait** for a later matching record from the other stream. The static side is fully available every batch; a stream side isn't."}]},{"id":"ch13-s12","title":"Delta as Streaming Source & Sink: Changes and Retention","hook":"Delta-to-Delta streaming is the backbone of medallion pipelines — with two operational landmines.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"bronze = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")","caption":"Stream the additions of a Bronze Delta table downstream"},{"t":"p","text":"Delta tables can be both **sources** and **sinks** of Structured Streaming. A Bronze table that keeps receiving rows can feed Silver incrementally."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Delta source is append-oriented","text":"A simple Delta streaming source naturally streams **appends**. If the source table gets **UPDATE / DELETE / MERGE**, downstream semantics need more care. For row-level changes, **Change Data Feed** is often the right tool (joined with CDC later)."},{"t":"p","text":"Accuracy note (standard Delta behaviour): by default a commit that changes existing data in the source makes the stream **fail** rather than silently re-emit rewritten files. The option `skipChangeCommits` ignores such commits (their changes are **not** propagated); reading the **Change Data Feed** propagates them properly."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"# ignore update/delete commits (changes NOT propagated)\nspark.readStream.option(\"skipChangeCommits\", \"true\").table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n\n# propagate row-level changes via Change Data Feed\nspark.readStream.option(\"readChangeFeed\", \"true\").table(\"ecommerce.silver.customers\")","caption":"Two ways to handle non-append source commits"},{"t":"p","text":"**Source retention caveat.** If a stream reading a Delta source is stopped much longer than the source's retention, the files/history it needs may be **vacuumed** — it may never catch up."},{"t":"diagram","text":"Friday:        stream stops (checkpoint: \"I need version X\")\nsource:        VACUUM retention = 7 days\n2 weeks later: restart\n               version X files no longer exist\n→ stream cannot recover incrementally","caption":"Retention vs a stopped stream"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Run at least once per retention window","text":"Databricks: a stream must run **at least once within the source retention window**, otherwise it may fail with a **missing-file** error and need a reset/full refresh. Design streaming operations and source retention **together**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"Don't 'fix' missing files with ignoreMissingFiles","text":"Do **not** set `spark.sql.files.ignoreMissingFiles=true` as a workaround for a stream that fell behind Delta retention — Databricks warns it can produce **silent incorrect results**. Correct fix: **increase retention**, or **recover/rebuild** the stream appropriately. Never suppress a correctness error."},{"t":"code","lang":"sql","code":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.bronze.events SET TBLPROPERTIES (\n  'delta.logRetentionDuration'         = 'interval 30 days',\n  'delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration' = 'interval 14 days'\n);","caption":"Increasing retention so a stopped stream can still catch up"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a Delta-source stream fails after a long stop","questions":["How long was the stream stopped, compared with the source's retention settings?","Did a VACUUM run on the source in the meantime (DESCRIBE HISTORY)?","Is the error about missing files for a version the checkpoint still needs?","Am I tempted to flip ignoreMissingFiles — and do I understand that hides data loss?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Q28 — why can a stream fail if it was stopped beyond Delta retention?","text":"Because the source versions/files it still needs to read were **vacuumed** before the stream caught up. The checkpoint points to data that physically no longer exists."}]},{"id":"ch13-s13","title":"foreachBatch, Streaming MERGE & Idempotency","hook":"foreachBatch turns each micro-batch into an ordinary DataFrame — full power, full responsibility.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"def process_batch(batch_df, batch_id):\n    ...\n\nquery = (\n    stream_df.writeStream\n        .foreachBatch(process_batch)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .start()\n)","caption":"The bridge between streaming and batch APIs"},{"t":"flow","items":["stream","micro-batch → batch DataFrame","your function(batch_df, batch_id)"],"caption":"Per micro-batch"},{"t":"p","text":"Inside the function you can use **batch-only** operations: **MERGE**, complex Delta DML, custom sink logic."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\ndef upsert(batch_df, batch_id):\n    target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"ecommerce.silver.customers\")\n    (target.alias(\"t\")\n        .merge(batch_df.alias(\"s\"), \"t.customer_id = s.customer_id\")\n        .whenMatchedUpdateAll()\n        .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n        .execute())\n\nquery = (\n    changes.writeStream\n        .foreachBatch(upsert)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .start()\n)","caption":"The classic streaming-upsert pattern"},{"t":"callout","kind":"pitfall","title":"foreachBatch must be idempotent","text":"If batch 10 retries, your function **may execute again**. `balance = balance + incoming_amount` twice → wrong. `MERGE` by a stable business key/version → can be idempotent. foreachBatch gives power, but **you** become responsible for sink-side idempotency."},{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Not idempotent","points":["`balance = balance + amount`","INSERT/append without a key","send_email(), call_api()","Replay = applied twice"]},{"title":"Idempotent","points":["MERGE on business key (+ version)","overwrite of a deterministic partition","Upsert where replay gives the same end state","Replay = same result"]}]},{"t":"p","text":"**batch_id** can help some external-sink patterns recognise a **replayed** batch. But don't treat batch_id alone as a universal business dedup key — the safest design depends on the target system."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Delta's built-in idempotent foreachBatch writes","text":"For plain Delta writes inside foreachBatch, Delta supports the writer options `txnAppId` (a stable id for the query) and `txnVersion` (e.g. the batch_id) so a replayed batch is skipped. (Standard Delta API, beyond the source.)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Update-like results to Delta.** A dashboard wants live counts (10:01 → click_count=1, 10:02 → 2). Delta doesn't support streaming update output mode as a sink, so implement update-like semantics with foreachBatch + MERGE:"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"def merge_counts(batch_df, batch_id):\n    target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"ecommerce.gold.live_event_counts\")\n    (target.alias(\"t\")\n        .merge(batch_df.alias(\"s\"),\n               \"\"\"t.window_start = s.window.start\n                  AND t.event_type = s.event_type\"\"\")\n        .whenMatchedUpdateAll()\n        .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n        .execute())\n\n(counts.writeStream\n    .outputMode(\"update\")          # emit changed aggregates to the function\n    .foreachBatch(merge_counts)\n    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt)\n    .start())","caption":"Window aggregates upserted into Delta"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Why update mode is fine here","text":"The **sink** is now your function, not the Delta sink — so `outputMode(\"update\")` simply hands each batch's **changed** aggregate rows to the function, which MERGEs them. (In practice flatten `window.start` into a real `window_start` column to match the target schema.)"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself before shipping a foreachBatch function","questions":["If this exact batch runs twice, is the end state the same?","Am I merging on a stable business key (and version/sequence)?","Do I trigger external side effects that can't be rolled back?","Can the micro-batch contain several rows per key that must be deduplicated before MERGE?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Q20 — why can foreachBatch duplicate external side effects even though the stream is 'exactly-once'?","text":"A failed/retried batch may invoke the function again, and external sinks don't participate in Spark/Delta transaction semantics. Solution (Q21): make side effects **idempotent** — business key/version, or transactional sink semantics."}]},{"id":"ch13-s14","title":"Performance: Latency, Throughput, Backlog, Small Files & Skew","hook":"A stream must not only be correct — every micro-batch must finish fast enough to keep up with arrivals.","blocks":[{"t":"compare","items":[{"title":"Small micro-batches (every 1 s)","points":["✅ lower latency","❌ more scheduling overhead","❌ more frequent commits","❌ more storage API calls","❌ smaller output files"]},{"title":"Large micro-batches (every 1 min)","points":["✅ better throughput efficiency","✅ larger batches, less overhead","❌ higher latency"]}]},{"t":"flow","items":["latency","throughput","cost","state size","correctness"],"caption":"Streaming design always balances"},{"t":"p","text":"**Small files in streaming**: every tiny micro-batch writing to Delta → batch 1 → 2 MB file, batch 2 → 3 MB, batch 3 → 1 MB … → **small-file pressure**."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Physical maintenance still matters","text":"Delta can compact later (**OPTIMIZE**, **predictive optimization**), and Delta's streaming integration addresses transactional and small-file concerns — but choose the latency requirement sensibly. Don't trigger every second if every minute is enough."},{"t":"p","text":"**Backpressure / backlog**: the source produces **50 MB/s**, the pipeline processes **20 MB/s** → **+30 MB/s** backlog."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"No checkpoint magic fixes throughput","text":"A checkpoint preserves correctness/progress; it does not create processing capacity. Sustained input > processing → latency ↑, backlog ↑, and eventually **source retention may be exceeded**."},{"t":"list","items":["more processing capacity","a better (cheaper) query","larger batches","less state","better partitioning","reduce skew"],"ordered":false},{"t":"p","text":"**Micro-batch = Spark job**, so it suffers the exact same issues as batch: skew, shuffle, bad joins, too few or too many partitions, spill, executor OOM, driver OOM. Streaming adds one more dimension: **it must finish fast enough to keep up**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"trigger intended: 5 s\n99 partitions → finish in 2 s\n 1 partition  → takes 30 s   (skew)\n→ every micro-batch ≥ 30 s → backlog grows","caption":"Skew in a stream (Phase 1 meets Phase 5)"},{"t":"p","text":"**Stateful + skew is especially bad.** One key `user_id = \"anonymous\"` holds 70% of events; `.groupBy(\"user_id\")` creates a **hot state partition**."},{"t":"list","items":["one task slow","one state partition huge","spill","increasing latency"],"ordered":false},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Key design matters in streaming","text":"Streaming partition/key design decides whether state is spread evenly. A hot key in state hurts every single micro-batch, forever."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a stream can't keep up","questions":["Is input rate permanently higher than processing rate, or was it a burst?","Is one task (or one state partition) much slower than the rest?","Is my trigger so small that overhead and tiny files dominate?","Will the backlog outlive the source's retention?"]},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: Q31 — input rate permanently exceeds processing rate. Would a checkpoint fix this?","text":"No. Checkpoint preserves correctness/progress; it doesn't create processing capacity. Backlog and latency keep growing (Q30) until you add capacity or make the query cheaper."}]},{"id":"ch13-s15","title":"Monitoring & the Production Debugging Tree","hook":"Streaming bugs show up as numbers: rates, durations, state rows, the watermark. Read them before you restart anything.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"query.status          # what is the query doing right now?\nquery.lastProgress    # metrics of the last micro-batch (dict/JSON)\nquery.recentProgress  # list of recent progress reports","caption":"Code-level monitoring"},{"t":"code","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"batchId\": 812,\n  \"numInputRows\": 1500000,\n  \"inputRowsPerSecond\": 100000.0,\n  \"processedRowsPerSecond\": 40000.0,\n  \"durationMs\": {\"triggerExecution\": 37500},\n  \"eventTime\": {\"watermark\": \"2026-09-28T10:15:00.000Z\"},\n  \"stateOperators\": [{\"numRowsTotal\": 48210933}]\n}","caption":"Illustrative lastProgress fields worth knowing"},{"t":"table","head":["Metric","What it tells you"],"rows":[["input rows / inputRowsPerSecond","How fast data arrives"],["processedRowsPerSecond","How fast you process it"],["batch duration","Is each micro-batch keeping up with the trigger?"],["state rows (numRowsTotal)","Is state bounded or growing forever?"],["watermark","How far event-time progress has moved"],["shuffle / task skew","Ordinary Spark problems inside the batch"]],"caption":"What you care about in Spark UI / Databricks monitoring"},{"t":"p","text":"Databricks positions the **Spark UI** (streaming tab) and the **StreamingQueryListener** as the production monitoring mechanisms."},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Input rate vs processing rate","text":"input 50,000 rows/s, processed 100,000 rows/s → capacity to spare. input 100,000, processed 40,000 **sustained** → backlog grows. Often more useful than staring at cluster CPU."},{"t":"p","text":"**State growth**: batch durations go 10 s → 12 s → 20 s → 40 s → 2 min … while input rate is **stable**. Inspect the stateful operators: is `numRowsTotal` in state increasing forever? Likely: **missing watermark, watermark too long, unbounded key cardinality, bad stateful join**."},{"t":"diagram","text":"STREAMING LATENCY HIGH\n        ↓\nIs input backlog growing?\n        ↓ yes\nWhich micro-batch is slow?\n        ↓\nSpark stage/task metrics\n        ↓\n┌──────────────┬───────────────┬───────────────┐\nSPARK          STATE           SOURCE/SINK\nshuffle        growth          throughput\nskew           watermark       API latency\nspill          state store     storage\njoin","caption":"The production debugging tree"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Streaming adds three factors","text":"Streaming adds **state**, **source** and **sink** factors on top of ordinary Spark performance (shuffle, skew, spill, join)."},{"t":"table","head":["Case","First things to check"],"rows":[["Stream stopped progressing","query active? source producing? new offsets/files? checkpoint corrupted? sink blocked? state batch stuck? executor failure? permission expired? source retention violated?"],["Batch durations increasing","state growth, checkpoint overhead, small files/output overhead, source metadata growth, sink slowdown"],["Missing late records","event_time, max observed event_time, configured watermark, watermark progress, output mode, window boundaries"],["Duplicates after failure","Delta sink? custom sink? foreachBatch? source duplicates? checkpoint reset? non-idempotent side effect?"],["State store huge","number of keys, watermark, window duration, late-data tolerance, join time bounds, dedup horizon, stateful key skew"]],"caption":"The five debugging cases (full playbooks in the Debug tab)"},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when the stream stopped making progress","questions":["Is the query active, or did it terminate with an exception?","Is the source still producing — are new offsets/files available?","Is the checkpoint corrupted or changed?","Is the sink blocked, or a state batch stuck?","Did an executor fail repeatedly?","Did a permission/credential expire?","Was source retention violated while it was stopped?"]},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when batch durations grow but input is stable","questions":["Is state (numRowsTotal) growing every batch?","Is checkpoint/state-store overhead rising?","Are small output files or source metadata growth adding per-batch cost?","Is the sink slowing down?"]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Don't restart blindly","text":"When a stream stops progressing, **inspect progress before restarting**. A restart can hide the evidence (and with a changed checkpoint, create duplicates)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"Missing late records: don't blame the checkpoint first","text":"Check the event-time chain (event_time → max observed → watermark → output mode → window boundaries). Blame the **checkpoint** only if evidence points there."},{"t":"callout","kind":"debug","title":"State store huge: maybe not a compute problem","text":"The fix isn't necessarily bigger compute — maybe the state is **semantically unbounded** (no watermark, no join time bound, infinite dedup horizon)."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Exactly-once is end-to-end only when every piece participates","text":"Duplicates after a failure mean some piece didn't participate: a non-transactional sink, a non-idempotent foreachBatch, a duplicating source or a reset checkpoint."},{"t":"ask","title":"Ask yourself when a stream is slow (the tree, as questions)","questions":["Is the input backlog actually growing (input rate vs processed rate)?","Which micro-batch is slow, and since when?","Inside that batch, which stage/task dominates?","Is it a Spark problem (shuffle, skew, spill, join)?","Is it a state problem (growth, watermark, state store)?","Is it a source/sink problem (throughput, API latency, storage)?"]}]},{"id":"ch13-s16","title":"Labs, Best-Practice Model & the Phase 5 Mini-Project","hook":"Eight small labs make the engine visible; one design checklist and one mini-project glue Phase 5 together.","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\nevents = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"rate\")\n        .option(\"rowsPerSecond\", 10)\n        .load()                       # columns: timestamp, value (0,1,2,...)\n)\ntransformed = events.withColumn(\"group_id\", F.col(\"value\") % 5)","caption":"Lab 1 — rate source: synthetic stream, no external ingestion complexity"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"positive = (\n    events\n        .filter(\"value % 2 = 0\")\n        .select(\"timestamp\", \"value\")\n)","caption":"Lab 2 — stateless: no aggregate state, each record independent"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"counts = (\n    events\n        .withColumn(\"group_id\", F.col(\"value\") % 5)\n        .groupBy(\"group_id\")\n        .count()\n)   # Spark must remember 0 → count, 1 → count, ...","caption":"Lab 3 — stateful aggregate: inspect progress/state metrics"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"windowed = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"timestamp\", \"30 seconds\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"timestamp\", \"10 seconds\"))\n        .count()\n)","caption":"Lab 4 — event-time windows: watch when windows appear/finalize per output mode"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"stream_df = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.orders\")\n(\n    stream_df.writeStream\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\",\n                \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/orders_to_silver\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.orders_incremental\")\n)\n# run again with no new data → nothing new to process","caption":"Lab 5 — AvailableNow on Delta"},{"t":"p","text":"**Lab 6 — intentional checkpoint reset** (sandbox only): run the stream, then use a **new checkpoint location** → the query behaves as a new stream. Proof that the checkpoint is part of query identity."},{"t":"p","text":"**Lab 7 — late data**: controlled events, arrival order 10:01 (event_time 10:01), 10:02 (10:02), 10:20 (event_time 10:03). Try watermark = 5 min and = 30 min and observe whether the late record can still affect state."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Make Lab 7 actually show the difference","text":"With only those three events, the max event time seen is **10:02**, so the 10:03 event isn't late in event-time terms under either watermark — it arrived 18 minutes late on the wall clock, but lateness is relative to event-time progress. Add an on-time event with event_time ≈ 10:20 **before** it (and let a micro-batch pass so the watermark advances): then 5 min → watermark ≈ 10:15 → dropped; 30 min → ≈ 09:50 → accepted."},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"def inspect_batch(df, batch_id):\n    print(f\"Batch: {batch_id}\")\n    print(f\"Rows: {df.count()}\")\n\nquery = (\n    events.writeStream\n        .foreachBatch(inspect_batch)\n        .start()\n)   # prints Batch 0, Batch 1, Batch 2, ...","caption":"Lab 8 — foreachBatch: micro-batches become physically visible"},{"t":"table","head":["Design question","Ask"],"rows":[["SOURCE","What are its ordering/duplicate guarantees?"],["EVENT TIME","Which timestamp represents reality?"],["LATE DATA","How late can valid records arrive?"],["STATE","What information must be remembered?"],["WATERMARK","When can old state safely disappear?"],["TRIGGER","How much latency do we need?"],["SINK","Can it commit idempotently?"],["CHECKPOINT","Where is durable progress/state stored?"],["RETENTION","Can the source retain data long enough to recover?"]],"caption":"Best-practice mental model — design these TOGETHER"},{"t":"callout","kind":"key","title":"Not independent choices","text":"Late-data tolerance sets the watermark, which bounds the state, which drives cost; the trigger sets latency and file sizes; retention must exceed your longest possible stop. Change one, re-check the others. Memory hook: **S-E-L-S-W-T-S-C-R** (\"**S**treams **E**at **L**ate **S**tate, **W**atch **T**he **S**ink, **C**heck **R**etention\")."},{"t":"p","text":"**Phase 5 mini-project** — extend the e-shop project:"},{"t":"flow","items":["Auto Loader","ecommerce.bronze.events","Structured Streaming clean/validate","ecommerce.silver.events","window aggregation","ecommerce.gold.events_5min"]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"bronze = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n\nsilver = (\n    bronze\n        .filter(\"event_id IS NOT NULL\")\n        .filter(\"event_time IS NOT NULL\")\n)\n(\n    silver.writeStream\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\",\n                \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/bronze_to_silver_events\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")\n)","caption":"Bronze → Silver (stateless, AvailableNow)"},{"t":"code","lang":"python","code":"gold = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .table(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"), \"event_type\")\n        .agg(F.count(\"*\").alias(\"events\"))\n)\n(\n    gold.writeStream\n        .outputMode(\"append\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\",\n                \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/events_gold_5min\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.events_5min\")\n)","caption":"Silver → Gold (stateful, watermark + 5-min windows, append)"},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Running the mini-project on serverless","text":"The Gold query above uses the **default** trigger. On serverless notebooks/jobs only AvailableNow (and deprecated Once) are supported, so add `.trigger(availableNow=True)` there too and schedule it — or run it on classic compute / a Lakeflow pipeline."},{"t":"p","text":"Then test it **deliberately** with:"},{"t":"list","items":["on-time events","late events within the watermark","very late events (beyond the watermark)","a stream restart","a new checkpoint (sandbox only)","duplicate business events","a prolonged stop that approaches source retention"],"ordered":true},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Q35 — How would you explain Structured Streaming?","text":"Structured Streaming treats an unbounded data source as an **incrementally growing table**. You define transformations with Spark's DataFrame model; the engine executes newly available data in **micro-batches** or supported low-latency modes. **Checkpoints** track source progress and commits, **state stores** preserve intermediate state for aggregations, joins and deduplication, and **watermarks** bound that state and define lateness tolerance for event-time processing."},{"t":"reveal","label":"Think first: in the mini-project, why is Bronze→Silver fine with AvailableNow and no watermark, while Silver→Gold needs one?","text":"Bronze→Silver is **stateless** (filters) — only progress is tracked. Silver→Gold is a **windowed aggregation in append mode**: without a watermark the windows could never be finalized (append isn't even allowed) and state would grow forever."}]}],"debug":[{"id":"ch13-d01","title":"Streaming latency high — the production debugging tree (master playbook)","section":"ch13-s15","symptom":"End-to-end latency keeps rising; dashboards are minutes or hours behind; batch durations exceed the trigger interval.","askYourself":["Is the input backlog actually growing — is inputRowsPerSecond above processedRowsPerSecond, sustained?","Which micro-batch got slow, and since when (a deploy, a data change, a key)?","Inside that batch, which stage and which tasks dominate?","Is it an ordinary Spark problem — shuffle, skew, spill, a bad join?","Is it a state problem — state growing, watermark not advancing, state-store commit time?","Is it a source/sink problem — source throughput, sink API latency, storage writes?","Am I about to 'fix' it with a restart or a new checkpoint instead of with evidence?"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare input vs processed rate in `query.lastProgress` / recentProgress","why":"Confirms a real backlog vs a one-off spike","code":"p = query.lastProgress\np['inputRowsPerSecond'], p['processedRowsPerSecond']"},{"do":"Find the slow micro-batch (durationMs over time)","why":"Pinpoints when the regression started"},{"do":"Open Spark UI stage/task metrics for that batch","why":"Median vs max task duration, shuffle read, spill"},{"do":"Check stateOperators (numRowsTotal, memory) and the eventTime watermark","why":"State growth / stuck watermark is streaming-specific"},{"do":"Check source and sink throughput/latency","why":"External bottlenecks don't show up as CPU"},{"do":"Fix the branch you found: capacity, query, partitioning/skew, state design, sink","why":"No checkpoint magic fixes throughput"}],"rootCauses":["ingestion rate > processing capacity","skew / shuffle / spill inside the micro-batch","unbounded or skewed state","slow source or sink"],"fix":"Follow the tree: backlog → slow batch → stage/task metrics → Spark vs state vs source/sink branch, then fix that branch (capacity, query, key design, watermark, sink).","mnemonic":"B-B-S then 3 S's: Backlog → Batch → Stage, then Spark / State / Source-sink"},{"id":"ch13-d02","title":"Stream stopped making progress","section":"ch13-s15","symptom":"No new rows reach the sink for a long time; the query may look 'running' or may have stopped.","askYourself":["Is the query actually active (status) or has it terminated with an exception?","Is the source still producing — are there new offsets/files available?","Is the checkpoint corrupted or pointing somewhere unexpected?","Is the sink blocked (locks, throttling, failing writes)?","Is a stateful batch stuck (huge state commit)?","Did an executor fail repeatedly?","Did a permission or credential expire?","Was source retention violated while it was stopped?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read `query.status`, `query.lastProgress` and the exception (if any)","why":"Evidence before action","code":"query.status\nquery.lastProgress\nquery.exception()"},{"do":"Check the source for new data/offsets","why":"Maybe there's simply nothing new"},{"do":"Inspect checkpoint location and recent commits","why":"Is progress being recorded?"},{"do":"Check sink, executors and permissions in the logs","why":"Blocked sink, executor loss and expired permissions all stall progress"},{"do":"Compare the stop duration with source retention","why":"Missing-file failures need recover/rebuild, not ignoreMissingFiles"}],"rootCauses":["query terminated","no new source data","corrupted/changed checkpoint","blocked sink","stuck state batch","executor failure","expired permission","source retention violated"],"fix":"Don't restart blindly. Identify which of the nine checks fails, fix that cause, then restart on the SAME checkpoint.","mnemonic":"ON → FED → STUCK → ALLOWED (active? source producing? something stuck? still permitted & retained?)"},{"id":"ch13-d03","title":"Batch durations increase while input is stable","section":"ch13-s15","symptom":"Batch duration goes 10 s → 12 s → 20 s → 40 s → 2 min … while inputRowsPerSecond stays flat.","askYourself":["Is state (numRowsTotal) growing every batch?","Is there a watermark at all — and is it far longer than real lateness?","Is the key cardinality unbounded (new keys forever)?","Is a stateful join or dedup missing time bounds?","Is checkpoint/state-store overhead rising (consider RocksDB + changelog checkpointing)?","Are tiny output files or source metadata growth slowing each batch?","Is the sink getting slower?"],"steps":[{"do":"Plot numRowsTotal from stateOperators across batches","why":"Endless growth = watermark/state design is the primary suspect"},{"do":"Review watermark, window duration, join bounds and dedup horizon","why":"Each is a state bound"},{"do":"Check state-store commit/checkpoint durations","why":"State-store architecture may matter more than compute"},{"do":"Check sink file sizes and sink latency","why":"Small files / sink slowdown add per-batch cost"}],"rootCauses":["state growth (missing/too long watermark, unbounded keys, bad stateful join)","checkpoint overhead","small files / output overhead","source metadata growth","sink slowdown"],"fix":"Bound the state (add/shorten watermark to realistic lateness, time-bound joins/dedup, fix key design), use RocksDB + changelog checkpointing for large state, compact small files.","mnemonic":"Stable in, slower out → suspect STATE first"},{"id":"ch13-d04","title":"Late records are missing from windowed results","section":"ch13-s07","symptom":"Some events that happened inside a window never show up in its aggregate; nothing failed.","askYourself":["What event_time did the missing records carry?","What was the maximum event time already observed when they arrived?","What watermark delay is configured, on which column?","Had the watermark already passed their window when they arrived?","Which output mode is used — and has the window been emitted yet?","Are the window boundaries what I think (start inclusive, end exclusive)?","Am I wrongly reasoning from arrival time instead of event time?"],"steps":[{"do":"Look up the missing rows' event_time in Bronze","why":"Starting point of the event-time chain"},{"do":"Read the watermark from lastProgress around their arrival","why":"Was the record behind the watermark?","code":"query.lastProgress['eventTime']['watermark']"},{"do":"Check withWatermark column and delay","why":"Watermark must be on event time, not ingest time"},{"do":"Check output mode and whether the window is final yet","why":"Append may simply not have emitted it yet"},{"do":"Only then look at the checkpoint","why":"Don't blame the checkpoint first without evidence"}],"rootCauses":["watermark too short for real lateness","watermark on the wrong (processing-time) column","window not yet final in append mode","misunderstood window boundaries"],"fix":"Size the watermark from measured lateness (accepting more state/latency), make sure it is on the event-time column, and explain append-mode finalization to consumers.","mnemonic":"E-M-W-P-O-W: Event time, Max seen, Watermark, Progress, Output mode, Window"},{"id":"ch13-d05","title":"Duplicates after a failure","section":"ch13-s10","symptom":"After a crash/restart, the target table or an external system contains the same business record twice.","askYourself":["Is the sink a managed Delta sink (exactly-once) or a custom/foreachBatch sink?","Does foreachBatch perform a non-idempotent side effect (append without key, balance += x, emails, API calls)?","Does the source itself contain duplicate business events?","Was the checkpoint reset, deleted or changed?","Are two queries writing the same target?"],"steps":[{"do":"Identify the sink type and the foreachBatch logic","why":"Exactly-once stops at non-transactional edges"},{"do":"Count duplicates in the source by business key","why":"Processing guarantee ≠ business dedup","code":"SELECT event_id, COUNT(*) FROM ecommerce.bronze.events\nGROUP BY event_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"},{"do":"Check checkpoint history/location changes","why":"A new checkpoint = new query = reprocessing"},{"do":"Make writes idempotent (MERGE by key/version) and add dedup with a watermark","why":"Replay must give the same end state"}],"rootCauses":["non-idempotent foreachBatch side effect replayed","source duplicates","checkpoint reset","custom sink without transactions"],"fix":"Exactly-once is end-to-end only where every piece participates: use Delta sinks or idempotent MERGE by business key, dedup source duplicates with watermark-bounded dropDuplicates, never reset checkpoints casually.","mnemonic":"Sink → Side effect → Source → Checkpoint"},{"id":"ch13-d06","title":"State store is huge","section":"ch13-s05","symptom":"State memory/rows keep growing, checkpoints get large, batches slow, executors spill or OOM.","askYourself":["How many keys does the state hold — is key cardinality unbounded?","Is there a watermark, and how long is it?","How long are the windows?","How much late-data tolerance did we really need?","Do stream-stream joins have time bounds on both sides?","What is the dedup horizon?","Is one stateful key skewed (e.g. 'anonymous')?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read stateOperators metrics (numRowsTotal, memory) per batch","why":"Quantify growth"},{"do":"Review watermark, window duration, join bounds, dedup horizon","why":"Each bounds or unbounds state"},{"do":"Check per-partition state size","why":"Skewed key → one hot state partition"},{"do":"Consider RocksDB with changelog checkpointing","why":"State-store architecture for large state"}],"rootCauses":["semantically unbounded state","watermark missing/too long","long windows","unbounded joins/dedup","key skew"],"fix":"The fix isn't necessarily bigger compute — bound the state semantically (watermark, join bounds, dedup horizon, key design), then choose the right state store.","mnemonic":"Keys, Clock, Joins, Dedup, Skew"},{"id":"ch13-d07","title":"Delta-source stream fails after a long stop (missing files)","section":"ch13-s12","symptom":"A stream reading a Delta table restarts after weeks and fails with a missing-file / file-not-found style error.","askYourself":["How long was the stream stopped vs the source's retention?","Did VACUUM run on the source meanwhile (DESCRIBE HISTORY)?","Which source version does the checkpoint still need?","Am I tempted to set ignoreMissingFiles — do I accept silent wrong results? (No.)"],"steps":[{"do":"Compare the stop window with retention properties","why":"Explains whether needed files were vacuumed","code":"SHOW TBLPROPERTIES ecommerce.bronze.events;"},{"do":"Check DESCRIBE HISTORY for VACUUM operations","why":"Confirms the physical deletion","code":"DESCRIBE HISTORY ecommerce.bronze.events;"},{"do":"Recover/rebuild the stream appropriately (reset + full refresh/backfill)","why":"Incremental catch-up is impossible"},{"do":"Increase retention / guarantee a run within the window","why":"Prevent recurrence"}],"rootCauses":["stream stopped longer than source retention","VACUUM removed files the checkpoint needs"],"fix":"Never use spark.sql.files.ignoreMissingFiles=true as a workaround. Rebuild/backfill now; design retention and run frequency together (run at least once per retention window).","mnemonic":"Retain, don't ignore"},{"id":"ch13-d08","title":"Append output looks delayed / Gold table empty for recent windows","section":"ch13-s08","symptom":"Events arrive in Bronze instantly, but the windowed Gold table (append mode) shows nothing for the last N minutes.","askYourself":["Is this a stateful aggregation in append mode with a watermark?","How far is the current watermark behind the max event time?","Has the watermark passed the end of the windows I expect?","Are batches actually fast (no backlog)?","Do consumers really need finalized rows, or live-updating rows?"],"steps":[{"do":"Read eventTime.watermark from lastProgress","why":"Windows emit only after the watermark passes their end"},{"do":"Check batch durations and rates","why":"Rule out real lag"},{"do":"If live values are required, switch to foreachBatch + MERGE (update-like)","why":"Delta sink has no update mode"}],"rootCauses":["intended append-mode finalization delay (window + watermark)","actual backlog (less likely if batches are fast)"],"fix":"Explain the expected delay (≈ window length + watermark delay), or provide a live table via foreachBatch + MERGE while keeping the append table for final results.","mnemonic":"Append waits for FINAL"},{"id":"ch13-d09","title":"Stateful query won't restart after a code deployment","section":"ch13-s09","symptom":"After deploying a new version, the streaming query fails at startup on the existing checkpoint (or produces odd state).","askYourself":["Is this query stateful?","Did the change touch state keys, state schema, source type or number/type of stateful operators?","Did I test this restart against a copy of the checkpoint?","Can I afford a new checkpoint + backfill?"],"steps":[{"do":"Diff the old and new query plans for stateful operators","why":"Find the incompatible change"},{"do":"Consult the checkpoint compatibility rules","why":"Some stateless changes are fine; stateful ones usually aren't"},{"do":"If incompatible: new checkpoint + planned full recomputation/backfill","why":"State schema is effectively production data"}],"rootCauses":["changed state keys","changed state schema","changed source type","changed number/type of stateful operators"],"fix":"Roll back, or deliberately start a new checkpoint with a planned backfill. Never hand-edit checkpoint folders.","mnemonic":"Same checkpoint ≠ seamless"},{"id":"ch13-d10","title":"Micro-batches overrun the trigger because of skew","section":"ch13-s14","symptom":"Trigger 5 s, yet every micro-batch takes 30+ s; one task is far slower than the rest; backlog grows.","askYourself":["Is the max task duration far above the median?","Does the slow task have the biggest shuffle read / spill?","Is it a stateful groupBy on a hot key (e.g. user_id = 'anonymous')?","Would more workers help — or is it one partition?"],"steps":[{"do":"Spark UI: compare median vs max task in the slow stage","why":"Skew signal"},{"do":"Find the hot key in the input","why":"Explains the hot state partition","code":"events.groupBy('user_id').count().orderBy('count', ascending=False).limit(10)"},{"do":"Redesign the key / handle the hot key separately","why":"Streaming key design matters"}],"rootCauses":["key skew in a shuffle or stateful operator","too few partitions"],"fix":"Fix the data distribution (key design, separate hot-key handling, partitioning) rather than only adding workers.","mnemonic":"Median vs max"},{"id":"ch13-d11","title":"Delta-source stream fails after UPDATE/DELETE on the source","section":"ch13-s12","symptom":"A stream reading a Delta table fails after someone ran UPDATE, DELETE or MERGE on that source table.","askYourself":["Is the source table append-only in my design, or does it receive row-level changes?","Do downstream consumers need those changes propagated?","Should I read the Change Data Feed instead?","If I skip change commits, am I OK with changes NOT being propagated?"],"steps":[{"do":"Check DESCRIBE HISTORY on the source for non-append operations","why":"Find the offending commits"},{"do":"Decide semantics: propagate (readChangeFeed) or ignore (skipChangeCommits)","why":"A simple Delta source is append-oriented"}],"rootCauses":["non-append commits on a Delta source read as a plain append stream"],"fix":"Use Change Data Feed to propagate row-level changes, or skipChangeCommits deliberately if they must be ignored; keep Bronze append-only where possible.","mnemonic":"Append in, CDF for changes"}],"pitfalls":[{"title":"read instead of readStream","text":"`spark.read` builds a bounded batch DataFrame; calling `.writeStream` on it fails.","fix":"Use `spark.readStream` for streaming sources."},{"title":"Expecting readStream to start processing","text":"readStream only defines a plan; nothing runs until a sink is started.","fix":"Start the query with `writeStream ... toTable()/start()`."},{"title":"Trigger interval ≠ completion guarantee","text":"A 5-second trigger with 12-second batches builds a backlog; batches don't run in parallel.","fix":"Treat sustained overrun as capacity problem; fix capacity/query/skew."},{"title":"Using Trigger.Once","text":"Once is deprecated.","fix":"Use `trigger(availableNow=True)`."},{"title":"processingTime on serverless","text":"Serverless notebooks/jobs don't support time-based or real-time triggers.","fix":"Use AvailableNow on a schedule, or a Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode for always-on."},{"title":"Real-time mode for ordinary ETL","text":"Ultra-low latency adds cost/complexity most lakehouse ETL doesn't need.","fix":"Default to micro-batch or AvailableNow."},{"title":"Unbounded state","text":"Stateful queries without a time bound (aggregation, dedup, joins) keep keys forever → memory, checkpoint size, slow batches, OOM.","fix":"Add watermarks, window/time bounds, dedup horizon; review key cardinality."},{"title":"Arrival time ≠ event time","text":"Windowing or watermarking on processing/ingest time puts late events in the wrong window or drops them.","fix":"Window and watermark on the event-time column."},{"title":"Watermark as a wall-clock timer","text":"Lateness is relative to max observed event time, not to now − event_time.","fix":"Reason: watermark ≈ max event time − delay."},{"title":"Watermark too short","text":"Valid late records beyond the tolerance may be dropped.","fix":"Size it from measured real lateness; accept the state/latency cost."},{"title":"Watermark as retention policy","text":"A watermark never deletes table rows; it only affects streaming state and late data.","fix":"Use DELETE/retention/VACUUM for data lifecycle."},{"title":"Watermark after the aggregation","text":"Defined after groupBy, it can't bound the aggregation's state.","fix":"Call withWatermark before groupBy, on the window's time column."},{"title":"Update mode into a Delta sink","text":"The Delta sink supports append and complete only.","fix":"Use foreachBatch + MERGE for update-like results."},{"title":"Complete mode on high-cardinality aggregates","text":"Complete mode doesn't evict state via watermark and rewrites everything.","fix":"Prefer append/update with watermark, or MERGE."},{"title":"Append output 'delayed'","text":"Append emits windows only once final (window end + watermark).","fix":"Explain expected delay or provide a live MERGE table."},{"title":"Deleting or changing the checkpoint","text":"The query restarts as new: reprocessing, duplicate side effects, lost state.","fix":"Keep one stable checkpoint per query; resets only deliberately (sandbox / planned backfill)."},{"title":"Assuming new code reuses the checkpoint","text":"Changing state keys/schema/source/stateful operators can be checkpoint-incompatible.","fix":"Check compatibility rules and test stateful deployments."},{"title":"Exactly-once ≠ no duplicates","text":"Source duplicates pass through; non-idempotent foreachBatch side effects replay.","fix":"Dedup business keys; make side effects idempotent."},{"title":"Non-idempotent foreachBatch","text":"Retries can call the function twice (balance += x, emails).","fix":"MERGE by business key/version or transactional sink semantics."},{"title":"Unbounded dropDuplicates","text":"dropDuplicates without a watermark remembers every key forever.","fix":"Watermark + include event_time, or dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark."},{"title":"Stream-stream join without watermarks","text":"Join state grows without bound; outer joins are rejected.","fix":"Watermarks on both inputs + time-range join condition."},{"title":"Using max multiple-watermark policy casually","text":"Faster progress but records of the slower stream may be dropped.","fix":"Keep the default min unless you accept the loss."},{"title":"Expecting stream-static joins to recompute history","text":"A changed dimension doesn't rewrite already-written outputs.","fix":"Use materialized/declarative patterns if recomputation is required."},{"title":"Stopping a Delta-source stream longer than retention","text":"Vacuumed files make incremental catch-up impossible.","fix":"Run at least once per retention window; increase retention."},{"title":"ignoreMissingFiles as a fix","text":"Hides missing data → silent incorrect results.","fix":"Increase retention or recover/rebuild the stream."},{"title":"UPDATE/DELETE on a Delta streaming source","text":"A plain Delta source is append-oriented; change commits break or get skipped.","fix":"Use Change Data Feed or skipChangeCommits deliberately."},{"title":"Tiny triggers → small files","text":"Every micro-batch writes small files and commits.","fix":"Choose a sensible latency; rely on OPTIMIZE/predictive optimization."},{"title":"Thinking a checkpoint adds throughput","text":"Checkpoint preserves progress; it doesn't create capacity.","fix":"Add capacity, cheaper query, less state, less skew."},{"title":"Skewed stateful key","text":"One hot key (e.g. 'anonymous') creates a hot state partition that slows every batch.","fix":"Redesign keys / handle hot keys separately."},{"title":"Checkpoint = 'last timestamp'","text":"A checkpoint stores offsets, commits, state and query metadata — not just a timestamp.","fix":"Treat it as the query's identity and durable memory."},{"title":"AvailableNow = run forever","text":"AvailableNow processes the currently available backlog incrementally and then stops.","fix":"Schedule it (e.g. hourly) for incremental batch; use continuous modes for always-on."},{"title":"Restarting blindly","text":"Restarts (especially with new checkpoints) destroy evidence and can duplicate data.","fix":"Inspect status/lastProgress first."}],"flashcards":[{"q":"What does Structured Streaming treat a stream as?","a":"An incrementally growing (unbounded) table processed by the same Spark engine.","section":"ch13-s01"},{"q":"Bounded vs unbounded dataset?","a":"Bounded: known end, process once and stop. Unbounded: keeps growing, processed incrementally.","section":"ch13-s01"},{"q":"Why can streaming reuse batch DataFrame syntax?","a":"Same DataFrame/query-planning model, executed incrementally over unbounded input.","section":"ch13-s01"},{"q":"read vs readStream?","a":"read = bounded batch read; readStream = streaming source with progress tracking.","section":"ch13-s02"},{"q":"Does readStream start processing?","a":"No — lazy plan only. writeStream ... toTable()/start() starts the query.","section":"ch13-s02"},{"q":"Three parts of every stream?","a":"Source → transformation → sink.","section":"ch13-s02"},{"q":"query.lastProgress vs query.status?","a":"lastProgress = metrics of the last micro-batch; status = what it's doing now.","section":"ch13-s02"},{"q":"What is a micro-batch?","a":"A bounded chunk of newly available data executed as a Spark job.","section":"ch13-s03"},{"q":"processingTime trigger meaning?","a":"How often Spark attempts a new micro-batch — not a completion guarantee.","section":"ch13-s03"},{"q":"Trigger 5 s, batch takes 12 s?","a":"Batches run sequentially → backlog builds, latency grows (ingest > capacity).","section":"ch13-s03"},{"q":"Default trigger?","a":"processingTime = 0: next micro-batch as soon as the previous finishes.","section":"ch13-s04"},{"q":"AvailableNow?","a":"Process all currently available unprocessed input incrementally, then stop.","section":"ch13-s04"},{"q":"Once vs AvailableNow?","a":"Once is deprecated; AvailableNow replaces it (can use several batches).","section":"ch13-s04"},{"q":"Serverless notebooks/jobs triggers?","a":"AvailableNow ✅, Once ✅ (deprecated), ProcessingTime ❌, RealTime ❌.","section":"ch13-s04"},{"q":"Always-on streaming on serverless?","a":"Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode.","section":"ch13-s04"},{"q":"Real-time mode?","a":"Classic-only ultra-low-latency (sub-second) operational streaming; not the default for ETL.","section":"ch13-s04"},{"q":"Stateless query definition?","a":"Keeps no intermediate state beyond source→sink progress tracking.","section":"ch13-s05"},{"q":"Stateful operators?","a":"Aggregations, distinct/dropDuplicates, stream-stream joins, custom stateful ops.","section":"ch13-s05"},{"q":"What is state?","a":"Info from previous micro-batches needed to process the next ones — the working memory.","section":"ch13-s05"},{"q":"Recommended state store for demanding workloads?","a":"RocksDB with changelog checkpointing.","section":"ch13-s05"},{"q":"Event time vs processing time?","a":"Event time = when it happened (data column). Processing time = when the engine processed it.","section":"ch13-s06"},{"q":"Why can events arrive late?","a":"Offline mobile, network delay, Kafka backlog, outage, retry, batch upload, clock skew.","section":"ch13-s06"},{"q":"Which window does event_time 10:03 land in (10-min tumbling)?","a":"[10:00–10:10), regardless of arrival time.","section":"ch13-s06"},{"q":"Tumbling vs sliding window?","a":"Tumbling: non-overlapping, 1 window per event. Sliding: overlapping, window/slide windows per event.","section":"ch13-s06"},{"q":"Watermark definition?","a":"Event-time threshold that lets Spark drop old state and control late data.","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"How is the watermark computed?","a":"≈ max observed event time − delay (at least the delay behind; not exact).","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"Max event 12:00, delay 10 min → watermark?","a":"≈ 11:50.","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"Short watermark pros/cons?","a":"Less state, lower latency; but less tolerance for late data.","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"Long watermark pros/cons?","a":"More late data accepted; more state, memory, checkpoint size, latency.","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"Is a watermark a retention policy?","a":"No — it never deletes table rows; only bounds state/late data.","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"Is 'late' = now − event_time?","a":"No — late is relative to event-time watermark progress.","section":"ch13-s07"},{"q":"Append / Update / Complete?","a":"Final rows / changed rows / entire result each trigger.","section":"ch13-s08"},{"q":"Output modes of the Delta sink?","a":"Append and complete — not update (use foreachBatch + MERGE).","section":"ch13-s08"},{"q":"Complete mode and watermark eviction?","a":"Complete mode doesn't evict aggregation state via watermark.","section":"ch13-s08"},{"q":"Why does append output look delayed?","a":"Stateful aggregate + append + watermark → waits until the window is final.","section":"ch13-s08"},{"q":"What's in a checkpoint?","a":"offsets, commits, state (stateful), metadata — not just a last timestamp.","section":"ch13-s09"},{"q":"Commit log purpose?","a":"Records which micro-batches committed to the sink — key to exactly-once.","section":"ch13-s09"},{"q":"Deleting the checkpoint?","a":"Next run is a new query: reprocessing, duplicate side effects, lost state.","section":"ch13-s09"},{"q":"Checkpoint-incompatible changes?","a":"State keys, state schema, source type, number/type of stateful operators.","section":"ch13-s09"},{"q":"Exactly-once trio for Delta sinks?","a":"Source offsets + checkpoint commits + Delta transaction log.","section":"ch13-s10"},{"q":"Where does exactly-once stop?","a":"At the edges: custom/non-transactional sinks and external side effects.","section":"ch13-s10"},{"q":"Does exactly-once remove source duplicates?","a":"No — processing guarantee ≠ business deduplication.","section":"ch13-s10"},{"q":"Bounded streaming dedup?","a":"withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"1 hour\").dropDuplicates([\"event_id\", \"event_time\"]).","section":"ch13-s10"},{"q":"Why do stream-stream joins need state?","a":"A record may wait for a later match from the other stream.","section":"ch13-s11"},{"q":"Outer stream-stream join requirement?","a":"Watermark (+ time constraint) — to know when unmatched rows are final.","section":"ch13-s11"},{"q":"Default multiple-watermark policy?","a":"min — the slowest input decides global progress (safer).","section":"ch13-s11"},{"q":"Stream-static join caveat?","a":"Dimension changes don't recompute already-written outputs.","section":"ch13-s11"},{"q":"Delta source with UPDATE/DELETE?","a":"Append-oriented source; use Change Data Feed (or skipChangeCommits deliberately).","section":"ch13-s12"},{"q":"Stream stopped beyond source retention?","a":"Needed files may be vacuumed → can't catch up; reset/full refresh needed.","section":"ch13-s12"},{"q":"ignoreMissingFiles=true as a fix?","a":"Never — can silently produce incorrect results.","section":"ch13-s12"},{"q":"foreachBatch signature?","a":"func(batch_df, batch_id) — each micro-batch as a batch DataFrame.","section":"ch13-s13"},{"q":"Classic streaming upsert?","a":"foreachBatch + DeltaTable MERGE (whenMatchedUpdateAll / whenNotMatchedInsertAll).","section":"ch13-s13"},{"q":"Main foreachBatch responsibility?","a":"Sink-side idempotency — a retried batch may run again.","section":"ch13-s13"},{"q":"Is batch_id a universal dedup key?","a":"No — it can help detect replays in some sinks; design depends on the target.","section":"ch13-s13"},{"q":"Small vs large micro-batches?","a":"Small: low latency, more overhead, small files. Large: throughput, higher latency.","section":"ch13-s14"},{"q":"50 MB/s in, 20 MB/s processed?","a":"+30 MB/s backlog; no checkpoint can fix it.","section":"ch13-s14"},{"q":"Signs of a hot stateful key?","a":"One slow task, one huge state partition, spill, increasing latency.","section":"ch13-s14"},{"q":"Input vs processed rate check?","a":"Sustained inputRowsPerSecond > processedRowsPerSecond → backlog grows.","section":"ch13-s15"},{"q":"Debugging tree branches?","a":"Spark (shuffle/skew/spill/join), State (growth/watermark/state store), Source/Sink (throughput/API latency/storage).","section":"ch13-s15"},{"q":"Batch durations grow, input stable — first suspect?","a":"State growth → watermark/state design.","section":"ch13-s15"},{"q":"Rate source columns?","a":"timestamp and value (option rowsPerSecond).","section":"ch13-s16"},{"q":"Best-practice design questions?","a":"Source, event time, late data, state, watermark, trigger, sink, checkpoint, retention — designed together.","section":"ch13-s16"}],"exercises":[{"id":"ch13-e001","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q1: Why can Spark Structured Streaming reuse batch-like DataFrame syntax?","options":["Because it converts streams into files first and runs a nightly batch","Because it uses Spark's DataFrame/query-planning execution model incrementally over unbounded input","Because streaming DataFrames are just pandas DataFrames refreshed every second","Because it only supports SQL, and SQL is the same everywhere"],"answer":1,"why":["No — it processes incrementally as data becomes available, not as a nightly batch.","Correct — same planner and engine, incremental execution.","No — Spark DataFrames are distributed; pandas has nothing to do with it.","No — the DataFrame API (Python) is reused, not only SQL."],"explain":"Structured Streaming is the same Spark engine: the same planner turns your DataFrame code into jobs, applied **incrementally** to new data. It does not secretly batch files nightly, and it is not pandas."},{"id":"ch13-e002","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Structured Streaming is a separate execution engine from batch Spark, with its own notion of jobs and tasks.","answer":false,"explain":"It is the **same** Spark execution engine. A micro-batch is a normal Spark job with stages, tasks and partitions; only the input is treated as unbounded and processed incrementally."},{"id":"ch13-e003","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Bounded or unbounded input?","buckets":["Bounded (batch)","Unbounded (streaming)"],"items":[{"text":"Yesterday's orders.csv export","bucket":0},{"text":"A Kafka topic of clicks","bucket":1},{"text":"A one-off 2 TB historical backfill","bucket":0},{"text":"JSON files landing in a Volume forever","bucket":1},{"text":"Rows appended continuously to a Bronze Delta table","bucket":1},{"text":"A fixed reference CSV of country codes","bucket":0}],"explain":"Bounded = known end, process once and stop. Unbounded = logically keeps growing, the query must keep (or repeatedly) process new data."},{"id":"ch13-e004","type":"order","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Put the Phase 5 mental model in order (top to bottom).","items":["streaming source","readStream","incremental DataFrame plan","trigger","micro-batch / real-time execution","stateless or stateful operators","writeStream","sink"],"explain":"Source → readStream defines it → a plan is built → the trigger decides when → micro-batch executes → operators (stateless/stateful) → writeStream → sink, with the checkpoint remembering progress + state alongside."},{"id":"ch13-e005","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the unbounded-table idea.","text":"Structured Streaming treats a stream as a table that keeps [[growing|growing forever]]: each new event is a new [[row]]. The engine runs your query [[incrementally]] over the new rows while the [[checkpoint]] remembers progress and state.","bank":["shrinking","column","once","cluster"],"explain":"The unbounded-table model is the core of Structured Streaming: same query, incremental execution, checkpointed progress."},{"id":"ch13-e006","type":"odd","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which item does NOT belong to the streaming mental model chain?","options":["readStream","trigger","checkpoint","VACUUM"],"answer":3,"explain":"readStream, trigger and checkpoint are all parts of a streaming query. **VACUUM** is Delta table file garbage collection — a storage maintenance command, not part of the streaming pipeline (though it can break a stopped stream, section 12)."},{"id":"ch13-e007","type":"free","section":"ch13-s01","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Explain in 3–4 sentences the difference between a bounded and an unbounded dataset, and why the second needs a checkpoint.","model":"A bounded dataset has a known end: you read it all, compute and stop. An unbounded dataset logically never ends — new records keep arriving — so the query keeps running or re-running on new data. Because it runs over and over, the engine must durably remember which input it already processed (and any intermediate state), otherwise a restart would reprocess or skip data. That memory is the checkpoint.","rubric":["bounded = known end, process once","unbounded = keeps growing","processed repeatedly/incrementally","checkpoint remembers progress (and state) across restarts"],"explain":"This is the first-principles core: unboundedness is what forces progress tracking."},{"id":"ch13-e008","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q3: `spark.readStream...load(path)` immediately starts processing files.","answer":false,"explain":"No. readStream only defines a streaming source / logical plan (lazy evaluation). Execution starts when a streaming sink is started with `writeStream ... toTable()` or `.start()`."},{"id":"ch13-e009","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Certification trap: what is the difference between `spark.read` and `spark.readStream`?","options":["None — readStream is just a faster read","read = bounded batch read; readStream = streaming source tracked incrementally","read works only on Delta; readStream only on Kafka","readStream reads the whole table every second"],"answer":1,"why":["They have different execution models, not different speeds.","Correct.","Both work with many formats (json, Delta, etc.).","No — it processes only new data incrementally."],"explain":"`spark.read` reads what exists now (bounded). `spark.readStream` treats the source as continuously evolving and tracks progress. Simple but frequently tested."},{"id":"ch13-e010","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"This cell fails with an error about `writeStream`. Which line is the bug?","lines":["events = (","    spark.read","        .format(\"json\")","        .schema(schema)","        .load(path)",")","events.writeStream.option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt).toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"events = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"json\")\n        .schema(schema)\n        .load(path)\n)","explain":"`spark.read` creates a **batch** DataFrame; `.writeStream` can only be called on a streaming DataFrame. Use `spark.readStream`."},{"id":"ch13-e011","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the streaming read.","text":"df = (\n    spark.[[readStream]]\n        .format(\"json\")\n        .[[schema]](schema)\n        .[[load]](path)\n)","bank":["read","start","table","option"],"asCode":true,"explain":"Streaming file sources take an explicit `.schema(...)` — then `.load(path)` builds the streaming DataFrame (still lazy)."},{"id":"ch13-e012","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the sink that STARTS the query.","text":"query = (\n    events.[[writeStream]]\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .option(\"[[checkpointLocation]]\", checkpoint)\n        .[[toTable]](\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n)","asCode":true,"explain":"`writeStream` defines the sink, `checkpointLocation` gives the query its durable memory, `toTable(...)` starts the query writing to a table."},{"id":"ch13-e013","type":"match","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each piece to its role.","pairs":[["readStream","define the source"],["filter/select/groupBy","define the computation"],["writeStream","define the sink"],["toTable() / start()","start the streaming query"],["checkpointLocation","durable progress + state"]],"explain":"Only the start call triggers execution; everything before it is plan-building."},{"id":"ch13-e014","type":"write","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a stream that reads the Delta table `ecommerce.bronze.events`, keeps rows where `event_type IS NOT NULL`, and writes to `ecommerce.silver.events` with checkpoint variable `checkpoint`.","solution":"events = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\nclean = events.filter(\"event_type IS NOT NULL\")\nquery = (\n    clean.writeStream\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")\n)","keywords":["readStream",".table(","filter","writeStream","checkpointLocation","toTable"],"lang":"python","explain":"Source → transformation → sink. Delta-to-Delta streaming needs only `readStream.table`, a transformation and `writeStream...toTable` with a checkpoint."},{"id":"ch13-e015","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Which StreamingQuery call blocks until the query stops — typical at the end of a job script?","options":["query.lastProgress","query.status","query.awaitTermination()","query.recentProgress"],"answer":2,"explain":"`awaitTermination()` blocks; `status` and `lastProgress`/`recentProgress` only report what's happening."},{"id":"ch13-e016","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s02","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"In production, a `writeStream` to a table should always set a durable `checkpointLocation`.","answer":true,"explain":"Without a durable checkpoint the query can't remember offsets, commits and state across restarts — it would behave as a new query each time."},{"id":"ch13-e017","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q2: What is a micro-batch?","options":["A single event processed by its own Spark job","A bounded chunk of newly available stream data executed as a Spark job","A small cluster dedicated to streaming","A Delta file smaller than 128 MB"],"answer":1,"why":["That's the misconception micro-batching avoids.","Correct.","It's a unit of work, not compute.","Unrelated to file sizes."],"explain":"Micro-batch = bounded chunk of new data → one Spark job (stages, tasks, partitions). Spark does not launch a job per event."},{"id":"ch13-e018","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Inside a micro-batch, concepts like shuffle, skew, spill and partitions no longer apply because streaming has its own engine.","answer":false,"explain":"Each micro-batch is a normal Spark job: job → stages → tasks → partitions. Shuffle, joins, spill and skew all still apply (Chapter 9)."},{"id":"ch13-e019","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Trigger = every 5 seconds, but each micro-batch needs 12 seconds. Batches run sequentially. How many micro-batches COMPLETE in the first 60 seconds?","answer":5,"tolerance":0,"unit":"batches","explain":"Batches don't overlap: they finish at 12, 24, 36, 48, 60 s → **5** batches. Twelve were 'scheduled' by the 5-s trigger, so a backlog builds and latency grows."},{"id":"ch13-e020","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Trigger every 5 s, a micro-batch takes 12 s. What does Spark do in the normal micro-batch model?","options":["Runs 3 batches in parallel to catch up","Skips the data of the missed triggers","Starts the next batch after the current one finishes; backlog and latency grow","Fails the query with a trigger timeout"],"answer":2,"explain":"Micro-batches are sequential. No data is skipped and no parallel batches run — the query just falls behind: processing backlog + growing latency."},{"id":"ch13-e021","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the 5-second trigger.","text":"query = (\n    events.writeStream\n        .format(\"delta\")\n        .outputMode(\"append\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .trigger([[processingTime]]=\"[[5 seconds]]\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n)","asCode":true,"explain":"`processingTime` sets how often Spark tries to start a new micro-batch."},{"id":"ch13-e022","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A processingTime trigger of 5 seconds guarantees every micro-batch completes within 5 seconds.","answer":false,"explain":"It only schedules **attempts**. If a batch takes longer, the next one starts late — the trigger can't create capacity."},{"id":"ch13-e023","type":"order","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Arrange the execution hierarchy of one micro-batch.","items":["new input","micro-batch N","Spark job","stages","tasks","partitions"],"explain":"Each micro-batch becomes a Spark job, split into stages, executed by tasks — one task per partition."},{"id":"ch13-e024","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s03","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Batches consistently take longer than the trigger interval. What is this production signal?","options":["The checkpoint is corrupted","Ingestion rate > processing capacity","The watermark is too short","The sink is in update mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sustained overrun = the pipeline can't process data as fast as it arrives. Fix with capacity, a cheaper query, less state or less skew — not with the trigger."},{"id":"ch13-e025","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q4: What is AvailableNow?","options":["Run continuously with the lowest latency","Process all currently available unprocessed input incrementally, then stop","Reprocess the entire source from the beginning, then stop","Process exactly one row, then stop"],"answer":1,"why":["That's processingTime/real-time, not AvailableNow.","Correct.","No — only unprocessed input; the checkpoint remembers the rest.","No such semantics."],"explain":"AvailableNow = catch up on everything available now (possibly in several batches), then stop. The checkpoint makes the next run start where this one ended."},{"id":"ch13-e026","type":"free","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q5: Why is AvailableNow useful for Data Engineering?","model":"Scheduled incremental jobs get checkpointed streaming semantics — incremental source tracking, checkpointing, state management, fault recovery — without an always-on process. You schedule it (e.g. hourly), it processes everything new since the last run and stops, so you pay only while it runs and never hand-write last_processed_timestamp logic.","rubric":["scheduled incremental processing","checkpoint tracks progress","no always-on cluster / cost","no manual watermark/timestamp tracking"],"explain":"This is why Databricks recommends AvailableNow especially on serverless jobs, and why Auto Loader jobs commonly use it."},{"id":"ch13-e027","type":"match","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Match the trigger to its behaviour.","pairs":[["no .trigger(...)","next micro-batch as soon as the previous finishes"],["processingTime=\"10 seconds\"","fixed-interval micro-batches"],["availableNow=True","process the available backlog, then stop"],["once=True","deprecated single-batch run"],["real-time mode","sub-second operational latency on supported classic"]],"explain":"Default = processingTime 0. Once was deprecated in favour of AvailableNow."},{"id":"ch13-e028","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Databricks recommends `Trigger.Once` over `Trigger.AvailableNow` for new scheduled jobs.","answer":false,"explain":"Reverse: Once is **deprecated** in favour of AvailableNow, which can split a large backlog into multiple batches and still stops at the end."},{"id":"ch13-e029","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"On serverless notebooks/jobs, is this trigger supported?","buckets":["Supported","Not supported"],"items":[{"text":"availableNow=True","bucket":0},{"text":"once=True (deprecated)","bucket":0},{"text":"processingTime=\"1 minute\"","bucket":1},{"text":"real-time mode","bucket":1}],"explain":"Serverless: AvailableNow ✅, Once ✅ (deprecated), ProcessingTime ❌, RealTime ❌. Always-on serverless streaming → Lakeflow pipeline continuous mode."},{"id":"ch13-e030","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Certification trap: you need an ALWAYS-RUNNING stream on serverless. What's the preferred architecture?","options":["Serverless job with .trigger(processingTime=\"10 seconds\")","Serverless job with .trigger(availableNow=True) in a while-true loop","A Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode","Real-time mode on a serverless SQL warehouse"],"answer":2,"why":["processingTime isn't supported on serverless notebooks/jobs.","A hack; AvailableNow is meant for scheduled runs.","Correct.","Real-time mode is for supported classic environments, not SQL warehouses."],"explain":"Time-based triggers aren't supported on serverless notebooks/jobs, and real-time mode is classic-only. For continuously running serverless workloads Databricks directs you to Lakeflow pipelines' continuous mode (Phase 6)."},{"id":"ch13-e031","type":"write","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the hourly incremental job: trigger that processes what's available then stops, checkpoint `checkpoint`, target `ecommerce.bronze.events`.","solution":"query = (\n    events.writeStream\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", checkpoint)\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\n)","keywords":["writeStream","availableNow=True","checkpointLocation","toTable"],"lang":"python","explain":"Schedule this hourly: run 1 processes A B C, run 2 processes only D E because the checkpoint remembers A B C."},{"id":"ch13-e032","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"An hourly AvailableNow job ran at 10:00 and processed files A, B, C. At 11:00 it runs again; only D and E are new.","steps":[{"prompt":"What does the 11:00 run process?","options":[{"text":"Only D and E, then stops","ok":true,"fb":"Right — the checkpoint knows A B C were processed."},{"text":"A, B, C, D, E again","ok":false,"fb":"Only if the checkpoint were deleted or changed."},{"text":"Nothing until a new trigger interval passes","ok":false,"fb":"AvailableNow doesn't wait for intervals; it processes the available backlog."}]},{"prompt":"At 12:00 nothing new has arrived. What happens?","options":[{"text":"It finds no new offsets and stops quickly","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — nothing new to process."},{"text":"It waits forever for new data","ok":false,"fb":"That's the default/processingTime behaviour, not AvailableNow."},{"text":"It fails because there is no data","ok":false,"fb":"An empty backlog is a normal outcome."}]}],"explain":"AvailableNow + checkpoint = incremental batch semantics without hand-written progress tracking."},{"id":"ch13-e033","type":"odd","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT something AvailableNow gives you for free compared with a hand-written `WHERE event_ts > last_ts` job?","options":["incremental source tracking","fault recovery via checkpoint","business-level deduplication of duplicate events","state management"],"answer":2,"explain":"The streaming engine tracks progress, recovers and manages state. It does **not** deduplicate business duplicates — that remains your job."},{"id":"ch13-e034","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s04","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Real-time mode should be the default choice for ordinary lakehouse ETL because lower latency is always better.","answer":false,"explain":"Real-time mode targets ultra-low-latency operational workloads. For normal lakehouse ETL, micro-batch or AvailableNow is simpler, cheaper and easier to operate."},{"id":"ch13-e035","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q6: Stateless vs stateful?","options":["Stateless never writes to a sink; stateful does","Stateless processing doesn't need historical intermediate state; stateful does","Stateless uses Python; stateful uses SQL","Stateless runs on serverless; stateful only on classic"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stateless keeps only progress tracking; stateful keeps intermediate state (counts, open windows, join buffers, dedup keys) across micro-batches."},{"id":"ch13-e036","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q7: In a streaming query, `groupBy(...).count()` is stateful.","answer":true,"explain":"Q8 — why: the aggregate result must accumulate information across micro-batches (customer 42: 5, then 8), so Spark keeps state."},{"id":"ch13-e037","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Stateless or stateful in Structured Streaming?","buckets":["Stateless","Stateful"],"items":[{"text":"filter(\"amount > 0\")","bucket":0},{"text":"select(\"event_id\", \"amount\")","bucket":0},{"text":"withColumn(\"group_id\", col(\"value\") % 5)","bucket":0},{"text":"groupBy(\"customer_id\").count()","bucket":1},{"text":"dropDuplicates([\"event_id\"])","bucket":1},{"text":"distinct()","bucket":1},{"text":"stream-stream join","bucket":1},{"text":"stream-static join (enrichment)","bucket":0}],"explain":"Aggregations, distinct/dropDuplicates, stream-stream joins and custom stateful operations keep state. A stream-static join doesn't buffer the stream side."},{"id":"ch13-e038","type":"free","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q8: Why is a streaming groupBy stateful? Use the customer-42 example.","model":"Because the result must accumulate across micro-batches. If batch 1 had 5 events for customer 42 and batch 2 brings 3 more, Spark can only output 8 if it remembered the 5 from batch 1. That remembered running count is state, kept in the distributed state store and checkpointed.","rubric":["accumulate across micro-batches","must remember previous count","5 + 3 = 8 example","stored in state store / checkpoint"],"explain":"State = information from previous micro-batches needed to process the next ones correctly."},{"id":"ch13-e039","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"`groupBy(\"customer_id\").count()` with no watermark; 1 million NEW customers appear every day and none is ever forgotten. How many million keys are in state after 30 days?","answer":30,"tolerance":0,"unit":"million keys","explain":"1M × 30 days = **30M** keys (365M after a year). State grows forever → more memory, bigger checkpoints, slower lookups, slower batches, potential OOM."},{"id":"ch13-e040","type":"order","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Unbounded state: order the consequences as they snowball.","items":["state keeps growing","more memory used","larger checkpoints","slower state lookups","slower batches","potential OOM"],"explain":"Growing state first costs memory and checkpoint size, then slows every lookup and batch, and finally can OOM."},{"id":"ch13-e041","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q34: Which state-store technology does Databricks currently recommend for demanding stateful workloads?","options":["An in-driver Python dictionary","RocksDB with changelog checkpointing","A Delta table rewritten every batch","Photon's vectorized cache"],"answer":1,"why":["State is distributed, not a driver dict.","Correct.","Not how state is stored.","Photon is an execution engine, not a state store."],"explain":"RocksDB state store + changelog checkpointing scales state and reduces state-checkpoint overhead/latency. State is distributed across executors — never a driver dict."},{"id":"ch13-e042","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"When state becomes the bottleneck, scaling up compute is always the right fix.","answer":false,"explain":"Not necessarily. The state design (watermark, key cardinality, join bounds) and the state-store architecture (RocksDB + changelog checkpointing) may matter more than node size."},{"id":"ch13-e043","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the definition.","text":"State is information from previous [[micro-batches|batches]] that is needed to correctly process the [[next|following]] ones. It is the [[working memory]] of the computation, not the final output.","bank":["files","previous","final output"],"explain":"That's why state must be checkpointed — losing it means wrong (restarted-from-zero) aggregates."},{"id":"ch13-e044","type":"odd","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Three of these are stateful operators. Which one is the odd one out?","options":["dropDuplicates","stream-stream join","windowed aggregation","select with a computed column"],"answer":3,"explain":"A projection with a computed column is stateless: each row is handled alone."},{"id":"ch13-e045","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"The state of `groupBy(\"session_id\").count()` grows by millions of rows every day and batches keep slowing down. There is no watermark.","steps":[{"prompt":"Why is state growing forever?","options":[{"text":"New session_ids keep appearing and nothing tells Spark it can forget old ones","ok":true,"fb":"Unbounded key cardinality with no time bound."},{"text":"The checkpoint is duplicated","ok":false,"fb":"No evidence of that."},{"text":"Append mode stores every row twice","ok":false,"fb":"Not how output modes work."}]},{"prompt":"What is the most effective fix?","options":[{"text":"Add an event-time window + watermark so old sessions' state can be evicted","ok":true,"fb":"Bound the state semantically."},{"text":"Double the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Buys time; state keeps growing."},{"text":"Use complete mode","ok":false,"fb":"Complete mode never evicts state."}]}],"explain":"State store huge: maybe the state is semantically unbounded — fix the semantics before the hardware."},{"id":"ch13-e046","type":"order","section":"ch13-s05","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"State store huge: order the checks (Keys, Clock, Joins, Dedup, Skew).","items":["number of keys","watermark / window duration / late tolerance","join time bounds","dedup horizon","stateful key skew"],"explain":"Each item is a reason state can't be evicted or is concentrated."},{"id":"ch13-e047","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q9: What is event time?","options":["When Databricks processed the record","When the event occurred, according to the event data","When the file was written to storage","The trigger interval"],"answer":1,"explain":"Event time is a column carried by the data (event_time). Processing time (Q10) is when the engine observes/processes the record."},{"id":"ch13-e048","type":"match","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q10 & certification trap: match each time concept.","pairs":[["Event time","when the business event happened"],["Processing time","when the engine saw/processed it"],["Watermark","event-time threshold for late data"],["Trigger","how often a micro-batch is attempted"]],"explain":"Watermarks are based on **event time**, triggers on **processing time** (wall clock)."},{"id":"ch13-e049","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Test Q11: which of these can make event time EARLIER than processing time?","buckets":["Can cause late arrival","Not a cause"],"items":[{"text":"mobile app offline","bucket":0},{"text":"network delay","bucket":0},{"text":"Kafka backlog","bucket":0},{"text":"retries","bucket":0},{"text":"batch upload of a day's events","bucket":0},{"text":"clock skew","bucket":0},{"text":"choosing append output mode","bucket":1},{"text":"using a Delta sink","bucket":1}],"explain":"Network delays, offline clients, queues/backlogs, outages, retries, batch uploads and clock skew all delay arrival. Output mode and sink type don't change when the event happened."},{"id":"ch13-e050","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"10-minute tumbling windows. An event has event_time 10:03 and arrives at 10:11. Which window START does it belong to? (answer as HHMM, e.g. 1000)","answer":1000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"HHMM","explain":"Windows are assigned by **event time**: 10:03 → [10:00–10:10), start 10:00. Arrival at 10:11 is irrelevant to the window choice."},{"id":"ch13-e051","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"5-minute tumbling windows. Event_time 10:47:30. Window START? (HHMM)","answer":1045,"tolerance":0,"unit":"HHMM","explain":"5-minute windows start at :00, :05, … :45, :50. 10:47:30 is in [10:45–10:50)."},{"id":"ch13-e052","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Sliding window: `F.window(\"event_time\", \"1 hour\", \"15 minutes\")`. In how many windows does each event land?","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"windows","explain":"Overlapping windows: window ÷ slide = 60 ÷ 15 = **4**. A tumbling window (no slide) puts each event in exactly 1."},{"id":"ch13-e053","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the revenue-per-10-minute-window aggregation.","text":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nsales = (\n    events\n        .groupBy(F.[[window]](\"[[event_time]]\", \"10 minutes\"))\n        .agg(F.[[sum]](\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n)","asCode":true,"explain":"`F.window(timeCol, duration)` creates tumbling event-time windows; group by it and aggregate."},{"id":"ch13-e054","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A purchase at event_time 10:03 that arrives at 10:11 is counted in the 10:10–10:20 window because that's when Spark saw it.","answer":false,"explain":"Event-time windows use event time: it belongs to 10:00–10:10. Using arrival time would put revenue in the wrong period."},{"id":"ch13-e055","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s06","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Late data trade-off: what happens if you keep every window open forever?","options":["Low state but lost late data","Correct for arbitrarily late data but infinite state","Exactly-once is broken","Windows close at wall-clock end"],"answer":1,"explain":"Waiting forever = correct but unbounded state. Closing immediately = low state but lost late data. The watermark is the compromise."},{"id":"ch13-e056","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q12: What does a watermark solve?","options":["It deletes old rows from the Delta table","It bounds state and sets a threshold for late-data handling/finalization","It speeds up the trigger","It guarantees no duplicates"],"answer":1,"why":["That's VACUUM/retention territory, not watermarks.","Correct.","Triggers are separate.","Dedup is a separate stateful op."],"explain":"Watermark = bound state + tolerated lateness/finalization. It is not a retention policy and doesn't touch table rows."},{"id":"ch13-e057","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q13: `withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")` means each event is accepted for exactly 10 wall-clock minutes after it was created.","answer":false,"explain":"No. The watermark is relative to the **maximum observed event time** (watermark ≈ max event time − 10 min), not to each row's wall-clock age."},{"id":"ch13-e058","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc"],"quick":true,"q":"Max event time seen so far = 12:00, watermark delay 10 minutes. Conceptual watermark? (HHMM)","answer":1150,"tolerance":0,"unit":"HHMM","explain":"watermark ≈ max event time − delay = 12:00 − 10 min = **11:50**. State older than that may be finalized/evicted."},{"id":"ch13-e059","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Max event time seen = 10:20, delay = 5 min. An event with event_time 10:03 arrives now. What happens?","options":["Accepted — it arrived within 20 wall-clock minutes","It is older than the watermark (≈10:15) → too late, may be dropped","It's assigned to the current window instead","The query fails"],"answer":1,"explain":"Watermark ≈ 10:20 − 5 = 10:15. 10:03 is behind it → too late. With a 30-min delay (watermark ≈ 09:50) it would still be accepted."},{"id":"ch13-e060","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 7 variant: max event time seen = 10:20, watermark delay 30 minutes. What is the conceptual watermark? (HHMM)","answer":950,"tolerance":0,"unit":"HHMM","explain":"10:20 − 30 min = **09:50**. An event at 10:03 is newer than that → accepted and still updates its window's state."},{"id":"ch13-e061","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Short (5 min) or long (2 h) watermark?","buckets":["Short watermark","Long watermark"],"items":[{"text":"less state, lower memory","bucket":0},{"text":"accepts more late events","bucket":1},{"text":"lower latency for append finalization","bucket":0},{"text":"bigger checkpoints","bucket":1},{"text":"late records beyond tolerance may be dropped","bucket":0},{"text":"higher result latency","bucket":1}],"explain":"Test Q14/Q15: short = less state and lower latency, but lower tolerance for late data. Long = more tolerance, more state, memory, checkpoint size and latency."},{"id":"ch13-e062","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Certification trap: a 7-day watermark on a streaming table makes Databricks delete table rows older than 7 days.","answer":false,"explain":"A watermark only affects streaming **state** and late-data handling. Historical rows stay; deletion is DELETE/retention/VACUUM territory."},{"id":"ch13-e063","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Late is relative to query progress: an event that arrives 20 minutes after it happened can still be accepted with a 10-minute watermark.","answer":true,"explain":"If the max event time seen is only, say, 5 minutes ahead of it, the watermark is still behind its event time → accepted. Lateness ≠ now − event_time."},{"id":"ch13-e064","type":"write","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Add a 15-minute watermark on `event_time`, then sum `amount` as `revenue` per 10-minute window and `country`.","solution":"from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nsales = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"15 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\"), F.col(\"country\"))\n        .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"revenue\"))\n)","keywords":["withWatermark","\"15 minutes\"","groupBy","F.window","\"10 minutes\"","country","F.sum","revenue"],"lang":"python","explain":"Watermark first (on the same event-time column used by the window), then the windowed aggregation — so old window state can be evicted."},{"id":"ch13-e065","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"This aggregation keeps growing state and the watermark never cleans anything. Which line is wrong?","lines":["counts = (","    events","        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"), \"event_type\")","        .count()","        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")",")"],"bugs":[4],"fix":"counts = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"), \"event_type\")\n        .count()\n)","explain":"The watermark must be defined **before** the aggregation, on the event-time column the window uses. Applied afterwards, it can't bound the aggregation's state (and append mode on the aggregate isn't allowed)."},{"id":"ch13-e066","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Late events are dropped although they are only minutes late in business terms. Spot the semantic bug.","lines":["events = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")","events = events.withColumn(\"ingest_ts\", F.current_timestamp())","agg = (events.withWatermark(\"ingest_ts\", \"10 minutes\")","       .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")).count())"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"agg = (events.withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")\n       .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")).count())","explain":"The watermark is on processing/ingest time, not on the event-time column used by the window. Watermarks must use **event time** — the timestamp that represents reality."},{"id":"ch13-e067","type":"free","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Interview: 'A watermark is just a timer per row.' Correct this statement.","model":"A watermark is an event-time progress threshold, not a per-row timer. Spark tracks the maximum event time it has observed and sets watermark ≈ max event time − delay; the actual value is guaranteed to lag at least by the delay but isn't an exact instantaneous cutoff (it's coordinated across partitions and updated between micro-batches). Events older than the watermark may be dropped and state for windows behind it can be finalized and evicted.","rubric":["event-time progress, not wall clock","max observed event time − delay","not exact / at least delay behind","lets state be evicted and late data be handled"],"explain":"Being precise here signals you understand why 'late' depends on query progress."},{"id":"ch13-e068","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 7: you send event_time 10:01 (arrives 10:01), 10:02 (arrives 10:02) and 10:03 (arrives 10:20) with a 5-minute watermark. The 10:03 event is still counted. A teammate says 'the watermark is broken'.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is the max event time seen when the 10:03 event arrives?","options":[{"text":"10:02","ok":true,"fb":"Right — only event times count, and the latest was 10:02."},{"text":"10:20","ok":false,"fb":"That's the arrival (processing) time, not event time."},{"text":"10:03","ok":false,"fb":"10:03 is the late event itself; before it, the max was 10:02."}]},{"prompt":"So is the 10:03 event late in event-time terms?","options":[{"text":"No — watermark ≈ 09:57, the event is ahead of it","ok":true,"fb":"Correct: lateness is relative to event-time progress."},{"text":"Yes, it's 18 minutes late so it must be dropped","ok":false,"fb":"Wall-clock delay doesn't define lateness."},{"text":"It depends on the trigger interval","ok":false,"fb":"Triggers don't define lateness."}]},{"prompt":"How do you make the lab show the difference between 5- and 30-minute watermarks?","options":[{"text":"First send an on-time event with event_time ≈ 10:20, let a batch pass, then send the 10:03 event","ok":true,"fb":"Now 5 min → watermark ≈ 10:15 (dropped) vs 30 min → ≈ 09:50 (accepted)."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint between runs","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to lateness and dangerous habit."},{"text":"Switch to complete mode","ok":false,"fb":"Complete mode doesn't evict state; it hides the effect."}]}],"explain":"Watermarks are event-time based: you must advance event time to make an event 'late'."},{"id":"ch13-e069","type":"order","section":"ch13-s07","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Missing late records: order the event-time chain (E-M-W-P-O-W).","items":["the record's event_time","max observed event time at arrival","configured watermark delay/column","watermark progress","output mode","window boundaries"],"explain":"Reason in event time. Only blame the checkpoint if evidence points there."},{"id":"ch13-e070","type":"match","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q22, Q23, Q24: match the output mode.","pairs":[["Append","emits rows once considered final"],["Update","emits changed aggregate rows each trigger"],["Complete","emits the complete aggregate result each trigger"]],"explain":"Append = finalized, Update = changed, Complete = everything."},{"id":"ch13-e071","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q25: The Delta streaming sink supports update output mode directly.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Delta sinks support append and complete. Update-like semantics into Delta are implemented with foreachBatch + MERGE."},{"id":"ch13-e072","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Batch 1: GR 100, DE 50. Batch 2 adds +50 for GR and a new FR 20. What does COMPLETE mode emit after batch 2?","options":["GR 150 only","GR 150 and FR 20","GR 150, DE 50, FR 20","Nothing until the watermark passes"],"answer":2,"explain":"Complete re-emits the whole result every trigger. Update would emit GR 150 and FR 20 (the changed rows). Append would wait for finality."},{"id":"ch13-e073","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Same data: what does UPDATE mode emit after batch 2?","options":["GR 150, DE 50, FR 20","GR 150, FR 20","DE 50","GR 50, FR 20"],"answer":1,"explain":"Update emits only rows whose result changed in this trigger: GR (100→150) and the new FR. DE didn't change."},{"id":"ch13-e074","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"This query fails at start. Which line is the problem?","lines":["counts = (events.withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")","          .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"), \"event_type\").count())","(counts.writeStream","    .outputMode(\"update\")","    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt)","    .toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.event_counts\"))"],"bugs":[3],"fix":"(counts.writeStream\n    .outputMode(\"append\")      # or foreachBatch + MERGE for live updates\n    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt)\n    .toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.event_counts\"))","explain":"The Delta table sink doesn't support update mode. Use append (finalized windows) or foreachBatch + MERGE for update-like behaviour."},{"id":"ch13-e075","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"This aggregation is rejected in append mode. Why?","lines":["counts = (","    events","        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"))","        .count()",")","(counts.writeStream.outputMode(\"append\")","    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt).toTable(\"gold.counts\"))"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"counts = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"))\n        .count()\n)","explain":"Append emits only final rows; without a watermark no aggregate can ever be final, so Spark refuses append for streaming aggregations without a watermark. Add `withWatermark` on the event-time column before the groupBy."},{"id":"ch13-e076","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"In complete mode, the watermark evicts old aggregation state just like in append/update mode.","answer":false,"explain":"Complete mode must re-emit the whole result, so aggregation state is **not** evicted by the watermark — expensive with large/unbounded cardinality."},{"id":"ch13-e077","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"For a stateless filter/select stream, choosing append vs update makes no real semantic difference.","answer":true,"explain":"No row ever changes later, so the output-mode distinction doesn't change operator behaviour (complete isn't allowed without an aggregation)."},{"id":"ch13-e078","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":3,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Event analytics example: 5-minute windows, 20-minute watermark, append mode. Roughly what max event time must the stream observe before window [10:00–10:05) is emitted? (HHMM)","answer":1025,"tolerance":1,"unit":"HHMM","explain":"The window is final when the watermark (max event time − 20 min) reaches its end 10:05 → max event time ≈ **10:25** (and the next micro-batch runs). That's why append output looks 'delayed'."},{"id":"ch13-e079","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A product manager complains: 'Events arrive instantly in Bronze, but ecommerce.gold.event_counts (5-min windows, 20-min watermark, append) shows nothing for the last half hour. The stream is lagging!'","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Compare the latest event times with the current watermark in query.lastProgress","ok":true,"fb":"Right — append only emits windows the watermark has passed."},{"text":"Restart the cluster","ok":false,"fb":"Restarting blindly hides evidence and doesn't change semantics."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint to 'reset' the stream","ok":false,"fb":"Dangerous: reprocessing and duplicates."}]},{"prompt":"Watermark is 20 min behind the max event time; batches are fast. Diagnosis?","options":[{"text":"Intended behaviour: windows are emitted once final","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — stateful aggregate + append + watermark = wait for finality."},{"text":"Backlog: input rate > processing rate","ok":false,"fb":"Batches are fast; rates would show it."},{"text":"Corrupted state store","ok":false,"fb":"No evidence points there."}]},{"prompt":"The PM really needs live-updating counts. What do you propose?","options":[{"text":"foreachBatch + MERGE into a live counts table (update-like semantics)","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — Delta sink doesn't support update mode directly."},{"text":"Switch the Delta sink to outputMode(\"update\")","ok":false,"fb":"Delta sinks don't support update mode."},{"text":"Set the watermark to 0 seconds","ok":false,"fb":"That drops valid late data and still isn't 'live update'."}]}],"explain":"Append output looking delayed is often intended. Live dashboards need update-like semantics via foreachBatch + MERGE."},{"id":"ch13-e080","type":"write","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Write the Gold writer for the event-analytics `counts` DataFrame: append mode, checkpoint `/Volumes/ecommerce/system/event_counts_ckpt`, table `ecommerce.gold.event_counts`.","solution":"query = (\n    counts.writeStream\n        .outputMode(\"append\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/event_counts_ckpt\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.event_counts\")\n)","keywords":["outputMode(\"append\")","checkpointLocation","/Volumes/ecommerce/system/event_counts_ckpt","toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.event_counts\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Append + watermark gives one final row per window and event_type, and lets window state be removed afterwards."},{"id":"ch13-e081","type":"order","section":"ch13-s08","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the lifecycle of one window in the event-analytics example.","items":["events arrive with event_time in [10:00–10:05)","Spark keeps counts in state per event_type","a late event (10:04) updates the window state","the watermark advances past the window end","append mode emits the final row once","the window's state is removed"],"explain":"That's the complete lifecycle: accumulate, absorb late data, finalize at the watermark, emit once, evict state."},{"id":"ch13-e082","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q16: What does a streaming checkpoint contain?","options":["Only the last processed timestamp","Offsets/progress, commit information, query metadata and state where applicable","A full copy of the sink table","The notebook source code"],"answer":1,"why":["The classic trap answer.","Correct.","The sink data lives in the sink.","Code isn't stored in the checkpoint."],"explain":"offsets/ (what each batch reads), commits/ (what was written), state/ (stateful operators) and metadata (query identity). Certification trap: it's not just a 'last timestamp'."},{"id":"ch13-e083","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q17: What happens if the checkpoint is deleted (or the checkpointLocation changed)?","options":["The stream resumes exactly where it stopped","The query starts as new and loses prior progress/state identity","Databricks rebuilds it from the Delta log automatically","Only the state is lost; offsets are kept in the sink"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deleting/changing the checkpoint = a new query: reprocessing, duplicate external side effects, lost accumulated state, maybe a full rebuild."},{"id":"ch13-e084","type":"match","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each checkpoint folder to what it records.","pairs":[["offsets/","source range each micro-batch reads"],["commits/","micro-batches successfully written to the sink"],["state/","state-store data of stateful operators"],["metadata","the query's identity"]],"explain":"Offsets are written before a batch runs, commits after the sink succeeds — that pair lets a restart decide skip vs retry."},{"id":"ch13-e085","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Deploying new code on the SAME checkpoint: usually OK or dangerous?","buckets":["Often OK (test it)","Dangerous / incompatible"],"items":[{"text":"adding a stateless filter","bucket":0},{"text":"renaming a projected column in a stateless query","bucket":0},{"text":"changing groupBy key customer_id → country","bucket":1},{"text":"changing count() to sum(amount)","bucket":1},{"text":"switching the source from Kafka to Delta","bucket":1},{"text":"adding a second stateful aggregation","bucket":1}],"explain":"Some stateless changes can reuse a checkpoint depending on the exact query. State keys, state schema, source type and the number/type of stateful operators are checkpoint-incompatible changes."},{"id":"ch13-e086","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A checkpoint folder is a generic progress folder that two different streaming queries can safely share.","answer":false,"explain":"A checkpoint encodes one query's identity, offsets, commits and state. Sharing it between queries corrupts progress semantics. One checkpoint ↔ one query."},{"id":"ch13-e087","type":"free","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","interview"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is 'state schema effectively production data'?","model":"Because a stateful query's checkpoint holds state with a specific schema (keys and values). If you change the state architecture, the old checkpoint becomes incompatible and you may need a new checkpoint plus a full recomputation/backfill — which is expensive. So changing state keys or aggregations must be planned and tested like a data migration.","rubric":["state lives in the checkpoint with a schema","incompatible changes need new checkpoint","requires full recomputation/backfill","expensive → plan/test like data"],"explain":"Stateless changes are cheap; stateful changes can cost a full rebuild."},{"id":"ch13-e088","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"You deploy a new version of a stateful query: groupBy changed from customer_id to country. You restart it on the existing checkpoint and it fails at start.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"text":"The new state schema is incompatible with the checkpoint's state","ok":true,"fb":"Right — state keys changed."},{"text":"The cluster is too small","ok":false,"fb":"It fails before processing any data."},{"text":"The source is out of retention","ok":false,"fb":"Possible in general, but the change you just made is the obvious suspect."}]},{"prompt":"What is the correct way forward?","options":[{"text":"New checkpoint + plan a full recomputation/backfill of the result","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — and do it deliberately, ideally tested in non-prod first."},{"text":"Delete files inside state/ until it starts","ok":false,"fb":"Hand-editing a checkpoint corrupts query identity."},{"text":"Point the old checkpoint at a different table","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't fix the state incompatibility."}]}],"explain":"Stateful changes can be checkpoint-incompatible; the way out is a new checkpoint + backfill."},{"id":"ch13-e089","type":"order","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Write-ahead sequence of one micro-batch (why restarts are safe).","items":["record the planned source range in offsets/","process the micro-batch","commit the result to the Delta sink","record the batch in commits/"],"explain":"Offsets first (plan), then process, then the sink commit, then the commit log. On restart, an offsets entry without a commit entry tells Spark which batch to check/redo."},{"id":"ch13-e090","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s09","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Fill in the checkpoint-reset consequences.","text":"Deleting a checkpoint can mean [[reprocessing]], duplicate external [[side effects]], loss of accumulated [[state]], and a full [[rebuild|backfill]].","bank":["compaction","faster batches"],"explain":"Per pipeline the impact differs — but never treat a checkpoint reset as harmless."},{"id":"ch13-e091","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q18: An exactly-once Delta sink means the source cannot contain duplicates.","answer":false,"explain":"Q19 — why not: processing guarantees don't deduplicate semantically duplicated input records. Two records with event_id=123 are two records."},{"id":"ch13-e092","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Which three pieces cooperate so a retried micro-batch never becomes a partial/duplicate commit in a Delta sink?","options":["trigger + output mode + watermark","source offsets + checkpoint commits + Delta transaction log","Photon + RocksDB + Unity Catalog","foreachBatch + batch_id + MERGE"],"answer":1,"explain":"Replayable source offsets, the checkpoint commit log and Delta's transactional log together give exactly-once into a Delta sink."},{"id":"ch13-e093","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Micro-batch 20 reads events 1000–1100 and writes to a Delta sink. The driver crashes right at the boundary.","steps":[{"prompt":"On restart, what question must the engine answer first?","options":[{"text":"Was batch 20 committed?","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly — offsets/20 exists; is there a commit?"},{"text":"Which events arrived during the crash?","ok":false,"fb":"Not yet relevant; batch 20's range is already fixed in offsets/."},{"text":"Should it switch to complete mode?","ok":false,"fb":"Output mode isn't part of recovery."}]},{"prompt":"Batch 20 IS in the Delta log but commits/20 is missing. What happens?","options":[{"text":"It is not applied again","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — Delta knows this query's batch 20 is committed."},{"text":"It is appended a second time","ok":false,"fb":"That's what exactly-once prevents for the Delta sink."},{"text":"Half of it is re-applied","ok":false,"fb":"Never 'half applied twice'."}]},{"prompt":"Batch 20 is NOT in the Delta log. What happens?","options":[{"text":"Batch 20 is retried with the same offsets 1000–1100","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — same range, deterministic retry."},{"text":"Events 1000–1100 are skipped","ok":false,"fb":"That would lose data."},{"text":"The stream starts from event 0","ok":false,"fb":"Only a lost checkpoint does that."}]}],"explain":"if committed → don't apply again; if not → retry. Never half applied twice."},{"id":"ch13-e094","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Because Structured Streaming is exactly-once, a `foreachBatch` that sends emails sends each email exactly once even after a crash.","answer":false,"explain":"Exactly-once stops at the boundaries. If the crash happens before the checkpoint commit, the batch replays and the emails are sent again. External services don't share Delta's transaction semantics."},{"id":"ch13-e095","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"A foreachBatch sends 100 emails per micro-batch. Batch 7 sends its 100 emails, then the process crashes before the checkpoint commit. After the retry succeeds, how many emails did batch 7's customers receive in total?","answer":200,"tolerance":0,"unit":"emails","explain":"The retry re-runs the function for the same batch → 100 + 100 = **200**. Make side effects idempotent (e.g. record sent ids keyed by business id/batch)."},{"id":"ch13-e096","type":"write","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Deduplicate a stream on `event_id` with bounded state: 1-hour watermark on `event_time`.","solution":"unique = (\n    events\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"1 hour\")\n        .dropDuplicates([\"event_id\", \"event_time\"])\n)","keywords":["withWatermark","\"1 hour\"","dropDuplicates","event_id","event_time"],"lang":"python","explain":"Including the watermarked event_time in the dedup keys lets Spark drop old keys as event time progresses. Without the watermark, every event_id is remembered forever."},{"id":"ch13-e097","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"This dedup stream ran fine for weeks; now batches slow down and memory keeps rising. Which line causes it?","lines":["events = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")","unique = events.dropDuplicates([\"event_id\"])","(unique.writeStream.option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt)","    .toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.events\"))"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"unique = (events.withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"1 hour\")\n               .dropDuplicates([\"event_id\", \"event_time\"]))","explain":"Without a time bound, dropDuplicates must remember every event_id ever seen → unbounded state. Add a watermark and include the event-time column."},{"id":"ch13-e098","type":"odd","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which statement does NOT belong with the others?","options":["Dedup is a stateful operation","Unbounded dedup needs to remember every key forever","A watermark lets dedup state be cleaned","Exactly-once processing removes duplicate business events automatically"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first three are true facts about streaming dedup. Exactly-once processing does NOT remove business duplicates — that's the trap."},{"id":"ch13-e099","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"You need to drop duplicates on `event_id` even when the duplicate copies carry slightly different `event_time` values, with bounded state. Which API fits best?","options":["dropDuplicates([\"event_id\"]) without watermark","withWatermark(...).dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark([\"event_id\"])","withWatermark(...).dropDuplicates([\"event_id\", \"event_time\"])","distinct()"],"answer":1,"why":["Unbounded state.","Correct (newer runtimes).","Bounded, but copies with different event_time aren't considered duplicates.","Unbounded and compares all columns."],"explain":"`dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark` dedups on the key alone within the watermark delay — copies with different timestamps still collapse. The classic `[event_id, event_time]` key treats different timestamps as different rows."},{"id":"ch13-e100","type":"order","section":"ch13-s10","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Duplicates after failure: order the questions.","items":["Delta sink or custom/foreachBatch sink?","Non-idempotent side effect in foreachBatch?","Does the source itself contain duplicates?","Was the checkpoint reset?"],"explain":"Sink type first (where exactly-once ends), then side effects, then source duplicates and checkpoint resets."},{"id":"ch13-e101","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q26: Why do stream-stream joins need state?","options":["Because joins always shuffle","A record from one stream may need to wait for a later matching record from the other","Because the static side is cached in state","Because Delta sinks require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"An order at 10:00 must be kept until its payment (10:02) arrives. Q27: watermarks decide when that waiting state can be discarded."},{"id":"ch13-e102","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Certification trap: for stream-stream OUTER joins, a watermark is required.","answer":true,"explain":"The engine must know when an unmatched record can be considered final and emitted with NULLs — that's exactly what watermark + time constraint provide."},{"id":"ch13-e103","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Orders watermark is at 13:50, payments watermark at 12:59. With the default multiple-watermark policy, what is the global watermark? (HHMM)","answer":1259,"tolerance":0,"unit":"HHMM","explain":"Default policy = **min** → the slowest input decides: 12:59. With `max` it would be 13:50 and late payments could be dropped."},{"id":"ch13-e104","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Why is 'min' the default multiple-watermark policy?","options":["It is faster","Global progress follows the slowest input, so state the slow stream still needs isn't discarded","It reduces state to zero","Outer joins don't support max"],"answer":1,"explain":"If orders are at 14:00 but payments only at 13:00, using the orders watermark could discard state while matching payments can still appear. min protects correctness; max trades correctness for progress."},{"id":"ch13-e105","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the stream-stream join setup.","text":"orders_w = orders.[[withWatermark]](\"order_time\", \"30 minutes\")\npayments_w = payments.withWatermark(\"[[payment_time]]\", \"30 minutes\")\njoined = orders_w.alias(\"o\").join(\n    payments_w.alias(\"p\"),\n    F.expr(\"\"\"o.order_id = p.order_id AND\n              p.payment_time [[BETWEEN]] o.order_time AND o.order_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR\"\"\"))","asCode":true,"explain":"Watermarks on both inputs + key + time-range condition let Spark discard obsolete join state."},{"id":"ch13-e106","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"This stream-stream left outer join is rejected. Which line is missing the essential piece?","lines":["orders_w = orders.withWatermark(\"order_time\", \"30 minutes\")","payments_s = payments","joined = orders_w.join(payments_s, F.expr(\"order_id = p_order_id AND payment_time BETWEEN order_time AND order_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR\"), \"left_outer\")"],"bugs":[1],"fix":"payments_s = payments.withWatermark(\"payment_time\", \"30 minutes\")","explain":"Outer stream-stream joins require watermarks so unmatched rows can be finalized; Databricks recommends watermarks on BOTH inputs. The payments side has none."},{"id":"ch13-e107","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"In a long-running stream-static join, if the products dimension table is updated, Spark automatically recomputes all previously written enriched rows.","answer":false,"explain":"Already-written outputs are not recomputed. New micro-batches may see the newer version, but history stays as it was. If correctness needs recomputation, consider materialized/declarative pipeline patterns (Phase 6)."},{"id":"ch13-e108","type":"write","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Enrich `orders_stream` with the static table `ecommerce.silver.products` on `product_id`.","solution":"products = spark.table(\"ecommerce.silver.products\")\nenriched = orders_stream.join(products, \"product_id\")","keywords":["spark.table","ecommerce.silver.products","join","product_id"],"lang":"python","explain":"Stream-static join: the static side is read with spark.table (batch). Very common for dimension enrichment."},{"id":"ch13-e109","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s11","difficulty":1,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Stream-static or stream-stream join?","buckets":["Stream-static","Stream-stream"],"items":[{"text":"orders stream + products table","bucket":0},{"text":"orders stream + payments stream","bucket":1},{"text":"needs watermarks on both sides","bucket":1},{"text":"static side may change without recomputing history","bucket":0},{"text":"buffers records waiting for a future match","bucket":1}],"explain":"Only stream-stream joins buffer both sides in state and need watermarks/time bounds."},{"id":"ch13-e110","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q28: Why can a stream fail if it was stopped beyond the Delta source's retention?","options":["The checkpoint expires automatically after 7 days","Required source versions/files may have been vacuumed before the stream catches up","Delta tables can't be read by streams after 7 days","The watermark deletes the source rows"],"answer":1,"explain":"The checkpoint says 'I need version X', but VACUUM removed the physical files. The stream can't recover incrementally."},{"id":"ch13-e111","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q29: To get a stream that fell behind Delta retention running again, set `spark.sql.files.ignoreMissingFiles=true`.","answer":false,"explain":"No. Databricks warns this can silently produce incorrect streaming results. Increase retention or recover/rebuild the stream appropriately."},{"id":"ch13-e112","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"A stream reading a Delta source stops on Friday. The source's file retention for VACUUM is 7 days. What is the maximum number of whole days the stream can stay stopped and still be sure to catch up (assuming VACUUM runs daily)?","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"days","explain":"It must run at least once within the retention window: after more than 7 days, files it needs may be vacuumed. The '2 weeks later' restart in the source example fails."},{"id":"ch13-e113","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"A Silver stream reading `ecommerce.bronze.events` was paused for a 3-week migration. On restart it fails with a missing-file error.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you check first?","options":[{"text":"Source retention settings and DESCRIBE HISTORY for VACUUM runs during the pause","ok":true,"fb":"Right — compare the pause with retention."},{"text":"The watermark delay","ok":false,"fb":"Watermarks don't delete source files."},{"text":"Whether the trigger is AvailableNow","ok":false,"fb":"Trigger type doesn't affect retention."}]},{"prompt":"VACUUM with 7-day retention ran weekly. A colleague suggests ignoreMissingFiles=true. Your answer?","options":[{"text":"No — it may silently produce incorrect results; recover/rebuild instead","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — don't suppress a correctness error."},{"text":"Yes, the missing files were probably empty","ok":false,"fb":"You have no evidence of that; data would silently go missing."},{"text":"Yes, but only for one run","ok":false,"fb":"One run is enough to lose data silently."}]},{"prompt":"How do you prevent it next time?","options":[{"text":"Design retention and streaming operations together: longer retention or guarantee runs within the window","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — a real production operational requirement."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint before every pause","ok":false,"fb":"That causes reprocessing and duplicates."},{"text":"Use complete mode","ok":false,"fb":"Irrelevant to source retention."}]}],"explain":"A stream must run at least once within the source retention window."},{"id":"ch13-e114","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"A Bronze Delta table used as a streaming source starts receiving UPDATE and DELETE commits. What is the right default thinking?","options":["Nothing changes — a Delta source streams row-level changes automatically","A simple Delta source is append-oriented; use Change Data Feed (or deliberately skip change commits) for row-level changes","Switch the sink to complete mode","Increase the watermark"],"answer":1,"explain":"A plain Delta streaming source handles appends; non-append commits need care. CDF (`readChangeFeed`) propagates row-level changes; `skipChangeCommits` ignores them (changes not propagated)."},{"id":"ch13-e115","type":"match","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Match the Delta-source option to its effect.","pairs":[["(default)","non-append change commits make the stream fail"],["skipChangeCommits","ignore update/delete commits; changes not propagated"],["readChangeFeed","stream row-level inserts/updates/deletes via CDF"],["ignoreMissingFiles=true","hides missing files — can give silent wrong results"]],"explain":"Pick the behaviour deliberately; never use ignoreMissingFiles as a retention workaround."},{"id":"ch13-e116","type":"write","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Increase the source table's retention so a stopped stream has more time to catch up: set `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` to 14 days and `delta.logRetentionDuration` to 30 days on `ecommerce.bronze.events`.","solution":"ALTER TABLE ecommerce.bronze.events SET TBLPROPERTIES (\n  'delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days',\n  'delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration' = 'interval 14 days'\n);","keywords":["alter table","set tblproperties","delta.logRetentionDuration","delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration","interval 14 days"],"lang":"sql","explain":"Increasing retention is one of the two correct fixes (the other: recover/rebuild the stream). It costs storage, so size it to your longest realistic stop."},{"id":"ch13-e117","type":"order","section":"ch13-s12","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Delta-source stream fails after a long stop: order the response.","items":["Compare stop duration with source retention","Confirm VACUUM ran (DESCRIBE HISTORY)","Reject the ignoreMissingFiles workaround","Recover/rebuild the stream (reset + backfill)","Increase retention / guarantee runs within the window"],"explain":"Diagnose, refuse the silent-wrong-results shortcut, rebuild, then prevent."},{"id":"ch13-e118","type":"free","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","interview"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q20 + Q21: Why can foreachBatch duplicate external side effects, and what's the solution?","model":"A failed or retried micro-batch may invoke the foreachBatch function again for the same data. External sinks (email, APIs, other databases) don't participate in Spark/Delta transaction semantics, so their effects happen twice. Solution: make the side effects idempotent — e.g. MERGE/upsert by a stable business key and version, or use transactional sink semantics, so that replaying a batch yields the same end state.","rubric":["retry re-invokes the function","external sinks not transactional with Spark/Delta","make it idempotent","business key/version or transactional sink"],"explain":"foreachBatch gives power, but sink-side idempotency becomes your responsibility."},{"id":"ch13-e119","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Complete the streaming upsert.","text":"from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\ndef upsert(batch_df, [[batch_id]]):\n    target = DeltaTable.[[forName]](spark, \"ecommerce.silver.customers\")\n    (target.alias(\"t\")\n        .[[merge]](batch_df.alias(\"s\"), \"t.customer_id = s.customer_id\")\n        .[[whenMatchedUpdateAll]]()\n        .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n        .execute())\n\nquery = changes.writeStream.[[foreachBatch]](upsert).option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt).start()","asCode":true,"explain":"The function receives each micro-batch as a batch DataFrame plus its batch_id; MERGE by key makes it idempotent."},{"id":"ch13-e120","type":"spotbug","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug","pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"This foreachBatch keeps account balances. After a retry, some balances are doubled. Which line is the bug?","lines":["def apply(batch_df, batch_id):","    for r in batch_df.collect():","        spark.sql(f\"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + {r.amount} WHERE id = {r.id}\")","query = tx.writeStream.foreachBatch(apply).option('checkpointLocation', ckpt).start()"],"bugs":[2],"fix":"-- record each transaction once, keyed by tx_id (replay inserts nothing new)\nMERGE INTO ledger t USING batch s ON t.tx_id = s.tx_id\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *\n-- balance = SUM(amount) per account, derived from the ledger","explain":"`balance = balance + amount` is not idempotent: a replayed batch applies the increment twice. Key the effect by a stable business id (tx_id) / version so a replay changes nothing. (Collecting to the driver is a second smell.)"},{"id":"ch13-e121","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"`batch_id` alone is a universal business deduplication key for any external sink.","answer":false,"explain":"batch_id can help some sinks recognise replayed batches, but it isn't a business key (and a new checkpoint restarts numbering). The safest design depends on the target system."},{"id":"ch13-e122","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Idempotent on replay, or not?","buckets":["Idempotent","Not idempotent"],"items":[{"text":"MERGE by customer_id, update to latest version","bucket":0},{"text":"balance = balance + incoming_amount","bucket":1},{"text":"send_email(customer)","bucket":1},{"text":"overwrite one deterministic partition with the batch's data","bucket":0},{"text":"plain INSERT/append without any key","bucket":1},{"text":"Delta write with txnAppId + txnVersion=batch_id","bucket":0}],"explain":"Idempotent = running the same batch twice gives the same end state."},{"id":"ch13-e123","type":"write","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Write a foreachBatch function `merge_counts` that MERGEs `batch_df` into `ecommerce.gold.live_event_counts` on window start and event_type (update all when matched, insert all otherwise), and start the stream from `counts` in update mode with checkpoint `ckpt`.","solution":"from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\ndef merge_counts(batch_df, batch_id):\n    target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"ecommerce.gold.live_event_counts\")\n    (target.alias(\"t\")\n        .merge(batch_df.alias(\"s\"),\n               \"t.window_start = s.window.start AND t.event_type = s.event_type\")\n        .whenMatchedUpdateAll()\n        .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n        .execute())\n\n(counts.writeStream\n    .outputMode(\"update\")\n    .foreachBatch(merge_counts)\n    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", ckpt)\n    .start())","keywords":["def merge_counts(batch_df, batch_id)","DeltaTable.forName","merge","window.start","event_type","whenMatchedUpdateAll","whenNotMatchedInsertAll","foreachBatch(merge_counts)","outputMode(\"update\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Update mode hands only changed aggregates to the function; MERGE turns them into update-like semantics on the Delta table — what the Delta sink can't do directly."},{"id":"ch13-e124","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s13","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Which of these can you do inside foreachBatch but NOT directly with a streaming Delta sink?","options":["append rows","MERGE (upsert) into a Delta table","use a checkpoint","complete mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"foreachBatch exposes each micro-batch as a batch DataFrame, so batch-only operations like MERGE, complex Delta DML or custom sink logic become available."},{"id":"ch13-e125","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q30: Input rate permanently exceeds processing rate. What happens?","options":["Spark automatically drops the excess input","Backlog and latency grow","Exactly-once guarantees break","The checkpoint fixes it on the next restart"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sustained mismatch → backlog grows, latency grows, and eventually source retention may be exceeded. Q31: a checkpoint doesn't create capacity."},{"id":"ch13-e126","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","pitfall"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q31: A checkpoint can fix a throughput mismatch between source and pipeline.","answer":false,"explain":"Checkpoint preserves correctness/progress; it doesn't create processing capacity. You need more capacity, a better query, larger batches, less state, better partitioning or less skew."},{"id":"ch13-e127","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["calc"],"quick":false,"q":"Source produces 50 MB/s, the pipeline processes 20 MB/s. How many MB of backlog accumulate in 10 minutes?","answer":18000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"MB","explain":"Net +30 MB/s × 600 s = **18,000 MB** (~18 GB) — and it keeps growing until capacity or efficiency changes."},{"id":"ch13-e128","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q33: One state partition dominates (one task slow, one state partition huge, spill). Likely issue?","options":["Too short watermark","Key skew (e.g. user_id = 'anonymous' with 70% of events)","Delta sink in append mode","Checkpoint on a Volume"],"answer":1,"explain":"A hot key in a stateful groupBy concentrates state and work in one partition. Streaming partition/key design matters."},{"id":"ch13-e129","type":"calc","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["calc","debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Trigger target = 5 s. In each micro-batch 99 partitions finish in 2 s, but one skewed partition takes 30 s. What is the minimum duration (s) of each micro-batch?","answer":30,"tolerance":0,"unit":"seconds","explain":"A stage finishes when its slowest task finishes → ≥ **30 s** per batch, 6× the trigger. Backlog grows — the Phase 1 ↔ Phase 5 connection."},{"id":"ch13-e130","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["compare"],"quick":false,"q":"Effect of SMALL micro-batches (every 1 s) vs LARGE ones (every 1 min)?","buckets":["Small micro-batches","Large micro-batches"],"items":[{"text":"lower latency","bucket":0},{"text":"better throughput efficiency","bucket":1},{"text":"more scheduling overhead","bucket":0},{"text":"more frequent commits & storage API calls","bucket":0},{"text":"smaller output files","bucket":0},{"text":"higher latency","bucket":1},{"text":"less per-batch overhead","bucket":1}],"explain":"Streaming design balances latency, throughput, cost, state size and correctness."},{"id":"ch13-e131","type":"odd","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Which is NOT a valid fix for a sustained backlog?","options":["more processing capacity","reduce skew / better partitioning","a new checkpoint location","larger batches / less state"],"answer":2,"explain":"A new checkpoint just restarts the query as new (reprocessing, duplicates) — it adds no capacity."},{"id":"ch13-e132","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Delta's streaming integration makes small files impossible, so trigger frequency never matters for file layout.","answer":false,"explain":"Tiny micro-batches still create small files (1–3 MB each). OPTIMIZE/predictive optimization can compact later, but choose latency requirements sensibly."},{"id":"ch13-e133","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s14","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A clickstream aggregation by `user_id` gets slower each day. Spark UI: in every micro-batch, one task takes 40 s while the others take 2 s, with spill. Input rate is stable.","steps":[{"prompt":"What is the first hypothesis?","options":[{"text":"Key skew: one user_id holds a huge share of events/state","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — one slow task + one huge state partition = hot key."},{"text":"The watermark is too short","ok":false,"fb":"That drops late data; it doesn't create one slow task."},{"text":"The Delta sink doesn't support append","ok":false,"fb":"It does."}]},{"prompt":"You find user_id = 'anonymous' is 70% of events. What do you do?","options":[{"text":"Rethink the key design (e.g. handle anonymous traffic separately / different grouping key)","ok":true,"fb":"Right — state partition/key design matters."},{"text":"Add more workers only","ok":false,"fb":"One hot partition stays one task; more workers don't split it."},{"text":"Delete the checkpoint","ok":false,"fb":"Loses state and reprocesses; skew remains."}]}],"explain":"Stateful streaming + skew is especially bad: the hot key hurts every batch forever."},{"id":"ch13-e134","type":"free","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam","debug"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q32: Batch durations increase while input rate stays stable. What should you inspect?","model":"State growth (numRowsTotal of stateful operators), checkpoint/state-store overhead, sink and source performance (small files/output overhead, source metadata growth, sink slowdown) and ordinary Spark execution metrics. If state metrics grow endlessly, the watermark/state design is the primary suspect.","rubric":["state growth / numRowsTotal","checkpoint/state-store overhead","source/sink performance, small files","Spark execution (stages/tasks)","watermark/state design primary suspect"],"explain":"Stable input + growing duration means each batch does more work for the same input — usually state."},{"id":"ch13-e135","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":true,"q":"lastProgress shows inputRowsPerSecond = 100,000 and processedRowsPerSecond = 40,000 for an hour. Conclusion?","options":["Healthy — capacity to spare","Backlog is growing; the pipeline can't keep up","The watermark is too long","Nothing — CPU is the only reliable metric"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sustained input > processed → backlog grows. Input 50,000 vs processed 100,000 would mean spare capacity. This ratio is often more useful than cluster CPU."},{"id":"ch13-e136","type":"order","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Walk the production debugging tree for 'streaming latency high'.","items":["Is input backlog growing?","Which micro-batch is slow?","Open Spark stage/task metrics","Classify: Spark (shuffle/skew/spill/join)","…or state (growth/watermark/state store)","…or source/sink (throughput/API latency/storage)"],"explain":"Backlog → slow batch → stage/task metrics → then branch into Spark, state or source/sink factors."},{"id":"ch13-e137","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Classify each finding into the tree's branches.","buckets":["Spark execution","State","Source/Sink"],"items":[{"text":"one task with huge shuffle read","bucket":0},{"text":"disk spill in the join stage","bucket":0},{"text":"numRowsTotal growing every batch","bucket":1},{"text":"watermark not advancing","bucket":1},{"text":"sink API calls slow / throttled","bucket":2},{"text":"storage writes slow","bucket":2},{"text":"RocksDB state-store commit time high","bucket":1}],"explain":"Streaming adds state and source/sink factors on top of ordinary Spark performance."},{"id":"ch13-e138","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"A stream has stopped making progress: no new rows in the sink for two hours. Your manager says 'just restart it'.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you do before restarting?","options":[{"text":"Inspect status/lastProgress and the query state first","ok":true,"fb":"Right — don't restart blindly; preserve the evidence."},{"text":"Restart with a new checkpoint to be safe","ok":false,"fb":"That creates a new query — reprocessing and duplicates."},{"text":"Increase the trigger interval","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't diagnose anything."}]},{"prompt":"The query is active and waiting for data. Next check?","options":[{"text":"Is the source still producing — are new offsets/files available?","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — maybe there's simply nothing new."},{"text":"Is the watermark too short?","ok":false,"fb":"Watermarks affect late data, not whether new data is read."},{"text":"Is the output mode complete?","ok":false,"fb":"Not related to progress."}]},{"prompt":"New files are there, but reading them fails with an authorization error. Likely cause?","options":[{"text":"A permission/credential used by the job expired or was revoked","ok":true,"fb":"One of the classic 'stopped progressing' causes."},{"text":"The checkpoint is too large","ok":false,"fb":"That would slow, not deny access."},{"text":"Key skew","ok":false,"fb":"Skew slows batches; it doesn't cause access errors."}]}],"explain":"Checklist: query active? source producing? new offsets? checkpoint corrupted? sink blocked? state batch stuck? executor failure? permission expired? retention violated?"},{"id":"ch13-e139","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug","exam"],"quick":false,"q":"Users report that some late purchase events are missing from the 10-minute revenue windows.","steps":[{"prompt":"What do you look at first?","options":[{"text":"The missing events' event_time vs the max observed event time and the watermark at their arrival","ok":true,"fb":"Yes — the event-time chain."},{"text":"The checkpoint folder","ok":false,"fb":"Don't blame the checkpoint first unless evidence points there."},{"text":"Cluster CPU","ok":false,"fb":"Missing late rows are a semantics question first."}]},{"prompt":"The watermark delay is 5 minutes; the events were 20 minutes behind the max event time. Conclusion?","options":[{"text":"They were beyond the watermark and may be dropped — the tolerance is too short for real lateness","ok":true,"fb":"Correct — measure real lateness and size the watermark."},{"text":"Exactly-once is broken","ok":false,"fb":"Exactly-once is about commits, not lateness."},{"text":"The window boundaries are wrong","ok":false,"fb":"They're assigned by event time correctly."}]},{"prompt":"What fix keeps late data without unbounded state?","options":[{"text":"Increase the watermark to cover realistic lateness (accept more state/latency)","ok":true,"fb":"That's the explicit latency/state/correctness trade-off."},{"text":"Remove the watermark entirely","ok":false,"fb":"Unbounded state — and append mode wouldn't be allowed."},{"text":"Set ignoreMissingFiles=true","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated and dangerous."}]}],"explain":"Missing late records: event_time → max observed → configured watermark → watermark progress → output mode → window boundaries."},{"id":"ch13-e140","type":"scenario","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":3,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"After a cluster failure, the gold customer table and an external CRM both show duplicate entries.","steps":[{"prompt":"Which question comes first?","options":[{"text":"Is the sink a managed Delta sink, a custom sink or a foreachBatch?","ok":true,"fb":"Exactly-once only holds where every piece participates."},{"text":"Is the trigger interval too short?","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't create duplicates."},{"text":"Is the watermark too long?","ok":false,"fb":"Doesn't create duplicates."}]},{"prompt":"It's a foreachBatch that MERGEs into Delta and POSTs to the CRM. Where do the CRM duplicates come from?","options":[{"text":"The retried batch re-ran the non-idempotent POST","ok":true,"fb":"External side effects aren't covered by Delta transactions."},{"text":"Delta's MERGE duplicated rows","ok":false,"fb":"MERGE by key is idempotent."},{"text":"The source can't contain duplicates","ok":false,"fb":"Sources can — but here the retry explains it."}]},{"prompt":"But the Delta table has duplicates too. What else do you check?","options":[{"text":"Whether the source itself contains duplicates or the checkpoint was reset","ok":true,"fb":"Both are on the duplicates checklist."},{"text":"Whether update mode is enabled","ok":false,"fb":"Not a duplicate mechanism."},{"text":"Whether Photon is on","ok":false,"fb":"Unrelated."}]}],"explain":"Checklist: Delta sink? custom sink? foreachBatch? source duplicates? checkpoint reset? non-idempotent side effect?"},{"id":"ch13-e141","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"State store is huge. Which item is NOT on the 'state store huge' checklist?","options":["number of keys","dedup horizon","join time bounds","trigger output file size"],"answer":3,"explain":"The checklist: number of keys, watermark, window duration, late-data tolerance, join time bounds, dedup horizon, stateful key skew. Output file size belongs to the sink/small-files branch."},{"id":"ch13-e142","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Complete the monitoring calls.","text":"query.[[status]]          # what is it doing right now?\nquery.[[lastProgress]]    # metrics of the last micro-batch\nquery.[[recentProgress]]  # recent progress reports","asCode":true,"explain":"status = current activity; lastProgress = metrics of the last batch (rates, durations, state, watermark); recentProgress = history."},{"id":"ch13-e143","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":1,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"When batch durations grow while input is stable, and state metrics grow endlessly, the primary suspect is the watermark/state design.","answer":true,"explain":"Stable input + growing state → each batch does more state work. Missing watermark, too long watermark, unbounded key cardinality or a bad stateful join."},{"id":"ch13-e144","type":"order","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Stream stopped making progress: order the checks as in the playbook (mnemonic ON → FED → STUCK → ALLOWED).","items":["Is the query active?","Is the source still producing / new offsets available?","Is the checkpoint corrupted?","Is the sink blocked?","Is a state batch stuck or an executor failing?","Did a permission expire?","Was source retention violated?"],"explain":"Start with the cheapest/most obvious (is it even running? is there new data?) before deeper causes; never restart blindly."},{"id":"ch13-e145","type":"order","section":"ch13-s15","difficulty":2,"tags":["debug"],"quick":false,"q":"Batch durations grow with stable input: order the investigation.","items":["Confirm input rate is stable but duration grows","Check state metrics (numRowsTotal) across batches","Review watermark / window / join bounds / dedup horizon","Check checkpoint/state-store overhead","Check small files and sink latency"],"explain":"State is the primary suspect when input is stable; then state-store overhead; then output/sink costs."},{"id":"ch13-e146","type":"free","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["interview","exam"],"quick":true,"q":"Test Q35 (interview): How would you explain Structured Streaming?","model":"Structured Streaming treats an unbounded data source as an incrementally growing table. The developer defines transformations using Spark's DataFrame model, while the engine executes newly available data in micro-batches or supported low-latency modes. Checkpoints track source progress and commits, and state stores preserve intermediate state for aggregations, joins and deduplication. Watermarks bound that state and define lateness tolerance for event-time processing.","rubric":["unbounded source as an incrementally growing table","DataFrame model, micro-batches / low-latency modes","checkpoints: progress + commits","state stores for aggregations/joins/dedup","watermarks bound state, lateness tolerance"],"explain":"A strong answer touches model, execution, durability, state and time — in that order."},{"id":"ch13-e147","type":"cloze","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":true,"q":"Lab 1: complete the rate source.","text":"events = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .format(\"[[rate]]\")\n        .option(\"[[rowsPerSecond]]\", 10)\n        .load()\n)\ntransformed = events.withColumn(\"group_id\", F.col(\"[[value]]\") % 5)","asCode":true,"explain":"The rate source produces `timestamp` and an increasing `value` — a synthetic stream without external ingestion complexity."},{"id":"ch13-e148","type":"match","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept"],"quick":true,"q":"Match each lab to what it teaches.","pairs":[["Lab 2 — filter value % 2 = 0","stateless: no aggregate state"],["Lab 3 — groupBy(group_id).count()","stateful: Spark remembers counts"],["Lab 5 — AvailableNow rerun","checkpoint remembers progress: nothing new"],["Lab 6 — new checkpoint location","checkpoint = query identity"],["Lab 8 — foreachBatch print","micro-batches become visible"]],"explain":"Each lab isolates one engine concept; Lab 4 (windows) and Lab 7 (late data) cover event time."},{"id":"ch13-e149","type":"order","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Order the design questions of the best-practice mental model.","items":["SOURCE: ordering/duplicate guarantees?","EVENT TIME: which timestamp is reality?","LATE DATA: how late can valid records be?","STATE: what must be remembered?","WATERMARK: when can old state go?","TRIGGER: how much latency?","SINK: idempotent commits?","CHECKPOINT & RETENTION: durable progress, recoverable source?"],"explain":"Mnemonic S-E-L-S-W-T-S-C-R. These are not independent choices — each one constrains the next."},{"id":"ch13-e150","type":"write","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":3,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Mini-project Gold: from `ecommerce.silver.events`, 20-minute watermark on `event_time`, count events per 5-minute window and `event_type` as `events`, append to `ecommerce.gold.events_5min` with checkpoint `/Volumes/ecommerce/system/events_gold_5min`.","solution":"gold = (\n    spark.readStream\n        .table(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")\n        .withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")\n        .groupBy(F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\"), \"event_type\")\n        .agg(F.count(\"*\").alias(\"events\"))\n)\n(\n    gold.writeStream\n        .outputMode(\"append\")\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/events_gold_5min\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.gold.events_5min\")\n)","keywords":["readStream","ecommerce.silver.events","withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"20 minutes\")","F.window(\"event_time\", \"5 minutes\")","event_type","alias(\"events\")","outputMode(\"append\")","events_gold_5min","toTable"],"lang":"python","explain":"Stateful windowed aggregation + watermark + append: one final row per window and event type; state evicted after finalization."},{"id":"ch13-e151","type":"write","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["syntax"],"quick":false,"q":"Mini-project Silver: stream `ecommerce.bronze.events`, drop rows with NULL `event_id` or `event_time`, write with AvailableNow to `ecommerce.silver.events`, checkpoint `/Volumes/ecommerce/system/bronze_to_silver_events`.","solution":"bronze = spark.readStream.table(\"ecommerce.bronze.events\")\nsilver = (\n    bronze\n        .filter(\"event_id IS NOT NULL\")\n        .filter(\"event_time IS NOT NULL\")\n)\n(\n    silver.writeStream\n        .trigger(availableNow=True)\n        .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/Volumes/ecommerce/system/bronze_to_silver_events\")\n        .toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")\n)","keywords":["readStream","event_id IS NOT NULL","event_time IS NOT NULL","availableNow=True","bronze_to_silver_events","toTable(\"ecommerce.silver.events\")"],"lang":"python","explain":"Stateless clean/validate step: only progress is tracked, so AvailableNow on a schedule is perfect."},{"id":"ch13-e152","type":"bucket","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["pitfall","concept"],"quick":false,"q":"Mini-project test plan: what do you EXPECT to see?","buckets":["Row appears/updates correctly","Row may be dropped","Problem: reprocessing/failure risk"],"items":[{"text":"on-time events","bucket":0},{"text":"late events within the 20-min watermark","bucket":0},{"text":"very late events beyond the watermark","bucket":1},{"text":"a normal stream restart with the same checkpoint","bucket":0},{"text":"restart with a new checkpoint (sandbox)","bucket":2},{"text":"prolonged stop beyond source retention","bucket":2}],"explain":"Testing deliberately teaches the semantics: watermark tolerance, checkpoint identity and retention. (Duplicate business events would also pass through — dedup is separate.)"},{"id":"ch13-e153","type":"tf","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":1,"tags":["pitfall"],"quick":false,"q":"Lab 6 (using a new checkpoint location to see the stream restart as new) is a reasonable quick fix to try in production when a stream misbehaves.","answer":false,"explain":"Only in a sandbox. In production a new checkpoint means a new query: reprocessing, duplicate side effects and lost state."},{"id":"ch13-e154","type":"mcq","section":"ch13-s16","difficulty":2,"tags":["exam"],"quick":false,"q":"You want to run the mini-project's Gold query on a serverless job. What must change?","options":["Nothing — the default trigger works everywhere","Add .trigger(availableNow=True) and schedule the job","Switch to complete mode","Use .trigger(processingTime=\"1 minute\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serverless notebooks/jobs support AvailableNow (and deprecated Once), not default/time-based triggers. For always-on, use a Lakeflow pipeline in continuous mode."}],"src":"part2"}],"counts":{"chapters":13,"sections":184,"exercises":1644,"playbooks":106,"flashcards":580},"builtAt":"2026-10-06T15:14:34+00:00","version":"f419aea82e952106"};
